insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('9c779fba-44a8-5603-aa20-5332ef223c38', '650c0d2d-1cae-5413-9bce-092bc4d145a0', 'data-preparation-and-descriptive-statistics', 'Data Preparation and Descriptive Statistics', 'Prepare raw survey data through coding, cleaning and screening, and calculate and interpret measures of central tendency, dispersion and distribution shape for business research data.', array['Data preparation', 'Coding and cleaning', 'Descriptive statistics']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Between collecting questionnaires and running statistical tests lies a stage that rarely appears in published articles but often decides the quality of a dissertation: **data preparation**. Responses must be coded into numbers, entered or exported, checked for errors, screened for missing values and outliers, and transformed where needed. Errors at this stage silently contaminate every later analysis. A single mistyped value can shift a mean dramatically; an undeclared missing-value code such as 99 can make a five-point scale appear to average 12.\n\nOnce data are clean, **descriptive statistics** summarise them: what is typical, how much responses vary, and what shape the distribution takes. Description is the foundation for inference and is often the first set of results examiners read. This lesson covers both stages with a fully worked calculation and practical guidance relevant to tools such as IBM SPSS Statistics, Excel, R or Python.","objectives":["Explain the stages of data preparation, from codebook design to data screening.","Apply coding conventions, including reverse coding and missing-value codes, to questionnaire data.","Calculate the mean, median, mode, range, interquartile range, variance and standard deviation for a small dataset.","Compare the suitability of different descriptive measures for nominal, ordinal and interval or ratio data and for skewed distributions.","Evaluate how data entry errors and outliers affect descriptive results and decide how to handle them."],"prerequisites":["Levels of measurement and questionnaire design (Module 5).","Basic arithmetic, squaring and square roots.","Familiarity with a spreadsheet or statistical package is helpful."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Codebook","definition":"A document that defines every variable in a dataset, including its name, label, values, value labels, missing-value codes and level of measurement."},{"term":"Data cleaning","definition":"Detecting and correcting (or removing) errors, inconsistencies and impossible values in a dataset."},{"term":"Missing data","definition":"Values that are absent because a respondent skipped an item, did not know, or was routed past it."},{"term":"Outlier","definition":"A value that lies far from most other values; it may be an error or a genuine extreme case."},{"term":"Measures of central tendency","definition":"Statistics that describe the typical value, namely the mean, median and mode."},{"term":"Measures of dispersion","definition":"Statistics that describe the spread of values, such as range, interquartile range, variance and standard deviation."},{"term":"Standard deviation","definition":"The square root of the variance; roughly, the typical distance of observations from the mean, in the original units."},{"term":"Skewness","definition":"The degree of asymmetry of a distribution; positive skew has a long tail of high values."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Stage 1: Design the codebook\n\nIdeally the codebook is written *before* data collection, alongside the questionnaire. Each variable receives a short, systematic name (for example `PU1` to `PU4` for perceived usefulness items), a descriptive label, numeric codes for categories (1 = Accra, 2 = Kumasi, 3 = Takoradi), the level of measurement, and codes for different kinds of missing data (for example, 98 = \"don''t know\", 99 = \"no response\"). Consistent naming makes syntax and scale construction far easier later.\n\n### Stage 2: Enter or export data\n\nPaper questionnaires require manual entry, ideally with **double entry** of a subset by a second person to estimate the error rate. Online tools export data directly, but exports still need checking: text responses may need coding, dates may be formatted inconsistently, and multiple-response questions may arrive as single text strings that must be split into separate yes/no variables.\n\n### Stage 3: Clean the data\n\nCleaning combines systematic checks with judgement.\n\n1. **Range checks.** Run frequencies for every variable. A value of 6 on a five-point scale, or 250 employees in a \"micro-enterprise\" category, signals an error.\n2. **Consistency checks.** Cross-check logically related answers: a respondent who says they do not use mobile money should not report weekly mobile money transactions.\n3. **Duplicate checks.** Online surveys can receive repeated submissions; check identifiers and timestamps.\n4. **Return to the source.** Where possible, correct errors against the original questionnaire rather than deleting data.\n5. **Document every change** in a cleaning log so that the process is transparent and reproducible.\n\n### Stage 4: Screen for missing data and outliers\n\n**Missing data** must be declared to the software as missing, otherwise codes such as 99 are treated as real values. The pattern matters: values missing completely at random are less problematic than values missing because, for example, less profitable firms refused to answer revenue questions. Common approaches include listwise deletion (dropping cases with any missing value on the variables analysed), pairwise deletion, and more advanced methods such as multiple imputation, which Field discusses. For an MSc study, students should at least report the amount and pattern of missing data and justify their handling.\n\n**Outliers** should first be checked for errors. A genuine extreme case, such as a single very large SME in a sample of micro-enterprises, should not be deleted simply because it is inconvenient; options include reporting results with and without it, using robust statistics such as the median, or transforming the variable.\n\n### Stage 5: Transform and compute variables\n\nTypical transformations include **reverse coding** negatively worded items (`6 − x` on a five-point scale), computing **scale scores** as the mean of a construct''s items, and **recoding** continuous variables into categories for presentation (for example, firm age into bands). Always keep the original variables so that transformations can be checked.\n\n### Describing data: central tendency\n\n- **Mean**: sum of values divided by the number of values. It uses all data but is sensitive to extreme values. Appropriate for interval and ratio data.\n- **Median**: the middle value when data are ordered (the average of the two middle values if *n* is even). Robust to outliers and appropriate for ordinal data and skewed distributions, such as income or revenue.\n- **Mode**: the most frequent value; the only measure of central tendency for nominal data.\n\n### Describing data: dispersion\n\n- **Range**: maximum minus minimum; simple but determined entirely by the two most extreme values.\n- **Interquartile range (IQR)**: the spread of the middle 50% of values (Q3 − Q1); robust to outliers and pairs naturally with the median.\n- **Variance**: the average squared deviation from the mean. For a sample, divide the sum of squared deviations by `n − 1`.\n- **Standard deviation (SD)**: the square root of the variance, expressed in the original units, and pairs naturally with the mean.\n\n### Describing data: shape\n\nHistograms and box plots reveal shape. In a **positively skewed** distribution, such as monthly online sales among SMEs where a few firms sell far more than the rest, the mean is pulled above the median. Skewness and kurtosis statistics can be reported, but visual inspection is usually more informative for interpretation."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Matching descriptive statistics to measurement levels\n\n| Level of measurement | Central tendency | Dispersion | Typical display |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nominal (sector, region) | Mode | Frequencies and percentages | Bar chart, frequency table |\n| Ordinal (single Likert item, education) | Median (mode) | IQR, range, percentages per category | Bar chart, stacked bar chart |\n| Interval or ratio, symmetric (scale scores) | Mean | Standard deviation | Histogram, mean ± SD table |\n| Interval or ratio, skewed (revenue, transaction counts) | Median (report mean too) | IQR | Histogram, box plot |\n\n### The data preparation pipeline\n\nData preparation is best understood as a reproducible pipeline: raw data are never overwritten; each step is scripted (for example, with SPSS syntax or R code) or logged; and the final analysis dataset can be regenerated from the raw data. This principle protects against errors and allows examiners or collaborators to verify the work."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Codebook designed with questionnaire\"] --> B[\"Data entry or export\"]\n    B --> C[\"Range, consistency and duplicate checks\"]\n    C --> D{\"Errors found?\"}\n    D -->|\"Yes\"| E[\"Correct against source and log change\"]\n    E --> C\n    D -->|\"No\"| F[\"Declare missing values; assess pattern\"]\n    F --> G[\"Investigate outliers: error or genuine?\"]\n    G --> H[\"Reverse-code and compute scale scores\"]\n    H --> I[\"Descriptive statistics and charts\"]\n    I --> J[\"Inferential analysis\"]\n```"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** Adwoa surveys ten small online retailers in Accra about their monthly digital sales in thousands of Ghana cedis (GH¢000). After cleaning, the values are:\n\n```\n12, 15, 9, 20, 14, 11, 18, 13, 16, 12\n```\n\n**Step 1: Mean.**\n\n```\nSum = 12 + 15 + 9 + 20 + 14 + 11 + 18 + 13 + 16 + 12 = 140\nMean = 140 / 10 = 14.0\n```\n\n**Step 2: Median and mode.** Order the data:\n\n```\n9, 11, 12, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20\n```\n\nWith *n* = 10, the median is the average of the 5th and 6th values: `(13 + 14) / 2 = 13.5`. The mode is 12 (it appears twice).\n\n**Step 3: Range and IQR.**\n\n```\nRange = 20 − 9 = 11\nLower half: 9, 11, 12, 12, 13 → Q1 = 12\nUpper half: 14, 15, 16, 18, 20 → Q3 = 16\nIQR = 16 − 12 = 4\n```\n\n(Software packages use slightly different quartile methods, so IQR values may differ marginally; state which method you used.)\n\n**Step 4: Variance and standard deviation.**\n\n| Value (x) | Deviation (x − 14) | Squared deviation |\n|---|---|---|\n| 12 | −2 | 4 |\n| 15 | 1 | 1 |\n| 9 | −5 | 25 |\n| 20 | 6 | 36 |\n| 14 | 0 | 0 |\n| 11 | −3 | 9 |\n| 18 | 4 | 16 |\n| 13 | −1 | 1 |\n| 16 | 2 | 4 |\n| 12 | −2 | 4 |\n| **Sum** | **0** | **100** |\n\n```\nSample variance s² = 100 / (10 − 1) = 11.11\nStandard deviation s = √11.11 = 3.33\n```\n\n**Interpretation:** typical monthly digital sales are about GH¢14,000, with sales typically about GH¢3,330 either side of that average. Mean and median are close (14.0 versus 13.5), suggesting a roughly symmetric distribution.\n\n**Step 5: The effect of a data entry error.** Suppose the value 20 had been mistyped as 200.\n\n```\nNew sum = 140 − 20 + 200 = 320\nNew mean = 320 / 10 = 32.0\nNew median = 13.5 (unchanged: the middle values are the same)\n```\n\nOne keystroke more than doubled the mean while leaving the median untouched. This illustrates why range checks matter and why the median is preferred for skewed or outlier-prone data. **Expected output:** mean 14.0, median 13.5, mode 12, range 11, IQR 4, SD 3.33, and a written interpretation including the outlier effect."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Data preparation challenges are common in Ghanaian business research.\n\n- **Currency and units.** Revenue may be reported in cedis, in thousands of cedis, or occasionally in old cedi denominations by older respondents, and some may answer in US dollars. The codebook should specify units, and cleaning should standardise them.\n- **Mobile money transaction data.** Researchers working with transaction logs from a firm (with permission) often face highly skewed distributions: many small transactions and a few very large ones. Medians and IQRs, or log transformations, give a more truthful summary than means alone.\n- **Multiple languages and open responses.** Open answers recorded in Twi, Ga or Pidgin English need consistent coding into categories, ideally by two coders who agree a coding scheme.\n- **Paper questionnaires in hospitals or markets.** Manual entry is common; double entry of a sample helps estimate error rates.\n- **Missing financial data.** SME owners frequently decline revenue questions. Reporting the proportion missing, and comparing respondents who answered with those who did not on other characteristics (such as sector or size), helps readers judge the risk of bias.\n- **Data protection.** Cleaned datasets should be stripped of names and phone numbers (pseudonymised) before analysis and sharing, consistent with the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843).\n\nFor student cybersecurity surveys, descriptive tables of awareness scores by faculty (means and SDs) and frequencies of risky behaviours (percentages) usually form the opening results section."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### The Reinhart-Rogoff spreadsheet error\n\nIn 2010, economists Carmen Reinhart and Kenneth Rogoff published an influential paper, \"Growth in a Time of Debt\", which was widely cited in debates about government austerity. In 2013, Thomas Herndon, Michael Ash and Robert Pollin of the University of Massachusetts Amherst attempted to replicate the results using the original spreadsheet. They reported several problems, including a spreadsheet coding error that excluded some countries from a key average, the selective exclusion of certain data, and an unconventional weighting of country averages. Their corrected analysis showed a considerably weaker relationship between high public debt and low growth than the original paper had suggested. Reinhart and Rogoff acknowledged the spreadsheet error while defending other aspects of their work.\n\nThe case is directly relevant to data preparation. First, it shows that errors in coding and data handling, not only in statistical theory, can change substantive conclusions. Second, the error was found only because the authors shared their spreadsheet, illustrating the value of transparency and reproducible workflows. Third, decisions about which cases to include and how to weight them are analytical choices that must be documented and justified. For MSc students, the practical lessons are to keep raw data untouched, script or log every transformation, check that averages include all intended cases, and be prepared to share the analysis file with supervisors and examiners."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** Cleaning data means deleting anything that looks unusual. **Correction:** Cleaning corrects errors; genuine extreme values should be investigated and handled transparently, not deleted for convenience.","**Misconception:** The mean is always the best summary of a typical value. **Correction:** For skewed data or data with outliers, the median often represents the typical case better.","**Misconception:** Missing values can be ignored because software handles them. **Correction:** Software only handles values declared as missing; undeclared codes such as 99 corrupt results, and the pattern of missingness can bias findings.","**Misconception:** Standard deviation and variance measure the same thing in the same units. **Correction:** Variance is in squared units; the standard deviation returns to the original units and is easier to interpret.","**Misconception:** Descriptive statistics are trivial and do not need interpretation. **Correction:** Descriptive results should be interpreted in context and often reveal patterns that shape later analysis."],"examMistakes":["Dividing by *n* rather than *n − 1* when computing a sample variance without stating which is used.","Reporting means for nominal variables such as sector or region.","Forgetting to order data before finding the median.","Presenting descriptive tables without any interpretation of what the numbers mean for the research question.","Omitting any mention of data cleaning, missing data or outliers in a methodology answer."],"criticalQuestions":["When is it legitimate to remove an outlier from a dataset, and what must the researcher report if they do so?","A survey of SME owners has 30% missing data on revenue. What risks does this create, and how would you decide whether to use listwise deletion or another approach?","Should single Likert items be summarised with means or medians? Defend a position and explain how you would report it to an examiner.","The Reinhart-Rogoff case was discovered through replication. How could MSc programmes encourage reproducible data preparation among students?"],"exercises":["**Build a codebook.** For a short questionnaire on student cybersecurity awareness (faculty, level, gender, four awareness items, one negatively worded item, weekly hours online), write a codebook. *Expected output:* a table with variable names, labels, value codes, missing-value codes, measurement level and a note that the negative item will be reverse-coded with `6 − x`.","**Compute descriptives.** The number of AI tools used by eight SMEs is `0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 5`. Compute the mean, median, mode and sample standard deviation. *Expected output:* mean = 2.0, median = 2, mode = 2; squared deviations sum to `4 + 1 + 1 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 1 + 9 = 16`, so `s² = 16 / 7 = 2.29` and `s = 1.51`.","**Spot the errors.** Given a dataset extract containing a value of 7 on a five-point item, a respondent aged 9 in an SME owner survey, and a code of 99 for an unanswered item, describe how you would detect and handle each. *Expected output:* three short explanations referring to range checks, consistency checks and declaring missing values."],"takeaways":["Data preparation (codebook, entry, cleaning, screening, transformation) determines the trustworthiness of every later result.","Declare missing-value codes, examine missingness patterns and document how missing data were handled.","Check outliers for errors; handle genuine extremes transparently.","Match descriptive statistics to measurement level and distribution shape: mean and SD for symmetric interval data; median and IQR for skewed or ordinal data; mode and percentages for nominal data.","Keep raw data untouched and log or script every change."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Field, A. (2018). *Discovering statistics using IBM SPSS statistics* (5th ed.). SAGE.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Saunders, M., Lewis, P., & Thornhill, A. (2019). *Research methods for business students* (8th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Herndon, T., Ash, M., & Pollin, R. (2014). Does high public debt consistently stifle economic growth? A critique of Reinhart and Rogoff. *Cambridge Journal of Economics, 38*(2), 257-279.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"NIST/SEMATECH. (n.d.). *e-Handbook of statistical methods*. National Institute of Standards and Technology.","url":"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"Khan Academy: Statistics and Probability","url":"https://www.khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability","description":"Khan Academy. Free official course with units on summarising quantitative data, including mean, median, IQR and standard deviation.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"IBM SPSS Statistics","url":"https://www.ibm.com/products/spss-statistics","description":"IBM. Official product page with links to documentation and learning resources for data preparation and descriptive statistics in SPSS.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Data preparation turns raw responses into a trustworthy dataset through a codebook, careful entry or export, range and consistency checks, declared missing values, investigated outliers and documented transformations. Descriptive statistics then summarise the typical value (mean, median, mode), the spread (range, IQR, variance, SD) and the shape of each distribution, chosen to suit the level of measurement.\n\n- Sample variance divides by `n − 1`; SD = √variance.\n- Mean is sensitive to outliers; median is robust.\n- Mode for nominal; median and IQR for ordinal or skewed; mean and SD for symmetric interval data.\n- Undeclared codes such as 99 corrupt means.\n- Log every cleaning decision; never overwrite raw data."}'::jsonb, 2699, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('ebf3fcf1-e670-587a-94f2-4f790074f018', '650c0d2d-1cae-5413-9bce-092bc4d145a0', 'inferential-statistics', 'Fundamentals of Inferential Statistics', 'Apply the logic of hypothesis testing, compute and interpret a confidence interval, a one-sample t statistic, a chi-square test and an effect size, and report p-values responsibly.', array['Inferential statistics fundamentals', 'Hypothesis testing', 'p-values', 'Confidence intervals']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Descriptive statistics tell you about the people or firms you actually studied. Most research, however, wants to say something about a wider population: *all* registered SMEs in Accra, *all* undergraduate students at a university, *all* retailers in a market. **Inferential statistics** provide the tools for moving from a sample to a population while quantifying the uncertainty involved.\n\nThis lesson explains the logic behind inference rather than treating it as a set of software buttons. You will learn why sampling distributions matter, how hypothesis tests and confidence intervals work, what a p-value does and does not mean, how to choose a basic test, and why effect sizes and confidence intervals deserve as much attention as statistical significance. Worked calculations use realistic Ghanaian research scenarios, and the international case study examines the American Statistical Association''s influential statement on p-values.","objectives":["Explain the logic of statistical inference, including sampling distributions and standard errors.","Apply the steps of null hypothesis significance testing to a research hypothesis.","Calculate and interpret a 95% confidence interval for a mean, a one-sample t statistic and Cohen''s d.","Calculate and interpret a chi-square test of independence for a 2 × 2 table.","Evaluate common misinterpretations of p-values and justify reporting effect sizes and confidence intervals."],"prerequisites":["Descriptive statistics, especially mean, standard deviation and variance (previous lesson).","Research hypotheses and their alignment with objectives (Module 2).","Probability sampling and margin of error (Module 5)."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Population parameter","definition":"A numerical characteristic of a population, such as the population mean μ, usually unknown."},{"term":"Sample statistic","definition":"A numerical characteristic calculated from a sample, such as the sample mean x̄, used to estimate a parameter."},{"term":"Standard error (SE)","definition":"The standard deviation of a statistic''s sampling distribution; for a mean, `SE = s / √n`."},{"term":"Null hypothesis (H0)","definition":"The hypothesis of no effect, no difference or no relationship that a significance test evaluates."},{"term":"p-value","definition":"The probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme as the one observed, assuming the null hypothesis and all model assumptions are true."},{"term":"Significance level (α)","definition":"The threshold, often 0.05, below which a p-value leads the researcher to reject H0."},{"term":"Confidence interval (CI)","definition":"A range of plausible values for a parameter, constructed by a procedure that captures the true value in a stated proportion (for example 95%) of repeated samples."},{"term":"Effect size","definition":"A measure of the magnitude of an effect or relationship, such as Cohen''s d or a correlation coefficient, that does not depend on sample size in the way p-values do."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Why inference is needed\n\nIf you drew many different random samples of 100 students and measured their cybersecurity awareness, each sample mean would differ slightly from the others because of chance. The distribution of these hypothetical sample means is the **sampling distribution**. The **central limit theorem** tells us that, for reasonably large samples, this distribution is approximately normal and centred on the population mean, with a spread equal to the **standard error**. Inference uses the standard error to judge how far a sample result is likely to be from the true population value.\n\nInference is only as good as the sample. Formulas assume probability sampling; with convenience samples, p-values and confidence intervals describe uncertainty under assumptions that may not hold, and must be interpreted cautiously.\n\n### Null hypothesis significance testing\n\nA significance test follows a standard sequence.\n\n1. **State hypotheses.** For example, H0: the mean awareness score equals 3.0 (the neutral midpoint); H1: it differs from 3.0.\n2. **Choose α**, conventionally 0.05, before seeing the data.\n3. **Select the test** appropriate to the data and design.\n4. **Check assumptions**, such as independence, approximate normality or adequate expected frequencies.\n5. **Compute the test statistic** and its p-value.\n6. **Decide**: if `p < α`, reject H0; otherwise, fail to reject H0.\n7. **Interpret substantively**, reporting the effect size and confidence interval.\n\n\"Fail to reject\" is not the same as \"accept\": a non-significant result may reflect a small sample rather than the absence of an effect.\n\n### Errors and power\n\n| Decision | H0 actually true | H0 actually false |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reject H0 | **Type I error** (false positive), probability α | Correct decision, probability = power |\n| Fail to reject H0 | Correct decision | **Type II error** (false negative), probability β |\n\n**Power** (`1 − β`) is the probability of detecting a true effect. It increases with sample size, effect size and α. Cohen proposed 0.80 as a conventional target. Underpowered studies are common in student research and produce unstable results.\n\n### One-tailed and two-tailed tests\n\nA two-tailed test asks whether a value differs in either direction; a one-tailed test examines one direction only. One-tailed tests should be specified in advance with strong theoretical justification; switching to a one-tailed test after seeing the data to obtain significance is inappropriate.\n\n### Confidence intervals\n\nA 95% confidence interval for a mean, with a reasonably large sample, is approximately:\n\n```\nx̄ ± 1.96 × SE\n```\n\nFor small samples, the multiplier comes from the t-distribution with `n − 1` degrees of freedom and is slightly larger than 1.96. A confidence interval conveys both the estimate and its precision. If a 95% CI for a mean excludes a hypothesised value, a two-tailed test at α = 0.05 would reject that value, so the CI contains the information of the test and more.\n\n### Choosing a basic test\n\n| Research question | Data | Common parametric test | Common non-parametric alternative |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Does a mean differ from a fixed value? | One interval variable | One-sample t-test | Wilcoxon signed-rank test |\n| Do two independent groups differ? | Interval outcome, two groups | Independent-samples t-test | Mann-Whitney U test |\n| Do the same people differ at two times? | Interval outcome, paired | Paired-samples t-test | Wilcoxon signed-rank test |\n| Do three or more groups differ? | Interval outcome, 3+ groups | One-way ANOVA | Kruskal-Wallis test |\n| Are two categorical variables associated? | Two nominal variables | Chi-square test of independence | Fisher''s exact test (small samples) |\n| Are two interval variables related? | Two interval variables | Pearson correlation | Spearman''s rank correlation |\n\nField''s *Discovering Statistics Using IBM SPSS Statistics* gives detailed guidance on assumptions and procedures for each test."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### What a p-value means and does not mean\n\nA p-value of 0.03 means: *if H0 and the model assumptions were true, results as extreme as these, or more so, would occur about 3% of the time.* It does **not** mean:\n\n- that there is a 3% probability that H0 is true;\n- that there is a 97% probability that the hypothesis is correct;\n- that the effect is large or important;\n- that the result will replicate.\n\n### Statistical versus practical significance\n\nWith very large samples, trivial effects become statistically significant; with small samples, important effects may not reach significance. **Effect sizes** address magnitude. Cohen''s d for a mean difference is:\n\n```\nd = (difference in means) / standard deviation\n```\n\nCohen suggested approximate benchmarks of 0.2 (small), 0.5 (medium) and 0.8 (large), while cautioning that what counts as important depends on context. A modest effect on phishing susceptibility may be practically valuable for a bank if it prevents costly incidents."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Research hypothesis\"] --> B[\"State H0 and H1; set alpha\"]\n    B --> C[\"Choose test from data type and design\"]\n    C --> D[\"Check assumptions\"]\n    D --> E[\"Compute test statistic and p-value\"]\n    E --> F{\"p below alpha?\"}\n    F -->|\"Yes\"| G[\"Reject H0\"]\n    F -->|\"No\"| H[\"Fail to reject H0\"]\n    G --> I[\"Report effect size and confidence interval\"]\n    H --> I\n    I --> J[\"Interpret in context: practical importance and limitations\"]\n```"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Part A: Confidence interval, t-test and effect size\n\n**Illustrative scenario.** Nana surveys a random sample of 100 undergraduate students at a Ghanaian university using a validated five-point cybersecurity awareness scale (1 = very low, 5 = very high). She wants to know whether average awareness differs from the neutral midpoint of 3.0.\n\n```\nn = 100,  x̄ = 3.40,  s = 0.80\n```\n\n**Step 1: Standard error.**\n\n```\nSE = s / √n = 0.80 / √100 = 0.80 / 10 = 0.08\n```\n\n**Step 2: 95% confidence interval.**\n\n```\n3.40 ± 1.96 × 0.08 = 3.40 ± 0.157\nCI = (3.24, 3.56)\n```\n\n(Using the t multiplier for 99 degrees of freedom, about 1.98, gives nearly the same interval.)\n\n**Step 3: One-sample t-test.** H0: μ = 3.0; H1: μ ≠ 3.0; α = 0.05.\n\n```\nt = (x̄ − μ0) / SE = (3.40 − 3.00) / 0.08 = 5.0,  df = 99\n```\n\nA t value of 5.0 with 99 degrees of freedom corresponds to `p < .001`. Nana rejects H0.\n\n**Step 4: Effect size.**\n\n```\nd = (3.40 − 3.00) / 0.80 = 0.50\n```\n\nThis is a medium effect by Cohen''s conventions.\n\n**Step 5: Report.** *\"Mean cybersecurity awareness (M = 3.40, SD = 0.80) was significantly above the scale midpoint, t(99) = 5.00, p < .001, d = 0.50, 95% CI [3.24, 3.56].\"* Interpretation: awareness is moderately above neutral, but even the upper end of the interval (3.56) is well below \"high\" (4), suggesting room for improvement.\n\n### Part B: Chi-square test of independence\n\n**Illustrative scenario.** Nana also records whether 200 students clicked a simulated phishing link, comparing those who had attended a training session with those who had not.\n\n| | Clicked | Did not click | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Trained | 12 | 68 | 80 |\n| Not trained | 36 | 84 | 120 |\n| **Total** | **48** | **152** | **200** |\n\n**Step 1: Expected frequencies** under H0 (no association): `E = (row total × column total) / grand total`.\n\n```\nTrained, clicked:       80 × 48 / 200 = 19.2\nTrained, not clicked:   80 × 152 / 200 = 60.8\nUntrained, clicked:     120 × 48 / 200 = 28.8\nUntrained, not clicked: 120 × 152 / 200 = 91.2\n```\n\nAll expected frequencies exceed 5, so the chi-square approximation is acceptable.\n\n**Step 2: Chi-square statistic:** `χ² = Σ (O − E)² / E`.\n\n```\n(12 − 19.2)² / 19.2 = 51.84 / 19.2 = 2.700\n(68 − 60.8)² / 60.8 = 51.84 / 60.8 = 0.853\n(36 − 28.8)² / 28.8 = 51.84 / 28.8 = 1.800\n(84 − 91.2)² / 91.2 = 51.84 / 91.2 = 0.568\nχ² = 5.92,  df = (2 − 1) × (2 − 1) = 1\n```\n\n**Step 3: Decide.** The critical value of χ² at α = 0.05 with 1 df is 3.84. Since 5.92 > 3.84, H0 is rejected; the p-value lies between 0.01 and 0.025.\n\n**Step 4: Interpret.** Click rates were 15% among trained students (12/80) and 30% among untrained students (36/120). Training is associated with a lower click rate. Because students chose whether to attend training, this is an association, not proof of causation; more security-conscious students may have been more likely to attend.\n\n**Expected output:** CI [3.24, 3.56], t(99) = 5.00, d = 0.50, χ²(1) = 5.92, with correct decisions and calibrated interpretations."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Inferential statistics underpin many decisions in Ghanaian organisations and research.\n\n- **Digital payment adoption.** A researcher might test whether the proportion of retailers accepting merchant payments differs between Accra and Tamale (chi-square), or whether mean perceived transaction cost differs between adopters and non-adopters (independent-samples t-test). Reporting the size of the difference, not just its significance, helps fintech managers judge whether the gap matters commercially.\n- **AI adoption among SMEs.** An SME support programme might compare mean efficiency scores before and after an AI bookkeeping pilot for the same firms (paired-samples t-test), while recognising the threats to internal validity discussed in Module 4.\n- **Healthcare technology.** Hospital managers may compare average patient waiting times across departments using a new system (ANOVA), but waiting times are often skewed, so a non-parametric test or a transformation may be more appropriate.\n- **Student cybersecurity awareness.** Comparisons across faculties often involve multiple tests. Running many tests at α = 0.05 inflates the chance of false positives; researchers should plan comparisons in advance or adjust for multiple testing.\n\nA recurring issue in Ghanaian MSc research is the use of inferential tests on convenience samples. Tests can still be run, but students should state that p-values assume random sampling and interpret them as indicative rather than definitive."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### The American Statistical Association''s statement on p-values\n\nIn 2016 the American Statistical Association (ASA) took the unusual step of issuing a formal statement on statistical significance and p-values, published by Ronald Wasserstein and Nicole Lazar in *The American Statistician*. The statement was prompted by concerns that misuse and misinterpretation of p-values were contributing to unreliable research findings across disciplines, a concern linked to the broader \"replication crisis\" discussed in Module 1.\n\nThe statement set out six principles. In summary: p-values can indicate how incompatible data are with a specified statistical model; they do not measure the probability that the studied hypothesis is true or that the data arose by chance alone; scientific conclusions and business or policy decisions should not be based only on whether a p-value passes a threshold; proper inference requires full reporting and transparency; a p-value does not measure the size of an effect or the importance of a result; and by itself a p-value does not provide a good measure of evidence regarding a model or hypothesis.\n\nThe statement did not ban p-values, but it encouraged researchers to supplement or replace them with estimation (confidence intervals), effect sizes and other approaches, and to report all analyses performed rather than only significant ones. For MSc students, its practical implications are clear: never write \"p < .05, therefore the hypothesis is proven\"; always report effect sizes and confidence intervals; describe all tests conducted; and interpret findings in light of design quality, sample and context."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** A p-value is the probability that the null hypothesis is true. **Correction:** It is the probability of data at least as extreme as observed, assuming H0 is true.","**Misconception:** A non-significant result proves there is no effect. **Correction:** It means the data are not sufficiently incompatible with H0; the study may have lacked power.","**Misconception:** Smaller p-values mean larger effects. **Correction:** p-values depend heavily on sample size; effect sizes measure magnitude.","**Misconception:** A 95% CI means there is a 95% probability that the true value lies in this particular interval. **Correction:** Strictly, 95% of intervals constructed by this procedure would contain the true value; any single interval either does or does not.","**Misconception:** Statistical significance means practical importance. **Correction:** Importance depends on effect size, costs, benefits and context."],"examMistakes":["Stating hypotheses about sample statistics (x̄) instead of population parameters (μ).","Writing \"accept H0\" instead of \"fail to reject H0\".","Choosing a test without considering the measurement level of the variables or the design (independent versus paired).","Reporting a significant result without an effect size or confidence interval.","Interpreting a significant chi-square result from observational data as evidence of causation."],"criticalQuestions":["If a study of 5,000 mobile money users finds a statistically significant but tiny difference in satisfaction between two providers, how should a manager interpret it?","Should MSc dissertations based on convenience samples report p-values at all? Justify your answer with reference to the ASA statement.","A student runs twenty t-tests comparing faculties and finds one significant at p = 0.04. What should the student conclude, and why?","How might a confidence interval change the way a hospital board interprets a reduction in average waiting time after a new system is introduced?"],"exercises":["**Confidence interval.** A random sample of 64 SMEs reports a mean AI-readiness score of 2.8 (SD = 1.2) on a five-point scale. Compute the standard error and the 95% CI. *Expected output:* `SE = 1.2 / 8 = 0.15`; `CI = 2.8 ± 1.96 × 0.15 = 2.8 ± 0.294 = (2.51, 3.09)`, interpreted as a plausible range that includes the midpoint 3.0.","**Hypothesis test.** Using the same data, test H0: μ = 3.0 at α = 0.05 and compute Cohen''s d. *Expected output:* `t = (2.8 − 3.0) / 0.15 = −1.33`, df = 63, not significant (|t| < about 2.00); `d = −0.2 / 1.2 = −0.17`, a small effect; conclusion: fail to reject H0, consistent with the CI including 3.0.","**Choose the test.** For each question, name the appropriate test: (a) Do adopters and non-adopters of e-commerce differ in mean firm age? (b) Is gender associated with password reuse (yes/no)? (c) Do nurses'' satisfaction scores differ before and after EHR training? *Expected output:* (a) independent-samples t-test (or Mann-Whitney U), (b) chi-square test of independence, (c) paired-samples t-test (or Wilcoxon signed-rank)."],"takeaways":["Inference uses the sampling distribution and standard error to generalise from sample to population under stated assumptions.","Hypothesis testing follows a fixed sequence, and \"fail to reject\" is not \"accept\".","Type I and Type II errors trade off; power depends on sample size, effect size and α.","Confidence intervals and effect sizes convey precision and magnitude that p-values cannot.","p-values do not measure the probability that a hypothesis is true, the size of an effect or its importance."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Field, A. (2018). *Discovering statistics using IBM SPSS statistics* (5th ed.). SAGE.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Cohen, J. (1988). *Statistical power analysis for the behavioral sciences* (2nd ed.). Lawrence Erlbaum Associates.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Wasserstein, R. L., & Lazar, N. A. (2016). The ASA''s statement on p-values: Context, process, and purpose. *The American Statistician, 70*(2), 129-133.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.2016.1154108","verification":"unverified"},{"text":"Saunders, M., Lewis, P., & Thornhill, A. (2019). *Research methods for business students* (8th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Khan Academy: Statistics and Probability","url":"https://www.khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability","description":"Khan Academy. Free official course with units on confidence intervals, significance tests and chi-square tests.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"MIT OpenCourseWare","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/","description":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology. Official open learning portal; search for introductory probability and statistics course materials.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Inferential statistics generalise from samples to populations by using the standard error to quantify chance variation. Significance tests evaluate how compatible data are with a null hypothesis, while confidence intervals and effect sizes convey precision and magnitude; responsible reporting combines all three and interprets results in light of design and context.\n\n- `SE = s / √n`; 95% CI ≈ `x̄ ± 1.96 × SE`.\n- One-sample t = `(x̄ − μ0) / SE`; Cohen''s d = difference / SD.\n- Chi-square: `E = row × column / total`; `χ² = Σ(O − E)²/E`; df = (r − 1)(c − 1).\n- Type I = false positive (α); Type II = false negative (β); power = 1 − β.\n- p-value ≠ probability H0 is true; significance ≠ importance."}'::jsonb, 2775, 2, true)
