insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('c8963a7d-37ea-5c6c-adc5-35f192812339', '323e77f6-364c-52b5-b9cc-91ec03945796', 'academic-writing-and-abstracts', 'Academic Writing and Abstract Writing', 'Write clear, critical and well-structured academic prose and compose a complete, informative abstract for a proposal or dissertation.', array['Academic writing', 'Abstract writing']::text[], 40, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Good research can be undermined by poor writing. Examiners cannot reward insight they cannot find, and a methodology that is brilliantly designed but vaguely described looks weak on paper. Academic writing is not about long words or complicated sentences; it is about making a precise, evidence-based argument that a specialist reader can follow, check and evaluate. For many MSc IT for Business students, who may be accustomed to technical documentation or business reports, the shift to academic argument is one of the most important adjustments of postgraduate study.\n\nThis lesson explains the characteristics of academic writing, the difference between descriptive and critical writing, techniques for structuring paragraphs and arguments, and the conventions of academic tone, hedging and clarity. It then focuses on one of the most-read and most-neglected parts of any research document: the **abstract**. You will learn what an abstract must contain, how proposal and dissertation abstracts differ, and how to draft and revise one step by step.","objectives":["Explain the defining characteristics of academic writing, including argument, evidence, precision and appropriate tone.","Distinguish descriptive from critical writing and transform descriptive passages into critical ones.","Apply paragraph structures such as point-evidence-analysis-link to build coherent arguments.","Use hedging and precise language to make claims proportionate to evidence.","Construct an informative abstract for a proposal and for a completed study, including purpose, method, findings and implications."],"prerequisites":["Familiarity with the literature review and synthesis (Module 3).","Understanding of research objectives, questions and designs (Modules 2 and 4).","General competence in written English; this lesson focuses on academic conventions rather than grammar basics."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Academic writing","definition":"Formal, evidence-based writing that presents a reasoned argument to a scholarly audience and acknowledges sources."},{"term":"Critical writing","definition":"Writing that evaluates, compares and interprets evidence to support an argument, rather than merely reporting it."},{"term":"Descriptive writing","definition":"Writing that reports what sources say or what happened without analysis or evaluation."},{"term":"Hedging","definition":"Using cautious language (for example \"suggests\", \"may\", \"appears to\") so that claims match the strength of the evidence."},{"term":"Topic sentence","definition":"The sentence, usually first in a paragraph, that states the paragraph''s main point."},{"term":"Signposting","definition":"Words and phrases that guide the reader through the structure of an argument."},{"term":"Abstract","definition":"A concise, self-contained summary of a research document''s purpose, methods, findings and implications."},{"term":"Structured abstract","definition":"An abstract organised under explicit headings such as purpose, design/methodology/approach, findings and originality/value."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Characteristics of academic writing\n\nAcademic writing differs from other genres in several ways:\n\n1. **It argues.** Every chapter advances a position: that a problem matters, that a gap exists, that a design is appropriate, that findings mean something. Writing that merely collects information lacks this spine.\n2. **It is evidence-based.** Claims are supported by data or by cited scholarship. \"Mobile money has transformed retail in Ghana\" needs a source or should be reframed.\n3. **It is precise.** Vague words (\"many\", \"a lot\", \"recently\", \"things\") are replaced with specific terms, defined constructs and accurate descriptions.\n4. **It is objective in tone, not necessarily impersonal.** Emotive language (\"shocking\", \"amazing\") is avoided. Use of \"I\" or \"we\" is increasingly accepted in some disciplines, particularly for describing what the researcher did, but you should follow your supervisor''s and institution''s guidance.\n5. **It acknowledges sources.** Ideas, data and words from others are cited consistently (see the APA 7 lesson).\n6. **It is structured.** Readers expect conventional sections, logical ordering and clear transitions.\n\n### Descriptive versus critical writing\n\nCompare two passages on the Technology Acceptance Model.\n\n> *Descriptive:* Davis (1989) developed TAM. It has two constructs, perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use. Many studies have used TAM.\n\n> *Critical:* Davis''s (1989) Technology Acceptance Model remains attractive because its two core beliefs, perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use, are parsimonious and easy to measure. However, this parsimony is also its weakness: the model largely omits social and organisational influences, which may be decisive where adoption decisions are made by owners on behalf of employees, as in many Ghanaian SMEs. This limitation partly motivated extended models such as UTAUT (Venkatesh et al., 2003).\n\nThe critical version evaluates (strength and weakness), interprets (why it matters in context) and connects (to later work and to the study). A useful test is to ask of each paragraph: *so what?* If the paragraph does not answer that question, it is probably descriptive.\n\n### Building paragraphs: PEAL\n\nA reliable structure for academic paragraphs is **Point-Evidence-Analysis-Link (PEAL)**:\n\n- **Point**: a topic sentence stating the claim.\n- **Evidence**: data, examples or citations supporting the claim.\n- **Analysis**: explanation of what the evidence shows, its limits and its significance.\n- **Link**: a sentence connecting back to the argument or forward to the next paragraph.\n\nParagraphs should normally develop one main idea. Very long paragraphs often contain two arguments that should be separated; one-sentence paragraphs usually signal an undeveloped idea.\n\n### Hedging and proportionate claims\n\nResearch rarely proves anything conclusively. Over-claiming (\"This study proves that AI increases efficiency\") invites criticism; under-claiming (\"It might perhaps possibly be that...\") obscures your contribution. Hedging aims for proportion:\n\n| Strength of evidence | Example phrasing |\n|---|---|\n| Strong and consistent | \"The results show...\", \"There is strong evidence that...\" |\n| Moderate | \"The findings suggest...\", \"This indicates...\" |\n| Weak or tentative | \"It is possible that...\", \"One explanation may be...\" |\n| Speculative | \"Future research could examine whether...\" |\n\n### Clarity at sentence level\n\n- Prefer active voice when it clarifies who did what (\"The researcher interviewed twelve managers\"), while recognising that passive voice is acceptable when the actor is unimportant (\"Data were analysed using thematic analysis\").\n- Put the main point near the start of the sentence.\n- Define acronyms on first use: \"small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs)\".\n- Use terms consistently: if you define \"digital payment adoption\", do not switch to \"e-payment usage\" without explanation.\n- Avoid unsupported generalisations (\"Everyone knows...\", \"It is obvious that...\").\n\n### Abstracts: purpose and content\n\nThe abstract is often the only part of a document that many readers, reviewers and examiners read first. It must stand alone, with no citations (in most cases), no undefined abbreviations and no information absent from the main text. Most guidance, including the APA *Publication Manual* (7th ed.), recommends a concise length; many universities and journals set a specific word limit, commonly in the range of 150-300 words, so always check your own requirement.\n\nAn informative abstract for a **completed study** answers five questions:\n\n1. **Context and problem**: Why does the study matter?\n2. **Purpose**: What did it aim to do?\n3. **Method**: How was it done (design, sample, data, analysis)?\n4. **Findings**: What were the main results?\n5. **Implications**: What do the results mean for theory, practice or policy?\n\nA **proposal abstract** follows the same logic but uses future tense for methods and replaces findings with **expected contributions**, since no data have been collected yet.\n\nSome journals, including many Emerald publications, require **structured abstracts** with headings such as *Purpose*, *Design/methodology/approach*, *Findings*, *Research limitations/implications*, *Practical implications* and *Originality/value*. Even when unstructured abstracts are required, drafting under these headings first is a valuable discipline."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The CARS model for introductions and abstracts\n\nSwales''s genre analysis of research introductions produced the **Create a Research Space (CARS)** model, explained in Swales and Feak''s *Academic Writing for Graduate Students*. It describes three \"moves\":\n\n1. **Establishing a territory**: showing that the research area is important.\n2. **Establishing a niche**: identifying a gap, problem or question in existing work.\n3. **Occupying the niche**: stating the purpose of the present study and how it fills the gap.\n\nThe opening sentences of a good abstract compress these moves into one or two sentences before moving on to method and findings.\n\n### The writing process as iteration\n\nExpert writers rarely produce polished prose in one draft. A practical model is: **plan** (outline the argument), **draft** (write freely without editing), **revise** (restructure for argument and flow), **edit** (sentence-level clarity) and **proofread** (errors and formatting). Separating these stages prevents the paralysis of trying to write perfect sentences before the argument is clear."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** A student''s first abstract draft for a completed study reads:\n\n> \"This study is about AI. AI is very important nowadays. SMEs in Ghana were studied. A questionnaire was used and the results were analysed. The results were interesting and showed many things. Recommendations are given.\"\n\n**Step 1: Diagnose.** The draft lacks a specific problem, purpose, sample, analysis method, actual findings and implications. Words such as \"very important\", \"interesting\" and \"many things\" are vague.\n\n**Step 2: Draft under structured headings.**\n\n- *Context/problem*: Ghanaian SMEs increasingly encounter AI-enabled tools, yet evidence on whether adoption improves operational efficiency is limited.\n- *Purpose*: Examine the relationship between AI adoption and perceived operational efficiency, and the role of organisational readiness.\n- *Method*: Cross-sectional survey of SME owner-managers in Accra; stratified sample; regression analysis.\n- *Findings*: (state the actual direction and nature of the main results).\n- *Implications*: For SME owners, technology vendors and policy-makers.\n\n**Step 3: Combine into prose (format example with placeholder findings).**\n\n> \"Although artificial intelligence (AI) tools are increasingly available to small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) in Ghana, little empirical evidence exists on whether their adoption improves operational efficiency. Drawing on the technology-organisation-environment framework, this study examined the relationship between AI adoption and perceived operational efficiency among SMEs in Accra, and whether organisational readiness moderates that relationship. A cross-sectional survey of owner-managers, selected through stratified random sampling by sector, was analysed using multiple regression. [State the principal findings here, for example the direction and significance of the main relationship and the moderating effect.] The findings contribute to technology adoption research by extending it to the SME context in Ghana and suggest that SMEs should invest in staff skills and data infrastructure alongside AI tools.\"\n\n**Step 4: Check.** Does the abstract stand alone? Are abbreviations defined? Are all claims in the main text? Is it within the required word limit? Are findings stated rather than promised? (In a real abstract, the placeholder sentence must be replaced with the actual results.)"},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"The skills in this lesson transfer directly into professional practice in Ghana. Managers in banks, telecommunications companies and public agencies routinely read executive summaries, which are close cousins of abstracts: a decision-maker at a Ghanaian ministry or a bank''s board needs the problem, approach, findings and recommendations in a page or less. An MSc graduate who can write a precise, evidence-based summary is more persuasive in proposing an ERP upgrade, a cybersecurity investment or a digital payments strategy.\n\nStudents should also be aware of contextual writing challenges. Many Ghanaian students are multilingual and may be fluent in English while translating complex ideas from other languages or professional jargon. Common issues include over-long sentences, inconsistent tense, and borrowing phrases from sources to \"sound academic\", which risks patchwriting. Writing centres, peer review groups and supervisors can help; reading high-quality articles in your area and analysing how authors build arguments is one of the most effective ways to improve."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Structured abstracts in management and information systems publishing\n\nEmerald Publishing, which publishes many management and information systems journals, has for many years required authors in most of its journals to submit structured abstracts under headings such as Purpose, Design/methodology/approach, Findings and Originality/value, with optional sections on limitations and implications. The rationale is that structured headings make abstracts more complete and easier to scan, and they prevent authors from omitting key information such as method or findings. Medical journals adopted structured abstracts earlier for similar reasons, and reporting guidelines in health research encourage complete reporting of methods and results in abstracts.\n\nThe lesson for MSc students is practical: even where your institution requires a single-paragraph abstract, drafting under structured headings first is a reliable way to ensure completeness. It also shows that writing conventions are not arbitrary; they evolve to help readers find, evaluate and use research efficiently."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Context and problem\"] --> B[\"Purpose or aim\"]\n    B --> C[\"Method: design, sample, analysis\"]\n    C --> D[\"Key findings (or expected contribution in a proposal)\"]\n    D --> E[\"Implications for theory and practice\"]\n```\n\nEach box usually needs only one or two sentences; together they form a complete, self-contained abstract."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** Academic writing means using complicated vocabulary and long sentences. **Correction:** Good academic writing is precise and clear; complexity should come from ideas, not from obscure wording.","**Misconception:** The abstract is an introduction to the topic. **Correction:** An abstract summarises the whole study, including methods and findings, not just background.","**Misconception:** Hedging shows weakness. **Correction:** Proportionate hedging shows that claims match the evidence, which examiners value.","**Misconception:** Critical writing means criticising sources negatively. **Correction:** Critical writing evaluates strengths and weaknesses, compares and interprets; it can be positive.","**Misconception:** The abstract should be written first and left unchanged. **Correction:** A draft abstract can guide writing, but the final abstract should be written or revised last so it reflects the finished document."],"examMistakes":["Producing descriptive summaries of literature instead of evaluative arguments.","Writing abstracts that omit the method or say \"results will be discussed\" instead of stating findings.","Overstating conclusions, for example using \"proves\" for correlational survey data.","Using undefined acronyms and inconsistent terms for the same construct.","Ignoring stated word limits or required abstract formats."],"criticalQuestions":["Is it ever appropriate to use the first person (\"I\") in a methodology chapter? What are the arguments for and against?","How can a writer be critical of a highly cited, foundational model without appearing to dismiss it?","Does a strict abstract word limit improve research communication, or does it encourage oversimplification of complex findings?","How should multilingual writers balance natural expression with the conventions of academic English?"],"exercises":["**Descriptive to critical.** Take a descriptive paragraph from your own literature notes and rewrite it using the PEAL structure. *Expected output:* the original and revised paragraphs, with each PEAL element labelled.","**Hedging calibration.** Rewrite five over-claiming sentences (for example \"The survey proves that trust causes adoption\") so that they match the strength of the evidence. *Expected output:* five revised sentences with a brief justification for each choice.","**Proposal abstract.** Write a 200-250 word abstract for your own planned study using the five-part structure, with methods in the future tense and expected contributions instead of findings. *Expected output:* a complete, self-contained proposal abstract."],"takeaways":["Academic writing is argued, evidence-based, precise, appropriately toned and properly referenced.","Critical writing evaluates and interprets; always answer the \"so what?\" question.","PEAL paragraphs and clear signposting create coherent arguments.","Hedging makes claims proportionate to evidence.","An abstract summarises context, purpose, method, findings and implications and must stand alone."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Swales, J. M., & Feak, C. B. (2012). *Academic writing for graduate students: Essential tasks and skills* (3rd ed.). University of Michigan Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"American Psychological Association. (2020). *Publication manual of the American Psychological Association* (7th ed.). American Psychological Association.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Davis, F. D. (1989). Perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, and user acceptance of information technology. *MIS Quarterly, 13*(3), 319-340.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Saunders, M., Lewis, P., & Thornhill, A. (2019). *Research methods for business students* (8th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"APA Style","url":"https://apastyle.apa.org/","description":"American Psychological Association. Official APA Style site with guidance on paper elements, including abstracts, and on clear, concise writing.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Purdue Online Writing Lab","url":"https://owl.purdue.edu/","description":"Purdue University. Official writing resource portal with guidance on academic writing, paragraphing and research writing.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Academic writing presents a precise, evidence-based argument to a specialist reader. It is critical rather than descriptive, structured through clear paragraphs and signposting, and careful to match claims to evidence through hedging. The abstract is a self-contained summary of the whole study.\n\n- Answer \"so what?\" in every paragraph; evaluate, compare, interpret.\n- Use PEAL: point, evidence, analysis, link.\n- Hedge proportionately; never \"prove\" with correlational data.\n- Abstract = context, purpose, method, findings (or expected contribution), implications.\n- Check word limits and required formats; write or revise the abstract last.\n- CARS moves: establish territory, establish niche, occupy niche."}'::jsonb, 2622, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('13c8d0d6-fc6c-5e05-9f5e-e681c4ac42d0', '323e77f6-364c-52b5-b9cc-91ec03945796', 'proposal-structure-introduction-and-literature', 'Proposal Structure, the Introduction and the Literature Review Chapter', 'Organise a research proposal into its standard components and write a persuasive introduction chapter and a thematically synthesised literature review chapter.', array['Proposal structure', 'Introduction', 'Literature review writing']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"A research proposal is an argument that a particular study is worth doing, can be done, and will be done well. Supervisors and review panels use proposals to judge whether the problem is significant, the questions are researchable, the design is appropriate and the student is ready to begin. A dissertation later expands the proposal, so a well-structured proposal becomes the skeleton of the final document.\n\nThis lesson examines the typical structure of a postgraduate proposal and dissertation, then focuses on the two chapters that establish the case for the study: the **introduction** (background, problem, aims, questions, significance and scope) and the **literature review** (theoretical foundations, empirical evidence, gaps and conceptual framework). Exact chapter titles, lengths and formatting requirements vary between institutions and supervisors, so always check your own programme''s guidance; the structure here reflects widely used conventions in business and information systems research.","objectives":["Explain the purpose and typical components of a postgraduate research proposal.","Apply the funnel structure to write a background that moves logically from broad context to a specific problem.","Construct an introduction chapter in which problem, objectives, questions and significance are aligned.","Organise a literature review chapter thematically and synthesise sources rather than summarising them one by one.","Evaluate a draft introduction or literature review against common examiner criteria."],"prerequisites":["Problem statements, objectives, questions and hypotheses (Module 2).","Literature searching, evaluation, synthesis and conceptual frameworks (Module 3).","The academic writing conventions covered in the previous lesson."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Research proposal","definition":"A document that sets out what a study will investigate, why, and how, so that its value and feasibility can be judged."},{"term":"Background to the study","definition":"The opening section that establishes the context and leads the reader to the research problem."},{"term":"Funnel structure","definition":"An organisation that moves from broad context to the specific problem the study addresses."},{"term":"Significance of the study","definition":"An explanation of who will benefit from the research and how it contributes to theory, practice or policy."},{"term":"Thematic literature review","definition":"A review organised around concepts, debates or variables rather than around individual authors."},{"term":"Empirical review","definition":"The part of the literature review that evaluates previous studies'' findings, methods and contexts."},{"term":"Conceptual framework","definition":"A diagram and explanation of the key constructs and the expected relationships the study will examine."},{"term":"Alignment","definition":"Consistency between problem, objectives, questions, framework, methods and analysis."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Typical structure of a proposal\n\n| Section | Purpose | Guiding question |\n|---|---|---|\n| Title | Communicate topic, variables, population and context | Could a reader predict the study from the title? |\n| Abstract (often required) | Summarise the proposal | Is the whole plan visible in one paragraph? |\n| Introduction / Chapter 1 | Background, problem, aim, objectives, questions, hypotheses, significance, scope, organisation | Why does this study matter and what exactly will it do? |\n| Literature review / Chapter 2 | Theoretical review, empirical review, gaps, conceptual framework | What is known, what is not, and how does this study fit? |\n| Methodology / Chapter 3 | Philosophy, design, population, sampling, instruments, data collection, analysis, quality, ethics | How will the study be done, and why this way? |\n| Work plan and budget | Timeline and resources | Is it feasible within the time available? |\n| References | APA 7 list of all cited works | Can every citation be traced? |\n| Appendices | Draft instruments, consent forms, permissions | Is the supporting material ready? |\n\nA full dissertation typically adds Chapter 4 (results or findings), Chapter 5 (discussion) and a conclusions and recommendations chapter, though some institutions combine results and discussion.\n\n### Writing the introduction chapter\n\n**1. Background.** Use a funnel. Begin with the broad context (for example global and African trends in digital payments), narrow to the Ghanaian context (mobile money growth, policy initiatives, characteristics of retailers), then narrow again to the specific phenomenon (uneven merchant adoption among market retailers). Each paragraph should be supported by credible sources, and the funnel should end at the doorstep of the problem.\n\n**2. Problem statement.** State precisely what is wrong, unknown or contested, the evidence that it matters, and the consequence of not addressing it. A strong problem statement includes both a **practical problem** (what is happening in the real world) and a **knowledge gap** (what research has not yet established).\n\n**3. Purpose, objectives and questions.** The general aim follows from the problem; specific objectives use measurable verbs (examine, determine, assess, explore); research questions mirror the objectives one to one; hypotheses, where appropriate, state testable predictions.\n\n**4. Significance.** Explain specifically who benefits and how: theory (extending a model to a new context), practice (guidance for SME owners or IT managers), policy (evidence for regulators) and future research. Avoid generic claims such as \"this study will benefit everyone\".\n\n**5. Scope (delimitations) and limitations.** State the boundaries you have chosen (geographical area, population, time, variables) and anticipated weaknesses beyond your control.\n\n**6. Organisation of the study.** Briefly outline the chapters.\n\n### Writing the literature review chapter\n\nThe literature review chapter has several jobs: define key concepts, present the theories that underpin the study, evaluate empirical evidence, identify the gap and justify the conceptual framework. A common structure is:\n\n1. **Introduction** to the chapter and its organisation.\n2. **Conceptual review**: definitions of key constructs, with debates about meaning (for example, what counts as \"AI adoption\" in an SME).\n3. **Theoretical review**: the theory or theories used (TAM, UTAUT, TOE, Diffusion of Innovations, DeLone and McLean IS success), why they were chosen, their strengths and criticisms.\n4. **Empirical review**: organised by theme or by relationship (for example, \"perceived usefulness and adoption intention\"), comparing findings, methods and contexts across studies.\n5. **Research gap**: a clear statement of what remains unknown, derived from the review.\n6. **Conceptual framework and hypotheses**: a diagram linking constructs, each relationship justified from theory and evidence.\n7. **Chapter summary**.\n\n### Synthesis, not summary\n\nThe most frequent weakness in student literature reviews is the \"author parade\": one paragraph per study, each beginning \"Author (Year) found...\" This structure reports but does not synthesise. A thematic review instead groups studies by what they show:\n\n> *Author parade:* Study A found that trust influenced mobile banking adoption. Study B found that trust influenced adoption. Study C found that trust was not significant.\n\n> *Synthesis:* Evidence on trust is mixed. Several survey studies of consumers report a positive relationship between trust and mobile banking intention, whereas studies of experienced users find weaker effects, suggesting that trust matters most at the initial adoption stage. Few studies examine merchants rather than consumers, a gap this study addresses.\n\n(In real writing, each claim is supported by citations to the actual studies; the placeholders above illustrate structure only.) Webster and Watson (2002) famously recommended a **concept-centric** rather than author-centric approach, often supported by a concept matrix that maps studies against the concepts they address."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The alignment chain\n\nThe introduction and literature review must align with later chapters. A practical model is the **alignment chain**:\n\nProblem → Aim → Objectives → Research questions → Hypotheses (if any) → Conceptual framework constructs → Instrument items → Analysis techniques → Findings → Conclusions.\n\nExaminers often test alignment by picking one objective and tracing it through the document. If an objective has no corresponding question, construct or analysis, or if the conclusions discuss variables never measured, the chain is broken.\n\n### Choosing theory deliberately\n\nTheory should not be decoration. Justify your choice by explaining why the theory''s constructs match your problem and context. For organisational adoption by SMEs, the technology-organisation-environment (TOE) framework of Tornatzky and Fleischer (1990) is often more suitable than TAM, which focuses on individual users. For consumer or employee adoption, TAM or UTAUT may fit better. Diffusion of Innovations (Rogers, 2003) helps explain adoption over time and attributes of innovations, while the DeLone and McLean model (2003) addresses information system success rather than adoption. Combining theories is acceptable when justified, not when used simply to include more variables."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** A student''s draft problem statement reads: \"Digital payments are important. Many retailers in Ghana do not use them. This study will look at digital payments.\"\n\n**Step 1: Identify weaknesses.** No evidence of the problem, no consequences, no knowledge gap, no specific population.\n\n**Step 2: Revise using a three-part structure (ideal situation, reality, consequence and gap).**\n\n> \"Policy-makers and payment providers in Ghana have promoted digital payments as a means of improving transaction efficiency and financial inclusion, and mobile money is widely used for person-to-person transfers. However, many small retailers in traditional markets continue to rely mainly on cash for sales, which may expose them to theft, limit their access to transaction records that could support credit applications, and reduce the benefits of digital payment infrastructure. Existing adoption research has focused largely on consumers rather than on merchants operating in informal market settings, and the factors shaping retailers'' acceptance of digital payments in this context remain insufficiently understood. This study therefore examines the factors influencing digital payment adoption among retailers in [named market or city].\"\n\n**Step 3: Derive aligned objectives.**\n\n1. To examine the influence of perceived usefulness on retailers'' intention to accept digital payments.\n2. To assess the effect of transaction costs on intention to accept digital payments.\n3. To determine whether trust in payment providers influences intention to accept digital payments.\n\n**Step 4: Derive matching research questions** (for example, \"What is the influence of perceived usefulness on retailers'' intention to accept digital payments?\") and, if the design is quantitative, hypotheses.\n\n**Step 5: Check significance.** Theory: extends adoption models to merchants in informal markets. Practice: informs payment providers'' merchant onboarding. Policy: provides evidence for digital inclusion initiatives.\n\nIn a real proposal, every factual claim in the revised problem statement would be supported by citations to verifiable sources."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Proposals in Ghanaian IT-for-business contexts are strengthened by careful use of local evidence. Credible sources for background sections include official publications from bodies such as the Bank of Ghana, the Ghana Statistical Service, the National Communications Authority and relevant ministries, alongside peer-reviewed research on Ghana and comparable African economies. Students should cite the actual document and avoid repeating statistics found on blogs or in news articles without checking the original source.\n\nThe significance section should speak to real stakeholders: SME owners deciding whether to adopt AI tools, hospital administrators planning electronic health records, universities designing cybersecurity awareness programmes, or fintech firms seeking merchant adoption. A literature review grounded in Ghanaian and wider African studies, alongside international theory, demonstrates that the student understands both the global debate and the local context. Where Ghanaian evidence is thin, stating that clearly is itself a contribution, because it helps establish the research gap."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Webster and Watson''s guidance in *MIS Quarterly*\n\nIn 2002, *MIS Quarterly*, one of the leading information systems journals, published an editorial by Jane Webster and Richard T. Watson titled \"Analyzing the Past to Prepare for the Future: Writing a Literature Review\". The authors observed that the field needed more high-quality review articles and set out practical guidance. Key recommendations included organising reviews around concepts rather than authors, using a concept matrix to track which studies address which concepts, identifying gaps systematically, and developing a conceptual model that leads to propositions for future research. They also advised structured searching, including searching leading journals and following citations backwards and forwards.\n\nAlthough written for journal review articles, the guidance applies directly to MSc literature review chapters. A concept matrix built in a spreadsheet, with studies as rows and constructs, theories, methods and contexts as columns, quickly reveals patterns: which relationships are well established, where findings conflict, and which contexts (such as Ghanaian SMEs or informal retailers) are under-studied. That pattern-finding is the heart of synthesis."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Broad context: global and regional trends\"] --> B[\"Ghanaian context\"]\n    B --> C[\"Specific phenomenon and population\"]\n    C --> D[\"Problem statement: practical problem and knowledge gap\"]\n    D --> E[\"Aim, objectives and research questions\"]\n    E --> F[\"Literature review: concepts, theory, empirical evidence\"]\n    F --> G[\"Research gap confirmed\"]\n    G --> H[\"Conceptual framework and hypotheses\"]\n    H --> I[\"Methodology chapter\"]\n```\n\nThe diagram shows the funnel of the introduction feeding into the literature review, which in turn justifies the conceptual framework and method."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** The background should cover everything about the topic. **Correction:** The background is a focused funnel that leads to the specific problem; irrelevant history dilutes it.","**Misconception:** A literature review is a list of summaries of all the studies found. **Correction:** It is a critical, thematic synthesis that builds an argument towards the gap and framework.","**Misconception:** More theories make a stronger study. **Correction:** Theories should be selected for fit; combining them requires justification.","**Misconception:** Significance means saying the study is important. **Correction:** Significance specifies who benefits and how, for theory, practice and policy.","**Misconception:** The research gap is simply \"no study has been done in Ghana\". **Correction:** A context gap is valid only if context plausibly changes the relationships; argue why Ghana''s conditions matter."],"examMistakes":["Writing problem statements without evidence or without a knowledge gap.","Listing objectives and questions that do not correspond one to one.","Presenting a conceptual framework with constructs not discussed in the literature review.","Using an \"author parade\" structure for the empirical review.","Citing outdated or unverifiable statistics in the background."],"criticalQuestions":["When is a contextual gap (\"not yet studied in Ghana\") a sufficient justification for an MSc study, and when is it weak?","Should a literature review be completed before data collection, or should it continue to evolve during the study? What are the implications for qualitative research?","How can a student demonstrate critical engagement with a theory they ultimately adopt?","Is it possible to over-align a study, so that alignment becomes a rigid template that discourages unexpected findings?"],"exercises":["**Funnel background.** Write a five-paragraph background for a study of e-commerce adoption among small businesses, moving from global context to a specific problem. *Expected output:* five paragraphs, each with a stated function (global, regional, Ghanaian, phenomenon, problem lead-in) and placeholders for citations you would verify.","**Alignment table.** Create a table with columns for objective, research question, hypothesis, construct and analysis technique for your own study. *Expected output:* a completed table where each row is fully aligned.","**Concept matrix.** Build a concept matrix for at least eight real studies you have read, with studies as rows and constructs, theory, method and context as columns. *Expected output:* the matrix and a paragraph identifying one pattern and one gap."],"takeaways":["A proposal argues that a study is worthwhile, feasible and well designed.","The introduction uses a funnel to move from context to a specific, evidenced problem with aligned objectives and questions.","Significance must name specific beneficiaries and contributions.","The literature review is a thematic, critical synthesis leading to the gap and conceptual framework.","Alignment from problem to analysis is a key examiner test."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Webster, J., & Watson, R. T. (2002). Analyzing the past to prepare for the future: Writing a literature review. *MIS Quarterly, 26*(2), xiii-xxiii.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Tornatzky, L. G., & Fleischer, M. (1990). *The processes of technological innovation*. Lexington Books.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Rogers, E. M. (2003). *Diffusion of innovations* (5th ed.). Free Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"DeLone, W. H., & McLean, E. R. (2003). The DeLone and McLean model of information systems success: A ten-year update. *Journal of Management Information Systems, 19*(4), 9-30.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Creswell, J. W., & Creswell, J. D. (2018). *Research design: Qualitative, quantitative, and mixed methods approaches* (5th ed.). SAGE.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"SAGE Research Methods","url":"https://methods.sagepub.com/","description":"SAGE Publishing. Official research methods platform with guidance on proposal writing and literature reviews (access may depend on subscription).","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Purdue Online Writing Lab","url":"https://owl.purdue.edu/","description":"Purdue University. Official writing resource portal including guidance on research proposals and literature reviews.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"A research proposal argues that a study is significant, feasible and well designed. Its introduction moves through a funnel from context to an evidenced problem, aligned objectives and questions, significance and scope; its literature review synthesises concepts, theory and empirical evidence thematically to reveal a gap and justify a conceptual framework.\n\n- Proposal: title, introduction, literature review, methodology, work plan, references, appendices.\n- Problem statement = practical problem + knowledge gap + consequence.\n- Objectives and questions map one to one.\n- Review by concept, not by author; use a concept matrix.\n- Theory chosen for fit (TOE for organisations, TAM/UTAUT for individuals).\n- Test alignment by tracing one objective through every chapter."}'::jsonb, 2641, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('d6d64a32-d236-53b2-98f1-07744969431a', '323e77f6-364c-52b5-b9cc-91ec03945796', 'methodology-results-discussion-and-conclusions', 'Writing the Methodology, Results, Discussion and Conclusions', 'Write a justified, replicable methodology chapter, present results accurately, discuss findings against the literature and draw evidence-based conclusions and recommendations.', array['Methodology writing', 'Results and discussion', 'Conclusion and recommendations']::text[], 50, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"If the introduction and literature review argue that a study is worth doing, the methodology, results, discussion and conclusion chapters prove that it was done well and that its conclusions follow from its evidence. These chapters are where examiners look most closely for rigour. A vague methodology suggests a careless study; results that mix findings with opinion suggest bias; a discussion that never returns to the literature suggests a lack of scholarly engagement; and recommendations that are not grounded in findings suggest wishful thinking.\n\nThis lesson explains what each chapter must achieve, how they differ in purpose and tense, and how to write them so that the whole document forms a coherent, aligned argument. It uses both quantitative and qualitative examples, because MSc IT for Business students use surveys, interviews, case studies and mixed methods. As always, follow any chapter structure your institution or supervisor prescribes; the conventions here are widely used in business and information systems research.","objectives":["Explain the purpose of the methodology, results, discussion and conclusion chapters and how they differ.","Construct a methodology chapter that justifies each design decision and is detailed enough to be replicated or audited.","Present quantitative and qualitative results accurately, without interpretation creeping into the results section.","Analyse findings in a discussion chapter by comparing them with prior literature and theory and explaining agreements and contradictions.","Formulate conclusions, recommendations, limitations and future research directions that are directly supported by the findings."],"prerequisites":["Research designs, sampling and data collection (Modules 4 and 5).","Quantitative and qualitative analysis techniques and interpretation of findings (Module 6).","Validity, reliability, bias and ethics (Module 7)."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Methodology chapter","definition":"The chapter that describes and justifies the research philosophy, design, population, sampling, instruments, procedures, analysis, quality measures and ethics."},{"term":"Replicability","definition":"The extent to which another researcher could repeat the study using the reported procedures."},{"term":"Results (findings) chapter","definition":"The chapter that reports what the data show, organised by objective or theme, without extended interpretation."},{"term":"Discussion chapter","definition":"The chapter that interprets findings, relates them to literature and theory, and explains their meaning and implications."},{"term":"Effect size","definition":"A measure of the magnitude of a relationship or difference, complementing statistical significance."},{"term":"Recommendation","definition":"A specific, actionable proposal for a stakeholder, derived from the findings."},{"term":"Contribution to knowledge","definition":"What the study adds to theory, method or practice."},{"term":"Future research direction","definition":"A justified suggestion for further study arising from the findings or limitations."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The methodology chapter: justify, do not just describe\n\nA common structure is:\n\n1. **Research philosophy** (paradigm) and its implications.\n2. **Research approach** (deductive, inductive or abductive).\n3. **Research design or strategy** (survey, case study, experiment, mixed methods) and **time horizon** (cross-sectional or longitudinal).\n4. **Population and sampling frame**.\n5. **Sample size and sampling technique**, with justification.\n6. **Data collection instruments** (questionnaire, interview guide), their sources and adaptation.\n7. **Pilot testing** and resulting changes.\n8. **Data collection procedure**.\n9. **Data analysis**, mapped to each objective.\n10. **Validity, reliability or trustworthiness**.\n11. **Ethical considerations**.\n\nThe key verb is **justify**. For each decision, state what you chose, why it suits your research questions and context, and what alternative you rejected. For example, \"A cross-sectional survey was adopted because the study tests hypothesised relationships among constructs across a large number of retailers at one point in time; a longitudinal design, though stronger for causal inference, was not feasible within the programme''s timeframe.\"\n\n**Tense.** In a proposal, the methodology is written in the future tense (\"will be collected\"). In the dissertation, it is written in the past tense (\"were collected\"). Mixing tenses is a common sign of an unrevised proposal.\n\n**Detail for replicability.** Report enough that another researcher could repeat or audit the study: how participants were contacted, response rates, interview durations, recording and transcription methods, software used, coding procedures and decision rules.\n\n### An analysis plan table\n\nA powerful device in the methodology chapter is a table linking each objective to its data and analysis:\n\n| Objective | Data source | Variables / focus | Analysis technique |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Examine the effect of perceived usefulness on intention | Questionnaire section B | PU (4 items), intention (3 items) | Multiple regression |\n| 2. Assess the effect of cost on intention | Questionnaire section C | Cost (3 items), intention | Multiple regression |\n| 3. Explore barriers experienced by non-adopters | Semi-structured interviews | Barriers, experiences | Thematic analysis |\n\n### The results chapter: report accurately\n\nThe results chapter presents what the data show. Typical content includes:\n\n- **Response rate and data screening** (missing data, outliers, assumption checks).\n- **Respondent profile** (demographics, firm characteristics).\n- **Measurement quality** (reliability coefficients, validity evidence).\n- **Descriptive statistics** for key variables.\n- **Inferential results**, organised by objective or hypothesis, with test statistics, degrees of freedom where relevant, p-values, confidence intervals and effect sizes.\n- For qualitative studies, **themes and sub-themes**, each illustrated with carefully selected quotations attributed by pseudonym.\n\nUse tables and figures purposefully, number and title them, and refer to them in the text: \"As Table 4.3 shows, perceived usefulness was positively associated with intention.\" Do not repeat every number from a table in prose; highlight what matters. Report non-significant and unexpected results honestly.\n\nKeep extended interpretation for the discussion. Statements such as \"This proves that Ghanaian traders are conservative\" do not belong in results; indeed, they probably do not belong anywhere unless strongly supported.\n\n### The discussion chapter: interpret and connect\n\nThe discussion answers: *What do these findings mean, and how do they relate to what was already known?* A useful pattern for each key finding is:\n\n1. **Restate** the finding briefly.\n2. **Compare** it with prior studies and theory: does it agree, extend or contradict?\n3. **Explain** why, considering context, method and theory. If results contradict earlier studies, offer plausible, evidence-based explanations (different population, measures, adoption stage, regulatory environment).\n4. **Implications**: what the finding means for theory and practice.\n\nThe discussion is where the study''s contribution becomes explicit. Good discussions also consider alternative explanations and acknowledge how limitations shape interpretation.\n\n### Conclusions, recommendations and limitations\n\nThe **conclusion** summarises the answers to each research question, without new data or new literature. It states the study''s contribution to knowledge clearly.\n\n**Recommendations** must follow from findings and be specific: who should do what, and why. \"Government should support SMEs\" is weak; \"Payment providers seeking merchant adoption in traditional markets should prioritise visible, low-cost transaction fee structures, given the strong negative association between perceived cost and intention found in this study\" is strong.\n\n**Limitations** explain how design choices and constraints affect interpretation and generalisability. **Future research** directions should flow from limitations and unanswered questions."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The hourglass model of a dissertation\n\nA helpful structural model is the **hourglass**. The introduction and literature review begin broad and narrow to a specific problem and framework. The methodology and results are the narrow neck, focused tightly on the study''s own data. The discussion and conclusion broaden out again, connecting findings to literature, theory, practice and future research.\n\n### Reporting guidelines as quality checklists\n\nHealth and social research communities have developed reporting guidelines to promote complete, transparent reporting. For example, the CONSORT statement guides reporting of randomised trials, and the Consolidated Criteria for Reporting Qualitative Research (COREQ) provide a checklist covering the research team, study design and analysis for interview and focus group studies (Tong, Sainsbury, & Craig, 2007). Although designed for health research, such checklists are useful self-audit tools for MSc students, especially those researching healthcare technology."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** A student''s study examined digital payment adoption intention among retailers. Regression results (illustrative figures for teaching purposes, not real data) were: perceived usefulness β = 0.41, p < .001; perceived cost β = −0.28, p = .002; trust β = 0.06, p = .41; R² = 0.37.\n\n**Results paragraph (descriptive, accurate):**\n\n> \"Multiple regression analysis was conducted to examine the influence of perceived usefulness, perceived cost and trust on intention to accept digital payments (Table 4.6). Together the predictors explained 37% of the variance in intention. Perceived usefulness had a significant positive effect (β = 0.41, p < .001), and perceived cost had a significant negative effect (β = −0.28, p = .002). Trust was not a significant predictor (β = 0.06, p = .41). Hypotheses H1 and H2 were therefore supported, while H3 was not supported.\"\n\n**Discussion paragraph (interpretive, connected):**\n\n> \"The strong positive effect of perceived usefulness is consistent with the core proposition of the Technology Acceptance Model (Davis, 1989) and with performance expectancy in UTAUT (Venkatesh et al., 2003). The non-significant effect of trust is more surprising, as trust is frequently emphasised in studies of consumer mobile banking. One plausible explanation is that retailers in this sample were already experienced mobile money users, so trust in the underlying service may have been established, leaving cost and usefulness as the differentiating factors. This interpretation should be treated with caution because adoption experience was not measured directly; future research could test whether experience moderates the role of trust.\"\n\n**Recommendation (specific, grounded):**\n\n> \"Payment providers should communicate concrete business benefits, such as faster reconciliation and transaction records usable for credit applications, and review merchant fee structures, as perceived cost was the second strongest predictor of intention.\"\n\nNotice how the results paragraph reports, the discussion interprets and compares, and the recommendation follows directly from findings. Note also the correct phrasing \"not supported\" rather than \"rejected the hypothesis as false\"."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"For Ghanaian IT-for-business dissertations, recommendations often target specific stakeholders: SME owners, banks and fintech firms, telecommunications operators, hospital management, universities, regulators and government agencies. Recommendations to public bodies should be realistic and framed as evidence-based suggestions rather than instructions, and they should not assume knowledge of internal policies the student has not verified. Where a study is conducted in a single organisation, recommendations may be detailed and practical for that organisation, but claims about the whole sector must be appropriately limited.\n\nMethodology chapters in Ghanaian contexts should describe practical realities honestly: data collected in English or translated into local languages, interviews conducted in markets or offices, connectivity issues affecting online surveys, and gatekeeper permissions obtained. These details are not weaknesses to hide; they are part of the transparency that allows readers to judge the study and that makes the research credible."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Reporting standards and the qualitative COREQ checklist\n\nIn 2007, Tong, Sainsbury and Craig published the Consolidated Criteria for Reporting Qualitative Research (COREQ) in the *International Journal for Quality in Health Care*. Recognising that qualitative studies were often reported incompletely, they compiled a 32-item checklist organised into three domains: the research team and reflexivity (for example, the interviewer''s characteristics and relationship with participants), study design (for example, sampling, setting and data collection), and analysis and findings (for example, how themes were derived and whether quotations were presented).\n\nCOREQ is widely used and requested by health journals. Its value for MSc students lies in demonstrating what \"transparent reporting\" means in concrete terms. A student writing up interviews with nurses about electronic health records can use COREQ to check whether the methodology reports who conducted the interviews, how participants were selected, where interviews took place, whether field notes were made, how many coders were involved and whether data saturation was discussed. Using a recognised checklist, and saying so in the methodology, signals rigour to examiners."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Research questions\"] --> B[\"Methodology: how and why\"]\n    B --> C[\"Results: what the data show\"]\n    C --> D[\"Discussion: what it means and how it relates to literature\"]\n    D --> E[\"Conclusions: answers to each question and contribution\"]\n    E --> F[\"Recommendations for stakeholders\"]\n    E --> G[\"Limitations and future research\"]\n    D -.-> H[\"Literature review chapter\"]\n    H -.-> D\n```\n\nThe dotted arrows show that the discussion must return explicitly to the literature reviewed earlier."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** The methodology chapter is a textbook summary of research philosophies and designs. **Correction:** It should briefly explain concepts only to justify the specific choices made for this study.","**Misconception:** Results and discussion are the same thing. **Correction:** Results report findings; discussion interprets them in relation to literature and theory, even when combined in one chapter.","**Misconception:** Statistical significance means a finding is important. **Correction:** Significance indicates evidence against a null hypothesis; effect sizes and context determine practical importance.","**Misconception:** Recommendations can include anything the student believes would help. **Correction:** Recommendations must be derived from the study''s findings.","**Misconception:** Limitations should be minimised to protect the grade. **Correction:** Thoughtful limitations demonstrate critical awareness and are expected."],"examMistakes":["Writing the dissertation methodology in the future tense copied from the proposal.","Stating \"data were analysed using SPSS\" without naming the analysis techniques linked to each objective.","Introducing new literature or new data in the conclusion.","Writing a discussion that restates results without comparing them to prior studies.","Making causal claims from cross-sectional correlational data."],"criticalQuestions":["When might it be better to combine results and discussion into a single chapter, and what risks does this create?","How should a researcher discuss a finding that contradicts a well-established theory without appearing to dismiss either the theory or the finding?","To what extent can recommendations from a single-organisation case study be useful to other organisations?","Is replicability a meaningful standard for interpretivist qualitative research, or should auditability replace it?"],"exercises":["**Justify a design decision.** For your own study, write a justification paragraph for your sampling technique that states the choice, the reasons, the alternative rejected and the implications. *Expected output:* a 120-180 word paragraph containing all four elements.","**Analysis plan table.** Create an objective-by-objective analysis plan table for your study. *Expected output:* a table mapping each objective to data source, variables or focus and analysis technique.","**Results versus discussion.** Using the illustrative regression figures in this lesson, write one results paragraph and one discussion paragraph for the cost variable. *Expected output:* two clearly distinct paragraphs, with the discussion citing relevant theory."],"takeaways":["Methodology chapters justify every design decision and provide enough detail for replication or audit.","Results report accurately and completely, including non-significant findings.","Discussions interpret findings and compare them explicitly with literature and theory.","Conclusions answer each research question and state the contribution; recommendations must follow from findings.","Limitations and future research show critical awareness and point the way forward."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Tong, A., Sainsbury, P., & Craig, J. (2007). Consolidated criteria for reporting qualitative research (COREQ): A 32-item checklist for interviews and focus groups. *International Journal for Quality in Health Care, 19*(6), 349-357.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Field, A. (2018). *Discovering statistics using IBM SPSS Statistics* (5th ed.). SAGE.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Braun, V., & Clarke, V. (2006). Using thematic analysis in psychology. *Qualitative Research in Psychology, 3*(2), 77-101.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Saunders, M., Lewis, P., & Thornhill, A. (2019). *Research methods for business students* (8th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Creswell, J. W., & Creswell, J. D. (2018). *Research design: Qualitative, quantitative, and mixed methods approaches* (5th ed.). SAGE.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"EQUATOR Network","url":"https://www.equator-network.org/","description":"EQUATOR Network. Official site of the initiative that collects reporting guidelines such as CONSORT and COREQ, with training resources on transparent reporting.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"SAGE Research Methods","url":"https://methods.sagepub.com/","description":"SAGE Publishing. Official research methods platform with guidance on writing up quantitative and qualitative research (access may depend on subscription).","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"The methodology chapter justifies how the study was done; the results chapter reports what the data show; the discussion explains what the findings mean relative to literature and theory; and the conclusion answers each research question, states the contribution and offers grounded recommendations, limitations and future research.\n\n- Justify every methodological choice; use future tense in proposals and past tense in dissertations.\n- Map each objective to data and analysis in a table.\n- Report statistics fully, including effect sizes and non-significant results.\n- Discussion: restate, compare, explain, imply.\n- Recommendations must be specific and derived from findings.\n- No new data or literature in the conclusion."}'::jsonb, 2549, 3, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('cb4c9632-8e41-5568-b92d-c221c5ab1778', '323e77f6-364c-52b5-b9cc-91ec03945796', 'apa7-referencing-and-plagiarism', 'APA 7 Referencing, Citation Management and Avoiding Plagiarism', 'Format in-text citations and reference list entries correctly in APA 7, manage sources efficiently with Zotero, and use sources in ways that avoid plagiarism.', array['APA 7 referencing', 'Citation management', 'Zotero', 'Avoiding plagiarism']::text[], 45, 'introductory', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Referencing is how scholars show where their ideas, evidence and words come from. It allows readers to check claims, credits other researchers for their work, and demonstrates that your argument is built on a foundation of scholarship. Poor referencing, by contrast, damages credibility: missing citations suggest unsupported claims, inconsistent formatting suggests carelessness, and unacknowledged use of others'' work is plagiarism, a serious breach of academic integrity.\n\nThis lesson teaches the essentials of **APA 7**, the referencing style set out in the *Publication Manual of the American Psychological Association* (7th ed., 2020) and widely used in business, information systems and social science research. It covers in-text citations and reference list formats for the most common source types, shows how to use **Zotero** to manage citations, and explains how to avoid plagiarism through good note-taking, paraphrasing and citation practice. The reference examples marked as format examples use clearly fictional authors and titles to illustrate structure; always check your own institution''s referencing requirements, which may modify or replace APA conventions.","objectives":["Explain the purposes of referencing and the principles of the APA 7 author-date system.","Apply APA 7 rules to format narrative and parenthetical in-text citations for one, two, three or more, and group authors.","Construct APA 7 reference list entries for journal articles, books, edited book chapters and webpages.","Use Zotero to collect, organise and insert citations and generate a reference list.","Distinguish quotation, paraphrase, summary, patchwriting and plagiarism, and evaluate whether a passage uses sources appropriately."],"prerequisites":["Experience of reading academic sources from the literature review module.","Basic word-processing skills (Microsoft Word, LibreOffice or Google Docs).","None beyond this regarding referencing; the lesson starts from first principles."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"APA 7","definition":"The seventh edition of the American Psychological Association''s style, using an author-date citation system."},{"term":"In-text citation","definition":"A brief reference in the body of the text, giving author and year (and page for quotations), that points to the full reference list entry."},{"term":"Narrative citation","definition":"An in-text citation where the author''s name forms part of the sentence, for example \"Davis (1989) argued...\"."},{"term":"Parenthetical citation","definition":"An in-text citation placed in brackets, for example \"(Davis, 1989)\"."},{"term":"Reference list","definition":"The alphabetical list at the end of a document giving full details of every source cited."},{"term":"DOI (Digital Object Identifier)","definition":"A persistent identifier for a digital object, presented in APA 7 as a URL beginning `https://doi.org/`."},{"term":"Paraphrase","definition":"Restating another author''s idea in your own words and sentence structure, with a citation."},{"term":"Patchwriting","definition":"Copying source text and making superficial changes such as swapping synonyms, while keeping the original structure."},{"term":"Plagiarism","definition":"Presenting someone else''s words, ideas, data or work as your own without proper acknowledgement."},{"term":"Reference manager","definition":"Software, such as Zotero, that stores source details and automatically formats citations and references."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Why reference?\n\nReferencing serves four purposes: **attribution** (credit to the original author), **verification** (readers can check your evidence), **positioning** (showing how your work relates to existing scholarship) and **integrity** (distinguishing your contribution from others''). You must cite whenever you use another person''s ideas, findings, data, theories, figures or words, whether quoted or paraphrased. Common knowledge in your field, such as \"Ghana is in West Africa\", does not need a citation, but specific facts, statistics and arguments do.\n\n### In-text citations in APA 7\n\nAPA 7 uses an author-date system. The table below shows the main patterns. The names are fictional format examples.\n\n| Situation | Parenthetical | Narrative |\n|---|---|---|\n| One author | (Mensah, 2021) | Mensah (2021) |\n| Two authors | (Mensah & Owusu, 2021) | Mensah and Owusu (2021) |\n| Three or more authors | (Mensah et al., 2021) | Mensah et al. (2021) |\n| Group author with abbreviation (first citation) | (Illustrative Statistics Agency [ISA], 2022) | Illustrative Statistics Agency (ISA, 2022) |\n| Group author (later citations) | (ISA, 2022) | ISA (2022) |\n| Direct quotation | (Mensah, 2021, p. 14) | Mensah (2021) stated that \"...\" (p. 14). |\n| Several works in one bracket | (Asante, 2019; Mensah, 2021) | n/a |\n| Same author, same year | (Owusu, 2020a, 2020b) | Owusu (2020a) |\n\nKey rules:\n\n- Use **\"&\"** inside parentheses and **\"and\"** in narrative text.\n- For three or more authors, use **\"et al.\"** from the first citation (a change from APA 6).\n- Multiple works in one parenthesis are ordered **alphabetically** by first author, separated by semicolons.\n- Direct quotations require a **page number** (p. or pp.) or, for unpaginated sources, another locator such as a paragraph number (para.).\n- Quotations of 40 words or more are formatted as **block quotations**, indented, without quotation marks.\n\nA real example: the Technology Acceptance Model was introduced by Davis (1989), or (Davis, 1989).\n\n### Reference list formats\n\nThe reference list appears at the end, titled **References**, in alphabetical order by first author''s surname, with a hanging indent and, in student papers, double spacing. APA 7 lists up to 20 authors in a reference entry. Publisher location is no longer included for books, and DOIs are written as `https://doi.org/...` without the label \"DOI:\".\n\n**Journal article**\n\nPattern: Author, A. A., Author, B. B., & Author, C. C. (Year). Title of article in sentence case. *Title of Journal in Title Case, volume*(issue), page-page. https://doi.org/xxxxx\n\n*Format example (fictional):*\nAsante, K. M., & Boateng, E. A. (2021). Mobile money use and record keeping among market traders. *Journal of Illustrative Information Systems, 12*(3), 45-67. https://doi.org/10.0000/example.2021.003\n\n*Real example:*\nDavis, F. D. (1989). Perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, and user acceptance of information technology. *MIS Quarterly, 13*(3), 319-340. https://doi.org/10.2307/249008\n\nNote that the article title is in sentence case (only the first word, the first word after a colon and proper nouns capitalised) and not italicised, while the journal title and volume number are italicised; the issue number in brackets is not.\n\n**Authored book**\n\nPattern: Author, A. A. (Year). *Title of book in sentence case: Subtitle also in sentence case* (edition if not first). Publisher. https://doi.org/xxxxx (if available)\n\n*Format example (fictional):*\nOwusu, J. K. (2020). *Digital transformation in West African enterprises* (2nd ed.). Illustrative Academic Press.\n\n*Real example:*\nSaunders, M., Lewis, P., & Thornhill, A. (2019). *Research methods for business students* (8th ed.). Pearson.\n\n**Chapter in an edited book**\n\nPattern: Author, A. A. (Year). Title of chapter. In E. E. Editor & F. F. Editor (Eds.), *Title of book* (pp. xx-xx). Publisher. https://doi.org/xxxxx (if available)\n\n*Format example (fictional):*\nMensah, A. B. (2019). Cybersecurity awareness in higher education. In C. D. Ofori & E. F. Darko (Eds.), *Information security in emerging economies* (pp. 101-125). Illustrative Academic Press.\n\nNote that editors'' initials come **before** their surnames in this position, and the book title, not the chapter title, is italicised.\n\n**Webpage on a website**\n\nPattern: Author, A. A., or Group Name. (Year, Month Day). *Title of page*. Site Name. URL\n\n*Format example (fictional):*\nIllustrative Payments Association. (2023, March 14). *Guide to merchant digital payments*. https://www.example.org/merchant-guide\n\nWhen the author and site name are the same, omit the site name. If there is no date, use (n.d.). Include a retrieval date only for pages that are designed to change over time.\n\n**Legislation and official reports.** Legal materials such as Ghanaian Acts follow special legal citation conventions in APA. A common practical approach is to give the Act''s name, year and number as it is officially styled, for example the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843), but you should check your institution''s guidance and APA''s official advice on legal references.\n\n### Citation management with Zotero\n\nZotero is a free, open-source reference manager. Its core workflow is:\n\n1. **Install** Zotero desktop and the Zotero Connector for your browser.\n2. **Capture**: while viewing an article on a publisher site, database or Google Scholar, click the Connector to save the reference and, where available, the PDF.\n3. **Check metadata**: automated capture is not perfect. Verify author names, title capitalisation, journal title, volume, issue, pages and DOI.\n4. **Organise** items into collections (for example by chapter or theme) and add tags and notes.\n5. **Cite** using the word processor plugin for Microsoft Word, LibreOffice or Google Docs: insert citations as you write and select APA 7 as the style.\n6. **Generate** the bibliography automatically; it updates as you add citations.\n7. **Back up and sync** your library, for example with a Zotero account.\n\nAlternatives include Mendeley and EndNote. Whichever you use, the software only formats what you give it: incorrect metadata produces incorrect references, so always proofread the final list.\n\n### Avoiding plagiarism\n\nPlagiarism takes several forms:\n\n- **Direct copying** without quotation marks and citation.\n- **Patchwriting**: rearranging or substituting words in a source passage while keeping its structure.\n- **Paraphrasing without citation**: rewording an idea is still using it.\n- **Self-plagiarism**: reusing your own previous assessed work without permission or acknowledgement.\n- **Contract cheating** or submitting work written by someone else.\n- **Unacknowledged use of AI-generated text** where your institution''s rules require disclosure or forbid such use. Policies on generative AI vary and change, so check your own institution''s current rules.\n\n**Effective paraphrasing** follows a sequence: read and understand the passage; close the source; write the idea in your own words and structure; compare with the original to check accuracy and that you have not copied phrasing; add the citation.\n\n*Original (fictional source text for practice):* \"Small firms often adopt digital tools in response to customer demand rather than as part of a deliberate strategy.\"\n\n*Patchwriting (unacceptable):* Small businesses frequently adopt digital tools because of customer demand instead of as part of a planned strategy (Owusu, 2020).\n\n*Acceptable paraphrase:* Owusu (2020) suggests that customer expectations, more than strategic planning, tend to drive digital adoption in small enterprises.\n\n### Similarity reports\n\nMany institutions use text-matching software such as Turnitin. A **similarity report** shows text that matches other sources; it is not a plagiarism verdict. Properly quoted and cited material, common phrases and reference lists can produce matches, while poorly paraphrased material may not. Use the report to check your own practice, not to \"game\" a percentage."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### A decision model for using sources\n\n| Your purpose | Technique | Citation needed? | Page number? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Exact wording is essential (definition, striking phrase) | Direct quotation | Yes | Yes |\n| Convey a specific idea in your own words | Paraphrase | Yes | Encouraged, not required |\n| Convey the main point of a whole work | Summary | Yes | No |\n| Combine several sources to make a point | Synthesis | Yes, all sources | No |\n| State common knowledge | Your own words | No | No |\n\nPostgraduate writing should rely mainly on paraphrase and synthesis; heavy quotation suggests that the writer has not processed the ideas."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** Correct the following student references and citations (all names are fictional format examples except Davis and Saunders et al.).\n\n1. *In text:* \"According to (Mensah, 2021), trust affects adoption.\"\n   *Error:* narrative sentence uses parenthetical form. *Correct:* \"According to Mensah (2021), trust affects adoption.\"\n2. *In text:* \"(Asante and Boateng, 2021)\"\n   *Error:* \"and\" inside parentheses. *Correct:* \"(Asante & Boateng, 2021)\".\n3. *In text:* \"(Ofori, Darko, and Mensah, 2022)\" on first citation.\n   *Error:* APA 7 uses et al. for three or more authors from the first citation. *Correct:* \"(Ofori et al., 2022)\".\n4. *Reference:* \"Davis, F. D. (1989). Perceived Usefulness, Perceived Ease Of Use, And User Acceptance Of Information Technology. MIS Quarterly, 13(3), 319-340.\"\n   *Errors:* article title in title case; journal title and volume not italicised; hyphen instead of en dash; DOI missing where available. *Correct:* Davis, F. D. (1989). Perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, and user acceptance of information technology. *MIS Quarterly, 13*(3), 319-340. https://doi.org/10.2307/249008\n5. *Reference:* \"Saunders, M., Lewis, P., & Thornhill, A. (2019). Research methods for business students. 8th edition. Harlow: Pearson.\"\n   *Errors:* title not italicised; edition format; publisher location included. *Correct:* Saunders, M., Lewis, P., & Thornhill, A. (2019). *Research methods for business students* (8th ed.). Pearson."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Ghanaian IT-for-business research often draws on official and grey literature: reports and statistical releases from bodies such as the Bank of Ghana, the Ghana Statistical Service and the National Communications Authority, as well as legislation, company annual reports and industry publications. These should be cited as group-authored reports or webpages, with the organisation as author. Students should cite the original official document rather than a news article that summarises it, record the date accessed for pages that change, and keep a copy of key documents in their reference manager, since official web pages are sometimes reorganised.\n\nIn professional settings, the same discipline applies. Consultancy reports, board papers and policy briefs prepared in Ghanaian organisations gain credibility when sources are traceable. Copying content from vendor white papers or websites into a business case without attribution can create intellectual property and reputational risks, just as plagiarism does in academic work."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### The Guttenberg doctoral plagiarism case\n\nIn 2011, Karl-Theodor zu Guttenberg, then Germany''s Federal Minister of Defence and one of the country''s most popular politicians, faced allegations that substantial parts of his doctoral thesis in law had been copied from other sources without proper attribution. Volunteers collaborating online documented numerous passages. The University of Bayreuth withdrew his doctorate in February 2011, and he resigned as minister at the beginning of March 2011.\n\nThe case illustrates several points relevant to MSc students. Plagiarism can be detected long after a degree is awarded, and consequences can be career-ending. Text-matching by many people working together found passages that had passed initial examination, showing that integrity cannot depend on whether software flags a passage. Finally, the case prompted wide public discussion in Germany about the standards of doctoral supervision and examination, reminding students that integrity is a shared responsibility of students, supervisors and institutions."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Find source\"] --> B[\"Capture in Zotero\"]\n    B --> C[\"Check and correct metadata\"]\n    C --> D[\"Read and take notes in own words\"]\n    D --> E[\"Write: paraphrase, synthesise, quote sparingly\"]\n    E --> F[\"Insert citations with plugin (APA 7)\"]\n    F --> G[\"Generate reference list\"]\n    G --> H[\"Proofread against APA 7 rules\"]\n```\n\nGood referencing begins at the note-taking stage: notes written in your own words, with source details captured immediately, are the best protection against accidental plagiarism."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** Paraphrased ideas do not need citations. **Correction:** Any use of another person''s ideas requires a citation, whether quoted or paraphrased.","**Misconception:** A low similarity percentage proves there is no plagiarism. **Correction:** Similarity software detects matching text only; patchwriting, translated copying and idea theft may not be detected.","**Misconception:** Reference managers always produce correct references. **Correction:** Output depends on the accuracy of captured metadata, which must be checked.","**Misconception:** In APA 7, all authors are listed in the first in-text citation. **Correction:** For three or more authors, APA 7 uses \"et al.\" from the first citation.","**Misconception:** Changing a few words in a sentence makes it your own writing. **Correction:** This is patchwriting; genuine paraphrase changes both wording and structure and still needs a citation."],"examMistakes":["Mixing referencing styles (for example Harvard and APA) in the same document.","Citing sources in the text that are missing from the reference list, or vice versa.","Using title case for article titles in APA 7 reference entries.","Including publisher locations for books, or \"Retrieved from\" before URLs for stable sources.","Relying on secondary citations (\"as cited in\") when the original source is readily available."],"criticalQuestions":["Why might a field prefer the author-date system of APA over numbered footnote systems? What does this suggest about how evidence is valued?","Where is the line between legitimate learning from a source''s structure and patchwriting?","How should universities respond to generative AI tools that can paraphrase text automatically? What would a fair policy look like?","Is self-plagiarism a genuine integrity issue, or simply an administrative rule? Defend your view."],"exercises":["**Format five references.** Choose five real sources from your literature search (two journal articles, one book, one edited chapter, one webpage) and format each in APA 7. *Expected output:* five correct reference entries plus one narrative and one parenthetical citation for each.","**Zotero set-up.** Install Zotero and the browser connector, create a collection for your proposal, capture ten sources, correct any metadata errors, and generate an APA 7 bibliography in a document. *Expected output:* a document with ten in-text citations and an automatically generated reference list, with notes on any corrections made.","**Paraphrase practice.** Select three short passages from sources you are using and write a genuine paraphrase and a summary of each. *Expected output:* three paraphrases and three summaries, each with an APA 7 citation, and a one-line note on how structure was changed."],"takeaways":["Referencing provides attribution, verification, positioning and integrity.","APA 7 in-text citations use author-date; use \"&\" in parentheses, \"and\" in narrative, and \"et al.\" for three or more authors.","Reference entries differ by source type; check sentence case, italics, edition, DOI format and the absence of publisher locations.","Zotero automates citations but requires metadata checking and proofreading.","Avoid plagiarism through notes in your own words, genuine paraphrase, consistent citation and honest use of tools."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"American Psychological Association. (2020). *Publication manual of the American Psychological Association* (7th ed.). American Psychological Association.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Swales, J. M., & Feak, C. B. (2012). *Academic writing for graduate students: Essential tasks and skills* (3rd ed.). University of Michigan Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Davis, F. D. (1989). Perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, and user acceptance of information technology. *MIS Quarterly, 13*(3), 319-340.","url":"https://doi.org/10.2307/249008","verification":"unverified"},{"text":"American Psychological Association. (n.d.). *APA Style*.","url":"https://apastyle.apa.org/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"APA Style","url":"https://apastyle.apa.org/","description":"American Psychological Association. Official APA Style site with references and in-text citation guidance, sample papers and instructional aids.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Zotero Documentation","url":"https://www.zotero.org/support/","description":"Zotero. Official documentation and quick-start guides for capturing sources, using word processor plugins and generating bibliographies.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Purdue Online Writing Lab","url":"https://owl.purdue.edu/","description":"Purdue University. Official writing resource portal with APA formatting guidance and advice on paraphrasing.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"APA 7 is an author-date style that requires an in-text citation for every use of others'' ideas or words and a matching, correctly formatted entry in an alphabetical reference list. Zotero and similar tools speed up referencing but require careful metadata checking. Plagiarism, including patchwriting and unacknowledged paraphrase, is avoided through good notes, genuine paraphrase and consistent citation.\n\n- In text: (Author, Year); Author (Year); \"&\" in brackets; \"et al.\" for 3+ authors; page numbers for quotations.\n- Journal: Author. (Year). Sentence-case title. *Journal, vol*(issue), pages. https://doi.org/...\n- Book: Author. (Year). *Title* (ed.). Publisher. (No location.)\n- Chapter: Author. (Year). Chapter title. In E. Editor (Ed.), *Book* (pp. x-y). Publisher.\n- Webpage: Author. (Year, Month Day). *Title*. Site. URL.\n- Similarity reports are evidence to review, not verdicts."}'::jsonb, 3008, 4, true)
