insert into public.course_modules (id, course_id, slug, title, description, sort_order, is_published) values ('21e77958-7f29-5606-b5c1-81b1f9ebd94d', 'adfca7e6-41fb-5885-8e5b-aa5b3ca0bcdf', 'business-process-management', 'Business Process Management', 'Seeing the organisation as a set of processes, and capturing them precisely.', 3, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('4ff4452b-c5a5-5415-a57c-ccb786502ace', '21e77958-7f29-5606-b5c1-81b1f9ebd94d', 'business-processes-and-identification', 'Business Processes and Process Identification', 'Define business processes, classify them as core, support or management processes, build a process architecture and apply importance, health and feasibility criteria to select processes for improvement.', array['Business processes', 'Process identification', 'Process architecture']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Organisation charts show who reports to whom, but they say almost nothing about how work actually gets done. A customer who orders medicines from a pharmacy wholesaler does not care that sales, credit control, the warehouse and dispatch report to different managers; the customer experiences one thing, the delivery of an order, and judges the organisation on how quickly, accurately and pleasantly that happens. Rummler and Brache (1990) famously described the greatest opportunities for improvement as lying in the \"white space\" between the boxes on the organisation chart, where work is handed from one department to another.\n\n**Business process management (BPM)** takes this process view seriously. It treats the organisation as a collection of processes that create value for customers, and it provides methods for identifying, discovering, analysing, redesigning, implementing and monitoring those processes. This lesson begins the BPM journey with its first and most strategic phase: understanding what a business process is, building a **process architecture** that shows how an organisation''s processes relate to one another, and choosing which processes deserve attention first.","objectives":["Define a business process and distinguish it from a function, a task and a project.","Classify processes as core (operational), support or management processes using appropriate examples.","Explain the BPM lifecycle and the position of process identification within it.","Construct a simple multi-level process architecture for an organisation.","Apply the criteria of strategic importance, health and feasibility to prioritise processes for improvement."],"prerequisites":["The module *Enterprise Systems Foundations*, especially functional silos and cross-functional processes.","General familiarity with how organisations are divided into departments."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Business process","definition":"A structured set of related activities, performed by people and systems, that transforms inputs into an output of value to a customer, whether internal or external."},{"term":"Process owner","definition":"The person accountable for the end-to-end performance and improvement of a process, regardless of which departments perform its activities."},{"term":"Core (operational) process","definition":"A process that directly creates value for external customers, such as order-to-cash or patient treatment."},{"term":"Support process","definition":"A process that enables core processes, such as recruitment, IT support or maintenance."},{"term":"Management process","definition":"A process that plans, directs and controls the organisation, such as strategic planning or budgeting."},{"term":"Process architecture","definition":"A structured, multi-level model of an organisation''s processes and their relationships, from a high-level landscape down to detailed sub-processes."},{"term":"Process identification","definition":"The BPM phase in which an organisation''s processes are delineated, organised into an architecture and prioritised for improvement."},{"term":"BPM lifecycle","definition":"The cycle of process identification, discovery, analysis, redesign, implementation, and monitoring and controlling."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### What is a business process?\n\nDavenport (1993) described a process as a structured, measured set of activities designed to produce a specified output for a particular customer or market, emphasising that processes have a clear beginning, end, inputs and outputs. Hammer and Champy (1993) similarly defined a process as a collection of activities that takes one or more kinds of input and creates an output of value to the customer. Several features follow from these definitions:\n\n- A process has a **customer**, who may be external (a patient) or internal (a manager receiving a report).\n- It has a **trigger** (an event that starts it) and an **outcome** (a result that ends it).\n- It usually **crosses functional boundaries**.\n- It can be **measured**: time, cost, quality and flexibility.\n\nIt is useful to distinguish neighbouring concepts. A **function** is an organisational unit (the finance department). A **task** is a single unit of work (approve an invoice). A **project** is a one-off endeavour with a defined end (implement an ERP). A **process** is repeatable: it runs every time its trigger occurs.\n\nProcesses are often named using \"X-to-Y\" labels that make their start and end explicit, such as **order-to-cash**, **procure-to-pay**, **hire-to-retire**, **record-to-report** and, in healthcare, **admission-to-discharge**. These names encourage end-to-end thinking.\n\n### Classifying processes\n\n| Category | Purpose | Examples in a hospital | Examples in a supermarket |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Core | Create value for external customers | Outpatient consultation, admission-to-discharge, laboratory testing | Replenish shelves, sell at checkout, home delivery |\n| Support | Enable core processes | Recruitment, equipment maintenance, IT support | Payroll, cleaning, IT support |\n| Management | Plan, direct and control | Clinical governance, budgeting, strategy | Category planning, budgeting, performance review |\n\nThe same activity can belong to different categories in different organisations. IT support is a support process in a hospital but a core process in an IT outsourcing firm. Classification therefore depends on the organisation''s value proposition.\n\n### The BPM lifecycle\n\nDumas, La Rosa, Mendling and Reijers (2018) describe a BPM lifecycle that structures this and the following lessons:\n\n1. **Process identification**: delineate and prioritise processes.\n2. **Process discovery**: document the current (as-is) process.\n3. **Process analysis**: identify problems and quantify their impact.\n4. **Process redesign**: develop an improved (to-be) process.\n5. **Process implementation**: put organisational and technical changes into effect.\n6. **Process monitoring and controlling**: measure performance and detect new problems, feeding back into the cycle.\n\nThe lifecycle is a cycle, not a project plan: processes degrade as circumstances change, so BPM is an ongoing capability rather than a one-off exercise."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Porter''s value chain as a starting point\n\nPorter''s (1985) value chain divides activities into primary and support activities, offering a ready-made first level for a process architecture. Primary activities map naturally to core processes, and support activities map to support processes. Its limitation is that it was designed for manufacturing firms; service organisations such as hospitals or logistics providers often need to adapt it.\n\n### Building a process architecture\n\nA process architecture is usually built in levels:\n\n- **Level 0: Process landscape**: the organisation''s main process areas, often drawn as management processes along the top, core processes in the middle (flowing from customer need to customer satisfaction) and support processes along the bottom.\n- **Level 1: Process groups**: for example, within \"Fulfil customer orders\": order capture, picking, delivery and invoicing.\n- **Level 2: Processes**: individual end-to-end processes with defined triggers and outcomes.\n- **Level 3 and below: Sub-processes and tasks**: detailed models used for analysis and automation.\n\nReference frameworks can accelerate this work. The APQC Process Classification Framework, for example, offers a standard hierarchy of process categories that organisations can adapt rather than starting from a blank page. SCOR, introduced in the previous module, serves the same purpose for supply chains.\n\n### Prioritising processes\n\nNot every process can be improved at once. Dumas et al. (2018) propose evaluating candidate processes against three criteria:\n\n1. **Strategic importance**: how much does the process contribute to strategic objectives and customer value?\n2. **Health (dysfunction)**: how poorly is it performing, and how much pain does it cause customers and staff?\n3. **Feasibility**: how susceptible is it to improvement, considering politics, culture, resources and dependencies?\n\nA simple scoring matrix makes these judgements explicit and open to challenge.\n\n### Measuring process performance\n\nTo judge a process''s health, evidence is needed. Dumas et al. (2018) describe four performance dimensions, often drawn as the *Devil''s Quadrangle* because improving one dimension frequently worsens another:\n\n- **Time**: cycle time from trigger to outcome, waiting time and processing time.\n- **Cost**: cost per case, including labour, materials and rework.\n- **Quality**: error rates, customer satisfaction and compliance with rules.\n- **Flexibility**: the ability to handle variations, exceptions and changes in volume.\n\nFor example, adding a second pharmacist check reduces dispensing errors (quality) but increases cost and time. Stating which dimension matters most for each process, and collecting even simple baseline measures such as a week of timed cases, makes later analysis and redesign far more convincing than opinion alone."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Ridge Valley Hospital*, a hypothetical private hospital in Accra, wants to choose two processes for improvement this year. Managers score candidates from 1 (low) to 5 (high) on each criterion, where a high \"health\" score means *high dysfunction*. Weights reflect the management team''s priorities: importance 0.4, dysfunction 0.4, feasibility 0.2.\n\n| Process | Importance | Dysfunction | Feasibility | Weighted score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Outpatient registration-to-consultation | 5 | 5 | 4 | 0.4×5 + 0.4×5 + 0.2×4 = 4.8 |\n| Insurance claims submission | 4 | 5 | 3 | 1.6 + 2.0 + 0.6 = 4.2 |\n| Equipment maintenance | 3 | 3 | 4 | 1.2 + 1.2 + 0.8 = 3.2 |\n| Staff recruitment | 2 | 3 | 5 | 0.8 + 1.2 + 1.0 = 3.0 |\n| Strategic planning | 4 | 2 | 2 | 1.6 + 0.8 + 0.4 = 2.8 |\n\n**Interpretation.** Outpatient registration-to-consultation and insurance claims submission rank highest: both are core to patient experience and revenue, and both are visibly dysfunctional (long queues; rejected or delayed claims). Recruitment is easy to change but less important. The scores do not make the decision; they structure a discussion. Managers should test the scores with evidence such as waiting-time samples and claim rejection records, and appoint a **process owner** for each selected process who is accountable across departmental boundaries."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Dell''s build-to-order process\n\nDell became well known in the 1990s for a direct, build-to-order business model in which customers configured computers by telephone or online, and Dell assembled each computer to order rather than building to forecast for retail channels. This is widely discussed in operations and information systems literature as an example of a firm whose competitive advantage rested on an end-to-end core process, from customer order through component procurement and assembly to delivery, supported by close information links with suppliers.\n\nFor process identification, the case illustrates two points. First, a firm''s most strategically important process is the one that embodies its value proposition: for Dell''s direct model, that was order-to-delivery. Second, the process crossed organisational boundaries, including suppliers, so a process architecture had to include external partners. Dell later expanded into retail channels as markets changed, a reminder that process architectures must be revisited when strategy shifts."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n  subgraph MGT[\"Management processes\"]\n    M1[\"Strategic planning\"]\n    M2[\"Budgeting and performance review\"]\n    M3[\"Clinical governance\"]\n  end\n  subgraph CORE[\"Core processes: patient need to patient outcome\"]\n    C1[\"Registration\"] --> C2[\"Consultation\"] --> C3[\"Diagnostics\"] --> C4[\"Treatment\"] --> C5[\"Billing and claims\"]\n  end\n  subgraph SUP[\"Support processes\"]\n    S1[\"Procurement\"]\n    S2[\"HR and payroll\"]\n    S3[\"IT and maintenance\"]\n  end\n  MGT --> CORE\n  SUP --> CORE\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**\"A process is the same as a department.\"** Departments are organisational units; processes flow across them and usually involve several departments.","**\"Support processes are unimportant.\"** They may not directly face customers, but failures in procurement or IT can halt core processes.","**\"Process identification means drawing every process in detail.\"** Identification produces an architecture and priorities; detailed modelling belongs to discovery and only for selected processes.","**\"The process with the worst performance should always be fixed first.\"** Importance and feasibility also matter; a dysfunctional but unimportant process may not justify investment."],"examMistakes":["Giving a definition of a process without identifying a customer, trigger and outcome.","Classifying processes without justifying the classification against the organisation''s value proposition.","Confusing the BPM lifecycle phases, for example treating analysis and redesign as the same phase.","Presenting a prioritisation score as an objective answer instead of a structured judgement that requires evidence.","Ignoring the role of the process owner."],"criticalQuestions":["Why might department heads resist the appointment of end-to-end process owners, and how can senior management address this?","Is the value chain a suitable basis for the process architecture of a Ghanaian hospital? What adaptations would you make?","How should an organisation weigh \"quick wins\" in feasible but less important processes against harder improvements to critical processes?","When a firm''s strategy changes, as Dell''s did, what should happen to its process architecture and priorities?"],"exercises":["**Name the processes.** For a restaurant, identify at least eight processes using \"X-to-Y\" names and classify each as core, support or management. *Expected output:* a table with names, triggers, outcomes and categories.","**Draw a process landscape.** Produce a Level 0 landscape for a logistics firm in Tema using the management-core-support layout. *Expected output:* a diagram with at least four core, three support and two management processes.","**Prioritisation matrix.** Score five processes in an organisation you know on importance, dysfunction and feasibility, choose weights and justify the top two. *Expected output:* a completed matrix and a half-page justification citing evidence you would collect.","**Explore modelling tools.** Install or open Bizagi Modeler (<https://www.bizagi.com/>) or Camunda Modeler (see <https://camunda.com/>) and create a simple diagram listing the Level 1 process groups of one core process. *Expected output:* a saved diagram file and a screenshot."],"takeaways":["A business process is a repeatable, measurable, cross-functional set of activities with a trigger, an outcome and a customer.","Processes are classified as core, support or management according to the organisation''s value proposition.","The BPM lifecycle runs identification, discovery, analysis, redesign, implementation and monitoring, continuously.","A process architecture organises processes into levels from landscape to tasks; reference frameworks such as the APQC PCF and SCOR help.","Prioritise using strategic importance, health and feasibility, supported by evidence, and appoint end-to-end process owners."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Dumas, M., La Rosa, M., Mendling, J., & Reijers, H. A. (2018). *Fundamentals of business process management* (2nd ed.). Springer.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Davenport, T. H. (1993). *Process innovation: Reengineering work through information technology*. Harvard Business School Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Rummler, G. A., & Brache, A. P. (1990). *Improving performance: How to manage the white space on the organization chart*. Jossey-Bass.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Porter, M. E. (1985). *Competitive advantage: Creating and sustaining superior performance*. Free Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Camunda","url":"https://camunda.com/","description":"Camunda. Official site with resources introducing process orchestration and BPMN-based process management.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Bizagi","url":"https://www.bizagi.com/","description":"Bizagi. Official site for Bizagi Modeler, a process modelling tool, with learning resources on process documentation.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"A business process is a repeatable, cross-functional set of activities that turns inputs into an output valued by a customer, with a clear trigger and outcome. Processes are core, support or management. BPM follows a continuous lifecycle beginning with process identification, which produces a multi-level process architecture and a prioritised list of processes. Prioritisation weighs strategic importance, health and feasibility, and each chosen process needs an accountable owner.\n\n- Function ≠ task ≠ project ≠ process.\n- \"X-to-Y\" names make start and end explicit.\n- BPM lifecycle: identify → discover → analyse → redesign → implement → monitor.\n- Architecture levels: landscape → groups → processes → sub-processes and tasks.\n- Prioritise on importance, dysfunction and feasibility (Dumas et al., 2018)."}'::jsonb, 2300, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('c40f1b2f-0fe0-5604-ae27-959fa5fe8b88', '21e77958-7f29-5606-b5c1-81b1f9ebd94d', 'process-mapping-and-documentation', 'Process Mapping and Process Documentation', 'Discover and document as-is business processes using interviews, workshops and observation, and represent them with SIPOC diagrams, swimlane maps and structured process documentation.', array['Process mapping', 'Process documentation', 'SIPOC', 'Swimlanes']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Once an organisation has chosen which processes to improve, it needs an accurate picture of how they work today. This is harder than it sounds. Managers often describe the process as it was designed or as they believe it runs; front-line staff know the workarounds, but each sees only part of the picture; and documents such as procedure manuals may be years out of date. A process map built only from a manager''s description can be dangerously misleading, because the problems that matter most usually live in the undocumented hand-offs and exceptions.\n\nThis lesson covers **process discovery** and **process documentation**: how to gather information about an as-is process, how to scope it with a **SIPOC** diagram, how to map it with **swimlanes** so that hand-offs between roles are visible, and how to document it so that others can understand, analyse and improve it. These techniques are notation-independent; the next lesson introduces BPMN 2.0, the standard notation that formalises them.","objectives":["Compare process discovery methods (interviews, workshops, observation, document analysis and process mining) and select appropriate methods for a situation.","Construct a SIPOC diagram that scopes a process by suppliers, inputs, process steps, outputs and customers.","Produce a swimlane process map that shows activities, decisions and hand-offs between roles.","Prepare structured process documentation, including a RACI matrix and performance measures.","Evaluate the quality of a process map against criteria of accuracy, completeness and appropriate level of detail."],"prerequisites":["The lesson *Business Processes and Process Identification*, particularly the BPM lifecycle.","No prior notation knowledge is required."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Process discovery","definition":"The BPM phase in which information about an existing process is gathered and organised into an as-is model."},{"term":"As-is model","definition":"A representation of how a process currently operates, including its problems and workarounds."},{"term":"SIPOC","definition":"A high-level scoping tool listing a process''s Suppliers, Inputs, Process steps, Outputs and Customers."},{"term":"Swimlane diagram","definition":"A process map divided into lanes, each representing a role, department or system, so that responsibilities and hand-offs are visible."},{"term":"Hand-off","definition":"The transfer of work or information from one role or system to another, a frequent source of delay and error."},{"term":"RACI matrix","definition":"A table that shows, for each activity, who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed."},{"term":"Process mining","definition":"The analysis of event logs from information systems to discover, check and improve actual process behaviour."},{"term":"Happy path","definition":"The most common, problem-free route through a process, as distinct from exceptions and variants."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Discovery methods\n\nDumas et al. (2018) group discovery methods into evidence-based, interview-based and workshop-based approaches. Each has strengths and blind spots.\n\n| Method | How it works | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Document analysis | Review procedures, forms, policies, system screens | Quick, cheap background | Documents may be outdated or idealised |\n| Observation | Watch work being performed, possibly following one case end to end | Reveals real practice and workarounds | Time-consuming; people may behave differently when observed |\n| Interviews | Talk to participants individually, often role by role | Detailed knowledge of each step | Each person sees only part; needs reconciliation |\n| Workshops | Bring participants together to build the map collectively | Resolves conflicts; builds ownership | Dominant voices; scheduling difficulty |\n| Process mining | Analyse timestamped event logs from systems | Objective, covers all cases and variants | Only covers steps recorded in systems; needs data preparation |\n\nA robust discovery effort **triangulates**: it combines several methods so that each compensates for the others'' weaknesses. For example, interviews build a first draft, observation tests it, and a workshop validates it with all roles present.\n\n### Scoping with SIPOC\n\nBefore mapping detail, a SIPOC diagram agrees the process boundary. It forces the team to state where the process starts and ends, who supplies what, and who receives the outputs. A typical SIPOC lists five to seven high-level process steps; detail comes later.\n\n### Mapping with swimlanes\n\nA swimlane map places each activity in the lane of the role that performs it. When the flow crosses from one lane to another, a hand-off occurs. The visual density of lane crossings is itself diagnostic: a process that zigzags between six lanes is likely to suffer waiting time and miscommunication.\n\n**Step-by-step mapping procedure:**\n\n1. **Confirm scope** from the SIPOC: trigger, outcome, customer.\n2. **Identify the roles** (people, departments, external parties, systems) and create one lane for each.\n3. **List activities** in sequence using verb-noun labels (\"Verify prescription\", not \"Prescription\").\n4. **Add decisions** where the flow branches, labelling each outgoing path with its condition.\n5. **Draw the happy path first**, then add the main exceptions (rework loops, rejections, escalations).\n6. **Mark hand-offs, waits and documents** that move between lanes.\n7. **Validate** with participants: walk through real recent cases and ask \"Is this what happened?\".\n8. **Annotate pain points** that participants raise, without trying to solve them yet.\n\n### Levels of detail\n\nA frequent error is to map at inconsistent levels: one box says \"Process order\" while the next says \"Click Save\". A good map keeps each activity at a similar level of granularity and uses sub-process boxes for detail that belongs in a separate map. The purpose determines the level: a map for executives may have eight steps; a map to configure a workflow system may have fifty.\n\n### Process documentation\n\nA map is only part of good documentation. A complete process description usually includes:\n\n- **Process name, owner, purpose and scope** (trigger, outcome, in and out of scope).\n- **SIPOC** summary.\n- **Process map** with lanes.\n- **Narrative description** of each step, including business rules (\"Prescriptions for controlled drugs must be checked by the superintendent pharmacist\").\n- **RACI matrix** for key activities.\n- **Inputs, outputs, forms and systems** used.\n- **Performance measures** such as cycle time, error rate and cost per case.\n- **Known issues** and their evidence.\n- **Version control**: date, author and approval."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Value stream mapping\n\nRother and Shook (1999) popularised **value stream mapping** in *Learning to See*, drawing on practices developed within Toyota. A value stream map records both material flow and information flow, together with data for each step such as process time, waiting time and inventory between steps. It differs from a swimlane map by emphasising time and flow rather than responsibilities, and it is particularly useful when the goal is to reduce lead time.\n\n### Process mining in brief\n\nProcess mining treats the event logs held by enterprise systems as evidence of how processes actually run. A usable event log needs at least three attributes for every recorded event: a **case identifier** (for example the prescription or order number), an **activity name** (\"Payment received\") and a **timestamp**. From such logs, process mining tools support three kinds of analysis:\n\n1. **Discovery**: automatically constructing a model of the paths cases actually followed, including rare variants.\n2. **Conformance checking**: comparing actual behaviour with an intended model to find deviations, such as payments made before approval.\n3. **Enhancement**: overlaying measures such as waiting times on the model to reveal bottlenecks.\n\nProcess mining is powerful because it covers every case rather than a sample, but it sees only steps recorded in systems. Paper forms, telephone calls and informal conversations remain invisible, so it complements rather than replaces interviews and observation. For organisations with an ERP or hospital system, it is often worth checking early in discovery whether suitable logs exist.\n\n### Process quality criteria\n\nWhatever technique is used, a good process model should be:\n\n- **Syntactically correct**: it follows the rules of its notation.\n- **Semantically correct**: it accurately represents reality.\n- **Pragmatically useful**: its intended audience can understand and use it.\n\nThese three qualities are widely used in the process modelling literature to evaluate models, and they apply equally to informal maps and BPMN diagrams."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Osu Community Pharmacy* is a hypothetical retail pharmacy in Accra. Customers complain about long waits when collecting prescription medicines.\n\n**SIPOC**\n\n| Suppliers | Inputs | Process | Outputs | Customers |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Prescribing clinics | Prescription | 1. Receive prescription | Dispensed medicine | Patient |\n| Wholesalers | Medicines in stock | 2. Verify prescription | Receipt | Patient''s insurer |\n| Insurer | Coverage rules | 3. Check stock and price | Counselling advice | Prescriber (if query) |\n| Patient | Payment (cash, mobile money, insurance) | 4. Take payment | Stock update | |\n| | | 5. Dispense and counsel | | |\n\n**Swimlane map (as-is)**\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  subgraph PT[\"Patient\"]\n    P1[\"Hand in prescription\"]\n    P5[\"Pay at cashier\"]\n    P8[\"Collect medicine\"]\n  end\n  subgraph CA[\"Counter assistant\"]\n    A2[\"Log prescription in book\"]\n    A3[\"Check shelf stock\"]\n    A4{\"In stock?\"}\n    A6[\"Offer alternative or order\"]\n  end\n  subgraph CS[\"Cashier\"]\n    C5[\"Calculate price and take payment\"]\n  end\n  subgraph PH[\"Pharmacist\"]\n    H7[\"Verify prescription and dispense\"]\n    H9[\"Counsel patient\"]\n  end\n  P1 --> A2 --> A3 --> A4\n  A4 -- \"Yes\" --> C5\n  A4 -- \"No\" --> A6\n  C5 --> P5 --> H7 --> P8 --> H9\n```\n\n**Observations from validation.** Walking through ten recent cases showed that (a) the pharmacist verifies the prescription only *after* payment, so a query with the prescriber means refunding the payment; (b) price calculation is manual and repeated by the cashier; (c) the patient crosses lanes four times, queuing at each. These are recorded as pain points for the analysis phase, not fixed yet.\n\n**RACI extract**\n\n| Activity | Counter assistant | Cashier | Pharmacist | Superintendent pharmacist |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Verify prescription | I | | R | A |\n| Take payment | | R | I | A |\n| Dispense medicine | | | R | A |\n| Counsel patient | | | R | A |"},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Learning to see at Toyota and beyond\n\nValue stream mapping, as described by Rother and Shook (1999), grew out of the material and information flow diagrams used within Toyota''s production system. Their book presented a structured way to map a current-state value stream, measure process and waiting times, and design a future-state map with reduced lead time. The technique spread widely from manufacturing into healthcare, logistics and office processes.\n\nThe case is relevant here because it shows the value of making *time and information* visible, not just activities. Many organisations discover through such mapping that the time spent actively working on a case is a small fraction of total lead time; most of the time is waiting between steps. That insight motivates the value-added analysis and bottleneck techniques covered in the business process re-engineering module."}],"misconceptions":["**\"The procedure manual shows how the process works.\"** Manuals show intended practice; discovery must establish actual practice, including workarounds.","**\"One interview with the manager is enough to map a process.\"** Managers see an overview; participants in each role hold the detailed knowledge, and views must be reconciled.","**\"A SIPOC is a detailed process map.\"** SIPOC is a scoping tool with only a handful of high-level steps.","**\"More detail always makes a better map.\"** The appropriate level of detail depends on the audience and purpose."],"examMistakes":["Drawing a flowchart without lanes when the question asks about responsibilities or hand-offs.","Using noun-only activity labels, or mixing levels of detail within one map.","Omitting decision conditions on branches.","Failing to mention validation with participants when describing discovery.","Confusing the RACI roles, especially \"Responsible\" and \"Accountable\"."],"criticalQuestions":["Staff may describe an idealised process in workshops for fear of criticism. How can a facilitator encourage accurate accounts?","What parts of a hospital''s admission process would process mining miss, and how would you fill the gaps?","When is a value stream map more useful than a swimlane diagram, and vice versa?","Who should own and maintain process documentation once a project ends, and how can it be kept current?"],"exercises":["**SIPOC for a restaurant.** Produce a SIPOC for \"order-to-table\" in a busy Accra restaurant. *Expected output:* a completed five-column table with five to seven process steps.","**Swimlane map.** Map the as-is process for receiving a parcel at a courier depot, with at least four lanes and two decision points. *Expected output:* a diagram in a tool such as Bizagi Modeler (<https://www.bizagi.com/>) or on paper, with verb-noun labels and labelled decision branches.","**RACI matrix.** For the courier process, assign R, A, C and I for six activities, ensuring exactly one \"A\" per activity. *Expected output:* a RACI table with a short justification of each accountable role.","**Discovery plan.** Plan the discovery of a clinic''s outpatient process, specifying which methods you will use, with whom, in what order and how you will triangulate. *Expected output:* a one-page plan."],"takeaways":["Process discovery should triangulate documents, observation, interviews, workshops and, where possible, process mining.","SIPOC scopes a process before detailed mapping by agreeing suppliers, inputs, steps, outputs and customers.","Swimlane maps make responsibilities and hand-offs visible; frequent lane crossings signal delay and error risk.","Good documentation adds narrative, business rules, RACI, measures, known issues and version control to the map.","Models should be syntactically correct, semantically accurate and pragmatically useful to their audience."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Dumas, M., La Rosa, M., Mendling, J., & Reijers, H. A. (2018). *Fundamentals of business process management* (2nd ed.). Springer.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Rother, M., & Shook, J. (1999). *Learning to see: Value stream mapping to add value and eliminate muda*. Lean Enterprise Institute.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Rummler, G. A., & Brache, A. P. (1990). *Improving performance: How to manage the white space on the organization chart*. Jossey-Bass.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Bizagi","url":"https://www.bizagi.com/","description":"Bizagi. Official site for Bizagi Modeler, a free-to-download process mapping and documentation tool.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Camunda Documentation","url":"https://docs.camunda.io/","description":"Camunda. Official documentation including guidance on modelling processes with Camunda Modeler.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Process discovery builds an accurate as-is picture by triangulating documents, observation, interviews, workshops and process mining. SIPOC scopes the process; swimlane maps show activities, decisions and hand-offs by role; structured documentation adds rules, RACI, measures and known issues. Value stream mapping emphasises time and information flow. Models should be correct, accurate and useful for their audience.\n\n- Triangulate discovery methods; validate with real cases.\n- SIPOC = Suppliers, Inputs, Process, Outputs, Customers.\n- Swimlanes expose hand-offs; label activities verb-noun.\n- RACI: one Accountable per activity.\n- Value stream maps reveal waiting time between steps."}'::jsonb, 2206, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('182d6e6a-4d8f-54ef-aea9-0046a934197e', '21e77958-7f29-5606-b5c1-81b1f9ebd94d', 'bpmn-modelling', 'Process Modelling with BPMN 2.0', 'Read and construct BPMN 2.0 process models using events, activities, gateways, pools, lanes and message flows, and build executable-ready diagrams in Camunda Modeler or Bizagi Modeler.', array['Process modelling', 'BPMN', 'Camunda Modeler', 'Bizagi Modeler']::text[], 60, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Informal flowcharts are useful for discussion, but they suffer from ambiguity: one analyst''s diamond means \"decision\", another''s means \"wait for approval\", and nobody can say precisely what happens when two branches run at the same time. As organisations began to automate processes with workflow and process engines, they needed a notation that business people could read and that software could interpret without ambiguity. **Business Process Model and Notation (BPMN)**, maintained by the Object Management Group (OMG), is the standard that fills this role. Version 2.0 added a formal meaning for each element and an XML interchange format, so that a diagram drawn by an analyst can be exchanged between tools and, with additional technical detail, executed by a process engine.\n\nThis lesson teaches the core BPMN 2.0 vocabulary: **events**, **activities**, **gateways**, **sequence flows**, **pools**, **lanes**, **message flows** and **data objects**. You will learn the behaviour each element implies, using the idea of a *token* moving through the model, and you will build a complete model of a Ghanaian hospital pharmacy process. Practical exercises use Camunda Modeler and Bizagi Modeler, two widely used tools. Because this platform renders diagrams in Mermaid, the diagrams here *approximate* BPMN shapes; in the modelling tools you will use the genuine BPMN symbols.","objectives":["Explain the purpose and scope of BPMN 2.0 and its relationship to process execution.","Identify and describe the main BPMN flow objects: events, activities and gateways, including common subtypes.","Distinguish sequence flows from message flows and pools from lanes, applying the rules that govern them.","Apply token semantics to predict the behaviour of exclusive, parallel, inclusive and event-based gateways.","Construct a correct, readable BPMN collaboration diagram for a realistic process using Camunda Modeler or Bizagi Modeler."],"prerequisites":["The lesson *Process Mapping and Process Documentation*, especially swimlanes and decision points.","Familiarity with the idea of a process trigger and outcome."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"BPMN","definition":"Business Process Model and Notation, an OMG standard graphical notation for modelling business processes, with defined semantics and an XML interchange format in version 2.0."},{"term":"Event","definition":"Something that happens during a process, drawn as a circle: a start event (thin border), intermediate event (double border) or end event (thick border)."},{"term":"Activity","definition":"Work performed in a process, drawn as a rounded rectangle; either a task (atomic) or a sub-process (compound)."},{"term":"Gateway","definition":"A diamond that controls how sequence flows diverge and converge, such as exclusive (XOR), parallel (AND), inclusive (OR) and event-based gateways."},{"term":"Sequence flow","definition":"A solid arrow showing the order of flow objects within a single pool."},{"term":"Message flow","definition":"A dashed arrow showing communication between two separate participants (pools)."},{"term":"Pool","definition":"A container representing a participant in a collaboration, such as an organisation or an external party."},{"term":"Lane","definition":"A subdivision of a pool, typically representing a role, department or system within that participant."},{"term":"Token","definition":"A conceptual marker used to explain BPMN behaviour; it travels along sequence flows, and gateways create, route or synchronise tokens."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### BPMN element categories\n\nBPMN elements fall into five broad categories:\n\n1. **Flow objects**: events, activities and gateways, which define behaviour.\n2. **Connecting objects**: sequence flows, message flows and associations.\n3. **Swimlanes**: pools and lanes.\n4. **Data**: data objects, data inputs and outputs, and data stores.\n5. **Artefacts**: text annotations and groups, which add information without affecting behaviour.\n\n### Events\n\nEvents are circles. Their **border** tells you *when* they occur and their **inner symbol** tells you *what kind* of event it is.\n\n| Position | Border | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Start event | Single thin line | Starts the process (creates a token) |\n| Intermediate event | Double line | Occurs during the process; may wait for (catch) or send (throw) something |\n| End event | Single thick line | Ends a path (consumes a token) |\n\nCommon event types include **none** (unspecified), **message** (envelope), **timer** (clock), **error** (lightning bolt), **signal** (triangle), **conditional** (lined page) and **terminate** (filled circle, end only, which ends the whole process instance immediately). A **boundary event** is an intermediate event attached to the edge of an activity: for example, a timer boundary event on \"Await insurer approval\" that escalates if no answer arrives within two days. Boundary events can be *interrupting* (the activity is cancelled) or *non-interrupting* (drawn with a dashed border; the activity continues).\n\n### Activities\n\nA **task** is a single unit of work. BPMN 2.0 defines task types that are especially important for automation:\n\n- **User task**: performed by a person using software (approve a claim on screen).\n- **Manual task**: performed without software (physically pack an order).\n- **Service task**: performed automatically by a system (call the stock API).\n- **Send task / receive task**: send or wait for a message.\n- **Script task**: executed by a script in the process engine.\n- **Business rule task**: evaluates business rules, for example a decision table.\n\nA **sub-process** groups activities and can be shown collapsed (with a small plus sign) or expanded. Activities can also carry markers for **loops** and **multi-instance** execution (three parallel or sequential bars), for example \"Check each prescription item\".\n\n### Gateways\n\nGateways are diamonds. Their behaviour is easiest to understand through token semantics.\n\n| Gateway | Marker | Splitting behaviour | Merging behaviour |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Exclusive (XOR) | X or empty | Token takes exactly one outgoing path whose condition is true | Passes each arriving token through; no waiting |\n| Parallel (AND) | + | Token is copied to all outgoing paths | Waits until a token has arrived on every incoming path |\n| Inclusive (OR) | O | Token is copied to every path whose condition is true (one or more) | Waits for all paths that were actually activated |\n| Event-based | Pentagon in double circle | Waits; the first of several following events to occur determines the path | Not used for merging |\n\nTwo practical rules prevent many errors. First, **split and join with the same gateway type**: if you split with a parallel gateway, join with a parallel gateway; merging parallel branches with an exclusive gateway lets tokens pass through separately, so later activities execute more than once. Second, **label exclusive and inclusive branch conditions** clearly (\"Stock available\", \"Stock not available\") and include a default flow where appropriate.\n\n### Pools, lanes and message flows\n\nA **pool** represents a participant, such as the hospital, a patient or an insurer. **Lanes** within a pool show who performs which activities inside that participant. The crucial rules are:\n\n- **Sequence flows stay inside a pool.** They may cross lanes within the same pool, but they cannot cross the pool boundary.\n- **Message flows connect different pools.** They show communication (a claim, a confirmation) between participants and never connect elements within the same pool.\n- An external participant can be shown as a **black-box pool** (collapsed, with no internal detail) when its internal process is unknown or irrelevant.\n\nA diagram with two or more pools exchanging messages is called a **collaboration** diagram.\n\n### Data\n\n**Data objects** (a page with a folded corner) show information produced or used by activities, such as \"Prescription\" or \"Invoice\"; **data stores** (a cylinder) show persistent stores such as a pharmacy stock database. They are linked to activities by dotted **associations** and do not affect token flow."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Modelling style guidelines\n\nSilver (2011) and Dumas et al. (2018) offer practical guidelines that make BPMN models easier to read and validate:\n\n1. Use **verb-noun labels** for activities (\"Verify prescription\") and **state labels** for end events (\"Medicine dispensed\", \"Prescription rejected\").\n2. Use **explicit start and end events**, with one start event per pool where possible.\n3. **Avoid implicit gateways** (multiple arrows leaving an activity without a gateway); make branching explicit.\n4. **Model the happy path first** in a straight line, then add exceptions.\n5. **Keep models small**: if a level has more than about ten to fifteen activities, introduce sub-processes.\n6. **Label every gateway question** (\"Insurance valid?\") and every outgoing flow.\n7. Ensure **every path ends** at an end event and that the model contains no **deadlocks** (a parallel join waiting for a token that will never arrive).\n\n### Descriptive versus executable models\n\nThe same notation serves different purposes. A **descriptive model** communicates a process to people and may omit technical detail. An **executable model** adds detail required by a process engine, such as task assignments, form fields, service endpoints and data mappings. Process engines such as Camunda''s interpret the BPMN XML and move real tokens through the model, assigning user tasks to work lists and calling services. Bizagi Modeler is widely used to create descriptive models and documentation, and its wider platform also supports automation. The modelling discipline is the same, but executable models must be complete and unambiguous."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Ridge Valley Hospital*, a hypothetical hospital in Accra, wants to model its outpatient prescription-to-dispensing process, including insurance coverage checks with an insurer.\n\n**Step 1: Participants.** Pools: *Ridge Valley Hospital* (lanes: Pharmacy assistant, Pharmacist, Cashier) and *Insurer* (black-box pool).\n\n**Step 2: Trigger and outcomes.** Start event: message start \"Prescription received\". End events: \"Medicine dispensed\", \"Prescription referred back to prescriber\".\n\n**Step 3: Happy path.** Check prescription (pharmacist, user task) → Check stock (service task) → Request coverage (send task, message flow to insurer) → Receive coverage decision (receive task) → Take payment or co-payment (cashier) → Dispense and counsel (pharmacist).\n\n**Step 4: Decisions and exceptions.**\n\n- Exclusive gateway \"Prescription valid?\": *No* → \"Contact prescriber\" → end event \"Prescription referred back\".\n- Exclusive gateway \"Item in stock?\": *No* → \"Offer alternative or order item\".\n- Timer boundary event on \"Receive coverage decision\": if no response in 30 minutes, \"Offer self-pay option\".\n- Parallel gateway: after payment, \"Update stock record\" (service task) and \"Dispense and counsel\" run in parallel and are joined by a parallel gateway before the end event.\n\n**Step 5: Mermaid approximation.** In the diagram below, circles approximate events, rounded boxes are tasks, diamonds are gateways (labelled with their type) and the dashed arrows represent message flows to the insurer pool.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  subgraph HOSP[\"Pool: Ridge Valley Hospital\"]\n    S((\"Start: prescription received\")) --> T1[\"Check prescription\"]\n    T1 --> G1{\"XOR: valid?\"}\n    G1 -- \"No\" --> T2[\"Contact prescriber\"] --> E2((\"End: referred back\"))\n    G1 -- \"Yes\" --> T3[\"Check stock (service task)\"]\n    T3 --> G2{\"XOR: in stock?\"}\n    G2 -- \"No\" --> T4[\"Offer alternative or order\"] --> T3\n    G2 -- \"Yes\" --> T5[\"Send coverage request\"]\n    T5 --> T6[\"Receive coverage decision\"]\n    T6 --> T7[\"Take payment or co-payment\"]\n    T6 -. \"Timer: 30 min\" .-> T8[\"Offer self-pay option\"] --> T7\n    T7 --> P1{\"AND split\"}\n    P1 --> T9[\"Update stock record\"]\n    P1 --> T10[\"Dispense and counsel\"]\n    T9 --> P2{\"AND join\"}\n    T10 --> P2\n    P2 --> E1((\"End: medicine dispensed\"))\n  end\n  subgraph INS[\"Pool: Insurer (black box)\"]\n    I1[\"Insurer\"]\n  end\n  T5 -. \"Message: coverage request\" .-> I1\n  I1 -. \"Message: coverage decision\" .-> T6\n```\n\n**Step 6: Validate.** Trace tokens: a valid prescription with stock and timely insurer response produces one token that splits into two at the AND split and rejoins before the end event, so the instance completes exactly once. An invalid prescription ends at \"Referred back\". No message flow connects activities inside the hospital pool, and no sequence flow crosses into the insurer pool."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### The standardisation of BPMN\n\nBPMN was first developed by the Business Process Management Initiative (BPMI), which released BPMN 1.0 in 2004. BPMI merged with the Object Management Group in 2005, and the OMG took over maintenance of the standard. BPMN 2.0, formally released by the OMG in 2011, renamed the standard Business Process Model and Notation, defined the meaning of elements more precisely and introduced an XML-based interchange format. A version of BPMN 2.0 was later also published as an international standard, ISO/IEC 19510.\n\nThis history matters for practitioners. Because BPMN 2.0 has a shared, standardised meaning and file format, a model created in one tool can, in principle, be opened in another, and process engines from different vendors can execute BPMN models. That interoperability is why BPMN skills transfer across tools such as Camunda Modeler and Bizagi Modeler, and why organisations can treat process models as long-lived assets rather than tool-specific drawings."}],"misconceptions":["**\"Lanes represent separate organisations.\"** Lanes subdivide a single participant; separate organisations are separate pools.","**\"Message flows and sequence flows are interchangeable.\"** Sequence flows order activities within a pool; message flows show communication between pools.","**\"An exclusive gateway can merge parallel branches.\"** Merging parallel branches with an XOR lets each token pass separately, causing downstream activities to run more than once; use an AND join.","**\"BPMN models must always be executable.\"** Many BPMN models are descriptive; executable models add technical detail for a process engine.","**\"Every decision in a process needs a gateway.\"** Gateways route flow; complex decision logic is often better placed in a business rule task or decision table."],"examMistakes":["Drawing sequence flows across pool boundaries.","Omitting start or end events, or leaving paths that never reach an end event.","Using gateways without labelled conditions on outgoing flows.","Confusing the inclusive (OR) gateway with the exclusive (XOR) gateway.","Labelling activities with nouns or system names rather than verb-noun phrases.","Describing BPMN elements without explaining their behaviour."],"criticalQuestions":["BPMN aims to be understandable by business people and precise enough for machines. Are these goals in tension? How would you manage the tension on a real project?","When should an external participant be modelled as a black-box pool rather than with its internal activities?","How could a timer boundary event improve patient experience in a hospital process, and what new risks might automated escalation create?","Why might an organisation keep separate descriptive and executable versions of the same process model?"],"exercises":["**Element identification.** Given the worked example, list every BPMN element used and state its category and behaviour. *Expected output:* a table with at least twelve elements.","**Model in Camunda Modeler.** Download Camunda Modeler (see <https://camunda.com/> and the documentation at <https://docs.camunda.io/>) and recreate the worked example using genuine BPMN symbols, including the insurer pool and message flows. *Expected output:* a saved `.bpmn` file and a screenshot; the modeller should show no validation errors.","**Model in Bizagi Modeler.** Using Bizagi Modeler (<https://www.bizagi.com/>), model a restaurant order-to-table process with lanes for waiter, kitchen and cashier, including a parallel gateway for preparing food and drinks. *Expected output:* a diagram and an exported documentation report.","**Gateway debugging.** Draw a model in which parallel branches are merged with an XOR gateway, trace the tokens and explain what goes wrong, then correct it. *Expected output:* before and after diagrams with a token-trace explanation.","**Collaboration diagram.** Model a logistics firm in Tema receiving a delivery booking from a customer and confirming a pickup time, with separate pools for customer and firm. *Expected output:* a collaboration diagram with at least two message flows and no sequence flows crossing pools."],"takeaways":["BPMN 2.0 is the OMG standard for process models that are readable by people and precise enough for tools and engines.","Events (start, intermediate, end) are circles; activities are rounded rectangles; gateways are diamonds that split and merge tokens.","Exclusive gateways choose one path, parallel gateways take all paths and synchronise, inclusive gateways take one or more, and event-based gateways choose by the first event to occur.","Sequence flows stay within a pool; message flows connect pools; lanes subdivide a pool by role.","Good style uses verb-noun labels, explicit start and end events, matching split and join gateways and small, layered models."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Object Management Group. (2011). *Business Process Model and Notation (BPMN), version 2.0*.","url":"https://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Silver, B. (2011). *BPMN method and style* (2nd ed.). Cody-Cassidy Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Dumas, M., La Rosa, M., Mendling, J., & Reijers, H. A. (2018). *Fundamentals of business process management* (2nd ed.). Springer.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Weske, M. (2012). *Business process management: Concepts, languages, architectures* (2nd ed.). Springer.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Camunda Documentation","url":"https://docs.camunda.io/","description":"Camunda. Official documentation including BPMN guides and instructions for using Camunda Modeler.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Bizagi","url":"https://www.bizagi.com/","description":"Bizagi. Official site for Bizagi Modeler with learning resources on BPMN modelling.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"OMG BPMN Specification","url":"https://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/","description":"Object Management Group. The official specification page for BPMN, including version history.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"BPMN 2.0 is the OMG''s standard process notation, with defined semantics and an XML format that allow models to be exchanged and executed. Events start, interrupt and end flows; activities (tasks and sub-processes) perform work; gateways route tokens (XOR one path, AND all paths with synchronisation, OR one or more, event-based by first event). Pools are participants, lanes are roles; sequence flows stay inside pools and message flows link pools. Model the happy path first and keep models small and labelled.\n\n- Event borders: thin = start, double = intermediate, thick = end.\n- Task types: user, manual, service, send, receive, script, business rule.\n- Split and join with the same gateway type.\n- Never draw sequence flows across pools.\n- Descriptive versus executable models; Camunda and Bizagi for practice."}'::jsonb, 2624, 3, true)
