insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('224bfd99-e26b-5e33-927e-114fd1b7b649', '2f69fb46-06b4-5abd-bb43-5149b3fa2871', 'pharmacy-and-hospital-systems', 'Pharmacy ERP and Hospital Management Systems', 'Analyse the as-is processes of an illustrative Ghanaian pharmacy chain and hospital, redesign them with BPMN-style thinking, and specify the enterprise technology, KPIs, risks and change management needed to support the redesign.', array['Pharmacy ERP', 'Hospital management systems']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Healthcare is one of the most demanding settings for enterprise systems. Medicines carry batch numbers and expiry dates, controlled drugs need strict accountability, patients move between registration, consultation, laboratory, pharmacy and billing, and insurance claims must be supported by accurate clinical and dispensing records. Errors are not merely costly; they can harm patients. At the same time, many healthcare organisations still rely on paper folders, standalone tills and spreadsheets that cannot share information.\n\nThis lesson applies everything in the course to two illustrative Ghanaian organisations: a retail pharmacy chain and a private hospital. For each, you will examine the current (as-is) process and its weaknesses, compare as-is and to-be process diagrams, specify the supporting enterprise technology, define KPIs, and plan for risks and change management. The lesson closes with a large, well-documented international programme that shows what happens when technology ambition outruns organisational readiness.","objectives":["Analyse as-is pharmacy and hospital processes to identify waste, delays, control weaknesses and patient-safety risks.","Apply process redesign principles to produce to-be processes supported by integrated enterprise systems.","Specify enterprise technology components, including batch and expiry management, electronic prescribing and claims integration.","Design KPIs with baselines and targets to evaluate the redesigned processes.","Evaluate implementation risks and propose change-management actions grounded in recognised models."],"prerequisites":["Process analysis, value-added analysis and BPMN modelling (earlier modules).","Implementation strategy, data migration, testing and training (Enterprise Implementation module).","Segregation of duties and role-based access (Enterprise Security lesson)."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"FEFO (first expired, first out)","definition":"A stock-rotation rule that issues the batch with the earliest expiry date first, reducing expired stock write-offs."},{"term":"Batch traceability","definition":"The ability to trace which batch of a product was received from which supplier and dispensed to which patient or sale, essential for recalls."},{"term":"Electronic prescribing (e-prescribing)","definition":"Creating prescriptions electronically in a clinical system and transmitting them directly to the pharmacy queue."},{"term":"Electronic medical record (EMR)","definition":"A digital record of a patient''s clinical information within one organisation."},{"term":"Hospital management system (HMS)","definition":"An integrated enterprise system covering patient administration, clinical workflows, pharmacy, laboratory, billing and reporting."},{"term":"Claims integration","definition":"Generating insurance claims directly from registration, clinical and dispensing records rather than re-keying them."},{"term":"Interoperability","definition":"The ability of different health information systems to exchange and use data, often using standards such as HL7 FHIR."},{"term":"Revenue leakage","definition":"Services or items provided but not billed or not claimed, through omission or error."}],"body":[{"heading":"Analytical Approach","markdown":"Each case is analysed with the same six-step method, which you should reuse in examinations:\n\n1. **Describe the as-is process**: actors, steps, hand-offs, documents and systems.\n2. **Diagnose weaknesses**: non-value-adding steps, waiting, rework, information gaps, control weaknesses and risks to customers or patients.\n3. **Redesign the process**: apply principles such as capturing data once at source, moving decisions to the point of work, removing unnecessary hand-offs and running steps in parallel.\n4. **Specify enabling technology**: the enterprise system modules, integrations and devices required.\n5. **Define KPIs**: baseline, target, owner and data source for each.\n6. **Plan for risks and change**: technical, data, people and regulatory risks, with mitigation and a change-management approach (for example Kotter''s eight steps or ADKAR).\n\nTechnology appears at step four, not step one. The redesign should be justified first; the system is then chosen to enable it."},{"heading":"Case A: A Pharmacy Chain (Illustrative scenario)","markdown":"### As-is process\n\n*Illustrative scenario.* \"HealWell Pharmacy\" has a head office in Accra and ten branches. Each branch uses a standalone till that records sales value but not batches. Stock is tracked on paper bin cards. When a pharmacist notices low stock, the branch phones a wholesaler directly and places an order. On delivery, a clerk checks quantities against the delivery note and files it. Expiry dates are checked visually during a monthly shelf walk. Cash and mobile money takings are reconciled by hand at close of day, and National Health Insurance Scheme (NHIS) claims for covered medicines are compiled manually from prescription copies at month-end.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **No central visibility**: head office cannot see stock or sales by branch in time to act, so one branch runs out while another holds surplus.\n- **Fragmented purchasing**: branches buy independently, losing volume discounts and creating inconsistent prices.\n- **Expiry losses and patient-safety risk**: without batch and expiry data, short-dated stock is not issued first and expired items may remain on shelves.\n- **No traceability**: a supplier recall cannot be traced to branches or sales.\n- **Control weaknesses**: the same person orders, receives and pays small suppliers; manual reconciliation of mobile money is slow and error-prone.\n- **Claim rejections and leakage**: manual claim compilation produces transcription errors and missed claims.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Pharmacist notices low stock on shelf\"] --> B[\"Phones wholesaler directly\"]\n    B --> C[\"Delivery arrives; clerk checks quantities only\"]\n    C --> D[\"Bin card updated by hand\"]\n    D --> E[\"Sale on standalone till: value only, no batch\"]\n    E --> F[\"Monthly visual expiry check\"]\n    E --> G[\"End-of-day manual cash and mobile money reconciliation\"]\n    E --> H[\"Month-end manual NHIS claim compilation\"]\n```\n\n### Redesigned process\n\nThe to-be process captures data once, at source, and lets the system drive replenishment:\n\n1. Every sale at the integrated point of sale deducts stock by batch using FEFO, and records the payment method; mobile money confirmations are matched automatically through the payment aggregator''s interface.\n2. When branch stock falls below a reorder point (calculated from sales history and lead time), the system raises a replenishment proposal. Head office procurement consolidates proposals into supplier purchase orders, or transfers surplus stock between branches.\n3. Purchase orders above a threshold are approved through workflow by the procurement manager, separating ordering from approval.\n4. On delivery, the clerk scans barcodes and records batch numbers and expiry dates; the system rejects short-dated stock below a configured shelf-life threshold and records the three-way match for accounts payable.\n5. The system produces a weekly near-expiry report so that short-dated stock can be moved or returned before it expires.\n6. Claims are generated from dispensing records, validated against scheme rules and submitted electronically where the scheme''s interfaces allow.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Sale at integrated POS: batch deducted by FEFO\"] --> B[\"Mobile money matched automatically\"]\n    A --> C{\"Stock below reorder point?\"}\n    C -->|\"Yes\"| D[\"Replenishment proposal to head office\"]\n    D --> E{\"Surplus in another branch?\"}\n    E -->|\"Yes\"| F[\"Inter-branch transfer\"]\n    E -->|\"No\"| G[\"Consolidated purchase order; workflow approval\"]\n    G --> H[\"Delivery scanned: batch and expiry captured\"]\n    H --> I[\"Three-way match and supplier payment by finance\"]\n    A --> J[\"Claim generated from dispensing record\"]\n    H --> K[\"Weekly near-expiry report\"]\n```\n\n### Enabling technology\n\n- A retail pharmacy ERP or general ERP with batch and expiry management, multi-location inventory, central procurement and finance.\n- Integrated POS terminals with offline mode, barcode scanners and receipt printers.\n- Payment integration with mobile money aggregators and banks.\n- Workflow for purchase approval; role-based access separating ordering, receiving and payment; restricted access and registers for controlled medicines.\n- A BI dashboard showing sales, margin, stock cover and near-expiry value by branch.\n\n### KPIs\n\n| KPI | Definition | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stock-out rate | Share of requested items unavailable at the point of sale | Service and patient access |\n| Expired stock value | Value of stock written off as expired per month | Waste and safety |\n| Inventory record accuracy | Share of cycle counts matching system quantities | Data reliability |\n| Days of inventory | 365 divided by stock turnover | Working capital |\n| Claim rejection rate | Share of submitted claims rejected | Revenue protection |\n| Daily reconciliation time | Staff time to close takings per branch | Efficiency |\n\nBaselines should be measured for at least two months before go-live.\n\n### Risks and change management\n\nKey risks include poor-quality product master data (duplicate items, inconsistent units), connectivity failures at branches, pharmacists'' resistance to scanning during busy periods, and over-centralisation that ignores local demand. Mitigation includes cleansing the product master before migration, offline POS, a pilot branch followed by waves, and involving senior pharmacists as super-users. Using Kotter''s model, leaders would build urgency around patient safety and expired stock losses, form a guiding coalition of pharmacists and finance, communicate the vision repeatedly, and celebrate short-term wins such as a fall in expiry write-offs at the pilot branch."},{"heading":"Case B: A Private Hospital (Illustrative scenario)","markdown":"### As-is process\n\n*Illustrative scenario.* \"Adepa Hospital\", a 120-bed private hospital in Kumasi, runs outpatient clinics on paper. A patient queues at records, where a clerk searches for a paper folder or creates a new one. The patient then waits for a nurse to take vital signs, then for a doctor, who writes a paper prescription and laboratory request. The patient walks to the cashier to pay or to have insurance details checked, then to the laboratory, then back to the doctor with results, and finally to the pharmacy. Billing and insurance claims are compiled from paper records by the accounts office.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Long waits and repeated queuing**: patients queue at every hand-off, and folders are frequently misplaced.\n- **Information gaps**: doctors may not see previous results or allergies; handwriting causes dispensing risk.\n- **Revenue leakage**: services provided are not always captured for billing; insurance claims are delayed or rejected.\n- **No management information**: clinic waiting times, doctor workload and bed occupancy are unknown.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Queue at records; folder search\"] --> B[\"Queue for vitals\"]\n    B --> C[\"Queue for doctor; paper prescription and lab request\"]\n    C --> D[\"Queue at cashier or insurance desk\"]\n    D --> E[\"Queue at laboratory\"]\n    E --> F[\"Return to doctor with paper results\"]\n    F --> G[\"Queue at pharmacy\"]\n    G --> H[\"Month-end manual claims compilation\"]\n```\n\n### Redesigned process\n\n1. Registration captures identity and insurance details once, and verifies cover electronically where available; the patient receives a unique hospital number and a queue ticket.\n2. Nurses record vital signs directly in the EMR at triage, so the doctor sees them immediately along with history and allergies.\n3. The doctor orders tests and prescribes electronically. Lab orders and prescriptions appear instantly in the laboratory and pharmacy queues, with drug-allergy alerts.\n4. Charges are created automatically for each service at the point of care. Self-paying patients pay once, at a single point, by cash, card or mobile money; insured patients'' claims are assembled from the record.\n5. Results flow back into the EMR and notify the doctor; the pharmacy prepares medicines while the patient completes the visit.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Registration: identity and insurance verified once\"] --> B[\"Triage: vitals in EMR\"]\n    B --> C[\"Consultation: e-orders and e-prescription\"]\n    C --> D[\"Lab queue receives order\"]\n    C --> E[\"Pharmacy queue receives prescription; allergy check\"]\n    C --> F[\"Charges posted automatically at point of care\"]\n    D --> G[\"Results to EMR; doctor notified\"]\n    F --> H[\"Single payment point or claim assembled from record\"]\n    E --> I[\"Dispense and counsel patient\"]\n```\n\n### Enabling technology\n\nAn integrated HMS with patient administration, EMR, order communications, laboratory and pharmacy modules, billing and claims, linked to the hospital''s ERP for finance, procurement and HR. Interoperability standards such as HL7 FHIR ease future exchange with laboratories, insurers and other providers. Role-based access must ensure that only clinicians view clinical notes, and personal health data must be protected in line with the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843).\n\n### KPIs, risks and change management\n\nUseful KPIs are outpatient waiting time from registration to consultation, total visit time, prescription turnaround, claim rejection rate, unbilled services, and the proportion of consultations with complete electronic records. The main risks are clinician resistance to typing during consultations, slow systems at peak, insufficient devices in clinics, and patient-safety errors during the transition. Mitigation includes clinician champions, performance testing at the morning peak, paper fallback procedures, phased roll-out by department, and ADKAR-based training that explains to clinicians *why* structured data improves safety before teaching *how* to use screens."},{"heading":"International Case Study: England''s National Programme for IT","markdown":"The National Programme for IT (NPfIT) in the National Health Service in England was launched in 2002 with the aim of creating national electronic care records and related systems across the health service. It was one of the largest civilian IT programmes attempted. Large contracts were let to suppliers for regions of the country. The programme was heavily criticised in reports by the National Audit Office and parliamentary committees, notably over delays in delivering care record systems and weak engagement with clinicians and local organisations. In 2011 the government announced that the programme would be dismantled, while some components continued under other arrangements.\n\nFor this lesson, the case reinforces the six-step method: technology was procured centrally before many local processes and clinical needs were understood, and change management with clinicians was widely seen as inadequate. Healthcare process redesign must begin with the people who deliver care."}],"misconceptions":["\"A hospital system mainly speeds up billing.\" Correction: its greatest value is clinical: complete records, safer prescribing and coordinated care, with billing benefits as a by-product.","\"Centralising pharmacy purchasing always reduces stock.\" Correction: without accurate branch data and sensible reorder points, centralisation can increase stock-outs; data quality comes first.","\"Paper fallback procedures are a sign of a weak system.\" Correction: fallback procedures are an essential control for continuity of care during outages."],"examMistakes":["Starting with a product name instead of analysing the as-is process.","Listing weaknesses without linking each to a specific redesign step.","Proposing KPIs without definitions or baselines.","Ignoring patient safety, data protection and clinician engagement.","Inventing facts or figures about real health programmes."],"criticalQuestions":["Which is harder to change in a hospital: the technology or clinical working practices? Justify your answer.","How should a pharmacy chain balance central purchasing efficiency against branch pharmacists'' knowledge of local demand?","What would you need to see before agreeing that the hospital''s redesign has improved patient experience?","To what extent do the lessons of NPfIT apply to a single private hospital?"],"exercises":["**BPMN as-is and to-be in Bizagi Modeler or Camunda Modeler.** Model HealWell''s as-is replenishment process and the to-be process with pools for branch, head office procurement and supplier. Expected output: two BPMN diagrams with lanes, gateways and message flows, and a table of removed or changed steps.","**Hospital value-added analysis.** Classify each step of Adepa''s as-is outpatient process as value-adding, business value-adding or non-value-adding. Expected output: a table with an estimate of waiting steps removed by the redesign.","**KPI plan.** Prepare KPI definition cards for four KPIs for either case. Expected output: cards with formula, data source, baseline method, target, owner and frequency.","**SAP Learning activity.** On SAP Learning, explore free material on batch management or inventory management in SAP S/4HANA. Expected output: a 250-word note explaining how batch and expiry data would support FEFO and recalls at HealWell."],"takeaways":["Analyse as-is, diagnose, redesign, then specify technology, KPIs, risks and change management, in that order.","Pharmacy redesign centres on batch-level data, FEFO, central replenishment, payment integration and claims from dispensing records.","Hospital redesign centres on capturing data once, electronic orders and prescriptions, point-of-care charging and parallel working.","Patient safety, data protection and clinician engagement are non-negotiable design criteria.","NPfIT shows the danger of procuring technology centrally ahead of local process understanding."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Dumas, M., La Rosa, M., Mendling, J., & Reijers, H. A. (2018). *Fundamentals of business process management* (2nd ed.). Springer.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Monk, E., & Wagner, B. (2013). *Concepts in enterprise resource planning* (4th ed.). Cengage Learning.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Kotter, J. P. (1996). *Leading change*. Harvard Business School Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Hammer, M., & Champy, J. (1993). *Reengineering the corporation: A manifesto for business revolution*. HarperBusiness.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"HL7 International. (n.d.). *FHIR specification*.","url":"https://hl7.org/fhir/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"SAP Learning","url":"https://learning.sap.com/","description":"SAP. Free learning journeys including inventory and batch management in SAP S/4HANA.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Camunda documentation","url":"https://docs.camunda.io/","description":"Camunda. Official guides for modelling as-is and to-be processes in BPMN.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"HL7 FHIR","url":"https://hl7.org/fhir/","description":"HL7 International. Official specification and introductory material for the FHIR healthcare interoperability standard.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Healthcare process redesign starts with the as-is process and its weaknesses, not with technology. The illustrative pharmacy chain suffers from fragmented purchasing, no batch or expiry data, weak controls and manual claims; the redesign uses integrated POS with FEFO, central replenishment, workflow approval, scanned deliveries and claims from dispensing records. The illustrative hospital suffers from repeated queuing, paper records and revenue leakage; the redesign captures data once, uses electronic orders and prescriptions and posts charges at the point of care. KPIs, risks and change management complete each analysis.\n\n- Six steps: describe, diagnose, redesign, enable, measure, manage change.\n- FEFO plus batch traceability reduce expiry losses and support recalls.\n- E-prescribing removes handwriting risk and enables parallel pharmacy preparation.\n- Point-of-care charging reduces revenue leakage.\n- Clinician engagement is a critical success factor.\n- NPfIT (2002-2011): central procurement ahead of local readiness."}'::jsonb, 2530, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('10a0f9b5-cb19-570e-a09f-e1c564604ea8', '2f69fb46-06b4-5abd-bb43-5149b3fa2871', 'grocery-pos-and-restaurant-systems', 'Grocery Inventory and POS, and Restaurant Ordering Systems', 'Analyse the as-is processes of an illustrative Ghanaian supermarket and restaurant, redesign them around integrated point of sale, inventory and kitchen display systems, and specify KPIs, risks and change management.', array['Grocery inventory and POS', 'Restaurant ordering and kitchen management']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Retail and hospitality businesses live on thin margins and fast-moving stock. A supermarket may carry thousands of product lines, many perishable, sold in high volumes through several tills. A restaurant converts raw ingredients into dishes within minutes, under pressure, while customers wait. In both, the gap between what the system says and what is physically on the shelf or in the kitchen determines profitability: shrinkage, waste, stock-outs and slow service all erode margin.\n\nThis lesson applies the six-step case method from the previous lesson to two illustrative Ghanaian businesses: a supermarket with point of sale (POS) and inventory, and a restaurant with ordering and kitchen management. For each you will analyse the as-is process and its weaknesses, compare as-is and to-be diagrams, specify supporting technology, define KPIs, and plan for risks and change. A widely reported international retail failure shows how inventory data quality can undermine an entire market entry.","objectives":["Analyse as-is supermarket and restaurant processes to identify shrinkage, waste, delay and control weaknesses.","Apply redesign principles to create to-be processes integrating POS, inventory, procurement and kitchen operations.","Specify enterprise technology components such as barcode-based POS, perpetual inventory, recipe-based stock deduction and kitchen display systems.","Calculate and interpret retail and restaurant KPIs, including shrinkage rate and food cost percentage.","Evaluate implementation risks and change-management needs in high-pressure frontline environments."],"prerequisites":["The six-step case method from the Pharmacy ERP and Hospital Management Systems lesson.","KPI calculation (gross margin, stock turnover) from the Business Intelligence lesson.","Data migration and master data quality concepts."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Point of sale (POS)","definition":"The system and devices at which sales are recorded and payments taken, ideally integrated with inventory and finance."},{"term":"Perpetual inventory","definition":"An inventory method in which every receipt, sale, transfer and adjustment updates stock records in real time."},{"term":"Shrinkage","definition":"Loss of inventory through theft, damage, spoilage or administrative error, measured as the gap between recorded and physical stock."},{"term":"Cycle counting","definition":"Counting a subset of products regularly, rather than all stock at once, to maintain inventory accuracy."},{"term":"Recipe (bill of materials)","definition":"The list of ingredients and quantities that make up a menu item, used to deduct stock when the item is sold."},{"term":"Kitchen display system (KDS)","definition":"Screens in the kitchen that receive orders from POS or waiter devices, route them to stations and track preparation times."},{"term":"Food cost percentage","definition":"The cost of ingredients used divided by food sales revenue, a core restaurant profitability measure."},{"term":"Product master data","definition":"The authoritative record for each product, including barcode, description, unit of measure, supplier, cost and price."}],"body":[{"heading":"Case A: A Supermarket with POS and Inventory (Illustrative scenario)","markdown":"### As-is process\n\n*Illustrative scenario.* \"MarketPlus\" is a two-branch supermarket in Accra and Tema. Each branch has five tills running basic cash-register software that records sales by department (for example, \"groceries\", \"toiletries\") but not by individual product. Prices are on shelf labels; cashiers sometimes key prices manually when barcodes fail. Stock is counted fully twice a year. Buyers reorder by walking the aisles and calling suppliers. Deliveries are checked against delivery notes by the store manager, who also approves supplier invoices. Mobile money and card payments are reconciled to bank statements by hand weekly.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **No product-level sales data**: buyers cannot see what sells, so slow lines accumulate and popular lines run out.\n- **High, invisible shrinkage**: with only twice-yearly counts, losses from theft, spoilage and errors are discovered months later and cannot be traced.\n- **Pricing errors**: manual price entry causes under-charging and over-charging, harming margin and trust.\n- **Perishables waste**: no expiry tracking for dairy, bakery and produce.\n- **Control weakness**: the store manager both receives goods and approves invoices.\n- **Slow reconciliation**: weekly manual matching of electronic payments delays the detection of errors.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Cashier scans or keys price; sale recorded by department\"] --> B[\"Cash, card or mobile money taken\"]\n    B --> C[\"Weekly manual payment reconciliation\"]\n    D[\"Buyer walks aisles; phones supplier\"] --> E[\"Delivery checked by store manager\"]\n    E --> F[\"Store manager approves invoice\"]\n    F --> G[\"Payment to supplier\"]\n    H[\"Full stock count twice a year\"] --> I[\"Shrinkage discovered months later\"]\n```\n\n### Redesigned process\n\n1. A product master is built with a unique barcode, unit of measure, supplier, cost and price for every line, owned by a head office category team.\n2. Every sale is scanned at product level; prices come only from the master, and manual price override requires a supervisor''s authorisation, which is logged.\n3. Perpetual inventory updates with each sale, receipt, transfer and write-off.\n4. The system proposes orders from minimum and maximum stock levels and sales velocity; buyers review and approve.\n5. Receiving staff scan deliveries against the purchase order; discrepancies are recorded; the three-way match is performed by accounts payable, not the store manager.\n6. Cycle counts of high-risk categories (for example, spirits, toiletries, mobile phone vouchers) run weekly; variances above a tolerance are investigated.\n7. Electronic payments are matched daily through payment provider interfaces.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Product master owned by category team\"] --> B[\"Scan at POS: price from master\"]\n    B --> C{\"Price override?\"}\n    C -->|\"Yes\"| D[\"Supervisor authorisation logged\"]\n    C -->|\"No\"| E[\"Perpetual inventory updated\"]\n    D --> E\n    E --> F[\"System order proposal; buyer approves\"]\n    F --> G[\"Receiving scans delivery against PO\"]\n    G --> H[\"Accounts payable three-way match\"]\n    E --> I[\"Weekly cycle counts; variance investigation\"]\n    B --> J[\"Daily automatic payment matching\"]\n```\n\n### Enabling technology\n\nA retail ERP or POS-plus-ERP combination with product-level POS, barcode scanners and scales, perpetual multi-branch inventory, replenishment, purchasing, accounts payable and BI. Handheld scanners support receiving and cycle counting. GS1 barcodes provide global product identifiers; locally packed goods receive internal codes. Offline POS mode protects trading during network outages.\n\n### Worked KPI example\n\n*Illustrative figures.* After go-live, a cycle count of the toiletries category finds physical stock at cost of GHS 46,500 against a system value of GHS 48,000.\n\n- **Shrinkage** = `48,000 − 46,500 = GHS 1,500`.\n- **Shrinkage rate** = `1,500 / 48,000 = 3.1%` of the category''s recorded stock value.\n\nBecause counts now run weekly by category, the loss can be located in time and place, and investigated (for example, by reviewing voids and price overrides by till). Other KPIs include stock-out rate on top-selling lines, perishables write-off as a share of perishables sales, price override rate per cashier, and inventory record accuracy.\n\n### Risks and change management\n\nThe biggest risk is **master data quality**: thousands of products must be set up accurately with correct barcodes, units and prices before go-live. Others include cashier resistance to stricter override controls, queue delays during the first weeks, and supplier non-compliance with barcode standards. Mitigation includes a dedicated data team, a full mock trading day before go-live, extra cashiers and floor supervisors in the first fortnight, and transparent communication that override logging protects honest staff."},{"heading":"Case B: A Restaurant with Ordering and Kitchen Management (Illustrative scenario)","markdown":"### As-is process\n\n*Illustrative scenario.* \"Chop Bar Royale\" is a busy restaurant in Osu, Accra, serving dine-in, takeaway and delivery-platform orders. Waiters write orders on paper pads and carry them to the kitchen, where tickets are pinned to a rail. Delivery orders arrive on a separate tablet for each platform and are re-written onto paper. The cashier totals bills from the paper copies. The chef orders ingredients by phone based on experience, and stock is counted monthly.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Lost and misread tickets**: handwriting causes wrong dishes and remakes; tickets are lost at peak times.\n- **No sequencing**: the kitchen cannot see order age or prioritise; dishes for the same table are not synchronised.\n- **Re-keying from delivery tablets**: delays and errors on delivery orders.\n- **Unbilled items**: dishes served but not written on the bill leak revenue.\n- **No food cost control**: ingredient usage is not linked to sales, so waste and theft are invisible.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Waiter writes paper order\"] --> B[\"Ticket pinned on kitchen rail\"]\n    C[\"Delivery platform tablets\"] --> D[\"Order rewritten on paper\"]\n    D --> B\n    B --> E[\"Chef prepares in rail order\"]\n    E --> F[\"Cashier totals bill from paper copy\"]\n    G[\"Chef phones suppliers from experience\"] --> H[\"Monthly stock count\"]\n```\n\n### Redesigned process\n\n1. Waiters enter orders on handheld devices at the table; delivery-platform orders flow into the same POS through integrations.\n2. The POS routes each item to the right KDS station (grill, soups, drinks), showing order age, table and modifiers, with colour changes when target preparation times are exceeded.\n3. The KDS coordinates \"fire\" timing so dishes for one table are ready together; cooks bump items when complete, recording preparation times.\n4. Bills are generated from the order record, so every item ordered is billed; payment by card or mobile money is taken at the table.\n5. Each sale deducts ingredients using recipes; the system compares theoretical usage with actual usage from weekly counts.\n6. Purchase proposals are generated from par levels and forecast covers, approved by the manager.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Handheld order at table\"] --> C[\"Integrated POS\"]\n    B[\"Delivery platform integration\"] --> C\n    C --> D[\"KDS routes items to stations with timers\"]\n    D --> E[\"Items bumped; prep times recorded\"]\n    C --> F[\"Bill from order record; pay at table\"]\n    C --> G[\"Recipe-based ingredient deduction\"]\n    G --> H[\"Theoretical versus actual usage report\"]\n    G --> I[\"Purchase proposals from par levels\"]\n```\n\n### Worked KPI example\n\n*Illustrative figures.* In a week, food sales are GHS 60,000. Opening ingredient stock was GHS 12,000, purchases were GHS 20,000 and closing stock is GHS 11,000.\n\n- **Actual food cost** = `opening + purchases − closing = 12,000 + 20,000 − 11,000 = GHS 21,000`.\n- **Actual food cost %** = `21,000 / 60,000 = 35%`.\n- Recipe-based **theoretical food cost** for the dishes sold is GHS 18,600, or `18,600 / 60,000 = 31%`.\n- **Variance** = `21,000 − 18,600 = GHS 2,400` (4 percentage points), indicating waste, over-portioning, unrecorded staff meals or theft to be investigated.\n\nOther KPIs include average ticket time per station, remake rate, table turn time, and delivery order acceptance-to-dispatch time.\n\n### Risks and change management\n\nRisks include Wi-Fi failures in the kitchen, staff resistance from experienced cooks used to paper, inaccurate recipes that make theoretical cost meaningless, and dependence on delivery-platform integrations that may change. Mitigation includes a resilient local network with a fallback printer, involving the head chef in recipe costing, training during quieter periods, and ADKAR-based coaching that demonstrates how the KDS reduces shouting and remakes."},{"heading":"Comparing the Two Cases","markdown":"Although a supermarket and a restaurant look different, the analysis reveals the same underlying pattern:\n\n| Dimension | Supermarket (MarketPlus) | Restaurant (Chop Bar Royale) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core data foundation | Product master with barcodes, units and prices | Menu items with accurate recipes and ingredient costs |\n| Capture at source | Product-level scanning at the till | Handheld ordering at the table and integrated delivery orders |\n| Main control | Override authorisation, cycle counts, independent three-way match | Billing from the order record, theoretical versus actual usage |\n| Main performance lever | Replenishment from sales velocity | Station routing and synchronised preparation on the KDS |\n| Critical KPI | Shrinkage rate and stock-out rate | Food cost variance and ticket time |\n| Main adoption risk | Cashiers and receiving staff under time pressure | Experienced cooks used to paper tickets |\n\nIn both cases the technology succeeds only if the data foundation is accurate and frontline staff accept the new routines during peak trading. This is why the redesign begins with master data and recipes, why go-live is planned for a quieter period, and why the change plan concentrates on supervisors who can coach staff on the floor. Students should notice that the controls in each redesign come from process design (who can override, who matches invoices, what is billed from which record), with technology enforcing and recording them."},{"heading":"International Case Study: Target''s Canadian Expansion","markdown":"Target Corporation, the US retailer, opened its first stores in Canada in 2013, expanding rapidly to well over a hundred locations. Shoppers widely reported empty shelves while distribution centres held excess stock of some lines. Detailed press accounts afterwards described serious problems with supply chain processes and with the quality of product data entered into new enterprise systems, such as incorrect dimensions, units and supplier details, which disrupted replenishment. In January 2015 Target announced that it would close its Canadian operations.\n\nThe case shows that in retail, the product master is the foundation of every process in the to-be design: replenishment, receiving, pricing and analytics all fail when item data is wrong. It also shows the danger of opening many stores at once on new systems without stabilising data and processes at a smaller scale first."}],"misconceptions":["\"A modern POS automatically controls shrinkage.\" Correction: controls come from process design, including override authorisation, cycle counts and investigation of variances.","\"Recipe-based costing is only for large chains.\" Correction: even a single restaurant benefits from comparing theoretical and actual food cost.","\"Kitchen display systems simply replace paper.\" Correction: their value lies in routing, timing, synchronisation and performance data, which paper cannot provide."],"examMistakes":["Describing technology features without linking them to diagnosed weaknesses.","Omitting master data and recipe accuracy as preconditions for the redesign.","Calculating KPIs without interpreting variances.","Ignoring frontline staff experience and peak-time pressures in change plans."],"criticalQuestions":["How far should a supermarket rely on automated replenishment rather than buyers'' judgement for fresh produce?","Could stricter price-override controls damage customer service? How would you balance control and speed at the till?","What does a persistent gap between theoretical and actual food cost tell a restaurant owner, and what would you investigate first?","Which lessons from Target Canada apply to a two-branch Ghanaian supermarket?"],"exercises":["**BPMN in Camunda Modeler or Bizagi Modeler.** Model MarketPlus''s to-be receiving process with pools for supplier and supermarket, lanes for receiving clerk and accounts payable, and a gateway for quantity discrepancies. Expected output: a BPMN diagram with message flows and an exception path.","**Restaurant redesign.** Produce a table listing each as-is weakness at Chop Bar Royale, the redesign step that addresses it and the KPI that will show improvement. Expected output: a three-column traceability table.","**Food cost calculation.** Food sales are GHS 45,000, opening stock GHS 9,000, purchases GHS 16,000, closing stock GHS 8,500 and theoretical cost GHS 14,400. Expected output: actual food cost, actual and theoretical percentages, variance and a short interpretation.","**SAP Learning activity.** On SAP Learning, explore free material on retail or inventory management processes. Expected output: a 200-word note on how perpetual inventory and cycle counting are supported."],"takeaways":["In retail and hospitality, process redesign rests on accurate product master and recipe data.","Product-level POS, perpetual inventory, cycle counts and override controls make shrinkage visible and manageable.","KDS and integrated ordering remove re-keying, synchronise preparation and generate performance data.","Comparing theoretical with actual usage is a powerful control on waste and theft.","Target Canada shows how item data quality failures can undermine an entire operation."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Dumas, M., La Rosa, M., Mendling, J., & Reijers, H. A. (2018). *Fundamentals of business process management* (2nd ed.). Springer.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Monk, E., & Wagner, B. (2013). *Concepts in enterprise resource planning* (4th ed.). Cengage Learning.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Kimball, R., & Ross, M. (2013). *The data warehouse toolkit: The definitive guide to dimensional modeling* (3rd ed.). Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"GS1. (n.d.). *GS1 standards*.","url":"https://www.gs1.org/standards","verification":"unverified"}],"videos":[{"title":"SAP Learning","url":"https://learning.sap.com/","description":"SAP. Free learning journeys including inventory management and retail processes.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Bizagi","url":"https://www.bizagi.com/","description":"Bizagi. Official site for Bizagi Modeler, used for modelling as-is and to-be retail processes.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"GS1 standards","url":"https://www.gs1.org/standards","description":"GS1. Official overview of barcode and product identification standards used in retail.","verification":"unverified"}],"revisionSummary":"The illustrative supermarket lacks product-level sales data, has invisible shrinkage, manual pricing and weak receiving controls; the redesign uses a governed product master, product-level scanning, perpetual inventory, system order proposals, scanned receiving with independent three-way match, cycle counts and daily payment matching. The illustrative restaurant suffers from paper tickets, re-keyed delivery orders, unbilled items and no food cost control; the redesign uses handheld ordering, integrated delivery platforms, KDS routing and recipe-based stock deduction.\n\n- Shrinkage rate = (system − physical) / system value.\n- Food cost % = ingredient cost used / food sales.\n- Theoretical versus actual usage exposes waste and theft.\n- Price overrides need authorisation and logging.\n- Master data and recipes are preconditions, not afterthoughts.\n- Target Canada (2013-2015): item data problems emptied shelves."}'::jsonb, 2468, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('28e34241-338e-5ab7-884a-029b631bb023', '2f69fb46-06b4-5abd-bb43-5149b3fa2871', 'logistics-and-distribution-systems', 'Logistics and Distribution Systems', 'Analyse the as-is order fulfilment process of an illustrative Ghanaian distributor, redesign it with warehouse management and route planning systems, and evaluate KPIs, risks and change management for the redesign.', array['Logistics and distribution systems', 'Warehouse management', 'Route planning']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Distribution firms sit between manufacturers or importers and the retailers, pharmacies and hospitals that serve end customers. Their value lies in getting the right goods to the right place at the right time, in the right condition, at the lowest cost. That depends on two tightly linked operations: the **warehouse**, where goods are received, stored, picked and packed, and **transport**, where deliveries are planned, dispatched and confirmed. In both, small inefficiencies multiply across thousands of order lines and hundreds of kilometres each day.\n\nThis final case lesson applies the six-step method to an illustrative Ghanaian distribution firm. You will analyse its as-is order fulfilment process, compare as-is and to-be diagrams, specify warehouse management and route planning technology integrated with the ERP, calculate logistics KPIs, and plan for risk and change. A well-documented international failure shows how ambitious warehouse automation combined with a new ERP can threaten an entire business.","objectives":["Analyse an as-is order-to-delivery process to identify delays, errors, wasted movement and control weaknesses.","Explain core warehouse management functions: receiving, putaway, slotting, picking strategies, packing and shipping.","Apply route planning principles and constraints to redesign delivery operations.","Calculate and interpret logistics KPIs such as on-time in-full (OTIF), pick accuracy and vehicle utilisation.","Evaluate risks of combining ERP, warehouse automation and transport systems, drawing on a documented case."],"prerequisites":["The six-step case method used in this module.","Integration patterns and data migration (Enterprise Implementation module).","Supply chain management concepts (CRM, SCM and HRIS lesson)."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Warehouse management system (WMS)","definition":"Software that directs and records warehouse operations at the level of individual locations, pallets and tasks."},{"term":"Putaway","definition":"Moving received goods to a storage location, ideally one directed by the system according to rules."},{"term":"Slotting","definition":"Assigning products to storage locations to minimise travel, for example placing fast movers near dispatch."},{"term":"Wave picking","definition":"Grouping many orders into a planned batch (wave) picked together, often aligned with vehicle departure times."},{"term":"Pick accuracy","definition":"The proportion of order lines picked with the correct item and quantity."},{"term":"Transport management system (TMS)","definition":"Software that plans loads and routes, assigns vehicles, tracks deliveries and records proof of delivery."},{"term":"Vehicle routing problem (VRP)","definition":"The optimisation problem of designing delivery routes for a fleet subject to constraints such as capacity, time windows and driver hours."},{"term":"Proof of delivery (POD)","definition":"Evidence that a delivery was received, such as an electronic signature, photograph and timestamp captured on a mobile device."},{"term":"OTIF (on-time in-full)","definition":"The proportion of orders delivered both on time and complete."}],"body":[{"heading":"Case: A Distribution Firm (Illustrative scenario)","markdown":"### As-is process\n\n*Illustrative scenario.* \"Kasa Distribution Ltd\" distributes packaged foods, beverages and household products from a warehouse in Tema to about 600 retail customers across Greater Accra, Eastern and Central regions. Sales representatives take orders by phone and WhatsApp and pass them to an order desk, which keys them into an accounting package. Printed order sheets go to the warehouse, where pickers walk the aisles from memory to find products. There are no fixed locations; fast and slow movers are mixed. Picked goods are checked by a supervisor and loaded onto trucks. The transport manager plans routes each morning on a whiteboard based on experience. Drivers return with signed paper delivery notes, which are filed and later used to raise invoices. Customer complaints about missing items are handled by phone.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Re-keying and delay**: orders pass through several hands before reaching the warehouse; errors in keying flow through to picking.\n- **Wasted movement**: without slotting or directed picking, pickers travel long distances and search for items.\n- **Pick errors**: memory-based picking produces wrong items and short picks, discovered only by customers.\n- **Inefficient routes**: whiteboard planning ignores time windows, traffic patterns and vehicle capacity, causing overtime and partially empty trucks.\n- **Slow cash cycle**: invoices are raised only after paper delivery notes return, delaying collection.\n- **No visibility**: neither customers nor sales staff know where an order is; disputes rely on paper.\n- **Stock inaccuracy**: unrecorded movements and the absence of locations make system stock unreliable.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Sales rep takes order by phone or WhatsApp\"] --> B[\"Order desk keys order\"]\n    B --> C[\"Printed order sheet to warehouse\"]\n    C --> D[\"Picker searches aisles from memory\"]\n    D --> E[\"Supervisor checks and loads truck\"]\n    F[\"Transport manager plans routes on whiteboard\"] --> E\n    E --> G[\"Driver delivers; paper delivery note signed\"]\n    G --> H[\"Notes returned and filed\"]\n    H --> I[\"Invoice raised days later\"]\n    G --> J[\"Complaints handled by phone\"]\n```\n\n### Redesigned process\n\nThe redesign integrates three systems around a single order record:\n\n1. **Order capture at source.** Sales representatives enter orders on a mobile app linked to the ERP, which checks credit limits, prices and available-to-promise stock in real time; key customers may order through a web portal.\n2. **Wave planning.** Orders are grouped into waves aligned with planned vehicle departures. The TMS proposes routes first, so that picking sequence matches loading sequence (last drop loaded first).\n3. **Directed picking.** The WMS sends pick tasks to handheld scanners, directing pickers along an optimised path through slotted locations. Scanning each item and location confirms accuracy.\n4. **Packing and shipping.** Scanned verification at the dispatch dock replaces manual supervisor checking for most orders; exceptions are flagged.\n5. **Route execution.** Drivers follow routes on a mobile app with digital addresses and customer time windows. Electronic POD (signature, photograph, timestamp and location) is captured at each drop; short deliveries or refusals are recorded with reasons.\n6. **Invoice on delivery.** Confirmed POD triggers the invoice immediately in the ERP, and customers can pay by mobile money linked to the invoice reference.\n7. **Continuous improvement.** BI dashboards show OTIF, pick accuracy and route performance; slotting is reviewed monthly using sales velocity.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Mobile order entry: credit, price and stock checked\"] --> B[\"ERP sales order\"]\n    B --> C[\"TMS route plan by vehicle and time window\"]\n    C --> D[\"WMS wave aligned to departures\"]\n    D --> E[\"Directed, scanned picking from slotted locations\"]\n    E --> F[\"Scanned dispatch verification; exceptions flagged\"]\n    F --> G[\"Driver app: route, digital address, time window\"]\n    G --> H[\"Electronic POD with photo, signature, location\"]\n    H --> I[\"Invoice triggered automatically; mobile money payment\"]\n    H --> J[\"OTIF and route dashboards\"]\n```\n\n### Warehouse management in more depth\n\n| WMS function | As-is practice | To-be practice | Benefit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Receiving | Count against delivery note | Scan against advance shipping notice or purchase order; record batches | Accurate stock from arrival |\n| Putaway | Wherever there is space | System-directed to slotted location | Findable stock, less travel |\n| Slotting | None | Fast movers near dispatch; heavy items low | Shorter pick paths, safer handling |\n| Picking | Paper sheet, memory | Wave picking on scanners with path optimisation | Higher productivity and accuracy |\n| Inventory control | Annual count | Cycle counts by location | Reliable available-to-promise |\n| Shipping | Supervisor visual check | Scanned load verification by route sequence | Fewer short and wrong deliveries |\n\n### Route planning in more depth\n\nRoute planning is a form of the vehicle routing problem. Exact optimisation is computationally hard for large instances, so TMS products use heuristics to find good routes quickly. The value comes from respecting **constraints** that whiteboard planning struggles to handle simultaneously: vehicle weight and volume capacity, customer receiving hours, driver working hours, road restrictions, and expected traffic at different times of day. In Ghanaian cities, digital addresses such as those from the GhanaPostGPS system can reduce time spent finding customers, and historic delivery-time data improves estimates of time per drop."},{"heading":"Worked Example: Logistics KPIs","markdown":"*Illustrative figures for one month after go-live.*\n\n| Measure | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Orders delivered | 4,000 |\n| Orders delivered on time | 3,680 |\n| Orders delivered in full | 3,800 |\n| Orders delivered both on time and in full | 3,560 |\n| Order lines picked | 52,000 |\n| Order lines picked correctly | 51,480 |\n| Vehicle capacity available on trips (tonnes) | 1,500 |\n| Tonnes actually carried | 1,170 |\n\n- **On-time rate** = `3,680 / 4,000 = 92%`.\n- **In-full rate** = `3,800 / 4,000 = 95%`.\n- **OTIF** = `3,560 / 4,000 = 89%`. OTIF must be counted from orders meeting *both* conditions; multiplying the separate rates (`0.92 × 0.95 ≈ 87.4%`) gives the wrong answer, because failures overlap.\n- **Pick accuracy** = `51,480 / 52,000 = 99.0%`.\n- **Vehicle utilisation (weight)** = `1,170 / 1,500 = 78%`.\n\n**Interpretation.** OTIF is limited more by lateness than by incompleteness, which points first to route planning and loading delays. A 1% pick error rate still means 520 wrong lines a month, each potentially a complaint or credit note, so scanning discipline and slotting deserve attention. Utilisation of 78% suggests scope to consolidate routes, but only if time windows allow. Each KPI needs a baseline from before go-live to show improvement."},{"heading":"Supporting Enterprise Technology","markdown":"- **ERP**: customers, products, pricing, credit control, sales orders, invoicing, accounts receivable and finance.\n- **WMS**: integrated with the ERP, either as an ERP module or a specialist product, with handheld scanners and label printers.\n- **TMS and driver app**: route planning, dispatch, tracking and electronic POD on rugged smartphones.\n- **Integration**: API-based exchange of orders, stock, shipments and POD between ERP, WMS and TMS, with monitored error queues; mobile money integration for collections.\n- **BI**: dashboards for OTIF, pick accuracy, cost per drop and customer service.\n- **Security**: role-based access separating credit limit changes, order entry, dispatch and cash posting; drivers cannot alter invoice values."},{"heading":"Risks and Change Management","markdown":"| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Inaccurate stock and location data at go-live | Full count and location labelling before cut-over; cycle counts thereafter |\n| Integration failures between ERP, WMS and TMS | End-to-end integration and volume testing; monitored error queues |\n| Picker and driver resistance to scanning and tracking | Involve supervisors in design; explain how scanning removes blame for errors; incentives tied to team accuracy |\n| Over-reliance on algorithms ignoring local knowledge | Let transport planners adjust routes; feed driver feedback into time-per-drop data |\n| Peak-season disruption | Go live in a low season, pilot one route cluster first |\n| Device loss or connectivity gaps on routes | Offline-capable apps with later synchronisation; device management |\n\nKotter''s model provides the change structure: create urgency using complaint and credit-note data; form a coalition of warehouse, transport, sales and finance leaders; communicate a vision of \"every order right, first time\"; remove obstacles such as poor shelving labels; and generate short-term wins by publicising improved OTIF on the pilot route cluster. ADKAR guides individual coaching of pickers and drivers, many of whom will be using handheld devices at work for the first time."},{"heading":"International Case Study: FoxMeyer Drug","markdown":"FoxMeyer Drug was a large US pharmaceutical distributor. In the mid-1990s it undertook an ambitious programme combining an SAP R/3 ERP implementation with highly automated warehouse systems, intended to improve efficiency and support growth on thin margins. The project encountered serious difficulties, including problems integrating the ERP with warehouse automation and handling the company''s very high order volumes, and the expected benefits did not materialise. FoxMeyer filed for bankruptcy in 1996. The bankruptcy trustee later brought lawsuits against SAP and the consulting firm involved, which were eventually settled.\n\nThe case is widely taught alongside Hershey as a cautionary tale. Its lessons for distribution firms are direct: integrating ERP, WMS and automation is technically demanding and must be proven under realistic volumes; projects justified by aggressive savings assumptions leave no margin for error; and changing warehouse technology and core business systems at the same time multiplies risk. A phased approach, piloting one warehouse or route cluster first, is far safer."}],"misconceptions":["\"Route optimisation software will always find the best route.\" Correction: it finds good routes within the constraints and data it is given; poor address data or unrealistic time-per-drop estimates produce poor plans.","\"OTIF equals the on-time rate multiplied by the in-full rate.\" Correction: OTIF must count orders meeting both conditions, because failures overlap.","\"Scanning slows pickers down.\" Correction: directed, scanned picking usually reduces search time and rework, although it may feel slower at first.","\"Warehouse automation is the first step to efficiency.\" Correction: accurate locations, slotting and process discipline come first; automating chaos amplifies it."],"examMistakes":["Describing a WMS or TMS without linking functions to diagnosed weaknesses.","Calculating OTIF incorrectly or presenting KPIs without baselines or interpretation.","Omitting integration between ERP, WMS and TMS.","Ignoring the human side of tracking technology for drivers and pickers.","Embellishing the FoxMeyer case with figures that cannot be verified."],"criticalQuestions":["Should Kasa Distribution buy a WMS module from its ERP vendor or a specialist WMS? What criteria would you use?","How might GPS tracking of drivers affect trust and morale, and how should it be governed?","When does warehouse automation (conveyors, sorters) become justified for a distributor in Ghana?","What would you need to see in testing before approving a combined ERP and WMS go-live?"],"exercises":["**BPMN as-is and to-be.** In Camunda Modeler or Bizagi Modeler, model Kasa''s as-is order-to-delivery process and the to-be process with pools for customer, distributor and driver. Expected output: two BPMN diagrams with lanes, a gateway for short deliveries and a message flow for POD.","**KPI calculation.** For a month with 3,000 orders, 2,790 on time, 2,850 in full and 2,700 both, calculate on-time, in-full and OTIF rates. Expected output: the three rates and a sentence explaining whether OTIF differs from the product of the other two rates, and why.","**Slotting analysis.** Given a list of 20 products with monthly picks, rank them and propose an ABC slotting plan. Expected output: a table classifying products as A, B or C with proposed zones and a justification.","**SAP Learning activity.** On SAP Learning, explore free material on warehouse management or transportation management in SAP S/4HANA. Expected output: a 250-word note mapping vendor functions to the redesigned process steps in this lesson."],"takeaways":["Distribution performance depends on integrated order capture, warehouse execution and transport planning around one order record.","WMS functions (receiving, putaway, slotting, directed picking, cycle counting, scanned shipping) target specific weaknesses.","Route planning tools are only as good as their constraints and data, including addresses and time-per-drop.","OTIF counts orders meeting both conditions; interpret KPIs together and against baselines.","FoxMeyer shows the danger of combining a new ERP and warehouse automation without proving them at scale."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Monk, E., & Wagner, B. (2013). *Concepts in enterprise resource planning* (4th ed.). Cengage Learning.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Dumas, M., La Rosa, M., Mendling, J., & Reijers, H. A. (2018). *Fundamentals of business process management* (2nd ed.). Springer.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Scott, J. E., & Vessey, I. (2002). Managing risks in enterprise systems implementations. *Communications of the ACM, 45*(4), 74-81.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Kotter, J. P. (1996). *Leading change*. Harvard Business School Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"SAP Learning","url":"https://learning.sap.com/","description":"SAP. Free learning journeys including warehouse and transportation management topics.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Microsoft Learn: Dynamics 365 training","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/dynamics365/","description":"Microsoft. Self-paced modules including warehouse management and supply chain processes.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Camunda documentation","url":"https://docs.camunda.io/","description":"Camunda. Official guides for modelling cross-organisational processes with pools and message flows.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"The illustrative distributor suffers from re-keyed orders, memory-based picking, whiteboard routing, paper delivery notes and delayed invoicing. The redesign captures orders at source with real-time checks, plans routes and waves together, directs scanned picking from slotted locations, verifies loads, captures electronic proof of delivery and invoices on delivery, all integrated through APIs and measured on BI dashboards. KPIs include OTIF, pick accuracy and vehicle utilisation; risks centre on data accuracy, integration and frontline adoption.\n\n- WMS: receive, put away, slot, pick, count, ship.\n- TMS: plan routes under capacity, time-window and driver-hour constraints.\n- OTIF = orders both on time and in full / total orders.\n- Electronic POD triggers invoicing and shortens the cash cycle.\n- Pilot one route cluster or warehouse first.\n- FoxMeyer (1996 bankruptcy): ERP plus warehouse automation at once."}'::jsonb, 2444, 3, true)
