insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('b34476ae-8459-57d7-bbae-6c1be6f713f2', '90762921-08a6-5e20-875e-13608b1b4891', 'malware-and-ransomware', 'Malware and Ransomware', 'Classify malware by propagation and payload, trace a ransomware intrusion through the Cyber Kill Chain and MITRE ATT&CK, and design layered defences and recovery arrangements.', array['Malware', 'Ransomware']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Malicious software, or malware, has evolved from pranks written by hobbyists into a professional criminal industry. Today''s most damaging variant, ransomware, can halt hospitals, ports and banks within hours, and criminal groups increasingly combine encryption of systems with theft and public exposure of data. For a business manager, malware is no longer simply an \"IT problem\": it is a business continuity, legal and reputational risk.\n\nThis lesson classifies malware families by how they spread and what they do, explains the modern ransomware business model, and introduces two attack lifecycle models used by defenders: the Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain and MITRE ATT&CK. You will apply these models to the WannaCry and NotPetya outbreaks of 2017 and to an illustrative Ghanaian scenario, and you will learn why tested, offline backups remain the single most important ransomware control.","objectives":["Classify malware types (virus, worm, Trojan, ransomware, spyware, rootkit, botnet agent) by propagation method and payload.","Explain the stages of the Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain and the structure of MITRE ATT&CK at overview level.","Analyse a ransomware incident by mapping attacker actions to kill chain stages and corresponding defences.","Compare WannaCry and NotPetya in terms of propagation, intent and lessons for defenders.","Evaluate a ransomware resilience plan for an organisation, including backup design and the decision whether to pay."],"prerequisites":["The CIA triad and the threat-vulnerability-risk model from Module 1.","Basic understanding of operating systems, file sharing and email."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Malware","definition":"Software intentionally designed to harm, exploit or gain unauthorised control of a device, network or data."},{"term":"Virus","definition":"Malware that attaches to a host file or program and spreads when that host is executed or shared."},{"term":"Worm","definition":"Self-propagating malware that spreads across networks by exploiting vulnerabilities without user action."},{"term":"Trojan","definition":"Malware disguised as legitimate software, relying on the user to install or run it."},{"term":"Ransomware","definition":"Malware that encrypts or locks data or systems and demands payment for restoration, often combined with data theft."},{"term":"Double extortion","definition":"A ransomware tactic in which attackers steal data before encrypting it and threaten to publish it if the ransom is not paid."},{"term":"Rootkit","definition":"Malware that hides its presence by subverting the operating system or firmware."},{"term":"Command and control (C2)","definition":"The communication channel attackers use to send instructions to compromised systems."},{"term":"Indicator of compromise (IOC)","definition":"Observable evidence, such as a file hash, domain or IP address, suggesting a system has been compromised."},{"term":"Lateral movement","definition":"Techniques attackers use to move from an initially compromised system to other systems within a network."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Classifying malware\n\nMalware is best classified on two independent dimensions: **how it spreads** (propagation) and **what it does** (payload).\n\n| Type | Propagation | Typical payload | Defensive focus |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Virus | Infects host files; spreads when files are shared or run | Varies: corruption, backdoor | Endpoint protection, application control |\n| Worm | Self-propagates via network vulnerabilities | Often carries other payloads | Patching, network segmentation, disabling legacy protocols |\n| Trojan | User installs disguised software | Backdoor, credential theft | User awareness, allow-listing, least privilege |\n| Spyware / infostealer | Usually Trojan or exploit delivery | Captures credentials, keystrokes, browser data | MFA, endpoint detection, credential hygiene |\n| Rootkit | Installed after compromise | Concealment, persistence | Secure boot, integrity monitoring, rebuild after compromise |\n| Botnet agent | Any delivery method | Joins a remotely controlled network for DDoS, spam or fraud | Egress filtering, monitoring for C2 traffic |\n| Ransomware | Phishing, exposed remote access, worms, supply chain | Encryption, data theft, extortion | Backups, MFA on remote access, segmentation, detection |\n\nReal campaigns blend types. A phishing attachment (Trojan) may install an infostealer, which gives attackers credentials; they then deploy ransomware manually across the network.\n\n### The ransomware business model\n\nModern ransomware is frequently run as **ransomware-as-a-service (RaaS)**: developers maintain the malware and payment infrastructure, while \"affiliates\" break into victims and share the proceeds. Some criminals specialise as **initial access brokers**, selling footholds in organisations to others. This division of labour means that even small organisations are targeted opportunistically.\n\nTypical stages of a human-operated ransomware attack are:\n\n1. **Initial access**: phishing, stolen credentials for remote desktop or VPN services without MFA, or exploitation of an unpatched internet-facing system.\n2. **Establish foothold and escalate privileges**: harvest passwords, exploit local weaknesses.\n3. **Discovery and lateral movement**: map the network, find domain controllers, file servers and backups.\n4. **Exfiltration**: copy valuable data to attacker-controlled storage (enabling double extortion).\n5. **Impact**: delete or encrypt backups, then encrypt systems and display a ransom note.\n\nThe gap between initial access and encryption can range from hours to weeks. That window is the defender''s opportunity: detection of lateral movement or unusual data transfer can stop an attack before impact.\n\n### Why \"pay or not pay\" is a governance question\n\nPaying a ransom does not guarantee decryption, may fund further crime, may breach sanctions laws in some jurisdictions and marks the victim as willing to pay. Many government agencies, including the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA), advise against payment. Yet a board facing a halted business may still consider it. The decision must be prepared *in advance* in an incident response plan, with legal advice, rather than improvised under pressure."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain\n\nIn 2011, Hutchins, Cloppert and Amin of Lockheed Martin described intrusions as a chain of seven stages. The key insight is that the attacker must succeed at *every* stage, while the defender needs to break only one link.\n\n| Stage | Attacker activity | Example defences |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Reconnaissance | Research targets: staff names, email formats, exposed services | Limit public information, monitor for scanning |\n| 2. Weaponisation | Build a payload, such as a malicious document | Threat intelligence on current lures |\n| 3. Delivery | Send the payload: email, website, USB, exposed service | Email filtering, web filtering, closing exposed ports |\n| 4. Exploitation | Trigger the vulnerability or trick the user | Patching, disabling macros, user awareness |\n| 5. Installation | Install persistent malware | Application allow-listing, endpoint detection and response |\n| 6. Command and control | Establish remote control channel | Egress filtering, DNS monitoring |\n| 7. Actions on objectives | Steal, encrypt, destroy | Segmentation, data loss prevention, offline backups |\n\nCritics note that the model is perimeter-centric and fits external malware intrusions better than insider threats or cloud identity attacks, which is one reason more granular models have emerged.\n\n### MITRE ATT&CK at overview level\n\nMITRE ATT&CK is a publicly available knowledge base of adversary behaviour based on real-world observations. It is organised as a matrix of **tactics** (the attacker''s goal at a stage, such as Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, Credential Access, Lateral Movement, Exfiltration and Impact) and **techniques** (how the goal is achieved, such as phishing or valid accounts). Each technique page lists detection and mitigation ideas. Defenders use ATT&CK to:\n\n- map their detections and identify coverage gaps;\n- describe incidents in a common language;\n- prioritise controls against techniques used by groups that target their sector.\n\nWhere the Kill Chain gives a simple linear story suitable for executives, ATT&CK provides the detailed vocabulary security teams use day to day. Note that ATT&CK describes behaviour for defensive purposes; it is not a guide to conducting attacks."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Mapping a ransomware incident to the kill chain\n\n**Illustrative scenario.** *Akwaaba Clinic* is a fictional private clinic in Kumasi with a small network: reception PCs, a file server holding patient records, and a remote desktop service exposed to the internet so the administrator can work from home.\n\n**Timeline of the (fictional) incident and mapping:**\n\n| Day | Event | Kill chain stage | ATT&CK tactic | Missed defence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Internet scan finds the exposed remote desktop port | Reconnaissance | Reconnaissance | Remote access exposed without VPN or MFA |\n| 2 | Attacker logs in using a password reused from a breached website | Delivery / Exploitation | Initial Access (valid accounts) | No MFA; no password reuse checks |\n| 2 | Attacker creates a new administrator account | Installation | Persistence | No alerting on new privileged accounts |\n| 3-9 | Attacker explores network and finds the file server and a backup drive mapped as a network share | Command and control / Actions | Discovery, Lateral Movement | Flat network; backups reachable from user systems |\n| 10 | Patient records copied out | Actions on objectives | Exfiltration | No egress monitoring |\n| 11 | Backups deleted, files encrypted, ransom note displayed | Actions on objectives | Impact | No offline or immutable backup |\n\n**Analysis.** Breaking any one link would have changed the outcome. MFA on remote access would probably have prevented initial access. Alerting on new administrator accounts would have given eight days'' warning. An offline backup would have turned a crisis into a recovery exercise. Because patient data was exfiltrated, the clinic also faces obligations under the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) regardless of whether it restores its systems.\n\n**Prioritised recommendations:** remove direct internet exposure of remote desktop and require VPN with MFA; adopt the 3-2-1 backup rule with at least one offline or immutable copy and regular restore tests; individual administrator accounts with alerting; segmentation separating reception PCs from the records server; patching and endpoint detection; an incident response plan with contact details for the Cyber Security Authority''s incident reporting channels."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Ghanaian organisations share the global ransomware risk but have particular exposures:\n\n- **SMEs with limited IT staff** often rely on a single external technician, use pirated or unsupported software that cannot be updated and expose remote access for convenience.\n- **Mobile money and banking malware** on Android phones can include fake apps that request accessibility permissions in order to read SMS codes or overlay login screens. Installing apps only from official stores and reviewing permissions are simple defences that customers can be taught.\n- **Removable media** remain common for sharing files between offices, so traditional viruses spread via USB drives still matter.\n- **Critical information infrastructure**: The Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038) provides for the designation of critical information infrastructure and incident reporting to the Cyber Security Authority. Hospitals, utilities and financial institutions should check whether these provisions apply to them.\n\nA practical recommendation for Ghanaian SMEs is a \"ransomware minimum\": licensed and updated operating systems, MFA on email and remote access, no daily use of administrator accounts and a weekly backup taken offline and test-restored."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### WannaCry and NotPetya (2017)\n\n**WannaCry (May 2017).** WannaCry was ransomware with worm capability. It spread by exploiting a vulnerability in the Server Message Block version 1 (SMBv1) protocol in Microsoft Windows, for which Microsoft had released a patch (MS17-010) in March 2017. The exploit, known as EternalBlue, had been leaked publicly the previous month. Infected machines scanned for other vulnerable systems, so the malware spread rapidly through networks with unpatched machines and SMB exposed. The UK''s National Health Service was severely disrupted: the UK National Audit Office later reported cancelled appointments and diverted ambulances. The spread slowed when a security researcher registered a domain name that the malware checked, which acted as a \"kill switch\". The UK and US governments later publicly attributed WannaCry to North Korean actors.\n\n**NotPetya (June 2017).** NotPetya first spread through a compromised software update for M.E.Doc, an accounting package widely used in Ukraine, which made it a **supply chain attack**. It then moved laterally using the EternalBlue exploit and stolen credentials. Although it displayed a ransom note, it was designed so that recovery through payment was not realistically possible, so it behaved as a destructive **wiper**. Multinationals with operations in Ukraine, including the shipping company Maersk and the pharmaceutical company Merck, suffered major disruption. The UK and US governments attributed NotPetya to the Russian military in 2018.\n\n**Lessons:**\n\n- Patch management and disabling legacy protocols such as SMBv1 directly reduce worm propagation.\n- Network segmentation limits how far self-propagating malware can travel.\n- Trusted software updates can become delivery channels; supplier risk management matters.\n- Credential theft enables spread even to patched machines, so privileged account protection is essential.\n- Offline backups and rebuild procedures determine how quickly an organisation recovers."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    R[Reconnaissance] --> W[Weaponisation]\n    W --> D[Delivery]\n    D --> E[Exploitation]\n    E --> I[Installation]\n    I --> C[\"Command and control\"]\n    C --> A[\"Actions on objectives (exfiltrate, encrypt)\"]\n    D -.-> D1[\"Email filter, close exposed ports\"]\n    E -.-> E1[\"Patching, MFA, awareness\"]\n    I -.-> I1[\"Allow-listing, EDR\"]\n    C -.-> C1[\"Egress filtering, DNS monitoring\"]\n    A -.-> A1[\"Segmentation, offline backups\"]\n```\n\nSolid arrows show the attacker''s progression; dotted arrows show the defences that can break each link."}],"misconceptions":["\"Antivirus is enough to stop ransomware.\" Human-operated ransomware often uses legitimate tools and stolen credentials that signature-based antivirus does not flag; layered controls and behavioural detection are needed.","\"If we restore from backup, the incident is over.\" Modern ransomware often steals data first, so restoration does not address the confidentiality breach, legal notification duties or persistence the attacker may have left.","\"Backups on a network share are safe.\" Attackers deliberately seek and delete reachable backups; at least one copy must be offline or immutable.","\"Only large companies are targeted.\" Opportunistic scanning and RaaS affiliates target any organisation with exposed weaknesses, including small clinics and shops.","\"NotPetya was just ransomware.\" Its design made recovery through payment unrealistic, so it functioned as destructive malware disguised as ransomware."],"examMistakes":["Confusing worms (self-propagating) with viruses (needing a host and user action).","Listing the kill chain stages without linking each to a specific defence.","Treating MITRE ATT&CK as a linear model rather than a matrix of tactics and techniques.","Describing WannaCry as a zero-day attack, when a patch had been available for about two months.","Recommending \"pay the ransom\" or \"never pay\" without discussing governance, legal advice and business impact."],"criticalQuestions":["Why might an organisation that had applied the March 2017 patch still have been affected by NotPetya?","Is the Cyber Kill Chain still useful for cloud-based organisations whose staff work from home? What would you add or change?","Should governments prohibit ransom payments? Consider the positions of victims, insurers and law enforcement.","How would you persuade the owner of a small Ghanaian business, who sees backups as an unnecessary expense, to invest in them?","What are the ethical and practical limits of sharing indicators of compromise publicly after an incident?"],"exercises":["**Kill chain mapping.** Using public reporting on WannaCry (for example the UK National Audit Office''s investigation), map the attack to the seven kill chain stages and state one defence per stage. Expected output: a seven-row table of stage, attacker action and defence.","**Explore ATT&CK safely.** Visit the MITRE ATT&CK website and open the Enterprise matrix. Choose three techniques under Initial Access and note the mitigations listed for each. Expected output: a table of technique, short description and two mitigations.","**Backup audit of your own data.** For a computer you own, document where your important files are stored, how many copies exist, whether one is offline and when you last restored a file. Expected output: a 3-2-1 assessment with one improvement implemented and a successful test restore recorded.","**Lab practice.** Complete the endpoint security or cyber threat management material on Cisco Skills for All, which provides a legal learning environment. Never download or run real malware samples on any system you do not own and control for that purpose. Expected output: a short reflection listing three detection techniques you learned."],"takeaways":["Malware is classified by propagation (virus, worm, Trojan) and payload (ransomware, spyware, rootkit, botnet agent).","Human-operated ransomware involves initial access, privilege escalation, lateral movement, exfiltration and impact, giving defenders a detection window.","The Cyber Kill Chain shows that breaking one link stops an attack; MITRE ATT&CK supplies detailed tactics and techniques.","WannaCry and NotPetya demonstrate the importance of patching, segmentation, supplier security and credential protection.","Offline or immutable, tested backups are the decisive ransomware recovery control, but they do not undo data theft."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Hutchins, E. M., Cloppert, M. J., & Amin, R. M. (2011). *Intelligence-driven computer network defense informed by analysis of adversary campaigns and intrusion kill chains*. Lockheed Martin Corporation.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"National Audit Office. (2017). *Investigation: WannaCry cyber attack and the NHS*. National Audit Office.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Stallings, W., & Brown, L. (2018). *Computer security: Principles and practice* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"The MITRE Corporation. (n.d.). *MITRE ATT&CK*.","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"MITRE ATT&CK","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/","description":"The MITRE Corporation. Official knowledge base with explanatory resources and training material on using adversary tactics and techniques for defence.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"CISA cybersecurity resources","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/","description":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency. Official agency site with ransomware guidance, advisories and awareness material for organisations.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Cisco Skills for All","url":"https://skillsforall.com/","description":"Cisco Networking Academy. Free courses that cover malware types and endpoint defence in a structured, legal learning environment.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Malware is classified by how it spreads and what it does; ransomware now combines encryption with data theft in a professional criminal ecosystem. The Cyber Kill Chain and MITRE ATT&CK help defenders map and break attacks, and WannaCry and NotPetya show the value of patching, segmentation, supplier assurance and offline backups.\n\n- Virus = host file; worm = self-propagating; Trojan = disguised.\n- Ransomware stages: access, escalate, move laterally, exfiltrate, encrypt.\n- Kill Chain: recon, weaponise, deliver, exploit, install, C2, actions.\n- ATT&CK: tactics (why) and techniques (how).\n- WannaCry: SMBv1 worm, patch available; NotPetya: supply chain wiper.\n- 3-2-1 backups with an offline or immutable copy, tested regularly."}'::jsonb, 2770, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('977b552f-439d-519e-9709-89af082c71ab', '90762921-08a6-5e20-875e-13608b1b4891', 'phishing-social-engineering-and-insiders', 'Phishing, Social Engineering and Insider Threats', 'Analyse why phishing and social engineering succeed, recognise mobile money and business email compromise patterns, and design proportionate people, process and technical controls including insider threat programmes.', array['Phishing', 'Social engineering', 'Insider threats']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Many of the most damaging security incidents begin not with clever code but with a convincing message or phone call. Attackers have learned that persuading a person to open a door is usually cheaper than breaking it down. Phishing emails, fraudulent calls claiming to be from a telecom operator, fake invoices and impersonated chief executives all exploit the same human tendencies: trust, helpfulness, fear and respect for authority.\n\nThe same organisational trust that social engineers exploit from outside can be abused from inside by employees, contractors and partners. Insider threats are difficult precisely because insiders already have legitimate access. This lesson explains the psychology of social engineering, the main phishing variants, the specifically Ghanaian patterns of mobile money fraud and SIM-swap abuse, and the design of insider threat controls that protect the organisation without creating a culture of suspicion.","objectives":["Explain the psychological principles that make social engineering effective.","Distinguish phishing, spear phishing, whaling, smishing, vishing and business email compromise.","Analyse a mobile money social engineering scheme and identify control points for the provider, the agent and the customer.","Compare malicious, negligent and compromised insiders and recommend controls for each.","Evaluate a security awareness programme against measurable outcomes rather than attendance."],"prerequisites":["The threat, vulnerability and risk concepts from Module 1.","General familiarity with email, SMS and mobile money services."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Social engineering","definition":"Manipulating people into performing actions or disclosing information that benefits the attacker."},{"term":"Phishing","definition":"Fraudulent messages, usually email, designed to trick recipients into revealing credentials, paying money or running malware."},{"term":"Spear phishing","definition":"Phishing tailored to a specific individual or organisation using researched details."},{"term":"Smishing and vishing","definition":"Phishing conducted by SMS (smishing) or by voice call (vishing)."},{"term":"Business email compromise (BEC)","definition":"Fraud in which an attacker impersonates or takes over a business email account to redirect payments or obtain sensitive data."},{"term":"Pretexting","definition":"Creating a fabricated scenario, such as posing as IT support, to justify a request for information or action."},{"term":"SIM swap","definition":"Fraudulently transferring a victim''s mobile number to a SIM card controlled by the attacker, allowing interception of calls and SMS codes."},{"term":"Insider threat","definition":"The potential for a person with authorised access to harm the organisation, deliberately or unintentionally."},{"term":"Segregation of duties","definition":"Dividing a sensitive task between people so that no single person can complete it alone."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Why social engineering works\n\nSocial engineering exploits predictable features of human judgement. The psychologist Robert Cialdini described principles of persuasion that attackers routinely abuse:\n\n| Principle | How attackers exploit it | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Authority | Impersonate a manager, regulator or bank official | \"This is the MD; process this transfer before the board meeting.\" |\n| Urgency and scarcity | Force fast decisions that bypass reflection | \"Your wallet will be blocked in 30 minutes unless you verify.\" |\n| Liking | Build rapport or use a familiar name | A friendly caller who knows your branch and your manager''s name |\n| Reciprocity | Offer help, then ask for something | \"I have fixed your account; just read me the code I sent.\" |\n| Social proof | Suggest that others have already complied | \"All staff in your department have updated their passwords here.\" |\n| Commitment and consistency | Obtain small agreements before a larger request | A survey that ends by asking for login details |\n\nAttackers also exploit **context**: busy end-of-month periods, public holidays, news events and organisational change. A message that would look suspicious on a calm Tuesday may seem plausible during payroll week.\n\n### The phishing family\n\n- **Bulk phishing** casts a wide net with generic lures (parcel delivery, account suspension).\n- **Spear phishing** uses research from social media, company websites and earlier breaches to target named individuals.\n- **Whaling** targets senior executives, whose authority makes successful compromise especially valuable.\n- **Smishing** uses SMS, where links are short and sender names can be spoofed; it is particularly relevant where mobile phones are the primary internet device.\n- **Vishing** uses voice calls and is often combined with smishing (\"we sent you a code; please read it back to confirm your identity\").\n- **Business email compromise** typically either spoofs a lookalike domain or uses a genuinely compromised mailbox to send convincing payment instructions, often by inserting itself into an existing email thread about an invoice.\n- **Credential harvesting sites** imitate login pages; increasingly, attackers use \"adversary-in-the-middle\" proxies that relay the victim''s credentials and one-time code to the real site in real time, defeating SMS or app codes. Phishing-resistant authentication, such as FIDO2 security keys or passkeys that are bound to the genuine website''s domain, counters this.\n\n### Insider threats\n\nInsiders fall into three broad categories:\n\n1. **Malicious insiders** deliberately misuse access for financial gain, revenge or ideology, for example a staff member who sells customer data or colludes in fraudulent transfers.\n2. **Negligent insiders** cause harm through carelessness, such as emailing data to a personal account or falling for phishing.\n3. **Compromised insiders** are legitimate users whose accounts or devices have been taken over by an external attacker.\n\nInsider risk increases when access exceeds need, when activity is not logged or reviewed, and when grievances, financial pressure or departures go unmanaged. Controls must therefore combine technical measures (least privilege, monitoring, data loss prevention) with human-resources processes (screening, clear policies, exit procedures) and culture (a safe way to report concerns)."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### A layered model of anti-phishing controls\n\nNo single control stops phishing. A defensible design layers controls across the attack path:\n\n| Layer | Purpose | Example controls |\n|---|---|---|\n| Email and SMS infrastructure | Reduce delivery of malicious messages | SPF, DKIM and DMARC email authentication; filtering; sandboxing attachments; operator SMS sender controls |\n| User interface | Help users notice risk | External-sender banners; warnings on lookalike domains |\n| People | Recognise and report | Role-based awareness training; simple \"report phishing\" button; positive reinforcement |\n| Process | Make a single deception insufficient | Call-back verification on payment detail changes; dual approval; \"never share codes\" rules |\n| Authentication | Make stolen credentials less useful | Phishing-resistant MFA; conditional access |\n| Detection and response | Limit damage quickly | Monitoring for impossible travel and new mailbox rules; rapid account suspension |\n\nThe **process** layer is often the most cost-effective: if every change to supplier bank details requires a call to a number already on file, a convincing BEC email alone cannot redirect money.\n\n### Measuring awareness\n\nAttendance at an annual lecture is a weak measure. Better metrics include the **reporting rate** (proportion of staff who report a simulated or real phishing message), **time to first report** and **repeat click rate**. Simulated phishing must be run ethically: with leadership approval, without humiliating individuals and with immediate constructive feedback. A rising reporting rate is generally a better sign of culture than a falling click rate alone."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Dissecting a mobile money \"wrong transfer\" scheme\n\n**Illustrative scenario.** Ama, a trader in Kumasi, receives an SMS that appears to be a mobile money credit alert for GH₵ 800. Minutes later a caller says, \"Madam, I sent money to you by mistake; it was my child''s school fees. Please send it back.\" Ama checks her phone, sees the \"alert\" and sends GH₵ 800 to the caller. Her actual balance had never increased; the SMS was a fake from an ordinary phone number formatted to look like an operator message.\n\n**Step 1: identify the principles exploited.** Urgency (school fees), liking and sympathy (a distressed parent), and authority of the apparent system message.\n\n**Step 2: identify the vulnerability.** Ama trusted the SMS text rather than verifying her balance through the official menu or app.\n\n**Step 3: identify control points.**\n\n| Party | Control | Type |\n|---|---|---|\n| Customer | Always check balance through the official USSD menu or app before acting on an SMS; reversals are handled by the operator, not by sending money back | Process / awareness |\n| Operator | Clear, consistent alert formats and education campaigns; a formal reversal process through customer service | Process |\n| Operator | Analytics to detect numbers that repeatedly receive \"returned\" funds from many customers | Detective |\n| Agent | Never disclose customers'' names or balances to callers | Policy |\n\n**Step 4: extend to SIM swap.** A related scheme targets account takeover: a fraudster gathers personal details (perhaps from a leaked customer list or a corrupt insider), persuades or bribes someone to issue a replacement SIM, then resets the victim''s wallet PIN using SMS codes. The control points are the operator''s SIM replacement process (strong identity verification against the Ghana Card, notifications to the old SIM, a cooling-off period before high-value transactions) and insider controls on staff who can issue replacements (individual accounts, logging, review of unusual volumes).\n\nThis analysis shows how a single fraud pattern crosses social engineering, process weakness and insider risk."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Social engineering is arguably the most common cyber-enabled fraud affecting ordinary Ghanaians and SMEs. Practical applications include:\n\n- **Banks and payment providers** should communicate a short, repeated message: staff will never ask for a PIN or one-time code. Consistency matters; if the institution''s own staff sometimes ask for codes, customers cannot distinguish legitimate from fraudulent calls.\n- **SMEs** paying suppliers by bank transfer or mobile money should adopt call-back verification for any change of payment details and dual approval above a threshold.\n- **Recruitment and off-boarding**: Organisations should remove system access on an employee''s last day and review the access of staff who move roles, reducing malicious and negligent insider risk.\n- **Data minimisation**: Agents and customer service staff should see only the customer data they need. This limits the value of any insider who is bribed and reduces exposure under the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843).\n- **Reporting**: Victims and organisations can report cyber incidents to national channels, including the Cyber Security Authority, and to the police. Organisations should publish internal reporting routes that staff can use without fear of blame."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### The July 2020 Twitter compromise\n\nIn July 2020, the Twitter accounts of many prominent individuals and companies posted messages promoting a cryptocurrency scam. Investigations, including a report by the New York State Department of Financial Services, found that attackers had used **phone-based spear phishing (vishing)**: they called Twitter employees while posing as internal IT staff and persuaded some to enter their credentials on a fake internal login page. With these credentials the attackers reached internal account-management tools and took over high-profile accounts. Several individuals were later charged.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n- **Initial access** was purely social: authority and pretexting (claiming to be IT support during a period of remote working).\n- **Insider dimension**: the employees were *compromised insiders*; their legitimate access to powerful tools was the real prize.\n- **Least privilege**: many staff had access to tools capable of altering any account, which magnified the impact.\n- **Lessons**: phishing-resistant MFA tied to genuine domains, tighter limits on who can use high-impact administrative tools, additional approval for sensitive actions on high-profile accounts and verified channels for IT support contacts.\n\nThe case demonstrates that a technologically sophisticated company can be compromised through people and process, and that limiting the blast radius of any single account is as important as preventing the initial deception."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Attacker research (social media, leaks, insiders)\"] --> B[\"Pretext and lure (authority, urgency)\"]\n    B --> C{Channel}\n    C --> C1[Email]\n    C --> C2[SMS]\n    C --> C3[Voice call]\n    C1 --> D[\"Victim action (click, disclose code, pay)\"]\n    C2 --> D\n    C3 --> D\n    D --> E[\"Outcome (account takeover, fraudulent payment, malware)\"]\n    F[\"Controls: filtering, awareness, call-back verification, phishing-resistant MFA, least privilege, monitoring\"] -.-> C\n    F -.-> D\n    F -.-> E\n```"}],"misconceptions":["\"Phishing only happens by email.\" SMS, voice calls, messaging apps, social media and even QR codes are widely used channels.","\"Clever people do not fall for phishing.\" Well-crafted, targeted lures succeed against experienced professionals, especially under time pressure; controls must not depend on perfect human vigilance.","\"Any multi-factor authentication stops phishing.\" SMS and app codes can be relayed in real time or captured via SIM swap; phishing-resistant methods bound to the genuine domain are far stronger.","\"Insider threat means malicious employees.\" Most insider incidents involve negligence or compromised accounts rather than deliberate malice.","\"Punishing staff who click improves security.\" Blame discourages reporting; rapid, safe reporting is what limits damage."],"examMistakes":["Describing phishing in general terms without naming the psychological principle exploited in the scenario.","Recommending only \"training\" and omitting process controls such as call-back verification and dual approval.","Treating SIM swap purely as a technical failure and ignoring the insider and identity-verification dimensions.","Failing to distinguish malicious, negligent and compromised insiders when proposing controls.","Proposing intrusive monitoring without considering proportionality, privacy law and staff trust."],"criticalQuestions":["How should a bank balance customer convenience against stronger identity checks for SIM replacement and PIN resets?","Is it ethical to send simulated phishing emails to staff? Under what conditions would you approve such a programme?","To what extent should employers monitor employees'' email and file activity to detect insider threats, given data protection obligations?","The Twitter attackers relied on employees working remotely. What changes to IT support processes would reduce vishing risk in a hybrid workplace?","Why might a rising number of reported phishing messages indicate improving, rather than worsening, security?"],"exercises":["**Phishing anatomy.** Collect three suspicious messages you have personally received (email or SMS). Without clicking any links, annotate each with the sender, the lure, the psychological principle and the red flags. Expected output: three annotated screenshots or transcripts with a short analysis of each.","**Payment verification procedure.** Draft a one-page procedure for an SME that requires call-back verification before changing supplier payment details. Expected output: a numbered procedure specifying who verifies, which phone number is used and how the check is recorded.","**Insider control matrix.** For a fictional bank branch, list five roles and, for each, state the access needed, a malicious, negligent and compromised insider risk, and one control. Expected output: a role-by-risk table.","**Guided practice.** Explore the OWASP project pages on authentication and session risks and the CISA guidance on phishing-resistant MFA. Do not set up phishing pages or contact real organisations. Expected output: a 200-word note explaining why phishing-resistant MFA defeats credential relay attacks."],"takeaways":["Social engineering exploits authority, scarcity and urgency, liking, reciprocity, social proof and commitment, often amplified by context.","Phishing variants include spear phishing, whaling, smishing, vishing and business email compromise.","Process controls such as call-back verification and dual approval are often the most cost-effective defences.","Insider threats may be malicious, negligent or compromised; least privilege, monitoring and HR processes all matter.","Mobile money fraud and SIM swap in Ghana combine social engineering, process weakness and insider risk."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Cialdini, R. B. (2021). *Influence, new and expanded: The psychology of persuasion*. Harper Business.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Mitnick, K. D., & Simon, W. L. (2002). *The art of deception: Controlling the human element of security*. Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Anderson, R. (2020). *Security engineering: A guide to building dependable distributed systems* (3rd ed.). Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Stallings, W., & Brown, L. (2018). *Computer security: Principles and practice* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"CISA cybersecurity resources","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/","description":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency. Official guidance and awareness material on phishing, social engineering and phishing-resistant MFA.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Cisco Skills for All","url":"https://skillsforall.com/","description":"Cisco Networking Academy. Free cybersecurity courses that include modules on social engineering and protecting personal data.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Social engineering manipulates people through predictable persuasion principles across email, SMS and voice. Effective defence layers infrastructure filtering, awareness, process controls, phishing-resistant authentication and rapid response. Insider threats, whether malicious, negligent or compromised, require least privilege, monitoring and good HR processes.\n\n- Cialdini principles: authority, scarcity (urgency), liking, reciprocity, social proof, commitment.\n- Phishing family: spear, whaling, smishing, vishing, BEC.\n- Call-back verification and dual approval stop many payment frauds.\n- Phishing-resistant MFA counters real-time credential relay and SIM swap.\n- Insiders: malicious, negligent, compromised.\n- Twitter 2020: vishing of staff plus over-broad admin tool access."}'::jsonb, 2546, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('bf2bf203-69a7-5838-b29a-bc097f9c9b04', '90762921-08a6-5e20-875e-13608b1b4891', 'denial-of-service-and-credential-attacks', 'Denial of Service and Credential Attacks', 'Explain how denial-of-service and credential attacks work, calculate password search spaces, distinguish password spraying from credential stuffing and design layered defences for online services.', array['Denial of service', 'Credential attacks', 'Password spraying', 'Credential stuffing']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Two families of attack threaten almost every online service, from a university portal to a mobile banking app. The first, **denial of service**, attacks availability: it tries to make a service unusable for legitimate users by exhausting its bandwidth, processing capacity or application resources. The second, **credential attacks**, attacks authentication: it tries to obtain or guess valid usernames and passwords so that the attacker can simply log in as a legitimate user.\n\nThese attacks are often underestimated because they seem crude. Yet large botnets built from insecure internet-connected devices have disrupted major internet infrastructure, and a single reused password on a remote access account has been reported as the entry point for a ransomware attack that halted fuel supplies in the United States. This lesson explains how the attacks work at a conceptual level, quantifies password strength with worked calculations and develops a defensive design that a Ghanaian bank or e-commerce firm could apply.","objectives":["Classify denial-of-service attacks as volumetric, protocol or application-layer and explain the role of botnets and reflection.","Distinguish brute force, dictionary, credential stuffing and password spraying attacks by their patterns.","Apply search-space calculations to compare password policies.","Analyse authentication logs to recognise signs of spraying and stuffing.","Evaluate defensive designs combining rate limiting, MFA, breached-password screening and DDoS mitigation services."],"prerequisites":["The CIA triad, particularly availability and authenticity.","Basic familiarity with how web logins and IP addresses work."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Denial of service (DoS)","definition":"An attack that prevents legitimate users from accessing a service by exhausting or disrupting its resources."},{"term":"Distributed denial of service (DDoS)","definition":"A DoS attack launched from many sources at once, typically a botnet."},{"term":"Botnet","definition":"A network of compromised devices remotely controlled by an attacker."},{"term":"Reflection and amplification","definition":"Techniques in which attackers send small requests with a spoofed source address to third-party servers, which send much larger responses to the victim."},{"term":"Brute force attack","definition":"Systematically trying all possible combinations of characters until the correct password is found."},{"term":"Dictionary attack","definition":"Trying likely passwords from lists of common words, leaked passwords and predictable patterns."},{"term":"Credential stuffing","definition":"Automatically testing username and password pairs leaked from one service against other services, exploiting password reuse."},{"term":"Password spraying","definition":"Trying a small number of common passwords against many accounts to avoid triggering per-account lockouts."},{"term":"Rate limiting","definition":"Restricting the number of requests a client can make in a given time window."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Denial of service\n\nA service becomes unavailable when some resource it depends on is exhausted. Attacks are commonly grouped into three categories:\n\n| Category | Resource targeted | Conceptual example | Main defences |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Volumetric | Network bandwidth | Flooding a link with more traffic than it can carry, often via reflection and amplification | Upstream scrubbing by ISP or DDoS mitigation provider; content delivery networks |\n| Protocol (state exhaustion) | Connection tables in servers, firewalls and load balancers | SYN floods that leave many half-open TCP connections | SYN cookies, connection limits, hardened network devices |\n| Application layer | Server CPU, memory, database | Repeated expensive requests such as searches or login attempts | Rate limiting, caching, web application firewalls, CAPTCHA or challenge pages |\n\n**Botnets** make DDoS cheap. Devices such as poorly secured cameras and routers, often still using default passwords, can be compromised in bulk and directed to send traffic. **Reflection** relies on internet services that respond to unauthenticated requests over UDP; because UDP does not verify the source address, an attacker can forge the victim''s address so that responses flood the victim. Where the response is much larger than the request, the attack is **amplified**.\n\nNot all denial of service is malicious. A legitimate surge of users on a salary day or during a promotion can overwhelm a system in the same way. Capacity planning and graceful degradation therefore protect availability against both attacks and success.\n\n### Credential attacks\n\nCredential attacks target the weakest part of most authentication systems: human-chosen, reused passwords.\n\n- **Online guessing** sends attempts to the live login service. It is slow and detectable, so attackers use spraying or stuffing to stay under thresholds.\n- **Offline cracking** happens after an attacker steals a database of password hashes. The attacker can then make guesses at the speed of their own hardware, without lockouts. How the passwords were hashed (covered in Module 4) then determines how long they survive.\n- **Credential stuffing** exploits reuse. Billions of leaked credentials circulate, and automated tools test them against banks, retailers and email providers. Success rates per attempt are low, but volume makes the attack profitable.\n- **Password spraying** exploits predictable choices. The attacker tries a handful of passwords such as a season and year or the organisation''s name followed by \"123\" against every account, perhaps one attempt per account per hour, never triggering lockout.\n- **Credential theft** by phishing, keylogging infostealers or interception (covered in the previous lesson) bypasses guessing entirely.\n- Inside a compromised Windows network, attackers may also reuse captured password hashes or authentication tickets directly (\"pass-the-hash\"), which is why privileged credentials must not be exposed on ordinary workstations.\n\n### The lockout dilemma\n\nAccount lockout after a few failures stops naive brute force against one account, but it creates a new denial-of-service risk: an attacker can lock out every customer deliberately. It also does little against spraying, which uses one or two attempts per account. Modern guidance, including NIST SP 800-63B, therefore favours **rate limiting and throttling** combined with MFA and screening of new passwords against lists of breached and commonly used values, rather than relying on aggressive lockout or forced frequent password changes."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Password search space\n\nThe number of possible passwords of length `L` drawn from an alphabet of `N` characters is `N^L`. On average, a brute-force attacker finds the password after searching half the space. This model applies only to truly random passwords; human-chosen passwords are far weaker than their length suggests because they follow patterns that dictionary attacks exploit.\n\n### Layered authentication defence\n\nA defensible login design combines:\n\n1. **Prevention of guessing**: per-account and per-source rate limits, progressive delays, bot detection.\n2. **Reduction of guessable passwords**: minimum length (NIST SP 800-63B recommends allowing long passphrases and requiring a reasonable minimum), screening against breached-password lists, no arbitrary composition rules that push users towards predictable patterns.\n3. **Making passwords insufficient**: multi-factor authentication, ideally phishing-resistant.\n4. **Detection**: monitoring for many failures across many accounts, logins from unusual locations and successful logins that follow bursts of failures.\n5. **Response**: forcing password resets for accounts found in breach data, notifying users of new device logins."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Part A: comparing password policies\n\nAssume, purely for illustration, an offline attacker who can test 10 billion (10^10) guesses per second against a fast, unsalted hash. (Real speeds vary greatly with hardware and hashing algorithm.)\n\n| Policy | Alphabet N | Length L | Search space N^L | Worst-case time at 10^10/s |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 8 lowercase letters | 26 | 8 | about 2.1 × 10^11 | about 21 seconds |\n| 8 characters, all printable ASCII | 95 | 8 | about 6.6 × 10^15 | about 7.7 days |\n| 12 lowercase letters | 26 | 12 | about 9.5 × 10^16 | about 110 days |\n| 16 lowercase letters | 26 | 16 | about 4.4 × 10^22 | about 140,000 years |\n\n**Calculation check for row 2:** 95^8 ≈ 6.63 × 10^15. Dividing by 10^10 gives 6.63 × 10^5 seconds; dividing by 86,400 seconds per day gives about 7.7 days.\n\n**Interpretation.** Length increases the search space faster than character variety: a 12-character lowercase random password is stronger than an 8-character complex one. However, these figures assume randomness. \"Password@2024\" technically uses all four character types but would fall quickly to a dictionary attack. Two lessons follow: encourage long passphrases, and use slow, salted password hashing (bcrypt or Argon2) so that each guess costs the attacker far more.\n\n### Part B: reading the logs\n\n**Illustrative scenario.** The security analyst at *Sankofa Online*, a fictional Accra e-commerce firm, reviews one hour of login logs.\n\n| Pattern | Observation | Likely attack |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | 1,200 failed logins for 1,200 different usernames from 15 IP addresses, all using the same password; three successes | Password spraying |\n| B | 40,000 attempts for 40,000 different username-password pairs from thousands of IP addresses, many usernames not registered on the site; 0.5% succeed | Credential stuffing with leaked pairs from another service |\n| C | 3,000 failed attempts against one administrator account from one IP address | Online brute force or dictionary attack |\n\n**Recommended responses:** For A, block or challenge the source addresses, force resets for the three compromised accounts and add the sprayed password to the blocked list. For B, enable bot detection, require MFA or step-up verification for logins from new devices, and screen all customer passwords against breach data. For C, confirm rate limiting is working, restrict administrator login to the corporate VPN and require phishing-resistant MFA for privileged accounts."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"- **Peak-period availability.** Ghanaian banks, telecom operators and government portals experience predictable surges, such as at month-end salary payments, examination result releases or tax deadlines. Capacity planning, content delivery networks and DDoS mitigation services from upstream providers protect against both attacks and legitimate surges. Many local organisations depend on a small number of international connectivity routes and data centres, so availability planning should consider upstream dependencies too.\n- **PIN-based mobile money.** Four- or five-digit PINs have tiny search spaces (10,000 or 100,000 combinations), so their security depends entirely on strict attempt limits and on binding the wallet to the SIM and device. This makes the integrity of SIM replacement processes critical, since a SIM swap can let an attacker reset a PIN.\n- **Password reuse among staff.** Staff often reuse the same password across personal email, social media and work systems. When a public service is breached, credential stuffing against corporate webmail or VPN follows. Ghanaian SMEs can reduce this risk cheaply by enabling MFA on email and remote access, which many cloud email platforms include at no extra cost.\n- **IoT devices.** Low-cost CCTV systems and routers installed in shops and offices are often left with default credentials and can be recruited into botnets. Changing default passwords and keeping firmware updated are basic responsibilities of installers and owners."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Mirai and the 2016 Dyn attack; Colonial Pipeline 2021\n\n**Mirai (2016).** The Mirai malware infected internet-connected devices such as cameras and home routers by trying a short list of factory default usernames and passwords over remote administration services. Infected devices formed a large botnet. In October 2016 a Mirai-based DDoS attack against Dyn, a major provider of Domain Name System (DNS) services, caused widespread disruption in reaching popular websites for users in parts of North America and Europe, because those sites depended on Dyn to resolve their names. The case links credential weakness (default passwords) to availability failure at internet scale, and demonstrates **dependency risk**: the target websites were not attacked directly, but their DNS provider was.\n\n**Colonial Pipeline (2021).** In May 2021, Colonial Pipeline, which operates a major fuel pipeline in the United States, was hit by ransomware attributed to the DarkSide group and shut down pipeline operations for several days as a precaution. In testimony to the US Congress, the company''s chief executive stated that the attackers had entered through a legacy VPN account that was no longer in use but still active, protected only by a password and without multi-factor authentication. The password was reported to have been found among leaked credentials.\n\n**Lessons:**\n\n- Default and reused credentials are an entry point for both botnet recruitment and targeted intrusions.\n- Dormant accounts must be removed; access reviews are a basic control.\n- MFA on all remote access is a minimum standard.\n- Critical services should avoid single points of failure in external dependencies such as DNS."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    U[\"Login request\"] --> RL{\"Rate limit and bot check passed?\"}\n    RL -- No --> BLK[\"Throttle, challenge or block\"]\n    RL -- Yes --> PW{\"Password correct?\"}\n    PW -- No --> LOG[\"Log failure; feed spraying and stuffing detection\"]\n    PW -- Yes --> BR{\"Password in breach list or new device?\"}\n    BR -- Yes --> MFA[\"Require MFA or step-up verification\"]\n    BR -- No --> MFA2[\"Standard MFA check\"]\n    MFA --> OK[\"Access granted and logged\"]\n    MFA2 --> OK\n    LOG --> SOC[\"Security monitoring alerts on patterns across accounts\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["\"Account lockout solves password attacks.\" Lockout does little against spraying and can itself be abused to deny service; rate limiting, MFA and breached-password screening are more effective.","\"Complex character rules make passwords strong.\" Composition rules often produce predictable patterns; length and randomness matter more.","\"A DDoS attack means we were hacked.\" A DDoS disrupts availability but does not necessarily involve any intrusion; however, it is sometimes used as a distraction during other attacks.","\"Credential stuffing is the victim site''s fault alone.\" It exploits passwords leaked elsewhere; the defending site must still detect and block it, and users must stop reusing passwords.","\"Forcing password changes every 30 days improves security.\" Frequent forced changes encourage predictable increments; NIST SP 800-63B advises changing passwords when there is evidence of compromise."],"examMistakes":["Confusing password spraying (one password, many accounts) with credential stuffing (many leaked pairs, many accounts).","Calculating search space as `L^N` instead of `N^L`.","Treating search-space calculations as valid for human-chosen passwords.","Recommending only firewalls against volumetric DDoS, when bandwidth must be protected upstream.","Ignoring the business impact of lockout policies on legitimate customers."],"criticalQuestions":["Should manufacturers of internet-connected devices be legally required to eliminate default passwords? Consider the Ghanaian market for low-cost imported devices.","How would you design login protection for a mobile money service whose customers mainly use basic phones and USSD rather than smartphone apps?","When an organisation detects credential stuffing, what responsibilities does it have towards customers whose accounts were accessed with correct passwords?","Is it reasonable for a small business to rely on its cloud or hosting provider for DDoS protection? What should it verify in the contract?","How should the trade-off between security and customer friction differ between a bank and a news website?"],"exercises":["**Search-space calculations.** Calculate `N^L` and worst-case time at an assumed 10^9 guesses per second for: a 6-digit PIN, a 10-character lowercase password and a passphrase of four words chosen randomly from a 7,776-word list (`7776^4`). Expected output: 10^6 combinations (about 0.001 s), about 1.4 × 10^14 (about 39 hours) and about 3.7 × 10^15 (about 42 days), with a sentence on why randomness matters.","**Check your own exposure.** Using a reputable breach-notification service, check whether your own email address appears in known breaches, and enable MFA on any account where it does. Check only addresses you own. Expected output: a short record of results and the accounts on which MFA is now enabled.","**Log analysis design.** Write three detection rules in plain English for spotting spraying, stuffing and single-account brute force in a login log. Expected output: three rules, each stating the fields used, the threshold and the response.","**Legal lab practice.** Complete the authentication labs in the PortSwigger Web Security Academy, which provides deliberately vulnerable practice applications intended for learning. Never test login pages of real organisations. Expected output: a summary of two lab findings and the defensive control that would have prevented each."],"takeaways":["Denial of service targets availability through volumetric, protocol or application-layer exhaustion; botnets and reflection amplify attacks.","Credential attacks include brute force, dictionary attacks, credential stuffing and password spraying, each with a recognisable log pattern.","Search space `N^L` shows length matters more than complexity, but only for random passwords.","Rate limiting, MFA, breached-password screening and monitoring outperform aggressive lockout and forced periodic changes.","Mirai and Colonial Pipeline show how default, reused and dormant credentials enable large-scale harm."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"National Institute of Standards and Technology. (2017). *Digital identity guidelines: Authentication and lifecycle management* (NIST Special Publication 800-63B).","url":"https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-63b","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Stallings, W., & Brown, L. (2018). *Computer security: Principles and practice* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Anderson, R. (2020). *Security engineering: A guide to building dependable distributed systems* (3rd ed.). Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"PortSwigger Web Security Academy","url":"https://portswigger.net/web-security","description":"PortSwigger. Free learning materials and legal practice labs, including authentication vulnerabilities and defences.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"OWASP","url":"https://owasp.org/","description":"OWASP Foundation. Official site of the open community project, with guidance on authentication, credential stuffing and automated threats to web applications.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"CISA cybersecurity resources","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/","description":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency. Official guidance on DDoS preparedness and multi-factor authentication.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Denial-of-service attacks exhaust bandwidth, connection state or application resources, usually via botnets; credential attacks guess, reuse or steal passwords. Strong defence combines upstream DDoS mitigation and capacity planning with rate limiting, long passwords screened against breach lists, MFA and log-based detection.\n\n- DoS types: volumetric, protocol, application layer.\n- Spraying = one password, many accounts; stuffing = leaked pairs, many accounts.\n- Search space = `N^L`; length beats complexity for random passwords.\n- Lockout can itself cause denial of service; prefer throttling and MFA.\n- Mirai 2016: default passwords built a botnet that hit DNS provider Dyn.\n- Colonial 2021: dormant VPN account, password only, no MFA."}'::jsonb, 2737, 3, true)
