insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('e68ea9c7-5b4b-58fd-a867-90238ad3effe', '00f5f0a3-7849-538e-9c2c-655e966f737f', 'symmetric-and-asymmetric-encryption', 'Symmetric and Asymmetric Encryption', 'Compare symmetric and asymmetric encryption, work through toy RSA and Diffie-Hellman examples by hand, explain block cipher modes and hybrid encryption, and select appropriate algorithms for business systems.', array['Encryption', 'Symmetric encryption', 'Asymmetric encryption']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Encryption is the technical heart of data protection. It lets a bank send balances to a customer''s phone across networks it does not control, lets a hospital store patient records so that a stolen disk reveals nothing, and lets two strangers on opposite sides of the world agree a secret key without ever meeting. Yet encryption is also widely misunderstood: managers assume that \"encrypted\" means \"secure\", while developers sometimes invent their own schemes or misuse sound algorithms.\n\nThis lesson explains the two great families of encryption. **Symmetric** encryption uses one shared secret key and is fast enough to protect bulk data. **Asymmetric** (public-key) encryption uses mathematically linked key pairs and solves the problem of distributing keys among people who have never met. You will work through small-number examples of Diffie-Hellman key agreement and RSA encryption by hand, so that the mathematics is concrete rather than mysterious, and then see how real systems combine both families in hybrid designs such as TLS and end-to-end encrypted messaging.","objectives":["Explain the goals of encryption and Kerckhoffs''s principle.","Compare symmetric and asymmetric encryption in terms of keys, speed, key distribution and typical uses.","Apply the RSA and Diffie-Hellman algorithms to small-number examples and verify the results.","Explain why block cipher modes matter, contrasting ECB, CBC and GCM.","Evaluate algorithm and key-length choices for a business system, including hybrid encryption and the transition to post-quantum cryptography."],"prerequisites":["The CIA triad and the secure protocols lesson, especially the TLS handshake.","Comfort with basic arithmetic, including remainders (modular arithmetic is explained here)."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Plaintext and ciphertext","definition":"Plaintext is the readable message; ciphertext is its encrypted, unreadable form."},{"term":"Key","definition":"A secret (or, in asymmetric cryptography, a public or private) value that controls the transformation between plaintext and ciphertext."},{"term":"Symmetric encryption","definition":"Encryption in which the same secret key is used to encrypt and decrypt."},{"term":"Asymmetric encryption","definition":"Encryption using a key pair, where data encrypted with the public key can be decrypted only with the matching private key."},{"term":"Block cipher","definition":"A symmetric algorithm that encrypts fixed-size blocks of data, such as AES with 128-bit blocks."},{"term":"Mode of operation","definition":"The method by which a block cipher is applied to messages longer than one block, such as CBC or GCM."},{"term":"Authenticated encryption","definition":"Encryption that also provides integrity and authenticity of the ciphertext, such as AES-GCM."},{"term":"Modular arithmetic","definition":"Arithmetic on remainders; `a mod n` is the remainder when `a` is divided by `n`."},{"term":"Hybrid encryption","definition":"Using asymmetric cryptography to establish or protect a symmetric key, then symmetric encryption for the data."},{"term":"Kerckhoffs''s principle","definition":"A cryptosystem should remain secure even if everything about it except the key is public."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Goals and principles\n\nEncryption primarily provides **confidentiality**. Combined with other mechanisms, modern schemes also provide integrity and authentication. Kerckhoffs''s principle, articulated in the nineteenth century, holds that security must rest on the secrecy of the key, not of the algorithm. This is why organisations should use published, extensively analysed standards such as AES rather than proprietary \"secret\" algorithms. The principle echoes Saltzer and Schroeder''s \"open design\".\n\nThe strength of a well-designed cipher against brute force depends on key length. A 128-bit key has `2^128` possible values, far beyond any foreseeable exhaustive search. In practice, encryption is almost never broken by brute-forcing a modern key; it fails through poor key management, misuse of modes, weak passwords used to derive keys, or implementation flaws.\n\n### Symmetric encryption\n\nIn symmetric encryption the sender and receiver share one secret key. Algorithms are fast and suitable for bulk data: disk encryption, database fields, VPN tunnels and the data phase of TLS.\n\n- **AES (Advanced Encryption Standard)**, standardised by NIST in 2001 after an open international competition, is the dominant block cipher. It uses 128-bit blocks and 128-, 192- or 256-bit keys.\n- **ChaCha20** is a modern stream cipher, often paired with the Poly1305 authenticator, that performs well on devices without AES hardware acceleration, such as some lower-cost phones.\n- Legacy algorithms such as DES (56-bit key, now trivially brute-forced), 3DES and RC4 should not be used for new systems.\n\nThe central weakness of symmetric encryption is **key distribution**: how do the parties obtain the shared key securely? With `n` parties who each need a private channel with every other, `n(n − 1)/2` keys are required; 100 staff would need 4,950 keys.\n\n### Block cipher modes\n\nA block cipher encrypts one block; a mode defines how to handle longer messages.\n\n| Mode | How it works | Security notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| ECB (Electronic Codebook) | Each block encrypted independently with the same key | Identical plaintext blocks give identical ciphertext blocks, leaking patterns; should not be used for data |\n| CBC (Cipher Block Chaining) | Each plaintext block is XORed with the previous ciphertext block before encryption; needs a random initialisation vector (IV) | Hides patterns; does not provide integrity on its own; vulnerable to padding oracle attacks when misused |\n| CTR (Counter) | Encrypts a counter to create a keystream that is XORed with the plaintext | Fast and parallel; nonce must never repeat under the same key; no integrity alone |\n| GCM (Galois/Counter Mode) | CTR encryption plus an authentication tag | Authenticated encryption; recommended default; nonce reuse is catastrophic |\n\nThe practical lesson is to prefer **authenticated encryption** (AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305) from well-reviewed libraries rather than assembling primitives yourself.\n\n### Asymmetric encryption\n\nIn 1976 Whitfield Diffie and Martin Hellman published \"New Directions in Cryptography\", which introduced public-key cryptography and a method for two parties to agree a shared secret over a public channel. In 1978 Ron Rivest, Adi Shamir and Leonard Adleman published the RSA algorithm, which supports both encryption and digital signatures.\n\nEach party has a **public key**, which can be shared freely, and a **private key**, which must be kept secret. Asymmetric schemes rely on mathematical problems that are easy in one direction and believed hard to reverse: RSA relies on the difficulty of factoring the product of two large primes; Diffie-Hellman and elliptic-curve cryptography (ECC) rely on the difficulty of discrete logarithms.\n\nAsymmetric operations are far slower than symmetric ones and are therefore used for **key establishment** and **digital signatures**, not bulk data. ECC achieves comparable security to RSA with much shorter keys, which is why modern TLS commonly uses elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman (such as X25519) and ECDSA or Ed25519 signatures.\n\n| Feature | Symmetric | Asymmetric |\n|---|---|---|\n| Keys | One shared secret key | Public and private key pair |\n| Speed | Fast | Much slower |\n| Key distribution | Hard: secret must be shared securely | Easier: public key can be published |\n| Typical uses | Bulk data, disk and database encryption, TLS data | Key exchange, digital signatures, certificates |\n| Examples | AES, ChaCha20 | RSA, Diffie-Hellman, ECDH, ECDSA |\n\n### Hybrid encryption\n\nReal systems combine both: an asymmetric step establishes a fresh symmetric key, which then encrypts the data. TLS 1.3 uses ephemeral ECDH to agree keys and AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305 for data. Encrypted email standards encrypt the message with a random symmetric key and then encrypt that key with the recipient''s public key.\n\n### Looking ahead: post-quantum cryptography\n\nA sufficiently large quantum computer running Shor''s algorithm would break RSA, Diffie-Hellman and ECC. Such machines do not exist at the required scale today, but data recorded now could be decrypted in future (\"harvest now, decrypt later\"). In August 2024 NIST published its first post-quantum cryptography standards, including the ML-KEM key-encapsulation mechanism (FIPS 203). Organisations with long-lived sensitive data should begin inventorying where they use public-key cryptography so they can migrate. Symmetric ciphers such as AES-256 are considered much less affected."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Part A: toy Diffie-Hellman key agreement\n\nThe parties publicly agree a prime `p = 23` and a generator `g = 5`.\n\n1. Ama chooses a private value `a = 6` and computes `A = g^a mod p = 5^6 mod 23`. Since `5^6 = 15625` and `15625 = 23 × 679 + 8`, `A = 8`. She sends 8 publicly.\n2. Kofi chooses a private value `b = 15` and computes `B = 5^15 mod 23 = 19`. He sends 19 publicly.\n3. Ama computes `B^a mod p = 19^6 mod 23 = 2`.\n4. Kofi computes `A^b mod p = 8^15 mod 23 = 2`.\n\nBoth obtain the **shared secret 2** without ever sending it. An eavesdropper sees `p`, `g`, 8 and 19 but must solve a discrete logarithm to find `a` or `b`. With real parameters (2048-bit primes or elliptic curves) this is infeasible. Note that basic Diffie-Hellman is **unauthenticated**: a man-in-the-middle could run separate exchanges with each party. This is why TLS adds the server''s signature (CertificateVerify).\n\n### Part B: toy RSA\n\n**Key generation.**\n\n1. Choose two primes: `p = 5`, `q = 11`.\n2. Compute the modulus `n = p × q = 55`.\n3. Compute `φ(n) = (p − 1)(q − 1) = 4 × 10 = 40`.\n4. Choose a public exponent `e` with no common factor with 40: `e = 3` (since gcd(3, 40) = 1).\n5. Find the private exponent `d` such that `e × d ≡ 1 (mod 40)`. Try `d = 27`: `3 × 27 = 81 = 2 × 40 + 1`. So `d = 27`.\n\nPublic key: `(e, n) = (3, 55)`. Private key: `(d, n) = (27, 55)`.\n\n**Encryption.** Encrypt the message `m = 7` (messages must be numbers smaller than `n`):\n\n`c = m^e mod n = 7^3 mod 55 = 343 mod 55`. Since `55 × 6 = 330`, `343 − 330 = 13`. So `c = 13`.\n\n**Decryption.** `m = c^d mod n = 13^27 mod 55`. Computing by repeated squaring (or a calculator that supports modular exponentiation) gives `7`, recovering the original message.\n\n**Signing (preview of the next lesson).** To sign `m = 9`, the private-key holder computes `s = 9^27 mod 55 = 4`. Anyone can verify with the public key: `4^3 mod 55 = 64 mod 55 = 9`, which matches the message.\n\n**Why the toy is insecure and what real RSA adds.** With `n = 55`, anyone can factor `n` into 5 × 11 and compute `d`. Real RSA uses moduli of at least 2048 bits, generated from large random primes. \"Textbook\" RSA as shown is also deterministic and malleable; real systems use padding schemes such as OAEP for encryption and PSS for signatures, implemented in vetted libraries."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Nsoromma Pay*, a fictional Accra fintech, offers a mobile wallet linked to customers'' bank accounts. Its encryption design illustrates sound choices:\n\n| Data or channel | Approach | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| App-to-server traffic | TLS 1.3 (ECDHE with AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305) | Hybrid encryption with forward secrecy; ChaCha20 performs well on low-cost Android phones |\n| Customer national ID numbers in the database | Field-level AES-256-GCM, keys held in a key management service or hardware security module, not on the database server | Limits exposure if the database is copied; supports Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) obligations |\n| Database backups | Encrypted with a separate key; key escrow arranged so backups remain recoverable | Protects confidentiality without risking availability |\n| Staff laptops | Full-disk encryption | Protects data if a laptop is stolen in transit or at home |\n| Partner bank API | Mutual TLS, with each side holding a certificate | Both parties authenticate, not only the server |\n\nThe company''s security lead rejects a proposal to use a developer''s \"custom XOR scheme\" for API payloads, citing Kerckhoffs''s principle and the availability of standard authenticated encryption. She also records in the risk register that encryption of personal data reduces impact if data is stolen but does not remove the obligation to protect the keys: if the keys sit on the same server as the data, encryption adds little."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### WhatsApp end-to-end encryption (2016)\n\nIn April 2016 WhatsApp announced that end-to-end encryption had been enabled by default for all messages, calls and attachments, using the Signal Protocol developed by Open Whisper Systems. WhatsApp published a technical white paper describing the design. In outline:\n\n- Each device generates long-term identity key pairs and additional pre-keys, whose public parts are registered with the server.\n- To start a conversation, the sender uses the recipient''s public keys in an elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman-based key agreement to derive a shared secret, even if the recipient is offline.\n- Messages are encrypted with symmetric keys that are continually updated (a \"ratchet\"), providing forward secrecy and limiting the damage if a key is compromised.\n- Users can compare \"security codes\" to verify that they hold each other''s genuine identity keys, defending against a man-in-the-middle.\n\n**Lessons:**\n\n- It is a clear example of **hybrid encryption** at enormous scale: asymmetric key agreement establishes symmetric keys for fast message encryption.\n- **Key verification** remains a human problem: most users never compare security codes, so trust in the service''s key distribution is still involved.\n- End-to-end encryption protects message content from the service provider but not metadata such as who communicates with whom and when, and it does not protect messages on a compromised or unlocked phone.\n- The deployment intensified policy debates, in many countries, about lawful access and encryption, which remain unresolved."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph SYM[\"Symmetric\"]\n      P1[Plaintext] -->|\"encrypt with shared key K\"| C1[Ciphertext]\n      C1 -->|\"decrypt with same key K\"| P2[Plaintext]\n    end\n    subgraph ASYM[\"Asymmetric\"]\n      P3[Plaintext] -->|\"encrypt with recipient public key\"| C2[Ciphertext]\n      C2 -->|\"decrypt with recipient private key\"| P4[Plaintext]\n    end\n    subgraph HYB[\"Hybrid (TLS, messaging)\"]\n      KX[\"Asymmetric key exchange (ECDH)\"] --> SK[\"Fresh symmetric session key\"]\n      SK --> DATA[\"Bulk data with AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305\"]\n    end\n```"}],"misconceptions":["\"Encrypted data is secure data.\" Encryption is only as strong as key management; keys stored beside the data or derived from weak passwords defeat it.","\"Asymmetric encryption is stronger than symmetric encryption.\" They serve different purposes; a 128-bit AES key and a 3072-bit RSA key are often treated as offering broadly comparable strength.","\"Secret algorithms are safer.\" Kerckhoffs''s principle and experience show that public, peer-reviewed algorithms are far more trustworthy than proprietary ones.","\"Any mode of AES is fine.\" ECB leaks patterns and unauthenticated modes allow tampering; authenticated modes such as GCM are recommended, with unique nonces.","\"Quantum computers have already broken RSA.\" No quantum computer of the required scale exists today, but long-lived data justifies planning migration now."],"examMistakes":["Computing `φ(n)` as `p × q` rather than `(p − 1)(q − 1)`.","Forgetting that the RSA message must be smaller than `n`.","Claiming RSA is used to encrypt bulk data in TLS 1.3; it is used, if at all, for signatures.","Describing Diffie-Hellman as authenticated on its own.","Recommending \"AES\" without specifying a mode, key management and integrity protection."],"criticalQuestions":["If an organisation encrypts its database but stores the key in a configuration file on the same server, what risks are actually reduced?","Should governments be able to require access to end-to-end encrypted communications? Discuss from security, human rights and law enforcement perspectives.","Why might a Ghanaian bank prefer ChaCha20-Poly1305 for mobile clients while using AES-GCM in its data centre?","How should an organisation decide which systems to prioritise in a post-quantum migration?","Is it ever acceptable for developers to implement cryptographic algorithms themselves rather than using libraries?"],"exercises":["**Toy RSA by hand.** Using `p = 3`, `q = 11` and `e = 7`, compute `n`, `φ(n)` and `d`, then encrypt `m = 4` and decrypt the result. Expected output: `n = 33`, `φ(n) = 20`, `d = 3` (since 7 × 3 = 21 ≡ 1 mod 20), `c = 4^7 mod 33 = 16` and decryption `16^3 mod 33 = 4`.","**Toy Diffie-Hellman.** With `p = 23`, `g = 5`, private values `a = 4` and `b = 3`, compute the public values and the shared secret. Expected output: `A = 4`, `B = 10`, shared secret `18` computed by both sides.","**Encrypt a file you own.** Use a reputable tool such as the encrypted-archive feature of an established archive manager or your operating system''s disk encryption to encrypt a test file or folder on your own device. Record the algorithm used and how the key or password is managed. Expected output: a short note and a successful decryption test.","**Guided labs.** Work through an introductory cryptography module on Cisco Skills for All. Expected output: a 150-word reflection comparing the course''s explanation of symmetric and asymmetric encryption with this lesson."],"takeaways":["Symmetric encryption is fast and suits bulk data but needs secure key distribution; asymmetric encryption solves distribution and enables signatures but is slow.","Kerckhoffs''s principle: security must depend only on the key; use public, vetted algorithms and libraries.","Use authenticated encryption (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305); avoid ECB and legacy ciphers.","RSA rests on factoring; Diffie-Hellman and ECC on discrete logarithms; toy examples show the mechanics but real keys must be large and padded.","Real systems use hybrid encryption, and long-lived data now requires planning for post-quantum migration."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Diffie, W., & Hellman, M. E. (1976). New directions in cryptography. *IEEE Transactions on Information Theory, 22*(6), 644-654.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Rivest, R. L., Shamir, A., & Adleman, L. (1978). A method for obtaining digital signatures and public-key cryptosystems. *Communications of the ACM, 21*(2), 120-126.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Katz, J., & Lindell, Y. (2020). *Introduction to modern cryptography* (3rd ed.). CRC Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Ferguson, N., Schneier, B., & Kohno, T. (2010). *Cryptography engineering: Design principles and practical applications*. Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Stallings, W., & Brown, L. (2018). *Computer security: Principles and practice* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"NIST Computer Security Resource Center","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/","description":"National Institute of Standards and Technology. Official home of cryptographic standards, including AES and the post-quantum cryptography project.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Cisco Skills for All","url":"https://skillsforall.com/","description":"Cisco Networking Academy. Free cybersecurity courses with introductory modules on cryptography.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Symmetric encryption uses one shared key and is fast; asymmetric encryption uses public and private keys and solves key distribution. Diffie-Hellman agrees secrets over public channels; RSA encrypts and signs using modular exponentiation. Real systems combine both in hybrid designs and should use authenticated encryption from vetted libraries.\n\n- AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305 for data; never ECB.\n- RSA: `n = pq`, `φ = (p−1)(q−1)`, `ed ≡ 1 mod φ`, `c = m^e mod n`.\n- Toy RSA: p = 5, q = 11, e = 3, d = 27; 7 encrypts to 13.\n- Toy DH: p = 23, g = 5, a = 6, b = 15, shared secret 2.\n- Diffie-Hellman alone is unauthenticated.\n- WhatsApp 2016: Signal Protocol, hybrid, forward secrecy."}'::jsonb, 2945, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('21312b82-0a4e-5788-bd56-36cb403743c0', '00f5f0a3-7849-538e-9c2c-655e966f737f', 'hashing-and-digital-signatures', 'Hashing and Digital Signatures', 'Explain the security properties of cryptographic hash functions, store passwords safely with salted, slow hashing such as bcrypt or Argon2, and trace how digital signatures provide integrity, authenticity and non-repudiation.', array['Hashing', 'Digital signatures', 'Password hashing']::text[], 50, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Encryption hides data, but many security problems are not about secrecy at all. A bank needs to know that a payment instruction has not been altered; a software vendor needs customers to be sure that an update really came from it; a website needs to check passwords without storing them in a form that thieves could reuse. Cryptographic hash functions and digital signatures solve these problems.\n\nA hash function turns any input into a short, fixed-length \"fingerprint\" that changes completely if a single character of the input changes. Digital signatures combine hashing with asymmetric cryptography so that anyone can verify who produced a document and that it has not been altered since. This lesson explains the properties that make hash functions secure, why ordinary fast hashes are dangerous for storing passwords, how bcrypt and Argon2 fix this, and how signatures provide non-repudiation. Real incidents, including the 2012 LinkedIn password leak and the Flame malware''s forged Microsoft signature, show what happens when these tools are misused.","objectives":["Explain the properties of cryptographic hash functions: preimage resistance, second-preimage resistance, collision resistance and the avalanche effect.","Distinguish hashing, encryption and message authentication codes, and choose the right one for a requirement.","Apply salted, slow password hashing (bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2) and explain why it defeats precomputed and large-scale cracking.","Describe step by step how a digital signature is created and verified, and what each step guarantees.","Evaluate the consequences of using deprecated algorithms such as MD5 and SHA-1."],"prerequisites":["Symmetric and asymmetric encryption, including the toy RSA example from the previous lesson.","Credential attacks and search-space calculations from Module 2."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Cryptographic hash function","definition":"A function that maps input of any length to a fixed-length output (digest) and is designed to be one-way and collision-resistant."},{"term":"Digest","definition":"The fixed-length output of a hash function, such as 256 bits for SHA-256."},{"term":"Preimage resistance","definition":"Given a digest, it is infeasible to find any input that produces it."},{"term":"Second-preimage resistance","definition":"Given an input, it is infeasible to find a different input with the same digest."},{"term":"Collision resistance","definition":"It is infeasible to find any two different inputs with the same digest."},{"term":"Salt","definition":"A unique random value combined with each password before hashing so that identical passwords produce different hashes."},{"term":"Key stretching","definition":"Deliberately making password hashing slow (and, for memory-hard functions, memory-intensive) to raise the cost of each guess."},{"term":"Message authentication code (MAC)","definition":"A short tag computed from a message and a shared secret key that verifies integrity and authenticity, for example HMAC."},{"term":"Digital signature","definition":"A value created with a private key over the hash of a message, which anyone with the public key can verify."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### What a hash function does\n\nA cryptographic hash function such as SHA-256 takes any input, from one character to a whole film, and produces a fixed-length digest. It is:\n\n- **Deterministic**: the same input always yields the same digest.\n- **Fast to compute** for ordinary inputs (a property that matters, as we will see, for passwords).\n- **Avalanche-sensitive**: a tiny change in input changes roughly half the output bits.\n\nFor example, the SHA-256 digests of two nearly identical payment instructions are completely different:\n\n| Input | SHA-256 digest (hexadecimal) |\n|---|---|\n| `Pay GHS 100 to Kofi` | `e1df7e8b044e3c9acfd3e7d100e3218d3901f9dcaabb98b0e6479b1c5e1c676f` |\n| `Pay GHS 900 to Kofi` | `a52be5dd2973c4edc7c3edf486398291f061d7f3e51764c90e6554179bb8d5f2` |\n\n### The three resistance properties\n\n1. **Preimage resistance** (one-wayness): from the digest you cannot work back to the input except by guessing.\n2. **Second-preimage resistance**: an attacker given a specific document cannot craft a different document with the same digest.\n3. **Collision resistance**: an attacker cannot find *any* two documents with the same digest. Because of the **birthday paradox**, finding a collision for an `n`-bit hash takes roughly `2^(n/2)` work rather than `2^n`. For SHA-256 this is about `2^128`, which remains infeasible; for a 128-bit hash such as MD5 it is about `2^64` in theory, and cryptanalysis has made MD5 collisions practical.\n\n**Algorithm status:** MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collision resistance and should not be used for signatures or certificates. In 2017 researchers from Google and CWI Amsterdam announced the first practical SHA-1 collision, two different PDF files with the same SHA-1 digest. SHA-256 and the rest of the SHA-2 family, and SHA-3, are current standards.\n\n### Hashing, encryption and MACs compared\n\n| Requirement | Tool | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Keep data secret and recover it later | Encryption | Reversible with the key |\n| Detect accidental or malicious change, where anyone may compute the check | Hash | No key needed; publish the digest via a trusted channel |\n| Detect tampering between two parties sharing a secret | MAC (such as HMAC-SHA256) or authenticated encryption | An attacker without the key cannot forge a valid tag |\n| Prove to third parties who produced a document | Digital signature | Only the private-key holder can sign; anyone can verify |\n| Verify a password without storing it | Slow, salted password hash | One-way; costly to guess at scale |\n\nA plain hash does not prove authenticity: an attacker who changes a file can simply recompute its hash. A MAC adds a secret key, but because both parties share it, either could have produced the tag, so a MAC cannot provide non-repudiation. Only a signature does.\n\n### Password storage\n\nA system that checks passwords should store neither the password nor anything that lets an attacker quickly recover it.\n\n- **Plaintext storage** is indefensible: a single database leak exposes every password.\n- **Encryption** is wrong in principle: whoever obtains the key can decrypt every password.\n- **Fast unsalted hashes** (MD5, SHA-1, even SHA-256) are dangerous. Identical passwords produce identical hashes, so one cracked hash reveals everyone using that password, and attackers can use **precomputed tables** (including rainbow tables) and hardware that computes billions of fast hashes per second.\n- **Salting** stores a unique random salt with each hash, computed as `hash(salt + password)`. Two users with the password `Accra2024!` now have different stored values, precomputed tables become useless and the attacker must crack each hash separately. Salts are not secret; their job is uniqueness.\n- **Slow, adaptive hashing** makes each guess expensive. **bcrypt** (Provos and Mazières, 1999) has a configurable cost factor; **scrypt** and **Argon2** are also **memory-hard**, which hinders attackers using graphics processors or custom hardware. Argon2 won the Password Hashing Competition in 2015 and is specified in RFC 9106. PBKDF2 with a high iteration count is an acceptable option where standards require it.\n- A **pepper**, a secret value held outside the database (for example in a hardware security module), can add protection if the database alone is stolen.\n\nThe cost factor should be tuned so that a single login takes a fraction of a second on the server; that is negligible for a user but multiplies an attacker''s work enormously across billions of guesses."},{"heading":"Step-by-Step Guide","markdown":"### Creating and verifying a digital signature\n\n**Signing (by the sender, Abena, the finance director):**\n\n1. Abena prepares the document, for example a payment authorisation.\n2. Her software computes the hash of the document: `h = SHA-256(document)`.\n3. The software signs `h` with her **private key**, using a signature algorithm such as RSA-PSS, ECDSA or Ed25519, producing the signature `s`.\n4. Abena sends the document, the signature and (usually) her certificate, which binds her identity to her public key.\n\n**Verifying (by the recipient, the bank):**\n\n5. The bank validates Abena''s certificate chain to a trusted certificate authority (covered in the next lesson).\n6. It computes its own hash of the received document: `h'' = SHA-256(received document)`.\n7. It uses Abena''s **public key** to verify `s` against `h''`.\n8. If verification succeeds, the bank knows that (a) the document has not changed since signing (**integrity**), (b) it was signed by the holder of Abena''s private key (**authenticity**) and (c) Abena cannot plausibly deny signing it, provided her private key was properly protected (**non-repudiation**).\n\n**Toy illustration with the RSA keys from the previous lesson** (`n = 55`, `e = 3`, `d = 27`): suppose the document hashes, in a toy hash, to the number 9. Signing: `s = 9^27 mod 55 = 4`. Verification: `4^3 mod 55 = 64 mod 55 = 9`, which equals the recomputed hash, so the signature is valid. If an attacker changed the document so that it hashed to 8, verification would yield 9 ≠ 8 and fail.\n\n**Why sign the hash rather than the document?** Hashing produces a short, fixed-length value, so signing is fast for documents of any size, and the hash''s collision resistance ensures that the signature effectively covers the entire document. This is also why a broken hash function undermines signatures: if an attacker can find two documents with the same hash, a signature on one is valid for the other."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Upgrading password storage at an online retailer\n\n**Illustrative scenario.** *Kumasi Kicks*, a fictional online footwear retailer, stores customer passwords as unsalted SHA-1 hashes. A security review recommends migration to Argon2id.\n\n**Step 1: assess the current exposure.** Unsalted SHA-1 means that any customers who share a password share a hash; an attacker who steals the table can crack common passwords almost instantly using precomputed tables and fast hardware.\n\n**Step 2: demonstrate the effect of salting.** Two customers both choose `Accra2024!`.\n\n| Customer | Salt | Stored value (first 16 hex characters of SHA-256(salt + password)) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Customer A | `a9f3` | `c5775757deb6a1b1` |\n| Customer B | `71c2` | `1a1b1cc2df9d848c` |\n\nThe stored values differ, so cracking one gives no information about the other. (In production, salts are much longer, typically 16 bytes, and the hash function is Argon2id, not SHA-256; SHA-256 is used here only to illustrate the principle.)\n\n**Step 3: plan migration without forcing every customer to reset.** Wrap existing hashes: compute `Argon2id(salt, SHA-1(password))` for every stored value now, so no weak hash remains in the database. When each customer next logs in, verify against the wrapped value, then replace it with a direct `Argon2id(salt, password)` hash.\n\n**Step 4: tune parameters.** Choose memory, iteration and parallelism parameters so that hashing takes a fraction of a second on the login server, and record them with each hash so they can be raised later.\n\n**Step 5: complementary controls.** Screen new passwords against breached-password lists, enable MFA and rate limit logins (Module 2). If the old database might already have been copied, prompt customers to change passwords and warn them about reuse."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"- **Electronic signatures and transactions.** Ghana''s Electronic Transactions Act, 2008 (Act 772) provides a legal framework for electronic records and signatures. Organisations adopting digital approval workflows should take legal advice on the evidential weight of different signature methods; cryptographic digital signatures with protected private keys provide far stronger evidence than a typed name or scanned image.\n- **Payment instructions.** Banks and fintechs can require that bulk payment files uploaded by corporate customers be digitally signed, so altered files are rejected automatically. This counters both insider tampering and business email compromise.\n- **Software and app integrity.** Ghanaian developers distributing Android apps sign their releases; customers should install apps only from official stores, which check signatures. Fake banking apps distributed through messaging platforms bypass this protection.\n- **Password storage under the Data Protection Act.** Storing customer passwords in plaintext or with fast unsalted hashes would be difficult to defend as an \"appropriate\" security measure under the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843).\n- **Document verification.** Universities, examination bodies and government agencies issuing certificates can publish hashes or signed records so that employers can verify authenticity, reducing certificate fraud."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### LinkedIn (2012) and the Flame malware (2012)\n\n**LinkedIn.** In June 2012 a large file of password hashes belonging to LinkedIn users was posted online. LinkedIn confirmed that some of its members'' passwords had been compromised. The hashes were **unsalted SHA-1**, so researchers and criminals cracked a large proportion within days. In 2016 it emerged that the 2012 breach was much larger than originally understood, with data on far more accounts offered for sale. Because many users reused passwords, the leak fuelled credential stuffing against other services for years. LinkedIn had already moved to salted hashing by the time of the later disclosures.\n\n**Flame.** In 2012 researchers analysed Flame, a sophisticated espionage malware found mainly in the Middle East. Microsoft reported that Flame''s authors had abused a weakness in a Microsoft certificate authority used for Terminal Server licensing, combined with an **MD5 collision attack**, to produce a fraudulent code-signing certificate that chained to a Microsoft root. This let the malware appear to be legitimately signed Microsoft software, enabling it to spread via a spoofed Windows Update mechanism. Microsoft issued an emergency security advisory, revoked the affected certificates and accelerated its removal of MD5 from certificate processes.\n\n**Lessons:**\n\n- Fast, unsalted hashes are unsuitable for passwords; slow, salted, memory-hard functions are the standard.\n- Deprecated hash functions are not merely theoretically weak: collision attacks against MD5 were used in a real attack to forge trust.\n- The security of a signature scheme depends on every component: the hash, the signature algorithm, the certificate authority''s processes and private key protection."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph SIGN[\"Signer (private key)\"]\n      D1[Document] --> H1[\"Hash: SHA-256\"]\n      H1 --> S1[\"Sign hash with private key\"]\n      S1 --> SIG[Signature]\n    end\n    D1 --> SEND[\"Send document, signature, certificate\"]\n    SIG --> SEND\n    subgraph VERIFY[\"Verifier (public key)\"]\n      SEND --> H2[\"Recompute hash of received document\"]\n      SEND --> CERT[\"Validate certificate, extract public key\"]\n      CERT --> V1[\"Verify signature against recomputed hash\"]\n      H2 --> V1\n      V1 --> R{Match?}\n      R -- Yes --> OK[\"Integrity, authenticity, non-repudiation\"]\n      R -- No --> BAD[\"Reject: altered or forged\"]\n    end\n```"}],"misconceptions":["\"Hashing is a form of encryption.\" Hashing is one-way and has no key or decryption step; encryption is reversible with a key.","\"SHA-256 is fine for storing passwords.\" It is a sound general-purpose hash but too fast for passwords; use bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2 with unique salts.","\"Salts must be kept secret.\" Salts are stored alongside hashes; their purpose is uniqueness, not secrecy. A pepper, by contrast, is kept secret elsewhere.","\"A MAC provides non-repudiation.\" Both parties hold the MAC key, so either could have created the tag; only digital signatures provide non-repudiation.","\"A digital signature encrypts the document.\" A signature provides integrity and authenticity; the document itself remains readable unless it is separately encrypted."],"examMistakes":["Confusing second-preimage resistance with collision resistance.","Stating that signatures are created with the public key and verified with the private key (it is the reverse).","Recommending encryption for password storage.","Omitting certificate validation when describing signature verification.","Describing MD5 or SHA-1 as acceptable for new signature or certificate use."],"criticalQuestions":["If a user''s private signing key is stored on a shared office PC without a passphrase, can the organisation still rely on non-repudiation? What would strengthen it?","Why might an organisation choose PBKDF2 rather than Argon2, and is that choice defensible?","How should a regulator respond if a licensed institution is found storing customer passwords with unsalted SHA-1?","Does publishing the SHA-256 digest of a software download on the same website as the download provide meaningful protection? When would it?","The Flame attack combined a cryptographic weakness with a process weakness at a certificate authority. Which was more important, and why?"],"exercises":["**Observe the avalanche effect.** Using a command-line tool on your own computer (for example `sha256sum` on Linux, `shasum -a 256` on macOS or `Get-FileHash` in Windows PowerShell), hash two text files that differ by one character. Expected output: two completely different 64-character digests and a sentence explaining the avalanche effect.","**Verify a download.** Download a file from a software publisher that provides an official SHA-256 checksum or signature, and verify it. Expected output: a record of the published and computed values and whether they match.","**Password hashing experiment.** In a local programming environment you control, use a well-known library to hash a test password with bcrypt or Argon2 at two different cost settings and time each. Use only test passwords. Expected output: the two timings and an explanation of how the cost factor affects an attacker.","**Toy signature.** With the RSA keys `n = 33`, `e = 7`, `d = 3` from the previous lesson''s exercise, sign the toy hash value 5 and verify it. Expected output: `s = 5^3 mod 33 = 26`; verification `26^7 mod 33 = 5`."],"takeaways":["Cryptographic hashes provide fixed-length fingerprints with preimage, second-preimage and collision resistance; MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collisions.","Hashes detect change, MACs add a shared secret for authenticity between two parties, and only digital signatures provide non-repudiation.","Passwords must be stored with unique salts and slow, adaptive, ideally memory-hard functions such as Argon2 or bcrypt.","Signing hashes the document, signs the hash with the private key, and verification recomputes the hash and checks it with the public key.","LinkedIn 2012 and Flame 2012 show the real-world cost of weak password hashing and deprecated hash functions."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Biryukov, A., Dinu, D., Khovratovich, D., & Josefsson, S. (2021). *Argon2 memory-hard function for password hashing and proof-of-work applications* (RFC 9106). Internet Engineering Task Force.","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9106","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Provos, N., & Mazières, D. (1999). A future-adaptable password scheme. In *Proceedings of the 1999 USENIX Annual Technical Conference*. USENIX Association.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Katz, J., & Lindell, Y. (2020). *Introduction to modern cryptography* (3rd ed.). CRC Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Ferguson, N., Schneier, B., & Kohno, T. (2010). *Cryptography engineering: Design principles and practical applications*. Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"OWASP","url":"https://owasp.org/","description":"OWASP Foundation. Official site of the open community, whose cheat sheet series includes guidance on password storage and cryptographic practice.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"NIST Computer Security Resource Center","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/","description":"National Institute of Standards and Technology. Official source for hash function and digital signature standards.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Hash functions create fixed-length, one-way fingerprints; their security depends on preimage, second-preimage and collision resistance. Passwords need unique salts and slow, memory-hard hashing. Digital signatures sign a hash with a private key so that anyone can verify integrity and authenticity with the public key, providing non-repudiation when the key is protected.\n\n- Collision work ≈ `2^(n/2)` (birthday bound).\n- MD5 and SHA-1 broken; use SHA-256, SHA-3.\n- Hash ≠ encryption; MAC ≠ signature.\n- Passwords: unique salt plus Argon2id, bcrypt or scrypt.\n- Sign with private key; verify with public key and certificate.\n- LinkedIn 2012 unsalted SHA-1; Flame 2012 MD5 collision forged signature."}'::jsonb, 2976, 2, true)
