insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('5d3b9fcc-0fce-5f6a-b739-dbfd4782ccd7', 'd4cc9a37-040d-5e5e-98a5-bd91c12b72d0', 'web-application-security-sqli-and-xss', 'Web Application Security, SQL Injection and Cross-Site Scripting', 'Explain the OWASP Top 10:2021 risk categories, analyse how SQL injection and cross-site scripting arise from mixing data with code, and apply parameterised queries, output encoding and Content Security Policy as layered defences.', array['Web application security', 'OWASP Top 10', 'SQL injection', 'Cross-site scripting']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Most organisations now meet their customers through a web browser or a mobile application that talks to web APIs. A Ghanaian microfinance company''s loan portal, a hospital''s appointment system and a retailer''s online shop all expose code to anyone on the internet. Unlike a firewall rule, which either allows or blocks traffic, a web application must accept untrusted input by design: names, search terms, file uploads and JSON payloads. Every one of those inputs is a potential route for an attacker to change what the application does.\n\nThis lesson introduces the OWASP Top 10 and then studies SQL injection (SQLi) and cross-site scripting (XSS) in depth. Both share one root cause: attacker-supplied *data* is interpreted as *code* by an interpreter (the database engine or the browser). Understand that, and the defences become a design principle rather than a list. Perform any demonstration only in legal training labs or on systems you own or are authorised to test.","objectives":["Explain the purpose and structure of the OWASP Top 10:2021 and identify each of its ten risk categories.","Analyse why SQL injection occurs and compare a vulnerable string-concatenated query with a parameterised query.","Distinguish stored, reflected and DOM-based cross-site scripting by data flow and sink.","Apply context-aware output encoding and a Content Security Policy as complementary XSS defences.","Evaluate the role of input validation, least privilege and web application firewalls as secondary, not primary, controls."],"prerequisites":["Basic understanding of how HTTP requests and responses work (methods, headers, parameters, cookies).","Introductory SQL (`SELECT`, `WHERE`, `INSERT`) and an awareness of HTML and JavaScript.","The CIA triad and the threat, vulnerability and risk vocabulary from Module 1."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"OWASP Top 10","definition":"An awareness document published by the Open Worldwide Application Security Project that ranks broad categories of web application security risk, based on contributed data and community survey."},{"term":"Injection","definition":"A class of flaw in which untrusted data is sent to an interpreter as part of a command or query, so that the interpreter executes attacker-controlled instructions."},{"term":"SQL injection (SQLi)","definition":"Injection into a database query, allowing an attacker to read, modify or delete data, or bypass authentication logic."},{"term":"Parameterised query (prepared statement)","definition":"A query in which the SQL structure is fixed in advance and user values are passed separately as bound parameters, so they can never change the query''s grammar."},{"term":"Cross-site scripting (XSS)","definition":"A flaw that lets an attacker cause a victim''s browser to run attacker-controlled script in the security context of a trusted site."},{"term":"Output encoding","definition":"Converting characters that have special meaning in a given output context (HTML body, HTML attribute, JavaScript, URL) into a safe representation before inserting data into that context."},{"term":"Content Security Policy (CSP)","definition":"An HTTP response header that tells the browser which sources of script, style and other content are permitted, limiting the damage an injected script can do."},{"term":"Sink","definition":"A function or property (for example `innerHTML` or `eval`) where data is interpreted as code or markup; dangerous sinks are central to DOM-based XSS."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The OWASP Top 10:2021\n\nThe OWASP Top 10 is not a standard or a complete checklist; it is a consensus awareness document intended to focus developers and managers on the most significant categories of risk. The 2021 edition, still widely referenced in teaching and procurement, contains the following categories:\n\n| Code | Category | Core idea |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | Broken Access Control | Users can act outside their intended permissions (for example viewing another customer''s account by changing an ID in the URL). |\n| A02 | Cryptographic Failures | Sensitive data is exposed because encryption is missing, weak or wrongly implemented. |\n| A03 | Injection | Untrusted data reaches an interpreter; SQLi and XSS both sit here in the 2021 edition. |\n| A04 | Insecure Design | Flaws in the design itself, which no amount of perfect coding can fix. |\n| A05 | Security Misconfiguration | Default accounts, verbose errors, unnecessary features or missing hardening. |\n| A06 | Vulnerable and Outdated Components | Libraries, frameworks and platforms with known, unpatched vulnerabilities. |\n| A07 | Identification and Authentication Failures | Weak login, session or credential handling. |\n| A08 | Software and Data Integrity Failures | Trusting updates, plugins or serialised data without verifying integrity. |\n| A09 | Security Logging and Monitoring Failures | Attacks are not detected or investigated because events are not logged or reviewed. |\n| A10 | Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) | The server can be tricked into making requests to internal or unintended destinations. |\n\nTwo observations matter for analysis. First, Broken Access Control rose to first place in 2021, reflecting how often applications fail to check *whether this user may do this to this object*. Second, the edition merged cross-site scripting into Injection, which reinforces the common-root-cause view taken in this lesson. OWASP revises the list periodically, so always check the project site for the current edition before quoting it in professional work.\n\n### SQL injection: the root cause\n\nConsider a login or search feature that builds SQL by gluing strings together. If a developer writes code that inserts the user''s input directly between quotation marks, the database cannot tell which characters came from the developer and which came from the user. A single quotation mark in the input ends the developer''s string early, and anything after it is parsed as SQL. The attacker is now co-authoring the query.\n\nThe consequences depend on what the database account can do and what the query is for. Typical impacts include bypassing a password check, extracting entire tables (customer records, password hashes), altering balances, or, on badly configured systems, executing operating-system commands. SQLi therefore threatens all three CIA properties.\n\nThe definitive fix is to keep code and data separate at the interface to the interpreter. With a parameterised query the SQL text is sent to the database with placeholders, the database parses and plans it, and only then are the user''s values bound to those placeholders as pure data. No value, however many quotation marks it contains, can alter the parsed structure.\n\n### Cross-site scripting: the same pattern in the browser\n\nXSS repeats the pattern with a different interpreter: the victim''s browser. If an application places user-controlled text into a page without encoding it, characters such as `<`, `>` and `\"` can open a `<script>` element or an event-handler attribute. Because the script appears to come from the trusted site, it can read the page, act as the logged-in user, capture keystrokes or silently submit requests. Three variants are recognised:\n\n- **Stored (persistent) XSS**: the payload is saved by the server (in a comment, profile field or support ticket) and delivered to every user who views it. It is often the most damaging because it needs no further interaction and can reach administrators.\n- **Reflected XSS**: the payload travels in the request (often a URL parameter) and is immediately echoed back in the response. The attacker must persuade a victim to follow a crafted link, frequently via phishing.\n- **DOM-based XSS**: the vulnerability lives entirely in client-side JavaScript, which reads attacker-influenced data (for example `location.hash`) and writes it to a dangerous sink such as `innerHTML`. The server may never see the payload, so server-side filtering cannot detect it."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Data-code separation as a design principle\n\nThe most useful model for injection is the *confused interpreter*: any time an application builds a string that another component will parse (SQL, HTML, shell commands, LDAP filters, XML), there is a risk that data will be parsed as instructions. The robust defences are therefore those that use an API in which structure and data are passed separately: prepared statements for SQL, templating engines with automatic contextual escaping for HTML, argument arrays rather than shell strings for operating-system calls.\n\n### Defence in depth for web applications\n\nNo single control is sufficient, so mature teams layer them:\n\n1. **Primary control at the interpreter boundary**: parameterised queries; context-aware output encoding through a framework that escapes by default.\n2. **Input validation**: allow-listing expected formats (a Ghana Card number pattern, a date, a numeric amount). Validation reduces attack surface and improves data quality but is *not* a substitute for parameterisation or encoding, because many legitimate values (such as the surname O''Brien) contain special characters.\n3. **Least privilege**: the application''s database account should hold only the permissions the feature needs. A read-only reporting page should not connect as a database owner.\n4. **Browser-enforced policy**: a Content Security Policy that blocks inline scripts and restricts script sources, plus cookie flags such as `HttpOnly`, `Secure` and `SameSite`.\n5. **Detection and shielding**: a web application firewall (WAF) and logging that alerts on injection patterns. A WAF buys time but can be bypassed and must not be treated as the fix.\n\n### Context-aware output encoding\n\nEncoding must match the context in which data is placed. HTML-body encoding converts `<` to `&lt;`; attribute encoding must also handle quotation marks; JavaScript string contexts and URL parameters need their own encoders. Placing untrusted data directly inside a `<script>` block or an event handler is high risk even with encoding, so good designs avoid those contexts altogether and pass data through safe mechanisms such as `data-` attributes read with `textContent`."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"The following comparison uses Python with a database driver that supports placeholders. The purpose is defensive: to recognise the vulnerable pattern in code review and replace it.\n\n```python\n# VULNERABLE: user input is concatenated into the SQL text.\ndef find_customer_unsafe(conn, phone):\n    sql = \"SELECT id, name FROM customers WHERE phone = ''\" + phone + \"''\"\n    return conn.execute(sql).fetchall()\n\n# SAFE: the SQL structure is fixed; the value is bound as a parameter.\ndef find_customer_safe(conn, phone):\n    sql = \"SELECT id, name FROM customers WHERE phone = ?\"\n    return conn.execute(sql, (phone,)).fetchall()\n```\n\nIn the unsafe version, an input containing a quotation mark followed by SQL syntax changes the `WHERE` clause, for instance into a condition that is always true, so the query returns every customer. In the safe version the database receives the statement `... WHERE phone = ?` and treats the entire input as one literal value to compare against the `phone` column; the attempted payload simply matches no rows. The same principle applies in Java (`PreparedStatement`), PHP (PDO) and most object-relational mappers, provided developers avoid raw-query escape hatches. Stored procedures are safe only if they do not themselves build dynamic SQL from their arguments.\n\nFor DOM-based XSS, compare two ways of displaying a search term taken from the URL:\n\n```javascript\n// VULNERABLE: interprets the value as HTML.\nresults.innerHTML = \"You searched for: \" + term;\n\n// SAFE: inserts the value as plain text; markup is not parsed.\nresults.textContent = \"You searched for: \" + term;\n```\n\nA layered response adds a CSP header such as:\n\n```text\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src ''self''; script-src ''self''; object-src ''none''; base-uri ''self''; frame-ancestors ''none''\n```\n\nThis policy forbids inline scripts and scripts from other origins, so even if an encoding mistake slips through, an injected `<script>` element or inline event handler will be refused by modern browsers. Rolling out CSP first in `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` mode lets a team discover legitimate inline scripts that need refactoring before enforcement."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    U[\"User input\"] --> V{\"How is it used?\"}\n    V -->|\"Concatenated into query or HTML\"| X[\"Interpreter parses data as code\"]\n    X --> I[\"Injection: data theft, account takeover\"]\n    V -->|\"Bound parameter or encoded output\"| S[\"Interpreter treats input as data only\"]\n    S --> OK[\"Intended behaviour\"]\n    OK --> L[\"Further layers: validation, least privilege, CSP, WAF, logging\"]\n```"},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Adom Savings and Loans* (a fictional Accra-based microfinance institution) launches a customer portal where borrowers can check balances using their phone number and a PIN, and a \"message us\" form that staff read in an internal dashboard. A routine authorised assessment finds two issues: the balance lookup concatenates the phone number into SQL, and the message form stores text that is later rendered unencoded in the staff dashboard, a classic stored XSS that could let a malicious \"customer\" run script in a loan officer''s browser and act with staff privileges.\n\nThe remediation plan the security team presents to management illustrates proportionate, layered thinking: replace every concatenated query with parameterised statements and add a code-review rule that rejects new ones; switch the dashboard to a templating engine that encodes by default; introduce a CSP; reduce the portal''s database account to `SELECT` on the specific views it needs; and log failed and anomalous queries to the security monitoring platform.\n\nThere is also a regulatory dimension. Borrowers'' personal data falls under Ghana''s Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843), which requires appropriate security measures, and financial institutions are subject to Bank of Ghana cyber and information security requirements. If the institution were designated as operating Critical Information Infrastructure under the Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038), it would also have obligations to the Cyber Security Authority, including incident reporting. Consult the primary texts for exact obligations."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"**TalkTalk (United Kingdom), 2015.** In October 2015 the UK telecommunications provider TalkTalk suffered a breach in which attackers used SQL injection against legacy web pages that the company had inherited when it acquired another business''s operations. Personal data of around 157,000 customers was accessed, including bank details for a subset. The UK Information Commissioner''s Office fined the company £400,000 in 2016, concluding that it had failed to implement basic security measures: the vulnerable pages were old, the vulnerability class was well known, and a patch for the underlying database software had been available for years.\n\nThree lessons follow. *Asset inventory matters*: forgotten legacy pages remained internet-facing. *Known vulnerability classes are not exotic*, and regulators treat failure to prevent them as a lack of basic care. *Accountability is organisational*: the penalty fell on the company, so senior managers must ensure secure development and testing are resourced."}],"misconceptions":["**\"Input validation alone prevents SQL injection.\"** Validation narrows what reaches the database but cannot safely handle every legitimate value; parameterised queries are the primary defence and validation is a supporting layer.","**\"Escaping quotation marks by hand is as good as prepared statements.\"** Manual escaping is error-prone, depends on character sets and database dialect, and is easily forgotten; prepared statements remove the problem structurally.","**\"XSS only matters if the site has a login.\"** Even public pages can be used to deliver malware, deface content or steal data entered into forms, and stored XSS in an admin view can compromise the whole site.","**\"A Content Security Policy fixes XSS.\"** CSP limits the impact of injected script; the underlying encoding flaw still needs to be fixed, and weak policies (for example allowing `''unsafe-inline''`) provide little protection."],"examMistakes":["Listing the OWASP Top 10 categories without explaining the underlying risk or giving an example of each.","Describing SQLi defences as \"use a firewall\" or \"sanitise input\" without mentioning parameterised queries.","Confusing reflected and DOM-based XSS; the key distinction is whether the vulnerable code runs on the server or entirely in client-side JavaScript.","Writing offensive step-by-step payloads instead of explaining mechanisms and defences, which misreads what a postgraduate security question rewards.","Forgetting the business and regulatory consequences (data protection obligations, reputational loss, regulatory penalties) when evaluating impact."],"criticalQuestions":["Why might the OWASP Top 10 be a poor choice as the *sole* acceptance criterion in a software procurement contract, and what would you add?","If a legacy application cannot be rewritten to use parameterised queries in the short term, which compensating controls would you prioritise and how would you justify the residual risk to management?","Should a regulator treat a breach caused by a well-known flaw such as SQLi differently from one caused by a previously unknown vulnerability? Justify your view."],"exercises":["**PortSwigger Web Security Academy (free, legal lab).** Register and complete the introductory SQL injection and cross-site scripting learning materials and their apprentice-level labs. *Expected output:* a one-page reflection describing, for each lab, the root cause in plain language and the specific code-level fix you would recommend.","**OWASP Juice Shop, run locally.** Install Juice Shop on your own computer (for example using the official container image) and use its score board to find one injection and one XSS challenge. *Expected output:* a short defect report for each, written as a developer would receive it: affected feature, root cause, risk rating, recommended fix and how to verify the fix.","**Code review exercise.** Write a small function in a language of your choice that searches a local test database by customer name using string concatenation, then refactor it to use a parameterised query. *Expected output:* both versions side by side with a commented explanation of why the second cannot be injected.","**CSP design.** For a simple static website you own, draft a Content Security Policy and deploy it in report-only mode. *Expected output:* the header, the reported violations and what you would refactor before enforcing."],"takeaways":["The OWASP Top 10:2021 is an awareness tool covering ten risk categories, with Broken Access Control first and XSS now grouped under Injection.","SQL injection and XSS share one root cause: untrusted data being interpreted as code by an interpreter.","Parameterised queries are the primary SQLi defence; context-aware output encoding is the primary XSS defence.","Input validation, least-privilege database accounts, CSP, secure cookie flags and WAFs are valuable supporting layers, not replacements.","Breaches through well-known flaws are a governance failure, not just a technical one."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"OWASP Foundation. (2021). *OWASP Top 10:2021*.","url":"https://owasp.org/Top10/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"OWASP Foundation. (n.d.). *OWASP Cheat Sheet Series* (see the SQL Injection Prevention and Cross Site Scripting Prevention cheat sheets).","url":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Stallings, W., & Brown, L. (2018). *Computer security: Principles and practice* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Anderson, R. (2020). *Security engineering: A guide to building dependable distributed systems* (3rd ed.). Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Republic of Ghana. (2012). *Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843)*.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"PortSwigger Web Security Academy","url":"https://portswigger.net/web-security","description":"PortSwigger. Free official learning portal with explanations and legal hands-on labs for SQL injection, XSS and other web vulnerabilities.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"OWASP Juice Shop","url":"https://owasp.org/www-project-juice-shop/","description":"OWASP Foundation. Official project page for a deliberately insecure training application you can run locally, with links to its companion guide.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"OWASP Top 10","url":"https://owasp.org/Top10/","description":"OWASP Foundation. Official pages describing each risk category with examples and prevention guidance.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"The OWASP Top 10:2021 groups web risks into ten categories (A01 Broken Access Control to A10 SSRF) and is an awareness tool, not a full standard. SQLi and XSS both arise when an interpreter (database or browser) receives data mixed with code.\n\n- SQLi: use parameterised queries or prepared statements; never concatenate input into SQL.\n- XSS types: stored (saved and served), reflected (echoed from the request), DOM-based (client-side sink such as `innerHTML`).\n- XSS defence: context-aware output encoding, safe sinks such as `textContent`, frameworks that escape by default.\n- CSP limits injected script; deploy in report-only mode first and avoid `''unsafe-inline''`.\n- Supporting layers: allow-list validation, least-privilege database accounts, cookie flags, WAF, logging.\n- Real breaches such as TalkTalk (2015) show that regulators treat failure to prevent known flaws as a governance failure."}'::jsonb, 3001, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('dec6a055-f7dd-5546-b9c1-727f8a95f0e6', 'd4cc9a37-040d-5e5e-98a5-bd91c12b72d0', 'authentication-and-authorisation', 'Authentication and Authorisation', 'Distinguish authentication from authorisation, evaluate authentication factors, MFA and password policy against NIST SP 800-63B, explain OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect, compare RBAC with ABAC and design secure session management.', array['Authentication', 'MFA', 'Authorisation', 'Session management']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Almost every security decision an application makes reduces to two questions: *who is this?* and *what are they allowed to do?* The first is authentication; the second is authorisation. When either fails, the consequences are severe. Stolen or guessed credentials remain one of the most common initial routes into organisations, and broken access control sits at the top of the OWASP Top 10:2021.\n\nThis lesson treats identity as a chain. A user proves who they are using one or more factors, the system issues a session or token that represents that proof, and every later request is checked against an access-control policy. A weakness at any link (a reused password, a missing second factor, a predictable session identifier, or a missing permission check on one API endpoint) breaks the chain. You will learn to evaluate each link and to recommend proportionate controls for Ghanaian businesses, where mobile phones are central to both customer identity and fraud.","objectives":["Distinguish identification, authentication, authorisation and accountability, and explain why each is needed.","Evaluate knowledge, possession and inherence factors and compare MFA methods by phishing resistance.","Apply the main password recommendations of NIST SP 800-63B to critique an organisational password policy.","Explain the roles of OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect and why OAuth alone is not an authentication protocol.","Compare role-based and attribute-based access control and recommend one for a given business context.","Design session management controls that resist hijacking and fixation."],"prerequisites":["Access control and least privilege concepts from the operating system security lesson.","Hashing and password hashing from the cryptography module.","Basic understanding of HTTP cookies and HTTPS."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Authentication","definition":"The process of verifying a claimed identity using one or more authenticators."},{"term":"Authorisation","definition":"The decision about whether an authenticated subject may perform a specific action on a specific resource."},{"term":"Multi-factor authentication (MFA)","definition":"Authentication that requires factors from at least two different categories: something you know, something you have and something you are."},{"term":"Phishing-resistant authenticator","definition":"An authenticator, such as a FIDO2/WebAuthn security key or passkey, that cryptographically binds the login to the genuine website so a fake site cannot relay it."},{"term":"OAuth 2.0","definition":"An authorisation framework (RFC 6749) that lets an application obtain limited, delegated access to a resource on a user''s behalf using access tokens."},{"term":"OpenID Connect (OIDC)","definition":"An identity layer built on OAuth 2.0 that returns a signed ID token describing the authenticated user, enabling single sign-on."},{"term":"Role-based access control (RBAC)","definition":"Permissions are assigned to roles and users acquire permissions by being assigned roles."},{"term":"Attribute-based access control (ABAC)","definition":"Access decisions evaluate attributes of the subject, resource, action and environment against policy rules."},{"term":"Session fixation","definition":"An attack in which the attacker causes a victim to use a session identifier known to the attacker, which then becomes authenticated when the victim logs in."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The identity chain\n\n*Identification* is the claim (\"I am kofi.mensah\"). *Authentication* tests the claim. *Authorisation* decides what the authenticated identity may do. *Accountability* records what was done, so actions can be attributed later. Many breaches occur because organisations invest in strong authentication but neglect authorisation checks on individual functions, a pattern known as broken object-level authorisation in APIs: the user is genuinely logged in but can fetch another user''s record by changing an identifier.\n\n### Authentication factors and MFA\n\nFactors fall into three categories. *Knowledge* factors (passwords, PINs) are cheap but can be guessed, phished or reused. *Possession* factors (a phone receiving an SMS code, an authenticator app generating time-based one-time passwords, a hardware key) prove control of a device. *Inherence* factors (fingerprint, face) are convenient but cannot be changed if compromised, so they are normally used to unlock a local authenticator rather than being sent to a server.\n\nMFA combines categories; two passwords are not MFA. MFA methods differ greatly in strength:\n\n| Method | Main weakness | Phishing resistance |\n|---|---|---|\n| SMS one-time code | SIM swap, interception, real-time phishing relay | Low |\n| Authenticator app code (TOTP) | Real-time phishing relay | Low to moderate |\n| Push approval | \"MFA fatigue\" from repeated prompts | Moderate if number matching is used |\n| FIDO2/WebAuthn key or passkey | Device loss (needs recovery process) | High |\n\nPhishing-resistant methods work because the authenticator signs a challenge that includes the website''s origin; a look-alike domain receives a signature it cannot use.\n\n### Password policy according to NIST SP 800-63B\n\nNIST Special Publication 800-63B reshaped password guidance by focusing on what actually reduces compromise rather than on complexity rituals. At a high level it recommends that verifiers:\n\n- require a reasonable minimum length and permit long passphrases (at least 64 characters), with all printable characters and spaces allowed;\n- check new passwords against lists of commonly used, expected or previously breached values and reject matches;\n- **not** impose arbitrary composition rules (such as \"one uppercase, one symbol\"), which push users towards predictable patterns;\n- **not** force periodic password changes, but require a change when there is evidence of compromise;\n- avoid knowledge-based \"security questions\" and password hints;\n- allow paste so that password managers work, and limit failed attempts through rate limiting.\n\nPasswords must be stored using a salted, deliberately slow password-hashing function. NIST has continued to revise the 800-63 suite, so practitioners should check the current revision for exact thresholds.\n\n### Session management\n\nAfter login, the server usually issues a session identifier in a cookie or a signed token. Good practice includes generating identifiers with a cryptographically secure random generator, **regenerating the session identifier at login** to defeat fixation, setting cookie flags `Secure`, `HttpOnly` and `SameSite`, enforcing idle and absolute timeouts, invalidating sessions on logout and password change, and re-authenticating users before high-risk actions such as changing a payout account."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect\n\nOAuth 2.0 solves *delegated authorisation*. Suppose a budgeting app wants read-only access to your transaction history held by a bank API. Rather than giving the app your banking password, you are redirected to the bank''s authorisation server, log in there, and consent to a limited *scope*. The app receives an *access token* it presents to the bank''s resource server. Four roles appear: resource owner (you), client (the app), authorisation server and resource server. For browser and mobile clients the recommended flow is the authorisation code flow with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange), which protects the code from interception.\n\nAn access token tells a resource server that *access was granted*; it is not a reliable statement of *who the user is* to the client. OpenID Connect adds that missing piece: an *ID token*, a signed JSON Web Token containing claims such as the issuer, the subject identifier and the audience. Clients must validate the signature, issuer, audience and expiry. OIDC is what sits behind \"Sign in with...\" buttons and enterprise single sign-on.\n\n### RBAC versus ABAC\n\n| Aspect | RBAC | ABAC |\n|---|---|---|\n| Decision basis | User''s role(s) | Attributes of user, resource, action and context |\n| Example rule | \"Loan officers can view loan files.\" | \"Loan officers can view loan files for their own branch, during working hours, from managed devices.\" |\n| Strengths | Simple to understand, audit and certify | Fine-grained, adapts to context, fewer roles needed |\n| Weaknesses | Role explosion when exceptions multiply | Policies harder to test and explain; needs reliable attribute data |\n\nMany organisations use a hybrid: RBAC for coarse entitlements, with attribute conditions for sensitive data. Whatever the model, enforcement must happen on the server for every request, with deny-by-default."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *KenteCart* (a fictional Kumasi-based online marketplace) has the following policy: passwords of at least eight characters with an uppercase letter, a digit and a symbol; mandatory changes every 60 days; SMS codes for all customers; and a single \"admin\" role shared by twelve staff.\n\nA structured critique against good practice:\n\n1. **Composition and expiry rules**: These contradict NIST SP 800-63B. Replace them with a longer minimum length, support for passphrases, a breached-password check and change-on-compromise.\n2. **SMS for everyone**: Acceptable as a baseline for low-risk customers, but staff and sellers who handle payouts should use phishing-resistant authenticators. Number-matching push or passkeys reduce fatigue and relay attacks.\n3. **Shared admin role**: Violates least privilege and accountability. Split into roles such as `catalogue-editor`, `refund-approver` and `platform-admin`; require individual accounts; and add an ABAC condition that refunds above a threshold need a second approver.\n4. **Sessions**: Ensure session regeneration at login, `HttpOnly` and `Secure` cookies, a short idle timeout for staff consoles, and re-authentication before changing a seller''s mobile money payout number.\n\nThe output is a prioritised list: the admin role split and staff MFA first (highest likelihood and impact), then password policy changes, then session hardening."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"User opens client app\"] --> B[\"Redirect to authorisation server\"]\n    B --> C[\"User authenticates with MFA\"]\n    C --> D[\"User consents to requested scope\"]\n    D --> E[\"Authorisation code returned to client\"]\n    E --> F[\"Client exchanges code plus PKCE verifier for tokens\"]\n    F --> G[\"ID token: who the user is (OIDC)\"]\n    F --> H[\"Access token: what the client may access\"]\n    H --> I[\"Resource server checks token and policy on every request\"]\n```"},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Ghana''s digital economy relies heavily on the mobile phone as an identity anchor. Mobile money wallets, bank alerts and many one-time codes are tied to a SIM card, and SIM registration has been linked to the Ghana Card. This makes possession of a phone number valuable to fraudsters: SIM-swap fraud and social engineering of customer-care agents directly undermine SMS-based authentication. Organisations should therefore treat SMS codes as a baseline rather than a strong control, apply additional checks (for example, a waiting period or extra verification after a recent SIM change, where telecom partners provide that signal) before high-value actions, and train staff to resist pressure to reset credentials without proper verification.\n\nFor internal staff, the Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038) gives the Cyber Security Authority responsibilities for protecting Critical Information Infrastructure, and sector regulators such as the Bank of Ghana expect strong access controls in financial institutions. Practically, privileged accounts for core banking, payment switches and cloud consoles should use phishing-resistant MFA, individual named accounts, and periodic access reviews."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"**Colonial Pipeline (United States), 2021.** In May 2021 Colonial Pipeline, which operates a major fuel pipeline on the US East Coast, was hit by ransomware attributed to the DarkSide group. The company shut down pipeline operations as a precaution, causing fuel supply disruption and public concern. In testimony to the US Congress, the company''s chief executive stated that the attackers had gained access through a legacy virtual private network (VPN) account that was no longer in active use but had not been deactivated, and that the account did not require multi-factor authentication. The password had reportedly appeared in a set of leaked credentials. The company paid a ransom, part of which the US Department of Justice later announced it had recovered.\n\nThe case illustrates three links in the identity chain failing together: account lifecycle management (a dormant account remained active), authentication strength (single-factor remote access) and credential hygiene (a reused or leaked password). None of these are exotic controls; they are basic identity governance. It also shows how an IT authentication failure can force a business-level operational decision."}],"misconceptions":["**\"Complex passwords that change often are the most secure.\"** NIST SP 800-63B finds that forced complexity and frequent expiry lead to predictable patterns; length, breached-password checks and change-on-compromise are more effective.","**\"Any MFA stops phishing.\"** SMS and TOTP codes can be relayed in real time by a phishing proxy; only origin-bound methods such as FIDO2/WebAuthn are phishing resistant.","**\"OAuth is a login protocol.\"** OAuth 2.0 delegates authorisation; OpenID Connect adds authentication through the ID token.","**\"If the user is logged in, the request is authorised.\"** Authentication does not imply permission; each request must check the user''s rights to the specific object."],"examMistakes":["Using \"authentication\" and \"authorisation\" interchangeably.","Listing MFA methods without comparing their resistance to phishing, SIM swap and fatigue attacks.","Quoting outdated password advice (mandatory 30- or 90-day changes) as current best practice without qualification.","Describing RBAC and ABAC without giving a concrete example rule for each.","Forgetting session management entirely when asked to secure a login process."],"criticalQuestions":["A Ghanaian bank argues that SMS one-time codes are the only practical MFA for its rural customers. How would you balance accessibility, cost and security in your recommendation?","When does the complexity of ABAC become a security risk in its own right?","Should organisations remove passwords entirely in favour of passkeys? What new risks would account recovery introduce?"],"exercises":["**Policy critique.** Obtain the password policy of an organisation you belong to (or a published example) and compare it with the NIST SP 800-63B recommendations in this lesson. *Expected output:* a table of each policy clause, whether it aligns, and a recommended change.","**PortSwigger Web Security Academy labs.** Complete the introductory authentication and access control learning materials. *Expected output:* a short note for each lab describing the flawed check and the server-side fix.","**RBAC and ABAC design.** For a small clinic''s patient records system, design four roles and two ABAC conditions. *Expected output:* a role-permission matrix and two policy rules written in plain English.","**Session review.** Using your browser''s developer tools on your *own* test application (or OWASP Juice Shop run locally), inspect session cookie attributes. *Expected output:* a list of flags present and missing, and the risk of each missing flag."],"takeaways":["Authentication proves identity; authorisation decides permitted actions; both must be enforced on every request.","MFA requires different factor categories, and methods vary widely in phishing resistance.","NIST SP 800-63B favours length, breached-password screening and change-on-compromise over composition rules and forced expiry.","OAuth 2.0 delegates access; OpenID Connect adds a verifiable statement of identity.","RBAC is simpler to govern; ABAC is more expressive; hybrids are common.","Secure sessions need random identifiers, regeneration at login, secure cookie flags and timeouts."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"National Institute of Standards and Technology. (2017). *Digital identity guidelines: Authentication and lifecycle management* (NIST Special Publication 800-63B). National Institute of Standards and Technology.","url":"https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-3/sp800-63b.html","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Hardt, D. (Ed.). (2012). *The OAuth 2.0 authorization framework* (RFC 6749). Internet Engineering Task Force.","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Hu, V. C., Ferraiolo, D., Kuhn, R., Schnitzer, A., Sandlin, K., Miller, R., & Scarfone, K. (2014). *Guide to attribute based access control (ABAC) definition and considerations* (NIST Special Publication 800-162). National Institute of Standards and Technology.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Stallings, W., & Brown, L. (2018). *Computer security: Principles and practice* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"PortSwigger Web Security Academy","url":"https://portswigger.net/web-security","description":"PortSwigger. Official free learning portal with topics and labs on authentication, access control and OAuth vulnerabilities.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"OpenID Foundation","url":"https://openid.net/","description":"OpenID Foundation. Official site for OpenID Connect specifications and explanatory material.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Cisco Skills for All","url":"https://skillsforall.com/","description":"Cisco Networking Academy. Official learning portal with free introductory cybersecurity courses covering identity and access concepts.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Identity is a chain: identification, authentication, authorisation and accountability. MFA combines different factor categories, with FIDO2/WebAuthn offering phishing resistance that SMS and TOTP lack. NIST SP 800-63B recommends length, breached-password checks and change-on-compromise rather than complexity rules and forced expiry.\n\n- OAuth 2.0 = delegated authorisation (access token); OIDC = authentication (ID token).\n- Use authorisation code flow with PKCE for browser and mobile clients.\n- RBAC: permissions via roles; ABAC: policy over subject, resource, action and environment attributes.\n- Enforce access control server-side, deny by default, on every request and object.\n- Sessions: secure random IDs, regenerate at login, `Secure`/`HttpOnly`/`SameSite`, timeouts, re-authentication for risky actions.\n- Colonial Pipeline (2021): dormant VPN account, no MFA, leaked password."}'::jsonb, 2549, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('239219a4-3d48-5413-8d0c-6867d086b9f4', 'd4cc9a37-040d-5e5e-98a5-bd91c12b72d0', 'secure-development-and-database-protection', 'Secure Software Development and Database Protection', 'Apply a secure software development lifecycle with threat-informed SAST, DAST and dependency testing, and design layered database protection using least privilege, encryption, auditing and row-level security.', array['Secure software development', 'Secure SDLC', 'Database protection']::text[], 50, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"The previous two lessons examined specific flaws: injection, cross-site scripting and broken authentication. Fixing flaws one at a time after release is expensive and never-ending. The more strategic question is how an organisation can build software that has fewer flaws in the first place and can detect the remainder before attackers do. That is the purpose of a secure software development lifecycle (secure SDLC).\n\nSoftware also exists to process data, and the most valuable data usually sits in databases. Even well-written applications can be bypassed: an attacker may steal a backup, compromise a database administrator''s laptop or abuse an over-privileged service account. Database protection therefore needs its own layered controls. This lesson connects both themes: building security into development, and protecting the data store as the final line of defence.","objectives":["Explain the phases of a secure SDLC and the security activities appropriate to each phase.","Compare SAST, DAST, software composition analysis and penetration testing by what they detect and when they are used.","Apply threat-informed testing by deriving test cases from a threat model and known attack patterns.","Design database protection controls covering least privilege, encryption, auditing and row-level security.","Evaluate the Equifax 2017 breach as a failure of patch and component management within the lifecycle."],"prerequisites":["SQL injection, XSS and access control concepts from earlier lessons in this module.","Symmetric encryption and key management from the cryptography module.","General understanding of how software projects move from requirements to deployment."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Secure SDLC","definition":"A software development lifecycle in which security requirements, design review, secure coding, testing and operational monitoring are integrated into every phase."},{"term":"SAST (static application security testing)","definition":"Analysis of source code, bytecode or binaries without running them, to find insecure patterns such as concatenated SQL."},{"term":"DAST (dynamic application security testing)","definition":"Testing a running application from the outside by sending crafted requests and observing responses."},{"term":"Software composition analysis (SCA)","definition":"Identifying third-party and open-source components in a product and matching them against known vulnerabilities and licences."},{"term":"Software bill of materials (SBOM)","definition":"A formal inventory of the components and dependencies that make up a piece of software."},{"term":"Transparent data encryption (TDE)","definition":"Database-level encryption of data files and backups at rest, handled by the database engine without application changes."},{"term":"Row-level security (RLS)","definition":"A database feature that filters which rows a given user or session may read or modify, based on a policy."},{"term":"Database activity monitoring","definition":"Recording and analysing database queries and administrative actions to detect misuse and support investigations."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Why \"shift left\" matters\n\nDefects found during design or coding are far cheaper to fix than those found in production, because late fixes require re-testing, redeployment and sometimes incident response. \"Shifting left\" means moving security activities earlier. It does not mean abandoning late-stage testing; it means each phase has security work appropriate to it.\n\n### Security activities by phase\n\n| Phase | Security activities | Example output |\n|---|---|---|\n| Requirements | Identify data classification, regulatory duties (for example Ghana''s Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843)), abuse cases | Security requirements in user stories |\n| Design | Threat modelling (for example STRIDE), secure architecture review | Threat model and mitigations |\n| Implementation | Secure coding standards, peer code review, SAST in the developer''s editor and pipeline | Merge requests blocked on high-severity findings |\n| Build | SCA, SBOM generation, signing of build artefacts, secrets scanning | Dependency report, signed releases |\n| Test | DAST, threat-informed test cases, penetration testing for high-risk releases | Test report with severity ratings |\n| Deploy | Hardened configuration, infrastructure-as-code scanning, change approval | Approved, reproducible deployment |\n| Operate | Logging, monitoring, vulnerability and patch management, incident response | Alerts, patch records, lessons learned |\n\nTwo public frameworks are useful anchors. Microsoft''s Security Development Lifecycle, described by Howard and Lipner (2006), popularised mandatory security activities in each phase. NIST''s Secure Software Development Framework (SP 800-218) organises practices into four groups: prepare the organisation, protect the software, produce well-secured software and respond to vulnerabilities. The OWASP Application Security Verification Standard (ASVS) provides testable requirements that can be written into contracts.\n\n### Comparing testing techniques\n\n- **SAST** sees the whole codebase, finds issues early and points to the exact line, but produces false positives and cannot see runtime configuration.\n- **DAST** tests the deployed application as an attacker would, finding configuration and runtime issues, but has limited code coverage and finds issues late.\n- **SCA** answers \"are we using a component with a known vulnerability?\" which neither SAST nor DAST reliably answers. Given how much modern software is assembled from open-source libraries, SCA is essential.\n- **Penetration testing** applies human creativity to chain weaknesses together, especially business-logic flaws that tools miss. It is periodic and should be scoped and authorised in writing.\n\n### Threat-informed testing\n\nTesting is most valuable when it is driven by realistic threats rather than generic checklists. A threat-informed approach takes the application''s threat model, the OWASP Top 10 categories most relevant to it and publicly documented attacker techniques, and converts them into specific test cases. For a mobile money merchant API, that might mean: \"a merchant cannot read another merchant''s transactions by changing the merchant ID\" (access control), \"replaying a payment request with the same reference is rejected\" (integrity), and \"rate limiting stops automated PIN guessing\" (authentication). Each test case links back to a threat, so coverage can be explained to management."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Layered database protection\n\nDatabase security applies defence in depth around the data itself:\n\n1. **Least privilege**: separate accounts for each application and administrator; application accounts limited to the tables, views or stored procedures they need; no shared `sa` or `root` use; administrative access through named accounts with MFA.\n2. **Network isolation**: databases on private network segments, reachable only from application servers and management hosts, never directly from the internet.\n3. **Encryption in transit**: TLS between application and database.\n4. **Encryption at rest**: TDE or storage encryption protects stolen disks and backups; column-level or application-level encryption protects especially sensitive fields (for example national ID numbers) even from some administrators. Keys must be stored separately, ideally in a key management service or hardware security module.\n5. **Auditing and monitoring**: logging of logins, privilege changes, schema changes and access to sensitive tables, with logs forwarded to a system that database administrators cannot alter.\n6. **Row-level security and masking**: restricting which rows a session can see and masking sensitive columns for support staff.\n7. **Patching and secure configuration**: removing default accounts and sample databases, applying vendor patches promptly.\n\n### Row-level security in practice\n\nSeveral database systems support RLS. The PostgreSQL example below ensures that branch staff only see loan records for their own branch, enforced inside the database rather than relying on every application query to include the right filter.\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE loans ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\n\nCREATE POLICY branch_isolation ON loans\n  USING (branch_id = current_setting(''app.branch_id'')::int);\n\n-- Application role gets only what it needs.\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE ON loans TO loan_app;\nREVOKE DELETE ON loans FROM loan_app;\n```\n\nThe application sets `app.branch_id` from the authenticated user''s attributes at the start of each session. A coding mistake that omits a `WHERE branch_id = ...` clause no longer exposes other branches'' data."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Sika Health* (a fictional Tamale-based health-technology start-up) is building a patient appointment and records system. The team of six developers releases weekly. Management asks for a pragmatic secure SDLC that does not slow delivery excessively.\n\n**Step 1: Requirements.** Classify patient health data as *restricted*. Record the duty under the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) to protect personal data and identify abuse cases: a receptionist browsing records of a celebrity patient; an attacker scraping the booking API.\n\n**Step 2: Design.** Run a one-hour STRIDE session on the data flow diagram for each major feature. Decide on server-side authorisation checks and RLS by clinic.\n\n**Step 3: Implementation and build.** Add SAST and SCA to the continuous integration pipeline; block merges on critical findings; add secrets scanning so database passwords are never committed.\n\n**Step 4: Test.** Run DAST against the staging environment nightly. Write threat-informed test cases for the top risks. Commission an authorised penetration test before launch.\n\n**Step 5: Database.** Create a dedicated application role with no `DROP` or `DELETE` rights on clinical tables; enable TDE and encrypt national ID numbers at column level; send audit logs of record access to a separate logging platform; review access to celebrity-flagged records weekly.\n\n**Step 6: Operate.** Subscribe to security advisories for every component in the SBOM and agree patch timelines (for example, critical internet-facing vulnerabilities within days rather than weeks).\n\nThe result is proportionate: most controls are automated in the pipeline, and human effort concentrates on threat modelling and high-risk testing."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    R[\"Requirements: classify data, abuse cases\"] --> D[\"Design: threat model\"]\n    D --> I[\"Implement: secure coding, SAST\"]\n    I --> B[\"Build: SCA, SBOM, signing\"]\n    B --> T[\"Test: DAST, threat-informed tests, pen test\"]\n    T --> P[\"Deploy: hardened config\"]\n    P --> O[\"Operate: monitor, patch, respond\"]\n    O -->|\"Lessons learned and new threats\"| R\n```"},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Many Ghanaian organisations, from fintech start-ups in Accra to public agencies digitising services, depend on outsourced development or off-the-shelf systems customised by vendors. A secure SDLC therefore has a procurement dimension. Contracts should require secure coding standards, evidence of SAST and SCA, remediation of critical findings before acceptance, an SBOM, and access to independent penetration test results. Acceptance testing should include security test cases, not only functional ones.\n\nWhere a system processes personal data, the organisation (as data controller) remains responsible under Act 843 even if a vendor built it. Where systems are designated as Critical Information Infrastructure under the Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038), additional obligations to the Cyber Security Authority apply. Database protection is particularly important in contexts such as hospital records, payroll systems and mobile money transaction stores, where a single over-privileged account can expose large volumes of sensitive data."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"**Equifax (United States), 2017.** In 2017 the credit reporting agency Equifax suffered a breach exposing personal information of around 147 million people, according to widely reported figures. Attackers exploited a known vulnerability in the Apache Struts web framework (CVE-2017-5638) in an online dispute portal. A patch for the vulnerability had been published in March 2017, but the affected system was not updated, and the attackers gained access from mid-May. Investigations by US authorities, including a report by the US Government Accountability Office, described failures in identifying vulnerable systems, in network segmentation and in monitoring; an expired digital certificate on a traffic inspection device meant that malicious traffic was not being inspected for a period. Equifax later agreed a settlement with US regulators.\n\nFrom a secure SDLC perspective, Equifax is a lesson in the *operate* phase and in component management. The organisation needed an accurate inventory of where Struts was used (what an SBOM and SCA provide), a process that turned an advisory into a tracked patch with a deadline, and monitoring that would detect large-scale data extraction from databases. Database-side controls such as limiting what the portal''s credentials could query, and alerting on unusual volumes, could have reduced the impact."}],"misconceptions":["**\"Secure SDLC means buying a scanning tool.\"** Tools support the lifecycle, but requirements, threat modelling, code review, training and patch processes are equally important.","**\"SAST and DAST find the same issues.\"** They have different blind spots; SAST misses runtime and configuration issues, DAST misses unexercised code paths, and neither reliably finds vulnerable dependencies.","**\"Encrypting the database stops SQL injection data theft.\"** TDE protects data at rest from stolen files; data queried through the application is decrypted automatically, so injection still exposes it.","**\"The application checks permissions, so the database does not need to.\"** Defence in depth means the database should also enforce least privilege and, where appropriate, row-level policies."],"examMistakes":["Describing the SDLC phases without stating a concrete security activity for each.","Treating penetration testing as a substitute for secure development rather than one assurance activity among many.","Ignoring third-party components and patching when discussing secure development.","Recommending \"encrypt the database\" without specifying at rest, in transit or column level, or how keys are managed.","Omitting auditing and monitoring from database protection answers."],"criticalQuestions":["How would you persuade a start-up founder, who measures success by release speed, to invest in a secure SDLC?","Under what circumstances is application-level encryption of a field preferable to database TDE, and what does it cost in functionality?","Who should be accountable when a vendor-built system processing personal data is breached through a vulnerability introduced by the vendor?"],"exercises":["**Pipeline design.** For an open-source project you own (or a personal practice repository), enable a free SAST and dependency scanning tool offered by your code hosting platform. *Expected output:* a screenshot or summary of findings, with your triage decision (fix, accept, false positive) for each.","**Threat-informed test cases.** Choose one feature of OWASP Juice Shop running locally and write five test cases, each linked to a named threat. *Expected output:* a table of threat, test case, expected secure result and observed result.","**Row-level security lab.** In a local PostgreSQL instance, create a `loans` table, apply the RLS policy shown in this lesson and test it with two sessions using different branch settings. *Expected output:* query results showing each session sees only its branch''s rows."],"takeaways":["A secure SDLC embeds security activities in every phase, from requirements to operations.","SAST, DAST, SCA and penetration testing are complementary; each has blind spots.","Threat-informed testing links test cases to realistic threats so coverage is meaningful.","Database protection layers least privilege, isolation, encryption, auditing and row-level security.","Equifax (2017) shows that unpatched components and weak monitoring can turn a known vulnerability into a major breach."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Howard, M., & Lipner, S. (2006). *The security development lifecycle*. Microsoft Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Souppaya, M., Scarfone, K., & Dodson, D. (2022). *Secure software development framework (SSDF) version 1.1: Recommendations for mitigating the risk of software vulnerabilities* (NIST Special Publication 800-218). National Institute of Standards and Technology.","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/218/final","verification":"verified"},{"text":"OWASP Foundation. (n.d.). *Application Security Verification Standard*.","url":"https://owasp.org/www-project-application-security-verification-standard/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Stallings, W., & Brown, L. (2018). *Computer security: Principles and practice* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"OWASP Juice Shop","url":"https://owasp.org/www-project-juice-shop/","description":"OWASP Foundation. Official project page for a locally run, deliberately insecure application useful for practising SAST, DAST and threat-informed testing.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"NIST Secure Software Development Framework","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/ssdf","description":"National Institute of Standards and Technology. Official project page for the SSDF with publications and supporting material.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"PostgreSQL Documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/","description":"PostgreSQL Global Development Group. Official documentation, including row security policies and role privileges.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"A secure SDLC integrates security into requirements, design, implementation, build, test, deployment and operations. Testing techniques are complementary, and threat-informed tests tie assurance to real risks. Databases need their own layered defences because applications can be bypassed.\n\n- SAST: code, early, false positives; DAST: running app, late, runtime issues.\n- SCA and SBOM: find known-vulnerable components; vital for patching.\n- Frameworks: Microsoft SDL, NIST SSDF (SP 800-218), OWASP ASVS.\n- Database: least privilege, network isolation, TLS, TDE or column encryption, auditing, RLS.\n- TDE protects files and backups, not data queried through a compromised application.\n- Equifax (2017): unpatched Apache Struts, weak inventory and monitoring."}'::jsonb, 2461, 3, true)
