insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('cd99bebe-fcca-5cc7-9b52-85c4186f405f', 'a010847e-43ed-5870-9355-17e182d92deb', 'cloud-security-and-identity-management', 'Cloud Security and Identity Management', 'Explain the cloud service and deployment models, apply the shared responsibility model to allocate security duties, and design cloud identity and access management based on least privilege, federation and temporary credentials.', array['Cloud security', 'Shared responsibility model', 'Identity management']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Cloud computing has changed how organisations of every size acquire IT. A start-up in Accra can rent servers, databases and machine-learning services by the hour from a global provider, and a long-established company can move its email and office productivity to a software-as-a-service suite in a weekend. These services are often more secure at the infrastructure level than anything a small organisation could build itself. Yet cloud breaches remain common, and most of them are not caused by the provider being hacked. They are caused by customers misunderstanding which security duties remain theirs, and above all by weaknesses in identity and access management.\n\nIn the cloud, identity is the new perimeter. There is no data-centre door to lock; anyone on the internet who holds a valid key or password can reach the management console or API. This lesson explains the shared responsibility model and then focuses on the identity and access controls that determine whether a cloud environment is safe.","objectives":["Explain the essential characteristics, service models and deployment models of cloud computing as defined by NIST SP 800-145.","Apply the shared responsibility model to allocate security duties between provider and customer for IaaS, PaaS and SaaS.","Design cloud identity and access management controls using least privilege, roles, federation and temporary credentials.","Analyse how a server-side request forgery flaw combined with an over-permissive role led to the Capital One 2019 breach.","Evaluate cloud adoption risks for a Ghanaian organisation, including regulatory considerations for personal data."],"prerequisites":["Authentication, MFA, RBAC and ABAC from the previous module.","Network segmentation and firewall concepts from Module 3.","Basic awareness of virtualisation."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Shared responsibility model","definition":"The division of security duties between the cloud provider (security *of* the cloud) and the customer (security *in* the cloud), which varies by service model."},{"term":"IaaS, PaaS, SaaS","definition":"Infrastructure, Platform and Software as a Service, offering progressively more provider-managed layers."},{"term":"Identity and access management (IAM)","definition":"The policies, processes and technologies that create, manage and enforce digital identities and their permissions."},{"term":"Principal","definition":"Any entity that can make a request to a cloud API, such as a human user, a group, an application or a workload."},{"term":"IAM role","definition":"An identity with permissions that can be assumed temporarily by users or workloads, issuing short-lived credentials rather than permanent keys."},{"term":"Federation","definition":"Allowing users authenticated by an organisation''s own identity provider to access cloud resources through standards such as SAML or OpenID Connect, without separate cloud passwords."},{"term":"Instance metadata service","definition":"An internal endpoint available to a virtual machine that provides configuration data and, in many clouds, temporary credentials for the machine''s role."},{"term":"Root or global administrator account","definition":"The highest-privilege account in a cloud tenancy, which should be protected with strong MFA and used only in emergencies."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### What cloud computing is\n\nNIST SP 800-145 (Mell & Grance, 2011) defines cloud computing through five essential characteristics: on-demand self-service, broad network access, resource pooling, rapid elasticity and measured service. It identifies three service models (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS) and four deployment models: public, private, community and hybrid. These definitions remain useful because each characteristic has a security implication. Self-service means anyone with the right permissions can create resources, including insecure ones, in seconds. Resource pooling means tenants share hardware, so isolation depends on the provider''s engineering. Broad network access means management interfaces are reachable from anywhere.\n\n### The shared responsibility model\n\nProviders secure the physical facilities, hardware, hypervisors and their own global network. What the customer must secure depends on the service model:\n\n| Layer | On-premises | IaaS | PaaS | SaaS |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Data, classification and accountability | Customer | Customer | Customer | Customer |\n| Identities, access and endpoints | Customer | Customer | Customer | Customer (provider supplies tools) |\n| Application | Customer | Customer | Customer | Provider |\n| Operating system, runtime, middleware | Customer | Customer | Provider | Provider |\n| Virtualisation, hardware, network | Customer | Provider | Provider | Provider |\n| Physical data centre | Customer | Provider | Provider | Provider |\n\nThe crucial pattern is the top two rows. **Data and identity are always the customer''s responsibility**, whatever the service model. A provider cannot know that a storage bucket containing payroll data should not be public, or that a former employee''s account should have been disabled. Each major provider publishes its own version of the model, and customers should read the one for every service they use, since boundaries differ between services.\n\n### Identity as the perimeter\n\nCloud management planes are reached through web consoles and APIs over the internet. An attacker with a valid access key can create, read or delete resources from anywhere. As a result, the most important cloud controls are identity controls:\n\n- **Protect the root account**: enable strong MFA, remove long-lived access keys from it, and reserve it for break-glass tasks.\n- **Federate human access**: staff sign in through the organisation''s identity provider with MFA and single sign-on, so leavers lose cloud access when their corporate account is disabled.\n- **Prefer roles and temporary credentials**: workloads should obtain short-lived credentials through roles attached to the compute resource, not static keys stored in code or configuration files.\n- **Least privilege**: grant only the actions and resources needed, scoped to specific resources rather than wildcards; review unused permissions regularly; use provider tools that analyse effective permissions.\n- **Separate environments**: use separate accounts, subscriptions or projects for production, development and security logging, limiting blast radius.\n- **Guardrails**: organisation-level policies that forbid dangerous actions (for example disabling logging or creating public storage) regardless of individual permissions.\n- **Logging**: enable the provider''s control-plane audit log in every region and send it to a protected, separate account.\n\n### Privileged access\n\nAdministrative roles should be eligible rather than permanently assigned where the platform supports it (just-in-time elevation with approval and time limits), with every elevation logged. Emergency break-glass accounts should be few, tightly monitored and tested."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Zero trust applied to cloud\n\nNIST SP 800-207 describes zero trust architecture as a model in which no implicit trust is granted based on network location; every request is authenticated, authorised and evaluated against policy. Cloud environments are a natural fit: rather than trusting anything inside a virtual network, access decisions consider the identity, device posture and context of each request. Workload identity (roles for compute) and federated user identity are the building blocks.\n\n### The Cloud Security Alliance perspective\n\nThe Cloud Security Alliance''s *Security Guidance for Critical Areas of Focus in Cloud Computing* organises cloud security into domains such as governance, compliance, identity and access management, data security and incident response. Its central message mirrors this lesson: moving to the cloud shifts *how* controls are implemented, but the customer remains accountable for governance, identity and data."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Volta Agritech* (a fictional company that connects cocoa and cashew farmers to buyers) runs a web application on IaaS virtual machines, uses a managed database (PaaS) and staff email on a SaaS suite. Management asks: \"What exactly are we responsible for?\"\n\n**Step 1: Map services to models.** Virtual machines (IaaS): Volta patches operating systems, configures host firewalls and secures the application. Managed database (PaaS): the provider patches the engine; Volta configures network access, encryption options, database users and backups retention. Email suite (SaaS): the provider runs the application; Volta configures MFA, sharing settings, retention and administrator roles.\n\n**Step 2: Identify identity risks.** The audit finds a developer''s long-lived access key committed to a code repository, the root account without MFA, and the web servers'' role permitted `*` on all storage.\n\n**Step 3: Remediate.** Revoke and rotate the exposed key; enable secrets scanning; enforce hardware-key MFA on root and remove its access keys; federate staff sign-in through the email suite''s identity provider; replace the wildcard with a policy allowing the web role to read and write only the `volta-uploads` bucket; enable organisation-wide audit logging to a separate security account.\n\n**Step 4: Verify.** Use the provider''s access analysis tool to confirm no unintended external access remains, and schedule quarterly access reviews."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Customer[\"Customer: security IN the cloud\"]\n        D[\"Data and classification\"]\n        I[\"Identities, roles and access policies\"]\n        C[\"Configuration of services\"]\n        A[\"Applications and OS (IaaS)\"]\n    end\n    subgraph Provider[\"Provider: security OF the cloud\"]\n        H[\"Hypervisor, hardware, global network\"]\n        P[\"Physical data centres\"]\n    end\n    I --> D\n    C --> D\n    A --> C\n    H --> A\n    P --> H\n```"},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Cloud adoption in Ghana brings real advantages: avoiding the cost of on-premises generators and cooling, reducing exposure to local power disruptions, and accessing advanced security tooling. It also raises questions boards must answer. Where will personal data be stored and processed, and does that satisfy the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) and any sector-specific rules? Regulated institutions, such as banks supervised by the Bank of Ghana, may face specific requirements or notification expectations for outsourcing and cloud use, which must be checked against current directives. If a service supports Critical Information Infrastructure as defined under the Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038), the organisation should consider its obligations to the Cyber Security Authority when designing incident reporting and access arrangements with the provider.\n\nPractically, small Ghanaian organisations often begin with a SaaS email and file-sharing suite. The highest-value first steps are usually the same everywhere: enforce MFA for all users (phishing-resistant for administrators), limit the number of global administrators, disable legacy authentication protocols that bypass MFA, review external sharing settings, and turn on audit logging."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"**Capital One (United States), 2019.** In July 2019 Capital One disclosed that an outside individual had obtained data relating to approximately 100 million people in the United States and about 6 million in Canada, mainly from credit card applications. The data was held in Amazon Web Services storage. According to court documents and public analyses, the attacker exploited a misconfigured web application firewall running on a cloud virtual machine to perform a server-side request forgery (SSRF): the firewall could be induced to send requests to the instance metadata service, which returned temporary credentials for the role attached to the machine. That role had permissions to list and read storage buckets containing sensitive data, which the attacker then copied. A former employee of the cloud provider was later convicted in a US federal court. US banking regulators imposed a civil penalty on Capital One, citing failures in risk assessment for its cloud migration.\n\nThe case is a precise illustration of shared responsibility. The provider''s infrastructure was not breached; the misconfiguration and the over-permissive role were within the customer''s domain. Lessons include: restrict workload roles to the minimum resources needed; harden metadata access (providers have since promoted session-based metadata service versions that resist simple SSRF); monitor for unusual storage listing and bulk reads; and treat SSRF (OWASP A10:2021) as a cloud-critical vulnerability."}],"misconceptions":["**\"The cloud provider is responsible for security.\"** Providers secure the underlying platform; customers always remain responsible for their data, identities and configuration.","**\"SaaS means there is nothing to configure.\"** SaaS tenants must still manage MFA, administrator roles, sharing settings, retention and logging.","**\"Long-lived access keys are fine if they are kept private.\"** Keys leak through code repositories, logs and laptops; roles with temporary credentials greatly reduce exposure.","**\"Being inside the virtual network means a request can be trusted.\"** Zero trust principles and incidents such as Capital One show that internal position is not proof of legitimacy."],"examMistakes":["Drawing the shared responsibility model without showing that data and identity remain with the customer in every model.","Confusing service models (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS) with deployment models (public, private, community, hybrid).","Describing the Capital One breach as \"AWS was hacked\" rather than as customer-side misconfiguration and excessive permissions.","Recommending \"use strong passwords\" for cloud security without mentioning MFA, federation, roles and least privilege."],"criticalQuestions":["If a SaaS provider suffers an outage that halts a Ghanaian company''s operations for two days, where does accountability lie, and what should the company have done beforehand?","Is it realistic for a small business to implement least privilege in the cloud, given the complexity of IAM policy languages? What tools or practices make it achievable?","How should data residency concerns be weighed against the security benefits of large global providers?"],"exercises":["**Responsibility mapping.** Choose three cloud services you or your employer use (for example an email suite, a hosting platform and a database service). *Expected output:* a table listing each service''s model and the customer''s specific responsibilities, citing the provider''s published shared responsibility documentation.","**Free-tier IAM lab.** In a free-tier cloud account you own, create a user with no permissions, then grant a policy allowing read-only access to a single storage bucket, and test that access to other buckets is denied. *Expected output:* the policy document and screenshots of allowed and denied requests. Delete resources afterwards.","**Root account hardening.** In the same personal account, enable MFA on the root or global administrator account and confirm no root access keys exist. *Expected output:* a checklist of completed hardening steps."],"takeaways":["NIST SP 800-145 defines cloud via five characteristics, three service models and four deployment models.","In every service model, the customer is responsible for data, identities and configuration.","Identity is the cloud perimeter: protect root, federate users, use roles and temporary credentials, and apply least privilege.","Separate environments, organisation-wide guardrails and protected audit logs limit blast radius and support investigations.","Capital One (2019) shows how SSRF plus an over-permissive role can expose large volumes of data without any provider failure."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Mell, P., & Grance, T. (2011). *The NIST definition of cloud computing* (NIST Special Publication 800-145). National Institute of Standards and Technology.","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/145/final","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Rose, S., Borchert, O., Mitchell, S., & Connelly, S. (2020). *Zero trust architecture* (NIST Special Publication 800-207). National Institute of Standards and Technology.","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Cloud Security Alliance. (2017). *Security guidance for critical areas of focus in cloud computing* (Version 4.0). Cloud Security Alliance.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Republic of Ghana. (2020). *Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038)*.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"AWS Shared Responsibility Model","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared-responsibility-model/","description":"Amazon Web Services. Official explanation of how security duties are divided between AWS and its customers.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/","description":"Microsoft. Official free training portal; search for cloud security fundamentals and identity modules.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Cloud Security Alliance","url":"https://cloudsecurityalliance.org/","description":"Cloud Security Alliance. Official site with cloud security guidance, research and training information.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Cloud computing (NIST SP 800-145) offers IaaS, PaaS and SaaS across public, private, community and hybrid deployments. The shared responsibility model gives the provider security of the cloud and the customer security in the cloud, with data and identity always the customer''s duty. Identity is the new perimeter.\n\n- Protect root with strong MFA; no root access keys.\n- Federate staff identities; enforce MFA; disable legacy authentication.\n- Use roles and temporary credentials instead of static keys.\n- Least privilege scoped to specific resources; review regularly.\n- Separate accounts, guardrail policies and protected audit logs.\n- Capital One (2019): SSRF to metadata service, over-permissive role, bulk storage reads."}'::jsonb, 2435, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('59531fd7-ed50-560d-a876-4dcf4bbc9a2b', 'a010847e-43ed-5870-9355-17e182d92deb', 'configuration-security-and-data-protection', 'Configuration Security and Data Protection', 'Apply secure configuration baselines and continuous misconfiguration detection, design a data classification scheme with handling rules, and select proportionate data protection controls such as encryption, key management, masking and data loss prevention.', array['Configuration security', 'Data protection', 'Data classification']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Many of the most damaging breaches of the past decade did not require a clever exploit. A storage bucket was left publicly readable, a database was exposed to the internet without a password, a cloud access key was committed to a code repository, or default administrator credentials were never changed. These are *configuration* failures, and the OWASP Top 10:2021 recognises their importance with its Security Misconfiguration category. In cloud environments, where a single setting can expose millions of records, configuration security has become a discipline of its own.\n\nConfiguration security protects systems; data protection protects what those systems hold. To protect data proportionately, an organisation must first know what data it has and how sensitive it is, which is the purpose of data classification. This lesson connects the three: secure configuration, classification, and the technical and procedural controls that follow from each classification level.","objectives":["Explain why misconfiguration is a leading cause of breaches, particularly in cloud environments.","Apply secure configuration baselines, infrastructure-as-code scanning and continuous monitoring to prevent and detect configuration drift.","Design a data classification scheme with clear labels and handling rules for a business context.","Select proportionate data protection controls, including encryption, key management, tokenisation, masking and data loss prevention.","Evaluate secrets management failures using the Uber 2016 breach."],"prerequisites":["The shared responsibility model and cloud IAM from the previous lesson.","Symmetric encryption and key management from the cryptography module.","The CIA triad."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Security misconfiguration","definition":"An insecure setting, default, permission or unnecessary feature that exposes a system, such as public storage or verbose error messages."},{"term":"Secure configuration baseline","definition":"A documented, approved set of security settings for a type of system, often derived from vendor guidance or CIS Benchmarks."},{"term":"Configuration drift","definition":"The gradual divergence of a system''s actual settings from its approved baseline through manual changes or updates."},{"term":"Infrastructure as code (IaC)","definition":"Defining infrastructure (networks, servers, storage, permissions) in version-controlled templates that can be reviewed, scanned and deployed repeatably."},{"term":"Cloud security posture management (CSPM)","definition":"Tools that continuously assess cloud configurations against policies and benchmarks and alert on risky settings."},{"term":"Data classification","definition":"Categorising data by sensitivity and criticality so that appropriate handling and protection rules can be applied."},{"term":"Tokenisation","definition":"Replacing a sensitive value (such as a card number) with a non-sensitive surrogate, with the mapping held in a secured vault."},{"term":"Data loss prevention (DLP)","definition":"Controls that detect and block unauthorised movement of sensitive data through email, web uploads, endpoints or cloud services."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Why misconfiguration dominates\n\nThree forces make misconfiguration common. **Speed and self-service**: cloud resources can be created in seconds by many people, often without security review. **Complexity**: modern platforms have thousands of settings, permission combinations and service-specific defaults. **Invisibility**: unlike a missing door lock, a public storage bucket looks no different to its owner. Attackers, by contrast, run automated scans of the internet for exposed databases, open storage and unauthenticated administration panels, so exposure is often discovered within hours.\n\n### Secure configuration in practice\n\nA mature configuration security programme typically includes:\n\n1. **Baselines**: hardened standards for each platform (operating systems, databases, cloud accounts, network devices), commonly based on the Center for Internet Security (CIS) Benchmarks or vendor security baselines. Baselines disable unused services, remove default accounts, enforce logging and set secure defaults.\n2. **Infrastructure as code with review**: changes are made through templates in version control, reviewed by a second person, and scanned automatically for risky settings (public access, unencrypted storage, wildcard permissions) before deployment.\n3. **Guardrails**: organisation-wide policies that make some insecure states impossible, such as blocking public access to storage at account level.\n4. **Continuous monitoring**: CSPM and configuration management tools compare running systems against baselines and alert on drift.\n5. **Secrets management**: passwords, API keys and certificates are stored in a dedicated secrets manager or key vault, injected at runtime, rotated, and never written into source code, images or tickets. Repositories are scanned for accidental commits.\n6. **Change and exception management**: deviations from the baseline are documented, risk-accepted by an accountable owner and time-limited.\n\n### Data classification\n\nClassification answers two questions: *how bad would it be if this data were disclosed, altered or unavailable?* and *therefore, how must it be handled?* A typical four-level scheme for a business is:\n\n| Level | Examples | Handling rules (illustrative) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Public | Published prices, marketing material | No restrictions on disclosure; integrity checks before publication |\n| Internal | Staff directory, internal procedures | Staff access only; no public sharing |\n| Confidential | Customer records, contracts, financial forecasts | Need-to-know access; encryption in transit and at rest; external sharing only with approval |\n| Restricted | Health records, national ID numbers, payment card data, encryption keys | Strict named access; strong encryption with managed keys; logging of every access; DLP; no storage on personal devices |\n\nGood classification schemes are *simple* (staff can apply them without a manual), *owned* (each dataset has a data owner who sets its level) and *actionable* (each label maps to concrete handling rules and technical controls). Labels can be applied manually, by default at the repository level, or automatically by tools that recognise patterns such as Ghana Card numbers or card numbers.\n\n### Data protection controls\n\nClassification drives control selection:\n\n- **Encryption in transit** (TLS) for all data crossing networks; **encryption at rest** for storage, databases and backups. For restricted data, customer-managed keys in a key management service give the organisation control over rotation and revocation.\n- **Key management**: separation of keys from data, rotation, least-privilege access to keys, and logging of key use. Encryption is only as strong as the protection of its keys.\n- **Tokenisation and masking**: tokenisation removes real values from most systems; masking shows only partial values (for example the last four digits of a phone number) to support staff.\n- **Data minimisation and retention**: collect only what is needed and delete it when no longer required, which reduces the impact of any breach.\n- **DLP and monitoring**: detect bulk downloads, uploads to personal cloud storage, and emails containing restricted patterns.\n- **Backup protection**: backups contain the same data and need the same classification-driven controls (covered in the next lesson)."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Privacy by design and data protection principles\n\nGhana''s Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843), like comparable laws elsewhere, is built on principles that include accountability, lawful processing, purpose specification, data quality, openness, security safeguards and data subject participation. The *security safeguards* principle requires data controllers to take appropriate, reasonable technical and organisational measures to prevent loss, damage or unauthorised access. Classification and the controls above are how an organisation demonstrates that its measures are \"appropriate\" to the sensitivity of the data. Privacy by design extends this thinking: build minimisation, access restriction and retention limits into systems from the start rather than adding them later.\n\n### The configuration management lifecycle\n\nConfiguration security can be modelled as a loop: *define* the baseline, *implement* it through code, *verify* continuously, *remediate* drift, and *improve* the baseline as threats and platforms change. NIST SP 800-53 captures related requirements in its Configuration Management (CM) control family, including baseline configuration, configuration change control and least functionality."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Asante Pharma Distributors* (fictional) uses a cloud storage service for invoices, a SaaS customer relationship management system and an on-premises warehouse database. A CSPM tool reports the following:\n\n| Finding | Classification of data affected | Risk | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Storage bucket `asante-invoices` allows public read | Confidential (customer invoices with names and phone numbers) | High: automated scanners find such buckets quickly | Block public access at account level; review access logs for prior downloads; assess whether a breach notification duty arises |\n| Database backups unencrypted in a secondary bucket | Restricted (pharmacist licence numbers, some patient prescriptions) | High | Enable encryption with customer-managed keys; restrict bucket access to the backup role |\n| CRM allows any user to export all contacts | Confidential | Medium | Restrict export to sales managers; enable DLP alerts on large exports |\n| Development server has SSH open to the internet | Internal | Medium | Restrict to the corporate VPN; adopt a hardened baseline |\n\nThe worked lesson is that *classification turns findings into priorities*. The same misconfiguration (public read) would be low priority for a bucket of public brochures and critical for prescriptions."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Inventory data and systems\"] --> B[\"Classify: public, internal, confidential, restricted\"]\n    B --> C[\"Define handling rules per level\"]\n    C --> D[\"Implement controls: baseline, encryption, access, DLP\"]\n    D --> E[\"Monitor: CSPM, drift detection, access logs\"]\n    E --> F{\"Drift or exposure found?\"}\n    F -->|\"Yes\"| G[\"Remediate and review root cause\"]\n    G --> D\n    F -->|\"No\"| E\n```"},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"For Ghanaian organisations, data classification is a practical way to show compliance with Act 843. The Data Protection Commission oversees the Act and maintains a register of data controllers. An organisation that can show a classification scheme, an inventory of where restricted data lives, and matching controls is far better placed to respond to a complaint, a regulator''s enquiry or a breach than one that cannot say where its customer data is stored.\n\nTypical restricted data in Ghana includes Ghana Card numbers, mobile money account and transaction histories, health records and payroll data. These often spread into spreadsheets, email attachments and messaging applications. A pragmatic programme starts by finding these locations, setting a few simple rules (for example, \"never send Ghana Card images through personal messaging apps; use the approved document system\"), and applying technical controls such as DLP alerts and encryption to the main repositories. Where systems are part of Critical Information Infrastructure under the Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038), secure configuration and monitoring also support obligations to the Cyber Security Authority."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"**Uber (2016 breach, disclosed 2017).** In late 2016 attackers obtained credentials that allowed them to access a private code repository used by Uber engineers. Within it they found cloud access credentials, which they used to access stored data containing names, email addresses and phone numbers of around 57 million riders and drivers worldwide, as well as driver''s licence numbers of hundreds of thousands of drivers in the United States. Rather than disclosing the breach, the company paid the attackers $100,000 through its bug bounty programme and had them sign non-disclosure agreements. New leadership disclosed the incident in November 2017. Uber later reached settlements with US regulators and state authorities, and its former chief security officer was convicted in 2022 on charges relating to concealment of the breach.\n\nThe configuration lesson is about secrets management: long-lived cloud credentials stored in a code repository turned one compromised developer account into a mass data breach. Secrets should live in a dedicated manager, workloads should use roles with temporary credentials, and repositories should be scanned for secrets. The governance lesson is equally stark: concealment converted a security incident into a legal and reputational crisis."}],"misconceptions":["**\"Misconfiguration is a minor housekeeping issue.\"** Public storage and exposed databases have caused some of the largest data exposures on record; misconfiguration deserves the same priority as software vulnerabilities.","**\"Private repositories are a safe place for credentials.\"** Repository access can be compromised; secrets belong in a secrets manager with rotation and access logging.","**\"Classify everything as confidential to be safe.\"** Over-classification makes rules impractical, so staff ignore them; levels must be meaningful and proportionate.","**\"Encryption solves data protection.\"** Encryption without key management, access control, minimisation and monitoring leaves major gaps."],"examMistakes":["Listing classification levels without stating the handling rules and controls that follow from each.","Recommending encryption without addressing who holds and manages the keys.","Treating configuration security as a one-off hardening exercise rather than continuous monitoring for drift.","Ignoring legal context, such as data controller duties under Act 843, when discussing data protection."],"criticalQuestions":["Who should own the classification of a dataset: IT, the business unit that creates it, or the data protection officer? Justify your answer.","Can a small organisation without a security team realistically prevent cloud misconfiguration? Which two or three controls give the greatest return?","Was the decision to conceal the Uber breach a failure of individuals, of governance, or of incentives? What structural controls would prevent a repeat?"],"exercises":["**Classification scheme.** Draft a four-level classification scheme for an organisation you know (or an illustrative SME). *Expected output:* a one-page table with levels, examples, owners and handling rules for storage, sharing, retention and disposal.","**Secrets scan.** On a code repository you own, run a free secrets-scanning tool or enable your hosting platform''s secret scanning. *Expected output:* a short report of findings (even if none) and the steps you would take to rotate any exposed secret.","**Baseline review.** Download the relevant CIS Benchmark (free registration may be required) for an operating system you use and compare five recommendations with your own machine''s settings. *Expected output:* a table of recommendation, current setting, compliant yes/no and remediation."],"takeaways":["Misconfiguration is a leading cause of breaches because cloud platforms are fast, complex and exposed to automated scanning.","Baselines, infrastructure as code with review, guardrails and continuous monitoring prevent and detect drift.","Secrets must be kept out of code and managed centrally, with rotation and temporary credentials.","Data classification turns sensitivity into concrete handling rules and control priorities.","Data protection combines encryption, key management, minimisation, masking, tokenisation and DLP, aligned with Act 843 duties."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Joint Task Force. (2020). *Security and privacy controls for information systems and organizations* (NIST Special Publication 800-53, Revision 5). National Institute of Standards and Technology.","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final","verification":"verified"},{"text":"OWASP Foundation. (2021). *OWASP Top 10:2021* (A05 Security Misconfiguration).","url":"https://owasp.org/Top10/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Republic of Ghana. (2012). *Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843)*.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Stallings, W., & Brown, L. (2018). *Computer security: Principles and practice* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"CIS Benchmarks","url":"https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks","description":"Center for Internet Security. Official page for consensus-based secure configuration guides for operating systems, cloud platforms and applications.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/","description":"Microsoft. Official free training portal; search for data classification, information protection and cloud posture management modules.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Cisco Skills for All","url":"https://skillsforall.com/","description":"Cisco Networking Academy. Official learning portal with free introductory cybersecurity courses that cover data protection basics.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Misconfiguration (OWASP A05:2021) is a major breach cause, especially in the cloud. Prevent it with baselines, reviewed infrastructure as code and guardrails; detect it with CSPM and drift monitoring. Classify data so that protection is proportionate, and keep secrets out of code.\n\n- Typical levels: public, internal, confidential, restricted, each with handling rules.\n- Data owners set classification; rules must be simple and actionable.\n- Encryption needs key management: separation, rotation, restricted access, logging.\n- Minimise, mask and tokenise to shrink what can be lost.\n- Act 843 requires appropriate security safeguards for personal data.\n- Uber (2016): cloud keys in a code repository, then concealment."}'::jsonb, 2372, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('9a1f3735-d02a-576c-aa3e-db2d6e379c34', 'a010847e-43ed-5870-9355-17e182d92deb', 'backup-and-disaster-recovery', 'Backup Strategies and Disaster Recovery', 'Apply the 3-2-1 backup rule and ransomware-resilient backup design, calculate whether a recovery strategy meets its recovery point and recovery time objectives, and evaluate disaster recovery site options and testing approaches.', array['Backup strategies', '3-2-1 rule', 'Disaster recovery', 'RPO and RTO']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Every organisation will eventually lose data or systems. Disks fail, staff delete the wrong folder, floods damage server rooms, power surges destroy equipment and, increasingly, ransomware encrypts everything an attacker can reach, including backups that were left online. Prevention reduces how often this happens; backup and disaster recovery determine whether the organisation survives when it does.\n\nThis lesson treats recovery as an engineering problem with measurable targets. You will learn the 3-2-1 rule and its modern extensions, how to design backups that ransomware cannot easily destroy, and how to use recovery point objective (RPO) and recovery time objective (RTO) to decide whether a backup strategy is adequate. A worked example shows how to test a strategy against its targets with simple arithmetic, the kind of reasoning boards and auditors expect.","objectives":["Explain the 3-2-1 backup rule and its extensions for immutable and offline copies.","Compare full, incremental and differential backups and their effect on storage and restore time.","Calculate whether a backup and recovery design meets stated RPO and RTO targets.","Evaluate hot, warm and cold disaster recovery sites and cloud-based recovery options.","Design a backup testing regime that provides evidence of recoverability."],"prerequisites":["Ransomware concepts from Module 2.","Data classification from the previous lesson.","The availability element of the CIA triad."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"3-2-1 rule","definition":"Keep at least three copies of data, on two different types of media or storage, with one copy off-site."},{"term":"Immutable backup","definition":"A backup that cannot be altered or deleted for a defined retention period, even by an administrator, typically using write-once storage or object lock features."},{"term":"Air-gapped (offline) backup","definition":"A copy that is physically or logically disconnected from production networks, so an attacker who compromises the network cannot reach it."},{"term":"Recovery point objective (RPO)","definition":"The maximum acceptable amount of data loss, measured as time between the last recoverable copy and the disruption."},{"term":"Recovery time objective (RTO)","definition":"The maximum acceptable time to restore a system or process to an agreed level of service after a disruption."},{"term":"Maximum tolerable downtime (MTD)","definition":"The total time a business process can be unavailable before the organisation suffers unacceptable harm; RTO must be shorter than MTD."},{"term":"Disaster recovery (DR)","definition":"The technical plans and capabilities for restoring IT systems and data after a disruptive event."},{"term":"Failover","definition":"Switching operations from a primary system or site to a standby system or site."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The 3-2-1 rule and its extensions\n\nThe 3-2-1 rule is a simple heuristic that protects against most common failure modes. *Three copies* (production plus two backups) protect against a single backup being corrupt. *Two media types* (for example disk and cloud object storage, or disk and tape) protect against a fault affecting one technology. *One off-site copy* protects against fire, flood or theft at the main site.\n\nRansomware has exposed a gap: attackers deliberately seek out and destroy backups before encrypting production systems, because that maximises pressure to pay. Many organisations now apply an extended form often described as **3-2-1-1-0**: three copies, two media, one off-site, **one immutable or offline**, and **zero** errors on restore verification. The extra \"1\" ensures at least one copy survives an attacker with administrator privileges; the \"0\" insists that backups are tested, not assumed.\n\n### Backup types\n\n| Type | What is copied | Storage | Restore |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Full | All selected data | Highest | Fastest and simplest: one set |\n| Incremental | Changes since the last backup of any type | Lowest | Slowest: last full plus every incremental since |\n| Differential | Changes since the last full backup | Moderate, grows daily | Moderate: last full plus latest differential |\n\nModern systems also offer snapshots (point-in-time images of volumes or virtual machines) and continuous replication to another site. Replication gives a very low RPO but faithfully copies corruption and encryption too, so it is not a substitute for point-in-time backups.\n\n### Protecting the backups themselves\n\nBackup systems are high-value targets and should be treated as restricted assets: separate administrative credentials (not domain administrator accounts used elsewhere), MFA on the backup console, encryption of backup data with keys stored separately, network segmentation, immutability settings, and alerts when retention policies are shortened or large numbers of backups are deleted.\n\n### RPO, RTO and MTD\n\nRPO and RTO translate business tolerance into technical requirements. They should come from a business impact analysis (covered in the governance module), not from IT guesswork. RPO drives **backup frequency** (or replication); RTO drives **restore capability**: bandwidth, hardware availability, standby sites and staff procedures. Both must fit within the MTD for the business process the system supports."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Disaster recovery site strategies\n\n| Option | Description | Typical RTO | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cold site | Space and power, but equipment and data must be brought in and restored | Days to weeks | Low |\n| Warm site | Some hardware and connectivity in place; data restored from recent backups | Hours to days | Medium |\n| Hot site | Fully equipped, data replicated, ready to take over quickly | Minutes to hours | High |\n| Cloud DR | Recovery into a cloud provider, from replicated images or backups, scaled up only when needed | Varies with design | Pay mainly when used |\n\nNIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 (Swanson et al., 2010) places disaster recovery within a wider contingency planning process: develop a policy, conduct a business impact analysis, identify preventive controls, create recovery strategies, develop the plan, test and exercise it, and maintain it. ISO 22301 provides a management-system standard for business continuity into which DR fits.\n\n### Testing levels\n\nRecovery capability is only proven by testing. Levels increase in realism and cost: checklist reviews, tabletop walk-throughs, restoration of individual files, full restoration of a system to an isolated environment, parallel tests (recovery site runs alongside production) and full interruption tests (production is switched off and the recovery site takes over). Each test should record actual restore times against RTO and any data loss against RPO."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Nkabom Pharmacies* (fictional), a chain of twelve pharmacies in Greater Accra, runs a central dispensing and stock database of 2 TB. The business impact analysis sets:\n\n- **RPO = 4 hours** (losing more than four hours of prescriptions would be unacceptable for patient safety and stock control).\n- **RTO = 8 hours** (beyond this, pharmacies must revert to paper and lose sales; MTD is assessed at 24 hours).\n\n**Current design:** one full backup nightly at 01:00 to a disk array in the same server room, plus a weekly copy to an external drive taken home by the IT manager. Restore throughput from the disk array is measured at 200 GB per hour.\n\n**Test 1: Does it meet RPO?** Suppose the database fails at 16:00. The last backup was at 01:00, so data loss = 16:00 − 01:00 = **15 hours**, far above the 4-hour RPO. Worst case (failure just before 01:00) is almost **24 hours**. The design fails RPO.\n\n**Test 2: Does it meet RTO?** Restore time = 2,000 GB ÷ 200 GB/hour = **10 hours**, plus an estimated 2 hours to provision hardware, verify integrity and reconnect pharmacies = **12 hours**, above the 8-hour RTO. If the server room itself is lost (fire), the only remaining copy is the weekly external drive, giving up to seven days of data loss and an unknown recovery time while new hardware is sourced. The design fails RTO and is not 3-2-1 compliant (the second copy is on similar media and sometimes in the same building).\n\n**Improved design:**\n\n1. Keep the nightly full backup but add incremental backups every 3 hours, giving a worst-case data loss of about 3 hours, within RPO.\n2. Copy backups automatically to cloud object storage in another region with object lock enabled for 30 days (off-site, different medium, immutable).\n3. Maintain a warm standby database server at a second location (or in the cloud), restored from backups daily, so that only the latest incremental changes need applying. Estimated recovery = 1 hour to apply incrementals + 1.5 hours to switch connections and verify = **2.5 hours**, within RTO.\n4. Protect the backup console with separate credentials and MFA; alert on deletion attempts.\n5. Test restores monthly for files and quarterly for a full failover, recording actual times.\n\nThe arithmetic makes the business case explicit: management can see precisely why the old design was inadequate and what the additional spending buys."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    P[\"Production database\"] -->|\"Nightly full plus 3-hourly incremental\"| B1[\"Local backup array (copy 2)\"]\n    B1 -->|\"Automated replication\"| B2[\"Cloud object storage with object lock, other region (copy 3, off-site, immutable)\"]\n    B2 -->|\"Daily restore\"| W[\"Warm standby server\"]\n    W -->|\"Failover when disaster declared\"| S[\"Pharmacies reconnect\"]\n    B1 -.->|\"Monthly and quarterly restore tests\"| T[\"Test report: actual RPO and RTO\"]\n```"},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Availability risks in Ghana include power disruptions and voltage fluctuations, which damage hardware and corrupt data if systems shut down uncleanly, as well as flooding in some urban areas during the rainy season, fire and theft. Uninterruptible power supplies, generators and surge protection are part of the resilience picture, but none replace off-site, tested backups.\n\nCloud object storage with immutability features gives small and medium-sized Ghanaian businesses an affordable way to achieve an off-site, ransomware-resistant copy without building a second data centre, subject to bandwidth for the initial upload and restore, and to data protection considerations under Act 843 regarding where personal data is stored. Organisations that operate Critical Information Infrastructure under the Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038) should consider recovery capability as part of their resilience and incident obligations, and regulated sectors such as banking should check their regulator''s specific continuity expectations."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"**A.P. Møller-Maersk and NotPetya, 2017.** In June 2017 the NotPetya malware, which spread initially through a compromised update of Ukrainian accounting software and then propagated rapidly across networks, severely disrupted the Danish shipping and logistics company Maersk. NotPetya presented itself as ransomware but was effectively destructive: it rendered encrypted systems unrecoverable. Maersk''s global operations were heavily disrupted, and the company reported costs in the hundreds of millions of US dollars.\n\nA widely reported detail is directly relevant to this lesson and to Ghana. Maersk''s Active Directory domain controllers, essential for authenticating users and systems, were synchronised with one another, so the malware destroyed them all. According to public accounts, including a detailed 2018 report in *Wired*, a single domain controller in Maersk''s office in Ghana survived because it had been offline due to a local power outage. Its disk was carried to the recovery team in the United Kingdom and used to rebuild the company''s directory, enabling the wider recovery.\n\nThe lessons are sobering. Replication is not backup: synchronised copies were destroyed together. Identity infrastructure needs its own offline, tested backups. And the company''s recovery depended partly on chance rather than design, which a 3-2-1-1-0 strategy with an immutable or offline copy of critical systems is intended to prevent."}],"misconceptions":["**\"Replication or RAID is a backup.\"** They protect against hardware failure but faithfully copy deletion, corruption and encryption; point-in-time, isolated backups are still needed.","**\"Cloud data is automatically backed up.\"** Under the shared responsibility model, backup and retention configuration for customer data is usually the customer''s responsibility, even in SaaS.","**\"If backups run successfully, we can restore.\"** A successful job does not prove restorability; only regular test restores, timed against RTO, provide evidence.","**\"A low RPO is always better.\"** Lower RPOs cost more; the right RPO is the one justified by business impact, not the smallest possible."],"examMistakes":["Defining RPO and RTO correctly but failing to calculate whether a scenario meets them.","Confusing RPO (data loss, drives backup frequency) with RTO (downtime, drives restore capability).","Proposing 3-2-1 without addressing ransomware''s targeting of online backups.","Omitting testing from a disaster recovery recommendation.","Choosing a hot site for every system without weighing cost against business impact."],"criticalQuestions":["How should an organisation decide which systems justify a hot site and which can tolerate a cold-site or rebuild approach?","The Maersk recovery relied partly on luck. What would a deliberately designed equivalent of the \"Ghana domain controller\" look like for a mid-sized company?","If cloud backups are immutable for 30 days but an attacker remains undetected for 45 days, what risk remains and how could it be mitigated?"],"exercises":["**Personal 3-2-1-1-0 audit.** Map where copies of your own important files (for example your dissertation drafts) are kept. *Expected output:* a diagram of copies, media and locations, a gap analysis against 3-2-1-1-0, and one improvement you implement.","**RPO/RTO calculation.** For an illustrative 500 GB database with nightly backups and a restore throughput of 100 GB per hour, calculate worst-case data loss and restore time, then propose changes to meet an RPO of 1 hour and an RTO of 4 hours. *Expected output:* calculations shown step by step and a justified design.","**Restore test.** On a computer you own, restore a folder from your backup tool to a different location and time it. *Expected output:* a short test record showing date, data restored, time taken, integrity check and issues found."],"takeaways":["3-2-1 protects against common failures; 3-2-1-1-0 adds an immutable or offline copy and verified restores to resist ransomware.","Full, incremental and differential backups trade storage against restore complexity and speed.","RPO drives backup frequency; RTO drives restore capability; both must fit within MTD from the BIA.","DR sites range from cold to hot, with cloud DR offering flexible cost; choose by business impact.","Only regular, timed restore tests prove recoverability; Maersk (2017) shows the cost of relying on chance."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Swanson, M., Bowen, P., Phillips, A. W., Gallup, D., & Lynes, D. (2010). *Contingency planning guide for federal information systems* (NIST Special Publication 800-34, Revision 1). National Institute of Standards and Technology.","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/34/r1/upd1/final","verification":"verified"},{"text":"International Organization for Standardization. (2019). *Security and resilience, Business continuity management systems, Requirements* (ISO 22301:2019). ISO.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Greenberg, A. (2018, August 22). The untold story of NotPetya, the most devastating cyberattack in history. *Wired*.","url":"https://www.wired.com/story/notpetya-cyberattack-ukraine-russia-code-crashed-the-world/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Stallings, W., & Brown, L. (2018). *Computer security: Principles and practice* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"CISA Stop Ransomware","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/stopransomware","description":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency. Official US government ransomware resource hub, including guidance on backups and recovery.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Cisco Skills for All","url":"https://skillsforall.com/","description":"Cisco Networking Academy. Official learning portal with free cybersecurity courses that cover availability, backup and recovery concepts.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"NIST Computer Security Resource Center","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/","description":"National Institute of Standards and Technology. Official portal for NIST security publications, including contingency planning guidance.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Backups and disaster recovery turn inevitable failures into survivable events. Apply 3-2-1, extended to 3-2-1-1-0 against ransomware, and measure designs against RPO and RTO derived from business impact. Test restores to prove capability.\n\n- RPO = maximum data loss; set backup or replication frequency to meet it.\n- RTO = maximum downtime; size restore throughput and standby capacity to meet it; RTO < MTD.\n- Worked check: data loss = failure time − last good copy; restore time = data size ÷ throughput + overheads.\n- Replication and RAID are not backups; keep isolated point-in-time copies.\n- Protect backup systems with separate credentials, MFA, encryption and immutability.\n- Maersk (2017): an offline domain controller in Ghana enabled recovery from NotPetya."}'::jsonb, 2487, 3, true)
