insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('69d06160-5476-5278-8d59-2b1212572290', 'c06717ce-0e2d-5378-955b-1742b47f7495', 'selecting-a-seminar-topic', 'Selecting and Refining a Seminar Topic', 'Choose a workable seminar topic, narrow it into a focused question, and justify its scope, relevance and feasibility.', array['Topic selection', 'Research questions', 'Scoping']::text[], 40, 'introductory', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Many weak seminars fail before the first slide is made, because the topic was too broad, too trivial, too vague or impossible to research in the time available. \"Artificial intelligence in business\" cannot be treated in fifteen minutes; neither can \"Is technology good for Ghana?\". A strong topic is narrow enough to be examined with real evidence, wide enough to contain a genuine question, and interesting enough to sustain weeks of reading.\n\nThis lesson teaches a disciplined route from a vague interest to a focused, defensible seminar question. You will learn how to generate candidate topics, test them against practical criteria, narrow them systematically, and express the result as a question or argument that guides all later work. Your department may allocate topics, offer a list or leave the choice to you. Where the topic is assigned, the same skills apply to scoping and framing it. Confirm the official arrangements before investing effort.","objectives":["Generate candidate seminar topics from course content, professional experience and current developments.","Evaluate candidate topics using criteria of scope, significance, feasibility, evidence availability and interest.","Narrow a broad topic using dimensions such as population, setting, technology, time and perspective.","Formulate a focused seminar question and a provisional argument.","Justify the choice and scope of a topic in a short rationale."],"prerequisites":["Understanding of the purpose and expectations of a seminar (earlier lessons in this course).","Basic familiarity with academic search tools such as Google Scholar."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Topic","definition":"A general area of interest, such as digital payments in small retail."},{"term":"Research question","definition":"A focused, answerable question that drives an investigation."},{"term":"Scope","definition":"The boundaries of what a study or presentation will and will not cover."},{"term":"Feasibility","definition":"Whether a topic can be completed with the time, access and skills available."},{"term":"Gap","definition":"An aspect of a subject that existing literature has not adequately addressed or resolved."},{"term":"Working title","definition":"A provisional title that is expected to change as understanding develops."},{"term":"Rationale","definition":"A short justification of why the topic matters and why it was chosen."},{"term":"Delimitation","definition":"A deliberate choice to exclude certain areas to keep the work manageable."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### From interest to topic\n\nTopics rarely arrive fully formed. Productive sources include:\n\n- **Your programme**: a concept from a lecture that you found puzzling, or a debate your lecturers mentioned.\n- **Your work experience**: a problem you have seen in an organisation, such as low adoption of a new system.\n- **Current developments**: new regulation, technology or a high-profile failure. Be careful here: a topic that is too recent may have little reliable scholarly literature.\n- **Literature itself**: the \"future research\" sections of review articles often suggest unanswered questions.\n\nA useful habit is to write down five to ten candidate topics without judging them, then test them.\n\n### Testing a candidate topic\n\nFive tests help to separate promising topics from problematic ones.\n\n| Test | Question to ask | Warning sign |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scope | Can this be treated well in the time available? | Topic is a whole field (\"e-commerce\") |\n| Significance | Does it matter to theory, practice or policy? | You cannot say who would care |\n| Evidence | Is there enough credible literature and data? | Only blogs and press releases exist |\n| Feasibility | Can I complete it with my access and skills? | Needs confidential company data you cannot obtain |\n| Interest | Will it sustain my attention? | You chose it only because it seemed easy |\n\nA topic does not need to pass every test perfectly, but a clear failure on scope or evidence should lead you to revise it.\n\n### Narrowing a topic\n\nBroad topics are narrowed by adding constraints. Common dimensions include:\n\n- **Who**: a specific population (for example, microfinance clients, bank managers, small retailers).\n- **Where**: a country, region, sector or type of organisation.\n- **What aspect**: adoption, security, cost, trust, governance, performance.\n- **Which technology or concept**: mobile money, cloud services, enterprise resource planning, data analytics.\n- **When**: a time period or stage of maturity.\n- **Which perspective**: customer, employee, regulator, investor.\n\nStarting with \"digital payments\", for example, one might narrow to \"factors influencing small retailers'' acceptance of mobile money payments in urban Ghana\". Each added constraint makes the topic more focused, but be cautious: too many constraints can leave too little literature.\n\n### From topic to question\n\nA topic names an area; a question demands an answer. Compare:\n\n- Topic: *Cybersecurity in banks.*\n- Descriptive question: *What cybersecurity measures do banks use?*\n- Analytical question: *To what extent do employee awareness programmes reduce phishing-related incidents in financial institutions, according to the available evidence?*\n- Evaluative question: *How convincing is the evidence that mandatory security training reduces phishing risk?*\n\nSeminars at postgraduate level should usually pose analytical or evaluative questions because they invite argument and judgement rather than only description. Good questions are specific, answerable using available literature, and open to more than one reasonable answer.\n\n### Arguments and provisional answers\n\nOnce you have a question, write a provisional answer, sometimes called a working thesis. For example: \"Awareness training reduces phishing incidents only when combined with technical controls and management follow-up.\" This may turn out to be wrong, and that is acceptable. Its value is that it directs your reading and gives you something to confirm, qualify or reject."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The funnel model of narrowing\n\nPicture a funnel. At the top is a broad field, then a subfield, then a topic, then a focused question. The narrowing continues until the question is answerable with a defined body of evidence. This model prevents two common errors: staying too high (a survey of everything) and dropping too low too early (a question for which no sources exist).\n\n### PICO-style framing adapted for IT and business\n\nHealthcare researchers use PICO (population, intervention, comparison, outcome) to frame answerable questions. A similar structure helps in IT and business: *In [population or setting], what is the effect of [technology or practice], compared with [alternative], on [outcome]?* For example: \"Among small retailers in Accra, how does accepting mobile money affect customer volume compared with accepting cash only?\" You will not always have a comparison, but thinking in these terms exposes missing elements.\n\n### SMART-style feasibility check\n\nProject managers use SMART criteria for objectives. Adapted to topics, ask whether the question is Specific, Measurable in terms of evidence, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound. A topic that cannot pass a feasibility check on time should be narrowed before you commit."},{"heading":"Step-by-Step Guide: Refining a topic","markdown":"1. **Confirm constraints.** Check the department''s rules on topic allocation, approval and length.\n2. **Brainstorm.** List at least eight candidate topics.\n3. **Do a quick scan.** For each, spend ten minutes on Google Scholar or a library database and note how many credible sources exist.\n4. **Apply the five tests.** Score each topic and eliminate weak ones.\n5. **Narrow the strongest.** Add constraints using the who, where, what, when and perspective dimensions.\n6. **Write the question.** Draft one analytical or evaluative question.\n7. **Draft a working thesis.** State a provisional answer.\n8. **Write a rationale.** In three to five sentences, explain why the topic matters and why it is feasible.\n9. **Seek feedback.** Share the question with a peer or lecturer and revise."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"Illustrative scenario. A student working in a bank is interested in \"AI in banking\".\n\n**Step 1: Scan.** A quick search shows thousands of results across fraud, credit scoring, chatbots, compliance and ethics. The topic is far too broad.\n\n**Step 2: Narrow.** She chooses an aspect (credit scoring), a perspective (fairness), and a context (emerging markets). Working title: *Algorithmic credit scoring and fairness in emerging markets*.\n\n**Step 3: Check evidence.** She finds academic and policy literature on algorithmic bias and on alternative data in lending, though evidence specific to Ghana appears limited. She decides to include Ghana only as an illustration and to say openly that local evidence is thin.\n\n**Step 4: Question.** *How strong is the evidence that algorithmic credit scoring using alternative data improves access to credit without introducing unfair bias in emerging markets?*\n\n**Step 5: Working thesis.** *Alternative-data scoring can widen access, but the evidence on fairness is weaker than the evidence on access, and safeguards are needed.*\n\n**Step 6: Rationale.** \"The topic is relevant to financial inclusion and to the governance of automated decisions. Sufficient peer-reviewed and policy literature exists to support an evaluative argument, and the scope is limited to one application.\"\n\nNotice how each step reduced the scope while increasing the intellectual content. Notice also that she modified the Ghana focus when the evidence did not support it, which is better than overclaiming."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Ghana offers many promising seminar topics for MSc IT for Business students, such as digital financial services, e-government and public sector digitalisation, the adoption of cloud and analytics tools by small and medium-sized enterprises, cybersecurity in organisations, data protection compliance under the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843), health information systems and digital skills. The important practical question is the evidence available. Local topics have the advantage of relevance and your own contextual knowledge, but published scholarly material specific to Ghana may be limited, and some official statistics may not be accessible or recent.\n\nA good approach is to combine strengths. You might frame an international question, for example about trust in mobile financial services, and then ask how far the findings plausibly apply in Ghana, stating clearly which claims are supported by Ghanaian evidence and which are extrapolated. Avoid assuming that a practice that is widespread is also well documented, and avoid quoting precise figures you cannot trace to an official source. If your topic touches an employer''s data or processes, check confidentiality rules before using any such material."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"Illustrative scenario. Two students at an overseas university choose the topic \"social media and business\". The first keeps the topic as stated and finds hundreds of sources, gives up on selecting among them and produces a general overview. The second, after consulting her supervisor, narrows to: \"What does empirical research suggest about the effect of customer reviews on purchase decisions for low-cost software services?\" and writes a provisional thesis that the effect is moderated by the perceived credibility of reviewers.\n\nThe second student could define search terms, set inclusion criteria and identify a small number of studies with conflicting results. Her seminar became a reasoned comparison of evidence. The first student''s seminar could only describe. The lesson is not that narrow topics are always better, but that a topic must be small enough to be mastered. A question that can be answered with a defined body of evidence converts reading into argument."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Broad field: e.g. digital finance\"] --> B[\"Subfield: e.g. mobile money\"]\n    B --> C[\"Topic: e.g. acceptance by small retailers\"]\n    C --> D[\"Focused question with scope and perspective\"]\n    D --> E[\"Working thesis\"]\n    E --> F{\"Passes scope, evidence and feasibility tests?\"}\n    F -- Yes --> G[\"Proceed to literature search\"]\n    F -- No --> H[\"Narrow or reframe\"]\n    H --> C\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**\"A broad topic gives me more to say.\"** A broad topic usually leads to superficial coverage. Depth comes from focus.","**\"The topic must be completely original.\"** A seminar topic does not need to be new; it needs a clear, well-supported question.","**\"A topic and a research question are the same.\"** A topic is an area; a question demands a specific answer.","**\"Local topics are always easier.\"** They may be more relevant, but evidence can be harder to find.","**\"My working thesis must be correct.\"** It is a provisional position that reading may revise."],"examMistakes":["Choosing a topic that is a whole field and cannot be treated in the time available.","Selecting a topic without checking that credible sources exist.","Writing a descriptive question when the task calls for analysis or evaluation.","Changing the topic late without updating the argument and slides.","Including local claims without local evidence, or quoting unverified statistics.","Failing to state delimitations, so that the audience expects coverage you will not provide."],"criticalQuestions":["Is it better to choose a topic you are passionate about or one for which evidence is abundant? What risks does each choice carry?","How would you judge whether a topic is too recent to support a scholarly seminar?","Why might a narrowly scoped question produce a stronger argument than a broad one, and when might narrowing go too far?","How would your topic and question differ if the audience were industry practitioners rather than academics?"],"exercises":["**Topic brainstorm and test.** List eight candidate topics from your programme and work experience. Score each on scope, significance, evidence, feasibility and interest (1-5). Expected output: a table with totals and a justified shortlist of two.","**Narrowing ladder.** Take one broad topic and narrow it in five steps, writing the revised topic at each step. Expected output: a five-row ladder ending in a focused topic.","**Question upgrade.** Convert a descriptive question into an analytical and an evaluative question on the same topic. Expected output: three questions labelled by type.","**Rationale paragraph.** Write a 100-word rationale for your chosen topic, covering significance, feasibility and scope. Expected output: one paragraph that states delimitations explicitly."],"takeaways":["A good seminar topic is narrow enough to master and significant enough to matter.","Test candidate topics for scope, significance, evidence, feasibility and interest.","Narrow topics by population, setting, aspect, technology, time and perspective.","Convert the topic into an analytical or evaluative question and draft a working thesis.","State your delimitations openly and be honest about gaps in the evidence.","Confirm your department''s topic arrangements before starting."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Booth, W. C., Colomb, G. G., & Williams, J. M. (2008). *The craft of research* (3rd ed.). University of Chicago Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Ridley, D. (2012). *The literature review: A step-by-step guide for students* (2nd ed.). SAGE.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Saunders, M., Lewis, P., & Thornhill, A. (2019). *Research methods for business students* (8th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Purdue Online Writing Lab","url":"https://owl.purdue.edu/","description":"Purdue University. Official writing resource portal with guidance on developing and narrowing a research topic.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Google Scholar","url":"https://scholar.google.com/","description":"Google. Search tool useful for scanning how much scholarly literature exists on a candidate topic.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Selecting a seminar topic means moving from a broad interest to a focused, answerable and feasible question. Candidate topics should be tested for scope, significance, evidence availability, feasibility and interest, then narrowed by population, setting, aspect, technology, time and perspective. Analytical or evaluative questions are preferred to descriptive ones, and a working thesis gives direction to reading. Rationale and delimitations make the choice transparent.\n\n- Test topics before committing.\n- Narrow until the question is answerable.\n- Prefer analytical or evaluative questions.\n- Draft a working thesis and expect to revise it.\n- Check that credible evidence exists.\n- Confirm department rules on topic allocation."}'::jsonb, 2385, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('438c1efd-01db-55f2-8995-e88b165e3319', 'c06717ce-0e2d-5378-955b-1742b47f7495', 'exploring-the-literature', 'Exploring the Literature for a Seminar', 'Plan an efficient literature search, evaluate the credibility of sources, and make notes that support a referenced seminar argument.', array['Literature search', 'Source evaluation', 'Note-making']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"A seminar is only as credible as the sources behind it. Yet students commonly stop at the first page of search results, rely on summaries written by other people, or collect so many articles that they cannot say what any of them contributed. Effective literature exploration is neither random browsing nor exhaustive reading. It is a planned process of searching, screening, evaluating and recording, designed to give you enough high-quality evidence to support a clear argument.\n\nThis lesson focuses on the specific needs of a seminar: limited time, a focused question and an audience that may ask where your evidence comes from. You will learn how to turn a question into search terms, use scholarly databases and citation chaining, distinguish credible from questionable sources, and keep notes that make writing and referencing straightforward. It builds on, and does not replace, any fuller instruction you have received on literature reviews in research methods.","objectives":["Convert a seminar question into search terms using synonyms, Boolean operators and filters.","Use scholarly databases, citation chaining and library resources to locate relevant literature.","Evaluate sources for authority, accuracy, currency, purpose and methodological quality.","Distinguish primary, secondary and grey literature and decide when each is appropriate.","Construct a source table and annotated notes that support later synthesis and APA 7 referencing."],"prerequisites":["A focused seminar question or working thesis (see the lesson on selecting and refining a seminar topic).","Basic understanding of what journal articles, books and reports are."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Primary source","definition":"A source reporting original research or first-hand data."},{"term":"Secondary source","definition":"A source that analyses or summarises primary sources, such as a review article or textbook."},{"term":"Grey literature","definition":"Material not published through commercial academic channels, such as reports from organisations, regulators and think tanks."},{"term":"Peer review","definition":"A process in which independent experts evaluate a manuscript before publication."},{"term":"Boolean operators","definition":"The words AND, OR and NOT used to combine search terms."},{"term":"Citation chaining","definition":"Finding further sources by following a paper''s reference list (backward) or the works that cite it (forward)."},{"term":"Predatory publisher","definition":"A publisher that charges fees without providing genuine editorial and peer-review services."},{"term":"Source table","definition":"A structured record of sources with key details, findings and quality notes."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Planning the search\n\nBefore typing anything, break your question into concepts. Suppose the question is: *How strong is the evidence that mobile money acceptance improves small retailers'' sales?* The core concepts are mobile money, small retailers and sales performance. For each concept, list alternative terms. Mobile money may also appear as mobile payments, mobile wallets or digital financial services. Small retailers may be small businesses, micro-enterprises or SMEs. Sales performance may be turnover, revenue or business performance.\n\nCombine the terms with Boolean operators. OR joins synonyms within a concept; AND joins different concepts:\n\n`(\"mobile money\" OR \"mobile payments\" OR \"mobile wallet\") AND (retailers OR \"small businesses\" OR SMEs) AND (sales OR revenue OR performance)`\n\nQuotation marks keep phrases together. A truncation symbol, often an asterisk, finds word variants (retail* finds retail, retailer and retailing), though symbols differ between databases. Run the search, scan titles, and refine. Poor results usually mean that your terms are too narrow, too broad or not the words authors use.\n\n### Where to search\n\n| Source type | Examples | Strengths | Cautions |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| General scholarly search | Google Scholar | Broad, quick, shows citation counts | Includes uneven quality; check each source |\n| Subject databases | Those subscribed to by your library | Better filters and quality control | Access depends on your institution |\n| Open access directories | DOAJ, CORE | Free full text | Quality varies; check journal standing |\n| Books and textbooks | Library catalogue | Good for theory and definitions | Can be dated |\n| Grey literature | Regulators, international bodies, central banks | Policy and local data | Not peer reviewed; consider purpose and bias |\n\nYour library''s staff can often advise on databases and access arrangements. Use them early, and check what your institution actually provides rather than assuming.\n\n### Citation chaining\n\nOnce you find one strong, relevant paper, use it as a map. **Backward chaining** examines its reference list for foundational work. **Forward chaining** uses a tool such as the \"Cited by\" link in Google Scholar to find later papers that built on or challenged it. Review articles are particularly useful starting points because they summarise a field, but you should still read key primary studies yourself before relying on a reviewer''s description.\n\n### Evaluating sources\n\nNot every source deserves equal weight. A practical set of questions is the following.\n\n- **Authority**: Who wrote it, and what are their credentials and affiliation? Is the publisher or journal reputable?\n- **Accuracy and method**: Are claims supported by evidence? Is the method described clearly enough to judge it? What are the sample size and sampling approach? Are limitations acknowledged?\n- **Currency**: Is the information still valid? In fast-moving areas such as technology, older studies may be outdated, though foundational theory can remain relevant.\n- **Relevance**: Does the source address your question, population and context?\n- **Purpose and bias**: Why was it produced? A vendor white paper may have a commercial purpose; an advocacy report may select evidence selectively.\n- **Peer review and standing of the venue**: Peer review does not guarantee quality, but it is a useful signal. Be cautious with journals you cannot verify.\n\nA source that is poor on one dimension may still be useful if you treat it properly, for example citing a company report as evidence of the company''s position rather than as neutral proof.\n\n### Primary, secondary and grey sources\n\nUse **primary sources** when you need to evaluate evidence yourself. Use **secondary sources** for overviews, definitions and connections between studies. Use **grey literature** for regulation, policy, official statistics and industry practice, noting its status. Be wary of relying on blogs, news articles and generated summaries for factual claims, and always trace a statistic to its original source rather than repeating what someone else said about it. If you cannot trace it, do not use it.\n\n### Making useful notes\n\nPoor notes lead to accidental plagiarism and wasted time. For each source record:\n\n1. Full reference details in APA 7 format.\n2. The research question or purpose.\n3. Method, sample and context.\n4. Key findings, in your own words.\n5. Strengths and limitations.\n6. How it relates to your argument, and a note of any direct quotation with its page number.\n\nA reference manager such as Zotero can store references and generate citations, but you still need to check each reference for accuracy."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The search-screen-appraise-record cycle\n\nA manageable model for seminars is a cycle of four stages. **Search** with planned terms. **Screen** titles and abstracts against simple inclusion criteria (for example, published within a given period, addresses small businesses, reports evidence). **Appraise** the shortlisted sources for quality. **Record** details and notes in a source table. If gaps appear, return to searching with revised terms. This is a lighter version of the systematic approach used in formal review methods.\n\n### The CRAAP test\n\nLibrarians commonly teach the CRAAP checklist: Currency, Relevance, Authority, Accuracy and Purpose. It is a convenient prompt for quick evaluation, though it is a screening aid and not a substitute for judging the quality of the method in an empirical paper.\n\n### Saturation as a stopping rule\n\nYou will never read everything. A practical sign that you have done enough for a seminar is saturation: new searches begin to return mainly the same authors, studies and arguments you already have, and you can answer the question \"What do we know, what is disputed and what is missing?\" with confidence."},{"heading":"Step-by-Step Guide: A seminar literature search in two to three weeks","markdown":"1. **List concepts and synonyms** from your question.\n2. **Build search strings** using AND, OR and quotation marks.\n3. **Search at least two sources**, for example Google Scholar and a library database.\n4. **Screen** by title and abstract using written inclusion criteria.\n5. **Chain** forwards and backwards from the two or three best papers.\n6. **Appraise** each shortlisted source and record strengths and limitations.\n7. **Build a source table** with 10-15 core sources, including at least a few primary studies.\n8. **Check for gaps**, such as missing perspectives or no evidence from your context, and search again.\n9. **Stop** when new results mostly repeat what you already have."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"Illustrative scenario. A student asks: *How strong is the evidence that data analytics adoption improves SME performance?*\n\nHer first search, \"data analytics SME\", returns a large and mixed set. Refining to `(\"data analytics\" OR \"business analytics\" OR \"big data\") AND (\"small and medium\" OR SME*) AND performance` improves relevance. After scanning abstracts, she shortlists twelve sources and builds a source table.\n\n| Source (illustrative labels) | Type | Method | Finding | Limitation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Study A | Journal article | Survey of firms in one country | Positive association with performance | Cross-sectional; self-reported performance |\n| Study B | Journal article | Interviews with managers | Skills shortage limits benefit | Small sample |\n| Report C | Consultancy white paper | Opinion and examples | Claims large gains | Commercial interest; method unclear |\n| Review D | Review article | Synthesis of studies | Mixed evidence | Depends on included studies |\n\nFrom the table she can already state an argument: evidence for a positive association exists but mostly rests on self-reported cross-sectional data, and commercially produced claims should be treated cautiously. She notes that Report C should be used only as evidence of what vendors claim. The gaps she identifies, such as limited evidence from West Africa, become something she can acknowledge honestly."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"A student researching Ghanaian IT and business topics will often find that scholarly evidence is uneven. Some areas, such as mobile financial services, have a growing body of research; others have little. Official and institutional sources, such as publications from regulators, the central bank and statistical agencies, can be valuable for context, but you should cite the originating document and date, and check whether it is still current. Where you cannot find a Ghana-specific source for a claim, say so and use international evidence with an explicit caveat.\n\nLocal libraries and university repositories may hold theses and reports not found in international databases. These can be useful starting points, but treat their quality like any other source. Be sceptical of statistics circulated on social media or in news summaries; trace them to the original organisation and release. Finally, if you obtain information from a company or an interview, ensure you have permission to use it and follow your institution''s ethical rules."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"In the years after 2010, Jeffrey Beall, a librarian at the University of Colorado Denver, compiled and publicised a list of publishers and journals he considered potentially predatory, meaning that they charged authors fees while offering little or no genuine peer review. The list attracted both support and criticism, and it was taken offline in January 2017. The episode is well documented in the scholarly publishing press and illustrates two things relevant to seminar preparation.\n\nFirst, the existence of a journal and of a polished article layout is not evidence of quality. Researchers and students need their own criteria for assessing a venue, such as checking editorial boards, indexing in recognised directories, transparency about peer review and the reputation of the publisher. Second, no single list or label should replace judgement. Lists can be incomplete or contested, and a journal''s quality can change. A student building a source table should therefore record why each source was trusted, so that if challenged in the seminar (\"How do you know this journal is credible?\") the answer rests on reasoning rather than on appearance."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Focused question\"] --> B[\"Concepts and synonyms\"]\n    B --> C[\"Search strings with AND / OR\"]\n    C --> D[\"Search databases\"]\n    D --> E[\"Screen titles and abstracts\"]\n    E --> F[\"Chain forward and backward\"]\n    F --> G[\"Appraise quality\"]\n    G --> H[\"Record in source table\"]\n    H --> I{\"Gaps or repetition?\"}\n    I -- Gaps --> C\n    I -- Repetition --> J[\"Stop: begin synthesis\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**\"The first page of results is the best evidence.\"** Search ranking reflects relevance signals and popularity, not necessarily quality.","**\"Peer-reviewed means correct.\"** Peer review improves quality control but does not guarantee accuracy.","**\"Newer is always better.\"** Recent work is important in fast-moving fields, but foundational theory and methods papers may be older.","**\"Reading abstracts is enough.\"** Abstracts can omit limitations. Read the method and findings of key sources.","**\"A statistic quoted in several places must be true.\"** Repetition is not verification; trace it to the original source."],"examMistakes":["Citing a source you have not read, relying on someone else''s summary of it.","Using a single database or only the first page of results.","Treating vendor reports and blog posts as neutral evidence.","Failing to record reference details at the time of reading, then guessing later.","Presenting a list of sources without comparing their quality or findings.","Quoting precise statistics with no traceable original source."],"criticalQuestions":["When might a non-peer-reviewed source such as a regulator''s report be more appropriate than a journal article?","How would you decide whether to trust a study whose sample is small but whose method is carefully described?","What are the risks of relying on a single review article for a whole seminar?","How can you tell that you have read enough, and what could make that judgement wrong?"],"exercises":["**Search string design.** Take your seminar question, identify three concepts, list at least three synonyms for each and write a Boolean search string. Expected output: a table of concepts and synonyms plus a final search string.","**Source appraisal.** Choose three sources on one topic, including at least one that is not a journal article, and appraise each using authority, accuracy, currency, relevance and purpose. Expected output: a short appraisal table with a recommendation on how each should be used.","**Citation chaining.** Starting from one strong paper, find three sources through its reference list and three through \"Cited by\". Expected output: a list of six sources with a note on what each adds.","**Source table.** Build a table of at least eight sources with reference, method, findings, limitations and relevance. Expected output: a completed table with APA 7 references and a one-sentence statement of the gap you can see."],"takeaways":["A literature search should be planned from concepts, synonyms and Boolean logic.","Use more than one search source and follow citation chains in both directions.","Evaluate authority, method, currency, relevance and purpose, not just the venue.","Distinguish primary, secondary and grey literature and use each appropriately.","Record notes and full references as you read.","Stop when searches saturate, and be honest about gaps in the evidence."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Ridley, D. (2012). *The literature review: A step-by-step guide for students* (2nd ed.). SAGE.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Booth, W. C., Colomb, G. G., & Williams, J. M. (2008). *The craft of research* (3rd ed.). University of Chicago Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"American Psychological Association. (2020). *Publication manual of the American Psychological Association* (7th ed.). American Psychological Association.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Google Scholar","url":"https://scholar.google.com/","description":"Google. Scholarly search engine with \"Cited by\" links for forward citation chaining.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Directory of Open Access Journals","url":"https://doaj.org/","description":"DOAJ. Directory of open access journals, useful for checking journal standing.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"Purdue Online Writing Lab","url":"https://owl.purdue.edu/","description":"Purdue University. Official writing resource portal with guidance on research and source evaluation.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Exploring the literature for a seminar means planning searches from concepts and synonyms, combining terms with Boolean operators, searching more than one source and following citation chains. Sources are evaluated for authority, accuracy, currency, relevance and purpose, and primary, secondary and grey literature serve different roles. Good notes and a source table, with full references recorded as you read, make later synthesis and referencing straightforward. Stopping when results saturate and admitting gaps honestly keeps the argument credible.\n\n- Plan searches from concepts and synonyms.\n- Use AND to combine and OR to widen.\n- Chain citations forward and backward.\n- Appraise quality, not just relevance.\n- Trace statistics to their original source.\n- Record references as you read."}'::jsonb, 2592, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('4cabb8f6-f69c-5ad4-9346-68ea870cfa00', 'c06717ce-0e2d-5378-955b-1742b47f7495', 'critical-analysis-and-argument', 'Critical Analysis and Building an Academic Argument', 'Evaluate sources critically, synthesise them across studies, and construct a reasoned, evidence-based argument for a seminar.', array['Critical analysis', 'Argumentation', 'Synthesis']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"By this stage you have a focused question and a set of credible sources. The remaining challenge is the one that most distinguishes postgraduate work from undergraduate summary: turning sources into an argument. A seminar that lists what Author A found, then what Author B found, then what Author C found, shows reading but not thinking. A seminar that asks which findings are most trustworthy, why they differ, what they jointly imply and what remains uncertain shows analysis, and that is what audiences and assessors reward.\n\nThis lesson explains what critical analysis is and is not, how to appraise the reasoning and evidence in a source, how to synthesise across sources, and how to build and test an argument. It introduces practical tools including the Toulmin model of argument, a synthesis matrix and a checklist of common reasoning fallacies. The goal is not cynicism about sources but disciplined judgement: being fair, specific and proportionate.","objectives":["Explain what critical analysis means and distinguish it from criticism and from description.","Appraise the claim, evidence, method and reasoning of a source.","Construct a synthesis matrix and use it to compare studies thematically.","Build an argument using claim, evidence, warrant and qualification, and anticipate counter-arguments.","Identify common reasoning fallacies in sources and in your own work.","Evaluate the strength of a conclusion and state it with proportionate confidence."],"prerequisites":["A seminar question and a set of appraised sources (see the previous lessons in this module).","Awareness of the difference between correlation and causation."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Critical analysis","definition":"The systematic examination of claims, evidence and reasoning to judge their strength and limits."},{"term":"Argument","definition":"A claim supported by reasons and evidence, intended to justify a conclusion."},{"term":"Claim","definition":"A statement that you ask the audience to accept."},{"term":"Warrant","definition":"The reasoning that links evidence to a claim."},{"term":"Synthesis","definition":"Combining ideas from several sources to create a new, organised understanding, rather than reporting each in turn."},{"term":"Counter-argument","definition":"A reasonable opposing view or piece of evidence that challenges your claim."},{"term":"Fallacy","definition":"A flaw in reasoning that makes an argument appear stronger than it is."},{"term":"Qualification","definition":"A statement of the conditions or limits under which a claim holds."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### What critical analysis is\n\n\"Critical\" in the academic sense does not mean negative. It means examining something carefully to decide how far to trust it. A critical reader asks: What is being claimed? What is the evidence? Does the method justify the conclusion? What else could explain the result? What is missing? How does this compare with other work? Strengths are as important as weaknesses, and a good critical judgement is balanced and specific.\n\nThree levels of engagement can be distinguished.\n\n| Level | Typical sentence | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| Description | \"Author X surveyed 250 firms and found a positive link.\" | Necessary but not enough |\n| Evaluation | \"The survey is large, but performance was self-reported, which may inflate the link.\" | Judges quality |\n| Synthesis and argument | \"Across three studies the link appears consistently, but all rely on self-reported data, so the case for a causal effect remains weak.\" | Builds a position |\n\n### Questions for appraising a source\n\nAppraise the logic and evidence, not only the credentials of the author.\n\n- **Claim and question**: What exactly is claimed, and does it answer a question relevant to yours?\n- **Design and method**: Is the design appropriate? A cross-sectional survey can show association but struggles to show cause.\n- **Sample and context**: Who was studied, how were they selected and does the context resemble yours?\n- **Measures**: Are the constructs measured validly and reliably?\n- **Analysis and inference**: Do the conclusions follow from the results, or do they overreach?\n- **Alternative explanations**: What other factors, such as confounders, selection effects or timing, could explain the findings?\n- **Limitations and bias**: Does the author acknowledge limitations? Are there conflicts of interest?\n- **Contribution**: What does it add to the field?\n\n### Synthesis: moving from sources to themes\n\nSynthesis organises ideas by theme, not by source. A **synthesis matrix** is a simple table with sources down one side and themes or questions across the top. Filling it in reveals patterns: where studies agree, where they conflict, which contexts are covered and where evidence is missing.\n\n| Source | Finding on benefits | Method | Context | Main limitation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Study A | Positive | Survey | Large firms | Self-report |\n| Study B | Mixed | Case study | SMEs | Single case |\n| Study C | Positive | Survey | SMEs | Cross-sectional |\n\nFrom such a matrix you can write synthesis sentences: \"Two of three studies report positive effects, but only Study C focuses on SMEs and it relies on cross-sectional data.\" Notice that the sentence compares, qualifies and points to a gap.\n\n### Explaining disagreement\n\nWhen studies disagree, resist the temptation to pick a side silently. Possible explanations include different definitions, different populations, different methods, different time periods and different measures. A strong seminar says: \"The conflict may be explained by...\" and then examines which explanation the evidence supports. Disagreement in the literature is not a problem to hide but often the most interesting part of the argument.\n\n### Common fallacies to watch for\n\n- **Correlation presented as causation**: assuming that because two things move together, one causes the other.\n- **Hasty generalisation**: drawing a broad conclusion from a few or unrepresentative cases.\n- **Appeal to authority**: accepting a claim because of who said it rather than the evidence.\n- **Cherry-picking**: selecting only evidence that supports the preferred view.\n- **False dichotomy**: presenting two options when more exist.\n- **Straw man**: misrepresenting an opposing view so that it is easy to attack.\n- **Appeal to popularity**: assuming that a widespread belief or practice is therefore correct.\n\nCheck your own arguments for these, not only other people''s."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The Toulmin model of argument\n\nStephen Toulmin''s model, set out in *The Uses of Argument*, breaks an argument into parts that make its structure visible.\n\n- **Claim**: what you want the audience to accept.\n- **Grounds (evidence)**: the facts or data offered in support.\n- **Warrant**: the principle that connects the grounds to the claim.\n- **Backing**: support for the warrant itself.\n- **Qualifier**: words that indicate strength, such as \"probably\" or \"in most cases\".\n- **Rebuttal**: the conditions under which the claim would not hold, or the strongest objection.\n\nFor example: *Claim*: Small firms should pilot analytics before full adoption. *Grounds*: Several studies report that benefits depended on staff skills. *Warrant*: Where benefits depend on skills, firms without skills risk waste. *Qualifier*: This probably applies most to firms with limited technical staff. *Rebuttal*: Firms that can hire expertise cheaply may not need a pilot.\n\n### Booth, Colomb and Williams on argument\n\nIn *The Craft of Research*, Booth, Colomb and Williams present argument as a conversation with the reader: you state a claim, give reasons, support them with evidence, acknowledge and respond to alternatives, and explain the principle connecting evidence to claim. This view is useful for seminars because it reminds you that you are answering the audience''s likely objections, not just proving yourself right.\n\n### Evidence hierarchy and proportionate confidence\n\nNot all evidence is equally strong. Broadly, systematic reviews and well-designed studies with strong controls offer more reliable bases for causal claims than single case studies, expert opinion or vendor reports. The hierarchy should be applied with judgement, since a rich qualitative study may be the best evidence for questions about experience and meaning. The key principle is proportion: the strength of the language in your conclusion should match the strength of the evidence."},{"heading":"Step-by-Step Guide: Building an argument from sources","markdown":"1. **Restate your question** and working thesis.\n2. **Build a synthesis matrix** with sources and themes.\n3. **Identify patterns**: agreement, disagreement, gaps, and context differences.\n4. **Appraise the strongest and weakest evidence** and note why.\n5. **Draft your central claim** in one sentence, with a qualifier.\n6. **List supporting points**, each with specific evidence.\n7. **Anticipate counter-arguments** and decide how to respond.\n8. **Check for fallacies** such as overgeneralisation and cherry-picking.\n9. **State limitations and gaps** openly.\n10. **Revise the thesis** if the evidence requires it.\n11. **Write a conclusion** that is no stronger than the evidence."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"Illustrative scenario. A student''s question is: *How strong is the evidence that mandatory cybersecurity awareness training reduces phishing incidents?*\n\nHer matrix contains five illustrative studies. Three report fewer incidents after training, one reports a short-lived effect that faded after several months, and one finds no effect. Three of the five rely on employees'' self-reports; two use logged click rates from simulated phishing exercises.\n\n**Appraisal.** The logged-data studies are stronger on measurement. The study showing fading effects suggests that duration matters. The study finding no effect involved a single short session.\n\n**Pattern and explanation.** Training seems to help in the short term, with effects depending on duration and reinforcement.\n\n**Argument (Toulmin form).**\n- Claim: Mandatory training probably reduces phishing risk in the short term, but the evidence for lasting effects is limited.\n- Grounds: Three studies show reductions, two using logged data; one shows fading; one shows none.\n- Warrant: Behaviour change that is not reinforced tends to decay, so durable effects require repeated activity.\n- Qualifier: \"Probably\", \"in the short term\".\n- Rebuttal: Self-reported results may overstate effects, and simulated phishing differs from real attacks.\n\n**Conclusion.** Training is worth including, but organisations should not rely on it alone and should measure outcomes over time.\n\nThe student avoided two common errors: she did not claim that training \"works\", and she did not dismiss the contrary study without explanation. She also noted the gap that none of the studies was conducted in a Ghanaian organisation, so applicability must be argued rather than assumed."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"In Ghanaian organisations, decisions about technology are often made with limited local evidence and under pressure to follow what has worked elsewhere. Critical analysis helps managers and analysts avoid two opposite mistakes: copying a foreign solution without checking whether its conditions hold locally, and rejecting useful evidence because it comes from elsewhere. A careful analyst asks: How similar are the contexts in terms of infrastructure, regulation, skills and customer behaviour? What does the evidence actually show, and what is only assumed?\n\nThe same discipline is valuable when you read consultancy reports, vendor claims or news summaries that circulate in business settings. Check how the figures were produced, who produced them and whether they are used beyond what the evidence supports. In a seminar on a Ghanaian topic, show the audience which of your claims rest on local evidence, which on international evidence and which on reasoning by analogy, and be clear about the strength of each. This transparency is a mark of academic honesty and builds credibility."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"In 2010, the economists Carmen Reinhart and Kenneth Rogoff published a paper on public debt and economic growth, which was widely cited in public debate about government debt and austerity. In 2013, Thomas Herndon, Michael Ash and Robert Pollin, at the University of Massachusetts Amherst, published a critique after attempting to replicate the results. They reported that the original analysis had omitted some available data through a spreadsheet coding error, had used particular choices about weighting countries and years, and had selected data in ways that affected the headline result. Reinhart and Rogoff acknowledged the coding error, while defending parts of their broader conclusion. The episode is well documented in the economics literature and in the press.\n\nFor a student, the case offers several lessons about critical analysis. First, a prominent, widely cited source can contain errors, so authority alone is not enough. Second, replication and transparency of data and method allow others to test claims. Third, reasonable people can agree that an error occurred while still debating how much it changes the conclusion, so critical judgement should be proportionate rather than all-or-nothing. Finally, it shows why you should check how conclusions follow from data and, where possible, from the method description, instead of trusting a summary of the headline finding."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Sources and appraisals\"] --> B[\"Synthesis matrix by theme\"]\n    B --> C[\"Patterns, conflicts and gaps\"]\n    C --> D[\"Central claim with qualifier\"]\n    D --> E[\"Evidence for each supporting point\"]\n    D --> F[\"Counter-arguments and responses\"]\n    E --> G[\"Check for fallacies\"]\n    F --> G\n    G --> H[\"Conclusion proportionate to evidence\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**\"Being critical means finding fault.\"** It means judging strength and limits, including recognising strengths.","**\"A summary of several sources is a synthesis.\"** Synthesis organises by theme and compares; a summary reports each source separately.","**\"If experts disagree, I cannot reach a conclusion.\"** You can reach a reasoned, qualified conclusion by explaining the disagreement.","**\"A strong argument ignores opposing views.\"** Strong arguments address the best opposing evidence.","**\"Famous papers are beyond criticism.\"** Famous papers can contain errors and limitations."],"examMistakes":["Describing each source in turn rather than organising by theme.","Presenting correlation from a survey as proof of cause.","Ignoring conflicting evidence, or mentioning it without explanation.","Using strong language (\"proves\", \"clearly shows\") for modest evidence.","Criticising sources vaguely (\"this study is weak\") without saying why.","Omitting the limitations of your own argument."],"criticalQuestions":["How would you respond if the best-designed study in your sample contradicts your working thesis?","When is it reasonable to rely on a lower-quality source, and how should you signal that to the audience?","How can a student without data of their own still make an original contribution to a seminar?","What is the difference between a qualified claim and a vague one?"],"exercises":["**Source appraisal sentences.** For three sources, write one sentence of description, one of evaluation and one of synthesis connecting it to another source. Expected output: nine sentences labelled by level.","**Synthesis matrix.** Build a matrix for at least five sources with columns for finding, method, context and limitation, and write a three-sentence synthesis paragraph. Expected output: a completed matrix and paragraph.","**Toulmin map.** Write your seminar argument in Toulmin form, including a qualifier and rebuttal. Expected output: a one-page map with labelled components.","**Fallacy hunt.** Find an opinion article or a vendor report on a technology topic and identify at least three reasoning weaknesses, naming each fallacy. Expected output: a list of excerpts with a name and a brief explanation for each."],"takeaways":["Critical analysis is a balanced, specific judgement of strength and limits, not fault-finding.","Appraise claim, method, sample, measures, inference and alternative explanations.","Synthesise by theme using a matrix, and explain disagreements.","Structure arguments with claim, grounds, warrant, qualifier and rebuttal.","Anticipate counter-arguments and check for fallacies.","Match the strength of your language to the strength of your evidence."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Toulmin, S. E. (2003). *The uses of argument* (Updated ed.). Cambridge University Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Booth, W. C., Colomb, G. G., & Williams, J. M. (2008). *The craft of research* (3rd ed.). University of Chicago Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Cottrell, S. (2017). *Critical thinking skills: Effective analysis, argument and reflection* (3rd ed.). Palgrave Macmillan.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Ridley, D. (2012). *The literature review: A step-by-step guide for students* (2nd ed.). SAGE.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Purdue Online Writing Lab","url":"https://owl.purdue.edu/","description":"Purdue University. Official writing resource portal with guidance on argument, evidence and synthesis.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"APA Style","url":"https://apastyle.apa.org/","description":"American Psychological Association. Official APA Style site with guidance on clear, evidence-based writing.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Critical analysis judges the strength and limits of claims, evidence and reasoning in a balanced, specific way. A synthesis matrix lets you compare sources by theme and explain agreement, conflict and gaps. Arguments are built from a claim, supporting evidence, a warrant linking them, a qualifier and a response to counter-arguments, as in the Toulmin model. Checking for fallacies and matching the strength of language to the strength of evidence keeps conclusions credible.\n\n- Critical means judging, not condemning.\n- Synthesise by theme, not source by source.\n- Explain why studies disagree.\n- Use claim, grounds, warrant, qualifier and rebuttal.\n- Beware correlation presented as cause.\n- Keep conclusions proportionate to evidence."}'::jsonb, 2579, 3, true)
