insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('4760665a-b00e-5dee-ad81-34828597d4d3', '5f1c3380-9d9a-565e-863a-09eda263ca73', 'research-communication-and-delivery', 'Research Communication and Delivery Skills', 'Deliver a research presentation clearly and confidently using voice, body language, preparation and practical strategies for managing presentation anxiety.', array['Research communication', 'Delivery', 'Presentation anxiety']::text[], 40, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Excellent content can be lost in poor delivery, and modest content can be made clear by good delivery. Delivery is the set of choices a speaker makes about voice, pace, movement, eye contact, use of notes and handling of the room. These choices are learnable skills, not talents some people are born with, and most confident speakers have simply rehearsed more and learned what to do when things go wrong.\n\nDelivery also matters for a reason particular to research: your audience judges how well you understand your work partly by how you talk about it. A presenter who can explain a method without reading from the slide shows command of it. This lesson covers the practical skills of research communication, including preparing for the room, using voice and body, using notes, dealing with technical failure, and managing the anxiety that most speakers feel. It does not set any official criteria for your seminar; those come from your department.","objectives":["Explain the elements of effective spoken delivery in an academic setting.","Apply techniques for voice, pace, pausing, eye contact and posture in a rehearsed presentation.","Select and use notes in a way that supports rather than replaces natural speech.","Develop a rehearsal and contingency plan covering timing, equipment and venue.","Evaluate strategies for managing presentation anxiety and choose ones suited to your own experience."],"prerequisites":["A structured presentation and prepared slides (see the earlier lessons in this module).","Awareness of the format and time limit of your seminar.","None beyond this: no previous public speaking experience is assumed."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Delivery","definition":"The way a presentation is spoken and physically performed, as distinct from its content and slides."},{"term":"Pace","definition":"The speed at which words are spoken, usually judged against the audience''s ability to follow."},{"term":"Pause","definition":"A deliberate silence used for emphasis, for thought or to let the audience absorb a point."},{"term":"Vocal variety","definition":"Variation in pitch, volume and tempo that keeps speech clear and engaging."},{"term":"Filler words","definition":"Sounds or words such as \"um\", \"like\" and \"you know\" used to fill silence."},{"term":"Eye contact","definition":"Looking at individual audience members for the length of a thought, rather than scanning rapidly or fixing on slides."},{"term":"Rehearsal","definition":"Deliberate practice of the full presentation aloud, ideally with timing and feedback."},{"term":"Presentation anxiety","definition":"The nervousness, physical arousal and worry many people feel before and during speaking."},{"term":"Contingency plan","definition":"A prepared response to foreseeable problems such as equipment failure or reduced time."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The audience''s point of view\n\nDelivery is for the listener. Ask what the audience needs: to hear every word, to know where you are in the argument, to see that you are engaged with them and to trust your command of the material. Each technique in this lesson serves one of those needs, and the right choice depends on the room, the size of the audience and the formality of the occasion.\n\n### Voice\n\n- **Volume.** Speak so that the back of the room hears without strain. Nerves often make voices quieter, so aim a little louder than feels natural. If a microphone is available, test it in advance and hold it at a steady distance.\n- **Pace.** Nervous speakers speed up. Slow down on key claims, numbers and technical terms, and speed up slightly on familiar background. A pace that feels slow to you is often just right for listeners who are meeting the ideas for the first time.\n- **Pauses.** A pause before and after an important point gives it weight. Pausing also replaces filler words: silence feels long to the speaker but natural to the audience.\n- **Variety.** A flat tone makes even strong content hard to follow. Vary pitch and emphasis to mark the difference between a main point and an aside.\n- **Clarity.** Pronounce technical terms, acronyms and names deliberately, and expand acronyms on first use. In a multilingual setting, clear articulation and a measured pace help everyone, including listeners for whom English is an additional language.\n\n### Body and presence\n\nBody language supports meaning and signals confidence. Stand with feet steady and shoulders relaxed, face the audience rather than the screen, and use open hand gestures that match your words. Avoid pacing, rocking or fiddling with a pen or clicker. Move purposefully, for example to a different position when you change section, rather than constantly.\n\nA frequently quoted claim says that most communication is nonverbal, based on a misreading of experiments by Albert Mehrabian on feelings and attitudes in very specific conditions. Mehrabian himself has said that the figures do not apply to communication in general. The sensible conclusion is more modest: content matters, and so do voice and presence, because they affect how credible and clear the content seems.\n\n### Eye contact and engagement\n\nLook at one person at a time for a sentence or thought, then move to another part of the room. This feels more natural to the audience than sweeping glances and helps you notice whether people are following. If looking at faces makes you anxious, look at foreheads or at friendly faces at the edges of the group. Avoid reading from the screen, because turning your back to the audience weakens your voice and eye contact. Glance at the slide to check, then return to the audience.\n\n### Notes and scripts\n\nThere are three common approaches.\n\n| Approach | Strengths | Risks |\n|---|---|---|\n| Full script read aloud | Accuracy, precise timing | Flat delivery, little eye contact, lost place if interrupted |\n| Key-word notes or speaker notes | Natural speech, flexibility | Rambling if poorly prepared |\n| Memorised delivery without notes | Fluent and engaged | Collapse if memory fails; rigid |\n\nFor most seminars, key-word notes or short prompts per slide give the best balance. A useful hybrid is to script and learn your opening and closing sentences exactly, since these are the most pressured moments, and speak from prompts in between. If your department requires a script or handout, follow that.\n\n### Rehearsal\n\nRehearsal is the most reliable method of improving delivery. Effective rehearsal has stages:\n\n1. **Silent run-through** to check the logic.\n2. **Aloud with a timer**, noting where time is lost.\n3. **Standing, with slides, as in the real event**, using the remote or clicker.\n4. **Recorded** on a phone, so you can see filler words, pace and posture.\n5. **In front of one or two peers** who give specific feedback.\n6. **Final dress rehearsal** in the venue if possible.\n\nRehearse the transitions and the answers to likely questions, not only the main content. Stop rehearsing in the final hours; rest and a short warm-up (reading aloud, breathing) are more useful than another run-through.\n\n### The venue and the technology\n\nReduce uncertainty by preparing the environment.\n\n- Arrive early and test the projector, display, adapter, microphone and clicker.\n- Carry the slides in more than one form: on a USB drive, in cloud storage and as a PDF.\n- Check fonts and embedded media on the venue computer if it is not yours.\n- Know where the lights, power sockets and exits are, and check that the room allows you to see the screen and the audience.\n- Prepare a no-slides fallback: you should be able to state your central claim, three main points and conclusion without any slide.\n\n### Managing presentation anxiety\n\nNervousness before speaking is normal and common, and it is not a sign of incompetence. The body''s response to perceived threat, a fast heartbeat, dry mouth and shaking hands, is similar to the response to excitement. Strategies that many speakers find helpful fall into three groups.\n\n**Before the event.**\n- Prepare thoroughly: uncertainty about content is the largest controllable source of anxiety.\n- Rehearse in conditions that resemble the real event.\n- Visualise the opening going well and plan your first sentences.\n- Look after basics: sleep, food and water, and avoiding excess caffeine.\n\n**Just before and at the start.**\n- Slow breathing, such as breathing out for longer than breathing in, can reduce physical tension.\n- Use a short physical release, such as relaxing the shoulders, before you stand.\n- Start with the learned opening sentences, since the first 30 seconds are often the hardest.\n\n**During.**\n- Pause and breathe if you lose your place; the silence is shorter for the audience than for you.\n- Reframe nerves as energy.\n- Focus on the message and the audience''s needs rather than on how you appear.\n- If you make a mistake, correct it briefly and continue.\n\nFor people whose anxiety is severe or interferes with study, university counselling and support services are the right resource, and requests for reasonable adjustments should go through your institution''s official procedures. GradPath cannot grant adjustments or give clinical advice."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The communication triangle\n\nClassic rhetoric, associated with Aristotle, describes persuasion through *ethos* (the speaker''s credibility), *logos* (the logic of the argument) and *pathos* (the emotional connection). An academic seminar relies mainly on logos, supported by ethos, which is built by accurate content, honest limitations and professional delivery. Used responsibly, a measure of pathos, such as a relevant example, helps an audience care about the question without manipulating them.\n\n### Deliberate practice\n\nThe concept of deliberate practice describes improvement through focused, repeated work on specific weaknesses with feedback. Applied to delivery, this means isolating one thing per rehearsal, such as reducing filler words or slowing the opening, rather than repeating the whole talk without a goal.\n\n### The performance anxiety curve\n\nA long-standing idea in psychology, often called the Yerkes-Dodson relationship, holds that moderate arousal can help performance while very high arousal harms it. The practical use is as a way of thinking: some tension sharpens focus, and the aim of anxiety management is to keep it at a useful level, not to remove it."},{"heading":"Step-by-Step Guide","markdown":"1. **Define your audience and venue**: size, knowledge, language, equipment.\n2. **Choose your notes system** and prepare prompts, with the opening and closing learned exactly.\n3. **Rehearse in stages**: silent, aloud with timer, standing with slides, recorded, then in front of peers.\n4. **Review the recording** for pace, filler words, eye contact and posture, picking one improvement for the next run.\n5. **Prepare the technology and backups**, including a PDF and a no-slides version.\n6. **Plan your anxiety strategy**: breathing, warm-up, opening lines.\n7. **On the day**, arrive early, test equipment, and take a moment before starting.\n8. **During the talk**, pause at transitions, look at people, and protect the conclusion if time is short.\n9. **After the talk**, write down what went well and what to change."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** Yaw rehearses a ten-minute seminar on the adoption of electronic health records. In his first recorded run he speaks for 8 minutes, reads most of his slides, uses \"you know\" frequently, faces the screen when describing charts and has a trembling voice in the opening.\n\n**Step 1: Diagnose.** The pace is too fast, the notes are too detailed, the filler words are habitual, the posture hides his face, and the opening is unrehearsed.\n\n**Step 2: Set one goal per run.** Run 2: replace full-sentence slides with key-word prompts, and speak from them. Run 3: pause at each transition for two seconds, which also cuts filler words. Run 4: stand facing the audience and use a glance at the screen then return. \n\n**Step 3: Fix the opening.** Yaw writes the first three sentences and learns them exactly, along with the closing two sentences.\n\n**Step 4: Prepare for problems.** He saves a PDF to a USB drive and cloud storage, and writes a one-paragraph version of the talk that he can deliver without slides.\n\n**Step 5: Anxiety plan.** Before the talk he takes five slow breaths, drinks water and checks the first slide; when his voice shakes in the first lines, he pauses once, breathes and continues.\n\n**Result.** His final timed rehearsal takes 9 minutes 20 seconds in a ten-minute slot, and a peer reports that the explanations sounded natural and that he looked at the audience for most of the talk."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Presenting skills are valued in Ghanaian workplaces, where analysts and IT staff present to managers, boards, clients and community stakeholders. Audiences are often multilingual, and speakers may switch between English and local languages in informal settings. In an academic or formal professional setting, a clear, measured pace, plain vocabulary for technical terms and brief definitions of acronyms help everyone follow. Greeting the audience courteously and acknowledging those present is a customary part of formal presentations in many Ghanaian settings, and a respectful tone toward senior colleagues and assessors is expected. Follow the conventions of your own department or organisation.\n\nPractical conditions deserve planning: power interruptions, variable connectivity and equipment differences can affect a presentation. Do not depend on live internet access for a demonstration; use screenshots or recorded clips as a fallback, carry slides in several formats, and have a plan to continue without slides. Preparing for these conditions reduces anxiety because the worst cases already have an answer."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Hans Rosling and the communication of data\n\nHans Rosling (1948-2017), a Swedish physician and professor of international health, became widely known for presentations about global development data, notably his 2006 talk at the TED conference. He used animated bubble charts, produced with software developed through the Gapminder Foundation that he co-founded, to show how health and income changed across countries over decades. His presentations are often cited for the way voice, movement and data worked together: he narrated changes as the bubbles moved, drew attention to specific countries, paused for effect and used simple language for complex material.\n\nThe lesson for a seminar speaker is not to imitate his style, which was distinctive and energetic, but to notice the principles behind it. He explained a visual while it was in motion rather than leaving it unexplained, used pace and emphasis to direct attention, kept technical vocabulary light, and spoke with evident interest in the question. His example also shows that delivery is compatible with rigorous content: the talks succeeded because the data were sound and the delivery made them accessible."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Content and structure\"] --> B[\"Notes and prompts\"]\n    B --> C[\"Staged rehearsal with timer\"]\n    C --> D[\"Recording and feedback\"]\n    D --> E[\"Refine: one goal per run\"]\n    E --> F[\"Venue, technology and backups\"]\n    F --> G[\"Anxiety plan\"]\n    G --> H[\"Deliver, then reflect\"]\n    E --> C\n```\n\nThe loop between feedback, refinement and rehearsal is the engine of improvement; the later steps prepare the conditions in which the practised talk is delivered."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** Confident speakers do not feel nervous. **Correction:** Many experienced speakers feel nervous; they have strategies for using and managing the energy.","**Misconception:** Memorising a script word for word is the safest approach. **Correction:** A memorised script can collapse after one slip and often sounds flat; prompts plus a learned opening and closing are safer.","**Misconception:** Pausing makes you look unprepared. **Correction:** Deliberate pauses signal control and give the audience time to think.","**Misconception:** Speaking quickly shows command of the material. **Correction:** Speed usually reduces comprehension; clarity shows command.","**Misconception:** Body language contributes 55 per cent of every message. **Correction:** This is a misreading of narrow experiments on feelings and does not describe communication in general."],"examMistakes":["Reading slides or a script aloud with minimal eye contact.","Speaking too fast and running out of time before reaching the conclusion.","Not rehearsing with a timer or a listener.","Relying on a live internet connection or a single copy of the slides.","Using unexplained acronyms and technical terms.","Apologising repeatedly (\"sorry, this is not very good\"), which undermines credibility.","Ignoring the audience when pointing at visuals."],"criticalQuestions":["Is it ethical to use persuasive delivery techniques to make a weak argument sound strong? Where is the line between clear and manipulative?","How should delivery adapt when presenting online, where eye contact, gestures and audience feedback are limited?","In what circumstances is reading a prepared script justified, and how can its weaknesses be minimised?","How can a department make seminar assessments fair for students with high anxiety or speech differences?"],"exercises":["**Recorded run.** Record a five-minute section of your seminar and note pace, filler words, eye contact and posture. *Expected output:* a short self-assessment listing three strengths and two priorities for improvement.","**Opening and closing.** Write and learn the first three sentences and the last two sentences of your talk, and deliver them to a peer from memory. *Expected output:* the written sentences and peer feedback on clarity and confidence.","**Contingency plan.** Prepare a one-page plan for equipment failure, reduced time and an unexpected interruption. *Expected output:* a plan listing the problem, your response and what you will carry."],"takeaways":["Delivery is a set of learnable skills: voice, pace, pauses, eye contact, posture and use of notes.","Prompts with a learned opening and closing work better than full scripts for most speakers.","Staged, timed and recorded rehearsal with one goal per run is the most effective preparation.","Prepare the venue, the technology and a no-slides fallback.","Nervousness is normal; preparation, breathing and reframing help manage it.","Seek institutional support if anxiety is severe."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Alley, M. (2013). *The craft of scientific presentations* (2nd ed.). Springer.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Anderson, C. (2016). *TED talks: The official TED guide to public speaking*. Houghton Mifflin Harcourt.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Reynolds, G. (2019). *Presentation Zen: Simple ideas on presentation design and delivery* (3rd ed.). New Riders.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Gapminder","url":"https://www.gapminder.org/","description":"Gapminder Foundation. Official site with tools and talks that show data communication in practice.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"TED","url":"https://www.ted.com/","description":"TED Conferences. Official site with recorded talks for studying delivery styles.","verification":"unverified"}],"revisionSummary":"Good delivery makes research clear and credible. Control voice, pace and pauses, look at the audience, use key-word prompts with a learned opening and closing, rehearse in stages with a timer and recording, prepare the venue and backups, and manage nerves with preparation, breathing and reframing.\n\n- Slow down; pause rather than use filler words.\n- Look at individuals for a full thought.\n- Key-word notes plus learned opening and closing.\n- Rehearse aloud, timed, recorded, with peers.\n- Backups: USB, cloud, PDF, no-slides version.\n- Nerves are normal; use support services if severe."}'::jsonb, 2967, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('609d16a5-6554-558a-adc3-e5ac8a48ac4f', '5f1c3380-9d9a-565e-863a-09eda263ca73', 'handling-questions-and-discussion', 'Handling Questions and Academic Discussion', 'Anticipate, listen to and answer questions professionally, respond constructively to criticism, and contribute to academic discussion as both presenter and participant.', array['Questions and answers', 'Academic discussion', 'Responding to criticism']::text[], 40, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"For many students the question session is the most feared part of a seminar, because it cannot be scripted. Yet questions are also where understanding becomes visible. A presenter who answers a challenge by returning to the evidence, admitting what the study cannot show and thinking aloud about alternatives demonstrates the intellectual qualities that postgraduate study aims to build. Questions are not an attack on the presenter; they are the audience''s way of engaging with the argument.\n\nDiscussion is a two-way skill. When you are in the audience you are expected to ask questions and contribute, and the quality of your questions shows how well you have understood the work of others. This lesson covers how to anticipate questions, how to listen and respond in a structured way, how to deal with difficult, hostile or off-topic questions, how to respond to criticism, and how to take part in discussion respectfully. The expected format of your seminar discussion is set by your department; this lesson describes general good practice.","objectives":["Classify the common types of question asked in academic seminars and explain their purposes.","Apply a structured method to listening to, clarifying and answering questions.","Prepare responses to anticipated questions using evidence from the study and the literature.","Evaluate strategies for handling difficult, hostile and unanswerable questions professionally.","Formulate constructive questions and comments as an audience member."],"prerequisites":["A completed or well-developed seminar argument supported by literature.","Familiarity with professional conduct in seminars (see the first module of this course).","None beyond this: experience of public speaking is helpful but not required."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Clarification question","definition":"A question asking the speaker to explain something that was unclear."},{"term":"Probing question","definition":"A question that tests the reasoning, evidence or assumptions behind a claim."},{"term":"Challenge","definition":"A question or comment that disputes a claim, offering a counter-argument or counter-evidence."},{"term":"Extension question","definition":"A question that asks how the argument applies to another context or what comes next."},{"term":"Paraphrasing","definition":"Restating a question in your own words to check understanding and help the audience hear it."},{"term":"Bridging","definition":"Linking a question to a point you have already made or can make from the evidence."},{"term":"Scope","definition":"The boundaries of what a study or argument claims to cover."},{"term":"Ad hominem","definition":"An attack on the person rather than the argument; not acceptable in academic discussion."},{"term":"Backup slide","definition":"A slide prepared in advance to answer an expected question, shown only if needed."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Why questions are asked\n\nUnderstanding why a question is asked changes how it feels. Questioners usually want one of the following:\n\n- **To understand**: they missed or did not follow something.\n- **To test**: they want to see whether the claim holds under pressure.\n- **To extend**: they see a connection to another area.\n- **To contribute**: they have relevant knowledge or experience to add.\n- **To assess**: examiners need evidence of understanding that the talk alone cannot show.\n\nRarely is the main purpose to humiliate. Even a sharp question usually reflects interest in the topic. Assuming good faith, unless clear evidence suggests otherwise, helps you stay calm and respond to the substance.\n\n### Types of question and typical responses\n\n| Type | Example | Response approach |\n|---|---|---|\n| Clarification | \"What did you mean by trust?\" | Define briefly, then link to the evidence |\n| Methodological | \"Why did you choose a survey?\" | State the reasoning and acknowledge trade-offs |\n| Evidence | \"What is the basis for that claim?\" | Cite the source and its strength, or admit the limit |\n| Scope | \"Does this apply outside Accra?\" | State what the evidence covers; mark inference as inference |\n| Alternative | \"Have you considered X?\" | Acknowledge, compare, explain why you did or did not use it |\n| Challenge | \"Your conclusion ignores Y.\" | Concede valid points, defend with evidence, or note disagreement |\n| Practical | \"What should firms do?\" | Give a qualified recommendation tied to findings |\n| Off-topic | A question about an unrelated area | Acknowledge, give a brief answer or offer to discuss afterwards |\n\n### A step-by-step method for answering\n\n1. **Listen completely.** Do not interrupt or begin forming your reply before the questioner finishes. Note key words if the question is long.\n2. **Pause.** A two-second pause shows that you are thinking, and it protects you from answering a different question from the one asked.\n3. **Clarify or paraphrase.** \"If I understand correctly, you are asking whether...\" confirms the question, helps everyone hear it, and gives you time. If a question has several parts, ask which the questioner would like first or answer them in order.\n4. **Answer directly.** Give the answer in the first sentence, then the reasoning and evidence. Avoid long background that delays the point.\n5. **Be honest about limits.** If you do not know, say so, explain what you do know that is relevant and how you could find out. A confident \"I do not know, but here is how I would find out\" is better than a guess.\n6. **Check.** \"Does that address your question?\" lets the questioner redirect and ends the exchange politely.\n7. **Be concise.** Answers of around a minute are usually enough. Longer answers invite follow-up and use up time for other questions.\n\n### Preparing for questions\n\nPreparation is the main protection against panic.\n\n- **List likely questions**: on definitions, method, sample and sources, claims and scope, limitations, contribution and practice. Ask peers for the hardest questions they can think of.\n- **Draft short answers** with the evidence you would cite, in your own words.\n- **Prepare backup slides** for detailed data, methods or alternative interpretations.\n- **Know your weakest point** and decide how you will describe it honestly before anyone raises it. Naming limitations in the talk often removes the sting from a challenge.\n- **Know the literature around your claim**, including the strongest opposing view.\n\n### Handling difficult situations\n\n**A question you cannot answer.** Do not bluff. Say what you do not know, what the evidence does show and what would be needed to answer fully. Offer to follow up if appropriate.\n\n**A hostile or dismissive tone.** Respond to the content, not the tone. Remain courteous, restate the substantive question, and answer it. If the exchange becomes unprofessional, the chair or lecturer has the responsibility to intervene, and you can say \"I would be glad to continue this discussion after the session.\"\n\n**A long statement instead of a question.** Find the implied question: \"Is your question whether the pattern also holds in rural settings?\" Then answer that.\n\n**A question that exposes a real mistake.** Acknowledge it plainly (\"You are right, that figure is wrong; thank you for catching it\"), explain how it affects the conclusion, and correct it. Correcting an error openly raises credibility.\n\n**Repeated challenges from the same person.** Acknowledge their point, state that you may see it differently, and suggest continuing the discussion later so that others can contribute.\n\n**A silent room.** Have a seed question ready: \"A question I am often asked is...\". This can start discussion without asking you to invent a question on the spot.\n\n**A leading or loaded question.** Notice the assumption and restate the question without it: \"Your question assumes that X; the evidence I have shows Y.\"\n\n### Responding to criticism\n\nA challenge can be valid, partly valid or mistaken. Respond by assessing it:\n\n- **Valid**: concede the point, state its effect on your conclusions and what you would do differently.\n- **Partly valid**: acknowledge the part that holds and explain why the rest does not, with evidence.\n- **Mistaken**: explain respectfully and offer the evidence that addresses it.\n- **A difference of interpretation**: state that reasonable scholars read this evidence differently, and say why you prefer your reading.\n\nDefensive responses tend to harm a presenter''s credibility more than the original criticism did. Stone and Heen (2014) point out that people often respond to feedback defensively because of how it makes them feel, and that separating the feeling from the content helps you learn from it.\n\n### Participating in discussion as an audience member\n\nGood participation is part of seminar conduct and is often valued by assessors. To contribute well:\n\n- **Listen actively** and take notes on points you may wish to raise.\n- **Ask one question at a time**, specific and linked to the speaker''s content (\"On slide 6, you reported... how did you handle...?\").\n- **Be constructive**: question the argument, not the person, and acknowledge what was strong before raising a concern.\n- **Avoid showing off**: a question should seek understanding or advance the discussion, not display your knowledge.\n- **Link to others'' points**: \"Building on what was said earlier...\" shows listening.\n- **Respect time**: be brief so that others can speak.\n- **Accept answers graciously**, including when they do not satisfy you; you can note disagreement and move on."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Argumentation: claim, grounds, warrant, rebuttal\n\nToulmin''s model of argument describes a claim supported by grounds (evidence) and a warrant (the reasoning connecting them), qualified where appropriate and open to rebuttal. It is useful in a question session because most challenges target one part: the evidence is weak, the reasoning does not follow, or exceptions exist. Identifying which part is being questioned helps you respond precisely.\n\n### The principle of charity\n\nIn philosophy and debate, the principle of charity asks you to interpret an opponent''s argument in its strongest reasonable form before responding. Applied to questions, it means restating a challenge fairly, even improving it, before answering. It models intellectual honesty and often defuses tension.\n\n### Blameless review in technology practice\n\nThe software industry''s practice of blameless post-incident reviews, described in the Google site reliability engineering literature (Beyer et al., 2016), focuses on understanding what happened and what to change rather than on blaming individuals. It offers a useful attitude for academic criticism: treat a flaw as information about the work."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** Akosua presents a literature-based seminar arguing that trust in providers is a stronger driver of mobile money use among small traders than transaction cost. During questions:\n\n**Question 1 (clarification).** \"What do you mean by trust here?\" She answers directly: \"I am using trust to mean a trader''s belief that the provider will keep funds safe and execute transactions correctly, as most of the studies I reviewed define it,\" and then adds that a few studies use narrower definitions.\n\n**Question 2 (challenge).** \"Your sources are mostly from other countries, so how can you say anything about Ghana?\" Akosua pauses, then replies: \"That is a fair limitation. The evidence I reviewed from Ghana is limited, so my claim is that the pattern is consistent with the international findings, and I flag the Ghanaian gap as a research need. I would not claim it is established locally.\" She concedes the valid part and keeps her claim proportionate.\n\n**Question 3 (long statement).** A participant speaks for two minutes on regulation. Akosua responds: \"If I understand, you are asking whether regulation could change the balance between trust and cost?\" The participant agrees. She answers briefly: the studies she reviewed did not examine this, so she would treat it as a promising extension.\n\n**Question 4 (unknown).** \"Which study first measured trust this way?\" She does not remember. She says, \"I do not recall the original source; I can check my notes and send it to you after the session.\"\n\n**Question 5 (hostile).** \"This is just a summary of other people''s work.\" She stays calm: \"The synthesis is the contribution: I compared how the studies measured trust and found that few measured it consistently, which is what my argument rests on. I am glad to discuss alternative approaches afterwards.\""},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"In Ghanaian professional and academic settings, courtesy and respect for seniority are part of the culture of discussion. Addressing assessors and senior colleagues respectfully, thanking questioners and avoiding a combative tone are expected in many settings. Respectful does not mean uncritical: well-framed, evidence-based disagreement is welcome in academic and professional life, and the techniques here help you disagree politely.\n\nIn workplace presentations, questions often come from managers who want to know cost, risk and practical consequences rather than methodological detail. Prepare short, business-oriented answers: what is the benefit, what is the risk, what is the evidence, and what would you recommend? Similarly, when you question a colleague''s proposal, linking your concern to the organisation''s goals (\"How would this affect customer onboarding time?\") is more effective than abstract criticism. In group or multilingual settings, rephrase questions slowly, invite clarification and check understanding, so that no participant is excluded by language."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Agile retrospectives and blameless post-incident reviews\n\nIn software development, many teams use retrospectives, structured meetings at the end of an iteration where the team examines what went well, what did not and what to change. Derby and Larsen (2006) describe a framework for running them, in which participants set the stage, gather data, generate insights, decide what to do and close the meeting. The emphasis is on learning rather than blame. Similarly, Beyer et al. (2016) describe a practice in Google''s site reliability engineering of conducting post-incident reviews that focus on systems and processes and do not seek to blame individuals, because blame discourages people from reporting problems honestly.\n\nThe link to seminar discussion is the attitude to criticism. A technology team that treats an outage as information about the system learns faster than one that looks for a culprit. A presenter who treats a challenge as information about the work, and a questioner who frames a challenge as an attempt to improve it, create a similar environment. The case also shows that constructive critique has structure: it starts from evidence, identifies specific causes and ends with a decision about what to change, which is a good template for both answering and asking questions."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Question asked\"] --> B[\"Listen fully and pause\"]\n    B --> C[\"Paraphrase or clarify\"]\n    C --> D{\"Can I answer from the evidence?\"}\n    D -->|Yes| E[\"Answer directly, then give reasoning and evidence\"]\n    D -->|Partly| F[\"Answer what the evidence supports; mark limits\"]\n    D -->|No| G[\"Say so; explain relevant knowledge; offer to follow up\"]\n    E --> H[\"Check the answer addresses the question\"]\n    F --> H\n    G --> H\n    H --> I[\"Invite next question\"]\n```\n\nAll three routes end with a check, which keeps the exchange two-way and tells the audience that you value the questioner."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** A good presenter can answer every question. **Correction:** No one can; a proportionate, honest \"I do not know\" is a strength.","**Misconception:** A critical question means the talk went badly. **Correction:** Challenging questions usually signal engagement with the argument.","**Misconception:** You must defend your position whatever the objection. **Correction:** Conceding a valid point and adjusting your claim is good scholarship.","**Misconception:** Longer answers show greater knowledge. **Correction:** Concise, direct answers with evidence are more convincing and leave time for others.","**Misconception:** Audience members who ask hard questions are being rude. **Correction:** Constructive challenge is normal in academic discussion; rudeness lies in tone and personal attacks, not in disagreement."],"examMistakes":["Interrupting the questioner or answering a different question.","Bluffing instead of admitting that something is not known.","Becoming defensive, sarcastic or dismissive.","Giving long, rambling answers that do not state a position.","Failing to link answers to the evidence from the study or literature.","As an audience member, asking multi-part questions, making speeches or attacking the speaker personally.","Not preparing backup slides or answers on obvious weaknesses."],"criticalQuestions":["When is it better to concede a criticism immediately and when should you defend your position?","How can a chair or lecturer design a question session so that quieter students and those with less confident English contribute?","Is it fair for assessors to use questions to test whether work is the student''s own? What makes such questioning legitimate?","How should a presenter respond to a question that rests on a factual mistake by the questioner?"],"exercises":["**Question bank.** Write twelve questions your audience might ask, covering definitions, method, evidence, scope, limitations and practice, and draft a 60-second answer to each. *Expected output:* a document of twelve questions and answers, with the weakest answers marked for revision.","**Mock panel.** Ask two peers to question you for ten minutes after a rehearsal, including one deliberately difficult question. Record the session. *Expected output:* the recording or notes and a self-assessment using the step-by-step method.","**Reframing.** Take three blunt or hostile questions (invented or from experience) and rewrite each as a neutral question, then write a courteous answer. *Expected output:* three paired rewrites and answers."],"takeaways":["Questions are an opportunity to show understanding, not a test of perfection.","Listen fully, pause, paraphrase, answer directly, give evidence and check.","Prepare likely questions, backup slides and an honest account of your weakest point.","Concede valid criticism, defend with evidence where warranted, and never bluff.","Stay courteous with hostile or unclear questions, and answer the substance.","As an audience member, ask specific, constructive questions aimed at the argument."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Beyer, B., Jones, C., Petoff, J., & Murphy, N. R. (Eds.). (2016). *Site reliability engineering: How Google runs production systems*. O''Reilly Media.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Derby, E., & Larsen, D. (2006). *Agile retrospectives: Making good teams great*. Pragmatic Bookshelf.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Stone, D., & Heen, S. (2014). *Thanks for the feedback: The science and art of receiving feedback well*. Viking.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Toulmin, S. E. (2003). *The uses of argument* (Updated ed.). Cambridge University Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"TED","url":"https://www.ted.com/","description":"TED Conferences. Official site with talks and Q&A sessions for studying how speakers respond to audiences.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"Three Minute Thesis","url":"https://threeminutethesis.uq.edu.au/","description":"The University of Queensland. Official site with examples of concise research communication.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Questions test and extend an argument. Listen fully, pause, paraphrase, answer directly with evidence and check that you have answered. Prepare likely questions and backup slides, be honest about limits, concede valid criticism and stay courteous when challenged. As a participant, ask specific, constructive questions about the argument.\n\n- Types: clarification, methodological, evidence, scope, alternative, challenge, practical, off-topic.\n- Method: listen, pause, paraphrase, answer, evidence, check.\n- Never bluff; say what you know and how you would find out.\n- Respond to substance, not tone.\n- Ask one specific, constructive question at a time.\n- Follow your department''s discussion format."}'::jsonb, 2934, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('673ac622-0486-5fd2-8aab-cac6372fffe6', '5f1c3380-9d9a-565e-863a-09eda263ca73', 'feedback-reflection-and-next-steps', 'Feedback, Reflection and Developing Seminar Work Further', 'Use feedback and structured reflection to improve your presentation skills and turn seminar work into a stronger paper, proposal or dissertation chapter.', array['Feedback', 'Reflective practice', 'From seminar to paper']::text[], 40, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"A seminar does not end when the last slide is closed. The most valuable learning often comes afterwards, when you gather the feedback you received, compare it with your own impressions and decide what to change. Students who treat the seminar as a one-off performance miss this opportunity, while those who treat it as a stage in a longer process of developing a piece of research gain skills and material they can use again.\n\nThis lesson covers how to seek, interpret and act on feedback, how to reflect in a structured way, and how to develop seminar work into written research such as a literature review, a paper or the first chapters of a dissertation. It also considers how to use the seminar to build professional skills that employers and supervisors value. Your department determines the form of feedback and any assessment you receive; GradPath does not set marks, criteria or deadlines, and this lesson offers general guidance only.","objectives":["Explain the purpose of feedback and distinguish its main forms and sources.","Apply a structured method to interpret, prioritise and act on feedback.","Use a reflective model to analyse a seminar experience and set specific improvement goals.","Plan the development of seminar material into a written paper, review or dissertation component.","Evaluate how seminar learning supports later research and professional practice."],"prerequisites":["Experience of preparing or delivering a seminar presentation, or of rehearsing one.","Familiarity with the structure of academic writing from earlier study.","None beyond this: no prior experience of reflective writing is assumed."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Feedback","definition":"Information about performance that helps the recipient understand the gap between current and desired performance."},{"term":"Feedforward","definition":"Feedback focused on what to do next rather than only judging what was done."},{"term":"Formative feedback","definition":"Comments intended to support improvement, usually before or alongside final assessment."},{"term":"Summative feedback","definition":"Evaluation of a final outcome, often accompanied by a mark."},{"term":"Reflective practice","definition":"The habit of examining experience deliberately in order to learn from it."},{"term":"Reflective cycle","definition":"A structured sequence of questions, such as description, feelings, evaluation, analysis, conclusion and action plan, for analysing an experience."},{"term":"Action plan","definition":"A short list of specific, time-bound steps for improvement."},{"term":"Peer feedback","definition":"Comments from fellow students, usually based on shared criteria."},{"term":"Research pipeline","definition":"The path from seminar topic to a conference-style paper, literature review or dissertation chapter."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### What feedback is for\n\nHattie and Timperley (2007) reviewed evidence on feedback and argued that it is most effective when it helps learners answer three questions: Where am I going? How am I going? Where to next? Feedback that only says whether work was good or bad, or that comments on the person, tends to help little. Feedback about the task, the process used and the learner''s self-regulation can improve later performance.\n\nIn a seminar, useful feedback tells you whether your central claim was clear, whether the evidence supported it, whether the delivery helped or hindered and what you should do next.\n\n### Sources of feedback\n\n- **Lecturer or assessor comments**, written or spoken, which usually reflect the criteria applied.\n- **Peer comments**, which show how the talk landed for people in a similar position.\n- **Questions from the audience**, which reveal what was unclear or contested.\n- **Your own recording or notes**, which show things you cannot see while speaking.\n- **Supervisor or mentor discussion**, if you are developing the work further.\n- **Behavioural signals**, such as which slides prompted puzzled faces, which are indirect but informative.\n\nEach source has strengths and blind spots. Peers may be polite, lecturers may focus on content, and recordings capture delivery but not audience reaction. Triangulating several sources gives a more reliable picture.\n\n### Seeking useful feedback\n\nVague requests such as \"Any comments?\" tend to produce vague answers. Ask specific questions: \"Was my central claim clear by the end of the first two minutes?\", \"Which slide did you find hardest to follow?\" or \"Did my answer to the question about sampling convince you?\" Provide peers with a short form listing two or three focus areas so that their feedback addresses your development goals. Where feedback is delayed, write down your own impressions straight after the talk, before memory fades.\n\n### Receiving and interpreting feedback\n\nStrong reactions to feedback are normal. Stone and Heen (2014) explain that feedback can trigger responses to the truth of the comment, to the person giving it, or to what it implies about you. A practical response is to separate the stages:\n\n1. **Receive**: read or listen without arguing, and thank the giver.\n2. **Understand**: ask for examples or clarification if a comment is unclear. \"Needs more depth\" is hard to act on, while \"the methods slide did not say how participants were chosen\" is concrete.\n3. **Evaluate**: decide whether the comment is accurate, partly accurate or based on a different expectation. Look for patterns: a point made by three people deserves more weight than one made by one.\n4. **Prioritise**: choose the few changes that would make the greatest difference, rather than attempting everything.\n5. **Act**: turn each into a specific task.\n\nFeedback can conflict. One person may praise your detailed methods slide while another finds it too dense. In such cases, return to your purpose and audience and decide, and if the conflict concerns assessment requirements, check with your lecturer.\n\n### Reflection\n\nReflection moves beyond \"what happened\" to \"what does it mean and what will I do?\" Schon (1983) wrote of the reflective practitioner who thinks about practice while doing it and afterwards. Gibbs (1988) offered a widely used cycle of six prompts, which is helpful for structured reflection:\n\n| Stage | Question |\n|---|---|\n| Description | What happened? |\n| Feelings | What was I thinking and feeling? |\n| Evaluation | What was good and bad about the experience? |\n| Analysis | Why did things go as they did? What makes sense of it? |\n| Conclusion | What else could I have done? What did I learn? |\n| Action plan | What will I do next time, and how? |\n\nStrong reflection is specific, evidence-based and honest. \"I was nervous and it went badly\" is description. \"I rushed the second block because I had not rehearsed the transition, which meant the audience lost the link between the problem and the evidence; next time I will script the transition and time that section\" is analysis leading to action.\n\nReflection may be assessed in some programmes, for example through a reflective log, and your department will specify the format. Whatever the format, avoid generic statements and support claims with examples from the experience.\n\n### Setting improvement goals\n\nConvert feedback and reflection into a small number of goals that are specific, measurable and time-bound. Instead of \"improve my delivery\", write \"reduce filler words by recording two rehearsals and counting them before the next presentation\" or \"add a limitations slide to the structure of every presentation\". Review the goals after your next speaking opportunity.\n\n### From seminar to written research\n\nA seminar presentation is often a stepping stone to written work. The central claim, the literature you assembled and the critical argument you built are raw material for:\n\n- **A literature review** or a section of one, expanding the evidence and the synthesis.\n- **A short academic paper**, where the seminar argument becomes the thesis and the structure is adapted for reading.\n- **A research proposal**, where the gap you identified becomes the problem statement.\n- **A dissertation chapter**, if the seminar topic relates to your research area.\n- **A conference abstract or poster**, if opportunities arise.\n\nThe move from talk to text involves changes. A reader needs fuller definitions, more detailed methodology, explicit reasoning and complete citations, but does not need the repeated signposts and shortened claims a listener needs. Questions raised in the seminar often point directly to the weak parts of the argument, so they are useful guides to revision.\n\n### Checking integrity when you reuse material\n\nIf you reuse seminar material in later assessed work, check whether your programme requires you to acknowledge the earlier submission, to avoid self-plagiarism. Discuss with your supervisor or lecturer how seminar work may be developed, and keep the references and notes organised so that the move to writing is accurate."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Feedback levels\n\nHattie and Timperley (2007) distinguish feedback about the task (is it correct?), the process (how was it done?), self-regulation (how did you monitor your work?) and the self as a person (praise). They argue that the first three are most useful, and personal praise adds little. Apply this by asking for feedback at the task and process levels.\n\n### Experiential learning\n\nKolb (1984) describes learning as a cycle of concrete experience, reflective observation, abstract conceptualisation and active experimentation. A seminar is the concrete experience; reflection and feedback provide the observation; identifying principles gives the conceptualisation; and the next presentation is the experiment. The model reminds you that learning completes only when you try again with changes.\n\n### Gibbs'' reflective cycle\n\nGibbs'' (1988) model provides the six-stage structure above. It is helpful for beginners because its prompts make you describe, evaluate and plan rather than only narrate."},{"heading":"Step-by-Step Guide","markdown":"1. **Collect all feedback** within a few days: written comments, peer forms, your recording and your own notes.\n2. **Group the comments** into themes such as argument, evidence, structure, slides, delivery and discussion.\n3. **Note patterns**, strengths to keep and issues that appear more than once.\n4. **Check unclear comments** by asking the giver for an example or clarification.\n5. **Write a reflection** using a model such as Gibbs'' cycle, with specific examples.\n6. **Choose three goals** and write them as measurable actions with dates.\n7. **Decide the next step for the content**: archive, develop into a review, turn into a proposal, or discuss with a supervisor.\n8. **Make a revision plan** that lists the sections to expand, the evidence to add and the questions to answer.\n9. **Review your goals** after your next presentation and update them."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** Naa presented a seminar on data privacy practices in small online retailers. Her feedback included: from her lecturer, \"clear structure, but the evidence for the central claim was thin and the argument relied on a few sources\"; from a peer, \"I got lost in the middle section\"; and from her recording, many filler words and a rushed ending.\n\n**Step 1: Group the comments.** Argument/evidence: thin evidence, few sources. Structure: middle section unclear. Delivery: filler words and rushed ending.\n\n**Step 2: Find patterns.** The unclear middle section and the rushed ending both point to timing and transitions, which she had not rehearsed.\n\n**Step 3: Reflect (Gibbs).** Description: the middle section had four slides on legal obligations. Feelings: anxious about time. Evaluation: strong opening, weak middle. Analysis: she added content late and cut rehearsal. Conclusion: she should have cut one slide and scripted the transitions. Action: below.\n\n**Step 4: Goals.** (1) By next Friday, add at least four additional peer-reviewed or official sources to her notes and check how they support or complicate her claim. (2) In the next presentation, plan a timing table with a buffer and script every transition. (3) Record one rehearsal and count filler words, aiming to halve them.\n\n**Step 5: Development plan.** Naa decides to expand the seminar into a literature review section: she turns the central claim into a thesis statement, builds a source matrix from the new readings, and drafts a first section, using the questions from the audience to check for gaps. She notes that she must cite carefully and check her department''s rule on reusing seminar material in assessed work."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Feedback is part of professional life in Ghana as elsewhere. Performance appraisals, project reviews and client comments require the same ability to receive critique, extract what is useful and act on it. Respect for seniority in many Ghanaian workplaces can make junior staff reluctant to ask for specific or critical feedback, while senior staff may hesitate to be direct. The practices in this lesson, such as asking specific questions and thanking the giver, offer a respectful way to obtain honest and useful comments.\n\nSeminar work can also develop into outputs with local value. A literature-based seminar on digital payments, e-government, data protection or SME technology adoption in Ghana might become a short paper, a policy brief for a professional body, or the background of a dissertation proposal. Check with your supervisor which options are appropriate, and cite the local publications and datasets that you rely on. Networking at departmental events, discussing your work with lecturers and joining relevant professional associations extend the benefit, though participation in any such body is a personal choice and GradPath does not recommend specific organisations."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Hattie and Timperley''s review of feedback\n\nJohn Hattie and Helen Timperley (2007) published a widely cited review in the journal *Review of Educational Research* on the power of feedback in education. Drawing on a large body of previous studies, they argued that feedback is among the influences that most strongly affect achievement, but that its effects vary considerably, and that some forms of feedback can even reduce performance. They proposed a model in which effective feedback answers three questions (Where am I going? How am I going? Where to next?) and operates at four levels: task, process, self-regulation and self.\n\nFor a seminar student the review supports several habits. Ask for feedback that links to the goal of the task, not only to a judgement. Prefer comments about how you carried out the task and monitored your own work to general praise or criticism of ability. Treat the \"where to next\" question seriously by finishing every round of feedback with a concrete plan. The review also shows why vague comments are frustrating: they say how you are going but not where to next. Their findings are about education generally, not seminars specifically, so use them as a framework for your own judgement rather than as a rule."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Seminar delivered\"] --> B[\"Collect feedback: lecturer, peers, recording, self\"]\n    B --> C[\"Group, find patterns, clarify\"]\n    C --> D[\"Reflect: Gibbs cycle\"]\n    D --> E[\"Set three measurable goals\"]\n    E --> F[\"Next presentation or speaking task\"]\n    D --> G[\"Develop content: review, paper, proposal, chapter\"]\n    G --> H[\"Supervisor discussion and drafting\"]\n    F --> B\n    H --> B\n```\n\nBoth loops return to feedback: skills improve through repeated speaking and the research improves through drafting and comment."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** Feedback is only useful when it is positive. **Correction:** Specific critical feedback is often the most useful, provided it is accompanied by a way forward.","**Misconception:** Reflection means describing what happened. **Correction:** Reflection requires analysis of why, evaluation and a plan for action.","**Misconception:** Once the seminar is over, the work has no further use. **Correction:** Seminar research and argument are valuable raw material for later written work.","**Misconception:** You must act on every comment. **Correction:** Evaluate and prioritise; some comments reflect different preferences, and you should focus on the changes that matter most.","**Misconception:** Feelings should be left out of reflection. **Correction:** Acknowledging feelings, then analysing them, helps explain behaviour; it does not make reflection less academic."],"examMistakes":["Writing reflections that only narrate events.","Responding to feedback defensively or dismissing it without evaluation.","Setting vague goals such as \"do better next time\".","Failing to link feedback to evidence from the actual presentation.","Reusing seminar text in later submissions without checking acknowledgement rules.","Treating the seminar slides as the finished written work instead of rewriting for a reader."],"criticalQuestions":["How should you weigh feedback from a peer with less experience against that from an assessor? What makes one source more reliable than another for a specific purpose?","Does structured reflection risk becoming a formulaic exercise? How can it remain genuine?","What are the advantages and risks of building a dissertation around a topic first explored in a seminar?"],"exercises":["**Feedback analysis.** Gather all the feedback you have received on a presentation, sort it into themes and mark patterns. *Expected output:* a table of themes, supporting comments and your judgement about each.","**Gibbs reflection.** Write a reflection of 400-500 words on a presentation or rehearsal using the six stages, including at least two specific examples. *Expected output:* a completed reflection ending in an action plan.","**Goal setting.** Write three goals, each specific, measurable and dated, and decide how you will check them. *Expected output:* three goals with success criteria."],"takeaways":["Feedback is most useful when specific, task- and process-focused and linked to next steps.","Seek feedback from several sources and ask specific questions.","Receive, understand, evaluate, prioritise and act, rather than reacting defensively.","Reflection needs description, analysis and a concrete action plan.","Seminar material can be developed into written research, with attention to integrity and reader needs.","Seminar skills transfer to professional life."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Derby, E., & Larsen, D. (2006). *Agile retrospectives: Making good teams great*. Pragmatic Bookshelf.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Gibbs, G. (1988). *Learning by doing: A guide to teaching and learning methods*. Further Education Unit.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Hattie, J., & Timperley, H. (2007). The power of feedback. *Review of Educational Research, 77*(1), 81-112.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Kolb, D. A. (1984). *Experiential learning: Experience as the source of learning and development*. Prentice Hall.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Schon, D. A. (1983). *The reflective practitioner: How professionals think in action*. Basic Books.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Stone, D., & Heen, S. (2014). *Thanks for the feedback: The science and art of receiving feedback well*. Viking.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Three Minute Thesis","url":"https://threeminutethesis.uq.edu.au/","description":"The University of Queensland. Official site with examples of research communication that can prompt reflection on your own work.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"APA Style","url":"https://apastyle.apa.org/","description":"American Psychological Association. Official site with guidance on writing and citation when developing seminar work into papers.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Feedback and reflection turn a single seminar into lasting improvement. Gather feedback from several sources, interpret and prioritise it, reflect using a structured model, set measurable goals and decide how to develop the content into written research with correct acknowledgement. The skills gained transfer to professional practice.\n\n- Feedback answers: where am I going, how am I going, where to next.\n- Receive, understand, evaluate, prioritise, act.\n- Gibbs: description, feelings, evaluation, analysis, conclusion, action plan.\n- Goals: specific, measurable, dated.\n- Pipeline: seminar to review, paper, proposal or chapter.\n- Check rules on reusing submitted material."}'::jsonb, 2967, 3, true)
