insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('dd193a1b-6e35-575c-a758-94825be0030a', 'dda3c643-6999-5f37-9e2d-9741da5ee243', 'b2b-b2c-c2c-and-c2b', 'B2B, B2C, C2C and C2B E-Commerce', 'Compare business-to-business, business-to-consumer, consumer-to-consumer and consumer-to-business e-commerce by participants, transaction characteristics, technologies and risks, and apply the classification to Ghanaian and international examples.', array['B2B', 'B2C', 'C2C', 'C2B']::text[], 45, 'introductory', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"When people think of e-commerce they usually picture a consumer buying a phone or a dress online. That is only one of several models. A large share of digitally enabled trade happens between businesses: manufacturers ordering raw materials, supermarkets replenishing stock from distributors, hospitals procuring medicines through tender portals. Meanwhile, individuals sell second-hand goods to one another on classified sites, and freelancers sell their skills to companies through online work platforms.\n\nClassifying e-commerce by *who sells to whom* is not merely an academic exercise. Each model has different transaction sizes, buying processes, pricing mechanisms, payment methods, trust problems and technology needs. A payment solution that suits a consumer paying GHS 80 for a meal is unsuitable for a distributor settling a GHS 400,000 invoice on 60-day credit terms. This lesson compares the four principal models, B2B, B2C, C2C and C2B, and shows how to apply the classification analytically.","objectives":["Define B2B, B2C, C2C and C2B e-commerce and identify the participants in each.","Compare the four models on transaction size, buying process, pricing, payment and trust.","Explain the main forms of B2B e-commerce, including e-procurement, private industrial networks and B2B marketplaces.","Apply the classification to real and illustrative Ghanaian businesses.","Evaluate the risks and success factors specific to each model."],"prerequisites":["The definitions of e-commerce and e-business (Module 1).","The revenue models introduced in the online business models lesson."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"B2B (business-to-business)","definition":"E-commerce in which businesses sell goods or services to other businesses."},{"term":"B2C (business-to-consumer)","definition":"E-commerce in which businesses sell to individual consumers."},{"term":"C2C (consumer-to-consumer)","definition":"E-commerce in which individuals sell to other individuals, usually through a platform that provides listings, trust and payment services."},{"term":"C2B (consumer-to-business)","definition":"E-commerce in which individuals offer products, services or information to businesses, which pay them."},{"term":"E-procurement","definition":"The use of digital systems to manage the purchasing process, from requisition and supplier selection to ordering, receipt and payment."},{"term":"Electronic Data Interchange (EDI)","definition":"The computer-to-computer exchange of structured business documents, such as purchase orders and invoices, in standard formats."},{"term":"Private industrial network","definition":"A digital network, usually owned by a large firm, that coordinates transactions and collaboration with its own suppliers and partners."},{"term":"Escrow","definition":"An arrangement in which a trusted third party holds payment until the buyer confirms satisfactory delivery."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### B2C: business-to-consumer\n\nB2C is the most visible model. Sellers include *e-tailers* (online-only retailers), *omnichannel retailers* (physical stores with online channels), *manufacturers selling direct*, *content providers* and *service providers* such as ride-hailing and food delivery. Transactions are typically small in value, high in volume and made by individuals who decide quickly and often emotionally. Success depends on customer experience, brand trust, convenient payment and reliable delivery. Marketing costs are high because each customer must be acquired individually.\n\n### B2B: business-to-business\n\nB2B differs from B2C in almost every dimension. Orders are larger and repeated; buying involves several people (a *buying centre* of users, budget holders, procurement officers and approvers); prices are often negotiated or set by contract; payment is commonly on credit terms by bank transfer or invoice; and relationships last years. Laudon and Traver note that B2B e-commerce is far larger in value than B2C, although less visible.\n\nB2B takes several forms:\n\n1. **Direct selling by a firm** through a dedicated portal where registered business customers see contract prices and place orders.\n2. **E-procurement** systems in the buying firm that route requisitions for approval, match purchase orders against invoices and goods-received notes (*three-way matching*), and pay suppliers.\n3. **EDI and integrations** linking buyer and supplier systems directly, so that orders and invoices flow without re-keying.\n4. **B2B marketplaces (exchanges)** where many buyers and sellers in an industry meet; examples include wholesale platforms such as Alibaba.com.\n5. **Private industrial networks** owned by a large firm to coordinate its supply chain, for example a retailer sharing sales data with suppliers so that they can replenish stock.\n\n### C2C: consumer-to-consumer\n\nIn C2C, individuals sell to individuals. The platform rarely owns the goods. Its role is to provide listings, search, a reputation system, communication and sometimes payment protection. The central problem in C2C is **trust between strangers**: the buyer fears paying for goods that never arrive; the seller fears delivering goods that are never paid for. Platforms address this with ratings, identity verification, escrow-style payment holding and dispute resolution. Revenue typically comes from listing fees, commissions, promoted listings or advertising.\n\n### C2B: consumer-to-business\n\nC2B reverses the usual flow. Individuals supply value to businesses: a freelance designer sells logo work to companies through an online work platform; a photographer licenses images to a stock photo site used by businesses; a social media creator is paid by brands to promote products; consumers sell their opinions through paid survey panels. Some reverse-auction models, in which consumers name a price and businesses decide whether to accept it, are also described as C2B. Success depends on the platform verifying quality and ensuring that individuals are paid reliably.\n\n### Related models\n\nTextbooks also describe **B2G/G2B** (businesses selling to government, for example through electronic tendering), **G2C** (government services to citizens, such as online payment of fees) and **mobile commerce (m-commerce)**, which is a *channel* rather than a separate party model. In Ghana, m-commerce is especially important because many consumers access the internet mainly through smartphones."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### A comparative framework\n\nThe table below provides a structured comparison that can be reproduced in examinations.\n\n| Dimension | B2B | B2C | C2C | C2B |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Seller | Business | Business | Individual | Individual |\n| Buyer | Business | Individual consumer | Individual | Business |\n| Typical transaction value | High | Low to medium | Low | Low to medium |\n| Buying process | Rational, multi-person, approval workflows | Individual, fast, often emotional | Individual, negotiation common | Business evaluates individual''s offer |\n| Pricing | Negotiated, contract, volume tiers | Posted list prices, promotions | Seller-set, auctions or negotiation | Fee per task, licence or bid |\n| Common payment | Bank transfer, invoice on credit terms | Card, mobile money, pay on delivery | Mobile money, platform-held payment | Platform payout, bank transfer |\n| Main trust problem | Supplier reliability, contract compliance | Product quality and delivery | Fraud between strangers | Quality of work and timely payment |\n| Key technologies | ERP integration, EDI, APIs, e-procurement | Storefront, catalogue, gateway, CRM | Listings, reputation systems, messaging, escrow | Work platforms, portfolio and rating systems |\n| Example | A distributor''s reseller ordering portal | An online fashion retailer | A classified-ads site for used goods | A freelance work platform |\n\n### Transaction cost reasoning\n\nA helpful theoretical lens is **transaction cost economics**, associated with Coase and Williamson, which argues that firms choose governance structures that minimise the costs of searching, negotiating, contracting and monitoring. Digital technology reduces many of these costs. In B2B, integration reduces the cost of processing orders and invoices; in C2C, reputation systems reduce the cost of assessing an unknown seller. Analysing *which* transaction costs a model reduces helps explain its value."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    B1[\"Business seller\"] -->|\"B2B: contract orders, invoices\"| B2[\"Business buyer\"]\n    B1 -->|\"B2C: posted prices, fast checkout\"| C1[\"Consumer\"]\n    C2[\"Individual seller\"] -->|\"C2C: via platform with ratings and escrow\"| C1\n    C3[\"Individual (freelancer, creator)\"] -->|\"C2B: services, content, data\"| B2\n    P[\"Platform or intermediary\"] -.-> B1\n    P -.-> C2\n    P -.-> C3\n```\n\nThe dotted lines show that intermediaries frequently sit between parties, providing search, trust and payment services."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Ama Agro Inputs* is a hypothetical company in Techiman that distributes fertiliser, seed and crop-protection products. Its managing director wants to know which e-commerce model to prioritise. It has three groups of customers: (1) 400 agro-input retailers across the Bono East and Ashanti regions that order monthly; (2) individual smallholder farmers who visit the warehouse; and (3) a regional government programme that issues tenders for subsidised inputs.\n\n**Step 1: Classify the relationships.** Retailers are B2B; farmers are B2C; the government programme is B2G.\n\n**Step 2: Compare value.** Suppose each retailer orders an average of GHS 15,000 per month (illustrative assumption). Annual B2B value = `400 × 15,000 × 12 = GHS 72,000,000`. Even if farmers buy far more often, their individual purchases are small. The B2B channel is where digital efficiency yields most value.\n\n**Step 3: Design the B2B solution.** A reseller ordering portal or a WhatsApp-integrated ordering system should show each retailer its negotiated prices and credit limit, allow reordering of previous baskets, and generate invoices automatically. Payment options should include bank transfer and mobile money for smaller retailers, with credit terms tracked against limits.\n\n**Step 4: Address B2C separately.** For farmers, the priority is not a website but a simple channel suited to their context: USSD or SMS price information, mobile money payment at the warehouse and perhaps orders through agro-dealers rather than direct delivery.\n\n**Step 5: Evaluate.** The B2B portal reduces order processing errors, speeds up collections and improves demand visibility. Risks include low adoption by retailers accustomed to phone ordering, so training and incentives (for example faster dispatch for portal orders) are needed.\n\nThe analysis shows that classifying customers by model leads directly to different technology and payment choices."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"All four models are visible in Ghana. **B2C** includes marketplaces such as Jumia and many independent online shops and social commerce sellers, with mobile money and payment on delivery as common payment methods. **B2B** activity includes distributors taking orders from retailers digitally, manufacturers integrating with suppliers, and banks and payment providers offering bulk payment services that businesses use to pay suppliers and staff. **C2C** occurs on classified-ads websites and especially on social media groups and WhatsApp, where individuals sell used phones, cars and household goods; the trust problem is acute here, and buyers are commonly advised to inspect goods before paying. **C2B** is growing through freelancing platforms used by Ghanaian designers, developers and writers who sell services to businesses at home and abroad, and through social media creators paid by brands for promotion.\n\nGhana''s government has also expanded **G2B** and **G2C** digital services. Electronic public procurement and online payment of fees are examples of how the state participates in e-commerce. When analysing these models in Ghana, consider payment realities (mobile money versus cards), the strength of trust mechanisms and the role of informal channels such as WhatsApp that blur the boundary between C2C and small-scale B2C."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Alibaba Group: multiple models within one ecosystem\n\nAlibaba, founded in 1999 in Hangzhou, China, by Jack Ma and colleagues, illustrates how a single group can operate several e-commerce models. **Alibaba.com** began as a B2B marketplace connecting Chinese manufacturers with business buyers worldwide. **Taobao**, launched in 2003, was a C2C marketplace on which individuals and small merchants sold to consumers. **Tmall** later provided a B2C marketplace for established brands.\n\nAlibaba''s treatment of the C2C trust problem is especially instructive. In 2004 it introduced **Alipay**, which initially functioned as an escrow-style service: the buyer''s payment was held until the buyer confirmed receipt of goods, protecting both parties. This addressed the central weakness of C2C trade between strangers and became the foundation for a major digital payments business. The case shows that (1) different models require different value propositions and revenue streams; (2) trust mechanisms are a decisive success factor in C2C; and (3) a payment innovation designed to solve an e-commerce problem can grow into a FinTech business in its own right, a theme developed in Module 4."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** B2C is the largest form of e-commerce by value. **Correction:** B2B e-commerce is generally far larger by value, even though it is less visible to consumers.","**Misconception:** C2C platforms own and sell the goods. **Correction:** C2C platforms usually only facilitate transactions between individuals by providing listings, trust and payment services.","**Misconception:** M-commerce is a fifth party model alongside B2B and B2C. **Correction:** M-commerce is a channel; a mobile transaction can be B2C, C2C, B2B or C2B.","**Misconception:** B2B e-commerce is simply B2C with bigger orders. **Correction:** B2B involves multi-person buying centres, negotiated prices, credit terms, approval workflows and system integration.","**Misconception:** C2B is only about reverse auctions. **Correction:** It includes freelancing, content licensing, influencer services and data supplied by individuals to firms."],"examMistakes":["Defining the models without comparing them on clear dimensions such as value, buying process, pricing, payment and trust.","Using only international examples when the question asks for Ghanaian application.","Classifying a marketplace by its name rather than by who is selling to whom on it.","Ignoring payment and credit differences between B2B and B2C.","Failing to identify the trust problem and the mechanisms that address it in C2C."],"criticalQuestions":["Many small Ghanaian sellers on WhatsApp are individuals running informal businesses. Should their sales be classified as C2C or B2C, and why does the classification matter for regulation and consumer protection?","Why has B2B e-commerce in many developing economies been slower to adopt full system integration than B2C has been to adopt social media selling?","Can reputation systems fully solve the trust problem in C2C markets? What are their weaknesses?","How might C2B freelancing platforms affect the Ghanaian labour market for graduates?"],"exercises":["**Classification table.** Identify eight digital transactions you or your organisation took part in over the past month and classify each as B2B, B2C, C2C, C2B, B2G or G2C, noting the payment method used. *Expected output:* an eight-row table with classification, payment method and a one-line justification.","**B2B process map.** Map the purchasing process of an organisation you know from requisition to payment, then identify which steps an e-procurement system could automate. *Expected output:* a flowchart and a list of at least four automation opportunities, including three-way matching.","**Trust design.** Design trust mechanisms for an illustrative C2C platform for used phones in Ghana. *Expected output:* a one-page design covering identity verification, ratings, payment holding and dispute resolution, with one limitation of each mechanism."],"takeaways":["E-commerce models are classified by who sells to whom: B2B, B2C, C2C and C2B, plus government variants.","B2B is larger by value and features negotiated prices, credit terms, buying centres and system integration.","B2C emphasises customer experience, fast checkout and delivery.","C2C depends on platforms solving the trust problem with ratings, verification and escrow-style payment.","C2B covers individuals supplying services, content or data to businesses.","Each model implies different technology and payment choices."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Laudon, K. C., & Traver, C. G. *E-commerce: Business, technology, society*. Pearson. (Consult the most recent edition available in your library.)","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Chaffey, D., Hemphill, T., & Edmundson-Bird, D. (2019). *Digital business and e-commerce management* (7th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Williamson, O. E. (1985). *The economic institutions of capitalism*. Free Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"UNCTAD. E-commerce and the digital economy.","url":"https://unctad.org/topic/ecommerce-and-digital-economy","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"UNCTAD E-commerce and Digital Economy","url":"https://unctad.org/topic/ecommerce-and-digital-economy","description":"UNCTAD. Official topic page with publications on e-commerce models and readiness in developing countries.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"MIT OpenCourseWare","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/","description":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology. Official open learning portal; search for supply chain and digital business materials relevant to B2B e-commerce.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"E-commerce models are defined by the parties involved. B2B is the largest by value and involves negotiated prices, credit, buying centres and integration through e-procurement, EDI and portals. B2C focuses on individual consumers, experience and fast checkout. C2C relies on platforms to solve the trust problem between strangers, while C2B reverses the flow so that individuals supply value to businesses.\n\n- Compare models on value, buying process, pricing, payment, trust and technology.\n- M-commerce is a channel, not a party model.\n- Escrow-style payment (for example early Alipay) addresses C2C trust.\n- Transaction cost economics explains why digital tools create value in each model.\n- In Ghana, mobile money, WhatsApp selling and freelancing platforms shape all four models."}'::jsonb, 2577, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('9959f400-42b1-536c-9e02-3c84c4ff109d', 'dda3c643-6999-5f37-9e2d-9741da5ee243', 'marketplaces-and-platform-business-models', 'Marketplaces and Platform Business Models', 'Explain how marketplaces and multi-sided platforms create value through network effects, analyse platform pricing and governance using Rochet and Tirole and Parker, Van Alstyne and Choudary, and evaluate platform strategies in Ghanaian and international markets.', array['Marketplaces', 'Platform business models', 'Network effects']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Many of the most valuable firms of the digital era own few of the products or services exchanged through them. Ride-hailing companies do not employ most of their drivers as traditional staff, accommodation platforms own few rooms, and marketplaces may hold little inventory. Their value lies in **orchestrating interactions** between groups of users: drivers and riders, hosts and guests, sellers and buyers. These are *platform businesses*, and they follow economic logic that differs sharply from that of a traditional retailer or manufacturer.\n\nThis lesson explains that logic. You will study two-sided markets and network effects, the \"chicken-and-egg\" problem of launching a platform, how platforms set prices across sides, and how they govern participants to maintain quality and trust. The ideas draw on Rochet and Tirole''s economic analysis of two-sided markets and Parker, Van Alstyne and Choudary''s *Platform Revolution*. The aim is to enable you to evaluate platform strategies, including those of marketplaces operating in Ghana, rather than simply admire their growth.","objectives":["Distinguish pipeline businesses from platform businesses and marketplaces from resellers.","Explain same-side and cross-side network effects, including negative network effects.","Analyse platform pricing decisions using the concept of a two-sided market.","Apply strategies for solving the chicken-and-egg problem to a platform launch.","Evaluate platform governance, multi-homing and market power concerns."],"prerequisites":["The B2B, B2C, C2C and C2B models (previous lesson).","The Business Model Canvas and revenue models (Module 1)."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Pipeline business","definition":"A firm that creates value in a linear sequence, buying inputs, transforming them and selling outputs to customers."},{"term":"Platform business","definition":"A firm that creates value by facilitating interactions between two or more interdependent groups of users."},{"term":"Two-sided (multi-sided) market","definition":"A market in which a platform serves distinct user groups whose benefit from the platform depends on participation by the other group(s)."},{"term":"Network effect","definition":"The impact that the number of users of a platform has on the value each user obtains."},{"term":"Cross-side network effect","definition":"The effect of the number of users on one side (for example sellers) on the value obtained by users on the other side (for example buyers)."},{"term":"Same-side network effect","definition":"The effect of the number of users on one side on the value obtained by others on the same side."},{"term":"Multi-homing","definition":"Users participating in several competing platforms at the same time."},{"term":"Platform governance","definition":"The rules, incentives and enforcement mechanisms by which a platform controls who participates, how they behave and how value is shared."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Pipelines and platforms\n\nParker, Van Alstyne and Choudary (2016) contrast **pipelines** with **platforms**. A pipeline creates value by controlling a linear chain of activities, as when a retailer buys stock and sells it at a margin. A platform creates value by connecting external producers and consumers and facilitating their interactions. Its key assets are not inventory or factories but its *community* and the data and tools that enable matching.\n\nThe distinction between a **reseller** and a **marketplace** is the e-commerce expression of this difference. A reseller (merchant model) takes ownership of goods, sets prices and bears inventory risk. A marketplace allows third-party sellers to list goods, typically earning a commission, while sellers retain ownership and pricing decisions. Many firms are hybrids: Amazon both sells its own stock and hosts third-party sellers, and some African marketplaces combine first-party retail with third-party listings.\n\n### The core interaction\n\nParker and colleagues argue that a platform should be designed around a **core interaction**, consisting of participants (producer and consumer), a *value unit* (a listing, a ride request, a video) and a *filter* (the algorithm or search that shows each consumer relevant value units). The platform performs three functions: **pull** (attracting users), **facilitate** (making interactions easy, for example through payment and messaging tools) and **match** (connecting the right producers and consumers using data).\n\n### Network effects\n\nNetwork effects are the main source of platform value. Katz and Shapiro (1985) analysed *network externalities*, where the value of a product to one user depends on how many others use it, such as telephones. Platforms add a two-sided dimension:\n\n| Type | Positive example | Negative example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cross-side | More sellers give buyers more choice; more buyers attract more sellers | Too many low-quality sellers reduce buyer trust |\n| Same-side | More users of a messaging app make it more useful to each user | More drivers on the same side compete for each fare, reducing earnings |\n\nPositive cross-side effects create a **virtuous circle**: buyers attract sellers, who attract more buyers. This circle explains why platforms can grow rapidly and why markets may tend towards a few dominant platforms. However, network effects are not automatically positive. Congestion, spam, fraud and declining quality are **negative network effects**, and much of platform management consists of preventing them.\n\n### The chicken-and-egg problem\n\nA new platform has little value to either side because neither side is present. Buyers will not visit a marketplace without sellers, and sellers will not list without buyers. Strategies identified in the platform literature include:\n\n1. **Subsidise one side**, for example free listings for sellers or discounts for early buyers.\n2. **Seed the platform** with the platform''s own supply (acting as a reseller first).\n3. **Follow the rabbit**: start with a small, dense niche where both sides are easy to bring together, such as one university campus or one city.\n4. **Piggyback** on an existing user base, such as an established social network or messaging community.\n5. **Create stand-alone value** for one side, such as free inventory or booking software for sellers, so that they join even before buyers arrive.\n\n### Multi-homing and competition\n\nIf users can easily use several platforms at once (multi-homing), network effects provide weaker protection. Many drivers in cities with several ride-hailing apps run more than one app simultaneously, and many riders compare prices. Platforms respond with loyalty incentives, exclusive features or differentiated service. Where switching costs are low and multi-homing is common, markets can remain competitive despite network effects."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Rochet and Tirole: pricing in two-sided markets\n\nRochet and Tirole (2003), in \"Platform competition in two-sided markets\", showed that platforms must choose not only the *level* of prices but their *structure* across sides. Because each side''s participation creates value for the other, it can be rational to charge one side little or nothing, or even to subsidise it, while charging the other side more. Their analysis was motivated partly by payment cards, where cardholders often pay little while merchants pay fees, and it applies equally to marketplaces and software platforms.\n\nThe side that is usually subsidised is the one that is more **price-sensitive** and whose presence creates more value for the other side. The side that pays is the one that values access to the subsidised side highly and is less sensitive to price. Getting this wrong can stall the virtuous circle.\n\n### Platform governance\n\nParker and colleagues stress that platforms must govern their ecosystems. Governance tools include:\n\n- **Curation and access rules**: who may join, and with what verification.\n- **Reputation systems**: ratings and reviews that reward good behaviour.\n- **Payment and dispute rules**: holding funds until delivery, refund policies.\n- **Algorithms and ranking**: which listings are shown first, which shapes behaviour.\n- **Value sharing**: commission levels that leave producers enough reward to stay.\n\nGood governance balances openness (to grow supply) with quality control (to prevent negative network effects). Governance also raises ethical and regulatory questions: platforms that rank their own products above those of rival sellers, or that change commission levels once sellers depend on them, face criticism and, in some jurisdictions, regulatory scrutiny."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    S[\"More sellers and listings\"] -->|\"cross-side effect: more choice\"| B[\"More buyers\"]\n    B -->|\"cross-side effect: more sales opportunities\"| S\n    B --> D[\"More transaction data\"]\n    S --> D\n    D --> M[\"Better matching and recommendations\"]\n    M --> B\n    M --> S\n    S -.->|\"negative effect if unmanaged\"| Q[\"Low-quality or fraudulent listings\"]\n    Q -.-> G[\"Governance: verification, ratings, rules\"]\n    G --> S\n```\n\nThe solid loop is the virtuous circle; the dotted path shows how governance counters negative network effects."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *TutorLink GH* is a hypothetical platform that connects private tutors with parents of senior high school students in Accra and Kumasi. Analyse its launch strategy.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the sides and core interaction.** Producers: tutors. Consumers: parents (and students). Value unit: a tutor profile with subjects, availability and ratings. Core interaction: a parent books and pays for a tutoring session.\n\n**Step 2: Identify network effects.** Cross-side: more tutors give parents better choice by subject and location; more parents give tutors more bookings. Same-side: more tutors in the same subject compete for bookings (negative same-side effect); more parents may create positive same-side effects through reviews.\n\n**Step 3: Choose which side to subsidise.** Parents are price-sensitive about platform fees but highly value trusted, vetted tutors; tutors value a steady flow of bookings. TutorLink decides to let parents search and book free and to charge tutors a 15 per cent commission on sessions, consistent with Rochet and Tirole''s logic of charging the side that values access more and is less price-sensitive to the fee.\n\n**Step 4: Solve chicken-and-egg.** Start in a dense niche: final-year science and mathematics tutoring in two Accra districts. Recruit 40 vetted tutors before launch and offer them zero commission for the first three months (subsidy and seeding). Give tutors free scheduling and payment tools (stand-alone value).\n\n**Step 5: Check the economics.** If, after launch, 300 sessions a month are booked at an average of GHS 150, monthly gross transaction value = `300 × 150 = GHS 45,000`, and commission revenue at 15 per cent = `45,000 × 0.15 = GHS 6,750`. (Figures are illustrative assumptions.)\n\n**Step 6: Governance and risks.** Risks include disintermediation (parents and tutors arranging sessions privately after the first meeting to avoid commission), safeguarding of minors and quality variation. Mitigations: in-platform scheduling, payment protection and session records that give both sides reasons to stay; background checks; ratings and refund policies. This illustrates that a platform must keep delivering value *after* matching, or users will leave."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Platform models are well established in Ghana. **Ride-hailing** services such as Uber and Bolt operate in Accra and other cities, connecting drivers and riders and earning commissions on trips; they show multi-homing in practice, as many drivers and riders use more than one app. **E-commerce marketplaces** such as Jumia, which has operated in Ghana, aggregate third-party sellers and provide payment and delivery services. **Food and parcel delivery platforms** connect restaurants, couriers and customers in a three-sided arrangement.\n\nMobile money itself has platform characteristics. An operator''s mobile money service connects customers, merchants and agents, and the value to each customer rises as more merchants and recipients accept it (a cross-side network effect). The introduction of interoperability between mobile money networks and banks in Ghana, discussed in Module 4, reduced the advantage that the largest network gained from its size and increased the value of the whole system to users.\n\nLocal conditions influence platform design. Trust concerns favour strong governance (verification, ratings and payment holding). Informal arrangements make disintermediation a real risk: once a buyer has a trader''s phone number, they may transact directly via WhatsApp. Platforms that provide ongoing value, such as payment protection, delivery tracking and credit, are better placed to retain users."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Airbnb: building trust in a two-sided accommodation market\n\nAirbnb was founded in 2008 in San Francisco and connects hosts who offer accommodation with guests who need it. It owns few properties; its value lies in matching and in making strangers comfortable enough to stay in each other''s homes.\n\nThe case illustrates several platform concepts. The **chicken-and-egg** problem was acute: guests would not come without listings, and hosts would not list without guests. The company''s early efforts included working directly with hosts to improve listings, a form of seeding supply quality. **Governance** has been central: two-way reviews after each stay, host and guest profiles, identity verification and payment held by the platform until after check-in reduce the risk of fraud. **Cross-side network effects** are strong: more listings attract more guests, which in turn attract more hosts.\n\nThe case also highlights **negative externalities beyond the platform**. Many cities have debated the impact of short-term rentals on housing availability and neighbourhoods, and several have introduced rules such as registration requirements or limits on rental days. This demonstrates that platform governance is not only internal: platforms operate within public regulation, and their growth can provoke policy responses that reshape the business model."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** Every website that sells products is a platform. **Correction:** A reseller that buys and sells its own stock is a pipeline; a platform facilitates interactions between external producers and consumers.","**Misconception:** Network effects are always positive. **Correction:** Congestion, fraud and low quality create negative network effects that must be governed.","**Misconception:** Charging both sides equally is the fairest and most effective pricing. **Correction:** Rochet and Tirole show that the price structure across sides matters, and subsidising one side is often optimal.","**Misconception:** Network effects guarantee a permanent monopoly. **Correction:** Multi-homing, low switching costs, poor governance and regulation can all erode a leading platform''s position.","**Misconception:** Once users are matched, the platform''s job is done. **Correction:** Without ongoing value, users disintermediate and transact directly."],"examMistakes":["Describing network effects without distinguishing cross-side from same-side effects.","Citing Rochet and Tirole without explaining price structure and the subsidised side.","Discussing platform growth without considering governance, quality and negative effects.","Ignoring multi-homing and disintermediation risks in local markets.","Treating marketplaces and resellers as identical when analysing revenue and risk."],"criticalQuestions":["In a Ghanaian ride-hailing market where most drivers multi-home, what sources of competitive advantage remain for a platform?","Should regulators treat a dominant marketplace that also sells its own products differently from one that only hosts third-party sellers? Why?","Under what conditions would subsidising sellers rather than buyers be the better launch strategy for a B2C marketplace?","Does mobile money interoperability strengthen or weaken network effects for individual operators, and who gains?"],"exercises":["**Network effect map.** Choose a platform used in Ghana and draw its sides, core interaction and network effects (positive and negative, same-side and cross-side). *Expected output:* a labelled diagram and a 250-word explanation.","**Pricing decision.** For an illustrative marketplace connecting artisans with hotel buyers, decide which side to subsidise and justify using Rochet and Tirole''s reasoning. *Expected output:* a pricing proposal with fees for each side and a justification addressing price sensitivity and cross-side value.","**Disintermediation defence.** List five features that would discourage users of the illustrative TutorLink GH platform from bypassing it. *Expected output:* five features, each with the value it gives both sides and one drawback."],"takeaways":["Platforms create value by orchestrating interactions between external producers and consumers rather than by controlling a linear pipeline.","Cross-side and same-side network effects, positive and negative, drive platform dynamics.","Price structure across sides matters; subsidising the more price-sensitive, value-creating side is often rational.","Launch strategies address the chicken-and-egg problem through subsidies, seeding, niches and stand-alone value.","Governance, multi-homing and disintermediation determine whether network effects become durable advantage."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Parker, G. G., Van Alstyne, M. W., & Choudary, S. P. (2016). *Platform revolution: How networked markets are transforming the economy and how to make them work for you*. W. W. Norton.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Rochet, J.-C., & Tirole, J. (2003). Platform competition in two-sided markets. *Journal of the European Economic Association, 1*(4), 990-1029.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/154247603322493212","verification":"unverified"},{"text":"Katz, M. L., & Shapiro, C. (1985). Network externalities, competition, and compatibility. *The American Economic Review, 75*(3), 424-440.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Shapiro, C., & Varian, H. R. (1999). *Information rules: A strategic guide to the network economy*. Harvard Business School Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"MIT OpenCourseWare","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/","description":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology. Official open learning portal; search for platform strategy and digital economy course materials.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"UNCTAD E-commerce and Digital Economy","url":"https://unctad.org/topic/ecommerce-and-digital-economy","description":"UNCTAD. Official topic page including work on digital platforms in developing economies.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Platform businesses create value by facilitating interactions between external user groups, unlike pipelines that control linear value chains. Cross-side and same-side network effects drive platform growth, but negative effects such as fraud and congestion must be governed. Rochet and Tirole show that the structure of prices across sides matters, so platforms often subsidise one side. Launch requires solving the chicken-and-egg problem, while multi-homing and disintermediation limit the durability of network effects.\n\n- Marketplace = third-party sellers and commission; reseller = owns inventory and margin.\n- Core interaction: participants, value unit, filter; functions: pull, facilitate, match.\n- Subsidise the price-sensitive side whose presence creates value for the other.\n- Launch tactics: subsidy, seeding, niche first, piggybacking, stand-alone value.\n- Governance: verification, ratings, payment rules, ranking, value sharing."}'::jsonb, 2773, 2, true)
