insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('3a174381-48c3-5f73-b258-6befad24c449', 'e09cc604-b642-50f6-acc7-46a7c60f4aac', 'storefronts-catalogues-and-carts', 'Online Storefronts, Product Catalogues and Shopping Carts', 'Explain the architecture of an online storefront, design product catalogue data structures, and analyse the shopping cart and checkout flow to reduce friction and abandonment.', array['Online storefronts', 'Product catalogues', 'Shopping carts']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Customers experience an online shop as a handful of pages: a home page, product listings, a product page, a cart and a checkout. Behind those pages sits a set of interacting systems: a web or mobile front end, application logic, a database of products and orders, integrations with payment and delivery providers, and infrastructure that keeps everything fast and secure. Decisions about these systems determine how quickly a business can launch, how much it pays, how well it scales and how many visitors complete a purchase.\n\nThis lesson examines the three components at the heart of any e-commerce operation: the **storefront** (and the architecture behind it), the **product catalogue** (the structured data describing what is for sale) and the **shopping cart and checkout** (the process that turns intent into an order). You will learn to compare platform options, model catalogue data and redesign a checkout to remove friction, with attention to conditions faced by Ghanaian SMEs.","objectives":["Describe the layers of an e-commerce storefront architecture and the role of each.","Compare hosted (SaaS), open-source and custom or headless storefront options.","Design a product catalogue data model using products, variants, attributes and categories.","Explain how shopping carts maintain state and why carts are abandoned.","Apply checkout design principles to reduce friction for mobile users in Ghana."],"prerequisites":["The B2C e-commerce model (Module 2).","Basic awareness of websites, databases and mobile apps."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Storefront","definition":"The customer-facing interface of an online shop, delivered through a website, mobile app or social commerce channel."},{"term":"SaaS e-commerce platform","definition":"A hosted service, paid by subscription or fees, that provides storefront, catalogue, cart and checkout functions without the merchant managing servers."},{"term":"Headless commerce","definition":"An architecture in which the front-end presentation layer is separated from the back-end commerce engine and connected through APIs."},{"term":"SKU (stock-keeping unit)","definition":"A unique identifier for each distinct sellable item, such as a shirt in a specific size and colour."},{"term":"Product variant","definition":"A version of a product that differs by attributes such as size, colour or capacity, usually with its own SKU, price and stock level."},{"term":"Product taxonomy","definition":"The hierarchical classification of products into categories and sub-categories that supports navigation and search."},{"term":"Cart abandonment","definition":"When a shopper adds items to a cart but leaves without completing the purchase."},{"term":"Guest checkout","definition":"Allowing customers to buy without creating an account."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Storefront architecture in layers\n\nA useful way to analyse a storefront is as a set of layers:\n\n1. **Presentation layer**: the web pages or app screens the customer sees, built with HTML, CSS and JavaScript or native mobile code. It must be responsive, because many Ghanaian shoppers browse on smartphones.\n2. **Application (commerce) layer**: the business logic: pricing rules, promotions, cart calculations, tax and delivery fees, customer accounts and order creation.\n3. **Data layer**: databases holding products, inventory, customers, carts and orders, plus a search index for fast product search.\n4. **Integration layer**: APIs connecting to payment gateways, delivery partners, accounting or ERP systems, messaging (SMS, email, WhatsApp) and analytics.\n5. **Infrastructure layer**: hosting, content delivery networks (CDNs) that cache images close to users, TLS certificates for HTTPS, backups and monitoring.\n\nPerformance and security cut across all layers. Slow pages reduce conversion, especially on mobile data connections, so image compression and caching matter. Security requires HTTPS, secure handling of credentials and, ideally, leaving card data handling to a certified payment provider (see Module 5).\n\n### Comparing storefront options\n\n| Option | Description | Advantages | Disadvantages | Suited to |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Social commerce | Selling through Instagram, Facebook or WhatsApp Business catalogues | Almost no cost; customers already there | Limited catalogue, manual order handling, platform dependence | Very small sellers testing demand |\n| Marketplace listing | Selling on a third-party marketplace | Instant traffic, trust and logistics | Commissions, little control over brand and customer data | Sellers needing reach |\n| SaaS platform | Hosted store builder (for example Shopify) | Fast launch, managed security and updates, app ecosystems | Monthly fees, transaction fees, less flexibility | Most SMEs |\n| Open-source | Self-hosted software (for example WooCommerce on WordPress) | Flexible, no licence fee, large community | Merchant responsible for hosting, updates and security | Firms with some technical capacity |\n| Custom or headless | Bespoke front end on a commerce engine via APIs | Maximum flexibility and performance | Highest cost and skills requirement | Large or fast-growing firms |\n\nThe right choice depends on budget, skills, the size and complexity of the catalogue, integration needs and growth expectations. A common path for Ghanaian SMEs is to begin with social commerce, add a SaaS or open-source store integrated with a local payment provider, and move to more complex architectures only when scale justifies it.\n\n### The product catalogue\n\nThe catalogue is the structured data that describes what is for sale. Poor catalogue data produces poor search results, confusing product pages, wrong stock levels and customer complaints. A sound catalogue model separates:\n\n- **Product**: the general item (for example \"Ankara wrap dress\").\n- **Variants**: combinations of attributes (size M, blue pattern), each with its own **SKU**, price, stock quantity and possibly image.\n- **Attributes**: structured properties (size, colour, fabric, weight) used for filtering.\n- **Categories**: a taxonomy (Women > Dresses > Wrap dresses) for navigation; a product can belong to several categories.\n- **Media and content**: images, descriptions, size guides and reviews.\n- **Pricing and promotions**: list price, sale price, start and end dates, and customer-group prices for B2B buyers.\n- **Inventory**: stock per location or warehouse, with reserved quantities for unpaid orders.\n\nCatalogue quality has a direct business effect. Consistent attributes allow filtering (\"size M under GHS 300\"), accurate weights allow correct delivery fees, and good images reduce returns.\n\n### Shopping carts and state\n\nThe web is stateless: each request to a server is independent. A cart must therefore persist the shopper''s selections. **Session carts** store items for the duration of a browsing session, often linked to a browser cookie. **Persistent carts** store items in the database against a customer account or long-lived identifier, so that a shopper can return days later or switch from phone to laptop. Carts also recalculate totals as items change, apply promotions, estimate delivery fees and check stock.\n\n### Why carts are abandoned\n\nCommon reasons for abandonment include unexpected extra costs (delivery fees revealed late), forced account creation, long or confusing forms, limited payment options, slow pages, concerns about security or returns, and simply browsing without strong intent. In Ghana, specific factors include uncertainty about delivery to locations without clear street addresses and the absence of a preferred payment method such as mobile money."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Checkout friction and usability principles\n\nUsability research, notably from the Baymard Institute and the Nielsen Norman Group, consistently recommends reducing the number of form fields, showing total costs early, offering guest checkout, displaying progress indicators, giving clear error messages and providing trust signals. These recommendations can be organised into a simple **friction audit**:\n\n| Principle | Question to ask | Example improvement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Transparency | Are all costs visible before the final step? | Show delivery fee on the product or cart page |\n| Minimal effort | Is every field necessary? | Ask for phone number and digital address instead of a long postal address |\n| Choice | Are preferred payment methods available? | Offer mobile money, card and, where appropriate, pay on delivery |\n| Reassurance | Does the shopper trust the process? | Display returns policy, contact number and secure payment provider |\n| Recovery | Can errors be corrected easily? | Inline validation of phone numbers; saved cart if payment fails |\n\n### The cart and checkout as a state machine\n\nIt is useful to model checkout as a sequence of **states** (cart, details, delivery, payment, confirmation) with defined transitions. This makes it easier to identify where users drop out, which data must be validated at each stage, and what should happen if payment fails or times out, such as keeping the cart and reserved stock for a limited period."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Browse catalogue and search\"] --> B[\"Product page: variants, price, stock\"]\n    B --> C[\"Add to cart\"]\n    C --> D[\"Cart: update quantities, see delivery estimate\"]\n    D --> E{\"Signed in?\"}\n    E -->|\"No\"| F[\"Guest checkout: name, phone, email\"]\n    E -->|\"Yes\"| G[\"Saved details pre-filled\"]\n    F --> H[\"Delivery: digital address or pick-up point\"]\n    G --> H\n    H --> I[\"Choose payment: mobile money, card, pay on delivery\"]\n    I --> J{\"Payment successful?\"}\n    J -->|\"Yes\"| K[\"Order confirmed: SMS and email receipt\"]\n    J -->|\"No\"| L[\"Retry or change method; cart and stock held\"]\n    L --> I\n```"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Kente & Co Accessories* is a hypothetical Accra-based seller of bags and jewellery. Its current website checkout has seven steps: create account, verify email, enter full postal address, choose delivery, see delivery fee, enter card details, confirm. Only card payment is offered. Analytics show that of 1,000 shoppers who reach the cart each month, 120 complete an order.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the cart-to-order rate.** `120 ÷ 1,000 = 12%`. Therefore 88 per cent of carts are abandoned at some stage.\n\n**Step 2: Apply the friction audit.**\n- *Transparency*: delivery fee appears late. Move a delivery estimate to the cart.\n- *Minimal effort*: forced account creation and email verification add two steps. Introduce guest checkout using phone number.\n- *Local fit*: a full postal address is unsuitable where many locations are described by landmarks. Accept a Ghana Post digital address or a pick-up point, with a landmark field.\n- *Choice*: card-only payment excludes many customers. Integrate a payment service provider that supports mobile money and cards.\n- *Reassurance*: add a returns policy, a WhatsApp contact and visible secure payment branding.\n\n**Step 3: Redesign.** New flow: cart (with delivery estimate) → details (name, phone, optional email) → delivery (digital address or pick-up point) → payment (mobile money or card) → confirmation by SMS. Four steps replace seven.\n\n**Step 4: Estimate impact.** If the redesign raises the cart-to-order rate to 18 per cent (an illustrative target, not a forecast), orders rise to `1,000 × 0.18 = 180` per month, an increase of 60 orders. With an average order value of GHS 220, additional monthly revenue = `60 × 220 = GHS 13,200`. The business should test the change and measure the actual effect rather than assume it.\n\n**Step 5: Catalogue fix.** The seller''s bags come in several colours, but each colour was listed as a separate product, cluttering search. Restructure as one product with colour variants, each with its own SKU and stock, so that shoppers see one listing and can choose a colour."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Ghanaian merchants have a growing range of storefront options. Many begin with **WhatsApp Business catalogues and Instagram shops**, which let customers browse products and message the seller, with payment by mobile money. As volumes grow, manual order handling becomes error-prone, prompting a move to a website or SaaS store.\n\nLocal **payment service providers** such as Paystack and Hubtel offer checkout integrations and plugins for common e-commerce platforms, allowing a merchant''s checkout to accept mobile money and cards through one integration. This addresses one of the main local abandonment drivers: lack of a preferred payment method.\n\n**Addressing and delivery** are distinctive challenges. Ghana introduced the GhanaPostGPS digital address system in 2017, which assigns location-based codes, and many online shops ask customers for a digital address or a nearby landmark. Pick-up points and agent locations are alternatives where home delivery is costly. Finally, mobile-first design and lightweight pages are essential because customers often browse on smartphones over mobile data, where large images slow loading and increase customers'' data costs."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Shopify: making storefront technology available to small merchants\n\nShopify was founded in 2006 in Ottawa, Canada. Its founders originally built an online store to sell snowboards and, frustrated with the available software, developed their own platform, which they then offered to other merchants. Shopify grew into one of the best-known SaaS e-commerce platforms, offering hosted storefronts, catalogue management, cart and checkout, and an app ecosystem through which third-party developers extend functionality.\n\nThe case illustrates several points from this lesson. First, SaaS platforms **lower the technical barrier** to e-commerce: merchants configure rather than build, and the platform manages hosting, security and updates. Second, the **app ecosystem** is itself a platform with network effects: more merchants attract more app developers, whose apps attract more merchants. Third, Shopify''s revenue combines **subscriptions** with **merchant solutions** such as payment processing, showing how business models (Module 1) and technology choices interlock. A critical evaluation should also note the trade-offs for merchants: recurring fees, dependence on the platform''s policies and limits on deep customisation compared with custom builds."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** A storefront is just a website design. **Correction:** It sits on application, data, integration and infrastructure layers that determine performance, security and capability.","**Misconception:** Each colour or size should be listed as a separate product. **Correction:** Variants with their own SKUs under one product keep the catalogue clean and stock accurate.","**Misconception:** Requiring account creation builds loyalty. **Correction:** Forced registration is a common cause of abandonment; guest checkout with an optional account afterwards usually works better.","**Misconception:** Cart abandonment always means lost interest. **Correction:** Many abandonments result from fixable friction such as hidden costs, payment gaps or slow pages.","**Misconception:** Custom-built storefronts are always superior. **Correction:** For most SMEs, SaaS or open-source platforms provide better value and security than bespoke builds."],"examMistakes":["Describing a storefront without discussing architecture layers or integration with payment and delivery.","Confusing products, variants and SKUs when designing a catalogue.","Listing reasons for cart abandonment without proposing specific, context-appropriate fixes.","Ignoring mobile-first design and local payment and addressing conditions in Ghana.","Recommending a platform option without justifying it against budget, skills and scale."],"criticalQuestions":["For a Ghanaian SME with a small catalogue and no in-house developer, is it better to rely on a marketplace or to build its own SaaS store? What evidence would you need to decide?","Payment on delivery reduces abandonment but adds cash-handling and failed-delivery costs. How should a merchant weigh these effects?","Does headless commerce offer meaningful benefits for SMEs, or is it relevant only to large enterprises?","To what extent can good catalogue data compensate for weak marketing?"],"exercises":["**Catalogue model.** Design a catalogue for an illustrative shoe shop with five styles, each in four sizes and two colours. *Expected output:* a table listing products, variants and SKU codes, attributes and categories, with the total number of SKUs (40) correctly calculated.","**Friction audit.** Complete a checkout on a Ghanaian e-commerce site of your choice (without paying) and audit it against the five friction principles. *Expected output:* a five-row audit table with evidence and one improvement per principle.","**Option appraisal.** Recommend a storefront option for an illustrative bakery in Cape Coast that wants online pre-orders. *Expected output:* a 300-word recommendation comparing at least three options against cost, skills, payment integration and delivery."],"takeaways":["A storefront rests on presentation, application, data, integration and infrastructure layers.","Storefront options range from social commerce to SaaS, open-source and headless, with trade-offs in cost, control and skills.","Catalogue data should separate products, variants (SKUs), attributes, categories, pricing and inventory.","Carts maintain state across a stateless web; persistent carts support multi-device shopping.","Checkout friction, hidden costs and missing payment methods drive abandonment; local addressing and mobile money matter in Ghana."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Laudon, K. C., & Traver, C. G. *E-commerce: Business, technology, society*. Pearson. (Consult the most recent edition available in your library.)","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Chaffey, D., Hemphill, T., & Edmundson-Bird, D. (2019). *Digital business and e-commerce management* (7th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Krug, S. (2014). *Don''t make me think, revisited: A common sense approach to web usability* (3rd ed.). New Riders.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Baymard Institute. E-commerce checkout usability research.","url":"https://baymard.com/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"Paystack Documentation","url":"https://paystack.com/docs/","description":"Paystack. Official developer documentation describing how online checkouts accept card and mobile money payments.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"Shopify Help Center","url":"https://help.shopify.com/","description":"Shopify. Official help resources explaining storefront, product and checkout configuration on a SaaS platform.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"An online storefront is the customer-facing layer of a multi-layer system that includes commerce logic, data, integrations and infrastructure. Merchants choose between social commerce, marketplaces, SaaS, open-source and headless options according to budget, skills and scale. Catalogue data must be structured into products, variants with SKUs, attributes, categories, prices and stock. Carts preserve state, and well-designed checkouts minimise friction to reduce abandonment.\n\n- Five layers: presentation, application, data, integration, infrastructure.\n- Variants share a product; each variant has its own SKU, price and stock.\n- Abandonment drivers: hidden costs, forced accounts, long forms, missing payment methods, slow pages.\n- Friction audit: transparency, minimal effort, choice, reassurance, recovery.\n- Ghana: mobile-first pages, mobile money via PSPs, digital addresses and pick-up points."}'::jsonb, 2667, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('5b64b618-a84f-5ef0-ba91-00711a1d957d', 'e09cc604-b642-50f6-acc7-46a7c60f4aac', 'order-and-customer-management', 'Order Management and Customer Management', 'Trace the e-commerce order lifecycle from placement to delivery and returns, compare fulfilment models, and apply customer relationship management concepts, including RFM segmentation, to retain and grow customers.', array['Order management', 'Customer management']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"A customer''s click on \"Place order\" is the start, not the end, of an e-commerce transaction. Payment must be confirmed, stock reserved, goods picked and packed, a courier booked, the customer kept informed and, sometimes, a return processed and a refund issued. Each step can fail: payment may time out, stock may be wrong, the rider may not find the address. Customers judge an online business largely on how well it handles these steps, and repeat purchases depend on that judgement.\n\nThis lesson covers two closely related capabilities. **Order management** coordinates everything from order capture to delivery and returns. **Customer management**, usually discussed as **customer relationship management (CRM)**, uses customer data and processes to acquire, serve and retain profitable customers. Together they turn one-off buyers into loyal customers. You will trace the order lifecycle, compare fulfilment models, and apply CRM techniques, including a worked RFM segmentation.","objectives":["Describe the stages of the e-commerce order lifecycle and the events that move an order between them.","Explain the functions of an order management system and its integration with inventory, payment and delivery systems.","Compare in-house, third-party logistics, dropshipping and marketplace fulfilment models.","Distinguish operational, analytical and collaborative CRM.","Apply RFM analysis to segment customers and propose targeted retention actions."],"prerequisites":["The storefront, catalogue and checkout concepts in the previous lesson.","Basic spreadsheet skills for the RFM exercise."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Order management system (OMS)","definition":"Software that captures, tracks and coordinates orders across payment, inventory, fulfilment, delivery and returns."},{"term":"Order lifecycle","definition":"The sequence of statuses an order passes through, from placement to completion, cancellation or return."},{"term":"Fulfilment","definition":"The process of receiving, picking, packing and shipping orders to customers."},{"term":"Third-party logistics (3PL)","definition":"An external provider that stores, picks, packs and delivers goods on behalf of a merchant."},{"term":"Dropshipping","definition":"A model in which the merchant sells goods that a supplier ships directly to the customer, so the merchant holds no stock."},{"term":"Reverse logistics","definition":"The processes for handling returned goods, including collection, inspection, restocking and refunds."},{"term":"Customer relationship management (CRM)","definition":"A strategy, set of processes and technologies for managing interactions with customers to build long-term, profitable relationships."},{"term":"RFM analysis","definition":"A segmentation method that scores customers on Recency, Frequency and Monetary value of their purchases."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The order lifecycle\n\nAlthough statuses vary between systems, a typical B2C order lifecycle includes:\n\n1. **Placed / pending payment**: the order is created; for mobile money, the customer may still need to approve the payment prompt on their phone.\n2. **Paid / confirmed**: the payment provider confirms success, usually by a callback or webhook to the merchant''s system.\n3. **Verified**: optional fraud or risk checks, especially for high-value orders or unusual patterns.\n4. **Allocated**: stock is reserved against the order at a specific warehouse or shop.\n5. **Picked and packed**: items are collected, checked and packaged, and a delivery label or reference is generated.\n6. **Dispatched / shipped**: handed to a rider or courier with a tracking reference.\n7. **Delivered**: confirmed by the courier, customer signature, photo or one-time code.\n8. **Completed**: the return window closes and the sale is final.\n\nBranches include **cancelled** (before dispatch), **failed delivery** (customer unavailable, address not found) and **returned / refunded**. For *pay on delivery* orders, payment is collected at the delivery step, which adds a cash reconciliation task: the rider''s collections must be matched against delivered orders.\n\n### What an OMS does\n\nAn OMS is the coordinating hub. Its main functions are:\n\n- **Order capture** from multiple channels (website, app, marketplace, WhatsApp, phone) into a single queue.\n- **Payment status tracking**, including handling of pending, failed and reversed payments.\n- **Inventory reservation and synchronisation**, so that stock sold on one channel is not oversold on another.\n- **Order routing** to the best fulfilment location.\n- **Delivery integration**, booking couriers and updating tracking.\n- **Customer notifications** by SMS, email or WhatsApp at each stage.\n- **Returns and refunds** management.\n- **Reporting** on fulfilment times, cancellation rates and delivery success.\n\nThe most common operational problem for growing SMEs is **overselling**: selling items that are not in stock because inventory is not synchronised between channels. A second is **lost orders**, where orders taken in chats are never recorded. A single OMS, even a simple one, addresses both.\n\n### Fulfilment models\n\n| Model | How it works | Advantages | Risks |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| In-house | Merchant stores, packs and arranges delivery | Control over quality and packaging | Space, staff and delivery costs; hard to scale |\n| 3PL | External provider stores and ships goods | Scalable, professional logistics | Fees, less direct control, integration needed |\n| Dropshipping | Supplier ships directly to customer | No inventory investment | Long delivery times, quality control, thin margins |\n| Marketplace fulfilment | Marketplace stores and delivers seller''s stock | Access to the marketplace''s logistics and trust | Fees, dependence on the marketplace |\n\n### Customer relationship management\n\nButtle and Maklan describe CRM as a core business strategy that integrates processes and functions to create and deliver value to targeted customers profitably, supported by customer data and technology. CRM is therefore not merely software. Three types are commonly distinguished:\n\n- **Operational CRM** automates customer-facing processes: sales, marketing campaigns and customer service (tickets, chat, call logs).\n- **Analytical CRM** analyses customer data to understand behaviour, segment customers, predict churn and estimate value.\n- **Collaborative CRM** shares customer information across departments and channels so that every interaction is informed by previous ones.\n\nA practical way to think about CRM is the **customer lifecycle**: *acquire* (attract and convert), *develop* (encourage repeat and higher-value purchases through cross-selling and up-selling), *retain* (service quality, loyalty programmes, personalised communication) and *win back* lapsed customers. Retention matters because acquiring new customers is usually more expensive than keeping existing ones. Reichheld and Sasser (1990) argued in the *Harvard Business Review* that reducing customer defections can have a large effect on profits, because loyal customers tend to buy more, cost less to serve and refer others.\n\n### Data protection\n\nCRM depends on personal data: names, phone numbers, addresses and purchase histories. In Ghana, the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) governs the processing of personal data and established the Data Protection Commission. Good practice includes collecting only necessary data, obtaining consent for marketing messages, securing data and respecting customers'' requests. This lesson''s treatment is educational; organisations should consult the Act and qualified advisers for compliance."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### RFM segmentation\n\nRFM is a simple but powerful analytical CRM technique. Each customer is scored, often from 1 (lowest) to 5 (highest), on:\n\n- **Recency**: how recently they purchased (more recent scores higher).\n- **Frequency**: how many purchases they made in the period.\n- **Monetary value**: how much they spent in the period.\n\nCombinations identify segments such as **Champions** (high R, F and M), **Loyal customers** (high F), **At risk** (low R but previously high F and M) and **New customers** (high R, low F). Each segment receives different treatment: champions might be invited to a loyalty tier, at-risk customers sent a personalised win-back offer.\n\n### Service recovery\n\nThe **service recovery** concept holds that how a firm responds to a failure, such as a late delivery, strongly affects customer loyalty. Fast acknowledgement, an apology, a fix and, where appropriate, compensation can retain customers who would otherwise leave. OMS and CRM data make proactive recovery possible: if the system shows an order delayed beyond its promised date, the customer can be contacted before they complain."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Order placed: pending payment\"] --> B{\"Payment confirmed?\"}\n    B -->|\"No, timed out or failed\"| X[\"Cancelled; stock released\"]\n    B -->|\"Yes, via webhook\"| C[\"Confirmed\"]\n    A -->|\"Pay on delivery\"| C\n    C --> D[\"Risk check (if required)\"]\n    D --> E[\"Stock allocated\"]\n    E --> F[\"Picked and packed\"]\n    F --> G[\"Dispatched with tracking reference\"]\n    G --> H{\"Delivered?\"}\n    H -->|\"Yes\"| I[\"Delivered; cash reconciled if pay on delivery\"]\n    H -->|\"No\"| J[\"Failed delivery: reschedule or return to store\"]\n    J --> G\n    I --> K{\"Return requested in window?\"}\n    K -->|\"No\"| L[\"Completed\"]\n    K -->|\"Yes\"| M[\"Return received and inspected\"]\n    M --> N[\"Refund issued; stock updated\"]\n```"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *GlowSkin GH*, a hypothetical online skincare retailer in Accra, wants to use its order history to plan retention campaigns. The table shows six customers over the last 12 months (data are invented for practice).\n\n| Customer | Days since last order | Orders in 12 months | Total spend (GHS) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Akosua | 10 | 9 | 2,700 |\n| Kwame | 200 | 6 | 1,900 |\n| Esi | 15 | 1 | 180 |\n| Yaw | 45 | 4 | 900 |\n| Abena | 300 | 1 | 150 |\n| Kofi | 25 | 7 | 2,100 |\n\n**Step 1: Define scoring bands (1-3 for simplicity).**\n- Recency: 0-30 days = 3; 31-120 days = 2; over 120 days = 1.\n- Frequency: 6 or more orders = 3; 2-5 = 2; 1 = 1.\n- Monetary: over GHS 1,500 = 3; GHS 500-1,500 = 2; under GHS 500 = 1.\n\n**Step 2: Score each customer.**\n\n| Customer | R | F | M | RFM code | Segment |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Akosua | 3 | 3 | 3 | 333 | Champion |\n| Kofi | 3 | 3 | 3 | 333 | Champion |\n| Yaw | 2 | 2 | 2 | 222 | Developing / potential loyalist |\n| Esi | 3 | 1 | 1 | 311 | New customer |\n| Kwame | 1 | 3 | 3 | 133 | At risk (valuable but lapsing) |\n| Abena | 1 | 1 | 1 | 111 | Lost / low value |\n\n**Step 3: Design actions.**\n- *Champions*: early access to new products, a loyalty tier and requests for reviews or referrals.\n- *Yaw*: cross-sell complementary products based on purchase history to increase frequency.\n- *Esi*: a welcome series with product guidance to encourage a second purchase, which is often the hardest step.\n- *Kwame*: priority win-back, because he has high historical value. Check service records for a problem (late delivery, complaint) and contact him personally with a tailored offer; this is service recovery in practice.\n- *Abena*: low-cost automated reminder only.\n\n**Step 4: Evaluate.** RFM is easy to calculate and explain, but it is backward-looking, ignores margins and product categories, and depends on accurate order data. This is why OMS and CRM must be integrated: without reliable order records, segmentation is unreliable."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Order and customer management in Ghana must accommodate several local realities. **Multi-channel orders** are normal: a single SME may take orders on Instagram, WhatsApp, a website and by phone. Without a central record, orders are lost and stock is oversold. Even a shared spreadsheet or low-cost OMS that brings all channels into one queue is a significant improvement.\n\n**Delivery** is commonly handled by motorbike riders and courier firms, and failed deliveries due to unclear locations are a frequent cost. Capturing digital addresses and landmarks at checkout and calling or messaging customers before dispatch reduce failures. **Pay on delivery** remains popular where trust is limited, so rider cash collections must be reconciled daily against delivered orders, a control discussed further in Module 5. Mobile money collection on delivery, using a merchant number or payment link, reduces cash-handling risk.\n\nFor **CRM**, WhatsApp Business is widely used as an operational CRM channel, with labels for customer stages and broadcast lists for promotions. However, marketing messages should be sent with consent and in line with the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843). As businesses grow, they benefit from moving customer histories out of individual staff phones into shared systems, which is collaborative CRM in practice and also protects the business when staff leave."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Zappos: customer service as strategy\n\nZappos, an American online shoe and clothing retailer founded in 1999, became widely known for treating customer service as its central competitive strategy. It publicised generous policies such as free shipping and free returns and encouraged customer service staff to spend as long as needed on calls rather than minimising call time. Amazon acquired Zappos in 2009.\n\nThe case links order management and CRM. Generous returns policies reduce the perceived risk of buying shoes online, where fit is uncertain, and so increase conversion. However, they make **reverse logistics** a core operational capability: returns must be collected, inspected and restocked efficiently, and their cost must be built into the business model. The emphasis on service also illustrates **operational CRM** (empowered service staff) and the logic of **retention** argued by Reichheld and Sasser: loyal customers who trust the returns process buy repeatedly and recommend the firm. A critical reading should ask whether such policies are affordable for SMEs with thin margins, and how a Ghanaian retailer might adapt the principle, for example by offering exchanges at pick-up points rather than free home collection."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** The sale is complete when the customer pays. **Correction:** Fulfilment, delivery, returns and service determine whether the customer is satisfied and returns.","**Misconception:** CRM is a software package. **Correction:** CRM is a customer-focused strategy supported by processes, data and technology.","**Misconception:** Dropshipping is a risk-free way to start e-commerce. **Correction:** It avoids inventory costs but brings long delivery times, quality control problems and thin margins.","**Misconception:** All customers deserve the same marketing effort. **Correction:** Segmentation such as RFM directs effort to where it creates most value.","**Misconception:** Collecting as much customer data as possible is always beneficial. **Correction:** Data protection law and good practice require collecting only necessary data with appropriate consent and security."],"examMistakes":["Listing order statuses without explaining the events and systems that move an order between them.","Ignoring failure paths such as payment failure, failed delivery and returns.","Defining CRM only as software, or failing to distinguish operational, analytical and collaborative CRM.","Performing RFM scoring without proposing differentiated actions for each segment.","Omitting data protection when discussing customer data in Ghana."],"criticalQuestions":["Should a Ghanaian SME offer free returns to compete with larger marketplaces? Under what conditions would this be sustainable?","RFM ignores profit margins. How would you adapt it for a retailer whose products have very different margins?","How might pay on delivery affect both order management costs and customer trust, and how could mobile money change this balance?","Is WhatsApp an adequate CRM tool for a growing business? What risks does relying on it create?"],"exercises":["**Lifecycle mapping.** Map the order lifecycle for an illustrative food delivery business, including at least three failure paths. *Expected output:* a flowchart with statuses, triggering events and the system responsible for each transition.","**RFM practice.** Create a dataset of 15 illustrative customers and apply the RFM bands from this lesson. *Expected output:* a scored table, segment labels and one targeted action per segment.","**Fulfilment choice.** Recommend a fulfilment model for an illustrative Takoradi electronics accessory seller expanding to Accra. *Expected output:* a comparison of at least three models against cost, control, speed and risk, with a justified recommendation."],"takeaways":["The order lifecycle runs from placement through payment, allocation, fulfilment and delivery to completion, with failure and return paths.","An OMS unifies multi-channel orders, synchronises inventory and coordinates payment, delivery and notifications.","Fulfilment models (in-house, 3PL, dropshipping, marketplace) trade control against cost and scalability.","CRM is a strategy supported by operational, analytical and collaborative capabilities across the customer lifecycle.","RFM segmentation directs retention efforts, but depends on accurate order data and ignores margins."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Buttle, F., & Maklan, S. (2019). *Customer relationship management: Concepts and technologies* (4th ed.). Routledge.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Reichheld, F. F., & Sasser, W. E. (1990). Zero defections: Quality comes to services. *Harvard Business Review, 68*(5), 105-111.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Laudon, K. C., & Traver, C. G. *E-commerce: Business, technology, society*. Pearson. (Consult the most recent edition available in your library.)","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Chaffey, D., Hemphill, T., & Edmundson-Bird, D. (2019). *Digital business and e-commerce management* (7th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"MIT OpenCourseWare","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/","description":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology. Official open learning portal; search for supply chain, logistics and operations management materials.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"HubSpot Academy","url":"https://academy.hubspot.com/","description":"HubSpot. Official free learning portal with courses on customer relationship management and customer service.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Order management coordinates the journey from order placement to payment confirmation, stock allocation, fulfilment, delivery, completion and returns, while handling failures such as payment timeouts and failed deliveries. Fulfilment can be in-house, outsourced to 3PLs, dropshipped or handled by marketplaces. CRM is a strategy for acquiring, developing, retaining and winning back customers, supported by operational, analytical and collaborative capabilities and constrained by data protection law.\n\n- Key OMS functions: multi-channel capture, payment tracking, inventory sync, routing, delivery, notifications, returns.\n- Overselling and lost orders are typical SME failures without a central OMS.\n- RFM = Recency, Frequency, Monetary; segments get differentiated actions.\n- Retention is usually cheaper than acquisition (Reichheld and Sasser).\n- In Ghana: multi-channel orders, rider delivery, pay-on-delivery reconciliation, Act 843."}'::jsonb, 2600, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('e6d3ebef-21b1-526b-93ca-c198e8ee3ae8', 'e09cc604-b642-50f6-acc7-46a7c60f4aac', 'digital-marketing-and-analytics', 'Digital Marketing and E-Commerce Analytics', 'Compare digital marketing channels, analyse an e-commerce conversion funnel, and calculate and interpret conversion rate, customer acquisition cost, average order value and customer lifetime value to guide marketing decisions.', array['Digital marketing', 'Analytics', 'Conversion funnels']::text[], 55, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"An online shop with no visitors makes no sales, and an online shop with many visitors but few buyers wastes its marketing budget. Digital marketing brings potential customers to the store; analytics tells the business which efforts work, where customers drop out and whether each new customer is worth what it cost to acquire. Because nearly every digital interaction can be measured, e-commerce managers can make decisions on evidence rather than intuition, provided they choose the right measures and interpret them correctly.\n\nThis lesson connects marketing and measurement. You will compare the main digital marketing channels, study the conversion funnel, and work through the core metrics that examiners and employers expect you to calculate: **conversion rate**, **customer acquisition cost (CAC)**, **average order value (AOV)** and **customer lifetime value (CLV)**. You will also consider attribution, experimentation and the limits of analytics, with applications to Ghanaian SMEs whose marketing often happens on social media and WhatsApp.","objectives":["Compare the main digital marketing channels by cost, reach, intent and measurability.","Explain the conversion funnel and identify where and why customers drop out.","Calculate conversion rate, CAC, AOV, CLV and return on ad spend from given data.","Evaluate marketing performance using the CLV-to-CAC ratio and funnel analysis.","Explain attribution models and A/B testing, and critique the limitations of analytics."],"prerequisites":["The storefront and checkout concepts and the CRM lesson earlier in this module.","Comfort with basic arithmetic: percentages, ratios and averages."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Conversion rate","definition":"The percentage of visitors (or sessions) that complete a desired action, usually a purchase."},{"term":"Customer acquisition cost (CAC)","definition":"Total sales and marketing cost in a period divided by the number of new customers acquired in that period."},{"term":"Average order value (AOV)","definition":"Total revenue divided by the number of orders."},{"term":"Customer lifetime value (CLV)","definition":"The estimated value, usually in gross margin terms, that a customer generates over the whole relationship with the business."},{"term":"Return on ad spend (ROAS)","definition":"Revenue attributed to advertising divided by the advertising cost."},{"term":"Attribution model","definition":"A rule for assigning credit for a conversion to the marketing touchpoints that preceded it."},{"term":"A/B test","definition":"A controlled experiment comparing two versions of a page, message or offer by randomly assigning users to each."},{"term":"Owned, paid and earned media","definition":"Channels the firm controls (website, email list), channels it pays for (ads), and exposure it earns from others (reviews, shares, press)."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Digital marketing channels\n\nChaffey and Ellis-Chadwick group digital marketing into a set of channels. Each differs in cost structure, the intent of the audience reached and how easily results are measured.\n\n| Channel | How it works | Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Search engine optimisation (SEO) | Improving content and site structure to rank in organic search results | Reaches high-intent searchers; no cost per click | Slow to build; depends on search engine algorithms |\n| Paid search (SEM / pay-per-click) | Bidding for ads shown against search queries | Immediate, high intent, precise measurement | Costs rise with competition; stops when spending stops |\n| Social media (organic and paid) | Content and ads on platforms such as Facebook, Instagram and TikTok | Large audiences, visual products, precise targeting | Lower purchase intent; organic reach is limited |\n| Influencer and affiliate marketing | Paying creators or partners to promote products, often per sale | Borrowed trust and reach | Hard to verify audience quality; disclosure duties |\n| Email and SMS marketing | Messages to customers who have opted in | Low cost, strong for retention | Requires consent and a good list; risk of spam |\n| Messaging (for example WhatsApp Business) | Catalogues, broadcasts and one-to-one chat | Personal, conversational, familiar to customers | Labour-intensive; consent and over-messaging risks |\n| Display advertising | Banner and video ads on websites and apps | Awareness at scale, retargeting | Low click rates; ad blindness; fraud risk |\n| Content marketing | Useful articles, videos and guides | Builds trust and SEO | Slow; quality content is costly |\n\nThe **owned, paid and earned** framework is useful for strategy: paid media can buy initial reach, owned media (an email list, a WhatsApp community) lowers the cost of reaching customers repeatedly, and earned media (reviews, shares) builds credibility that paid media cannot buy.\n\n### The conversion funnel\n\nA conversion funnel represents the stages a visitor moves through before buying: **visit → view product → add to cart → begin checkout → purchase**. Fewer people reach each stage. Analysing the **step conversion rate** between stages shows where the largest losses occur. A sharp drop from product view to add-to-cart suggests problems with price, product information or images; a drop from checkout to purchase points to payment problems, hidden costs or trust concerns (as discussed in the storefront lesson).\n\nThe **AIDA** model (Attention, Interest, Desire, Action) is an older marketing framework that maps onto the funnel and reminds marketers that channels play different roles: display and social ads build attention; content and product pages build interest and desire; checkout design and payment options enable action.\n\n### Core metrics and their formulas\n\n- `Conversion rate = purchases ÷ visitors × 100`\n- `CAC = total acquisition marketing and sales cost ÷ new customers acquired`\n- `AOV = total revenue ÷ number of orders`\n- `ROAS = revenue attributed to ads ÷ ad spend`\n- A simple CLV estimate: `CLV = AOV × purchases per year × gross margin % × expected customer lifespan in years`\n\nMore sophisticated CLV models discount future cash flows and use retention probabilities, but the simple version is adequate for many SME decisions and examinations. A widely used rule of thumb is that CLV should comfortably exceed CAC; if CAC approaches CLV, the business loses money acquiring customers.\n\n### Analytics tools and data\n\nWeb analytics platforms such as Google Analytics collect data about sessions, traffic sources, pages viewed and events such as add-to-cart and purchase. **UTM parameters** added to campaign links identify which campaign brought each visitor. Social media platforms report their own ad metrics (impressions, clicks, cost per click), and payment and order systems provide the authoritative revenue figures. Analysts must reconcile these sources, because each platform tends to measure differently."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Attribution models\n\nCustomers often interact with several touchpoints before buying: an Instagram ad, a Google search, a WhatsApp message. Attribution decides how to share the credit.\n\n| Model | Rule | Bias |\n|---|---|---|\n| Last-click | All credit to the final touchpoint | Over-credits search and retargeting, under-credits awareness channels |\n| First-click | All credit to the first touchpoint | Over-credits awareness channels |\n| Linear | Equal credit to all touchpoints | Ignores differing influence |\n| Time-decay | More credit to touchpoints closer to purchase | Still assumes a fixed pattern |\n| Data-driven | Credit estimated statistically from observed paths | Requires substantial data and is less transparent |\n\nNo model is \"true\"; each embodies assumptions. Managers should be aware that changing the model can change which channel appears most profitable.\n\n### Experimentation: A/B testing\n\nAn A/B test randomly assigns visitors to version A (control) or B (variant) and compares outcomes, such as conversion rate. Randomisation means differences can be attributed to the change rather than to other factors. Good practice includes defining the metric and sample size in advance, running the test long enough to cover weekly patterns, and avoiding stopping as soon as a difference appears, which inflates false positives. For small SMEs with low traffic, tests may take too long to reach reliable conclusions, so qualitative evidence (customer interviews, chat logs) is also valuable.\n\n### Limits of analytics\n\nAnalytics measure behaviour, not motivation. Cookie restrictions, privacy controls and users switching devices create gaps. Correlation in dashboards is not causation: a channel may appear to drive sales because it reaches people who would have bought anyway. Data protection obligations, including Ghana''s Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843), limit how personal data can be collected and used."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Visitors: 20,000\"] -->|\"40% view a product\"| B[\"Product views: 8,000\"]\n    B -->|\"15% add to cart\"| C[\"Add to cart: 1,200\"]\n    C -->|\"50% begin checkout\"| D[\"Begin checkout: 600\"]\n    D -->|\"66.7% complete payment\"| E[\"Purchases: 400\"]\n    E --> F[\"Overall conversion rate: 2%\"]\n```"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *StyleHub GH* is a hypothetical online fashion retailer in Accra. In one month (all figures are invented for practice):\n\n- Visitors: 20,000\n- Product page views (unique visitors): 8,000\n- Add to cart: 1,200\n- Began checkout: 600\n- Orders: 400, of which 300 came from new customers\n- Revenue: GHS 100,000\n- Marketing spend on acquisition (social ads, influencer fees, paid search): GHS 30,000, of which GHS 18,000 was social ads generating GHS 54,000 of attributed revenue\n- Gross margin: 40 per cent\n- Customers buy on average 3 times a year and remain customers for about 2 years\n\n**Step 1: Conversion rate.** `400 ÷ 20,000 × 100 = 2%`.\n\n**Step 2: Funnel step rates.** View rate `8,000 ÷ 20,000 = 40%`; add-to-cart rate `1,200 ÷ 8,000 = 15%`; checkout start `600 ÷ 1,200 = 50%`; checkout completion `400 ÷ 600 ≈ 66.7%`. The weakest step is product view to add-to-cart (15 per cent), suggesting that product pages, pricing or sizing information should be investigated first.\n\n**Step 3: AOV.** `100,000 ÷ 400 = GHS 250`.\n\n**Step 4: CAC.** `30,000 ÷ 300 = GHS 100` per new customer.\n\n**Step 5: ROAS for social ads.** `54,000 ÷ 18,000 = 3.0`, meaning GHS 3 of revenue per GHS 1 of ad spend. Because gross margin is 40 per cent, each GHS 1 of ad spend produced `3.0 × 0.40 = GHS 1.20` of gross margin, a modest positive return before other costs.\n\n**Step 6: CLV.** `250 × 3 × 0.40 × 2 = GHS 600` of gross margin over the relationship.\n\n**Step 7: CLV-to-CAC ratio.** `600 ÷ 100 = 6`. Each new customer is expected to generate six times their acquisition cost in gross margin, which suggests acquisition spending is sustainable, provided the retention assumptions hold.\n\n**Step 8: Interpretation and decisions.**\n1. Improve the add-to-cart step: add size guides, more photos and clearer delivery fees. If the add-to-cart rate rose from 15 to 18 per cent with other step rates unchanged, purchases would rise to `8,000 × 0.18 × 0.5 × 0.667 ≈ 480`, an increase of about 80 orders without extra traffic.\n2. Validate the CLV assumptions with real repeat-purchase data from the CRM; if customers actually buy only once, CLV falls to `250 × 1 × 0.40 × 1 = GHS 100`, equal to CAC, and the acquisition strategy would no longer be profitable.\n3. Shift some budget towards owned media (email, WhatsApp community) to lower the cost of repeat purchases.\n\nThe example shows why metrics must be interpreted together: a healthy ROAS or CLV-to-CAC ratio can hide a weak funnel step or optimistic assumptions."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Digital marketing in Ghana is strongly social and mobile. Many SMEs rely on Instagram, Facebook, TikTok and WhatsApp to show products, answer questions and close sales. Influencer marketing is common, especially in fashion, beauty and food. These channels are effective for reach and trust, but they create a **measurement challenge**: when a sale closes in a WhatsApp chat and is paid by mobile money, it may not appear in web analytics at all. SMEs can respond with simple techniques: unique discount codes per influencer, campaign-specific WhatsApp links or numbers, asking customers \"how did you hear about us?\" at checkout, and recording the source of each order in the order management system.\n\nSMS marketing remains useful where customers have basic phones or limited data, but bulk messaging without consent damages trust and may breach data protection requirements. Payment and messaging providers such as Hubtel offer business messaging alongside payment services, which can help link campaigns to transactions. Finally, Ghanaian consumers are often price- and data-sensitive, so lightweight landing pages and clear delivery costs can matter as much as ad creativity in improving the funnel."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Dollar Shave Club: content, subscription and lifetime value\n\nDollar Shave Club, a United States company that sold razors by subscription, gained wide attention in 2012 with a low-budget, humorous launch video shared widely online. The video is often cited as an example of earned media and content marketing: a single piece of content generated large awareness at little paid cost. Unilever acquired the company in 2016.\n\nThe case is useful for analytics because the business model depended on **lifetime value** rather than first-order profit. A subscription razor service earns modest revenue per shipment, so acquisition spending is justified only if customers stay subscribed for many months. This makes **retention and churn** central metrics, and it illustrates why CLV-to-CAC thinking is essential for subscription e-commerce. It also illustrates the limitation of viral marketing: earned attention is hard to repeat on demand, so sustained growth requires a reliable mix of paid, owned and earned channels and careful measurement of which customers stay."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** More traffic always means more sales. **Correction:** If the funnel is weak, extra traffic raises costs without proportionate sales; improving conversion can be cheaper.","**Misconception:** ROAS above 1 means advertising is profitable. **Correction:** ROAS uses revenue, not profit; with a 40 per cent margin, a ROAS below 2.5 loses money before other costs.","**Misconception:** Last-click attribution shows the true value of each channel. **Correction:** It is one assumption among several and tends to undervalue awareness channels.","**Misconception:** CLV is a fact. **Correction:** CLV is an estimate built on assumptions about frequency, margin and retention that must be validated.","**Misconception:** A test that shows a difference after one day proves the variant is better. **Correction:** Small samples and early stopping produce unreliable results."],"examMistakes":["Calculating CAC using all customers instead of new customers, or AOV using visitors instead of orders.","Computing CLV from revenue without applying gross margin, then comparing it with CAC.","Reporting metrics without interpretation or recommendations.","Ignoring the measurement gap created by social and WhatsApp sales in Ghana.","Describing channels without comparing cost, intent and measurability."],"criticalQuestions":["Should a Ghanaian fashion SME prioritise influencer marketing or search advertising? What data would you collect to decide?","If privacy controls reduce the data available to analytics platforms, how should e-commerce firms adapt their measurement?","Is a CLV-to-CAC ratio of 6 always a sign of a healthy business? What might it conceal?","How should a business balance personalisation with customers'' expectations of privacy?"],"exercises":["**Metric calculation.** An illustrative shop has 15,000 visitors, 300 orders, revenue of GHS 54,000, acquisition spend of GHS 16,000 and 200 new customers. Calculate conversion rate, AOV and CAC. *Expected output:* conversion rate 2%, AOV GHS 180, CAC GHS 80, with a sentence interpreting each.","**CLV sensitivity.** Using AOV GHS 180, gross margin 35 per cent and a lifespan of 2 years, calculate CLV for purchase frequencies of 1, 2 and 4 times a year and compare each with CAC of GHS 80. *Expected output:* CLVs of GHS 126, GHS 252 and GHS 504, with CLV-to-CAC ratios and a conclusion.","**Tracking plan.** Design a measurement plan for an SME that sells through Instagram, WhatsApp and a website. *Expected output:* a table of channels, tracking method (UTM links, discount codes, order source field) and the metric reported."],"takeaways":["Digital channels differ in cost, intent, reach and measurability; combine paid, owned and earned media.","The conversion funnel reveals where customers drop out; fix the weakest step first.","Conversion rate, CAC, AOV, ROAS and CLV must be calculated correctly and interpreted together.","CLV should comfortably exceed CAC, but CLV rests on assumptions that need validation.","Attribution models and A/B tests guide decisions but have biases and limits; privacy and consent constrain data use."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Chaffey, D., & Ellis-Chadwick, F. (2019). *Digital marketing* (7th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Kohavi, R., Tang, D., & Xu, Y. (2020). *Trustworthy online controlled experiments: A practical guide to A/B testing*. Cambridge University Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Chaffey, D., Hemphill, T., & Edmundson-Bird, D. (2019). *Digital business and e-commerce management* (7th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Google. Google Analytics Help.","url":"https://support.google.com/analytics/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"Google Skillshop","url":"https://skillshop.withgoogle.com/","description":"Google. Official free training portal with courses on Google Analytics and Google Ads.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"HubSpot Academy","url":"https://academy.hubspot.com/","description":"HubSpot. Official free learning portal with courses on inbound, content and email marketing.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Digital marketing channels (SEO, paid search, social, influencer and affiliate, email and SMS, messaging, display and content) differ in cost, intent and measurability, and are best combined across paid, owned and earned media. The conversion funnel shows where visitors drop out. Core metrics are conversion rate, CAC, AOV, ROAS and CLV; CLV should comfortably exceed CAC, but its assumptions must be tested. Attribution models and A/B tests support decisions but have limitations.\n\n- Conversion rate = purchases ÷ visitors × 100.\n- CAC = acquisition cost ÷ new customers; AOV = revenue ÷ orders.\n- CLV = AOV × frequency × margin × lifespan (simple model).\n- ROAS uses revenue; convert to margin before judging profitability.\n- In Ghana, track social and WhatsApp sales with codes, links and order-source fields."}'::jsonb, 2699, 3, true)
