insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('bc4d46ef-74d1-5bba-bcbe-fe7370a6dee8', '998d23b2-73cf-5989-a5c8-16d3349e3a25', 'fintech-and-digital-banking', 'FinTech and Digital Banking', 'Classify the main categories of FinTech, explain how digital banking channels, open banking and bank-FinTech partnerships work, and evaluate the opportunities and risks they create for customers, institutions and regulators in Ghana and internationally.', array['FinTech', 'Digital banking']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Financial services have always depended on information: who owns what, who owes whom, and whether a borrower can be trusted. Because finance is so information-intensive, it has been reshaped repeatedly by technology, from the telegraph to the automated teller machine. The current wave, usually called **FinTech**, combines mobile devices, cloud computing, application programming interfaces (APIs), data analytics and new business models to deliver payments, credit, savings, insurance and investment in new ways and often by new kinds of firms.\n\nIn Ghana, the effect is visible in everyday life: people pay bills by mobile money, check balances through USSD codes, open accounts on mobile apps and receive small loans to their phones. This lesson explains what FinTech is, maps its main categories, examines how digital banking works and evaluates the risks that accompany innovation. It is the foundation for the following lessons on mobile money and payment gateways. The content is educational and is not financial, investment or regulatory advice.","objectives":["Define FinTech and explain why finance is especially open to digital innovation.","Classify FinTech activities into payments, lending, savings and investment, insurance, regulatory technology and infrastructure.","Describe digital banking channels, including internet banking, mobile apps, USSD and agency banking.","Explain open banking and the role of APIs in bank-FinTech collaboration.","Evaluate the opportunities and risks of FinTech for customers, incumbents and regulators."],"prerequisites":["The platform and network effect concepts from Module 2.","General awareness of how bank accounts, loans and payments work."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"FinTech","definition":"Technology-enabled innovation in financial services that may produce new business models, applications, processes or products."},{"term":"Digital banking","definition":"The provision of banking services through digital channels such as internet banking, mobile apps and USSD, potentially including fully digital account opening and lending."},{"term":"Neobank (digital-only bank)","definition":"A bank or banking service that operates primarily through digital channels without a traditional branch network."},{"term":"USSD (Unstructured Supplementary Service Data)","definition":"A mobile network protocol that provides menu-based interactive sessions on basic phones without internet access."},{"term":"Agency banking","definition":"Delivery of selected banking services through third-party agents, such as shops, on behalf of a licensed institution."},{"term":"Open banking","definition":"A framework in which banks, with customer consent, share account data or initiate payments through secure APIs to authorised third parties."},{"term":"Regulatory sandbox","definition":"A controlled environment in which a regulator allows firms to test innovative products with real customers under specified conditions and safeguards."},{"term":"RegTech","definition":"Technology used to make regulatory compliance and supervision more efficient, for example automated transaction monitoring."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### What makes FinTech different\n\nArner, Barberis and Buckley (2016) describe FinTech as the latest stage in a long history of interaction between finance and technology, distinguished by the speed of change and by the entry of non-bank firms, including technology companies and start-ups, into activities once dominated by banks. Philippon (2016) argued that financial intermediation had remained costly despite earlier technological advances, and that FinTech had the potential to lower costs and widen access.\n\nThree features explain why finance is so open to digital innovation. First, financial products are largely **information**, so they can be created and delivered digitally. Second, **network effects** are strong in payments: a payment method is more valuable the more people and merchants use it. Third, **data** about transactions allows new ways of assessing risk, such as using mobile money histories to estimate creditworthiness.\n\n### Categories of FinTech\n\n| Category | What it does | Examples of activity |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payments and transfers | Moving money between people, businesses and governments | Mobile money, payment gateways, remittance apps, QR payments |\n| Digital lending | Credit decisions and disbursement through digital channels | Mobile micro-loans, buy-now-pay-later, SME invoice finance, peer-to-peer lending |\n| Savings and investment (WealthTech) | Digital savings, investment and asset management | Mobile savings products, robo-advisers, digital access to treasury bills |\n| InsurTech | Digital distribution, pricing and claims in insurance | Micro-insurance sold via mobile phones, usage-based insurance |\n| RegTech and SupTech | Technology for compliance and supervision | Automated anti-money-laundering monitoring, digital regulatory reporting |\n| Infrastructure | Underlying systems used by other providers | Core banking in the cloud, APIs, identity verification, switches |\n| Digital assets | Cryptocurrencies, tokenisation and distributed ledgers | Covered in Module 6 |\n\nCategories overlap. A mobile money service starts in payments, then adds savings, loans and insurance through partnerships, becoming a financial platform.\n\n### Digital banking channels\n\nBanks deliver digital services through several channels, each suited to different customers:\n\n1. **Internet banking**: browser-based access, common for business customers handling bulk payments and statements.\n2. **Mobile banking apps**: smartphone apps offering balance checks, transfers, bill payments, card controls and sometimes account opening with identity verification.\n3. **USSD banking**: menu sessions accessed by dialling a short code, working on basic phones without data; widely used in Ghana for its accessibility, although sessions are short and the interface limited.\n4. **Agency banking**: agents provide cash deposits and withdrawals on behalf of a bank, extending reach without branches.\n5. **ATMs, cards and point-of-sale**: still important, increasingly integrated with mobile channels.\n\nBehind these channels sit the **core banking system** (accounts and ledgers), **middleware and APIs** that expose services to channels and partners, and **security systems** for authentication and fraud monitoring.\n\n### Incumbents, challengers and partnerships\n\nFinTech is sometimes described as a contest between banks and start-ups, but collaboration is more common. Banks hold licences, deposits, customer trust and compliance expertise; FinTechs bring speed, design skills and new data-driven models. Typical arrangements include FinTechs using a bank''s licence and accounts (\"banking as a service\"), banks partnering with mobile money operators to offer savings and loans to wallet holders, and banks acquiring or investing in FinTechs. Large technology firms, sometimes called *BigTech*, enter finance through payments embedded in their platforms, raising new questions about competition and data."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Open banking\n\nOpen banking rests on the idea that customers own their financial data and may authorise third parties to use it. Banks expose two main kinds of API: **account information** (reading balances and transactions, with consent) and **payment initiation** (instructing a payment from the customer''s account). This lets third parties build budgeting tools, lending products that analyse real cash flows, or checkout options that pay directly from a bank account. The framework depends on standards, strong customer authentication, consent management and clear liability rules.\n\n### Risk framework for FinTech\n\nA structured way to evaluate FinTech is to consider risks to four groups:\n\n| Stakeholder | Opportunities | Risks |\n|---|---|---|\n| Customers | Lower cost, convenience, access for the unbanked | Fraud, over-indebtedness from easy credit, data misuse, unclear redress |\n| Incumbent banks | Efficiency, new channels, partnerships | Loss of customer relationship, margin pressure |\n| FinTech firms | Rapid growth, new markets | Funding dependence, regulatory uncertainty, cyber attack |\n| Regulators and the system | Inclusion, competition, efficiency | Consumer harm, money laundering, operational and concentration risk |\n\nRegulators commonly respond with **activity-based** licensing (regulating what a firm does rather than what it is called), **regulatory sandboxes**, and supervision supported by RegTech and SupTech."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    C[\"Customer\"] --> CH[\"Channels: app, USSD, internet, agent\"]\n    CH --> API[\"API and middleware layer\"]\n    API --> CORE[\"Core banking: accounts and ledger\"]\n    API --> SEC[\"Authentication and fraud monitoring\"]\n    API --> FT[\"Authorised third parties via open APIs (with consent)\"]\n    FT --> P1[\"Budgeting and analytics apps\"]\n    FT --> P2[\"Digital lenders using cash-flow data\"]\n    FT --> P3[\"Payment initiation at checkout\"]\n    CORE --> SW[\"Payment switches and settlement systems\"]\n```"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *QuickCedi* is a hypothetical digital lender in Ghana, partnered with a licensed institution, offering 30-day micro-loans to mobile money users. It is considering offering GHS 500 loans with a fee of 6 per cent. This example teaches risk-based reasoning; it is not lending or pricing advice, and real lending is subject to regulation and consumer protection rules.\n\n**Step 1: Understand expected loss.** Lenders estimate expected loss with the formula `Expected loss = PD × LGD × EAD`, where PD is the probability of default, LGD the loss given default (share not recovered) and EAD the exposure at default.\n\n**Step 2: Apply initial estimates.** Using a simple scoring model, QuickCedi estimates PD = 8 per cent, LGD = 90 per cent and EAD = GHS 500. Expected loss per loan = `0.08 × 0.90 × 500 = GHS 36`.\n\n**Step 3: Estimate expected fee income.** Only non-defaulting borrowers pay the fee: `0.92 × (500 × 0.06) = 0.92 × 30 = GHS 27.60`.\n\n**Step 4: Compare.** Expected contribution before operating costs = `27.60 − 36 = −GHS 8.40` per loan. The product loses money even before costs.\n\n**Step 5: Consider responses.**\n- *Better data and scoring*: if transaction-history data reduce PD to 4 per cent for approved applicants, expected loss = `0.04 × 0.90 × 500 = GHS 18`, and fee income = `0.96 × 30 = GHS 28.80`, giving a contribution of `+GHS 10.80`.\n- *Smaller first loans*: starting customers at lower limits and increasing them after on-time repayment reduces exposure while data accumulates.\n- *Raising fees*: may appear to fix the numbers but can increase over-indebtedness and attract riskier borrowers (adverse selection), and can raise consumer protection concerns.\n\n**Step 6: Evaluate.** The example shows why data is central to FinTech lending: the business model works only if digital data genuinely improve risk assessment. It also shows the ethical dimension: profitable lending should not rely on borrowers falling into repeated debt."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Ghana''s financial system shows a strong FinTech presence. **Mobile money** is widely used for transfers, bill payments and merchant payments, and operators have partnered with regulated financial institutions to offer savings and micro-credit products to wallet holders. **Banks** offer mobile apps and USSD banking, and many use agents to extend cash services. **Payment service providers** such as Paystack and Hubtel provide merchants with integrations that accept cards, mobile money and other methods.\n\nThe regulatory framework is led by the **Bank of Ghana**. The Payment Systems and Services Act, 2019 (Act 987) provides for the licensing and regulation of payment systems, electronic money issuers and payment service providers. The Bank of Ghana has also engaged with innovation through measures such as a regulatory sandbox framework for testing new products, and the Ghana Interbank Payment and Settlement Systems (GhIPSS), a subsidiary of the Bank of Ghana, operates national payment infrastructure including interoperability services discussed in the next lesson. Students analysing a Ghanaian FinTech should therefore consider licensing category, partnerships with banks or operators, consumer protection, data protection under the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) and cyber security. For specific compliance questions, the official texts and qualified advisers must be consulted."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Open banking in the United Kingdom\n\nIn the United Kingdom, the Competition and Markets Authority concluded a market investigation into retail banking in 2016 and required the largest banks to implement open banking. Common API standards were developed by an implementation entity set up for the purpose, and open banking went live in January 2018. Alongside this, the European Union''s revised Payment Services Directive (PSD2) required banks to give authorised third parties access to payment accounts with customer consent.\n\nThe case illustrates several points. First, open banking was introduced primarily as a **competition** measure: by allowing customers to share data, it aimed to reduce the advantage incumbents gained from holding exclusive customer information. Second, it required **standards and governance**: without common API standards, security requirements and dispute rules, third parties would face a different integration for every bank. Third, it created new products, such as budgeting apps and account-to-account payments, but adoption depended on customer trust in sharing data. For Ghana and other emerging markets, the lesson is that APIs alone do not create an open ecosystem; regulation, standards, consent management and trust are equally important."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** FinTech means start-ups replacing banks. **Correction:** Most FinTech involves partnership between banks, operators and technology firms, and incumbents are major FinTech adopters.","**Misconception:** Digital banking is just a mobile app. **Correction:** It includes USSD, internet banking and agency banking, supported by core systems, APIs and security controls.","**Misconception:** Open banking means banks publish customer data. **Correction:** Data are shared only with authorised third parties and only with the customer''s consent.","**Misconception:** Easy digital credit always promotes inclusion. **Correction:** Poorly designed digital credit can cause over-indebtedness; responsible lending and transparency are essential.","**Misconception:** Regulators either ban or ignore FinTech. **Correction:** Many use activity-based licensing, sandboxes and supervisory technology to balance innovation and protection."],"examMistakes":["Defining FinTech vaguely without classifying its categories or giving examples.","Ignoring USSD and agency banking when discussing digital banking in Ghana.","Describing open banking without mentioning consent, APIs and standards.","Evaluating FinTech only in terms of benefits, without risks to consumers and the financial system.","Stating regulatory details that are not certain; refer to the Bank of Ghana and Act 987 in general terms unless you know the specifics."],"criticalQuestions":["Does FinTech increase or reduce competition in Ghana''s financial services market? Consider network effects and partnerships.","Should digital lenders be allowed to use mobile money transaction data and phone metadata for credit scoring? What safeguards would you require?","Would open banking APIs be valuable in Ghana given the importance of mobile money wallets alongside bank accounts?","How should regulators balance financial inclusion goals against consumer protection when approving new digital credit products?"],"exercises":["**Category mapping.** Identify eight financial services available on a Ghanaian smartphone or basic phone and classify each into a FinTech category. *Expected output:* an eight-row table with category, provider type (bank, operator, FinTech, partnership) and channel.","**Expected loss calculation.** For a GHS 1,000 loan with PD 6 per cent and LGD 80 per cent, calculate expected loss, and the fee rate needed for expected fee income from non-defaulters to cover it. *Expected output:* expected loss of GHS 48 and a required fee of about 5.1 per cent (`48 ÷ (0.94 × 1,000)`), with a comment on operating costs and fairness.","**Risk assessment.** Choose a FinTech product used in Ghana and complete the four-stakeholder risk framework. *Expected output:* a completed table and a 200-word evaluation."],"takeaways":["FinTech is technology-enabled innovation in financial services, spanning payments, lending, savings, insurance, RegTech and infrastructure.","Finance is information-intensive and network-driven, which makes it especially open to digital innovation.","Digital banking includes apps, USSD, internet banking and agency banking, built on core systems and APIs.","Open banking allows consented data sharing and payment initiation through standardised APIs.","FinTech creates opportunities for inclusion and efficiency but also risks of fraud, over-indebtedness and systemic concentration.","This lesson is educational and not financial, investment or regulatory advice."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Arner, D. W., Barberis, J., & Buckley, R. P. (2016). The evolution of FinTech: A new post-crisis paradigm? *Georgetown Journal of International Law, 47*(4), 1271-1319.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Philippon, T. (2016). *The FinTech opportunity* (NBER Working Paper No. 22476). National Bureau of Economic Research.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Demirgüç-Kunt, A., Klapper, L., Singer, D., & Ansar, S. (2022). *The Global Findex Database 2021: Financial inclusion, digital payments, and resilience in the age of COVID-19*. World Bank.","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/globalfindex","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Bank of Ghana. Official website.","url":"https://www.bog.gov.gh/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"World Bank Global Findex","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/globalfindex","description":"World Bank. Official page for the Global Findex database and related material on financial inclusion and digital payments.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Bank of Ghana","url":"https://www.bog.gov.gh/","description":"Bank of Ghana. Official central bank website with publications on payment systems and financial technology.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"FinTech is technology-enabled innovation in financial services that brings new firms, channels and business models into payments, lending, savings, insurance and compliance. Digital banking delivers services through apps, USSD, internet banking and agents, supported by core systems and APIs. Open banking allows customers to share data and initiate payments through authorised third parties. FinTech offers inclusion and efficiency but creates consumer, operational and systemic risks that regulators address with licensing, sandboxes and supervision.\n\n- Categories: payments, lending, WealthTech, InsurTech, RegTech/SupTech, infrastructure, digital assets.\n- Channels: app, USSD, internet, agency, cards and ATMs.\n- Open banking: consent + APIs (account information, payment initiation) + standards.\n- Expected loss = PD × LGD × EAD; data quality makes digital lending viable.\n- Ghana: Bank of Ghana, Act 987, GhIPSS, mobile money partnerships, Act 843."}'::jsonb, 2580, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('2972e880-4bff-5a77-a19f-61c6a3f95e98', '998d23b2-73cf-5989-a5c8-16d3349e3a25', 'mobile-money-and-digital-wallets', 'Mobile Money and Digital Wallets', 'Explain how mobile money works, including e-money issuance, trust accounts, agents, float and cash-in/cash-out, compare types of digital wallets, and evaluate the role of interoperability in Ghana and the lessons of M-Pesa in Kenya.', array['Mobile money', 'Digital wallets', 'Interoperability']::text[], 55, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"For many people in Ghana, the first digital financial service they used was not a bank account or a credit card but a mobile money wallet. With a basic phone, a SIM card and an identity document, a customer can receive money from relatives, pay for electricity, buy airtime, pay a trader and save small amounts, all without visiting a bank branch. Behind this apparent simplicity sits a carefully designed system of electronic money, trust accounts, agent networks and settlement arrangements.\n\nThis lesson opens that system up. You will learn how mobile money creates and backs electronic value, how agents convert cash into digital value and back again, why agent **float** management is a daily operational challenge, how digital wallets differ, and why **interoperability** between operators and banks matters. The lesson draws on Ghana''s experience and on the well-documented case of M-Pesa in Kenya. It is educational and not financial or regulatory advice.","objectives":["Explain how mobile money e-value is issued and backed by funds held in trust or custodial accounts.","Describe the roles of customers, agents, merchants, operators and banks in a mobile money ecosystem.","Apply the concepts of cash-in, cash-out and float to analyse an agent''s liquidity position.","Compare stored-value, bank-linked and pass-through digital wallets.","Evaluate the benefits and challenges of mobile money interoperability, with reference to Ghana."],"prerequisites":["The FinTech categories and digital banking channels in the previous lesson.","The concept of network effects from Module 2."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Mobile money","definition":"A service that lets users store electronic value in an account linked to a mobile phone number and use it to send, receive and pay, typically accessible through USSD menus or apps."},{"term":"Electronic money (e-money)","definition":"Monetary value stored electronically, issued on receipt of funds and accepted as a means of payment, redeemable for cash."},{"term":"Trust (custodial) account","definition":"A bank account in which the issuer holds funds equal to the outstanding e-money, protecting customers'' value."},{"term":"Agent","definition":"A registered business, often a small shop or kiosk, that performs cash-in and cash-out and registers customers on behalf of a mobile money provider, earning commissions."},{"term":"Float (e-float)","definition":"The electronic value an agent holds in their agent wallet to serve cash-in transactions; agents also need physical cash to serve cash-out."},{"term":"Cash-in / cash-out","definition":"Converting physical cash to e-money (cash-in) and e-money to physical cash (cash-out)."},{"term":"Interoperability","definition":"The ability of customers of different providers or systems to transact with one another, for example sending money from one operator''s wallet to another''s or to a bank account."},{"term":"KYC (know your customer)","definition":"Procedures to verify customer identity, often applied in tiers that link transaction limits to the level of verification."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### How e-money is created and backed\n\nMobile money does not create new money in the way a bank does when it lends. When a customer gives an agent GHS 100 in cash and receives GHS 100 of e-value, the system has simply converted cash into an electronic claim. The provider (an electronic money issuer, frequently a subsidiary of a mobile network operator) holds funds equivalent to all outstanding e-money in **trust or custodial accounts** at one or more banks. The total e-value in all wallets should equal the funds held in these accounts. This 1:1 backing is what makes e-money safe for users, and regulators typically specify how the funds must be held and reconciled.\n\n### The ecosystem and its participants\n\n- **Customers** register, often with a national identity card, and use a PIN to authorise transactions.\n- **Agents** provide the human interface: registering customers, accepting cash-in, paying cash-out and explaining services. They are the cash network that makes mobile money usable where cash dominates.\n- **Merchants** accept payments into merchant wallets, sometimes by entering a merchant code or scanning a QR code.\n- **The operator / e-money issuer** runs the platform, sets prices and commissions, manages risk and complies with regulation.\n- **Banks** hold trust funds and increasingly connect wallets to bank accounts for transfers, savings and loans.\n- **The regulator**, in Ghana the Bank of Ghana, licenses issuers and oversees consumer protection, anti-money-laundering controls and system stability.\n\n### Agent mechanics: cash-in, cash-out and float\n\nAn agent must hold two forms of liquidity: **physical cash** and **e-float**.\n\n- In a **cash-in**, the customer gives cash to the agent and the agent transfers e-value from the agent''s wallet to the customer''s wallet. The agent''s cash *rises* and e-float *falls*.\n- In a **cash-out**, the customer transfers e-value to the agent and receives cash. The agent''s e-float *rises* and cash *falls*.\n\nThe agent''s total liquidity (cash plus e-float) changes only by commissions earned, but its *composition* shifts with every transaction. If an agent in a busy market receives many cash-ins, its e-float runs out and it must turn customers away; if a rural agent mainly serves cash-out (for example recipients of remittances), it runs out of cash. **Rebalancing** means converting cash into e-float or e-float into cash, typically by visiting a bank branch, a super-agent or an aggregator that manages liquidity for groups of agents. Poor liquidity management is one of the most common reasons for failed transactions and customer frustration.\n\n### Types of digital wallets\n\n| Wallet type | How value is held | Example | Key feature |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Stored-value (e-money) wallet | Value held by the wallet provider as e-money, backed by trust funds | Mobile money wallets such as MTN MoMo | Works without a bank account; agent network for cash |\n| Bank-linked wallet | Wallet is an interface to a bank account | A bank''s own mobile wallet | Funds stay in a bank deposit |\n| Pass-through (tokenised card) wallet | Stores a token representing a card; payment is charged to the card | Smartphone wallets such as Apple Pay or Google Pay | Security through tokenisation; requires a card |\n| Closed-loop wallet | Value usable only within one merchant or platform | Store or ride-hailing credit | Limited acceptance |\n\nDistinguishing these types matters for analysis: a stored-value wallet raises questions about trust funds and agents; a pass-through wallet raises questions about card networks and tokenisation (Module 5).\n\n### Interoperability\n\nWithout interoperability, mobile money networks are **closed loops**: a customer on one network cannot easily send money to a customer on another, and moving value between a wallet and a bank account is cumbersome. Users may hold several SIM cards and wallets, and the largest network enjoys a strong advantage from network effects. **Interoperability** connects the loops so that a transaction can flow between wallets of different operators and between wallets and bank accounts. It typically requires a central **switch** that routes messages between participants, common rules and technical standards, agreed fees, and a **settlement** mechanism in which participants'' net obligations are settled through their accounts at the settlement bank or central bank."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Network effects and interoperability\n\nMobile money displays strong **cross-side network effects**: customers value a wallet more when more merchants and recipients accept it, and merchants value acceptance more when more customers hold wallets. In a closed-loop market, these effects favour the largest provider. Interoperability changes the economics. It increases the value of the *whole system* to users, because anyone can pay anyone, but it reduces the exclusive network advantage of the largest provider. This explains why interoperability is often promoted by regulators, and why providers may differ in their enthusiasm for it. Competition then shifts towards price, reliability, agent quality and additional services.\n\n### Risk-based (tiered) KYC\n\nFinancial inclusion depends on letting people with limited documentation open accounts, while anti-money-laundering rules require identity verification. A widely used approach is **tiered KYC**: basic accounts with minimal verification have low balance and transaction limits, while fuller verification unlocks higher limits. The approach reflects risk-based regulation endorsed internationally by bodies such as the Financial Action Task Force."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    C1[\"Customer gives GHS 100 cash\"] --> A[\"Agent\"]\n    A -->|\"Cash-in: agent sends GHS 100 e-value\"| W1[\"Customer wallet +100\"]\n    W2[\"Customer wallet -50\"] -->|\"Cash-out: customer sends GHS 50 e-value\"| A\n    A -->|\"Agent pays GHS 50 cash\"| C2[\"Customer receives cash\"]\n    A -->|\"Rebalance cash and e-float\"| SA[\"Bank branch, super-agent or aggregator\"]\n    OP[\"E-money issuer platform\"] --- W1\n    OP --- W2\n    OP --> TA[\"Trust accounts at banks: funds equal total e-money\"]\n    OP --> SW[\"Interoperability switch\"]\n    SW --> OT[\"Other operators'' wallets\"]\n    SW --> BK[\"Bank accounts\"]\n```"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Esi''s Shop* is a hypothetical mobile money agent in a busy Kumasi market. She starts Monday with GHS 5,000 in cash and GHS 5,000 in e-float (total liquidity GHS 10,000). Commissions are ignored for simplicity.\n\n**Step 1: Morning transactions.** Traders deposit their takings before travelling to buy stock: cash-ins total GHS 4,200, and cash-outs total GHS 800.\n- Cash = `5,000 + 4,200 − 800 = GHS 8,400`\n- E-float = `5,000 − 4,200 + 800 = GHS 1,600`\n- Total liquidity = `8,400 + 1,600 = GHS 10,000` (unchanged, as expected).\n\n**Step 2: A problem appears.** At midday a trader wants to deposit GHS 2,500. Esi has only GHS 1,600 e-float, so she can serve only part of the deposit or must turn the customer away. Her cash is plentiful but cannot be used for a cash-in.\n\n**Step 3: Rebalance.** Esi takes GHS 4,000 cash to a nearby bank branch or super-agent and converts it to e-float. Now cash = `8,400 − 4,000 = GHS 4,400` and e-float = `1,600 + 4,000 = GHS 5,600`. She can serve the GHS 2,500 cash-in.\n\n**Step 4: Analyse the pattern.** Esi''s location produces a predictable *cash-in-heavy* morning. She can plan by starting the day with more e-float and less cash, or arrange a scheduled midday rebalance. A rural agent serving remittance recipients would face the opposite pattern and need more cash.\n\n**Step 5: Consider risks.** Holding GHS 8,400 in cash creates theft risk; rebalancing trips cost time and transport; and agents must guard against fraud such as fake \"reversal\" calls asking them to send e-value back. Liquidity management therefore combines forecasting, security and fraud awareness."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Mobile money is a central part of Ghana''s payments landscape. MTN Mobile Money (MTN MoMo) is the most widely known service, and other mobile network operators offer competing services, such as Telecel Cash (formerly Vodafone Cash). Customers use mobile money for person-to-person transfers, airtime and data, utility bills, school fees, merchant payments and, through partnerships with financial institutions, savings and small loans. A large agent network provides cash-in and cash-out across cities, towns and rural areas.\n\nThe regulatory basis is provided by the **Bank of Ghana**, and the Payment Systems and Services Act, 2019 (Act 987) provides for licensing of electronic money issuers and payment service providers. A key milestone was **mobile money interoperability**, introduced in 2018 through the Ghana Interbank Payment and Settlement Systems (GhIPSS), a subsidiary of the Bank of Ghana. GhIPSS provides the switching platform that allows customers to send money between wallets on different mobile money networks and between mobile money wallets and bank accounts. In terms of this lesson''s theory, interoperability connected closed loops into a national system, increased the value of mobile money for all users and shifted competition towards service quality and price.\n\nFor SMEs, the practical implications are significant. A shop with a merchant wallet can be paid by customers of different networks; payment service providers such as Paystack and Hubtel allow online merchants to accept mobile money from multiple networks through one integration; and businesses can pay suppliers and staff in bulk. Remaining challenges include fraud and social engineering scams targeting users and agents, agent liquidity constraints, transaction charges and levies that affect usage, and the need for financial literacy. Fees, limits and rules change, so check the Bank of Ghana and provider websites for current information."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### M-Pesa in Kenya\n\nM-Pesa was launched in Kenya in 2007 by Safaricom, a mobile network operator in which Vodafone held a stake. It allowed users to store value on their phones and send it to others by SMS-based menus, with a network of agents providing cash-in and cash-out. Adoption grew rapidly, and M-Pesa became one of the most studied examples of mobile money worldwide.\n\nResearch by economists William Jack and Tavneet Suri has been particularly influential. Jack and Suri (2014), in the *American Economic Review*, found that households with access to M-Pesa were better able to cope with negative shocks such as illness or crop failure, because the service made it cheaper and faster to receive support from relatives and friends across distances. In other words, mobile money reduced transaction costs and improved **risk sharing**. A later study by Suri and Jack (2016) in *Science* found evidence that access to mobile money was associated with reduced poverty, with notable effects for female-headed households.\n\nThe M-Pesa case highlights several lessons relevant to Ghana. First, the **agent network** was as important as the technology: without reliable cash-in and cash-out, e-money is of limited use. Second, **regulatory openness** allowed a non-bank to offer payments, with safeguards such as holding customer funds in trust accounts at banks. Third, strong **network effects** helped a single provider achieve a dominant position in Kenya, which illustrates why regulators in other markets have emphasised interoperability. A critical analysis should avoid assuming that M-Pesa''s results transfer automatically to other countries, because market structure, regulation and social networks differ."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** Mobile money operators create money when customers deposit. **Correction:** E-money is issued in exchange for funds that are held in trust or custodial accounts; it converts cash into an electronic claim rather than creating new money through lending.","**Misconception:** An agent with plenty of cash can serve any customer. **Correction:** Cash-in requires e-float; an agent rich in cash but short of e-float cannot accept deposits.","**Misconception:** All digital wallets work like mobile money. **Correction:** Pass-through wallets store card tokens, bank-linked wallets front bank accounts, and closed-loop wallets work only within one platform.","**Misconception:** Interoperability only benefits smaller operators. **Correction:** It raises the value of the whole system for users and merchants, although it reduces the largest provider''s exclusive network advantage.","**Misconception:** Mobile money replaces the need for banks. **Correction:** Banks hold trust funds, provide settlement and increasingly partner with operators for savings and credit."],"examMistakes":["Describing cash-in and cash-out without explaining their opposite effects on agent cash and e-float.","Failing to mention trust or custodial accounts when explaining why e-money is safe.","Treating interoperability as purely technical, without discussing switching, rules, fees and settlement.","Citing M-Pesa research inaccurately or exaggerating its findings; describe Jack and Suri''s results carefully.","Inventing specific Ghanaian statistics, fees or limits rather than using qualitative statements."],"criticalQuestions":["Interoperability reduces the network advantage of the largest operator. Why might regulators still favour it, and what might operators do in response?","How do transaction charges and taxes on electronic transfers influence the balance between mobile money and cash usage?","Should mobile money providers be permitted to pay interest on wallet balances? What risks and benefits arise?","To what extent can M-Pesa''s impact in Kenya be expected in Ghana, given differences in market structure and regulation?"],"exercises":["**Agent liquidity simulation.** An illustrative agent starts with GHS 3,000 cash and GHS 2,000 e-float. Transactions: cash-in 1,500; cash-out 700; cash-in 900; cash-out 300. Calculate cash and e-float after each transaction and identify any transaction that could not be completed. *Expected output:* a running table ending with cash GHS 4,400 and e-float GHS 600, and a note that the third transaction would have reduced e-float to GHS 300 safely, so all transactions succeed, with a rebalancing recommendation.","**Wallet comparison.** Compare a stored-value mobile money wallet with a pass-through smartphone wallet on how value is held, security, acceptance and suitability for unbanked users. *Expected output:* a comparison table and a 150-word conclusion.","**Interoperability flow.** Draw the flow of a transfer from a customer on one Ghanaian mobile money network to a bank account at another institution through an interoperability switch. *Expected output:* a labelled diagram showing authorisation, switching, crediting and settlement."],"takeaways":["Mobile money e-value is issued against funds held in trust or custodial accounts, so total e-money should equal backing funds.","Agents convert cash to e-value and back; cash-in consumes e-float and cash-out consumes cash, so rebalancing is essential.","Digital wallets differ: stored-value, bank-linked, pass-through and closed-loop.","Interoperability connects closed loops through switching, rules and settlement, raising system value and changing competition.","Ghana introduced mobile money interoperability through GhIPSS in 2018; M-Pesa research shows mobile money can improve risk sharing.","This lesson is educational and not financial or regulatory advice."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Jack, W., & Suri, T. (2014). Risk sharing and transactions costs: Evidence from Kenya''s mobile money revolution. *American Economic Review, 104*(1), 183-223.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.104.1.183","verification":"unverified"},{"text":"Suri, T., & Jack, W. (2016). The long-run poverty and gender impacts of mobile money. *Science, 354*(6317), 1288-1292.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aah5309","verification":"unverified"},{"text":"Demirgüç-Kunt, A., Klapper, L., Singer, D., & Ansar, S. (2022). *The Global Findex Database 2021: Financial inclusion, digital payments, and resilience in the age of COVID-19*. World Bank.","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/globalfindex","verification":"verified"},{"text":"GSMA. Mobile money resources.","url":"https://www.gsma.com/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Ghana Interbank Payment and Settlement Systems (GhIPSS).","url":"https://ghipss.net/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"GSMA","url":"https://www.gsma.com/","description":"GSMA. Official site of the mobile industry association, with research and programmes on mobile money.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"GhIPSS","url":"https://ghipss.net/","description":"Ghana Interbank Payment and Settlement Systems. Official site describing Ghana''s national payment infrastructure, including interoperability services.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Mobile money stores electronic value against funds held in trust accounts and relies on agents to convert cash to e-value and back. Cash-in consumes an agent''s e-float while cash-out consumes cash, so agents must rebalance liquidity. Wallets may be stored-value, bank-linked, pass-through or closed-loop. Interoperability, introduced in Ghana through GhIPSS in 2018, connects networks and banks, increasing the system''s value and shifting competition. M-Pesa research by Jack and Suri shows mobile money can reduce transaction costs and improve risk sharing.\n\n- E-money = cash converted into a backed electronic claim, not new credit.\n- Agent liquidity: cash + e-float constant (ignoring commissions); composition shifts.\n- Tiered KYC balances inclusion and anti-money-laundering.\n- Interoperability needs a switch, rules, fees and settlement."}'::jsonb, 2998, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('3748932f-a591-55eb-814e-f59d069ecef7', '998d23b2-73cf-5989-a5c8-16d3349e3a25', 'payment-gateways-and-electronic-transactions', 'Payment Gateways and Electronic Transactions', 'Explain the parties and stages of card and mobile money transactions, including authentication, authorisation, capture, clearing and settlement, describe how payment gateways integrate with merchant systems, and evaluate costs, failure handling and reconciliation.', array['Payment gateways', 'Electronic transactions', 'Transaction lifecycle']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"When a customer taps \"Pay\" on an online shop, the confirmation appears within seconds. Yet in those seconds, messages travel between the merchant''s website, a payment gateway, a processor, a card network or mobile money operator, and the customer''s bank or wallet provider. Funds do not actually move at that moment: the money usually reaches the merchant later, through clearing and settlement processes that run in batches. Understanding this gap between *approval* and *money in the bank* is essential for anyone designing, managing or auditing digital commerce.\n\nThis lesson explains the architecture and lifecycle of electronic payment transactions. You will learn who the parties are, the difference between authentication, authorisation, capture, clearing and settlement, how card and mobile money flows differ, how a merchant''s system integrates with a gateway through APIs and webhooks, and what happens when things go wrong. Ghanaian payment service providers and international practice illustrate the concepts. The lesson is educational and is not financial, legal or regulatory advice.","objectives":["Identify the parties in card and mobile money transactions and explain their roles.","Distinguish authentication, authorisation, capture, clearing and settlement.","Describe how a payment gateway integrates with a merchant''s checkout using APIs, redirects, callbacks and webhooks.","Compare the lifecycle of a card payment with that of a mobile money payment.","Evaluate merchant costs, failure scenarios, refunds, chargebacks and reconciliation controls."],"prerequisites":["Mobile money mechanics and digital wallets (previous lesson).","The checkout and order lifecycle from Module 3."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Payment gateway","definition":"A service that securely captures payment details from a merchant''s checkout and transmits transaction requests to processors and networks, returning the result."},{"term":"Payment service provider (PSP)","definition":"A firm that provides merchants with the ability to accept electronic payments, often combining gateway, processing, multiple payment methods and settlement services."},{"term":"Acquirer (acquiring bank)","definition":"The financial institution that contracts with merchants to accept card payments and receives funds on their behalf through the card network."},{"term":"Issuer (issuing bank)","definition":"The financial institution that issues the customer''s card and approves or declines transactions against the cardholder''s account."},{"term":"Card scheme (network)","definition":"An organisation such as Visa or Mastercard that sets rules and operates the network connecting issuers and acquirers."},{"term":"Authorisation","definition":"The issuer''s or wallet provider''s approval of a transaction, confirming available funds and placing a hold or debit."},{"term":"Capture","definition":"The merchant''s instruction to finalise an authorised card transaction so that it is submitted for clearing and settlement."},{"term":"Settlement","definition":"The transfer of funds between financial institutions, and ultimately to the merchant, to discharge payment obligations."},{"term":"Chargeback","definition":"A reversal of a card transaction initiated through the issuer when a cardholder disputes it, under card scheme rules."},{"term":"Webhook","definition":"An automated HTTP message sent by a payment provider to a merchant''s server to report an event such as a successful payment."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The parties in a card transaction\n\nThe card system is often called a **four-party model**: the **cardholder**, the **merchant**, the **acquirer** and the **issuer**, connected by a **card scheme** that sets rules and routes messages. In e-commerce, further intermediaries sit between the merchant and the acquirer: a **payment gateway** to capture payment details securely and a **processor** to handle message routing. Many merchants deal with a single **PSP** that bundles gateway, processing, acquiring relationships and settlement, so they never deal with acquirers directly. This is how providers such as Paystack serve online merchants.\n\n### Five stages that are often confused\n\n1. **Authentication**: verifying that the person paying is the legitimate cardholder or wallet owner. For cards online this commonly uses 3-D Secure, where the issuer may challenge the customer with a one-time password or app approval. For mobile money it is the customer''s PIN entry on their own phone.\n2. **Authorisation**: the issuer checks that the card is valid and funds or credit are available and returns an approval code, placing a **hold** on the funds. The money has not yet moved.\n3. **Capture**: the merchant confirms that it wants to collect the authorised amount. Many online merchants capture automatically at authorisation (\"sale\" transactions); others authorise first and capture later, for example when goods are shipped or a hotel stay ends. Uncaptured authorisations expire after a period set by scheme rules.\n4. **Clearing**: the exchange of transaction details between acquirer and issuer through the scheme, calculating what each party owes, usually in batches.\n5. **Settlement**: the actual movement of funds: the issuer pays the scheme or acquirer, and the acquirer or PSP pays the merchant, net of fees, according to an agreed schedule.\n\nThe gap between authorisation (seconds) and settlement (often one or more business days later) explains why merchants must reconcile what their system *recorded as paid* with what *actually arrived* in their bank account.\n\n### The mobile money transaction lifecycle\n\nA mobile money payment through a PSP follows a different pattern:\n\n1. The customer selects mobile money at checkout and enters their wallet number.\n2. The merchant''s server asks the PSP to initiate a charge.\n3. The PSP sends a debit request to the mobile money operator.\n4. The customer receives a prompt on their phone (for example a USSD pop-up) and approves by entering their PIN. Some flows instead ask the customer to generate a voucher or approve via an app.\n5. The operator debits the wallet and credits the PSP''s collection wallet, then informs the PSP.\n6. The PSP notifies the merchant by **webhook** and updates the transaction status; the merchant should **verify** the status with the PSP before fulfilling the order.\n7. The PSP later **settles** collected funds to the merchant''s bank account or wallet, net of fees.\n\nCompared with cards, mobile money payments are typically *pull requests approved by the customer on a separate device*, are usually completed as a single debit rather than an authorisation hold followed by capture, and rely on the operator''s wallet ledger rather than an issuer-acquirer network. A distinctive failure mode is the **pending** state: the customer may not see or respond to the prompt, the session may time out, or network delays may leave the status uncertain for a while.\n\n### Gateway integration patterns\n\n| Pattern | How it works | Merchant responsibility | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hosted payment page / redirect | Customer is sent to the PSP''s page to pay, then returned | Lowest; card data never touches merchant servers | SMEs, quick integrations |\n| Embedded (inline) checkout | PSP''s script displays a payment form within the merchant page | Low to moderate | Better user experience while limiting data exposure |\n| Direct API integration | Merchant collects details and calls the PSP''s API | Highest; may bring the merchant fully into card security compliance scope | Large merchants with security capacity |\n| Payment links and invoices | PSP generates a link the merchant sends by chat, SMS or email | Minimal | Social commerce, invoices |\n\nWhatever the pattern, robust integrations follow common principles: create a unique **transaction reference** for each attempt; never trust the browser''s redirect alone as proof of payment; confirm via **webhook** and a server-side **verification** call; make webhook handling **idempotent** so that duplicate notifications do not create duplicate orders; and log every event for reconciliation. Security standards for card data, including PCI DSS, are covered in Module 5.\n\n### Refunds, reversals and chargebacks\n\nA **void** cancels an authorisation before capture. A **refund** returns funds after settlement, initiated by the merchant. A **chargeback** is initiated by the cardholder through the issuer, for reasons such as fraud or goods not received, and the merchant may contest it with evidence. Mobile money reversals follow operator and PSP rules. Merchants need clear policies and records for each."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The economics of card acceptance\n\nThe merchant pays a **merchant discount rate (MDR)** or PSP fee on each transaction. In card systems, part of this typically goes to the issuer as an **interchange fee**, part to the scheme as scheme fees, and the remainder to the acquirer or PSP. This is a direct application of two-sided market theory (Rochet and Tirole, 2003, discussed in Module 2): cardholders often pay little or nothing per transaction, and may even earn rewards, while merchants pay fees, because the platform charges the side that values access to the other more and is less price-sensitive. Mobile money pricing similarly balances fees between senders, receivers and merchants.\n\n### Authorisation is not settlement: a control perspective\n\nFrom an internal control perspective, the key principle is **three-way agreement**: the merchant''s order records, the PSP''s transaction reports and the bank''s settlement statements should agree. Differences arise from fees, refunds, chargebacks, timing (transactions near the end of the day settling the next day), failed or pending transactions and errors. Systematic **reconciliation** is therefore a core payment process, developed further in Module 5."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"The sequence diagram below shows an online card payment through a PSP, from authentication and authorisation to capture and later settlement.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant C as Customer\n    participant M as Merchant site\n    participant G as Gateway / PSP\n    participant N as Card scheme\n    participant I as Issuer\n    participant A as Acquirer\n    C->>M: Place order and choose card\n    M->>G: Create payment (amount, reference)\n    G->>C: Secure card form and 3-D Secure challenge\n    C->>I: Authenticate (OTP or app approval)\n    G->>N: Authorisation request\n    N->>I: Route request\n    I-->>N: Approved, funds held\n    N-->>G: Approval code\n    G-->>M: Webhook: payment successful\n    M->>G: Verify transaction status\n    M-->>C: Order confirmed\n    G->>N: Capture and clearing batch\n    N->>I: Clearing records\n    I->>A: Settlement funds via scheme\n    A->>G: Funds for merchant\n    G->>M: Payout to merchant bank, net of fees\n```"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *AccraTech Gadgets* is a hypothetical online shop that uses a PSP to accept cards and mobile money. On one day it records the following. All fees and figures are invented for practice and do not represent any provider''s actual pricing.\n\n- 40 successful mobile money payments totalling GHS 12,000; PSP fee 1.5 per cent.\n- 10 successful card payments totalling GHS 8,000; PSP fee 2.5 per cent.\n- 1 card refund of GHS 600 processed today for an order from last week.\n- 3 mobile money payments of GHS 900 in total that remained **pending** at day end and later failed.\n- The order system wrongly marked the 3 pending mobile money orders as paid because it trusted the browser redirect.\n\n**Step 1: Expected gross collections.** Successful payments = `12,000 + 8,000 = GHS 20,000`.\n\n**Step 2: Fees.** Mobile money: `12,000 × 0.015 = GHS 180`. Cards: `8,000 × 0.025 = GHS 200`. Total fees = `GHS 380`.\n\n**Step 3: Expected net settlement.** `20,000 − 380 − 600 (refund deducted) = GHS 19,020`.\n\n**Step 4: Compare with the order system.** The order system shows paid orders of `20,000 + 900 = GHS 20,900`, because it counted the pending orders. The difference between the order system and the PSP''s successful total is GHS 900, exactly the failed pending payments.\n\n**Step 5: Diagnose and fix.** The root cause is integration design: the system treated the redirect as proof of payment. The fix is to mark orders as paid only after a verified webhook or server-side verification call returns success, to hold pending orders in a \"payment pending\" state with a timeout, and to notify customers to retry. Had the shop shipped the three orders, it would have lost GHS 900 of goods.\n\n**Step 6: Reconcile settlement.** When the PSP pays out GHS 19,020 to the bank account (according to its settlement schedule), the finance team matches the payout to the PSP report (successful transactions minus fees and refunds) and the PSP report to the order system. Any remaining difference is investigated."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"In Ghana, merchants commonly accept payments through PSPs that aggregate several methods in one integration. **Paystack**, founded in Nigeria and acquired by Stripe in 2020, operates in Ghana and provides online checkout supporting cards and mobile money, with developer documentation describing APIs and webhooks. **Hubtel**, a Ghanaian company, offers payment collection, checkout and business messaging services to merchants. Both illustrate the PSP model: a merchant integrates once and gains access to multiple payment methods, consolidated reporting and settlement to its account.\n\nMobile money is especially important for Ghanaian e-commerce because many customers prefer it to cards. Merchants should design for its specific behaviour: clear instructions telling customers to approve the prompt on their phone, a visible pending state, automatic status checks and a retry option. Because customers may be on any network, interoperability through GhIPSS and PSP integrations with multiple operators allows a single checkout to serve customers across networks.\n\nRegulation sits with the **Bank of Ghana**, and the Payment Systems and Services Act, 2019 (Act 987) provides the framework for licensing payment service providers and electronic money issuers. Merchants should choose licensed providers, read their terms on fees, settlement schedules, refunds and disputes, and keep transaction records for reconciliation and tax purposes. Current fees and requirements should be checked directly with providers and the regulator."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Stripe: developer-first payment integration\n\nStripe was founded in 2010 by brothers Patrick and John Collison and became known for making online payment acceptance simpler for developers. Before such services, accepting cards online often required a merchant account with an acquirer, a separate gateway contract and complex integration. Stripe offered a single account, well-documented APIs and client libraries, allowing a developer to accept payments with relatively little code. It later expanded internationally and, in 2020, acquired Paystack, extending its reach into African markets.\n\nThe case illustrates key concepts of this lesson. Stripe''s model **abstracts the four-party system**: merchants interact with one PSP that manages relationships with acquirers and schemes. Its documentation emphasises practices such as using webhooks to receive asynchronous events, verifying events and making request handling idempotent, which reflect the integration principles discussed above. It also shows how **payment infrastructure becomes a platform**, with additional services such as billing, fraud screening and marketplace payouts built on top. A critical view notes that convenience increases merchants'' dependence on a single provider, so merchants should understand settlement terms, dispute processes and data portability."}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** When a payment is approved, the money is in the merchant''s account. **Correction:** Authorisation only approves and holds funds; settlement to the merchant happens later, net of fees.","**Misconception:** Authentication and authorisation are the same. **Correction:** Authentication verifies the payer''s identity; authorisation is the issuer''s approval of the transaction.","**Misconception:** The customer being redirected to the success page proves payment. **Correction:** Redirects can be interrupted or manipulated; merchants should rely on verified webhooks and server-side checks.","**Misconception:** Refunds and chargebacks are the same thing. **Correction:** A refund is merchant-initiated; a chargeback is a cardholder dispute processed through the issuer under scheme rules.","**Misconception:** Mobile money payments follow the card authorisation-and-capture model. **Correction:** They are typically customer-approved wallet debits with distinctive pending and timeout states."],"examMistakes":["Listing transaction stages in the wrong order or treating clearing and settlement as identical.","Omitting the issuer, acquirer or scheme when describing a card transaction.","Describing mobile money payments without the customer approval prompt, webhook and settlement steps.","Ignoring failure scenarios (pending, timeout, duplicate notifications) and reconciliation.","Quoting specific provider fees or regulatory limits without a verified source."],"criticalQuestions":["Why might a merchant choose separate authorisation and capture rather than immediate capture? What risks arise?","Merchants usually pay card fees while cardholders pay little. Is this fair, and how does two-sided market theory explain it?","How should an online merchant in Ghana balance the user experience of mobile money payments against the risk of pending and failed transactions?","What are the risks of a market in which a small number of PSPs process most online payments?"],"exercises":["**Lifecycle mapping.** Draw the lifecycle of a mobile money payment through a PSP from checkout to settlement, including a timeout path. *Expected output:* a diagram with at least eight steps and a labelled failure branch.","**Settlement calculation.** An illustrative merchant has card sales of GHS 15,000 (fee 2.5 per cent), mobile money sales of GHS 25,000 (fee 1.5 per cent) and refunds of GHS 1,200. Calculate expected net settlement. *Expected output:* fees of GHS 375 and GHS 375, net settlement of GHS 38,050, with steps shown.","**Integration review.** Using the official Paystack documentation, identify how a merchant should confirm that a transaction succeeded. *Expected output:* a short note (200 words) explaining webhooks, verification and why the redirect alone is insufficient, with references to the relevant documentation pages."],"takeaways":["Card payments involve cardholder, merchant, acquirer, issuer and scheme, with gateways and PSPs as intermediaries.","Authentication, authorisation, capture, clearing and settlement are distinct stages; approval is not settlement.","Mobile money payments are customer-approved wallet debits with pending and timeout states, notified by webhook and settled later.","Robust integrations use unique references, verified webhooks, idempotent processing and reconciliation.","Merchant fees reflect two-sided market pricing; refunds, chargebacks and reversals require clear policies.","This lesson is educational and not financial, legal or regulatory advice."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Rochet, J.-C., & Tirole, J. (2003). Platform competition in two-sided markets. *Journal of the European Economic Association, 1*(4), 990-1029.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/154247603322493212","verification":"unverified"},{"text":"Laudon, K. C., & Traver, C. G. *E-commerce: Business, technology, society*. Pearson. (Consult the most recent edition available in your library.)","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Paystack. Paystack documentation.","url":"https://paystack.com/docs/","verification":"unverified"},{"text":"Stripe. Stripe documentation.","url":"https://docs.stripe.com/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Bank of Ghana. Official website.","url":"https://www.bog.gov.gh/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"Paystack Documentation","url":"https://paystack.com/docs/","description":"Paystack. Official developer documentation explaining payment initiation, verification and webhooks for cards and mobile money.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"Stripe Documentation","url":"https://docs.stripe.com/","description":"Stripe. Official documentation describing online payment flows, webhooks and idempotent requests.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Electronic payments involve several parties and stages. In card payments, the gateway or PSP passes the merchant''s request through the scheme to the issuer, which authenticates the cardholder (for example through 3-D Secure) and authorises the transaction by holding funds; capture, clearing and settlement follow later, and the merchant receives funds net of fees. Mobile money payments are customer-approved wallet debits confirmed by webhook and settled later. Robust integrations verify payments server-side and reconcile order records, PSP reports and bank settlements.\n\n- Order: authenticate → authorise → capture → clear → settle.\n- Four-party model: cardholder, merchant, acquirer, issuer, plus scheme.\n- Never trust the redirect alone; verify via webhook and API.\n- Idempotent webhook handling prevents duplicate orders.\n- Refund (merchant) ≠ chargeback (cardholder dispute via issuer)."}'::jsonb, 2899, 3, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
