insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('f3bb9bdd-dfea-5b58-817c-6aad8b901831', 'e6a916e0-30da-5768-a630-929602b5b22a', 'fintech-regulation-and-compliance', 'FinTech Regulation, Compliance and Consumer Protection', 'Explain why digital finance is regulated, apply licensing, AML/CFT, tiered KYC, data protection and consumer protection concepts to a FinTech product, and evaluate regulatory sandboxes and compliance by design.', array['Financial regulation', 'Compliance', 'Consumer protection', 'Regulatory sandboxes']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Every digital payment, wallet top-up or instant loan rests on a promise: the customer''s money will be there tomorrow, the provider will not misuse personal data, and criminals will not be able to use the service to launder proceeds or finance harm. Regulation is the machinery that makes this promise credible. For a FinTech founder, a bank product manager or a mobile network operator, regulation is not an obstacle that appears after the product is built; it shapes who may offer the service, how customers are identified, how funds are held and how complaints are resolved.\n\nThis lesson builds on the earlier lessons on FinTech, mobile money and the Ghanaian ecosystem. It explains the objectives of financial regulation, the main regulatory instruments, and the compliance functions a digital finance business must run. It then examines regulatory sandboxes and the idea of designing compliance into products from the start. The lesson describes Ghanaian and international frameworks at a general level. Licence categories, thresholds, fees and deadlines change frequently, so always consult current publications of the Bank of Ghana and other authorities. This material is educational and is not legal, regulatory or financial advice.","objectives":["Explain the main objectives of financial regulation and why digital finance raises distinctive regulatory challenges.","Distinguish licensing, prudential supervision, conduct supervision and market integrity as regulatory functions.","Describe how AML/CFT obligations and tiered KYC work, and evaluate their effect on financial inclusion.","Apply data protection principles and consumer protection rules to the design of a digital financial product.","Evaluate regulatory sandboxes and compliance by design as approaches to balancing innovation and risk."],"prerequisites":["The Digital Finance lessons on FinTech and digital banking and on mobile money and digital wallets.","The Ghanaian mobile money ecosystem lesson, especially the roles of the Bank of Ghana and GhIPSS.","The payment security module (fraud types, authentication)."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism (AML/CFT)","definition":"Measures that prevent financial services from being used to disguise criminal proceeds or fund terrorism."},{"term":"Know Your Customer (KYC)","definition":"Procedures for identifying and verifying customers and understanding the purpose of the account."},{"term":"Tiered KYC","definition":"A risk-based approach in which accounts with low limits need lighter identification, and higher limits require fuller verification."},{"term":"Regulatory sandbox","definition":"A supervised, time-limited environment in which firms test innovations with real customers under tailored regulatory conditions."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Why financial services are regulated\n\nFinancial services are regulated because markets for money have predictable failure modes. Four rationales recur. **Stability**: a failing provider can harm many customers at once, so prudential rules require, for e-money, that funds equal to outstanding balances are held safely and apart from the issuer''s own assets. **Consumer protection**: providers know more than customers about fees and risks, and first-time users can be misled or sold unsuitable products. **Market integrity**: payment systems can move illicit funds quickly, so providers must know who uses them and report suspicious activity. **Competition and inclusion**: regulators increasingly aim to widen access and keep markets contestable, for example through interoperability rules.\n\nDigital finance does not remove these rationales; it changes how they apply. Speed means that fraud and errors scale quickly. Non-bank providers, such as telecom operators and software firms, perform bank-like functions without being banks. Data becomes both an asset and a risk. Products are assembled from partners (a wallet provider, a bank, a cloud host, a scoring vendor), which blurs who is responsible when something goes wrong.\n\n### The regulatory architecture\n\nIt is useful to separate four functions that a single authority may perform or that several may share.\n\n| Function | Question it answers | Typical tools |\n|---|---|---|\n| Licensing and authorisation | Who may offer this service? | Licence categories, fit and proper tests, minimum capital, business plans |\n| Prudential supervision | Will the provider stay safe and solvent? | Safeguarding of customer funds, capital, liquidity, audits, reporting |\n| Conduct supervision | Are customers treated fairly? | Disclosure rules, complaint handling, agent oversight, marketing controls |\n| Market integrity | Is the system protected from abuse? | AML/CFT rules, sanctions screening, fraud reporting, cybersecurity standards |\n\nIn Ghana, the **Bank of Ghana** is the principal authority for banks, specialised deposit-taking institutions, payment systems and electronic money. The **Payment Systems and Services Act, 2019 (Act 987)** provides the framework for licensing and supervising payment service providers, e-money issuers and payment system operators, and the Bank of Ghana''s **FinTech and Innovation Office** is the point of engagement for innovators and runs its regulatory sandbox. The Bank of Ghana also operates under its governing legislation and the banking legislation (the Banks and Specialised Deposit-Taking Institutions Act, 2016 (Act 930)). Other relevant bodies include the **Data Protection Commission** under the **Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843)**, the **Financial Intelligence Centre** under the **Anti-Money Laundering Act, 2020 (Act 1044)**, the **Cyber Security Authority** under the **Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038)**, and sector bodies such as the Securities and Exchange Commission, the National Insurance Commission and the National Pensions Regulatory Authority for investment, insurance and pension FinTech. A product that spans payments, savings and insurance can therefore fall under several regulators at once, and a founder must map this at the start.\n\n### AML/CFT and KYC\n\nMoney laundering is commonly described in three stages: **placement** (getting cash into the financial system), **layering** (moving it through transactions to obscure its origin) and **integration** (returning it to the economy as apparently legitimate wealth). Mobile money and instant payments are attractive for layering because transfers are fast and cheap. International standards for AML/CFT are set by the **Financial Action Task Force (FATF)**, an intergovernmental body whose recommendations countries implement through national law. Ghana''s Anti-Money Laundering Act, 2020 (Act 1044) is part of that national implementation.\n\nProviders typically operate customer due diligence (identifying and verifying the customer and understanding expected use, with enhanced checks for higher-risk customers such as politically exposed persons), ongoing monitoring for patterns such as rapid in-and-out movement or structuring below thresholds, escalation of suspicious transactions to the compliance function and, where required, to the national financial intelligence unit, sanctions screening, and record keeping and staff training that extend to agents.\n\nA **risk-based approach** is central. It means that a low-value wallet used mainly for airtime and small transfers can be treated lightly, whereas a high-volume merchant account receiving payments from many countries warrants closer scrutiny. This principle underlies **tiered KYC**: a basic account might need only limited identity information and carry low balance and transaction limits, a mid-level account needs a national identity document, and a fully verified account carries higher limits. Tiering exists because strict identification can exclude people who lack documents, and exclusion pushes them back to cash or informal channels that are harder to monitor. In Ghana, national identification, the Ghana Card, and the linking of SIM registration to identity data have become important elements of the verification landscape; the exact rules and deadlines are set by regulators and have changed over time, so consult current sources.\n\nTiered KYC illustrates a general tension that students should be ready to discuss: strict controls reduce financial crime risk but can reduce inclusion, while light controls widen access but raise risk. The regulatory skill lies in calibrating controls to risk, supported by transaction limits, monitoring and digital identity.\n\n### Data protection\n\nDigital finance generates detailed behavioural data: who pays whom, when, how much and from where. Under the **Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843)**, organisations that process personal data are expected to follow recognised principles, which are widely shared with other data protection laws. In plain terms they include lawfulness and fairness, purpose limitation (wallet data collected for payments should not be quietly reused for loan marketing without a proper basis), minimality, accuracy, limited retention, security safeguards, openness to the data subject, and accountability, which usually includes registration with the Data Protection Commission as the law requires.\n\nFor FinTech, the practical questions are consent design for credit scoring that uses phone data, sharing data with partners and third-party SDKs, storing data abroad, and responding to breaches. A breach is both a security incident and a potential data protection violation.\n\n### Consumer protection and complaints\n\nConduct rules protect customers from harms that financial-crime rules do not address. Typical expectations are **transparent pricing** (fees and the total cost of credit disclosed before the transaction, in plain language and in the channels customers actually use, including USSD), **fair treatment and suitability** of products, **agent accountability** (the provider is generally responsible for its agents, including overcharging), responsible **digital credit** (very short, high-cost loans have drawn international concern about over-indebtedness, opaque pricing and abusive collection), accessible **complaints and redress** with stated resolution times and escalation to the regulator, and clear rules on **fraud liability** and reversal of wrongly sent funds.\n\nThe Bank of Ghana has issued directives and guidelines on consumer protection and complaint handling for regulated institutions; the current instruments should be checked on its website. A useful teaching point is that effective protection depends on **enforcement and awareness** as much as on rules: a customer who does not know where to complain, or cannot afford to, gets little benefit from a good rule.\n\n### Regulatory sandboxes and innovation offices\n\nRegulators face a recurring dilemma: intervene too early and innovation may be stifled, wait too long and harms may spread (a version of the Collingridge dilemma in technology policy). Sandboxes, pilots and staged licensing are attempts to gather evidence while keeping the stakes small (see Arner, Barberis and Buckley, 2016).\n\nA **regulatory sandbox** lets firms test products with real customers, within limits, under close supervision. A typical sandbox has an application and eligibility stage (the innovation must be genuine, beneficial and not fit existing rules easily), agreed boundaries (number of customers, transaction caps, duration), safeguards (customer disclosure, compensation arrangements, exit plan) and regular reporting. At the end the firm either exits, proceeds to full authorisation, or stops. Regulators gain evidence about new risks; firms gain regulatory clarity earlier and sometimes faster access to the market.\n\nSandboxes are not a free pass. They do not waive consumer protection or AML basics, and they do not guarantee later approval. Critics note that they take regulatory resources, may favour well-resourced applicants and can create a perception of endorsement. **Innovation offices** (sometimes called innovation hubs) offer a lighter alternative: a contact point where firms can ask questions without entering a formal test. The Bank of Ghana''s FinTech and Innovation Office and its regulatory sandbox are examples of this approach.\n\n### Compliance by design\n\nCompliance by design treats regulatory requirements as product requirements. In practice this means mapping regulators, licences and laws to each feature at concept stage; minimising data before forms and databases are built; building KYC tiers, limits, screening and audit logs into the transaction flow so a back-end error cannot bypass them; segregating customer funds with reconciliation reports; logging decisions, consents and complaints as evidence; governing third parties such as aggregators, agents and cloud providers, since the regulator holds the licensed firm responsible; and using RegTech tools for identity verification, monitoring and reporting, while supervisors increasingly use SupTech to analyse the data they receive."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Designing the compliance layer for a wallet product\n\n**Illustrative scenario:** A hypothetical start-up, \"KasaPay\", plans a savings and payments wallet for market traders. It wants onboarding by phone, a micro-credit feature scored from transaction history, and cross-border transfers to a neighbouring country in a later phase.\n\n**Step 1: map the perimeter.** Holding customer balances and issuing e-value points to e-money rules; processing transfers points to payment service licensing; micro-credit raises lending rules; cross-border transfers add foreign exchange and remittance rules. KasaPay decides to partner with a licensed issuer for phase one instead of seeking its own licence, and documents that the licensed partner remains accountable to the regulator.\n\n**Step 2: design tiered KYC.**\n\nBasic tier: name, phone number and basic identity data, with very low limits so new users can try the service. Standard tier: a national identity number verified against a trusted source, with moderate limits. Enhanced tier: additional documents, business registration and source of funds, with higher limits. The limits are placeholders: the real values come from current regulatory instruments and the partner''s licence conditions.\n\n**Step 3: data protection.** KasaPay records a lawful basis and a clear consent screen for the credit-scoring feature, states that scoring uses only in-app transaction history, avoids collecting phone contacts, sets a retention period, and appoints a person responsible for data protection.\n\n**Step 4: consumer protection.** The app and USSD menu show fees before confirmation. The credit feature displays the total repayment amount and due date in simple language. A complaint button leads to a ticket with a reference number and a promised response time, and unresolved cases are told how to escalate to the regulator.\n\n**Steps 5 and 6: monitor and evaluate.** Rules flag accounts that receive many small deposits and quickly forward the sum (a layering pattern), a compliance officer reviews and records each alert, and KasaPay tracks tier completion, alert volumes, complaint resolution times and confirmed fraud, adjusting tier limits with its partner and the regulator if they block normal use."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"A Ghanaian FinTech, bank or MNO subsidiary can use this framework as a launch checklist. First, determine which authorities are relevant to each feature: the Bank of Ghana for payments and e-money, the Data Protection Commission for personal data, the Financial Intelligence Centre for AML reporting, the Cyber Security Authority for security incidents, and sector regulators for investment, insurance or pensions. Second, decide whether to obtain a licence or partner with a licensed institution, remembering that partnership does not remove conduct and data duties. Third, engage the Bank of Ghana''s FinTech and Innovation Office early and consider the sandbox for genuinely novel products. Fourth, design for customers with limited documents, limited literacy and feature phones: tiered KYC, plain-language disclosures and accessible complaint channels. Compliance cost is real for small firms, so the strategic question is how to spread it through shared services, aggregators and RegTech."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### The United Kingdom''s regulatory sandbox\n\nThe UK Financial Conduct Authority launched one of the first formal regulatory sandboxes in 2016, accepting cohorts of firms to test products with real customers under agreed safeguards. Many jurisdictions, including several in Africa and Asia, later introduced sandboxes or innovation hubs, adapting the model to local capacity. Three lessons stand out. A sandbox works best with clear eligibility criteria and defined exit routes, so that testing leads to a decision. Regulators learned about emerging risks and sometimes revised guidance, so a sandbox is also a learning tool for the supervisor. And it is resource-intensive, so smaller regulators often prefer a narrower design or an innovation office. Evidence on outcomes is still debated, so treat the sandbox as one tool among several."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  P[\"New FinTech product idea\"] --> M[\"Regulatory mapping\"]\n  M --> L{\"Licence needed?\"}\n  L -->|\"Yes\"| A[\"Apply directly or partner with licensed firm\"]\n  L -->|\"Unclear or novel\"| S[\"Engage innovation office / sandbox\"]\n  A --> D[\"Design controls\"]\n  S --> D\n  D --> K[\"Tiered KYC and limits\"]\n  D --> DP[\"Data protection by design\"]\n  D --> CP[\"Consumer protection and complaints\"]\n  D --> AML[\"Monitoring and reporting\"]\n  K --> O[\"Launch and supervise\"]\n  DP --> O\n  CP --> O\n  AML --> O\n  O --> R[\"Review: incidents, complaints, alerts\"]\n  R --> D\n```"}],"misconceptions":["\"Regulation only slows innovation.\" Well-designed rules build the trust that adoption depends on; the problem is poorly calibrated rules, not regulation as such.","\"A sandbox exempts a firm from the law.\" Sandbox participants still owe core protections to customers and must follow the conditions agreed with the regulator.","\"Tiered KYC means weaker AML.\" Tiers apply light checks only where limits keep risk low, with monitoring continuing at every tier.","\"Partnering with a licensed bank removes my compliance duties.\" The partner is accountable to the regulator, but the FinTech still owes conduct, data protection and contractual duties.","\"Data protection is only an IT matter.\" It involves legal basis, consent design, retention, governance and customer rights, not only encryption."],"examMistakes":["Naming laws without explaining what each requires or which function (licensing, AML, data, cyber) it serves.","Quoting specific limits, fees or deadlines from memory instead of explaining the principle and directing to current sources.","Presenting strict KYC as always good, ignoring the inclusion trade-off.","Describing a sandbox as deregulation rather than supervised experimentation.","Treating consumer protection as only a complaint hotline, ignoring disclosure, fair pricing and agent conduct."],"criticalQuestions":["When should a regulator prefer activity-based rules over entity-based licensing for FinTech, and what risks does each approach create?","How would you decide whether the inclusion benefits of a lighter KYC tier outweigh its added financial crime risk?","A lender wants to use phone metadata to score borrowers. Which data protection and consumer protection questions should be answered before launch?"],"exercises":["**Regulatory map.** Choose a FinTech product (for example a merchant lending app) and list each Ghanaian authority and law that may apply, with one sentence on why. *Expected output:* a one-page table citing official sources and the date consulted.","**KYC tier design.** Design a three-tier KYC scheme for a hypothetical wallet, stating the information required at each tier and justifying the limits as placeholders. *Expected output:* a table and a 200-word justification linking each tier to risk and inclusion."],"takeaways":["Financial regulation addresses stability, consumer protection, market integrity and competition or inclusion; digital finance changes how these apply.","Licensing, prudential, conduct and market integrity functions can sit with different authorities, so mapping the perimeter is the first compliance task.","AML/CFT works through a risk-based approach, and tiered KYC links identification effort to account limits to protect inclusion.","Data protection and consumer protection require lawful, transparent, minimal processing and accessible complaint and redress mechanisms.","Regulatory sandboxes support evidence-based supervision of novel products but do not waive core obligations.","Compliance by design embeds requirements in the product and evidence trail from the outset."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Arner, D. W., Barberis, J., & Buckley, R. P. (2016). FinTech, RegTech, and the reconceptualization of financial regulation. *Northwestern Journal of International Law & Business, 37*(3), 371-413.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Financial Action Task Force. *The FATF Recommendations: International standards on combating money laundering and the financing of terrorism and proliferation*. (Current edition published by the FATF.)","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Payment Systems and Services Act, 2019 (Act 987) (Ghana).","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) (Ghana).","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Anti-Money Laundering Act, 2020 (Act 1044) (Ghana).","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Bank of Ghana","url":"https://www.bog.gov.gh/","description":"Bank of Ghana. Official source for licensing, directives, consumer protection notices and information on the FinTech and Innovation Office.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Financial Action Task Force","url":"https://www.fatf-gafi.org/","description":"FATF. Official site for AML/CFT standards, guidance and publications.","verification":"unverified"}],"revisionSummary":"Financial services are regulated to secure stability, protect consumers, preserve market integrity and widen access. Regulators license who may offer services, supervise safety and conduct, and impose AML/CFT duties based on a risk-based approach, of which tiered KYC is a key example. Data protection law limits how personal financial data may be collected and used, and consumer protection rules demand transparent pricing, accountable agents and effective complaint handling. Sandboxes and innovation offices let regulators learn about new products under supervision, while compliance by design makes regulatory requirements part of the product itself.\n\n- Four functions: licensing, prudential, conduct, market integrity.\n- Risk-based approach: tiered KYC trades control against inclusion.\n- Act 987, Act 843, Act 1044 and Act 1038 each address a different risk.\n- Sandbox is supervised testing, not an exemption.\n- Compliance by design: map, minimise, embed controls, keep evidence."}'::jsonb, 3184, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('bab4f766-c3d4-5871-9c48-5f249c165a9d', 'e6a916e0-30da-5768-a630-929602b5b22a', 'customer-experience-and-digital-strategy', 'Customer Experience and Digital Business Strategy', 'Map customer journeys, use CX metrics critically, design omnichannel and USSD-inclusive experiences, and evaluate how banks, mobile network operators and FinTechs position themselves strategically.', array['Customer experience', 'Customer journey', 'Digital business strategy']::text[], 50, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"When several providers offer similar wallets, transfers and bill payments at similar prices, customers choose on the basis of how the service feels to use and whether they trust it. A payment that fails at a market stall, a menu that cannot be read on a basic phone, or a loan offer with unclear charges can send a customer back to cash within days. Customer experience (CX) is therefore not a decorative extra; in digital finance it is a main source of advantage, and it is closely tied to strategy: which customers a firm serves, through which channels, and with what capabilities.\n\nThis lesson shows how to analyse experience systematically through customer journey mapping, how to measure it with indicators such as Net Promoter Score (NPS) and Customer Satisfaction (CSAT) without being misled by them, and how to design omnichannel services that work for customers on smartphones and on feature phones. It then connects experience to digital business strategy and compares the positions of banks, mobile network operators (MNOs) and FinTechs. The lesson is educational. Market positions change, so the examples are described qualitatively and should be checked against current sources.","objectives":["Construct a customer journey map for a digital financial service and identify moments of truth and pain points.","Select and interpret CX metrics (NPS, CSAT, customer effort, completion and drop-off rates) while recognising their limitations.","Design omnichannel and USSD, app and agent experiences that include customers with differing devices, literacy and connectivity.","Explain how trust is built and lost in digital finance and apply design principles that support it.","Evaluate digital business strategy options and the competitive positions of banks, MNOs and FinTechs using established frameworks."],"prerequisites":["The Digital Finance lessons on FinTech, mobile money and payment gateways.","Ghana''s mobile money ecosystem and financial inclusion lessons.","The e-business foundations module (business models and value propositions)."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Customer experience (CX)","definition":"The cumulative perception a customer forms from all interactions with a provider across the relationship."},{"term":"Customer journey","definition":"The sequence of stages and touchpoints a customer passes through, from awareness and onboarding to use, support and exit."},{"term":"Touchpoint","definition":"Any interaction between the customer and the provider, such as an agent visit, a USSD session, an SMS or a call."},{"term":"Moment of truth","definition":"A touchpoint that disproportionately shapes the customer''s overall judgement, such as the first transfer or the first failed transaction."},{"term":"Net Promoter Score (NPS)","definition":"A measure derived from the question \"How likely are you to recommend us?\" on a 0-10 scale, calculated as the percentage of promoters minus the percentage of detractors."},{"term":"Customer Satisfaction score (CSAT)","definition":"The share of customers rating a specific interaction as satisfactory, usually on a short scale."},{"term":"Customer Effort Score (CES)","definition":"A measure of how easy it was to get something done."},{"term":"Omnichannel","definition":"An approach in which channels are integrated so that the customer experiences one consistent relationship and context carries across channels."},{"term":"USSD (Unstructured Supplementary Service Data)","definition":"A session-based menu service available on basic phones without data or an app."},{"term":"Digital business strategy","definition":"A firm''s integrated plan that uses digital resources and capabilities to create and capture value and to compete."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Customer experience as a system\n\nLemon and Verhoef (2016) describe customer experience as a journey made of touchpoints across stages, shaped by the firm''s design, by the customer''s own context, and by other parties such as agents, social contacts and media. For digital finance, three consequences follow. First, experience is **cumulative**: one smooth transfer does not offset three failed ones. Second, it is **multi-actor**: a mobile money customer''s experience depends on the issuer, the agent, the network, the merchant and the bank at the other end. A failed payment to a merchant is blamed on the wallet even if the merchant''s system caused it. Third, it is **emotional as well as functional**: money is tied to anxiety, status and security, so reassurance matters as much as speed.\n\n### Customer journey mapping\n\nA journey map is a structured diagram of what the customer does, thinks and feels at each stage, what the firm and its partners do in response, and where things go wrong. A useful map for a digital financial product has the following steps.\n\n1. **Define the persona and goal.** Be specific: not \"customers\" but \"a market trader in Kumasi who uses a feature phone and wants to pay a supplier in Accra\". Base personas on interviews and observation, not assumption.\n2. **List the stages.** A typical financial journey includes awareness, registration and identity verification, first funding, first use, regular use, problem resolution, and expansion into other products or exit.\n3. **Identify touchpoints per stage.** Include channels the firm does not control, such as an agent''s shop or a relative''s advice.\n4. **Record actions, thoughts and emotions.** Use qualitative research to capture what customers say, the questions they ask and their worries (for example, \"Will I lose my money if I press the wrong number?\").\n5. **Mark pain points and moments of truth.** Examples include a failed identity check, an unexpected fee at confirmation, a reversal that takes days, or an agent who is out of float.\n6. **Attach metrics and owners.** For each pain point, define how it will be measured (drop-off rate, time to resolution, complaint volume) and who is responsible.\n7. **Prioritise and redesign.** Rank issues by customer impact and business value, and test redesigns with real users.\n\nA journey map also reveals **handoffs**, where responsibility moves between teams or firms. Many failures occur at handoffs, such as when a dispute raised with a merchant must be resolved by the wallet provider but neither takes ownership.\n\n### Measuring experience carefully\n\nMetrics make experience manageable, but each metric answers a different question and has weaknesses.\n\n| Metric | What it measures | Strengths | Cautions |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NPS | Overall willingness to recommend | Simple, comparable over time, associated with loyalty in some sectors (Reichheld, 2003) | Single question hides reasons; cultural differences in rating style; 0-10 scales may confuse less literate respondents; correlation with growth is debated |\n| CSAT | Satisfaction with a specific interaction or product | Actionable at touchpoint level | Easily biased by who responds; satisfaction does not guarantee loyalty |\n| CES | Ease of completing a task | Strong link to repeat use in service settings (Dixon, Freeman and Toman, 2010) | Needs clear task definitions |\n| Completion and drop-off rates | Share finishing each step | Objective, from system logs | Do not explain why people leave |\n| Time to resolve and first-contact resolution | Support effectiveness | Closely tied to trust | Can be gamed by closing tickets early |\n| Complaints per thousand transactions | Pain at scale | Hard evidence of failure | Under-reports where customers do not know how to complain |\n\nPrinciples for using metrics **carefully**: triangulate survey scores with behavioural data and interviews; mind sampling and response bias (satisfied or angry customers answer more often, and SMS or USSD surveys exclude some people); avoid target gaming by tying incentives to outcomes such as resolved cases and repeat use rather than to scores; compare like with like, since benchmarks differ by industry and country; report sample sizes and uncertainty; and link experience indicators to retention, cost to serve and revenue.\n\n### Omnichannel design and inclusion\n\nCustomers move between channels: they register with an agent, transact by USSD, check balances in an app and call a help line. **Omnichannel** design makes these feel like one relationship. For an inclusive financial service this means:\n\n- **USSD as a first-class channel.** USSD works on every handset and needs no data bundle. Good menus are short, in the customer''s language, show fees before confirmation and confirm before sending. Limits are small screens, session time-outs and weaker security than encrypted apps, so sensitive actions may need extra verification.\n- **Apps for richer features.** QR payments, biometrics, notifications and statements, but they need a smartphone and data, so they must be light and tolerate patchy connectivity.\n- **Agents as human infrastructure.** Agents provide cash services, onboarding and trust, and need training, working float and escalation paths.\n- **Local language, voice and accessibility.** Voice prompts, local-language content, larger text and simple icons widen the market and reduce errors.\n- **Consistent context.** A support agent should see the transaction history of a failed payment without asking for every detail again.\n\nDesign for the **least capable device and context** in your target segment, then enhance for others. Inclusion is both a social and a commercial argument, since the largest untapped segments are often those not served by app-only products.\n\n### Trust in digital finance\n\nTrust is built through reliability, transparency, security and fair treatment, and it is lost quickly. Practical trust signals include clear receipts and confirmation messages, fees shown before the customer commits, visible licence information, simple dispute processes, fraud warnings and prompt reversals when mistakes occur. Social engineering and SIM swap fraud, discussed in the payment security module, damage trust beyond the victim''s own account. Design should therefore include **friction in the right places**: confirming the recipient''s name before sending, warning on unusual transfers, and cooling-off steps before a new device accesses an account. Removing all friction improves conversion but may increase fraud and regret. Interfaces that push customers into costly choices, such as hidden fees or pre-ticked consent for credit, may raise short-term revenue but breach conduct expectations and corrode trust."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Digital business strategy\n\nBharadwaj and colleagues (2013) argue that digital technology should no longer be treated as a functional-level strategy that supports business strategy; **digital business strategy** is the organisational strategy formulated and executed by leveraging digital resources to create differential value. For financial services, capabilities such as data, APIs, platforms and agent networks are therefore sources of competitive advantage, not mere support tools.\n\n### Porter''s Five Forces applied to digital payments\n\nPorter (2008) offers five forces that shape industry profitability. Applied to a payments market:\n\n| Force | Digital payments illustration |\n|---|---|\n| Rivalry among competitors | Banks, MNO-linked issuers and FinTechs compete on price, reach and features |\n| Threat of new entrants | Lower technology barriers, but licences, trust and agent networks are barriers |\n| Bargaining power of buyers | Customers can hold several wallets when interoperability and low switching costs exist; large merchants negotiate fees |\n| Bargaining power of suppliers | Mobile networks, switches, card schemes and cloud providers can control critical inputs |\n| Threat of substitutes | Cash, informal savings groups, cards and QR payments |\n\nThe analysis shows why interoperability and regulation affect profitability as well as customer outcomes.\n\n### Competitive positioning: banks, MNOs and FinTechs\n\n| Player | Typical strengths | Typical weaknesses | Strategic options |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Banks | Trusted brands, licences, balance sheets, regulated lending, corporate relationships | Legacy systems, branch cost, slower product cycles, limited reach to low-income segments | Modernise core systems, partner with FinTechs, offer wallet-linked savings and credit |\n| MNOs and their e-money entities | Huge customer bases, agent networks, billing and distribution, usage data | Regulatory limits on credit and deposits, variable brand trust | Expand into savings, credit and insurance via partners, open platforms to merchants |\n| FinTechs | Speed, focused user experience, willingness to experiment, data science | Smaller balance sheets, high acquisition costs, dependence on licensed partners | Specialise in a niche, become an embedded provider, partner with banks or MNOs, or be acquired |\n\nThese positions are tendencies, not rules. The key strategic pattern is **coopetition**: the same firms compete for customers and cooperate on infrastructure such as shared switches, wallet-to-bank links and API partnerships. Platform thinking (Parker, Van Alstyne and Choudary, 2016) adds that value rises with participation on each side, so firms must decide whom to subsidise to reach critical mass and how open to be to third parties."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Mapping and improving a first-transfer journey\n\n**Illustrative scenario:** A hypothetical wallet provider, \"NkwaPay\", notices that many newly registered users never complete a first transfer. Registration is by agent, followed by a USSD PIN set-up.\n\n**Step 1: assemble the data.** The funnel figures below are invented for the exercise.\n\n| Stage | Users entering | Users completing | Completion rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Agent registration | 1,000 | 900 | 90% |\n| PIN set-up by USSD | 900 | 630 | 70% |\n| First cash-in | 630 | 504 | 80% |\n| First transfer | 504 | 252 | 50% |\n\n**Step 2: find the largest losses.** The biggest absolute loss is at PIN set-up (270 users), followed by first transfer (252 users), and first transfer has the lowest completion rate (50%). Overall, only about 25 of every 100 registrants complete a first transfer.\n\n**Step 3: diagnose with qualitative research.** Interviews with ten drop-outs reveal that many did not know how to start a transfer, feared sending money to the wrong number, and were unsure about the fee. At PIN set-up, the USSD menu timed out before users finished.\n\n**Step 4: redesign and measure.** NkwaPay lengthens the session time-out, shows the recipient''s registered name before sending, displays the fee on the confirmation screen, and trains agents to walk each new customer through a small first transfer. It then tracks completion by stage, repeat transfers within thirty days, fraud reports and wrong-transfer complaints, because a rise in completions accompanied by a rise in errors would not be an improvement.\n\nThe funnel shows where to look; interviews explain why. Neither alone would have revealed both the scale and the fear of mistakes."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Ghana offers a rich setting for CX strategy. Mobile money is widely used, customers often hold SIMs from more than one network, and usage spans smartphones and feature phones, so USSD remains important alongside apps. Interoperability means customers can pay across wallets and banks, which makes reliability and experience quality more decisive than network size alone. A Ghanaian bank competing with MNO-linked wallets might emphasise secure savings, credit and merchant services, integrating with wallets rather than ignoring them. A FinTech may focus on a niche such as SME bookkeeping linked to payments, using licensed partners and aggregators. All must handle fraud, including impersonation and fake agents, with visible safeguards and customer education in local languages. Market traders, farmers and informal workers form large segments whose journeys differ from those of salaried urban users, so segmentation by context, device and trust needs is essential. The strategic questions are: where does our firm have a distinct capability, which partners fill the gaps, and what experience will keep customers using the service after promotional offers end?"},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Kenya''s M-Pesa and competition on experience\n\nSafaricom''s M-Pesa, launched in Kenya in 2007, became a standard example of a mobile money service whose growth was linked to a simple proposition (send money to relatives), a large agent network and an experience that worked on basic phones through a SIM-based menu. Over time the service widened to bill payments, merchant payments, and savings and credit products with banking partners, while banks and FinTechs responded with their own mobile products and partnerships. The case illustrates that an MNO-based provider can build a platform from distribution and trust, that incumbent banks can respond by integrating with the wallet or competing on specialised products, and that an inclusive channel such as USSD can sustain mass adoption long after smartphones spread. It also shows risks: concentration in one provider and public concern about the pricing and conduct of digital credit. Compare this with Ghana, where several providers are connected through national interoperability, and consider how market structure affects customer choice, innovation and bargaining power."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Awareness\"] --> B[\"Registration and ID check\"]\n  B --> C[\"First funding\"]\n  C --> D[\"First transfer or payment\"]\n  D --> E[\"Regular use\"]\n  E --> F[\"Support and dispute resolution\"]\n  F --> G[\"Expansion: savings, credit, insurance\"]\n  F -.->|\"Poor resolution\"| X[\"Exit to cash or rival\"]\n  D -.->|\"Failed or unclear first use\"| X\n  B -.->|\"Document or PIN problems\"| X\n```"}],"misconceptions":["\"High NPS means customers are loyal.\" NPS shows stated willingness to recommend; loyalty must be confirmed with behaviour such as repeat use and retention.","\"A good app is enough.\" Many customers rely on USSD and agents, so an app-only experience excludes them.","\"Removing friction is always good.\" Some friction, such as confirming a recipient, prevents costly errors and builds trust.","\"Banks will lose to FinTechs and MNOs.\" Each type has structural strengths and weaknesses; partnership and coopetition are common outcomes."],"examMistakes":["Describing a journey as a list of features instead of stages, touchpoints, emotions and pain points.","Quoting NPS formulae or benchmark scores without discussing limitations.","Treating USSD as obsolete rather than a key inclusion channel.","Applying Five Forces as a list of headings without evidence or a conclusion.","Ignoring regulation and trust when recommending strategy for a financial service."],"criticalQuestions":["A provider''s NPS rises while active usage falls. What explanations are possible, and what data would you collect to distinguish them?","Should a financial service add friction, such as extra confirmation screens, if it reduces conversion but also reduces fraud losses? Justify your position.","How might interoperability change the basis of competition between banks, MNOs and FinTechs?"],"exercises":["**Journey map.** Choose a digital financial service and map a journey for a defined persona across at least six stages, marking touchpoints, emotions, pain points and metrics. *Expected output:* a labelled diagram or table and a 250-word analysis of the two most critical moments of truth.","**Funnel analysis.** Using the invented NkwaPay figures, calculate the completion rate at each stage and overall, then propose two experiments to improve the weakest stage. *Expected output:* a table of rates and a short experiment plan with success measures.","**USSD menu design.** Design a five-step USSD menu for a bill payment that shows fees, confirms the recipient and handles errors. *Expected output:* a menu flow diagram with the exact wording of each screen."],"takeaways":["Customer experience is cumulative, multi-actor and emotional, and is a main differentiator in digital finance.","Journey mapping combines qualitative insight and quantitative data to locate pain points and moments of truth.","NPS, CSAT, CES and funnel metrics each have uses and limits, and should be triangulated and linked to behaviour.","Inclusive omnichannel design treats USSD, agents and apps as parts of one relationship and designs for the least capable device first.","Trust depends on reliability, transparency, security and fair treatment, and some friction supports it.","Banks, MNOs and FinTechs hold different strengths, and coopetition shapes strategy."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Bharadwaj, A., El Sawy, O. A., Pavlou, P. A., & Venkatraman, N. (2013). Digital business strategy: Toward a next generation of insights. *MIS Quarterly, 37*(2), 471-482.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Dixon, M., Freeman, K., & Toman, N. (2010). Stop trying to delight your customers. *Harvard Business Review, 88*(7-8), 116-122.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Lemon, K. N., & Verhoef, P. C. (2016). Understanding customer experience throughout the customer journey. *Journal of Marketing, 80*(6), 69-96.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Parker, G. G., Van Alstyne, M. W., & Choudary, S. P. (2016). *Platform revolution: How networked markets are transforming the economy and how to make them work for you*. W. W. Norton.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Porter, M. E. (2008). The five competitive forces that shape strategy. *Harvard Business Review, 86*(1), 78-93.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Reichheld, F. F. (2003). The one number you need to grow. *Harvard Business Review, 81*(12), 46-54.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"GSMA Mobile for Development","url":"https://www.gsma.com/mobilefordevelopment/","description":"GSMA. Research and reports on mobile money, digital inclusion and customer needs in emerging markets.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"CGAP","url":"https://www.cgap.org/","description":"Consultative Group to Assist the Poor. Publications on customer-centred design and financial inclusion.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Bank of Ghana","url":"https://www.bog.gov.gh/","description":"Bank of Ghana. Official notices relevant to payment services and consumer protection.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Customer experience in digital finance is the cumulative result of touchpoints across a journey involving the provider, agents, partners and the customer''s context. Journey mapping identifies stages, emotions, pain points and moments of truth, and metrics such as NPS, CSAT, CES and funnel completion help to measure them but must be triangulated and linked to behaviour. Inclusive omnichannel design treats USSD, agents and apps as one relationship and builds in trust through transparency and sensible friction. Digital business strategy uses frameworks such as Five Forces and platform thinking to compare banks, MNOs and FinTechs, whose strengths and weaknesses lead them to compete and cooperate.\n\n- Experience is cumulative, multi-actor and emotional.\n- Funnel shows where, interviews show why.\n- NPS is a trend indicator, not proof of loyalty.\n- Design for the least capable device first.\n- Coopetition shapes bank, MNO and FinTech strategy."}'::jsonb, 3302, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('64451f85-bb50-5329-b017-02f7e25ac019', 'e6a916e0-30da-5768-a630-929602b5b22a', 'international-fintech-developments', 'International FinTech Developments', 'Compare open banking, instant payment systems, mobile money, cross-border payment innovation, central bank digital currencies and embedded finance internationally, and evaluate what Ghana can learn from each.', array['Open banking', 'Instant payment systems', 'Central bank digital currencies', 'Cross-border payments']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Digital finance is shaped by decisions taken in many countries at once. The European Union required banks to open customer data to authorised third parties. India built a public instant payment interface that private apps use. Brazil''s central bank launched an instant payment system open to banks and non-banks. Kenya demonstrated that a mobile network operator could run a national payment network. Several central banks have experimented with digital versions of their currencies, with very different results. Studying these developments allows a Ghanaian student or practitioner to see which design choices produce which outcomes, and which lessons transfer to a different institutional context.\n\nThis lesson surveys six themes: open banking and APIs, instant payment systems, mobile money beyond Ghana, cross-border and remittance innovation, central bank digital currencies (CBDCs), and embedded finance. It distinguishes **established practice**, where there is a track record, from **emerging** or **speculative** developments, where evidence is still limited. Descriptions are kept at the level of widely documented facts, and no statistics are given unless widely established. The lesson is educational and is not financial or legal advice.","objectives":["Explain how open banking and API-based data sharing work and compare the regulation-led and market-led approaches.","Compare account-based instant payment systems such as India''s UPI and Brazil''s Pix with Ghana''s arrangements.","Analyse the factors behind the success and limits of mobile money in different countries.","Evaluate innovations in cross-border payments and remittances, including their regulatory and cost challenges.","Distinguish retail CBDCs, wholesale CBDCs and other digital money, and critically assess eNaira and the Bank of Ghana''s eCedi pilot.","Draw reasoned lessons for Ghana, distinguishing transferable principles from context-dependent choices."],"prerequisites":["The Digital Finance lessons on FinTech, mobile money and payment gateways.","The Ghanaian mobile money ecosystem lesson and the regulation and compliance lesson in this module.","The blockchain and digital finance module, especially cryptocurrencies and distributed ledgers."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Open banking","definition":"A framework in which banks share customer account data and payment initiation capabilities with authorised third parties, with the customer''s consent, through secure APIs."},{"term":"PSD2","definition":"The EU''s second Payment Services Directive, which introduced requirements for strong customer authentication and for banks to give licensed third-party providers access to customer accounts."},{"term":"Instant payment system","definition":"A system that clears and settles account-to-account payments within seconds, around the clock."},{"term":"UPI (Unified Payments Interface)","definition":"India''s real-time payment system, run by the National Payments Corporation of India."},{"term":"Pix","definition":"Brazil''s instant payment system, introduced by the Central Bank of Brazil."},{"term":"Central bank digital currency (CBDC)","definition":"A digital form of a country''s currency issued as a direct liability of the central bank."},{"term":"Embedded finance","definition":"The integration of financial services such as payments, credit or insurance into non-financial platforms and journeys."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"Before copying any foreign model, ask what problem it solved, who led it, which enabling conditions (digital identity, mobile penetration, trusted institutions) existed, and what trade-offs it accepted.\n\n### Open banking and APIs\n\nOpen banking turns bank-held data into a resource that customers can direct to other providers. With the customer''s consent, a third party can retrieve transaction histories, which supports budgeting tools and credit assessment, or initiate payments directly from the customer''s bank account, which can bypass card networks. Technically, the model relies on secure APIs, standard data formats, customer consent management and strong authentication.\n\nTwo broad approaches are visible.\n\n| Approach | Description | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Regulation-led | Law or regulator requires banks to open APIs to licensed providers | The EU''s PSD2; the UK''s open banking programme, which began with a competition authority order on the largest banks and was supported by a standards body |\n| Market-led | Banks and providers develop access through industry agreements and commercial deals, sometimes supported by regulator guidance | Various jurisdictions where banks offer APIs commercially |\n\nWidely discussed benefits are more competition, better personal finance tools and richer data for lending to people with thin credit files. Concerns include privacy, liability when something goes wrong, third-party security, who bears API costs, and whether customers truly understand consent. Evidence on long-run outcomes varies by market, so state conclusions cautiously.\n\nOpen banking extends into broader **open finance** (insurance, pensions, investments), still emerging in many places. A related trend is **data portability rights** in data protection law, which give customers a basis for moving their data.\n\n### Instant payment systems\n\nInstant payment systems let customers send money from one bank account to another in seconds at any time. Established examples include the UK''s Faster Payments service (introduced in 2008), the EU''s SEPA Instant Credit Transfer scheme, and the United States'' FedNow service (launched in 2023). Two public-sector-led systems have attracted special attention in emerging markets.\n\n**India''s UPI**, developed by the National Payments Corporation of India and launched in 2016, lets customers link bank accounts to apps and pay using virtual addresses or QR codes. Key design features are open, interoperable standards that allow many third-party apps to operate on the same rails, very low or zero merchant fees for many transaction types (a policy choice), and linkage with India''s national digital identity infrastructure. UPI has been widely studied as an example of public digital infrastructure.\n\n**Brazil''s Pix**, introduced by the Central Bank of Brazil in late 2020, is run by the central bank and open to banks and authorised payment institutions. It supports payments by key (such as a phone number) or QR code, around the clock, and participation obligations on larger institutions helped drive rapid adoption.\n\nWhat the cases share is **central coordination of an open, interoperable rail**, a user-friendly proxy identifier, and a public interest design that lowers prices. What differs is who operates the system and how fees are set. In Ghana, the **Ghana Interbank Payment and Settlement Systems (GhIPSS)** operates national infrastructure including instant interbank payments (known as GhIPSS Instant Pay), the gh-link card network, mobile money interoperability and the GhQR code. This places Ghana in the category of countries with a national switch coordinating multiple providers, so comparisons with UPI and Pix focus on pricing, merchant acceptance, ease of use and the inclusion of non-bank wallets.\n\n### Mobile money beyond Ghana\n\nMobile money first scaled in East Africa. **M-Pesa** in Kenya (launched in 2007) is the best-known case, supported by an agent network, a simple remittance proposition and a flexible regulatory approach. Elsewhere in Africa, mobile money has grown in West and East African markets with varying structures: some countries have telecom-led e-money issuers, others require bank-led models. Outside Africa, **bKash** in Bangladesh is a frequently cited example of a mobile financial service operating through agents, while in the Philippines, wallet providers such as GCash grew alongside regulatory support for e-money. In China, **Alipay** and **WeChat Pay** show a different pattern: super-apps from technology platforms that embedded payments into social and commerce ecosystems, using QR codes.\n\nComparing these cases highlights the factors that shape outcomes: starting conditions (how inconvenient cash and bank alternatives were), agent distribution and profitability, the regulatory model (e-money safeguards and tiered KYC), market structure (one dominant provider versus several connected by interoperability), and whether payments grew into savings, credit and insurance under adequate consumer protection.\n\nMobile money''s contribution to inclusion is well documented, though it does not by itself guarantee broad financial health; usage depth, quality of credit products and customer protection remain important.\n\n### Cross-border payments and remittances\n\nCross-border payments have traditionally relied on chains of correspondent banks, which can make them slow, opaque and expensive, especially for small remittances. International bodies, including the G20 and the Financial Stability Board, have set out roadmaps to make cross-border payments faster, cheaper, more transparent and more inclusive. Innovation takes several forms. Traditional networks have added tracking and speed, for example SWIFT''s gpi service. Specialist remittance firms and apps pool funds locally and pay out locally, reducing cross-border movements. Links between mobile money schemes and between national instant payment systems allow a wallet in one country to pay an account in another. Regional systems such as the **Pan-African Payment and Settlement System (PAPSS)**, supported by Afreximbank and the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA) Secretariat, aim to settle intra-African payments in local currencies instead of routing them through foreign currencies and external banks; adoption has progressed in stages, so check its current status. Stablecoins and crypto-assets for cross-border transfers are **emerging and contested**: they may offer speed and lower fees but raise questions of reserve backing, volatility, AML compliance, consumer protection and capital flow management, so treat claims of cost savings cautiously.\n\nFor Ghana, remittances from the diaspora are economically important, and the cost, speed and traceability of inbound transfers, as well as the use of mobile wallets as payout channels, are key policy and business questions.\n\n### Central bank digital currencies\n\nA CBDC is a digital liability of the central bank in the national unit of account. It differs from commercial bank deposits (a liability of a bank), from e-money (a liability of a licensed issuer backed by trust funds) and from cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin (no issuer). A 2020 joint report by several central banks and the Bank for International Settlements set out foundational principles, including that a CBDC should coexist with cash and other forms of money and should not harm monetary or financial stability.\n\nDesign choices include retail versus wholesale; account-based or token-based; direct issuance or an intermediated model in which banks and payment firms serve the public; offline functionality; privacy features; and holding limits. Stated motives include inclusion, payment efficiency, resilience and monetary sovereignty. Risks include **disintermediation of banks** if people move deposits to the central bank, privacy and surveillance concerns, and cyber and operational burden.\n\nThe Bahamas'' Sand Dollar was among the first national retail CBDCs. Nigeria''s eNaira, launched by the Central Bank of Nigeria in October 2021, was a major African retail CBDC, and public reports indicated that uptake remained modest, which is often read as evidence that a CBDC must offer clear advantages over existing mobile money and bank apps. The Bank of Ghana has described its eCedi as a digital currency initiative in a pilot stage, with aims including inclusion and improved payments; because its status can change, rely on current Bank of Ghana statements. Wholesale CBDC experiments involving several central banks and the BIS are mainly **experimental**. In markets where mobile money and interoperability already work well, the added value of a retail CBDC is less obvious, and offline capability, privacy and resilience are the more plausible distinctive benefits. Claims that CBDCs will transform finance remain speculative until evidence accumulates.\n\n### Embedded finance\n\nEmbedded finance means that non-financial platforms, such as marketplaces, ride-hailing apps, software providers or telecom services, offer financial products within their own journeys, often via licensed partners through APIs (\"banking as a service\"). Examples include a marketplace offering working capital to sellers based on their sales data, or an accounting platform that offers payments and lending to small firms. The model promises convenience, better data for underwriting and new distribution. Risks include blurred responsibility between platform and licensed provider, conduct risks if credit is pushed at the point of sale, data protection concerns, and concentration of dependencies on a few infrastructure providers. Regulators increasingly stress that the licensed entity remains accountable and that customers must know who provides the product."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Public infrastructure versus private platforms\n\nOne framework distinguishes **public digital infrastructure** (open rails operated or coordinated by public authorities, on which many private actors innovate) from **private platform ecosystems** (closed networks run by firms). UPI and Pix lean to the first, Alipay and WeChat Pay to the second, and M-Pesa to a telecom-led hybrid shaped by regulation. Each affects competition, data control, resilience and innovation differently. Questions to ask: who sets the rules, who captures the data, who earns fees, and what happens if the operator fails?\n\n### Policy transfer checklist\n\nWhen considering a foreign model for Ghana, apply a five-step test.\n\n1. **Problem fit**: is the problem the same?\n2. **Prerequisites**: are identity, connectivity, data protection, interoperability and supervisory capacity in place?\n3. **Incentives**: do banks, MNOs, merchants and users gain enough to participate?\n4. **Risks and safeguards**: what new risks, such as concentration, privacy or financial stability, appear, and how would they be managed?\n5. **Evidence and sequencing**: can it be piloted and measured before scaling?"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Scoring a policy option against the checklist\n\n**Illustrative scenario:** A hypothetical Ghanaian policy team is asked whether Ghana should require banks to offer standardised open banking APIs to licensed third parties. They use the policy transfer checklist and a simple weighted score. The scores are invented for teaching and are not evidence.\n\n| Criterion | Weight | Score (1-5) | Weighted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Problem fit (limited use of data for SME lending) | 0.25 | 4 | 1.00 |\n| Prerequisites (identity, data protection law, API capability in banks) | 0.25 | 3 | 0.75 |\n| Incentives (banks may resist, FinTechs and SMEs gain) | 0.20 | 2 | 0.40 |\n| Risk safeguards (consent, security, liability rules) | 0.20 | 3 | 0.60 |\n| Evidence and sequencing (pilot possible in sandbox) | 0.10 | 4 | 0.40 |\n| **Total** | 1.00 | | **3.15** |\n\n**Interpretation.** The total of 3.15 out of 5 indicates a moderately favourable case, not an automatic yes. The team notes that the weakest criterion is incentives for banks. They therefore recommend a **staged approach**: first a voluntary standard and a sandbox pilot with willing banks, then review the experience, and only then consider mandatory obligations. They also state that the weights and scores are judgements which stakeholders should debate and revise, and that the method structures reasoning rather than producing a scientific answer."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Ghanaian firms can use these international developments as strategic scenarios. A bank can prepare for open banking by building secure APIs for its own channels and partners, since the capability is useful even without mandatory rules. An SME lender can use consented transaction data from wallets or bank accounts to assess borrowers with no formal credit history, within data protection law. A payment aggregator can position itself for cross-border demand through regional initiatives and remittance corridors, and an e-commerce platform can embed payments and working capital for sellers through licensed partners. At national level, GhIPSS interoperability, GhQR and the Ghana Card are building blocks similar to those behind UPI and Pix. Any plan involving CBDC or crypto-assets should rely on current Bank of Ghana and legal guidance, because policy is evolving."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Brazil''s Pix\n\nThe Central Bank of Brazil launched Pix in November 2020 as an instant payment system operated by the central bank itself, available around the clock. Larger financial institutions were required to participate, and users could pay with a simple key such as a phone number, or by QR code. The design combined a public-interest rail, open participation for banks and authorised non-banks, and a user experience built around simple keys. Observers have noted rapid adoption by individuals and businesses, and effects on how payments are made for small merchants and person-to-person transfers. The case is useful for three reasons. First, it shows that a central bank can act as infrastructure provider, not only regulator. Second, mandatory participation helped create network effects quickly, which parallels the way interoperability in Ghana expanded network effects across providers. Third, it raises questions about competition with private payment methods, data governance and the role of the public sector. For Ghana, the transferable lesson is the combination of simple identifiers, universal QR, fair access and pricing policy; the context-specific parts are the scale of the Brazilian banking sector and its institutional structure."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  P[\"Policy or product idea from abroad\"] --> A[\"Problem fit?\"]\n  A -->|\"No\"| X[\"Do not transfer\"]\n  A -->|\"Yes\"| B[\"Enabling conditions present?\"]\n  B -->|\"No\"| Y[\"Build prerequisites: ID, APIs, supervision\"]\n  B -->|\"Yes\"| C[\"Incentives for banks, MNOs, merchants, users?\"]\n  Y --> C\n  C --> D[\"Risk safeguards: privacy, concentration, stability\"]\n  D --> E[\"Pilot or sandbox and measure\"]\n  E --> F[\"Scale, adapt or stop\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["\"UPI and Pix can simply be copied.\" They depend on identity, interoperability, bank participation and policy choices that differ by country.","\"A CBDC is the same as a cryptocurrency.\" A CBDC is issued by the central bank in the national currency, while cryptocurrencies have no issuer backing.","\"Open banking means banks share data with anyone.\" Access is limited to authorised providers, with customer consent and security requirements.","\"Mobile money only works where there is a single dominant provider.\" Ghana shows that several providers connected by interoperability can operate at scale.","\"Stablecoins have already solved cross-border payments.\" They remain contested, with unresolved regulatory, reserve and consumer protection questions."],"examMistakes":["Giving statistics on adoption or transaction volumes from memory instead of describing patterns qualitatively.","Presenting emerging developments, such as CBDC adoption or stablecoin payments, as proven success stories.","Describing foreign systems without analysing what problem they solved and under what conditions.","Confusing open banking (data and payment access for third parties) with open-source software or with public APIs generally.","Concluding that Ghana should adopt a model without checking prerequisites and risks."],"criticalQuestions":["Why might a regulation-led open banking regime produce different results from a market-led one, and which is more suitable for Ghana''s current conditions?","If mobile money and instant interbank payments already work in a country, what additional value could a retail CBDC provide, and what are the risks?","What design features of Pix and UPI could improve merchant acceptance in Ghana, and which would be hard to replicate?"],"exercises":["**Comparative table.** Compare two instant payment systems (for example UPI or Pix, and GhIPSS Instant Pay) on operator, participants, identifiers, QR use and pricing approach, using official sources. *Expected output:* a table with sources and dates, plus a 200-word comparison.","**Open banking consent design.** Design a consent screen for a budgeting app that requests account data, showing purpose, duration, data types and how to withdraw. *Expected output:* a wireframe or text mock-up with a short rationale linked to data protection principles.","**CBDC evaluation.** Write a 400-word assessment of whether Ghana''s eCedi should prioritise offline payments, financial inclusion or cross-border use, citing the Bank of Ghana''s current statements. *Expected output:* a short report with a recommendation and risks."],"takeaways":["International models differ in problem, leadership, enabling conditions and trade-offs, so transfer requires analysis, not imitation.","Open banking uses consented API access to data and payments, and can be regulation-led or market-led.","Instant payment systems such as UPI and Pix combine open rails, simple identifiers and QR codes with public coordination.","Mobile money outcomes depend on distribution, regulation, market structure and product expansion.","Cross-border innovation includes better messaging, remittance firms, scheme links and regional systems like PAPSS, while stablecoin use remains contested.","CBDCs vary widely in design and results, and their added value over existing digital payments must be demonstrated.","Embedded finance extends reach but raises accountability and conduct questions."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Bank for International Settlements, Bank of Canada, European Central Bank, Bank of Japan, Sveriges Riksbank, Swiss National Bank, Bank of England, & Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System. (2020). *Central bank digital currencies: Foundational principles and core features*. Bank for International Settlements.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Demirgüç-Kunt, A., Klapper, L., Singer, D., & Ansar, S. (2022). *The Global Findex Database 2021: Financial inclusion, digital payments, and resilience in the age of COVID-19*. World Bank.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Jack, W., & Suri, T. (2014). Risk sharing and transactions costs: Evidence from Kenya''s mobile money revolution. *American Economic Review, 104*(1), 183-223.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Directive (EU) 2015/2366 of the European Parliament and of the Council on payment services in the internal market (PSD2).","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Bank for International Settlements","url":"https://www.bis.org/","description":"BIS. Publications and innovation hub projects on CBDCs, payments and cross-border arrangements.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Central Bank of Brazil","url":"https://www.bcb.gov.br/","description":"Banco Central do Brasil. Official information on Pix.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"National Payments Corporation of India","url":"https://www.npci.org.in/","description":"NPCI. Official information on UPI.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"Afreximbank","url":"https://www.afreximbank.com/","description":"African Export-Import Bank. Official information on PAPSS and intra-African trade finance.","verification":"unverified"}],"revisionSummary":"International developments show several design families. Open banking opens bank data and payment initiation to authorised third parties through APIs. Instant payment systems such as UPI and Pix combine open rails, simple identifiers and QR codes with public coordination, and GhIPSS plays a comparable infrastructure role in Ghana. Mobile money spread through agents and flexible regulation, cross-border payments are being improved by scheme and regional links, and CBDCs such as eNaira and the eCedi pilot must prove added value over existing digital payments. Embedded finance brings financial products into non-financial platforms. Ghana should test each model for problem fit, prerequisites, incentives, risks and pilot evidence.\n\n- Open banking: consented API access, regulation-led or market-led.\n- UPI and Pix: open rails, simple identifiers, public coordination.\n- Mobile money: distribution, regulation and market structure drive outcomes.\n- CBDC: not a cryptocurrency; value over mobile money must be shown.\n- Test every foreign model: problem, prerequisites, incentives, risks, pilot."}'::jsonb, 3440, 3, true)
