insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('31d0a2bd-60aa-5875-936f-94845aacbc75', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', 'business-model-and-payment-process-projects', 'Projects: E-Commerce Business Model and Online Payment Process', 'Design an e-commerce business model with unit economics for a Ghanaian SME and specify a secure, reconcilable online payment process with sequence and state diagrams, deliverables, evaluation criteria and risk analysis.', array['E-commerce business model', 'Online payment process']::text[], 60, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"The earlier modules of this course built a toolkit: business models and value creation, e-commerce technology, digital finance, payment security, blockchain and Ghana''s digital payments ecosystem. This lesson asks you to apply that toolkit to two practical projects of the kind an MSc IT for Business graduate might be asked to produce for an employer, client or capstone assessment.\n\n**Project A** designs an e-commerce business model for a Ghanaian SME, grounded in the Business Model Canvas and tested with unit economics. **Project B** designs the online payment process that the business model depends on, from checkout to confirmation, settlement and reconciliation, documented with a sequence diagram and a payment state model. Both projects use the same **Illustrative scenario**, so that the business model and the payment design reinforce each other. Each project specifies deliverables, evaluation criteria and risks. These are supplementary practice projects; they are not official University of Ghana assessments, and any payment or regulatory content is educational rather than professional advice.","objectives":["Apply the Business Model Canvas to design an e-commerce model for a Ghanaian SME.","Calculate contribution margin per order and evaluate the viability of a model using unit economics.","Design an online payment process covering mobile money and cards, with security and failure handling.","Construct a sequence diagram and a payment state model for a checkout flow.","Evaluate project deliverables against explicit criteria and identify key project risks."],"prerequisites":["Online business models and the Business Model Canvas from the E-Business Foundations module.","Payment gateways, the transaction lifecycle and payment security from the Digital Finance and Payment Security modules.","Reconciliation and fraud controls from the Payment Security module."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Business Model Canvas","definition":"Osterwalder and Pigneur''s nine-block framework describing how an organisation creates, delivers and captures value."},{"term":"Unit economics","definition":"Revenue and costs measured per unit, such as per order or per customer, to test whether growth creates or destroys value."},{"term":"Contribution margin","definition":"Revenue per unit minus variable costs per unit, available to cover fixed costs and profit."},{"term":"Customer acquisition cost (CAC)","definition":"The average marketing and sales cost of acquiring one paying customer or order."},{"term":"Sequence diagram","definition":"A diagram showing the order of messages exchanged between participants in a process."},{"term":"Payment state model","definition":"A definition of the states a payment or order can be in and the events that move it between them."},{"term":"Webhook","definition":"A server-to-server notification by which a PSP informs a merchant system that a payment''s status has changed."},{"term":"Idempotency","definition":"The property that repeating the same request produces the same result once, preventing duplicate charges."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The shared scenario\n\n**Illustrative scenario:** Asante Shea Naturals is a hypothetical Tamale-based SME producing shea butter skincare. It currently sells at local markets and to a few customers via WhatsApp. The owners want to sell nationally online, and later to the Ghanaian diaspora, accepting mobile money on all networks and cards.\n\n### Project A: e-commerce business model design\n\n**Task.** Design an e-commerce business model for Asante Shea Naturals and test its viability.\n\n**Method.**\n\n1. **Customer segments.** Distinguish, for example, urban professionals in Accra and Kumasi buying natural skincare, gift buyers, small salons reselling products and, in a later phase, diaspora customers.\n2. **Value propositions.** Authentic Northern Ghana shea, natural ingredients, traceable sourcing from women''s cooperatives and convenient delivery. Each proposition should be linked to a segment.\n3. **Channels.** Instagram and TikTok for discovery, WhatsApp for customer service, an own online store for checkout, and possibly a marketplace listing for reach.\n4. **Customer relationships.** Community-led content, loyalty rewards and responsive chat support.\n5. **Revenue streams.** Product sales, bundles and gift sets, and wholesale to salons.\n6. **Key resources and activities.** Production, quality control, content creation, order fulfilment and payment operations.\n7. **Key partners.** Shea cooperatives, packaging suppliers, a licensed PSP or aggregator, delivery firms.\n8. **Cost structure.** Raw materials, packaging, delivery subsidies, payment fees, marketing and platform subscriptions.\n9. **Unit economics.** Test whether each order makes money after variable costs and marketing.\n\n**Deliverables.** A completed canvas with justification for each block; a unit-economics model with assumptions stated; a competitor comparison of at least three alternatives available to the target segment; and a 1,000-1,500-word business model report with recommendations.\n\n**Evaluation criteria.**\n\n| Criterion | What good looks like |\n|---|---|\n| Coherence | Value propositions, segments, channels and revenue streams fit together logically |\n| Evidence | Assumptions are explicit and supported by observation or credible sources, not invented statistics |\n| Viability | Unit economics show positive contribution after acquisition costs, or a credible path to it |\n| Local fit | The model reflects Ghanaian payment habits, logistics and trust issues |\n| Critical reflection | Weaknesses and alternative models are discussed honestly |\n\n**Risks.** Over-optimistic assumptions about demand or costs; underestimating delivery costs outside major cities; dependence on social media algorithms; product regulatory requirements for cosmetics, which must be checked with the relevant authority; and diaspora shipping complexity.\n\n### Project B: online payment process design\n\n**Task.** Design the end-to-end online payment process for the Asante Shea Naturals store.\n\n**Design requirements.**\n\n- Accept mobile money on all major networks, cards and, optionally, bank transfer through a licensed aggregator.\n- Use a hosted or embedded checkout so that card data never touches the merchant''s servers.\n- For mobile money, use merchant-initiated payment prompts so customers approve on their own handsets with a PIN.\n- Mark orders as paid only after a **verified webhook** or a server-side status query, never on browser redirect alone.\n- Use an **idempotency key** for each payment request to prevent duplicate charges.\n- Handle pending, failed, expired and reversed payments explicitly.\n- Store PSP references with each order to enable daily reconciliation.\n\n**Payment state model.**\n\n| State | Entered when | Next possible states |\n|---|---|---|\n| CREATED | Customer confirms basket | PENDING, CANCELLED |\n| PENDING | Payment request sent to PSP | PAID, FAILED, EXPIRED |\n| PAID | Verified webhook confirms success | FULFILLED, REFUNDED |\n| FAILED | PSP reports failure | CREATED (retry) |\n| EXPIRED | No confirmation within the time limit | CREATED (retry) |\n| FULFILLED | Order dispatched | REFUNDED (return) |\n| REFUNDED | Refund confirmed by PSP | n/a |\n\n**Deliverables.** A process narrative; a sequence diagram; the state model; a controls table mapping each risk to a control; a reconciliation procedure; and a test plan with at least ten test cases, including duplicate clicks, network timeouts, a forged callback and a customer who closes the browser after paying.\n\n**Evaluation criteria.** Correctness and completeness of the flow; security (no card data stored, signatures verified, PINs never collected); robustness to failures; reconcilability; usability for customers on low-end phones; and clarity of documentation.\n\n**Risks.** Integration errors leading to unpaid orders being dispatched; webhook delays causing customer confusion; PSP outages during campaigns; fraud through stolen cards or social engineering; and data protection obligations when storing customer details."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Unit economics for Asante Shea Naturals\n\nAll figures are hypothetical assumptions for teaching.\n\n| Item per order | GHS |\n|---|---|\n| Average order value | 180.00 |\n| Cost of goods (shea products) | −70.00 |\n| Packaging | −8.00 |\n| Delivery subsidy (portion not charged to customer) | −15.00 |\n| Payment fee (2% of 180) | −3.60 |\n| **Contribution margin before marketing** | **83.40** |\n| Customer acquisition cost per order | −40.00 |\n| **Contribution after marketing** | **43.40** |\n\n**Interpretation.** Each order contributes GHS 43.40 towards fixed costs such as salaries, rent and software. If monthly fixed costs were, say, GHS 6,500, the break-even volume would be 6,500 ÷ 43.40 ≈ 150 orders a month. The model is most sensitive to acquisition cost and delivery subsidy: if CAC rose to GHS 70, contribution after marketing would fall to GHS 13.40, and break-even would rise to about 485 orders. This sensitivity suggests prioritising repeat purchases, which have lower acquisition costs, through loyalty programmes and excellent service.\n\n### The payment sequence\n\nThe sequence for a mobile money checkout is shown in the diagram section. Key design points:\n\n1. The storefront server, not the browser, creates the payment request with an idempotency key.\n2. The PSP triggers a prompt on the customer''s phone; the customer enters the PIN on the handset.\n3. The PSP sends a signed webhook; the server verifies the signature and checks amount and reference before marking the order paid.\n4. The browser polls the server for status and shows confirmation, so a closed browser does not affect the order.\n5. Settlement reports are imported daily for reconciliation."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"Both projects must reflect Ghanaian realities. Customers expect to pay by mobile money from any network, so an aggregator is usually preferable to integrating with a single wallet. Many customers use low-end Android phones on mobile data, so the checkout should be lightweight and the prompt-and-PIN approach avoids typing long card numbers. Cash on delivery may still be offered at first, with a plan to encourage digital payment as trust grows. Delivery outside major cities can be expensive and slow, which affects both the unit economics and the payment design, for example by holding payment confirmation until dispatch is confirmed for remote areas. The PSP should be licensed or authorised under the Payment Systems and Services Act, 2019 (Act 987), and customer data should be handled in line with the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843)."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Payment process design in hosted checkout platforms\n\nInternational PSPs such as Stripe publish developer documentation that illustrates widely adopted good practice in payment process design: server-side creation of payment intents, client-side collection of payment details in provider-hosted fields, signed webhooks as the source of truth for payment status, and idempotency keys for safe retries. Hosted e-commerce platforms similarly let small merchants plug in a payment provider without handling card data. These practices emerged because early integrations that trusted browser redirects or stored card numbers suffered fraud, disputes and breaches. For Ghanaian SMEs, the lesson is that a small business can achieve robust payment design by adopting proven patterns through a reputable PSP, rather than building them from scratch."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n  participant C as Customer\n  participant B as Browser\n  participant S as Store server\n  participant P as PSP / aggregator\n  participant W as Mobile money issuer\n  C->>B: Confirm basket and choose mobile money\n  B->>S: Create order request\n  S->>P: Payment request with idempotency key\n  P->>W: Initiate collection\n  W->>C: Prompt on handset\n  C->>W: Approve with PIN\n  W->>P: Payment result\n  P->>S: Signed webhook (status, amount, reference)\n  S->>S: Verify signature, amount and reference\n  S-->>B: Order status PAID (via polling)\n  B-->>C: Show confirmation and receipt\n  P-->>S: Daily settlement report for reconciliation\n```\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  CR[\"CREATED\"] --> PE[\"PENDING\"]\n  CR --> CA[\"CANCELLED\"]\n  PE --> PA[\"PAID\"]\n  PE --> FA[\"FAILED\"]\n  PE --> EX[\"EXPIRED\"]\n  FA --> CR\n  EX --> CR\n  PA --> FU[\"FULFILLED\"]\n  PA --> RE[\"REFUNDED\"]\n  FU --> RE\n```"}],"misconceptions":["\"A business model is just how the business makes money.\" It also covers value propositions, segments, channels, relationships, resources, partners and costs.","\"Positive gross margin means the model is viable.\" Delivery, payment fees and acquisition costs can turn a healthy gross margin into a loss per order.","\"Payment is finished when the customer sees a success page.\" The source of truth is the verified PSP notification, followed by settlement and reconciliation.","\"Retry buttons are harmless.\" Without idempotency, retries can create duplicate charges."],"examMistakes":["Filling in the canvas blocks without showing how they connect or justifying choices.","Presenting invented market statistics instead of clearly labelled assumptions.","Drawing a payment flow that omits failure, timeout and refund paths.","Ignoring reconciliation in a payment process design.","Omitting evaluation criteria and risks from a project proposal."],"criticalQuestions":["How would the business model change if Asante Shea Naturals prioritised wholesale to salons over direct online sales?","Which single assumption in the unit-economics model would you test first, and how?","Should the store offer cash on delivery at launch? Argue both sides and reach a conclusion.","How would the payment design need to change to serve diaspora customers paying by international card?"],"exercises":["**Project A submission.** Produce the canvas, unit-economics model and report for Asante Shea Naturals or another clearly labelled illustrative Ghanaian SME. *Expected output:* the deliverables listed for Project A, assessed against the evaluation criteria.","**Project B submission.** Produce the process narrative, sequence diagram, state model, controls table, reconciliation procedure and test plan. *Expected output:* a design pack of six to ten pages.","**Peer review.** Exchange designs with a classmate and review each other''s payment flow against the design requirements. *Expected output:* a review note listing at least three strengths, three weaknesses and prioritised fixes."],"takeaways":["A coherent business model links segments, value propositions, channels and revenue, and must survive unit-economics testing.","Contribution after acquisition costs, and its sensitivity to assumptions, determines whether growth creates value.","Robust payment design uses hosted checkout, handset PIN approval, verified webhooks, idempotency and explicit failure states.","Sequence diagrams and state models make payment designs precise and testable.","Every project needs clear deliverables, evaluation criteria and a risk analysis."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Osterwalder, A., & Pigneur, Y. (2010). *Business model generation: A handbook for visionaries, game changers, and challengers*. Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Laudon, K. C., & Traver, C. G. (n.d.). *E-commerce: Business, technology, society*. Pearson. (Consult the chapters on business models and payment systems in the latest edition.)","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"PCI Security Standards Council. (2022). *Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard: Requirements and testing procedures* (Version 4.0).","url":"https://www.pcisecuritystandards.org/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Parker, G. G., Van Alstyne, M. W., & Choudary, S. P. (2016). *Platform revolution: How networked markets are transforming the economy and how to make them work for you*. W. W. Norton.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Stripe documentation","url":"https://docs.stripe.com/","description":"Stripe. Developer documentation illustrating payment intents, webhooks and idempotent requests as general design patterns.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Strategyzer","url":"https://www.strategyzer.com/","description":"Strategyzer. Official resources on the Business Model Canvas from its creators.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Mermaid documentation","url":"https://mermaid.js.org/","description":"Mermaid. Official guide to writing sequence diagrams and flowcharts in text.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Project A designs an e-commerce business model with the Business Model Canvas and tests it with unit economics: contribution margin per order after cost of goods, packaging, delivery, payment fees and acquisition costs, with sensitivity analysis. Project B designs the payment process: hosted checkout, handset PIN prompts, server-created requests with idempotency keys, verified webhooks as the source of truth, explicit failure and refund states and daily reconciliation. Each project specifies deliverables, evaluation criteria and risks, adapted to Ghanaian payment habits and logistics.\n\n- Canvas blocks must cohere.\n- Contribution after CAC drives break-even.\n- Webhook, not redirect, confirms payment.\n- Idempotency prevents duplicate charges.\n- Define states, failures and reconciliation."}'::jsonb, 2246, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('e14606a0-208d-5b40-8e8d-8672302b05c3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', 'ecosystem-strategy-and-solution-design-projects', 'Projects: Mobile Money Ecosystem Analysis, SME Strategy and Payment Solution Design', 'Conduct a structured mobile money ecosystem analysis, develop a phased digital commerce strategy for a Ghanaian SME and design a digital payment solution with requirements, architecture, controls, a business case, deliverables, evaluation criteria and risks.', array['Mobile money ecosystem analysis', 'SME digital commerce strategy', 'Digital payment solution design']::text[], 60, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"This final lesson brings the course together in three connected projects. **Project C** analyses Ghana''s mobile money ecosystem from the viewpoint of a business that wants to use it. **Project D** turns that analysis into a phased digital commerce strategy for an SME. **Project E** designs a concrete digital payment solution that implements the strategy, complete with requirements, architecture, security controls and a business case. Working through the three projects in sequence mirrors how consultants and in-house IT-for-business teams operate: understand the environment, set direction, then design and justify a solution.\n\nAll three projects use one **Illustrative scenario**. The organisation is hypothetical and must not be treated as real. The projects are supplementary practice exercises, not official University of Ghana assessments. Where they touch on regulation, the content is educational, and real implementations require current guidance from the Bank of Ghana and qualified advisers.","objectives":["Analyse a mobile money ecosystem using stakeholder mapping, PESTEL and value network analysis.","Develop a phased SME digital commerce strategy with measurable key performance indicators.","Specify functional and non-functional requirements for a digital payment solution and prioritise them.","Design a solution architecture with security, reconciliation and compliance controls.","Evaluate a solution using a business case, explicit evaluation criteria and a risk register."],"prerequisites":["All earlier modules, especially Ghana''s mobile money ecosystem, SME commerce and PSPs, payment security and reconciliation.","The previous projects lesson on business models and payment process design."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Stakeholder map","definition":"A diagram identifying the parties affected by or able to influence a system, with their interests and power."},{"term":"PESTEL analysis","definition":"A framework examining political, economic, social, technological, environmental and legal factors in a business environment."},{"term":"Value network","definition":"A map of the exchanges of value, money and information among participants in an ecosystem."},{"term":"Key performance indicator (KPI)","definition":"A measurable value that shows progress towards a strategic objective."},{"term":"Functional requirement","definition":"A statement of what a system must do, such as \"send a payment request to the customer''s wallet\"."},{"term":"Non-functional requirement","definition":"A statement of how well a system must perform, covering security, availability, performance and usability."},{"term":"MoSCoW prioritisation","definition":"Classifying requirements as Must have, Should have, Could have or Won''t have this time."},{"term":"Payback period","definition":"The time taken for cumulative benefits of an investment to equal its cost."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The shared scenario\n\n**Illustrative scenario:** Nkabom Provisions is a hypothetical wholesale distributor in Accra supplying rice, cooking oil, tinned goods and toiletries to about two hundred small retail shops in Greater Accra and the Eastern Region. Retailers order by phone or WhatsApp, pay in cash to delivery drivers or by mobile money to staff members'' personal numbers, and many buy on short credit. The owner reports frequent disputes about balances, cash losses on delivery routes, slow collections and difficulty knowing which retailers are reliable.\n\n### Project C: mobile money ecosystem analysis\n\n**Task.** Analyse the mobile money ecosystem as it affects Nkabom Provisions and its retailers.\n\n**Method.**\n\n1. **Stakeholder map.** Identify retailers, drivers, Nkabom staff, e-money issuers, agents, banks, PSPs and aggregators, GhIPSS, the Bank of Ghana, tax authorities and suppliers. Classify each by interest and power.\n2. **Value network.** Trace money, goods and information flows: goods to retailers, cash and e-value back, settlement into bank accounts, data into records.\n3. **PESTEL.** For example: *political and legal* (Act 987, data protection, AML and transfer taxes; *economic*) inflation, fees and working capital; *social* (trust, cash habits and digital skills; *technological*) interoperability, GhQR, network reliability; *environmental*, fuel use on delivery routes.\n4. **Opportunities and threats.** Summarise what the ecosystem enables (cross-network collections, bulk disbursements, transaction data) and what could go wrong (fraud, outages, fee changes).\n\n**Deliverables.** A stakeholder map, a value network diagram, a PESTEL table with evidence, and a 1,000-word analysis citing official sources such as Bank of Ghana, GSMA and World Bank publications in general terms.\n\n**Evaluation criteria.** Completeness of stakeholders; accuracy of flows; evidence-based PESTEL with no invented statistics; insight into implications for Nkabom; and clarity of diagrams.\n\n**Risks.** Relying on outdated information about providers, fees or regulation; treating the ecosystem only technically; and overlooking retailers'' perspectives.\n\n### Project D: SME digital commerce strategy\n\n**Task.** Develop a 12-month digital commerce strategy for Nkabom Provisions.\n\n**Method.**\n\n1. **Current-state assessment** using the maturity stages from the SME commerce lesson. Nkabom is at stage 2 at best, with informal ordering and personal wallets.\n2. **Strategic objectives**, for example: reduce cash on delivery routes, shorten collection times, cut balance disputes and build reliable credit data on retailers.\n3. **Phased roadmap.**\n\n| Phase | Months | Initiatives |\n|---|---|---|\n| Foundation | 0-3 | Merchant collection account via a licensed aggregator; unique retailer IDs; WhatsApp Business catalogue; daily reconciliation |\n| Expansion | 3-6 | Payment links on invoices; QR codes for drivers; order capture form; digital statements to retailers |\n| Optimisation | 6-12 | Ordering portal or app; credit limits based on payment history; analytics dashboard; possible supplier payments by bulk disbursement |\n\n4. **KPIs** such as share of collections received digitally, average days to collect, number of balance disputes per month, cash carried per route and repeat order frequency. Baselines must be measured, not assumed.\n5. **Change management.** Retailer onboarding sessions, driver training, incentives such as faster delivery for digital payers, and a transition period in which cash remains accepted.\n\n**Deliverables.** A strategy document of 1,500-2,000 words with roadmap, KPIs, change plan and budget outline.\n\n**Evaluation criteria.** Alignment with the Project C analysis; realism of phasing; measurable KPIs with baselines; attention to people and change; and justification of priorities.\n\n**Risks.** Retailer resistance, driver collusion or resistance, staff turnover, over-ambitious technology choices and underestimated training needs.\n\n### Project E: digital payment solution design\n\n**Task.** Design the payment solution for the Foundation and Expansion phases.\n\n**Functional requirements (MoSCoW).**\n\n- *Must:* accept mobile money from all major networks and bank transfer; tag every payment with a retailer ID and invoice number; send instant payment receipts; produce daily reconciliation against PSP settlement reports; show each retailer''s running balance.\n- *Should:* send payment links by WhatsApp or SMS; generate a QR code per driver for on-delivery payments; issue monthly digital statements.\n- *Could:* accept cards; offer automated credit limits; integrate with accounting software.\n- *Won''t (this phase):* cryptocurrency acceptance or a custom wallet.\n\n**Non-functional requirements.** Security (no PIN collection; signed webhooks; role-based access; audit logs); availability during business hours with a fallback process during outages; usability on basic phones; data protection under the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843); and scalability to several hundred retailers.\n\n**Architecture.** Ordering channels feed an order and invoicing module; a payment service integrates with a licensed aggregator; webhooks update a retailer ledger; a reconciliation job matches ledger, settlement reports and bank credits; dashboards report KPIs. Drivers use QR codes linked to invoices rather than carrying cash.\n\n**Controls.** Segregation of duties between staff who record sales and those who reconcile; daily exception reports; dual approval for refunds and credit-limit changes; staff awareness training on social engineering.\n\n**Deliverables.** A requirements specification, architecture diagram, controls matrix, test plan, implementation plan and business case.\n\n**Evaluation criteria.** Traceability from strategy to requirements; soundness of architecture; strength and proportionality of controls; quality of the business case; and a realistic implementation plan.\n\n**Risks.** Integration delays, PSP dependency, low adoption by retailers, data breaches, and regulatory changes affecting fees or taxes."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### A simple business case for Project E\n\nAll figures are hypothetical assumptions for teaching. Nkabom estimates a one-off investment of **GHS 18,000** (configuration, integration, training and QR materials) and ongoing costs of **GHS 600** per month (software subscriptions). It expects monthly benefits as follows:\n\n| Benefit | Assumption | GHS per month |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reduced cash losses on routes | Losses estimated from past incidents | 1,200 |\n| Staff time saved on reconciliation and disputes | 40 hours × GHS 25 | 1,000 |\n| Faster collections reducing overdraft interest | Estimated from bank charges | 800 |\n| **Total monthly benefits** | | **3,000** |\n\nPSP fees on collections are estimated at **GHS 1,000** per month, partly offset by savings on cash transport already included above.\n\n**Net monthly benefit** = 3,000 − 600 − 1,000 = **GHS 1,400**.\n\n**Payback period** = 18,000 ÷ 1,400 ≈ **12.9 months**.\n\n**Sensitivity.** If retailer adoption is slower and benefits reach only 70% (GHS 2,100), net benefit becomes 2,100 − 600 − 1,000 = 500, and payback stretches to 36 months. If the business negotiates fees down to GHS 700, the base-case net benefit becomes 1,700 and payback about 10.6 months. The business case is therefore most sensitive to adoption and fees, which suggests focusing early effort on retailer onboarding and fee negotiation. Unquantified benefits (retailer credit data, fewer disputes and better customer relationships) should be described qualitatively rather than given invented values."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"The projects are designed around real features of the Ghanaian environment: interoperable mobile money, the universal QR code, licensed aggregators, Ghana Card-based KYC and a large population of small retailers who buy on credit. A distributor such as the hypothetical Nkabom Provisions can use these features to digitise collections without forcing retailers to change wallets or buy smartphones, because payment prompts and USSD approval work on basic phones. The strategy respects cash habits by keeping cash as a fallback during transition, and it addresses trust by giving retailers instant receipts and clear statements, which often resolves the balance disputes that damage relationships. Analysts must, however, verify current regulatory details, provider capabilities and fees at the time of the project."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Merchant and distributor payments on M-Pesa\n\nIn Kenya, Safaricom extended M-Pesa beyond person-to-person transfers to merchant payments through **Lipa na M-Pesa** and to business services such as paybill numbers used for invoices, utility bills and school fees. Distributors and fast-moving consumer goods companies have used mobile money to collect from small retailers, reducing the cash carried by delivery staff. The Kenyan experience illustrates the progression this lesson models: once a mobile money ecosystem is widely used for transfers, businesses can build structured collections, reconciliation and data on top of it. It also shows that merchant adoption depends on pricing, settlement speed and the convenience of tools for small businesses: the same factors evaluated in Projects D and E."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  R[\"Retailers (phone, WhatsApp, basic phones)\"] --> O[\"Order and invoice module\"]\n  O --> L[\"Payment links and driver QR codes\"]\n  L --> AG[\"Licensed aggregator / PSP\"]\n  AG --> MM[\"Mobile money wallets (all networks)\"]\n  AG --> BT[\"Bank transfer\"]\n  AG -->|\"Signed webhooks\"| LG[\"Retailer ledger and receipts\"]\n  AG -->|\"Settlement reports\"| RC[\"Daily reconciliation\"]\n  BK[\"Bank statement\"] --> RC\n  LG --> RC\n  RC --> EX[\"Exception reports\"]\n  LG --> DB[\"KPI dashboard and credit data\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["\"A strategy is a list of technologies to buy.\" Strategy sets objectives, phasing, KPIs and change management; technology serves it.","\"Retailers will adopt digital payment once it is available.\" Adoption requires trust, incentives, training and a transition period.","\"A short payback period proves a project is right.\" Payback ignores benefits after the payback point and is sensitive to assumptions; it should be used with sensitivity analysis.","\"Non-functional requirements are optional extras.\" Security, availability and usability often determine whether a payment solution succeeds."],"examMistakes":["Producing a PESTEL list without linking factors to implications for the organisation.","Setting KPIs without baselines or targets.","Writing requirements that cannot be tested.","Presenting a business case with no stated assumptions or sensitivity analysis.","Omitting controls such as segregation of duties and exception reporting."],"criticalQuestions":["Which stakeholder in the Nkabom ecosystem has the most power to make or break the project, and how should the strategy address them?","How would the solution design change if most retailers insisted on paying in cash?","What ethical issues arise when payment histories are used to set retailers'' credit limits?","Is a payback period of about a year acceptable for an SME of this kind? What other appraisal methods could you use?"],"exercises":["**Project C submission.** Produce the stakeholder map, value network, PESTEL table and analysis for Nkabom Provisions or another clearly labelled illustrative Ghanaian SME. *Expected output:* the Project C deliverables assessed against its criteria.","**Project D submission.** Write the 12-month strategy with a phased roadmap, KPIs with baselines and a change plan. *Expected output:* a strategy document of 1,500-2,000 words.","**Project E submission.** Produce a MoSCoW-prioritised requirements specification, architecture diagram, controls matrix, test plan and business case with sensitivity analysis. *Expected output:* a solution design pack with every assumption stated."],"takeaways":["Ecosystem analysis combines stakeholder mapping, value networks and PESTEL to reveal opportunities and threats.","An SME strategy needs objectives, a phased roadmap, measurable KPIs and change management.","Solution design traces strategy to prioritised functional and non-functional requirements, architecture and controls.","Business cases must state assumptions and test sensitivity, especially to adoption and fees.","Every project needs explicit deliverables, evaluation criteria and risks."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Osterwalder, A., & Pigneur, Y. (2010). *Business model generation: A handbook for visionaries, game changers, and challengers*. Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Jack, W., & Suri, T. (2014). Risk sharing and transactions costs: Evidence from Kenya''s mobile money revolution. *American Economic Review, 104*(1), 183-223.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Demirgüç-Kunt, A., Klapper, L., Singer, D., & Ansar, S. (2022). *The Global Findex Database 2021: Financial inclusion, digital payments, and resilience in the age of COVID-19*. World Bank.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Venkatesh, V., Morris, M. G., Davis, G. B., & Davis, F. D. (2003). User acceptance of information technology: Toward a unified view. *MIS Quarterly, 27*(3), 425-478.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Payment Systems and Services Act, 2019 (Act 987) (Ghana).","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Bank of Ghana","url":"https://www.bog.gov.gh/","description":"Bank of Ghana. Official source for payment systems regulation and licensed providers, essential for verifying project assumptions.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"GhIPSS","url":"https://ghipss.net/","description":"Ghana Interbank Payment and Settlement Systems. Information on interoperability, GhQR and national payment infrastructure.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"CGAP","url":"https://www.cgap.org/","description":"CGAP. Research and guidance on digital finance for small businesses and responsible inclusion.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Project C analyses the mobile money ecosystem through stakeholder mapping, value networks and PESTEL. Project D builds a phased SME strategy with objectives, KPIs with baselines and change management. Project E designs the payment solution: MoSCoW-prioritised functional and non-functional requirements, an architecture centred on a licensed aggregator, signed webhooks, a retailer ledger and daily reconciliation, proportionate controls and a business case with payback and sensitivity analysis. Each project has deliverables, evaluation criteria and risks.\n\n- Analyse, then strategise, then design.\n- KPIs need baselines and targets.\n- MoSCoW keeps scope realistic.\n- Payback = investment ÷ net monthly benefit; test sensitivity.\n- Keep cash as a transition fallback."}'::jsonb, 2311, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('7e397756-849e-5664-99b7-a53cecbbca5f', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', '31d0a2bd-60aa-5875-936f-94845aacbc75', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp01', 'short_answer', 'apply', 'medium', 'Calculate contribution margin per order', 'An order of GHS 180 has cost of goods of GHS 70, packaging of GHS 8, a delivery subsidy of GHS 15 and a 2% payment fee. What is the contribution margin before marketing, in GHS?', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('7e397756-849e-5664-99b7-a53cecbbca5f', '{"answers":["83.40","83.4","GHS 83.40","GHS 83.4"]}'::jsonb, 'The payment fee is 2% × 180 = 3.60, so contribution = 180 − 70 − 8 − 15 − 3.60 = 83.40. Counting payment fees and delivery subsidies is essential, because they can turn a healthy gross margin into a loss per order.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('4d05912a-434d-5524-b7e6-7fe015f43eb8', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', '31d0a2bd-60aa-5875-936f-94845aacbc75', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp02', 'mcq', 'apply', 'medium', 'Apply break-even analysis to unit economics', 'Each order contributes GHS 43.40 after acquisition costs, and monthly fixed costs are GHS 6,500. Approximately how many orders a month are needed to break even?', null, null, '[{"id":"a","text":"About 43"},{"id":"b","text":"About 150"},{"id":"c","text":"About 485"},{"id":"d","text":"About 6,500"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('4d05912a-434d-5524-b7e6-7fe015f43eb8', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'Break-even volume = fixed costs ÷ contribution per order = 6,500 ÷ 43.40 ≈ 150. About 485 would apply if contribution fell to GHS 13.40, which shows how sensitive the result is to acquisition cost. The other options misapply the formula.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('bed85c59-396d-5449-a578-b9995d95bfb8', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', '31d0a2bd-60aa-5875-936f-94845aacbc75', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp03', 'true_false', 'understand', 'easy', 'Identify the source of truth for payment status', 'In a well-designed online payment process, an order should be marked as paid when the customer''s browser is redirected to the success page.', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('bed85c59-396d-5449-a578-b9995d95bfb8', '{"value":false}'::jsonb, 'Redirect parameters can be altered or lost if the browser closes. The source of truth is a verified, signed PSP webhook or a server-side status query, checked against amount and reference.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('077fb7fc-7900-51f3-a486-2b9302f638dc', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', '31d0a2bd-60aa-5875-936f-94845aacbc75', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp04', 'matching', 'understand', 'medium', 'Match payment design features to the problems they solve', 'Match each payment design feature to the problem it addresses.', null, null, '{"left":[{"id":"l1","text":"Idempotency key"},{"id":"l2","text":"Hosted checkout fields"},{"id":"l3","text":"Handset PIN prompt"},{"id":"l4","text":"EXPIRED state with retry"}],"right":[{"id":"r4","text":"Payments that never receive a confirmation"},{"id":"r2","text":"Card data entering the merchant''s servers"},{"id":"r1","text":"Duplicate charges when a customer clicks pay twice"},{"id":"r3","text":"Customers revealing PINs to merchants"}]}'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('077fb7fc-7900-51f3-a486-2b9302f638dc', '{"pairs":{"l1":"r1","l2":"r2","l3":"r3","l4":"r4"}}'::jsonb, 'Each feature targets a specific failure mode. Linking design choices to the problems they solve is what turns a flow diagram into a robust design.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('f192aee8-e0d5-591a-add5-1280034cf1aa', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', '31d0a2bd-60aa-5875-936f-94845aacbc75', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp05', 'scenario', 'analyse', 'hard', 'Analyse a missing path in a payment state model', 'What is the most important improvement to the state model?', 'A student''s payment state model contains only CREATED, PENDING and PAID. During testing, a customer approves a mobile money prompt but the PSP reports that the customer''s wallet had insufficient funds, and another customer never responds to the prompt.', null, '[{"id":"a","text":"Remove the PENDING state to simplify the model"},{"id":"b","text":"Add FAILED and EXPIRED states with transitions that allow a retry"},{"id":"c","text":"Mark both orders as PAID and follow up by phone"},{"id":"d","text":"Add a FULFILLED state only"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('f192aee8-e0d5-591a-add5-1280034cf1aa', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'The model needs explicit states for failure and timeout, with defined transitions back to a retry, so that orders are neither left in limbo nor dispatched unpaid. Removing PENDING hides real states; marking unpaid orders as paid is a serious control failure; and FULFILLED alone does not handle the problem.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('6600e6b4-6521-537f-b733-58bc10b649b6', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', '31d0a2bd-60aa-5875-936f-94845aacbc75', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp06', 'mcq', 'evaluate', 'hard', 'Evaluate evidence quality in a business model report', 'Which approach to market assumptions best meets the evaluation criteria for Project A?', null, null, '[{"id":"a","text":"Quote precise national market-size figures from memory to make the report persuasive"},{"id":"b","text":"State assumptions explicitly, label them as estimates, support them with observation or credible sources and test their sensitivity"},{"id":"c","text":"Avoid numbers entirely so that no assumption can be challenged"},{"id":"d","text":"Use the most optimistic figures to show the model''s potential"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('6600e6b4-6521-537f-b733-58bc10b649b6', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'Good project reports make assumptions transparent and test them. Invented or unsourced statistics breach academic integrity, avoiding numbers prevents viability testing, and optimistic figures bias the conclusion.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('f52df204-918e-5047-a3b2-42a6b9e75b73', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', '31d0a2bd-60aa-5875-936f-94845aacbc75', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp07', 'short_answer', 'recall', 'easy', 'Recall the term for server-to-server payment notifications', 'What is the common term for the server-to-server notification a PSP sends to a merchant when a payment''s status changes?', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('f52df204-918e-5047-a3b2-42a6b9e75b73', '{"answers":["webhook","webhooks","callback","payment callback"]}'::jsonb, 'Webhooks, or callbacks, notify the merchant''s server of status changes. The merchant must verify their signature, amount and reference before acting on them.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('bbfc8e08-7773-54da-a5d3-4a523d7c5536', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', 'e14606a0-208d-5b40-8e8d-8672302b05c3', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp08', 'matching', 'apply', 'medium', 'Apply PESTEL categories to a Ghanaian payments context', 'Match each factor affecting a Ghanaian distributor''s payment strategy to its PESTEL category.', null, null, '{"left":[{"id":"l1","text":"Taxes or levies on electronic transfers"},{"id":"l2","text":"Inflation raising the cost of working capital"},{"id":"l3","text":"Retailers'' habit of paying in cash"},{"id":"l4","text":"Mobile money interoperability and GhQR"},{"id":"l5","text":"Data protection obligations under Act 843"}],"right":[{"id":"r2","text":"Economic"},{"id":"r5","text":"Legal"},{"id":"r1","text":"Political"},{"id":"r4","text":"Technological"},{"id":"r3","text":"Social"}]}'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('bbfc8e08-7773-54da-a5d3-4a523d7c5536', '{"pairs":{"l1":"r1","l2":"r2","l3":"r3","l4":"r4","l5":"r5"}}'::jsonb, 'Classifying factors is only the first step; a good analysis then explains the implications of each for the organisation. Tax policy can be treated as political or economic, but here it reflects government policy choices.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('9c7d0bdf-d0fe-52ce-9e85-ed3818198087', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', 'e14606a0-208d-5b40-8e8d-8672302b05c3', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp09', 'mcq', 'understand', 'easy', 'Distinguish functional from non-functional requirements', 'Which of the following is a non-functional requirement?', null, null, '[{"id":"a","text":"The system must send a payment request to the retailer''s wallet"},{"id":"b","text":"The system must tag each payment with an invoice number"},{"id":"c","text":"The system must be available during business hours, with a manual fallback during outages"},{"id":"d","text":"The system must produce a daily reconciliation report"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('9c7d0bdf-d0fe-52ce-9e85-ed3818198087', '{"option":"c"}'::jsonb, 'Availability describes how well the system performs, so it is non-functional. Sending payment requests, tagging payments and producing reports describe what the system does, so they are functional requirements.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('65817edf-fb64-54de-9ef3-bff1c84a120a', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', 'e14606a0-208d-5b40-8e8d-8672302b05c3', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp10', 'short_answer', 'apply', 'medium', 'Calculate a payback period', 'A payment solution costs GHS 18,000 and produces a net benefit of GHS 1,400 per month. To the nearest whole month, what is the payback period?', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('65817edf-fb64-54de-9ef3-bff1c84a120a', '{"answers":["13","13 months","about 13 months"]}'::jsonb, 'Payback = 18,000 ÷ 1,400 ≈ 12.9, about 13 months. Payback is simple to communicate but ignores benefits after the payback point, so it should be combined with sensitivity analysis.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('73f10928-cc0b-5b83-bbd1-33f42dc0095e', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', 'e14606a0-208d-5b40-8e8d-8672302b05c3', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp11', 'true_false', 'analyse', 'medium', 'Analyse the sensitivity of a business case', 'In the Nkabom business case, if benefits fall to 70% of the estimate while costs stay the same, the payback period roughly triples.', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('73f10928-cc0b-5b83-bbd1-33f42dc0095e', '{"value":true}'::jsonb, 'Benefits of GHS 2,100 less GHS 600 of subscriptions and GHS 1,000 of fees leave GHS 500 a month, so payback becomes 36 months instead of about 13. Because net benefit is a small difference between larger numbers, payback is highly sensitive to adoption.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('f10a52cc-4449-5b3a-85a8-3655639e7fbe', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', 'e14606a0-208d-5b40-8e8d-8672302b05c3', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp12', 'scenario', 'apply', 'medium', 'Apply MoSCoW prioritisation', 'Which requirement should be classified as a Must have?', 'Nkabom Provisions has a limited budget for its first two phases. Its core problems are cash losses on delivery routes, slow collections and disputes about retailer balances.', null, '[{"id":"a","text":"Cryptocurrency acceptance for international customers"},{"id":"b","text":"Tagging every payment with a retailer ID and invoice number"},{"id":"c","text":"An augmented-reality product catalogue"},{"id":"d","text":"A custom-built mobile wallet"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('f10a52cc-4449-5b3a-85a8-3655639e7fbe', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'Payment tagging directly addresses balance disputes and enables reconciliation, so it is essential. Cryptocurrency, AR catalogues and a custom wallet do not address the core problems and would be Won''t have in this phase.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('e8f45698-767e-5c03-a49d-a4a0ddbca380', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', 'e14606a0-208d-5b40-8e8d-8672302b05c3', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp13', 'mcq', 'evaluate', 'hard', 'Evaluate a change management approach', 'Which change approach is most likely to succeed for a distributor moving small retailers from cash to digital payment?', null, null, '[{"id":"a","text":"Stop accepting cash immediately so retailers have no choice"},{"id":"b","text":"Run a transition period with cash as a fallback, onboarding sessions, instant receipts and incentives for digital payers"},{"id":"c","text":"Change systems silently and inform retailers later"},{"id":"d","text":"Require all retailers to buy smartphones"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('e8f45698-767e-5c03-a49d-a4a0ddbca380', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'A phased transition builds trust, effort expectancy and habit, consistent with adoption theory. Abrupt removal of cash risks losing customers, silent changes damage trust, and requiring smartphones is unnecessary because prompts and USSD work on basic phones.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('2974f578-4225-550e-8a0c-7d82adada9fb', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', 'e14606a0-208d-5b40-8e8d-8672302b05c3', 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp14', 'true_false', 'evaluate', 'medium', 'Evaluate KPI design', 'A KPI such as "increase the share of collections received digitally" is fully specified once the target is written down, even without a measured baseline.', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('2974f578-4225-550e-8a0c-7d82adada9fb', '{"value":false}'::jsonb, 'Without a baseline, progress cannot be measured and the target cannot be judged realistic. A good KPI has a definition, a measured baseline, a target, a time frame and an owner.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('ca28261a-ae68-5368-9f90-1bb84cebc1bf', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', null, 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp15', 'case_analysis', 'evaluate', 'hard', 'Design and evaluate a digital payment solution for a Ghanaian SME', 'Using the case, produce a structured solution design outline and critically evaluate its business case and risks.', null, 'Illustrative scenario: Tamale Grains Hub is a hypothetical aggregator of maize and soya from about three hundred smallholder farmers in the Northern Region. It pays farmers in cash at collection points and sells to feed mills that pay by bank transfer. Cash payments require staff to travel with large sums, there have been disputes about weights and prices, and farmers have asked to be paid by mobile money. The business proposes a solution in which weights are recorded on a tablet at collection, farmers receive SMS receipts and are paid by bulk mobile money disbursement through a licensed PSP, and feed-mill receipts are reconciled automatically. Estimated investment is GHS 24,000, with net monthly benefits estimated at GHS 2,000.', null, 30, array['Analyse stakeholders and ecosystem factors', 'Specify prioritised functional and non-functional requirements', 'Outline architecture, controls and reconciliation', 'Evaluate the business case with payback and sensitivity', 'Identify risks and a phased implementation plan']::text[], '[{"criterion":"Stakeholder and ecosystem analysis","marks":5,"descriptor":"Identifies farmers, staff, feed mills, PSP, e-money issuers, agents for cash-out and the regulator, and relevant ecosystem factors such as agent liquidity in rural areas."},{"criterion":"Requirements quality","marks":6,"descriptor":"Testable, MoSCoW-prioritised requirements including non-functional needs such as offline capture and usability."},{"criterion":"Architecture and controls","marks":7,"descriptor":"Sound design with segregation of duties, approval of disbursement batches, audit trails, verified callbacks and reconciliation."},{"criterion":"Business case evaluation","marks":7,"descriptor":"Correctly calculates payback of 12 months, tests sensitivity and recognises unquantified benefits and costs, including farmers'' cash-out fees."},{"criterion":"Risks and implementation","marks":5,"descriptor":"Realistic phased plan with risks such as connectivity, agent cash shortages and fraud, noting that the analysis is educational."}]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('ca28261a-ae68-5368-9f90-1bb84cebc1bf', '{}'::jsonb, 'Examiners look for traceability from problem to requirements and design, proportionate controls, a correct and critically tested business case, attention to farmers'' interests and a realistic phased plan.', 'A strong answer begins with stakeholders: farmers, who value prompt, transparent payment; collection staff; feed mills; the PSP and e-money issuers; rural agents who must supply cash when farmers withdraw; and the Bank of Ghana as regulator. Ecosystem factors include patchy connectivity, agent liquidity in rural areas and farmers'' trust in digital payments.

Must-have requirements include recording weight, grade and agreed price per delivery, linked to a farmer ID verified at onboarding; instant SMS receipts; bulk disbursement through a licensed PSP; and reconciliation of disbursements and feed-mill receipts. Non-functional requirements include offline data capture with later synchronisation, simple interfaces for staff, role-based access and audit logs. Card acceptance or farmer credit could be later options.

The architecture links the tablet app to a central ledger, which prepares disbursement batches. Controls should separate recording from approval, require a second person to approve each batch, verify PSP callbacks and reconcile daily against PSP and bank reports. Disputes about weights are reduced by printed or SMS receipts showing weight and price.

The payback period is 24,000 ÷ 2,000 = 12 months. Sensitivity analysis should test lower benefits, for example if half the farmers prefer cash, and higher fees. The answer should note who pays the cash-out fee: if farmers bear it, their real payment falls, which may harm adoption and fairness, so the business might absorb it. Unquantified benefits include staff safety and better data on farmer supply.

Implementation should begin with a pilot at one collection point, keep cash as a fallback, check agent liquidity near collection points, and be reviewed before rollout.')
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('c2fd70c1-ad99-55f7-992c-7b55864f12ce', '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', null, 'csit-613/practical-projects/pp16', 'essay', 'evaluate', 'hard', 'Evaluate the relationship between business model design and payment process design', '"A business model is only as strong as the payment process that supports it." Critically discuss this statement with reference to a Ghanaian SME e-commerce context.', null, null, null, 20, array['Explain business model components and unit economics', 'Explain how payment design affects conversion, cost, trust and cash flow', 'Analyse examples where payment design undermines or strengthens a model', 'Consider factors beyond payments such as logistics and product quality', 'Reach a balanced conclusion']::text[], '[{"criterion":"Understanding of business models and payments","marks":5,"descriptor":"Accurately explains the canvas, unit economics and payment process elements."},{"criterion":"Critical analysis","marks":7,"descriptor":"Shows how payment choices affect revenue, costs, trust and risk, while recognising other success factors."},{"criterion":"Ghanaian context and examples","marks":5,"descriptor":"Uses relevant local factors such as multi-network mobile money, COD and delivery."},{"criterion":"Argument and conclusion","marks":3,"descriptor":"Coherent argument with a justified conclusion."}]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('c2fd70c1-ad99-55f7-992c-7b55864f12ce', '{}'::jsonb, 'Examiners reward essays that link payment design explicitly to business model components and unit economics, use Ghanaian context, and challenge the statement rather than simply agreeing.', 'The statement captures an important dependency. A business model specifies how value is created, delivered and captured, and the payment process is where value capture actually happens. Payment design affects conversion (customers abandon checkouts that do not support their wallet or are confusing), costs (PSP fees and failed-payment handling enter unit economics), trust (instant receipts and buyer protection reduce perceived risk) and cash flow (settlement speed determines working capital).

In Ghana, an SME that accepts only one mobile money network or relies on personal wallets may lose customers, struggle to reconcile and face disputes. Conversely, an aggregator that supports all networks and cards, with merchant-initiated PIN prompts and verified confirmations, can increase conversion and reduce fraud. COD illustrates the link clearly: it supports trust but shifts failed-delivery and cash-handling costs to the seller, changing the model''s economics.

However, the statement overstates the case. A flawless payment process cannot rescue a model with weak demand, poor product quality, unreliable delivery or acquisition costs that exceed contribution. Logistics, addressing and customer service are equally important in Ghanaian e-commerce.

A balanced conclusion is that payment design is a necessary but not sufficient condition: a weak payment process can undermine a strong model, but a strong payment process cannot compensate for a weak value proposition or poor unit economics.')
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('a799a344-a713-5e5c-892f-9c7115919867', null, '289f40af-c81b-540f-85f0-62d76d5630d3', '4a740486-82fe-5753-a2f8-0ee2b8bf934a', '31d0a2bd-60aa-5875-936f-94845aacbc75', 'What do unit economics test in an e-commerce business model?', 'Whether each order or customer creates value after variable costs such as cost of goods, packaging, delivery, payment fees and acquisition cost.', 'supplementary', 'csit-613/practical-projects/3f524e5e39bd', true)
