-- CSIT 617 Professional & Ethical Issues in IT
insert into public.courses (id, code, slug, title, description, status, status_note, classification, credit_slots, accent, outcomes, sort_order, source, is_published) values ('ebd2bdb9-0aee-5267-8b90-6a20cc7690c9', 'CSIT 617', 'csit-617', 'Professional & Ethical Issues in IT', 'The professional responsibilities of IT practitioners and the ethical, social and legal questions raised by information technology. Students learn ethical theories, apply structured ethical reasoning and produce defensible recommendations on privacy, intellectual property, AI, workplace conduct and cybercrime. Legal content is educational and is not legal advice.', 'listed', 'Core course in the MSc IT for Business Semester 1 programme (3 credit slots).', 'core', 3, 'amber', array['Explain the meaning of professionalism, accountability and competence in IT.', 'Apply major ethical theories and a structured decision-making process to IT dilemmas.', 'Analyse the social implications of technology, including digital inclusion and digital rights.', 'Evaluate privacy, data protection and intellectual property issues in organisational contexts.', 'Critically assess the ethics of AI systems and recommend safeguards.', 'Construct well-reasoned, defensible recommendations for realistic ethical case studies.']::text[], 7, 'supplementary', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set code = excluded.code, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, status = excluded.status, status_note = excluded.status_note, classification = excluded.classification, credit_slots = excluded.credit_slots, accent = excluded.accent, outcomes = excluded.outcomes, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.course_modules (id, course_id, slug, title, description, sort_order, is_published) values ('5d4c0926-b05f-53e3-9de4-3501fc9f4a7c', 'ebd2bdb9-0aee-5267-8b90-6a20cc7690c9', 'it-professionalism', 'IT Professionalism', 'What it means to be a professional in information technology.', 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('a262c8aa-1226-5bfe-a151-d5e42a5255d5', '5d4c0926-b05f-53e3-9de4-3501fc9f4a7c', 'professional-identity-and-responsibility', 'Professional Identity, Responsibility and Accountability', 'Explain what makes IT work a profession and distinguish responsibility, accountability and liability when analysing who should answer for the harms caused by information systems.', array['Professional identity', 'Professional responsibility', 'Accountability']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Information technology now sits inside almost every consequential decision an organisation makes: who receives a loan, which patient is prioritised, how a salary is calculated, whether a mobile money transfer succeeds. The people who design, build, configure and operate these systems therefore exercise real power over other people''s lives, often without those people ever knowing who was involved. That power raises a simple but demanding question: *what does it mean to be a professional in IT, and what do IT professionals owe to the people affected by their work?*\n\nThis lesson examines the idea of a profession, asks whether computing satisfies the traditional criteria, and then develops three concepts that are frequently confused in both everyday speech and examination answers: **responsibility**, **accountability** and **liability**. You will learn why computerised systems make it unusually easy for nobody to answer for harm, and how professionals and organisations can design accountability back in. These ideas underpin every later module of the course, from privacy and intellectual property to AI and whistleblowing.","objectives":["Explain the characteristics traditionally used to distinguish a profession from an occupation.","Evaluate the extent to which IT work in Ghana and internationally satisfies those characteristics.","Distinguish role responsibility, causal responsibility, moral responsibility, accountability and legal liability.","Analyse Nissenbaum''s barriers to accountability in computerised systems using a realistic case.","Apply the concepts to recommend practical accountability mechanisms for an IT project."],"prerequisites":["None beyond general familiarity with how organisations develop and operate information systems.","Helpful but not essential: some experience of working in, or with, an IT department or software project."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Profession","definition":"An occupation whose members apply specialised, theory-based knowledge in the service of clients and the public, typically governed by a professional body, a code of ethics and recognised standards of entry."},{"term":"Professional identity","definition":"A practitioner''s sense of themselves as a member of a profession, including the values, obligations and standards they accept as part of that role."},{"term":"Role responsibility","definition":"The duties attached to a particular position, such as a database administrator''s duty to maintain backups."},{"term":"Causal responsibility","definition":"Being part of the chain of events that brought about an outcome, whether or not one is blameworthy."},{"term":"Moral responsibility","definition":"Being properly subject to praise or blame for an outcome because one acted (or failed to act) knowingly and freely."},{"term":"Accountability","definition":"The obligation to explain and justify one''s conduct to a legitimate forum and to accept the consequences of that forum''s judgement."},{"term":"Liability","definition":"A legal concept: the obligation, imposed by law, to compensate for harm or to bear a penalty, which may exist with or without moral fault (strict liability)."},{"term":"Problem of many hands","definition":"The difficulty of attributing responsibility when an outcome results from the contributions of many people, none of whom individually caused it."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### What is a profession?\n\nSociologists and philosophers have long tried to identify what separates professions such as medicine, law and engineering from other occupations. No single definition is universally accepted, but most accounts converge on a cluster of characteristics:\n\n1. **A specialised body of knowledge** grounded in theory rather than only routine skill, acquired through extended formal education.\n2. **Controlled entry**, through examinations, accredited degrees, certification or licensing.\n3. **A professional association** that sets standards, represents members and can discipline them.\n4. **A code of ethics** that commits members to standards beyond what the law or the market alone would require.\n5. **Autonomy and judgement**: practitioners exercise discretion that clients cannot easily evaluate for themselves.\n6. **A public-service orientation**: the profession claims to serve a social good, such as health, justice or safety, and in return society grants it trust and a degree of self-regulation.\n\nThe last two points are central to the ethics of professions. Because clients cannot readily judge whether a surgeon or an engineer has done good work, they must *trust* the practitioner. Professional ethics exists largely to make that trust reasonable. The philosopher Michael Davis argued that a code of ethics is best understood as a convention among members of a profession, an agreement that each will serve the public ideal even when it is personally costly, so that the profession as a whole deserves the public''s trust.\n\n### Is IT a profession?\n\nComputing is an interesting borderline case. It clearly possesses specialised theoretical knowledge, and it has international professional associations: the Association for Computing Machinery (ACM), the IEEE Computer Society and the British Computer Society (BCS), each with a code of conduct. Many practitioners hold degrees and vendor or professional certifications.\n\nHowever, several features weaken the comparison with medicine or law:\n\n- **Entry is largely uncontrolled.** In most countries, including Ghana, anyone may call themselves a software developer, systems analyst or IT consultant without a licence. Many highly skilled practitioners are self-taught.\n- **Membership of a professional body is usually voluntary**, so codes of ethics bind only those who choose to join, and expulsion rarely ends a career.\n- **Practitioners are often employees**, not independent advisers, so their judgement can be overridden by managers whose priorities are commercial.\n- **The field changes quickly**, making it harder to define a stable body of required knowledge.\n\nThere are signs of movement towards stronger professionalisation in specific areas. In Ghana, the Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038) gives the Cyber Security Authority a role in licensing cybersecurity service providers and accrediting cybersecurity professionals, a step towards controlled entry in one specialism. Regulated sectors, such as banking under the Bank of Ghana or telecommunications under the National Communications Authority, also impose obligations on the organisations that employ IT staff. A balanced conclusion is that IT is an **emerging or partial profession**: it has many professional attributes, and individual practitioners can and should act as professionals, even where the law does not require it.\n\n### Why professional identity matters\n\nProfessional identity is the internalised answer to the question \"what kind of practitioner am I?\" A developer who sees themselves merely as someone who converts tickets into code may feel no responsibility for how the code is used. A developer who sees themselves as a professional accepts obligations to the public, to clients and employers, to colleagues and to the profession itself. Professional identity shapes behaviour precisely in the grey areas where no rule applies and nobody is watching: whether to report a security weakness, whether to admit that an estimate was unrealistic, whether to refuse a task one is not competent to perform."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Four senses of responsibility\n\nThe word \"responsible\" carries several meanings, and careful ethical analysis requires separating them.\n\n| Concept | Core question | IT example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Role responsibility | What duties come with this position? | The release manager must ensure regression tests pass before deployment. |\n| Causal responsibility | Did this person''s action or omission contribute to the outcome? | A configuration change made by an engineer caused the outage. |\n| Moral responsibility (blameworthiness) | Did the person act knowingly, freely and without adequate excuse? | The engineer knew the change was untested and deployed it anyway to meet a deadline. |\n| Accountability | Who must explain and justify what happened, and to whom? | The head of IT must report to the board and the regulator. |\n| Liability | Who must legally compensate or be penalised? | The company may be liable to customers under contract or statute. |\n\nThese concepts can come apart. A junior developer may be *causally* responsible for a bug but not *morally* blameworthy if they followed reasonable procedures and the defect was not foreseeable. A chief information officer may be *accountable* for a breach they did not personally cause, because accountability attaches to their role. A company may be *legally liable* even when no individual was negligent. Conversely, an individual can be morally blameworthy without facing any legal liability at all.\n\n### Forward-looking and backward-looking responsibility\n\nBackward-looking responsibility asks who is to blame for something that has already happened. Forward-looking responsibility asks who should ensure that something good happens, or something bad does not, in future. Professional ethics emphasises the forward-looking sense: a professional is not only someone who accepts blame after a failure, but someone who takes ownership of preventing foreseeable harm. Deborah Johnson''s work on computer ethics stresses that responsibility in IT is often best understood as this kind of care for outcomes, rather than mere avoidance of blame.\n\n### Nissenbaum''s barriers to accountability\n\nIn an influential 1996 article, Helen Nissenbaum argued that computerised society systematically erodes accountability. She identified four barriers:\n\n1. **The problem of many hands.** Systems are built by large teams, using third-party libraries and services, and operated by yet more people. When harm occurs, each contributor can point to someone else.\n2. **Bugs.** Because software is known to contain defects, errors are often treated as an unavoidable natural hazard rather than as the result of choices about testing, design and resourcing.\n3. **The computer as scapegoat.** People blame \"the system\" for outcomes that were in fact determined by human decisions about how the system was designed or used.\n4. **Ownership without liability.** Software producers often claim strong property rights over their products while disclaiming responsibility for failures through licence terms.\n\nNissenbaum''s argument is not that accountability is impossible, but that it must be deliberately designed into the organisations and contracts that produce software."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### The Therac-25 radiation therapy accidents (1985-1987)\n\nThe Therac-25 was a computer-controlled radiation therapy machine. Between 1985 and 1987 it delivered massive radiation overdoses to several patients in North America, some of whom died or were seriously injured. The investigation by Nancy Leveson and Clark Turner, published in 1993, is one of the most studied cases in software engineering ethics.\n\nKey documented features of the case include:\n\n- Earlier models had **hardware safety interlocks**; the Therac-25 relied much more heavily on software for safety.\n- The software contained **race conditions**: under certain sequences of fast operator input, the machine could be configured incorrectly without the software detecting it.\n- Error messages were **cryptic** and frequent, so operators learned to override them.\n- When early incidents were reported, the manufacturer initially **doubted that the machine could be responsible**, delaying effective action.\n\nThe case illustrates all of Nissenbaum''s barriers. Many hands were involved (programmers, the manufacturer, hospitals, operators). Bugs were treated as improbable rather than investigated systematically. Staff and the company tended to look elsewhere for the cause. The lesson for IT professionals is that safety and accountability cannot be delegated to the software itself: they depend on design choices, honest incident investigation and a willingness to accept that one''s own system may be at fault.\n\n### A more recent example: the UK Post Office Horizon scandal\n\nBetween 1999 and 2015, many sub-postmasters in the United Kingdom were prosecuted for theft, fraud or false accounting on the basis of shortfalls shown by the Horizon accounting system, supplied by Fujitsu. In 2019, a High Court judgment in the group litigation *Bates v Post Office* found that Horizon contained bugs, errors and defects capable of causing shortfalls. Convictions were subsequently overturned in large numbers and a statutory public inquiry was established. The case is a powerful illustration of the \"computer as scapegoat\" barrier in reverse: the presumption that the computer was right was used against individuals, and the professionals who understood the system''s weaknesses did not ensure that this knowledge reached the courts."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"### Illustrative scenario: a payroll failure at a district assembly contractor\n\n*Illustrative scenario.* Kumasi PayServe Ltd, a fictional software company, supplies payroll software to several public and private employers. After an update, a rounding error in the tax module under-deducts income tax for some staff for three months. When the error is discovered, employees face large recovery deductions and the client faces penalties.\n\nAnalysing the case with the concepts above:\n\n- **Causal responsibility** lies with the developer who wrote the rounding logic, the tester who did not test boundary values, and the release manager who approved the update.\n- **Moral responsibility** depends on what each knew. If the test plan was cut because the managing director insisted on an early release, a significant share of blame shifts to management.\n- **Accountability** rests with PayServe''s leadership, who must explain to the client what happened, and with the client''s finance director, who is accountable to staff and the tax authority.\n- **Liability** is a matter for the contract and Ghanaian law; the licence agreement may attempt to limit PayServe''s liability, an example of \"ownership without liability\". This analysis is educational, not legal advice.\n\nA professional response would include transparent disclosure to the client, a root-cause analysis that looks beyond the individual developer, compensation or remediation proposals, and process changes such as mandatory boundary-value tests for financial calculations."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Harm occurs (e.g. payroll error)\"] --> B{\"Who contributed causally?\"}\n  B --> C[\"Developers, testers, managers, vendors\"]\n  C --> D{\"Did they act knowingly and freely?\"}\n  D -- Yes --> E[\"Moral responsibility (blame)\"]\n  D -- No --> F[\"Causal role only; examine process\"]\n  A --> G{\"Who must explain and justify?\"}\n  G --> H[\"Accountability: role holders report to a forum\"]\n  A --> I{\"What does law and contract require?\"}\n  I --> J[\"Liability: compensation or penalty\"]\n  E --> K[\"Forward-looking responsibility: prevent recurrence\"]\n  F --> K\n  H --> K\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**\"Accountability and responsibility mean the same thing.\"** Responsibility concerns a person''s role or contribution to an outcome; accountability is the obligation to explain and answer for it to a forum. A manager can be accountable without being causally responsible.","**\"If something is legal, a professional has done nothing wrong.\"** Liability is a legal concept; moral responsibility can exist where the law imposes no liability, and professional codes deliberately set standards higher than the law.","**\"Software bugs are nobody''s fault.\"** Some defects are unforeseeable, but decisions about testing, review and resourcing are human choices that can be evaluated morally.","**\"IT cannot be a profession because there is no licence.\"** Licensing is only one characteristic. Individuals can adopt professional standards, and some specialisms, such as cybersecurity in Ghana, are moving towards formal accreditation.","**\"The person who wrote the faulty line of code is the one to blame.\"** The problem of many hands means blame must be assessed across designers, managers, vendors and organisational processes."],"examMistakes":["Listing characteristics of a profession without evaluating whether IT actually satisfies them.","Using \"responsible\", \"accountable\" and \"liable\" interchangeably in a case analysis.","Treating legal liability as the end of the ethical analysis.","Describing the Therac-25 or Horizon case at length without drawing the professional lessons.","Assigning all blame to a single individual without considering organisational and managerial factors.","Offering vague recommendations (\"be more careful\") instead of concrete accountability mechanisms."],"criticalQuestions":["Should IT practitioners in Ghana be subject to compulsory licensing, as doctors and lawyers are? Identify the benefits, the risks and the specialisms where licensing would be most justified.","Is it fair to hold a chief information officer accountable for a breach caused by a contractor''s employee they never met? What does your answer imply about the meaning of accountability?","Which of Nissenbaum''s four barriers is most significant in modern cloud and AI systems, where organisations depend on services they do not control?","Can an employee who follows instructions from a manager still be morally responsible for harm? Under what conditions?","To what extent does a software licence that disclaims all liability conflict with the idea of professional responsibility?"],"exercises":["**Responsibility mapping.** Choose an information system failure reported in the news or one you have experienced. Produce a table listing each party involved and classify their role responsibility, causal responsibility, likely moral responsibility, accountability and possible liability. *Expected output:* a five-column table with a one-sentence justification for each cell and a paragraph identifying where responsibility was most diffused.","**Profession audit.** Assess IT work in Ghana against the six characteristics of a profession. *Expected output:* a 400-word evaluation that rates each characteristic as strong, partial or weak, with reasons, and a concluding judgement.","**Designing accountability.** For the Kumasi PayServe illustrative scenario, propose five mechanisms (for example, code review sign-off, incident reporting, contractual clauses) that would counter Nissenbaum''s barriers. *Expected output:* a list mapping each mechanism to the barrier it addresses."],"takeaways":["A profession combines specialised knowledge, controlled entry, a professional body, a code of ethics, autonomy and a public-service commitment; IT satisfies these only partially.","Professional identity matters most in grey areas where no rule applies and the practitioner''s own standards determine behaviour.","Responsibility (role, causal, moral), accountability and liability are distinct concepts that must be separated in analysis.","Computerised systems create barriers to accountability: many hands, bugs, the computer as scapegoat and ownership without liability.","Professionals should embrace forward-looking responsibility, designing processes that prevent harm rather than only allocating blame afterwards."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Davis, M. (1991). Thinking like an engineer: The place of a code of ethics in the practice of a profession. *Philosophy & Public Affairs, 20*(2), 150-167.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Johnson, D. G. (2009). *Computer ethics* (4th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Leveson, N. G., & Turner, C. S. (1993). An investigation of the Therac-25 accidents. *Computer, 26*(7), 18-41.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Nissenbaum, H. (1996). Accountability in a computerized society. *Science and Engineering Ethics, 2*(1), 25-42.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Quinn, M. J. (2020). *Ethics for the information age* (8th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy. Computer and information ethics.","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-computer/","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"Markkula Center for Applied Ethics","url":"https://www.scu.edu/ethics/","description":"Santa Clara University. Official portal with articles, cases and short videos on technology and professional ethics.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"ACM Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.acm.org/code-of-ethics","description":"Association for Computing Machinery. Official page for the ACM Code, including supporting material on applying it in practice.","verification":"unverified"}],"revisionSummary":"IT is best described as a partial or emerging profession: it has specialised knowledge, professional bodies and codes of ethics, but entry is largely uncontrolled and practitioners are often employees. Professional identity governs conduct in grey areas. Keep the concepts distinct: responsibility concerns roles and contributions, accountability concerns answering to a forum, and liability is a legal obligation. Computerised systems erode accountability unless it is deliberately designed in.\n\n- Six characteristics of a profession: knowledge, entry control, association, code, autonomy, public service.\n- Role, causal and moral responsibility differ; moral responsibility requires knowledge and freedom.\n- Accountability attaches to roles; liability is imposed by law and may be strict.\n- Nissenbaum''s barriers: many hands, bugs, computer as scapegoat, ownership without liability.\n- Therac-25 and Horizon show the cost of assuming the system is right.\n- Recommend concrete mechanisms, not just individual blame."}'::jsonb, 2977, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('8ef871bc-40d1-5fa6-b484-68bda8d38b26', '5d4c0926-b05f-53e3-9de4-3501fc9f4a7c', 'codes-of-conduct-and-competence', 'Codes of Conduct and Professional Competence', 'Explain the purpose and structure of the ACM and IEEE codes of ethics, apply them to IT dilemmas and evaluate how competence and continuing professional development sustain professional trust.', array['Codes of conduct', 'ACM Code of Ethics', 'IEEE Code of Ethics', 'Professional competence']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"When an IT professional faces a difficult choice, such as whether to ship software with a known weakness or whether to accept a project beyond their expertise, they rarely have time to reconstruct moral philosophy from first principles. Professional codes of ethics exist to help. They condense the collective experience of a profession into a set of commitments that members can use to guide their judgement, justify their decisions to employers and hold one another to account.\n\nCodes are often misunderstood, however. Some students treat them as legal rulebooks, others as public-relations documents with no practical force. This lesson examines what codes are for, summarises the structure and core commitments of the two most widely referenced computing codes, the ACM Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct (2018) and the IEEE Code of Ethics, and then explores professional competence and continuing professional development (CPD), the commitment that underpins all others. Throughout, the codes are summarised in original words; you should read the full official texts, which are freely available online.","objectives":["Explain the functions and limitations of professional codes of ethics.","Describe the structure and principal commitments of the ACM Code (2018) and the IEEE Code of Ethics.","Compare the ACM and IEEE codes and identify where they converge and differ in emphasis.","Apply relevant code principles to justify a recommendation in an IT dilemma.","Evaluate professional competence and CPD as ethical obligations rather than merely career strategies."],"prerequisites":["The characteristics of a profession and the distinction between responsibility, accountability and liability (previous lesson).","General familiarity with software development and IT operations roles."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Code of ethics","definition":"A statement of the values and principles a profession expects its members to uphold, usually framed as aspirations and obligations."},{"term":"Code of conduct","definition":"A more specific set of rules of behaviour, often enforceable through a disciplinary process; the terms are frequently used interchangeably."},{"term":"Public interest","definition":"The welfare of the public as a whole, which professional codes typically place above the interests of employers, clients or the practitioner."},{"term":"Professional competence","definition":"The combination of knowledge, skill, judgement and awareness of one''s limits required to perform professional work to an acceptable standard."},{"term":"Continuing professional development (CPD)","definition":"Planned, ongoing learning that maintains and extends a professional''s competence throughout their career."},{"term":"Conflict of interest","definition":"A situation in which a professional''s personal interests could improperly influence their professional judgement."},{"term":"Paramountcy clause","definition":"A provision stating that one value, usually public safety and welfare, overrides others when they conflict."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### What codes are for\n\nProfessional codes serve several functions at once:\n\n1. **Guidance.** They identify the considerations a competent professional should weigh, especially in unfamiliar situations.\n2. **Support.** A practitioner who refuses an unethical instruction can point to the code: \"my professional obligations do not permit this\". This gives individuals leverage against organisational pressure.\n3. **Education.** Codes introduce new members to the values of the profession.\n4. **Discipline.** Professional bodies can use codes as the basis for sanctioning members who breach them.\n5. **Public trust.** A published code tells the public what it may expect from members.\n\nCodes also have limitations. They are often **abstract**, so applying them requires judgement. Principles can **conflict**, for example confidentiality to an employer against honesty to the public. Enforcement is **weak** where membership is voluntary. And a code can never anticipate every technology; in James Moor''s terms, new technologies create *policy vacuums* that codes may not yet address. A code is therefore a starting point for ethical reasoning, not a substitute for it.\n\n### The ACM Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct (2018)\n\nThe ACM, the world''s largest computing society, updated its code in 2018 to address issues such as ubiquitous computing, data and security. The 2018 code is organised into four parts. The summary below paraphrases the commitments; consult the official text for exact wording and the explanatory guidance attached to each principle.\n\n**Part 1: General ethical principles.** Computing professionals should:\n\n- contribute to society and human well-being, recognising that all people are stakeholders in computing;\n- avoid harm, including unintended harm, and take care to minimise negative consequences;\n- be honest and trustworthy, including being transparent about capabilities and limitations of systems;\n- be fair and act to avoid discrimination;\n- respect the effort that goes into new ideas, inventions, creative works and computing artefacts;\n- respect privacy;\n- honour confidentiality.\n\n**Part 2: Professional responsibilities.** Computing professionals should strive for high quality in both process and product; maintain high standards of competence, conduct and ethical practice; know and respect the rules that apply to their work; give and accept professional review; carry out thorough evaluations of systems and their impacts, including risks; work only in areas where they are competent; foster public understanding of computing; access computing resources only when authorised or when compelled by the public good; and design and implement systems that are robustly and usably secure.\n\n**Part 3: Professional leadership principles.** People in leadership roles carry additional obligations: keeping the public good central to computing work, articulating and supporting the social responsibilities of their organisations, managing people and resources to enhance the quality of working life, creating opportunities for others to grow professionally, taking care when modifying or retiring systems that people depend on, and giving special attention to systems that become part of the infrastructure of society.\n\n**Part 4: Compliance with the code.** Members commit to uphold, promote and respect the code and recognise that serious violation is inconsistent with membership.\n\nA notable feature of the 2018 code is its explicit statement that the public good is the paramount consideration, and its recognition that harm can arise from systems that work exactly as designed if the design itself is harmful.\n\n### The IEEE Code of Ethics\n\nThe IEEE, a global association for electrical, electronic and computing engineers, maintains a shorter code within its governing policies. In its current form, the code groups its commitments into three broad parts. Paraphrased, members commit:\n\n- **To uphold high standards of integrity, responsible behaviour and ethical conduct**: placing the safety, health and welfare of the public first; striving to comply with ethical design and sustainable development practices; protecting the privacy of others; promptly disclosing factors that might endanger the public or the environment; improving public understanding of technology and its consequences; avoiding real or perceived conflicts of interest and disclosing them when unavoidable; avoiding unlawful conduct and rejecting bribery; seeking, accepting and offering honest criticism of technical work and correcting errors; being honest and realistic in claims and estimates; maintaining and improving technical competence and undertaking technological tasks only when qualified by training or experience, or after full disclosure of limitations; and properly crediting the contributions of others.\n- **To treat all persons fairly and with respect**: not engaging in harassment or discrimination on grounds such as race, religion, gender, disability, age or national origin.\n- **To strive to ensure the code is upheld by colleagues**: supporting colleagues in following the code and not retaliating against those who report violations.\n\n### Comparing the two codes\n\n| Dimension | ACM Code (2018) | IEEE Code of Ethics |\n|---|---|---|\n| Length and style | Longer, with explanatory guidance for each principle | Short list of commitments |\n| Paramount value | Public good and well-being of all stakeholders | Safety, health and welfare of the public |\n| Privacy and confidentiality | Separate principles for privacy and for confidentiality | Protection of others'' privacy |\n| Security | Explicit duty to build robustly and usably secure systems | Implied through public safety and disclosure of dangers |\n| Leadership | Dedicated section on leadership obligations | Not separately addressed |\n| Competence | Work only within competence; maintain high standards | Maintain competence; undertake tasks only when qualified or after disclosure |\n| Colleagues and reporting | Professional review; compliance section | Support colleagues; no retaliation against reporters |\n\nThe codes converge strongly: both put the public first, both require honesty and competence, both address discrimination and privacy. The ACM code is more detailed about computing-specific issues such as security and the retirement of systems, while the IEEE code is broader, reflecting its engineering membership, and gives explicit attention to sustainable development and the environment.\n\nOther codes are worth knowing. The BCS Code of Conduct, used by the British Computer Society, organises obligations around the public interest, professional competence and integrity, duty to relevant authority (the employer or client) and duty to the profession. Many organisations also publish internal codes, which typically emphasise compliance and confidentiality more than public welfare."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Competence as an ethical obligation\n\nCompetence is sometimes treated as a purely technical matter, but every major code frames it as an ethical duty. The reasoning is straightforward: clients and the public rely on professionals precisely because they cannot evaluate the work themselves. A practitioner who accepts work they are not competent to perform exploits that trust, even if they mean well.\n\nProfessional competence has several dimensions:\n\n1. **Knowledge**: understanding of relevant theory, technologies, standards and law.\n2. **Skill**: the ability to apply knowledge reliably in practice.\n3. **Judgement**: the capacity to decide what to do in situations that rules do not cover.\n4. **Awareness of limits**: knowing what one does *not* know, and being honest about it.\n5. **Currency**: keeping all of the above up to date as technology changes.\n\nThe fourth dimension is the most ethically significant. Incompetence is not itself a moral failing, since everyone begins as a novice, but concealing one''s limitations is. Both codes allow a professional to undertake unfamiliar work *after full disclosure* and with appropriate support; what they prohibit is pretending.\n\n### Continuing professional development\n\nBecause IT changes quickly, competence decays unless renewed. CPD is the structured response. A practical CPD cycle has four stages:\n\n1. **Reflect** on current role, emerging technologies and gaps in competence.\n2. **Plan** learning activities: courses, certifications, reading, conferences, mentoring or project rotation.\n3. **Act** by completing the activities.\n4. **Evaluate** the impact on practice and record it, then repeat.\n\nProfessional bodies such as the BCS and the IEEE encourage members to keep CPD records, and some certifications require evidence of continuing education for renewal. For an MSc student, the programme itself is a significant CPD activity, but competence also requires deliberate practice in the workplace."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Applying the codes to a dilemma\n\n*Illustrative scenario.* Abena is a database developer at Coastline Microfinance, a fictional lender in Takoradi. Her manager asks her to set up the company''s first cloud data warehouse within two weeks for a board demonstration. Abena has never configured cloud security controls. She suspects that if she rushes, customer data, including national ID numbers and loan histories, could be exposed.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the relevant principles.**\n\n- ACM: avoid harm; work only in areas of competence; design robustly and usably secure systems; respect privacy; be honest about limitations.\n- IEEE: hold public welfare first; protect privacy; undertake tasks only when qualified or after full disclosure of limitations; be realistic in estimates.\n\n**Step 2: Identify the conflict.** Loyalty to her employer and career interest push towards accepting the deadline; competence, privacy and honesty push towards caution.\n\n**Step 3: Consider options.**\n\n| Option | Assessment against the codes |\n|---|---|\n| Accept silently and do her best | Breaches competence and honesty obligations; risks harm to customers |\n| Refuse outright | Protects customers but fails to help the employer find a solution |\n| Disclose limitations and propose a plan | Satisfies honesty and competence; offers a constructive path |\n\n**Step 4: Recommend.** Abena should disclose her lack of cloud security experience in writing, propose either a demonstration using synthetic or anonymised data, or bringing in a qualified security specialist, and set a realistic timeline for the production build. This is the option the codes most clearly support because it combines honesty, competence and service to the employer.\n\n**Step 5: Plan CPD.** Abena should also plan targeted learning, such as a recognised cloud security course, so that she can take on such work competently in future."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"In Ghana, adherence to international codes is voluntary unless an employer or certification body requires it, but the codes align closely with obligations that do exist in law and regulation. For example, the duty to respect privacy and confidentiality aligns with the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843); the duty to build secure systems aligns with expectations under the Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038) and sector regulators'' directives. Ghanaian employers increasingly ask for internationally recognised certifications, which often carry their own codes of conduct and CPD requirements.\n\nA practical recommendation for Ghanaian organisations is to **adopt an internal IT code of conduct** that references the ACM or IEEE principles, to build CPD into performance appraisal and to create a safe channel through which staff can raise concerns that a task exceeds their competence or creates risk to customers. These measures turn abstract codes into everyday organisational practice."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### The Volkswagen emissions software case\n\nIn 2015, the United States Environmental Protection Agency announced that Volkswagen had installed software in certain diesel vehicles that detected when the car was undergoing emissions testing and altered engine performance to pass, while emitting far more nitrogen oxides on the road. The company admitted the use of the \"defeat device\", paid very large penalties and faced prosecutions in several countries. An engineer involved in developing the software pleaded guilty in the United States and was sentenced to prison in 2017.\n\nFor codes of ethics the case is instructive. The engineers'' technical competence was not in doubt; the failure was one of integrity, honesty and public welfare. Every principle about placing the public first, avoiding harm and being honest was breached. The case also shows that \"I was following instructions\" does not discharge a professional''s obligations, and that organisational culture can make breaches routine unless individuals and leaders actively resist."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Dilemma arises\"] --> B[\"Identify relevant code principles\"]\n  B --> C{\"Do principles conflict?\"}\n  C -- No --> D[\"Act on the clear principle\"]\n  C -- Yes --> E[\"Apply paramountcy: public welfare first\"]\n  E --> F[\"Generate options\"]\n  F --> G[\"Test options against code, competence and honesty\"]\n  G --> H[\"Recommend and document\"]\n  H --> I[\"Reflect and plan CPD\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**\"A code of ethics is a legal document.\"** Codes are professional commitments; breaching one may lead to expulsion from a body but is not in itself a legal offence, although many code duties overlap with legal duties.","**\"If my employer instructs me, the code no longer applies.\"** Both codes place public welfare above employer interests; following instructions does not remove professional responsibility.","**\"Competence just means technical skill.\"** Competence includes judgement, currency and awareness of one''s limits, and honesty about those limits is an ethical duty.","**\"Codes give a single correct answer to every dilemma.\"** Codes identify considerations; principles can conflict and must be weighed with judgement.","**\"CPD is only about passing certifications.\"** CPD is any structured learning that maintains competence, including mentoring, reflective practice and project experience."],"examMistakes":["Quoting or listing code principles without applying them to the facts of the case.","Claiming that the codes are identical, or failing to note differences in emphasis.","Inventing clause numbers or wording; paraphrase accurately instead.","Ignoring the paramountcy of public welfare when principles conflict.","Treating competence purely as a career matter and missing its ethical dimension.","Recommending refusal without offering a constructive alternative."],"criticalQuestions":["If membership of the ACM or IEEE is voluntary and enforcement is weak, why should a Ghanaian IT professional take these codes seriously?","Should an organisation''s internal code of conduct ever override a professional code? Give an example where they might conflict.","Is it ethical to accept a project that is slightly beyond your competence if you disclose this and commit to learning quickly? Where is the line?","The ACM code includes duties for leaders. Why might leadership obligations be more important than individual obligations in preventing cases like Volkswagen?","How could CPD requirements be made meaningful rather than a box-ticking exercise?"],"exercises":["**Code comparison.** Read the full ACM and IEEE codes on their official websites. *Expected output:* a table comparing at least eight themes (for example public welfare, privacy, competence, honesty, discrimination, security, environment, reporting) with a paraphrase from each code and a comment on differences.","**Personal CPD plan.** Using the reflect-plan-act-evaluate cycle, produce a 12-month CPD plan for your current or intended role. *Expected output:* a one-page plan with three competence gaps, matching learning activities, target dates and evidence you will keep.","**Code-based recommendation.** Write a 300-word memo from Abena to her manager in the worked example. *Expected output:* a professional memo that discloses limitations, cites relevant principles in paraphrase and proposes at least two alternatives."],"takeaways":["Codes guide, support, educate, discipline and build public trust, but they are abstract, can conflict and are weakly enforced.","The ACM Code (2018) has four parts: general principles, professional responsibilities, leadership principles and compliance.","The IEEE Code groups commitments under integrity and public welfare, fair treatment of persons, and upholding the code among colleagues.","Both codes place public welfare first and treat competence and honesty as ethical duties.","Competence includes knowledge, skill, judgement, awareness of limits and currency; CPD keeps it current."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Association for Computing Machinery. (2018). *ACM code of ethics and professional conduct*.","url":"https://www.acm.org/code-of-ethics","verification":"unverified"},{"text":"Davis, M. (1991). Thinking like an engineer: The place of a code of ethics in the practice of a profession. *Philosophy & Public Affairs, 20*(2), 150-167.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"IEEE. (n.d.). *IEEE code of ethics*.","url":"https://www.ieee.org/about/corporate/governance/p7-8.html","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Moor, J. H. (1985). What is computer ethics? *Metaphilosophy, 16*(4), 266-275.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Quinn, M. J. (2020). *Ethics for the information age* (8th ed.). Pearson.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Tavani, H. T. (2016). *Ethics and technology: Controversies, questions, and strategies for ethical computing* (5th ed.). Wiley.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"ACM Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct","url":"https://www.acm.org/code-of-ethics","description":"Association for Computing Machinery. Official home of the 2018 code with supporting guidance and case material.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"IEEE Code of Ethics","url":"https://www.ieee.org/about/corporate/governance/p7-8.html","description":"IEEE. Official page presenting the current IEEE Code of Ethics within IEEE policies.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Markkula Center for Applied Ethics","url":"https://www.scu.edu/ethics/","description":"Santa Clara University. Official portal with resources on technology ethics and professional practice.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Professional codes condense a profession''s values into commitments that guide, support and discipline members and signal trustworthiness to the public, although they are abstract and weakly enforced. The ACM Code (2018) is organised into general principles, professional responsibilities, leadership principles and compliance; the IEEE Code groups commitments around integrity and public welfare, fair treatment and upholding the code. Both put the public first and treat competence and honesty as ethical duties.\n\n- Five functions of codes: guidance, support, education, discipline, public trust.\n- ACM 2018: four parts; distinctive duties on security, leadership and system retirement.\n- IEEE: public safety first; sustainability; conflicts of interest; no retaliation against reporters.\n- Competence = knowledge, skill, judgement, awareness of limits, currency.\n- Undertake unfamiliar work only after full disclosure and with support.\n- CPD cycle: reflect, plan, act, evaluate."}'::jsonb, 3064, 2, true)
