insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('a57f52aa-0594-5af9-98fd-e3ee604240db', '79cfef66-3103-5a91-92f2-01fbb40f4768', 'distributed-ledgers-and-consensus', 'Distributed Ledgers and Consensus', 'Explain how distributed ledgers and blockchains maintain a shared record without a central authority, compare proof of work, proof of stake and Byzantine fault-tolerant consensus, and distinguish permissioned from permissionless designs.', array['Distributed ledgers', 'Consensus', 'Proof of work', 'Proof of stake']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Most business records are kept by a trusted central party. A bank maintains account balances, a land registry maintains title records, and a cocoa buyer maintains its purchase ledger. Other parties accept these records because they trust the record-keeper, or because law and audit hold it to account. But what happens when several organisations that do not fully trust each other need to share one version of the truth, or when no central party is acceptable to everyone? Reconciliation between separate ledgers is slow and costly, and a single record-keeper can make mistakes, be corrupted or become a single point of failure.\n\nDistributed ledger technology (DLT) offers a different approach: many participants hold copies of a ledger and follow a **consensus protocol** to agree which updates are valid and in what order. Blockchain is the best-known form, introduced in Satoshi Nakamoto''s 2008 Bitcoin paper. This lesson explains how blockchains are structured, why consensus is a hard problem, how proof of work, proof of stake and practical Byzantine fault tolerance solve it in different ways, and how permissioned and permissionless designs suit different business contexts. The next lesson builds on these foundations to examine smart contracts and when blockchain should, and should not, be used.","objectives":["Explain how a blockchain links blocks using cryptographic hashes and why this makes the ledger tamper-evident.","Describe the Byzantine generals problem and the double-spending problem and why they require consensus mechanisms.","Compare proof of work, proof of stake and PBFT-style consensus on security assumptions, finality, performance and energy use.","Distinguish permissionless from permissioned ledgers and identify suitable business contexts for each.","Evaluate claims about blockchain \"immutability\" and decentralisation critically."],"prerequisites":["Basic understanding of databases and networked systems.","No prior cryptography is required; hash functions are explained in the lesson."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Distributed ledger","definition":"A database replicated across multiple participants, who use a consensus protocol to agree on its contents without relying on a single central administrator."},{"term":"Blockchain","definition":"A distributed ledger that groups transactions into blocks, each containing the cryptographic hash of the previous block, forming a chain."},{"term":"Cryptographic hash function","definition":"A function that maps data of any size to a fixed-size output such that it is practically infeasible to find two inputs with the same output or to reverse the output; any change to the input changes the output unpredictably."},{"term":"Consensus mechanism","definition":"The rules by which nodes in a distributed network agree on which transactions are valid and in what order."},{"term":"Proof of work (PoW)","definition":"A consensus mechanism in which participants compete to solve a computationally expensive puzzle; the winner proposes the next block, and the chain with the most accumulated work is accepted."},{"term":"Proof of stake (PoS)","definition":"A consensus mechanism in which validators lock up (stake) cryptocurrency and are selected to propose and attest to blocks, with stakes at risk of penalty (\"slashing\") for misbehaviour."},{"term":"Byzantine fault tolerance (BFT)","definition":"The ability of a distributed system to reach correct agreement even when some participants fail or act maliciously."},{"term":"Finality","definition":"The point at which a transaction can no longer be reversed; it may be probabilistic (PoW) or deterministic (BFT-style protocols)."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### From central ledgers to shared ledgers\n\nA conventional database has an administrator who can add, change or delete records. In a distributed ledger, each participating **node** holds a copy and validates updates according to shared rules. No single participant can unilaterally rewrite history, and every participant can verify that the ledger follows the rules. The trade-off is that agreement among many nodes is slower and more complex than writing to a single database.\n\n### How a blockchain is structured\n\nEach block contains a set of transactions and a **header** that includes, among other fields, a time stamp, a summary of the block''s transactions (often a Merkle root, a single hash that summarises all transactions in the block) and the **hash of the previous block**. Because each block points to its predecessor''s hash, the blocks form a chain back to the first, or \"genesis\", block.\n\nHash functions make the chain **tamper-evident**. If someone alters a transaction in an old block, that block''s hash changes. The next block''s stored \"previous hash\" no longer matches, so the alteration is immediately detectable. To hide the change, the attacker would need to recompute every later block and persuade the network to accept the altered chain, which consensus mechanisms are designed to make prohibitively difficult.\n\n### Two problems consensus must solve\n\n**The Byzantine generals problem.** Lamport, Shostak and Pease (1982) described generals surrounding a city who must agree on a common plan by exchanging messages, while some generals may be traitors sending contradictory messages. The problem formalises how distributed systems can reach agreement when some components behave arbitrarily or maliciously. Their analysis showed that, with oral (unsigned) messages, agreement is possible only if fewer than one-third of participants are faulty.\n\n**The double-spending problem.** Digital money is just data, and data can be copied. Without a central bank checking balances, what stops someone sending the same digital coin to two recipients? A distributed ledger must establish a single agreed order of transactions so that the second spend is recognised as invalid.\n\n### Proof of work\n\nNakamoto (2008) solved both problems for an open network in which anyone can participate anonymously. Miners collect transactions into a candidate block and repeatedly change a number in the header (the nonce), hashing the header until the result falls below a target value set by the network''s difficulty. Finding such a hash requires enormous numbers of attempts, but verifying it takes one hash calculation. The first miner to succeed broadcasts the block and receives a reward. Nodes accept the valid chain with the **most accumulated work**.\n\nPoW''s security rests on the assumption that honest participants control the majority of computing power. An attacker controlling more than half could, in principle, rewrite recent history, the so-called **51% attack**. Finality is **probabilistic**: each additional block built on top of a transaction makes reversal exponentially less likely, which is why recipients often wait for several confirmations. The key drawbacks are **energy consumption**, since security is literally purchased with computation, and limited throughput.\n\n### Proof of stake\n\nPoS replaces computational work with economic stake. Validators deposit cryptocurrency as collateral and are chosen, typically with randomness weighted by stake, to propose blocks, while others attest to their validity. Validators who act dishonestly, for example by signing conflicting blocks, can have part of their stake destroyed (\"slashing\"). Security assumes that a majority (often a supermajority) of stake is controlled by honest validators. PoS uses far less energy than PoW. Criticisms include concerns that it may concentrate influence among large stakeholders and that its security properties are more complex to analyse.\n\n### Practical Byzantine fault tolerance\n\nIn settings where participants are known, such as a consortium of banks or shipping firms, voting-based protocols are practical. Castro and Liskov (1999) introduced **Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance (PBFT)**. A leader proposes an order for requests, and nodes exchange several rounds of messages; once a quorum of nodes agrees, the decision is final. PBFT tolerates `f` faulty nodes if there are at least `3f + 1` nodes in total, with decisions requiring agreement from `2f + 1`. It provides **deterministic finality** and high throughput with modest energy use, but message volume grows rapidly with the number of nodes, so it suits tens of validators rather than thousands, and it requires known identities."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Comparing consensus mechanisms\n\n| Feature | Proof of work | Proof of stake | PBFT-style BFT |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Participation | Open; anyone with hardware | Open; anyone with stake (in public chains) | Known, admitted validators |\n| Security assumption | Honest majority of computing power | Honest majority or supermajority of stake | Fewer than one-third faulty nodes |\n| Sybil resistance (preventing fake identities) | Cost of computation | Cost of capital at risk | Identity and admission control |\n| Finality | Probabilistic | Economic or protocol-defined finality after checkpoints | Deterministic once quorum agrees |\n| Energy use | Very high | Low | Low |\n| Scalability in number of validators | Very large | Very large | Limited by message overhead |\n| Typical example | Bitcoin | Ethereum (since 2022) | Enterprise and consortium platforms |\n\n### Permissionless versus permissioned ledgers\n\n- **Permissionless (public) ledgers** allow anyone to read, submit transactions and participate in consensus. They maximise openness and censorship resistance but expose transaction data publicly (even if pseudonymous) and depend on cryptoeconomic incentives such as mining or staking rewards.\n- **Permissioned ledgers** restrict participation to approved organisations, often with different roles for reading, writing and validating. They offer privacy controls, known accountability and faster consensus, but reintroduce trust in the governance body that admits members. Hyperledger Fabric, an open-source enterprise platform hosted under the Linux Foundation''s decentralised-trust initiative, is a widely used example.\n\nThe choice is not purely technical. It reflects **governance**: who decides the rules, who can join and how disputes are resolved."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Part A: demonstrating tamper-evidence\n\nConsider a simplified three-block chain. Real hashes are long hexadecimal strings; here they are shortened for illustration.\n\n| Block | Transactions | Previous hash | Block hash |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Farmer group A sells 20 bags to Buyer X | 0000 | 7A3F |\n| 2 | Buyer X transfers 20 bags to Warehouse W | 7A3F | C91B |\n| 3 | Warehouse W ships 20 bags to Exporter E | C91B | 5D02 |\n\n**Step 1.** Someone alters Block 1 to say 25 bags. Recomputing Block 1''s hash gives a completely different value, say `E44C`.\n\n**Step 2.** Block 2 still records `7A3F` as its previous hash, which no longer matches `E44C`. Every node checking the chain detects the inconsistency.\n\n**Step 3.** To hide the change, the attacker would need to recompute Blocks 2 and 3 and, under PoW, redo the work faster than the honest network, or, under BFT, persuade a quorum of validators to accept the altered history.\n\n**Interpretation.** The ledger is *tamper-evident* and tamper-resistant, but not literally immutable: a majority of computing power, stake or validators could still rewrite it. Moreover, if the original entry was *false* (Farmer group A actually sold 15 bags), the blockchain will faithfully preserve the false record. This is the \"garbage in, garbage out\" or **oracle problem**, which is crucial for supply chain applications.\n\n### Part B: sizing a PBFT consortium\n\nA consortium wants to tolerate up to 2 faulty or malicious validators.\n\n- Required nodes: `n = 3f + 1 = 3 × 2 + 1 = 7`.\n- Quorum for decisions: `2f + 1 = 5`.\n- With only 6 nodes, the consortium could tolerate only `f = 1` fault, because `3 × 2 + 1 = 7 > 6`.\n\nIf two members are likely to be offline for maintenance at the same time as one may be compromised, the consortium needs `f = 3`, so `n = 10`. Adding validators improves fault tolerance but increases message overhead and coordination cost."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** A consortium of four licensed cocoa buying companies, two warehouse operators and an exporters'' association in Ghana (all hypothetical) is exploring a shared ledger to record movements of cocoa bags from purchase points to warehouses and ports. Today each party keeps its own records, and reconciling discrepancies is slow.\n\n**Design analysis.**\n\n- **Permissionless or permissioned?** Commercial data such as volumes and prices is sensitive, participants are known organisations and regulators need clear accountability. A **permissioned** ledger is more appropriate.\n- **Consensus.** With a small number of known validators, a BFT-style protocol provides deterministic finality and good performance. With seven validators, the network tolerates two faulty nodes.\n- **Governance.** The consortium needs rules on membership, validator operation, data access, dispute resolution and cost sharing. Without these, technical consensus is meaningless.\n- **Data entry and the oracle problem.** The ledger records what participants enter. Weighing scales, bag tags (for example QR codes or RFID), GPS-tagged farm records and field verification determine whether entries reflect reality.\n- **Integration with national systems.** Any solution must work with the requirements and systems of the national cocoa regulator and with buyers'' traceability demands, which have intensified as international markets, including the European Union, have introduced deforestation-related due-diligence rules.\n- **Alternative.** The consortium should compare the ledger with a well-governed shared database operated by a trusted neutral party. The next lesson provides a decision tree for that comparison.\n\nA broader Ghanaian context is the growth of digital financial infrastructure. The Bank of Ghana has publicly piloted a central bank digital currency, the eCedi. Students should note that a central bank digital currency is a form of central bank money issued and governed by the central bank; whether it uses blockchain technology at all is a design choice, which illustrates that \"digital currency\" and \"blockchain\" are not synonyms."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Ethereum''s switch from proof of work to proof of stake\n\nEthereum, a public blockchain launched in 2015 that supports programmable smart contracts, originally used proof of work, as Bitcoin does. After years of research and testing, including a separate proof-of-stake chain known as the Beacon Chain launched in 2020, Ethereum completed a transition called \"The Merge\" in September 2022, after which the network''s consensus has been provided by proof of stake. The Ethereum Foundation has stated that the change reduced the network''s energy consumption by more than 99%.\n\n**Analysis.**\n\n- The case shows that consensus mechanisms are **design choices with major consequences**: the same network changed its security model, energy profile and economic incentives.\n- Under PoS, validators must stake a significant amount of the network''s cryptocurrency (ether) to participate directly, and misbehaviour can be penalised through slashing. Many holders stake through pools or services, which raises debate about concentration of influence.\n- The transition demonstrated **governance** in a permissionless system: changes require coordination among developers, node operators, validators, exchanges and users, without a single owner who can impose them.\n- For businesses evaluating public blockchains, energy use and environmental reporting obligations were important objections to PoW; the Merge changed that argument for Ethereum, though not for PoW networks such as Bitcoin."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    G[\"Genesis block\"] --> B1[\"Block 1: header with previous hash, Merkle root, timestamp\"]\n    B1 --> B2[\"Block 2: stores hash of Block 1\"]\n    B2 --> B3[\"Block 3: stores hash of Block 2\"]\n    B3 --> N[\"New block proposed\"]\n    N --> C{\"Consensus mechanism\"}\n    C -->|\"Proof of work\"| W[\"Most accumulated work wins; probabilistic finality\"]\n    C -->|\"Proof of stake\"| S[\"Stake-weighted validators attest; slashing for misbehaviour\"]\n    C -->|\"PBFT\"| P[\"Known validators vote; final when 2f+1 agree\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**\"Blockchains are immutable.\"** They are tamper-evident and resistant; a controlling majority of work, stake or validators could rewrite history, and governance can choose to fork.","**\"Data on a blockchain is true.\"** The ledger preserves what was entered; inaccurate inputs remain inaccurate (the oracle problem).","**\"Blockchain and cryptocurrency are the same thing.\"** Cryptocurrencies are one application; permissioned ledgers for business often have no public cryptocurrency.","**\"Proof of stake is just proof of work without electricity.\"** PoS has different security assumptions, based on capital at risk and slashing, and different concentration risks.","**\"More validators always means better.\"** In BFT protocols more validators improve fault tolerance but increase message overhead and slow consensus."],"examMistakes":["Describing hashing as encryption; hashing is one-way and is not designed to be decrypted.","Stating that PoW prevents all attacks rather than explaining the honest-majority assumption and the 51% attack.","Confusing permissioned/permissionless (who can participate) with public/private data visibility without explanation.","Using the formula `3f + 1` incorrectly, for example claiming 4 nodes tolerate 2 faults."],"criticalQuestions":["If a permissioned ledger depends on a governance body to admit members, how is it different from a shared database run by that body? When does the difference matter?","Is the energy consumption of proof of work a fatal objection for business use, or a legitimate cost of security for certain applications? Justify your view.","How could the oracle problem undermine a cocoa traceability ledger in Ghana, and what non-blockchain controls would address it?"],"exercises":["Calculate the minimum number of validators needed to tolerate 1, 3 and 5 Byzantine faults, and the quorum size for each. Expected output: a table with `f`, `n` and quorum values.","Prepare a comparison table recommending a consensus mechanism for (a) a global public payment network, (b) a consortium of five Ghanaian banks sharing know-your-customer records and (c) a university''s certificate verification service. Expected output: a table with the mechanism, permission model and justification for each."],"takeaways":["Distributed ledgers replicate records across participants who agree on updates through consensus, removing reliance on a single record-keeper.","Hash-linked blocks make tampering evident, but blockchains are not literally immutable and do not guarantee that inputs are true.","PoW secures open networks through computational cost with probabilistic finality and high energy use; PoS secures them through capital at risk with much lower energy use.","PBFT-style protocols suit known validators, tolerate `f` faults with `3f + 1` nodes and give deterministic finality.","Permissioned versus permissionless is fundamentally a governance choice about who can participate and who is trusted."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Nakamoto, S. (2008). *Bitcoin: A peer-to-peer electronic cash system*.","url":"https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Lamport, L., Shostak, R., & Pease, M. (1982). The Byzantine generals problem. *ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems, 4*(3), 382-401.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Castro, M., & Liskov, B. (1999). Practical Byzantine fault tolerance. In *Proceedings of the Third Symposium on Operating Systems Design and Implementation*. USENIX Association.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Narayanan, A., Bonneau, J., Felten, E., Miller, A., & Goldfeder, S. (2016). *Bitcoin and cryptocurrency technologies: A comprehensive introduction*. Princeton University Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Ethereum Developer Documentation","url":"https://ethereum.org/developers/docs/","description":"Ethereum.org. Official documentation explaining blocks, consensus, proof of stake and the Merge.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Hyperledger Foundation","url":"https://www.lfdecentralizedtrust.org/","description":"LF Decentralized Trust. Home of open-source enterprise ledger projects such as Hyperledger Fabric, with learning resources.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"IBM Think","url":"https://www.ibm.com/think","description":"IBM. Explainers on blockchain and distributed ledger technology for business.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Distributed ledgers let many participants keep synchronised copies of a record and agree on updates through consensus. Blockchains link blocks by storing each predecessor''s hash, making tampering evident, though not literally impossible, and preserving false inputs as faithfully as true ones. Consensus addresses the Byzantine generals and double-spending problems: proof of work uses computational cost with probabilistic finality and high energy use; proof of stake uses staked capital and slashing with low energy use; PBFT uses voting among known validators, tolerating `f` faults with `3f + 1` nodes and giving deterministic finality. Permissionless ledgers are open; permissioned ledgers restrict participation and rely on governance.\n\n- Hash links make tampering evident, not impossible.\n- Oracle problem: garbage in, garbage preserved.\n- PoW: work, probabilistic finality, high energy.\n- PoS: stake, slashing, low energy; Ethereum since 2022.\n- PBFT: n = 3f + 1, quorum 2f + 1, deterministic finality.\n- Permission model is a governance choice."}'::jsonb, 3000, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('25d15bb0-033f-57b1-8554-529d650968af', '79cfef66-3103-5a91-92f2-01fbb40f4768', 'smart-contracts-and-enterprise-blockchain', 'Smart Contracts, Enterprise Blockchain and Business Applications', 'Explain how smart contracts work and where they fail, compare enterprise blockchain platforms and use cases, and apply a structured decision tree to judge when a blockchain is, and is not, the right solution for a business problem.', array['Smart contracts', 'Enterprise blockchain', 'Business applications']::text[], 55, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"The previous lesson showed how distributed ledgers let mutually distrustful parties agree on a shared record. The next question for business is what that record can *do*. Smart contracts, programs stored on a blockchain that execute automatically when conditions are met, promise to automate agreements such as payments on delivery, insurance pay-outs or the release of trade documents, without relying on a single intermediary to enforce them. Enterprise blockchain platforms adapt these ideas for consortia of known organisations, promising faster reconciliation, better traceability and new forms of collaboration.\n\nThe reality has been mixed. Some applications have delivered genuine value; many pilots never reached production; and a number of high-profile platforms have closed. This lesson therefore balances explanation with critical evaluation. You will learn how smart contracts work, the technical and legal risks they carry, the main enterprise platform designs, and the business applications most often proposed. Most importantly, you will learn a structured decision tree for deciding when *not* to use blockchain, because recommending a conventional database when it is the better solution is a mark of professional judgement, not a lack of ambition.","objectives":["Explain what smart contracts are, how they execute and why oracles are needed.","Analyse the technical, legal and governance risks of smart contracts, using The DAO incident as an example.","Compare enterprise blockchain designs, such as Hyperledger Fabric, with public smart-contract platforms.","Apply a decision tree to determine whether a blockchain, and which kind, is appropriate for a business problem.","Evaluate enterprise blockchain initiatives using documented successes and failures."],"prerequisites":["Distributed ledgers, consensus and permissioned versus permissionless designs from the previous lesson.","Basic understanding of business processes such as procurement, payment and supply chains."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Smart contract","definition":"A program stored on a blockchain that executes predefined logic automatically when specified conditions are met, with its execution verified by the network."},{"term":"Oracle","definition":"A service that supplies external, real-world data (such as prices, delivery confirmations or weather readings) to a smart contract."},{"term":"Gas","definition":"On Ethereum, the unit measuring the computational effort required to execute operations, paid for in the network''s currency to compensate validators and limit abuse."},{"term":"Chaincode","definition":"The term used in Hyperledger Fabric for smart contract code."},{"term":"Endorsement policy","definition":"In Hyperledger Fabric, a rule specifying which organisations must approve (endorse) a transaction before it is valid."},{"term":"Consortium blockchain","definition":"A permissioned ledger governed jointly by a group of organisations, typically in the same industry or supply chain."},{"term":"Tokenisation","definition":"Representing an asset or right, such as a warehouse receipt or a share of property, as a digital token on a ledger."},{"term":"Reentrancy vulnerability","definition":"A smart contract flaw in which an external call allows an attacker to re-enter a function repeatedly before the contract updates its state, for example to withdraw funds multiple times."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### What smart contracts are\n\nNick Szabo described the idea of smart contracts in the 1990s, using the analogy of a vending machine: insert the right money, select an item, and the machine delivers the product and change automatically, without a shop assistant. On a blockchain, a smart contract is code deployed to the ledger. Transactions call its functions; every validating node executes the code and must reach the same result, so outcomes are verified by the network rather than by one party.\n\nOn Ethereum, contracts are commonly written in the Solidity language and run on the Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM). Every operation consumes **gas**, which prevents infinite loops and compensates validators. On Hyperledger Fabric, smart contracts are called **chaincode** and can be written in general-purpose languages such as Go, Java or JavaScript.\n\n### Properties that matter for business\n\n- **Determinism.** All nodes must produce identical results, so contracts cannot directly call external websites or use local clocks freely. External data must come through oracles.\n- **Automatic execution.** Once conditions are met, the code runs as written. This removes discretion, which is valuable for routine actions but problematic when exceptions arise.\n- **Persistence.** Deployed code is difficult or impossible to change on public chains, so bugs can be permanent unless the contract was designed with controlled upgrade mechanisms, which themselves reintroduce trust in whoever controls upgrades.\n- **Transparency.** On public chains, contract code and transactions are visible to everyone, which aids verification but may expose commercial information.\n\n### The oracle problem revisited\n\nA smart contract that pays a cocoa exporter when goods arrive at a port depends on *someone* reporting that arrival. If the oracle is a single company''s system, the contract is only as trustworthy as that company. Approaches to reduce oracle risk include using several independent data sources, IoT devices with secure identities, and economic penalties for false reporting, but none removes the need for trust entirely. The oracle problem is the main reason many supply chain blockchain promises have been harder to fulfil than expected.\n\n### Legal status\n\nA smart contract is not automatically a legal contract. Whether code forms, evidences or performs a legally binding agreement depends on the law of the relevant jurisdiction and the surrounding documentation. Businesses typically pair smart contracts with conventional legal agreements that specify governing law, dispute resolution and what happens when code and intention diverge. This content is educational, not legal advice.\n\n### Risks illustrated by The DAO\n\nIn 2016 an Ethereum-based investment organisation called The DAO raised a large amount of ether from participants. An attacker exploited a reentrancy vulnerability in its smart contract code to drain a substantial portion of the funds. The Ethereum community responded with a contentious \"hard fork\" that altered the chain''s history to return funds to investors; a minority who rejected the change continued the original chain, which became known as Ethereum Classic. The episode demonstrated that code can contain costly bugs, that \"code is law\" can conflict with participants'' expectations of fairness, and that so-called immutability can be overridden by social consensus.\n\n### Enterprise blockchain platforms\n\nEnterprise platforms are typically permissioned and designed for consortia.\n\n| Feature | Public smart-contract platform (for example Ethereum) | Enterprise platform (for example Hyperledger Fabric) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Participation | Open to anyone | Admitted organisations with identities issued by membership services |\n| Consensus | Proof of stake | Pluggable ordering service; endorsement by specified organisations |\n| Privacy | Transactions publicly visible | Channels and private data collections restrict visibility |\n| Transaction costs | Gas fees paid in cryptocurrency | No native cryptocurrency; infrastructure costs shared by members |\n| Governance | Community and developer processes | Consortium agreements and administrators |\n| Typical uses | Tokens, decentralised finance, public verification | Supply chains, trade finance, inter-company reconciliation |\n\nHyperledger Fabric uses an **execute-order-validate** model: endorsing peers simulate the transaction, an ordering service sequences transactions into blocks, and peers validate them against endorsement policies before committing. **Channels** allow subsets of members to share a ledger privately. These features address enterprise concerns about confidentiality and performance.\n\n### Common business applications\n\n- **Supply chain traceability**: recording custody and provenance of goods such as food, minerals or cocoa.\n- **Trade finance**: sharing letters of credit, bills of lading and other documents among banks, exporters, importers and shippers.\n- **Credentials and identity**: issuing verifiable academic certificates or professional licences.\n- **Asset registries and tokenisation**: recording ownership of warehouse receipts, carbon credits or property interests.\n- **Inter-company reconciliation and payments**: settling obligations among members of a consortium."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### When to use blockchain: a decision tree\n\nWüst and Gervais (2018) proposed a structured way of asking whether a blockchain is needed. The version below adapts their logic for business analysis.\n\n1. **Do you need to store shared state?** If not, no ledger is needed.\n2. **Are there multiple writers?** If only one organisation writes the data, a conventional database is sufficient.\n3. **Can you use an always-online trusted third party?** If all writers are willing to trust an operator, such as a regulator, an industry body or a cloud-hosted service with audit, a centralised database is simpler, faster and cheaper.\n4. **Are all writers known?** If not, a **permissionless** blockchain may be appropriate.\n5. **Are all known writers trusted?** If yes, a conventional replicated database suffices.\n6. **Is public verifiability needed?** If writers are known but not fully trusted, a **permissioned** blockchain fits, with public or private read access depending on whether outsiders must verify the data.\n\nAdditional business tests should follow: Is the problem really about **trust between parties**, or about poor data quality, process or incentives? Can the **oracle problem** be managed? Will enough participants **join** to create network value? Who will **govern and fund** the platform?\n\n### Network effects and consortium economics\n\nEnterprise blockchains are network goods: their value depends on how many relevant parties participate. Yet each participant may fear that the platform operator, often a powerful firm in the industry, will capture value or gain competitive information. This tension between **network value** and **neutral governance** explains many consortium failures. Successful consortia typically have a neutral governance structure, clear benefits for each participant and a credible path to industry-wide adoption."},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"### Part A: a smart contract for payment on delivery\n\n**Illustrative scenario.** An exporter and a European chocolate maker agree that payment will be released when a cocoa shipment''s arrival is confirmed and a quality certificate is issued. The logic can be expressed in pseudo-code:\n\n```\ncontract CocoaShipmentEscrow:\n    state: AWAITING_DEPOSIT, FUNDED, DELIVERED, PAID, REFUNDED\n    buyer, seller, portOracle, qualityOracle, amount, deadline\n\n    deposit():           only buyer; require amount; state = FUNDED\n    confirmArrival():    only portOracle; require state == FUNDED; arrived = true\n    confirmQuality(ok):  only qualityOracle; require arrived\n                         if ok: state = DELIVERED; transfer amount to seller; state = PAID\n                         else: open dispute process\n    refund():            only buyer; require now > deadline and state == FUNDED\n                         transfer amount to buyer; state = REFUNDED\n```\n\n**Analysis of the design.**\n\n1. **Trust shifts to oracles.** The port and quality oracles determine payment. If either is compromised or mistaken, the contract executes the wrong outcome automatically.\n2. **Exceptions need off-chain processes.** A partial delivery or disputed quality grade is not handled by simple code; a dispute mechanism and a legal agreement are required.\n3. **Security.** Funds should be transferred only after state is updated, to avoid reentrancy-type flaws, and the code should be independently audited.\n4. **Currency.** Payment in a cryptocurrency introduces price volatility and regulatory questions; a consortium might instead use the contract to trigger a conventional bank payment.\n\n### Part B: applying the decision tree to three proposals\n\n| Proposal | Key answers | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|\n| A supermarket chain''s internal stock records | Single writer (the chain) | Conventional database |\n| Verification of degree certificates issued by one university | Single issuer; outsiders need to verify | Digitally signed certificates with a verification portal; blockchain optional and unnecessary for trust |\n| Trade documents shared among several banks, shippers, exporters and customs agencies across countries, with no party trusted by all to run the system | Multiple known writers, not fully trusted, no acceptable central operator | Permissioned blockchain is plausible, subject to governance, adoption and oracle tests |\n\nThe exercise shows that blockchain is justified only in a narrow set of conditions; many proposals fail at step 2 or step 3."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** A coalition of hypothetical organisations in Ghana, comprising several licensed cocoa buying companies, a warehouse operator, two banks and an exporters'' group, wants to digitise warehouse receipts so that farmers'' cooperatives can use stored cocoa as collateral for loans. Today, paper receipts can be lost, duplicated or forged, and banks are cautious about lending against them.\n\n**Applying the decision tree.**\n\n- *Shared state?* Yes: the receipt''s ownership and status.\n- *Multiple writers?* Yes: warehouses issue receipts, banks record liens, buyers record sales.\n- *Trusted third party?* This is the critical question. If a regulator or a neutral warehouse-receipt registry is acceptable to all and can operate reliably, a **central registry database** with strong audit trails may be the best solution. If participants do not accept any single operator, a **permissioned ledger** among them is reasonable.\n- *Oracle problem.* The ledger only helps if the physical cocoa matches the receipt. Independent warehouse inspection, tamper-evident storage and periodic audits are essential.\n- *Smart contract use.* A contract could record a bank''s lien automatically when a loan is issued and release it when repaid, preventing the same receipt from being pledged twice.\n- *Inclusion.* Cooperatives need simple access, for example via USSD or agents, and must understand how their collateral is recorded.\n\nThe best recommendation may be a phased approach: start with a well-governed central digital registry, design data standards that could later be moved to a shared ledger, and revisit blockchain only if governance disputes make a central operator unworkable."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Contrasting outcomes: food traceability and TradeLens\n\n**Food traceability at Walmart.** Walmart worked with IBM on a blockchain-based food traceability solution built on Hyperledger Fabric, which became part of the IBM Food Trust offering. In widely reported pilots, the time needed to trace the origin of a food product, mangoes in one well-known demonstration, fell from days to seconds. Walmart subsequently asked certain suppliers of leafy green vegetables to provide traceability data through the system. The case shows the value of a powerful buyer who can require participation, a clear problem (rapid response to food safety incidents) and standardised data.\n\n**TradeLens.** In 2018 the shipping company Maersk and IBM launched TradeLens, a blockchain-enabled platform for sharing shipping and trade documents among carriers, ports, customs authorities and shippers. Despite attracting a number of participants, the partners announced in late 2022 that the platform would be discontinued, with Maersk stating that it had not reached the level of commercial viability needed and that full global industry collaboration had not been achieved.\n\n**Comparative analysis.**\n\n- Both cases used enterprise blockchain technology; their different outcomes reflect **governance and network economics** more than technology.\n- In food traceability, Walmart''s purchasing power created participation incentives. In TradeLens, competitors of Maersk may have been reluctant to join a platform led by a rival, illustrating the tension between network value and neutral governance.\n- Neither case shows that blockchain was strictly necessary; similar traceability might be achieved with shared databases and data standards. A rigorous evaluation asks what the ledger added beyond digitisation and standardisation."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A{\"Need to store shared state?\"} -->|\"No\"| X[\"No ledger needed\"]\n    A -->|\"Yes\"| B{\"Multiple writers?\"}\n    B -->|\"No\"| DB[\"Conventional database\"]\n    B -->|\"Yes\"| C{\"Always-online trusted third party acceptable?\"}\n    C -->|\"Yes\"| DB2[\"Central database run by trusted party\"]\n    C -->|\"No\"| D{\"Are all writers known?\"}\n    D -->|\"No\"| PL[\"Permissionless blockchain\"]\n    D -->|\"Yes\"| E{\"Are all writers trusted?\"}\n    E -->|\"Yes\"| DB3[\"Replicated database\"]\n    E -->|\"No\"| F{\"Public verifiability needed?\"}\n    F -->|\"Yes\"| PP[\"Permissioned blockchain, public read\"]\n    F -->|\"No\"| PR[\"Permissioned blockchain, private read\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**\"Smart contracts are legally binding contracts.\"** Legal effect depends on jurisdiction and accompanying agreements; code may perform an agreement without being the agreement.","**\"Smart contracts remove the need for trust.\"** Trust shifts to code authors, auditors, oracles and whoever controls upgrades.","**\"Blockchain is needed for traceability.\"** Traceability requires data capture, standards and participation; a shared database may provide it more cheaply when a trusted operator exists.","**\"Enterprise blockchain failures were technical failures.\"** Many failed because of governance, incentives and insufficient participation.","**\"Code deployed on a blockchain can never be changed.\"** Upgrade patterns and, as The DAO showed, social consensus forks can change outcomes."],"examMistakes":["Recommending blockchain without applying a structured test of whether a database would suffice.","Ignoring the oracle problem in supply chain proposals.","Describing smart contracts as \"AI\" or as self-enforcing legal documents.","Citing case outcomes with invented figures; describe documented facts qualitatively if unsure of numbers.","Overlooking governance, funding and participation incentives in consortium designs."],"criticalQuestions":["In the warehouse receipt scenario, what evidence would convince you that no central operator could be trusted, justifying a permissioned ledger?","\"Code is law\" conflicted with fairness in The DAO incident. Which should prevail in business smart contracts, and how should contracts be designed to reflect your answer?","Why might a dominant firm''s sponsorship both help and hinder an industry blockchain platform?","Could tokenisation of farmland or cocoa receipts improve access to finance for Ghanaian farmers, or would it introduce new risks? Discuss.","If many blockchain benefits come from digitisation and data standards, why do organisations still choose blockchain? Evaluate the possible reasons."],"exercises":["Apply the decision tree to a proposal of your choice in Ghana (for example land title registration, pharmaceutical authentication or university transcripts). Expected output: the answers at each node, the resulting recommendation and two additional business tests.","Extend the escrow pseudo-code to handle partial delivery and a dispute raised by the buyer. Expected output: revised pseudo-code and a paragraph identifying which decisions must remain off-chain.","Write a one-page governance charter for a consortium ledger among five organisations. Expected output: sections on membership, validator responsibilities, data access, cost sharing, dispute resolution and exit."],"takeaways":["Smart contracts are programs on a ledger that execute automatically, but they depend on oracles, careful coding and supporting legal agreements.","The DAO incident showed that smart contract bugs can be costly and that immutability can yield to social consensus.","Enterprise platforms such as Hyperledger Fabric add identity, privacy channels and endorsement policies for consortia of known organisations.","A decision tree, adapted from Wüst and Gervais (2018), shows that many proposals are better served by conventional databases.","Governance, participation incentives and the oracle problem determine enterprise blockchain success more than technology does."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Szabo, N. (1997). Formalizing and securing relationships on public networks. *First Monday, 2*(9).","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Wüst, K., & Gervais, A. (2018). Do you need a blockchain? In *2018 Crypto Valley Conference on Blockchain Technology (CVCBT)*. IEEE.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Iansiti, M., & Lakhani, K. R. (2017). The truth about blockchain. *Harvard Business Review, 95*(1), 118-127.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Nakamoto, S. (2008). *Bitcoin: A peer-to-peer electronic cash system*.","url":"https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf","verification":"verified"}],"videos":[{"title":"Ethereum Developer Documentation","url":"https://ethereum.org/developers/docs/","description":"Ethereum.org. Official documentation on smart contracts, the EVM, gas and oracles.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Hyperledger Foundation","url":"https://www.lfdecentralizedtrust.org/","description":"LF Decentralized Trust. Information and learning resources for Hyperledger Fabric and other enterprise ledger projects.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"IBM Think","url":"https://www.ibm.com/think","description":"IBM. Explainers on smart contracts and enterprise blockchain use cases.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Smart contracts are programs stored on a ledger that execute automatically and are verified by the network; they need oracles for real-world data, careful security engineering and supporting legal agreements, as The DAO incident of 2016 showed. Enterprise platforms such as Hyperledger Fabric provide permissioned membership, private channels and endorsement policies for consortia. Business applications include traceability, trade finance, credentials and asset registries, but a decision tree (Wüst & Gervais, 2018) shows that blockchain is justified only when multiple untrusted writers share state and no acceptable trusted operator exists. Walmart''s food traceability and the closure of TradeLens show that governance and participation decide outcomes.\n\n- Smart contract = code plus oracles plus legal wrapper.\n- The DAO: reentrancy bug, hard fork, Ethereum Classic.\n- Fabric: chaincode, channels, endorsement policies.\n- Decision tree: shared state, multiple writers, no trusted third party.\n- Oracle problem limits supply chain claims.\n- Governance and network effects decide consortium success."}'::jsonb, 2981, 2, true)
