insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'quantum-computing-fundamentals', 'Quantum Computing Fundamentals', 'Explain qubits, superposition, entanglement and measurement, judge what quantum computers can and cannot be expected to do, and plan a business response to post-quantum cryptography.', array['Quantum computing fundamentals', 'Qubits', 'Post-quantum cryptography']::text[], 50, 'advanced', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Quantum computing attracts more hype and more confusion than almost any other emerging technology. Press coverage often suggests that quantum computers \"try every answer at once\" and will soon replace today''s servers. Neither claim is accurate. Quantum computers are a fundamentally different kind of machine that exploits the rules of quantum mechanics to solve a narrow, but strategically important, set of problems far more efficiently than any known classical method. For most everyday business computing they offer no advantage at all.\n\nFor an IT-for-business manager the most urgent quantum issue is not buying a quantum computer. It is cryptography. A sufficiently large, error-corrected quantum computer running Shor''s algorithm would break the public-key cryptography (RSA and elliptic-curve schemes) that protects online banking, mobile money, VPNs and digital signatures. Because encrypted data can be stolen today and decrypted later, organisations must start planning a migration to post-quantum cryptography long before such a machine exists. This lesson gives you the conceptual foundations needed to separate fact from hype and to lead that planning.","objectives":["Explain the concepts of qubits, superposition, entanglement and measurement without relying on misleading metaphors.","Describe, at an intuitive level, how quantum gates and interference allow quantum algorithms to work.","Distinguish the problem types for which quantum computers are and are not expected to offer an advantage.","Explain conceptually what Shor''s and Grover''s algorithms do and why they matter for cryptography.","Evaluate the implications of the NISQ era and the \"harvest now, decrypt later\" threat for business risk.","Apply a structured approach to planning migration to post-quantum cryptography, referring to NIST''s 2024 standards."],"prerequisites":["A basic understanding of binary data (bits) and how computers store information.","General awareness of encryption, passwords and digital certificates from everyday IT use.","No physics or advanced mathematics is assumed; concepts are explained intuitively."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Qubit","definition":"The basic unit of quantum information; a two-level quantum system whose state can be a weighted combination (superposition) of the states labelled 0 and 1."},{"term":"Superposition","definition":"A quantum state that is a combination of basis states, described by complex numbers called amplitudes that determine the probabilities of each measurement outcome."},{"term":"Entanglement","definition":"A correlation between qubits so strong that the joint state cannot be described as separate states of each qubit; measuring one constrains the outcomes of the others."},{"term":"Measurement","definition":"The act of reading a qubit, which yields a definite classical result (0 or 1) with probabilities set by the amplitudes and disturbs the superposition."},{"term":"Quantum gate","definition":"A reversible operation that transforms the state of one or more qubits; sequences of gates form a quantum circuit."},{"term":"Interference","definition":"The adding or cancelling of amplitudes, used by quantum algorithms to amplify correct answers and suppress wrong ones."},{"term":"Decoherence","definition":"The loss of quantum behaviour caused by unwanted interaction with the environment, introducing errors."},{"term":"NISQ","definition":"Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum; a term introduced by John Preskill for the current era of quantum devices with tens to a few thousand noisy qubits and no full error correction."},{"term":"Post-quantum cryptography (PQC)","definition":"Classical cryptographic algorithms designed to resist attack by both classical and quantum computers."},{"term":"Harvest now, decrypt later","definition":"An attack strategy in which adversaries collect encrypted data today in order to decrypt it once a capable quantum computer becomes available."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### From bits to qubits\n\nA classical bit is always either 0 or 1. A qubit is a physical system with two distinguishable states, conventionally written |0⟩ and |1⟩, but it can also exist in a superposition of both. Mathematically, a single-qubit state is written as `a|0⟩ + b|1⟩`, where `a` and `b` are amplitudes. Amplitudes are complex numbers, and the probability of observing 0 when the qubit is measured is `|a|²`, while the probability of observing 1 is `|b|²`; these probabilities sum to 1.\n\nThe important point is that amplitudes are not probabilities. Probabilities are always positive and simply add. Amplitudes can be positive, negative or complex, so they can reinforce or cancel one another. This is the property that gives quantum computing its power, and it is why the popular claim that a qubit is \"both 0 and 1 at the same time\" is at best incomplete.\n\nPhysical qubits are built in several ways: superconducting circuits cooled close to absolute zero, trapped ions held by electromagnetic fields, neutral atoms manipulated with lasers, photons, and other approaches. Each technology involves trade-offs in speed, error rates, connectivity and scalability, and no single approach has yet clearly won.\n\n### Many qubits and exponential state spaces\n\nA register of `n` classical bits holds exactly one of `2^n` possible values at a time. A register of `n` qubits is described by `2^n` amplitudes simultaneously. Fifty qubits require roughly a thousand million million amplitudes to describe, which is why simulating even modest quantum systems on classical computers becomes infeasible. However, this does not mean that a quantum computer gives you `2^n` answers. When you measure the register you obtain only one `n`-bit result. The art of quantum algorithm design is to choreograph interference so that, by the time you measure, the amplitude of the correct or useful answer is large and the amplitudes of wrong answers are small.\n\n### Entanglement\n\nWhen qubits interact through certain gates they can become entangled. In the simplest example, two qubits are prepared in a state in which measuring both always gives either 00 or 11, each with probability one half, but never 01 or 10. Before measurement neither qubit has its own definite value; the information resides in the correlation between them. Entanglement is a resource that quantum algorithms, quantum communication and quantum error correction all rely on. It does not allow faster-than-light communication: the outcome of each individual measurement is random, so no message can be sent by entanglement alone.\n\n### Measurement\n\nMeasurement converts quantum information into classical information. It is probabilistic and irreversible: once a qubit in superposition is measured, the superposition is lost and the qubit is left in the state corresponding to the observed result. Because outcomes are probabilistic, quantum programs are typically run many times (\"shots\") and the distribution of results is analysed. Unwanted interaction with the environment acts like an accidental, partial measurement, which is why decoherence is such a serious engineering problem.\n\n### Quantum gates at an intuitive level\n\nQuantum gates are the quantum equivalent of logic gates, with one key difference: they are reversible. A few gates illustrate the idea:\n\n- The **X gate** flips |0⟩ to |1⟩ and vice versa, like a classical NOT.\n- The **Hadamard (H) gate** turns |0⟩ into an equal superposition of |0⟩ and |1⟩. Applying it twice returns the original state, because the amplitudes interfere and cancel. This simple fact demonstrates interference in action.\n- **Phase gates** change the relative sign or phase of amplitudes without changing measurement probabilities directly; phases matter because they decide how amplitudes interfere later.\n- The **CNOT gate** flips a target qubit only if a control qubit is 1. Applied after a Hadamard, it creates entanglement.\n\nA quantum algorithm is therefore a circuit: prepare qubits, apply a carefully designed sequence of gates that creates superposition, entanglement and interference, then measure.\n\n### Error correction and the gap between physical and logical qubits\n\nToday''s physical qubits make errors far more often than classical transistors. Quantum error correction encodes one reliable **logical qubit** in many physical qubits, continuously detecting and correcting errors without directly measuring the protected information. Estimates of how many physical qubits are needed per logical qubit vary with hardware quality and code choice, but the overhead is large. The machines that would threaten RSA require thousands of high-quality logical qubits running very long circuits, which is far beyond present devices. Headline qubit counts therefore say little on their own; error rates, connectivity and the ability to run deep circuits matter at least as much."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### What quantum computers are and are not expected to be good at\n\nA useful managerial framework is to classify problems by the expected quantum benefit.\n\n| Category | Examples | Expected quantum benefit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Strong, proven speed-up | Factoring and discrete logarithms (Shor) | Exponential speed-up over best known classical methods; breaks RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography |\n| Natural fit | Simulating molecules, materials and chemical reactions | Potentially large; quantum systems are naturally described by quantum computers |\n| Modest, proven speed-up | Unstructured search (Grover) | Quadratic: roughly √N steps instead of N |\n| Uncertain, actively researched | Optimisation, some machine learning, finance (portfolio and risk) | Unclear; heuristic algorithms may or may not beat strong classical methods |\n| No expected benefit | Email, web servers, databases, spreadsheets, ERP, most analytics | None; classical computers remain cheaper, faster and more reliable |\n\nTwo further cautions apply. First, loading large classical datasets into a quantum computer is slow, which undermines many proposed \"quantum big data\" applications. Second, classical algorithms keep improving; several early claims of quantum advantage have later been matched by better classical simulation techniques.\n\n### Shor''s algorithm, conceptually\n\nPeter Shor''s algorithm (published in the mid-1990s) factors large integers and computes discrete logarithms efficiently on a fault-tolerant quantum computer. The security of RSA rests on the difficulty of factoring the product of two large primes; Diffie-Hellman and elliptic-curve cryptography rest on the difficulty of discrete logarithms. Shor''s insight was to convert factoring into a problem of finding the period of a repeating mathematical function and then to use the quantum Fourier transform, an interference-based procedure, to reveal that period. Classically, finding the period takes an impractically long time for numbers of cryptographic size; quantumly it becomes feasible. The consequence is stark: once a large enough error-corrected machine exists, today''s public-key encryption and digital signatures can be broken.\n\n### Grover''s algorithm, conceptually\n\nLov Grover''s algorithm searches an unstructured space of `N` possibilities for a marked item in about `√N` steps, compared with about `N/2` on average classically. It works by repeatedly amplifying the amplitude of the correct answer through interference. For cryptography, Grover effectively halves the security level of symmetric keys and hash preimages: brute-forcing a 128-bit key would need on the order of `2^64` quantum steps. The standard response is simply to use longer keys, such as AES-256, which is why symmetric cryptography is considered far less threatened than public-key cryptography.\n\n### The NISQ era\n\nPreskill (2018) argued that near-term devices with tens to hundreds of noisy qubits would be valuable research tools but that their practical business value was uncertain. NISQ devices can run only shallow circuits before noise overwhelms the result. Research focuses on hybrid quantum-classical algorithms, such as variational methods, and on error mitigation. In 2019 Google researchers reported in *Nature* that their Sycamore processor performed a specific sampling task that they estimated would take a classical supercomputer an impractically long time; IBM publicly disputed the size of that gap. The episode illustrates both real progress and the importance of scrutinising \"quantum advantage\" claims, which often concern contrived benchmark tasks rather than business problems.\n\n### Post-quantum cryptography and NIST''s standards\n\nThe threat to public-key cryptography is addressed not by quantum technology but by new classical algorithms based on mathematical problems believed hard for quantum computers too, such as problems on lattices and hash-based constructions. After a multi-year public competition, the US National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) published its first three PQC standards in August 2024:\n\n- **FIPS 203 (ML-KEM)**: a module-lattice-based key-encapsulation mechanism, derived from CRYSTALS-Kyber, for establishing shared secret keys.\n- **FIPS 204 (ML-DSA)**: a module-lattice-based digital signature algorithm, derived from CRYSTALS-Dilithium.\n- **FIPS 205 (SLH-DSA)**: a stateless hash-based digital signature algorithm, derived from SPHINCS+, offering a design based on different mathematical assumptions as a hedge.\n\nThese are US federal standards, but they are widely referenced internationally by vendors, browser developers and regulators. Many organisations are adopting **hybrid** approaches that combine a classical and a post-quantum algorithm during the transition, so that security holds as long as either remains unbroken.\n\n### Harvest now, decrypt later and Mosca''s inequality\n\nAdversaries can record encrypted traffic or steal encrypted archives today and store them until decryption becomes possible. Data with a long confidentiality life, such as health records, land registries, state secrets, intellectual property and long-term financial contracts, is therefore already at risk. The cryptographer Michele Mosca popularised a simple test: if `x` is how long data must remain confidential, `y` is how long migration will take, and `z` is how long until a cryptographically relevant quantum computer exists, then an organisation has a problem whenever `x + y > z`. Because `z` is uncertain and `y` for a large organisation can be many years, the rational response is to start now."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Adwenpa Savings and Loans* (a fictional Ghanaian financial institution) offers mobile banking integrated with mobile money wallets. Its systems use TLS for its app and web services, RSA-based certificates, elliptic-curve signatures on transaction messages and an encrypted archive of customer identity documents that must be retained for many years.\n\nThe IT risk committee asks whether quantum computing is a real concern. Applying Mosca''s inequality, the team estimates that customer identity data must remain confidential for well over a decade (`x`), and that replacing cryptography across core banking, card systems, the mobile app, partner APIs and hardware security modules could take several years (`y`). Even under optimistic assumptions about `z`, the sum is uncomfortable.\n\nThe committee therefore adopts a staged response:\n\n1. **Cryptographic inventory**: catalogue every system, protocol, library, certificate and vendor product that uses public-key cryptography.\n2. **Data classification by confidentiality lifetime**: prioritise long-lived sensitive data.\n3. **Crypto-agility**: require new systems to make algorithms configurable rather than hard-coded.\n4. **Vendor engagement**: ask core-banking, HSM and payment-switch suppliers for their PQC roadmaps aligned with FIPS 203, 204 and 205.\n5. **Pilot hybrid key exchange** on internal services before customer-facing ones.\n6. **Governance**: report progress to the board as part of operational risk, and monitor guidance from the Bank of Ghana and the Cyber Security Authority as it develops.\n\nNotice that the business decision is about risk management and migration, not about buying quantum hardware. A smaller organisation might rely largely on its cloud and software vendors but should still ask the right questions in procurement."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Early adopters of post-quantum protection in messaging\n\nTwo widely reported deployments show how PQC is entering mainstream products. In 2023 the Signal messaging protocol introduced PQXDH, which adds a post-quantum key-encapsulation mechanism to its existing elliptic-curve key agreement. In 2024 Apple announced PQ3 for iMessage, likewise combining classical and post-quantum cryptography. Both designs are hybrid, both explicitly cite the harvest-now-decrypt-later threat as their motivation, and both were introduced years before any quantum computer capable of breaking elliptic-curve cryptography is publicly known to exist.\n\nLessons for managers:\n\n- Leading technology firms treat the quantum threat as a present-day risk to long-lived confidentiality.\n- Hybrid designs reduce the risk that a new, less battle-tested algorithm contains an undiscovered weakness.\n- Migration is easier for organisations that control their own software stack; firms dependent on many vendors must manage the transition through procurement and contracts."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Prepare qubits in |0>\"] --> B[\"Apply gates: superposition (H)\"]\n    B --> C[\"Entangle qubits (CNOT)\"]\n    C --> D[\"Interference: amplify useful answers\"]\n    D --> E[\"Measure: one classical result\"]\n    E --> F[\"Repeat many shots and analyse distribution\"]\n    G[\"Shor''s algorithm\"] --> H[\"Breaks RSA and ECC\"]\n    H --> I[\"Response: migrate to PQC (FIPS 203/204/205)\"]\n    J[\"Grover''s algorithm\"] --> K[\"Weakens symmetric keys\"]\n    K --> L[\"Response: longer keys, e.g. AES-256\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** A quantum computer tries every possible answer in parallel and picks the right one. **Correction:** Measurement returns only one result; speed-ups come from carefully engineered interference, which works only for problems with suitable structure.","**Misconception:** Quantum computers will replace classical computers for business IT. **Correction:** They are specialised accelerators for narrow problem classes and will operate alongside classical systems, typically accessed through the cloud.","**Misconception:** Post-quantum cryptography requires quantum hardware. **Correction:** PQC algorithms are classical algorithms that run on ordinary computers; quantum key distribution is a separate, hardware-based approach.","**Misconception:** Because no large quantum computer exists, there is nothing to do yet. **Correction:** Harvest-now-decrypt-later attacks and long migration times mean that planning must begin now.","**Misconception:** Grover''s algorithm breaks AES. **Correction:** Grover gives only a quadratic speed-up; doubling the key length, for example using AES-256, restores a comfortable security margin.","**Misconception:** More qubits always means a more powerful computer. **Correction:** Error rates, coherence time, connectivity and error correction determine useful capability."],"examMistakes":["Describing superposition as a qubit \"being 0 and 1 simultaneously\" without mentioning amplitudes, probabilities and measurement.","Claiming entanglement allows faster-than-light communication.","Stating that quantum computers break all encryption, rather than distinguishing public-key (badly affected by Shor) from symmetric cryptography (modestly affected by Grover).","Confusing post-quantum cryptography with quantum key distribution.","Giving a business recommendation to \"buy a quantum computer\" when the question concerns cryptographic risk.","Ignoring timescales and uncertainty, or presenting speculative applications as proven."],"criticalQuestions":["Why is \"quantum advantage\" on a benchmark sampling task not the same as business value? What evidence would convince you that a quantum service offered real value to a logistics firm?","Apply Mosca''s inequality to a Ghanaian land registry digitisation project. Which data has the longest confidentiality life, and how should that shape procurement?","Should a mid-sized Ghanaian retailer invest staff time in PQC now, or rely on its cloud and software vendors? Justify your position.","What are the risks of moving too quickly to new post-quantum algorithms, and how do hybrid schemes mitigate them?","How might quantum simulation of molecules change competition in pharmaceuticals, fertilisers or cocoa processing over the long term?"],"exercises":["**Quantum or not?** Classify ten business computing tasks (for example payroll, route optimisation, fraud detection, drug discovery, factoring, inventory search) as \"strong\", \"natural fit\", \"modest\", \"uncertain\" or \"no expected benefit\". *Expected output:* a table with a one-sentence justification per task.","**Cryptographic inventory starter.** For an organisation you know (or an illustrative one), list ten systems that use public-key cryptography and record the protocol, the data protected and its confidentiality lifetime. *Expected output:* a prioritised inventory with the top three migration candidates identified.","**Mosca calculation.** Choose plausible values for `x`, `y` and three scenarios for `z` (early, central, late) for a hospital records system. *Expected output:* a short memo stating in which scenarios action is urgent and why.","**Vendor questionnaire.** Draft six procurement questions about PQC readiness for a payment-switch supplier. *Expected output:* questions referring to crypto-agility, hybrid modes and alignment with FIPS 203, 204 and 205."],"takeaways":["Qubits carry amplitudes; measurement yields one probabilistic result, and algorithms rely on interference.","Entanglement creates correlations essential to quantum algorithms but does not enable faster-than-light signalling.","Quantum computers are specialised; most business workloads will remain classical.","Shor''s algorithm threatens RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography; Grover''s only weakens symmetric keys.","We are in the NISQ era; fault-tolerant machines need many high-quality logical qubits.","NIST''s 2024 standards (FIPS 203, 204, 205) provide the basis for PQC migration, which should begin now because of harvest-now-decrypt-later."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Nielsen, M. A., & Chuang, I. L. (2010). *Quantum computation and quantum information* (10th anniversary ed.). Cambridge University Press.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Preskill, J. (2018). Quantum computing in the NISQ era and beyond. *Quantum, 2*, 79.","url":"https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2018-08-06-79","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Shor, P. W. (1997). Polynomial-time algorithms for prime factorization and discrete logarithms on a quantum computer. *SIAM Journal on Computing, 26*(5), 1484-1509.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Grover, L. K. (1996). A fast quantum mechanical algorithm for database search. In *Proceedings of the 28th Annual ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing* (pp. 212-219). ACM.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Arute, F., et al. (2019). Quantum supremacy using a programmable superconducting processor. *Nature, 574*, 505-510.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"National Institute of Standards and Technology. (2024). *Module-lattice-based key-encapsulation mechanism standard* (FIPS 203). U.S. Department of Commerce.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"NIST Post-Quantum Cryptography Project","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/post-quantum-cryptography","description":"National Institute of Standards and Technology. Official project page with background, standards and presentations on the PQC programme.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"IBM Quantum","url":"https://www.ibm.com/quantum","description":"IBM. Official portal with introductory learning material and explanations of quantum computing concepts.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"MIT OpenCourseWare","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/","description":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology. Open learning portal; search for quantum computation and quantum information lectures.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Qubits are two-level quantum systems described by amplitudes; superposition and entanglement allow quantum algorithms to use interference to amplify useful answers, but measurement returns only one probabilistic classical result. Quantum computers are specialised: strong speed-ups exist for factoring and discrete logarithms (Shor), modest speed-ups for search (Grover), natural applications in simulating quantum systems, and no expected benefit for routine business IT. Current devices are noisy (NISQ). The key business risk is cryptographic: plan migration to PQC now.\n\n- Amplitudes can cancel; probabilities cannot. Interference is the engine.\n- Shor breaks RSA/ECC; Grover halves symmetric key strength, so use AES-256.\n- Logical qubits need many physical qubits; headline counts mislead.\n- NIST FIPS 203 (ML-KEM), 204 (ML-DSA), 205 (SLH-DSA), published August 2024.\n- Harvest now, decrypt later: if x + y > z, act now.\n- Steps: inventory, prioritise, crypto-agility, vendor roadmaps, hybrid pilots, governance."}'::jsonb, 3489, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'hpc-and-advanced-data-processing', 'High-Performance Computing and Advanced Data Processing', 'Explain how parallelism, clusters and GPUs deliver high-performance computing, compare batch and stream processing, and design a data-processing architecture for a business problem.', array['High-performance computing', 'Advanced data processing', 'Stream processing']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Many of the most valuable business analytics problems are simply too large or too fast for a single computer. A national weather forecast, a fraud-detection system that must score every mobile money transaction in milliseconds, a bank''s overnight risk calculation, or the training of a large machine-learning model all require computing power and data-handling techniques beyond the ordinary desktop or single server.\n\nHigh-performance computing (HPC) and advanced data processing address this challenge from two complementary directions. HPC focuses on *computation*: splitting heavy calculations across many processors so they finish quickly. Big data and stream processing focus on *data*: storing, moving and analysing datasets that are very large, arrive continuously or come in many formats. Modern cloud platforms have blurred the boundary between the two, making capabilities once confined to national laboratories available on demand. This lesson explains the core concepts, the main architectural patterns and how a business should decide which approach fits its needs.","objectives":["Explain the principles of parallel computing, including data and task parallelism, and the limits described by Amdahl''s law.","Compare HPC clusters, GPUs and cloud-based HPC as ways of delivering high performance.","Distinguish batch processing from stream processing and describe the characteristics of big data.","Apply a worked speed-up calculation to judge whether parallelising a workload is worthwhile.","Evaluate business use cases and design a suitable data-processing architecture for a Ghanaian organisation."],"prerequisites":["Familiarity with basic computer components (CPU, memory, storage) and with cloud computing models from earlier modules.","A general understanding of databases and analytics.","Comfort with simple arithmetic involving fractions and percentages."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"High-performance computing (HPC)","definition":"The use of aggregated computing power, typically many processors working in parallel, to solve problems far faster than a single machine could."},{"term":"Parallelism","definition":"Executing parts of a computation simultaneously on multiple processing units."},{"term":"Cluster","definition":"A group of interconnected computers (nodes) that work together, usually coordinated by a scheduler and linked by a fast network."},{"term":"GPU (graphics processing unit)","definition":"A processor with thousands of simple cores optimised for performing the same operation on many data elements at once; widely used for machine learning and simulation."},{"term":"Amdahl''s law","definition":"A formula showing that the speed-up from parallelisation is limited by the fraction of a program that must run sequentially."},{"term":"Batch processing","definition":"Processing a large, bounded set of data collected over time, typically on a schedule."},{"term":"Stream processing","definition":"Continuously processing unbounded data as events arrive, producing results with low latency."},{"term":"Data lake","definition":"A repository that stores large volumes of raw data in its native formats for later processing."},{"term":"Event","definition":"A record that something happened at a point in time, such as a payment, a sensor reading or a click."},{"term":"Windowing","definition":"Grouping streaming events into finite chunks (for example, per minute) so that aggregates can be computed."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Why parallelism?\n\nFor decades, processors became faster mainly by increasing clock speed. Physical limits on heat and power ended that trend in the mid-2000s, so performance gains now come chiefly from *more* processing units rather than faster ones. Exploiting many cores, many machines or many GPU threads requires software designed for parallel execution.\n\nTwo broad forms exist. **Data parallelism** applies the same operation to different pieces of data at the same time, for example calculating credit scores for one million customers by giving each of 100 workers 10,000 customers. **Task parallelism** runs different operations at the same time, for example one process cleans data while another trains a model and a third generates reports. Problems that divide into fully independent pieces are called \"embarrassingly parallel\" and scale very well; problems in which pieces must constantly exchange information, such as weather simulation where each grid cell depends on its neighbours, need fast interconnects and careful programming.\n\n### HPC clusters\n\nA traditional HPC system is a cluster of many compute nodes connected by a low-latency, high-bandwidth network, sharing a parallel file system and controlled by a job scheduler. Users submit jobs that request a number of nodes for a period of time. Programs often use the Message Passing Interface (MPI) to coordinate work between nodes. The largest such systems are ranked twice yearly in the TOP500 list using a standard linear-algebra benchmark. Typical HPC workloads include climate and weather modelling, computational fluid dynamics in engineering, genomics, seismic analysis for oil and gas exploration, and financial risk simulation.\n\n### GPUs and accelerators\n\nGPUs were designed to render graphics, which involves the same calculation on millions of pixels. That design suits any workload dominated by large matrix and vector operations, especially training and running neural networks. A GPU contains thousands of simpler cores and very high memory bandwidth, so for suitable workloads it can be dramatically faster and more energy-efficient than a CPU. Other accelerators, including tensor processing units and field-programmable gate arrays, target specific workloads. The business implication is that the cost and availability of accelerators, increasingly accessed through cloud providers, now shapes what AI projects are feasible.\n\n### Cloud HPC\n\nCloud providers offer HPC-style instances, GPU clusters and managed batch services on a pay-per-use basis. This allows an organisation to run a large simulation for a few days without owning a supercomputer. The trade-offs include data transfer time and cost, possible data-sovereignty concerns, and the fact that sustained, continuous workloads can become more expensive in the cloud than on owned infrastructure.\n\n### Big data characteristics\n\nBig data is often described with the \"Vs\": **volume** (scale), **velocity** (speed of arrival), **variety** (structured tables, text, images, logs, sensor data), and frequently **veracity** (quality and trustworthiness) and **value**. Traditional relational databases on a single server struggle when all of these grow together. Distributed approaches therefore store data across many machines and move the computation to the data rather than the data to the computation.\n\n### Batch processing: MapReduce to Spark\n\nGoogle''s MapReduce model, described by Dean and Ghemawat, showed how to process enormous datasets on clusters of commodity machines: a *map* step processes chunks of data independently, and a *reduce* step combines intermediate results. The open-source Hadoop ecosystem popularised the idea. Apache Spark later improved performance for many workloads by keeping intermediate data in memory and offering a unified engine for SQL, machine learning and graph processing. Batch processing suits tasks such as end-of-day reconciliation, monthly customer segmentation and model training on historical data.\n\n### Stream processing\n\nMany decisions lose value if they are made late. Detecting a fraudulent transaction after the money has left, or discovering a failing pump after it has broken, is much less useful than acting immediately. Stream processing treats data as an unbounded sequence of events. A typical architecture has:\n\n1. **Producers** that emit events (payment systems, apps, sensors).\n2. A **durable event log or message broker**, such as Apache Kafka, that stores events in order and lets many consumers read them.\n3. A **stream processing engine**, such as Apache Flink, Spark Structured Streaming or Kafka Streams, that filters, joins, enriches and aggregates events, often within time windows.\n4. **Sinks** that receive results: alerting systems, dashboards, databases or downstream applications.\n\nImportant concepts include the difference between *event time* (when something happened) and *processing time* (when the system saw it), handling late-arriving data, and processing guarantees such as \"at-least-once\" and \"exactly-once\" semantics. The dataflow model described by Akidau and colleagues at Google formalised many of these ideas."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Amdahl''s law\n\nAmdahl''s law states that if a fraction `p` of a program can be parallelised and the remainder `(1 − p)` must run sequentially, the maximum speed-up on `N` processors is:\n\n`Speed-up = 1 / ((1 − p) + p / N)`\n\nAs `N` grows very large, the speed-up approaches `1 / (1 − p)`. The sequential part becomes the bottleneck. Gustafson''s law offers a complementary view: in practice, people often use extra processors to solve *bigger* problems in the same time, so the parallel portion grows with the resources.\n\n### Lambda and Kappa architectures\n\nTwo well-known patterns combine batch and stream processing. The **Lambda architecture** runs a batch layer (accurate, comprehensive, slow) alongside a speed layer (fast, approximate) and merges their outputs in a serving layer. Its weakness is maintaining the same logic in two systems. The **Kappa architecture** treats everything as a stream: historical results are produced by replaying the event log through the same streaming code. Many modern organisations prefer Kappa-style designs or \"lakehouse\" platforms that unify storage for both.\n\n### Choosing an approach\n\n| Need | Suitable approach |\n|---|---|\n| Heavy numerical simulation with tightly coupled calculations | HPC cluster with fast interconnect and MPI |\n| Training or running deep learning models | GPU clusters, on-premises or cloud |\n| Large historical analysis, periodic reports | Batch processing (for example Spark on a data lake) |\n| Decisions within seconds or milliseconds | Stream processing on an event log |\n| Occasional peaks of large computation | Cloud HPC or managed batch services |"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** An analytics team runs a nightly credit-risk simulation that takes 10 hours on one server. Profiling shows that 90% of the run time (`p = 0.9`) consists of independent simulations that can be parallelised, while 10% (data loading, final aggregation) is sequential.\n\nStep 1: Speed-up on 16 servers.\n`Speed-up = 1 / (0.1 + 0.9 / 16) = 1 / (0.1 + 0.05625) = 1 / 0.15625 = 6.4`\nNew run time = `10 / 6.4 ≈ 1.56 hours`.\n\nStep 2: Speed-up on 64 servers.\n`Speed-up = 1 / (0.1 + 0.9 / 64) = 1 / (0.1 + 0.0140625) = 1 / 0.1140625 ≈ 8.77`\nNew run time = `10 / 8.77 ≈ 1.14 hours`.\n\nStep 3: Maximum possible speed-up.\n`1 / (1 − 0.9) = 10`, so the job can never finish in less than 1 hour however many servers are added.\n\nStep 4: Business interpretation. Quadrupling servers from 16 to 64 saves only about 25 minutes. If the business only needs the result before 6 a.m., 16 servers are sufficient; the better investment may be to reduce the sequential portion, for example by parallelising data loading, which would raise the ceiling. This shows why managers should ask \"what is the sequential bottleneck?\" before buying more hardware."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Kente Pay* (a fictional Ghanaian mobile money and payments provider) processes a high volume of wallet transfers, merchant payments and cash-out transactions daily, with sharp peaks around month-end salary payments and festive seasons. It faces three challenges: detecting fraud such as SIM-swap-linked account takeovers in near real time; reconciling transactions with partner banks every night; and producing regulatory reports.\n\nA suitable architecture combines both processing styles:\n\n- Every transaction is published as an event to a replicated event log.\n- A stream processing job enriches each event with customer risk features (recent SIM change, device fingerprint, unusual location, velocity of transfers in the last ten minutes) and scores it with a fraud model. High-risk transactions are held for step-up verification within seconds.\n- The same events are written to a data lake. Nightly batch jobs reconcile with bank records, retrain fraud models on GPU instances, and generate reports for management and regulators.\n- Burst capacity for model retraining is rented from a cloud provider, while customer data handling is designed to respect Ghana''s Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) and any data-residency requirements applicable to the firm.\n\nManagerial issues include the cost of always-on streaming infrastructure, scarcity of engineers skilled in distributed systems, the need for explainable fraud decisions to handle customer complaints, and resilience when connectivity or power is interrupted."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### CERN and the Worldwide LHC Computing Grid\n\nThe Large Hadron Collider at CERN, the European particle physics laboratory near Geneva, generates far more raw data than any single data centre could store or analyse. CERN''s response combines two strategies examined in this lesson. First, real-time \"trigger\" systems filter collision events as they happen, discarding the vast majority and keeping only potentially interesting ones, a form of extreme stream processing. Second, the retained data is distributed for storage and analysis across the Worldwide LHC Computing Grid, a federation of computing centres in many countries organised in tiers, with CERN''s own data centre at the core.\n\nLessons for business:\n\n- Filtering early (at the edge or at ingestion) is often more practical than storing everything.\n- Federated, distributed computing can pool resources owned by many organisations under common standards.\n- Software, data management and governance are as critical as raw hardware.\n- Technologies developed for scientific HPC, such as distributed storage and workload scheduling, often diffuse into business computing."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Producers: apps, payments, sensors\"] --> B[\"Event log (e.g. Kafka)\"]\n    B --> C[\"Stream processing: enrich, score, aggregate\"]\n    C --> D[\"Real-time actions: alerts, holds, dashboards\"]\n    B --> E[\"Data lake storage\"]\n    E --> F[\"Batch processing (e.g. Spark)\"]\n    F --> G[\"Reports, reconciliation\"]\n    F --> H[\"Model training on GPU cluster\"]\n    H --> C\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** Adding more processors always makes a job proportionally faster. **Correction:** Amdahl''s law shows that the sequential portion caps the speed-up; communication overhead can even slow jobs down.","**Misconception:** GPUs are only for graphics and games. **Correction:** GPUs are general-purpose parallel processors that now dominate machine-learning training and many scientific workloads.","**Misconception:** Stream processing makes batch processing obsolete. **Correction:** Batch remains efficient for large historical analysis, model training and periodic reporting; most organisations use both.","**Misconception:** Big data simply means a lot of data. **Correction:** Velocity, variety and veracity often create the real difficulty, and value depends on asking the right questions.","**Misconception:** HPC is only for scientists and governments. **Correction:** Cloud HPC makes high-performance resources available to businesses on demand for risk modelling, design, analytics and AI."],"examMistakes":["Applying Amdahl''s law incorrectly, for example dividing the whole run time by the number of processors.","Describing stream processing without mentioning events, low latency or windowing.","Listing the \"Vs\" of big data without linking them to a business problem.","Recommending a supercomputer when the scenario actually requires real-time stream processing, or vice versa.","Ignoring costs, skills and governance when proposing an architecture."],"criticalQuestions":["When would a Ghanaian bank be better served by owning a GPU cluster rather than renting cloud GPUs? What factors would tip the decision?","A firm claims its fraud system is \"real time\" but scores transactions every 15 minutes. Is that adequate? For which fraud types?","How do event time and processing time differ, and why does the distinction matter when mobile connectivity is intermittent?","Is the Lambda architecture''s duplication of logic a price worth paying for accuracy? When would you choose Kappa instead?","What ethical issues arise when stream processing is used to make automated decisions about customers in milliseconds?"],"exercises":["**Amdahl calculation.** A report-generation job takes 6 hours; 75% is parallelisable. Calculate the run time on 4, 8 and 32 processors and the theoretical maximum speed-up. *Expected output:* a short table of run times and a recommendation on the number of processors.","**Batch or stream?** For eight business tasks (payroll, card fraud detection, monthly churn analysis, warehouse temperature alerts, social media sentiment monitoring, annual audit, ride-hailing pricing, inventory replenishment), decide whether batch, stream or both is appropriate. *Expected output:* a table with a justification referring to latency needs.","**Architecture sketch.** Design a data-processing architecture for a Ghanaian agro-processor that collects moisture and temperature readings from warehouses and wants both alerts and quarterly quality reports. *Expected output:* a labelled diagram and a 200-word explanation."],"takeaways":["Performance growth now comes from parallelism across cores, nodes and GPUs.","Amdahl''s law limits speed-up; find and reduce the sequential bottleneck before buying hardware.","HPC clusters suit tightly coupled simulation; GPUs suit machine learning and vectorised workloads.","Batch processing handles large historical datasets; stream processing handles unbounded events with low latency.","Event logs, windowing and processing guarantees are core stream-processing concepts.","Cloud HPC broadens access, but cost, data transfer, sovereignty and skills must be evaluated."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Dean, J., & Ghemawat, S. (2008). MapReduce: Simplified data processing on large clusters. *Communications of the ACM, 51*(1), 107-113.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Zaharia, M., et al. (2016). Apache Spark: A unified engine for big data processing. *Communications of the ACM, 59*(11), 56-65.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Akidau, T., et al. (2015). The dataflow model: A practical approach to balancing correctness, latency, and cost in massive-scale, unbounded, out-of-order data processing. *Proceedings of the VLDB Endowment, 8*(12), 1792-1803.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Kleppmann, M. (2017). *Designing data-intensive applications*. O''Reilly Media.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"TOP500","url":"https://www.top500.org/","description":"TOP500 project. Official site of the twice-yearly ranking of the world''s most powerful supercomputers, with explanatory material on HPC trends.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Apache Spark","url":"https://spark.apache.org/","description":"Apache Software Foundation. Official project site with documentation and introductory material on large-scale batch and streaming processing.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Apache Kafka","url":"https://kafka.apache.org/","description":"Apache Software Foundation. Official project site explaining event streaming concepts and use cases.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"High-performance computing uses parallelism across many cores, cluster nodes and GPUs to solve heavy computational problems; Amdahl''s law shows that the sequential fraction limits speed-up. Advanced data processing handles volume, velocity and variety through distributed batch processing (MapReduce, Spark) and continuous stream processing (event logs, stream engines, windowing). Most businesses combine batch and stream approaches and increasingly rent HPC and GPU capacity from the cloud.\n\n- Speed-up = 1 / ((1 − p) + p / N); maximum = 1 / (1 − p).\n- Data parallelism versus task parallelism; embarrassingly parallel problems scale best.\n- Clusters + MPI for coupled simulation; GPUs for AI.\n- Batch = bounded, periodic; stream = unbounded, low latency.\n- Lambda (batch + speed layers) versus Kappa (stream only, replay the log).\n- Evaluate cost, skills, data transfer and governance, not just speed."}'::jsonb, 2855, 2, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('75cdfd36-20dc-5712-a9b1-368d619d98bd', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac01', 'mcq', 'understand', 'medium', 'Explain superposition in terms of amplitudes and measurement', 'Which statement most accurately describes a single qubit in superposition?', null, null, '[{"id":"a","text":"It stores both 0 and 1 and returns both values when read"},{"id":"b","text":"It is described by amplitudes that determine the probabilities of obtaining 0 or 1 when measured"},{"id":"c","text":"It is a classical bit whose value is unknown to the programmer"},{"id":"d","text":"It switches rapidly between 0 and 1 many times per second"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('75cdfd36-20dc-5712-a9b1-368d619d98bd', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'A qubit state a|0> + b|1> is described by amplitudes; measurement yields 0 with probability |a|² and 1 with probability |b|². It never returns both values. It is not merely an unknown classical bit, because amplitudes can interfere (cancel or reinforce), which classical uncertainty cannot. Nor is it oscillating between values over time.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('4ea1f1cd-5163-506f-bd84-4883d00d9577', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac02', 'true_false', 'understand', 'medium', 'Explain the limits of entanglement', 'Entanglement allows two parties to send messages to each other instantaneously, faster than light.', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('4ea1f1cd-5163-506f-bd84-4883d00d9577', '{"value":false}'::jsonb, 'Entangled qubits show strong correlations, but each individual measurement outcome is random, so neither party can choose what the other observes. Without a classical communication channel no information is transferred, so entanglement cannot be used for faster-than-light signalling.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('79d78ee0-015f-5802-9332-87b77cac1d62', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac03', 'matching', 'recall', 'easy', 'Match NIST post-quantum standards to their functions', 'Match each NIST standard or algorithm to its description.', null, null, '{"left":[{"id":"l1","text":"FIPS 203 (ML-KEM)"},{"id":"l2","text":"FIPS 204 (ML-DSA)"},{"id":"l3","text":"FIPS 205 (SLH-DSA)"},{"id":"l4","text":"Shor''s algorithm"},{"id":"l5","text":"Grover''s algorithm"}],"right":[{"id":"r5","text":"Quantum search giving a quadratic speed-up against symmetric keys"},{"id":"r1","text":"Lattice-based key-encapsulation mechanism for establishing shared keys"},{"id":"r2","text":"Lattice-based digital signature algorithm"},{"id":"r4","text":"Quantum algorithm that threatens RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography"},{"id":"r3","text":"Stateless hash-based digital signature algorithm"}]}'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('79d78ee0-015f-5802-9332-87b77cac1d62', '{"pairs":{"l1":"r1","l2":"r2","l3":"r3","l4":"r4","l5":"r5"}}'::jsonb, 'NIST published FIPS 203, 204 and 205 in August 2024. ML-KEM (from CRYSTALS-Kyber) establishes keys; ML-DSA (from CRYSTALS-Dilithium) and SLH-DSA (from SPHINCS+) provide signatures, the latter based on hash functions as a hedge against weaknesses in lattice assumptions. Shor and Grover are the quantum algorithms that motivate the migration.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('92c0dc7f-f6b2-5bab-be67-b5f0ae41e731', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac04', 'scenario', 'apply', 'medium', 'Apply Grover''s algorithm implications to symmetric cryptography', 'What should the insurer do about Grover''s algorithm specifically?', 'A Ghanaian insurer encrypts its stored claim files with AES-128 and protects key exchange for its customer portal with RSA-2048. Its CISO asks which change most directly addresses the threat from Grover''s algorithm.', null, '[{"id":"a","text":"Replace AES with RSA-4096 for file encryption"},{"id":"b","text":"Move from AES-128 to AES-256 for stored data"},{"id":"c","text":"Stop encrypting stored data until quantum-safe hardware is available"},{"id":"d","text":"Do nothing, because Grover''s algorithm breaks all symmetric ciphers regardless of key length"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('92c0dc7f-f6b2-5bab-be67-b5f0ae41e731', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'Grover gives a quadratic speed-up, roughly halving the effective security of a symmetric key, so moving to AES-256 restores a large margin. RSA is a public-key scheme threatened by Shor, so switching to it would make matters worse. Stopping encryption is reckless. Grover does not break symmetric ciphers regardless of key length; longer keys counter it. The RSA key exchange needs a separate PQC migration.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('8259f481-a1ab-5930-934f-4d50f68b6037', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac05', 'short_answer', 'recall', 'easy', 'Identify the term for the current era of noisy quantum devices', 'What acronym, introduced by John Preskill in 2018, describes today''s era of noisy quantum devices without full error correction?', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('8259f481-a1ab-5930-934f-4d50f68b6037', '{"answers":["NISQ","noisy intermediate-scale quantum"]}'::jsonb, 'NISQ stands for Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum. Preskill used it to describe devices with tens to hundreds (now sometimes thousands) of noisy qubits that can run only shallow circuits; their practical business value remains uncertain.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('ae795994-b2c0-535b-8d67-3df57604adaf', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac06', 'scenario', 'evaluate', 'hard', 'Evaluate urgency of PQC migration using Mosca''s inequality', 'Which conclusion is best supported by Mosca''s inequality (x + y > z)?', 'Illustrative scenario. A land administration agency must keep digitised title records confidential for 25 years. Its consultants estimate that replacing public-key cryptography across all systems and vendors will take 7 years. Expert opinion on when a cryptographically relevant quantum computer might exist ranges widely, with some estimates within 15 to 20 years.', null, '[{"id":"a","text":"No action is needed until a quantum computer is publicly demonstrated"},{"id":"b","text":"Action is urgent, because x + y equals 32 years, which exceeds plausible values of z"},{"id":"c","text":"Only the symmetric encryption needs upgrading, so migration can wait"},{"id":"d","text":"The agency should buy a quantum computer to protect its records"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('ae795994-b2c0-535b-8d67-3df57604adaf', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'With x = 25 and y = 7, x + y = 32 years, exceeding even generous estimates for z, so data encrypted today could be harvested and decrypted while still sensitive. Waiting for a public demonstration ignores harvest-now-decrypt-later. Upgrading only symmetric encryption leaves public-key key exchange exposed to Shor. PQC runs on classical computers, so buying quantum hardware is irrelevant.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('dc8b230f-73a0-58d3-ad15-7b839dd045e2', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac07', 'mcq', 'analyse', 'hard', 'Distinguish problem types with expected quantum advantage', 'Which business workload is LEAST likely to benefit from quantum computing in the foreseeable future?', null, null, '[{"id":"a","text":"Simulating a new catalyst molecule for fertiliser production"},{"id":"b","text":"Running a supermarket''s point-of-sale and ERP transaction processing"},{"id":"c","text":"Factoring large integers used in RSA keys"},{"id":"d","text":"Researching heuristic algorithms for complex optimisation"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('dc8b230f-73a0-58d3-ad15-7b839dd045e2', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'Routine transaction processing involves large data movement and simple logic that classical computers do cheaply and reliably; there is no known quantum speed-up. Molecular simulation is a natural fit for quantum computers, factoring has a proven exponential speed-up via Shor, and optimisation is an active, if uncertain, research area.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('d5e5ccd1-7d35-549a-b2d1-43570e9dd995', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac08', 'scenario', 'apply', 'medium', 'Apply Amdahl''s law to estimate speed-up', 'Using Amdahl''s law, what is the approximate run time on 8 servers?', 'A bank''s overnight risk job runs for 8 hours on one server. Profiling shows 80% of the work is parallelisable and 20% is sequential.', null, '[{"id":"a","text":"1 hour"},{"id":"b","text":"About 2.4 hours"},{"id":"c","text":"About 4 hours"},{"id":"d","text":"About 6.4 hours"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('d5e5ccd1-7d35-549a-b2d1-43570e9dd995', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'Speed-up = 1 / (0.2 + 0.8/8) = 1 / (0.2 + 0.1) = 1 / 0.3 ≈ 3.33, so run time ≈ 8 / 3.33 ≈ 2.4 hours. One hour wrongly assumes a perfect eightfold speed-up, ignoring the sequential 20%. Four hours and 6.4 hours misapply the formula. Note the ceiling is 1 / 0.2 = 5, so the job can never run in less than 1.6 hours.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('8328bcae-6a7a-5072-8d04-6523ae8b0103', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac09', 'true_false', 'understand', 'easy', 'Explain the role of batch processing alongside streaming', 'Once an organisation adopts stream processing, batch processing becomes unnecessary.', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('8328bcae-6a7a-5072-8d04-6523ae8b0103', '{"value":false}'::jsonb, 'Stream processing suits low-latency decisions on unbounded events, but batch processing remains efficient for large historical analysis, model training, reconciliation and periodic reporting. Most organisations run both, sometimes on unified platforms.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('e002561f-59ce-5dee-b423-28951b59f632', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac10', 'matching', 'understand', 'medium', 'Match workloads to appropriate processing approaches', 'Match each workload to the most suitable processing approach.', null, null, '{"left":[{"id":"l1","text":"Tightly coupled weather simulation"},{"id":"l2","text":"Training a deep neural network"},{"id":"l3","text":"Scoring each card payment for fraud within milliseconds"},{"id":"l4","text":"Monthly customer segmentation on historical data"},{"id":"l5","text":"Occasional large simulation once a quarter"}],"right":[{"id":"r5","text":"Rented cloud HPC capacity"},{"id":"r2","text":"GPU cluster"},{"id":"r4","text":"Batch processing on a data lake"},{"id":"r3","text":"Stream processing on an event log"},{"id":"r1","text":"HPC cluster with fast interconnect and MPI"}]}'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('e002561f-59ce-5dee-b423-28951b59f632', '{"pairs":{"l1":"r1","l2":"r2","l3":"r3","l4":"r4","l5":"r5"}}'::jsonb, 'Coupled simulations need low-latency communication between nodes; neural networks are dominated by matrix operations suited to GPUs; millisecond fraud scoring requires streaming; historical segmentation is a bounded batch job; and infrequent peaks are cheaper to rent than to own.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('3afc0588-f78b-5189-881a-7e44e23d0e55', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac11', 'short_answer', 'recall', 'easy', 'Identify the term for grouping streaming events into finite chunks', 'What term describes grouping unbounded streaming events into finite time-based chunks so that aggregates such as counts per minute can be computed?', null, null, null, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('3afc0588-f78b-5189-881a-7e44e23d0e55', '{"answers":["windowing","windows","time windowing"]}'::jsonb, 'Windowing (tumbling, sliding or session windows) turns an infinite stream into finite groups so that totals, averages and counts can be calculated, for example transfers per account in the last ten minutes.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('66a93060-9bca-57fd-bda7-ae24b5da6b55', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac12', 'mcq', 'analyse', 'hard', 'Analyse the trade-off between Lambda and Kappa architectures', 'What is the main weakness of the Lambda architecture that the Kappa architecture seeks to remove?', null, null, '[{"id":"a","text":"Lambda cannot process historical data"},{"id":"b","text":"Lambda requires maintaining equivalent processing logic in separate batch and speed layers"},{"id":"c","text":"Lambda only works with relational databases"},{"id":"d","text":"Lambda does not support real-time results"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('66a93060-9bca-57fd-bda7-ae24b5da6b55', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'Lambda runs a batch layer and a speed layer in parallel, so the same business logic must be implemented and kept consistent in two systems. Kappa treats everything as a stream and recomputes history by replaying the event log through one code base. Lambda does process history (its batch layer), does give real-time results (its speed layer) and is not tied to relational databases.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('03d6d9a3-0bd1-566f-acca-cd9844410cef', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac13', 'scenario', 'evaluate', 'hard', 'Evaluate a hardware investment using Amdahl''s law', 'Which response best evaluates the vendor''s claim?', 'Illustrative scenario. A Ghanaian analytics firm''s reporting job is 95% parallelisable. On 20 servers it already finishes well before its deadline. A vendor proposes upgrading to 100 servers "to make it five times faster".', null, '[{"id":"a","text":"Accept, because five times as many servers gives five times the speed"},{"id":"b","text":"Reject the claim, because Amdahl''s law caps the speed-up at 20 and the extra servers add little business value when the deadline is already met"},{"id":"c","text":"Accept, because the sequential part disappears with more servers"},{"id":"d","text":"Reject, because parallel computing never improves performance"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('03d6d9a3-0bd1-566f-acca-cd9844410cef', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'Speed-up on 20 servers = 1/(0.05 + 0.95/20) ≈ 10.3; on 100 servers ≈ 1/(0.05 + 0.0095) ≈ 16.8, so the job becomes about 1.6 times faster, not five, and the ceiling is 1/0.05 = 20. Since the deadline is already met, the business value is low. Speed does not scale linearly, the sequential part never disappears, and parallelism clearly does help up to the ceiling.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('d495c58a-f288-5e9d-9fad-f212683363d1', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac14', 'mcq', 'understand', 'medium', 'Explain why GPUs suit machine learning', 'Why are GPUs particularly well suited to training neural networks?', null, null, '[{"id":"a","text":"They have a higher clock speed than any CPU"},{"id":"b","text":"They contain thousands of simpler cores and high memory bandwidth that perform the same operation on many data elements in parallel"},{"id":"c","text":"They store more data than hard disks"},{"id":"d","text":"They eliminate the need for training data"}]'::jsonb, 1, '{}'::text[], '[]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('d495c58a-f288-5e9d-9fad-f212683363d1', '{"option":"b"}'::jsonb, 'Neural network training is dominated by large matrix and vector multiplications that can be executed in parallel; GPUs are designed for exactly this data-parallel pattern. Their advantage is not clock speed or storage capacity, and they certainly do not remove the need for data.', null)
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('5d4dd51e-45a6-53d6-a0a8-abeed6181134', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', null, 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac15', 'case_analysis', 'evaluate', 'hard', 'Evaluate a post-quantum migration and data-processing strategy for a financial firm', 'Critically evaluate the firm''s position and recommend a prioritised plan covering both its post-quantum cryptography risk and its data-processing architecture.', null, 'Illustrative scenario. Sika Mobile Finance is a fictional Ghanaian fintech offering wallets, micro-loans and merchant payments. Its systems use RSA certificates for its APIs, elliptic-curve signatures on transactions, and a nightly batch job that scores loan applications and detects fraud using the previous day''s data. Fraud losses from account takeovers have risen, and the board has read headlines claiming "quantum computers will break all encryption next year". A vendor offers a "quantum-powered fraud engine" subscription. The CTO has a limited budget and a small engineering team.', null, 20, array['Separate hype from evidence about quantum computing and the vendor offer', 'Assess cryptographic exposure using Shor, Grover and harvest-now-decrypt-later', 'Propose PQC migration steps aligned with NIST FIPS 203, 204 and 205', 'Diagnose the fraud problem as a latency issue and propose stream processing', 'Prioritise actions given budget and skills, with risks and governance']::text[], '[{"criterion":"Accurate quantum concepts and critique of hype","marks":5,"descriptor":"Correctly explains what quantum computers can and cannot do, challenges the \"next year\" claim and the vendor offer with reasoned argument"},{"criterion":"Cryptographic risk analysis","marks":5,"descriptor":"Distinguishes public-key from symmetric exposure, applies harvest-now-decrypt-later and Mosca''s inequality to the firm''s data"},{"criterion":"PQC migration plan","marks":4,"descriptor":"Inventory, crypto-agility, vendor engagement, hybrid pilots, reference to NIST standards"},{"criterion":"Data-processing architecture","marks":4,"descriptor":"Recognises nightly batch as the cause of late detection and proposes an event-log and stream-scoring design while retaining batch for training and reporting"},{"criterion":"Prioritisation and justification","marks":2,"descriptor":"Realistic sequencing given budget and team, with clear justification"}]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('5d4dd51e-45a6-53d6-a0a8-abeed6181134', '{}'::jsonb, 'Examiners look for accurate quantum concepts, rejection of hype with reasons, correct distinction between Shor and Grover impacts, a credible PQC plan referencing NIST standards, and recognition that the fraud problem is solved by stream processing rather than quantum technology.', 'A strong answer first deflates the hype. No publicly known quantum computer can break RSA or elliptic-curve cryptography; that requires thousands of error-corrected logical qubits, far beyond today''s NISQ devices. The claim of "next year" is unsupported. The "quantum-powered fraud engine" should be challenged: fraud detection is not a problem with a demonstrated quantum advantage, and the firm''s real fraud issue is latency.

The cryptographic risk is nonetheless real over the longer term. Shor''s algorithm would break RSA certificates and elliptic-curve signatures; Grover only weakens symmetric keys, addressed by AES-256. Because customer identity and loan data must remain confidential for years, harvest-now-decrypt-later means traffic captured today could be decrypted later. Applying Mosca''s inequality, a long confidentiality period plus a multi-year migration may exceed the time to a capable machine. The firm should build a cryptographic inventory, demand crypto-agility and PQC roadmaps from vendors, plan for ML-KEM (FIPS 203) for key establishment and ML-DSA or SLH-DSA (FIPS 204/205) for signatures, and pilot hybrid modes, largely through its cloud and library providers given its small team.

On fraud, a nightly batch means account takeovers are detected a day late. Publishing each transaction to an event log and scoring it in a stream processor using velocity, SIM-change and device features would allow holds within seconds; batch would remain for model retraining and regulatory reporting, possibly on rented cloud GPUs. Managed cloud streaming services reduce the skills burden.

Priority: stream-based fraud detection first (immediate losses), then PQC inventory and vendor requirements (low cost, long lead time), with board reporting on both.')
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.quiz_questions (id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, external_ref, type, bloom, difficulty, objective, prompt, scenario, case_text, options, marks, answer_structure, rubric, source, is_published) values ('cc1fbf60-ac3e-52c4-9fcf-b95d08f74858', 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', null, 'csit-621/advanced-computing/ac16', 'essay', 'evaluate', 'hard', 'Evaluate the business relevance of quantum and high-performance computing', '"For most businesses in Ghana, quantum computing is irrelevant, but high-performance computing is essential." Critically discuss this statement.', null, null, null, 15, array['Define quantum computing and HPC and their maturity', 'Identify where quantum is irrelevant and where it is relevant (notably cryptography)', 'Discuss HPC, GPUs, batch and stream processing as accessible through the cloud', 'Consider constraints such as cost, skills, power and connectivity', 'Reach a nuanced conclusion']::text[], '[{"criterion":"Conceptual accuracy","marks":4,"descriptor":"Correct explanation of quantum computing capabilities and HPC concepts"},{"criterion":"Critical evaluation of the statement","marks":5,"descriptor":"Shows that quantum hardware is largely irrelevant for operations but quantum risk to cryptography is relevant now; questions whether HPC is \"essential\" for all"},{"criterion":"Ghanaian context and examples","marks":4,"descriptor":"Uses relevant illustrative or documented examples such as mobile money, banking, agriculture and weather"},{"criterion":"Structure and conclusion","marks":2,"descriptor":"Coherent argument ending in a justified, balanced conclusion"}]'::jsonb, 'ai_practice', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, type = excluded.type, bloom = excluded.bloom, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objective = excluded.objective, prompt = excluded.prompt, scenario = excluded.scenario, case_text = excluded.case_text, options = excluded.options, marks = excluded.marks, answer_structure = excluded.answer_structure, rubric = excluded.rubric, source = excluded.source, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct, explanation, model_answer) values ('cc1fbf60-ac3e-52c4-9fcf-b95d08f74858', '{}'::jsonb, 'Examiners reward nuance, rejecting both extremes, accurate concepts, explicit recognition of the cryptographic dimension of quantum risk, and grounded Ghanaian examples.', 'A good essay agrees in part. Quantum computers are specialised machines in the NISQ era with no demonstrated advantage for routine business workloads, so very few Ghanaian firms should invest in quantum hardware or services today. However, calling quantum "irrelevant" is wrong in one crucial respect: Shor''s algorithm threatens RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography, and harvest-now-decrypt-later attacks mean that banks, mobile money operators, insurers, hospitals and government agencies with long-lived confidential data must begin PQC planning now, guided by NIST''s FIPS 203, 204 and 205. This is a governance and procurement task rather than a technology purchase.

The claim that HPC is "essential" also needs nuance. Few Ghanaian SMEs need supercomputers, but many need capabilities derived from HPC: GPU-based AI services, distributed batch analytics, and stream processing for fraud detection and IoT monitoring. Cloud provision has made these accessible on a pay-per-use basis, although cost, foreign-exchange exposure, data transfer, data-protection obligations under Act 843, skills shortages and power reliability shape what is feasible. Examples include near-real-time fraud scoring in mobile money, crop and weather modelling for agriculture, and risk calculation in banking.

A balanced conclusion is that quantum hardware is largely irrelevant for operations but quantum risk is relevant now, while HPC-derived capabilities are increasingly important but should be adopted selectively according to latency, scale and value.')
  on conflict (question_id) do update set correct = excluded.correct, explanation = excluded.explanation, model_answer = excluded.model_answer;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('f1cad828-6695-5192-beb9-43012cc1c1d9', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'Why is "a qubit is both 0 and 1 at once" an incomplete description of superposition?', 'A qubit is described by amplitudes, which can be negative or complex and can interfere. Measurement returns only one result, with probabilities given by the squared magnitudes of the amplitudes. The power comes from interference, not from reading out both values.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/744ddafd9e51', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('8265a7c2-bf2c-53aa-9f45-eb454d13c664', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'What does entanglement provide, and what does it NOT provide?', 'It provides correlations between qubits that cannot be described qubit by qubit, a resource for algorithms, error correction and quantum communication. It does not allow faster-than-light messaging, because individual outcomes are random.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/7bdfa309d141', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('e1aa02e8-e486-594f-925c-6cb709c60bcb', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'Contrast the cryptographic impact of Shor''s and Grover''s algorithms.', 'Shor gives an exponential speed-up for factoring and discrete logarithms, breaking RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography. Grover gives only a quadratic speed-up for search, roughly halving symmetric key strength, which is countered by longer keys such as AES-256.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/73514c86fed1', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('e552e670-ed95-527f-8c97-5e10a9c0433b', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'What are FIPS 203, 204 and 205?', 'NIST''s first post-quantum cryptography standards (August 2024), ML-KEM for key encapsulation, ML-DSA for lattice-based signatures and SLH-DSA for hash-based signatures. They are classical algorithms that run on ordinary computers.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/3bfba1b80ad4', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('b7712073-8886-56bb-9863-04c3c3360938', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'State Mosca''s inequality and its business meaning.', 'If x (how long data must stay secret) + y (migration time) > z (time until a cryptographically relevant quantum computer), the organisation is already exposed, because of harvest-now-decrypt-later attacks.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/704473e64db0', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('f4e79276-f743-50de-ac98-aaed5fc12d63', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', '13ce58a9-2059-5958-a62f-b051da0df9b5', 'Why do headline qubit counts mislead decision-makers?', 'Useful capability depends on error rates, coherence, connectivity and error correction. Many noisy physical qubits are needed per reliable logical qubit; breaking RSA needs thousands of logical qubits running deep circuits.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/4f7e644416ec', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('3c75fa61-ab94-5c57-b085-65714e637cd3', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'State Amdahl''s law and its key managerial lesson.', 'Speed-up = 1 / ((1 − p) + p / N). The sequential fraction caps speed-up at 1 / (1 − p), so reduce the bottleneck before buying more processors.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/0bae369d1726', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('c105e3ca-d43d-5b2f-92b9-46ecd96f9b18', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'Distinguish data parallelism from task parallelism.', 'Data parallelism applies the same operation to different chunks of data simultaneously; task parallelism runs different operations at the same time. Independent ("embarrassingly parallel") work scales best.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/e7d6a11a4d8f', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('1641a74b-682d-558b-80b9-fcd6a5a60aaa', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'When is stream processing preferable to batch processing?', 'When value depends on acting within seconds or milliseconds on continuous, unbounded events, fraud detection, sensor alerts, dynamic pricing. Batch suits bounded historical analysis, training and periodic reports.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/2c515fd635bb', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('c358a59d-2c98-5521-853c-298b9feef047', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'What are the four main components of a streaming architecture?', 'Producers that emit events; a durable event log or broker (e.g. Kafka); a stream processing engine that enriches, aggregates and windows events; and sinks such as alerts, dashboards or databases.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/319aa8813f36', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('46623f43-c210-5452-81b4-317e2886831c', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'Why does the difference between event time and processing time matter?', 'Events may arrive late or out of order (for example with intermittent mobile connectivity). Aggregates based on processing time can then be wrong; robust systems compute by event time and handle late data.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/56c4314cc70f', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.flashcards (id, user_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, front, back, source, external_ref, is_published) values ('4e150801-c976-534f-9fa8-ad369787a23b', null, 'efc99e8b-9466-5435-a078-ba0143f3c2ea', 'c11f0aba-216b-503a-8021-5fb06099a4db', 'ec7a1d28-8483-54f3-bfe2-138c141c55e2', 'Compare the Lambda and Kappa architectures.', 'Lambda runs parallel batch and speed layers and merges results, at the cost of duplicated logic. Kappa uses a single streaming path and recomputes history by replaying the event log.', 'supplementary', 'csit-621/advanced-computing/9dbc609a654e', true)
  on conflict (id) do update set user_id = excluded.user_id, course_id = excluded.course_id, module_id = excluded.module_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, front = excluded.front, back = excluded.back, source = excluded.source, external_ref = excluded.external_ref, is_published = excluded.is_published;
