insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('968a94fa-ddca-5ead-8cf2-728efbb32192', '5b3b4db5-3611-52d0-8ee3-a393924d5603', 'green-computing-and-energy-efficiency', 'Green Computing and Energy Efficiency', 'Explain the principles of green computing, calculate and interpret Power Usage Effectiveness, recommend energy-efficiency measures for data centres and devices, and evaluate e-waste management with reference to Ghana.', array['Green computing', 'Energy efficiency', 'PUE', 'E-waste']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Information technology is often presented as weightless: data \"in the cloud\", services delivered \"virtually\". In reality every email, transaction and AI query runs on physical equipment that consumes electricity, requires cooling, is manufactured from mined materials and eventually becomes waste. As digital services grow, so does this physical footprint. Data centres, networks and billions of end-user devices together form a significant and growing source of electricity demand and material use.\n\nGreen computing is the discipline of designing, using and disposing of IT in ways that minimise environmental harm while still delivering business value. It is also good management: energy is a major operating cost for data centres, particularly in Ghana where electricity tariffs are high, supply interruptions push organisations towards diesel generators, and a hot, humid climate increases cooling loads. This lesson explains the principles of green computing, the most widely used data centre efficiency metric, Power Usage Effectiveness (PUE), practical efficiency measures, and the challenge of electronic waste, including a careful account of Accra''s Agbogbloshie site.","objectives":["Explain the scope of green computing across the IT lifecycle: design, manufacture, use and disposal.","Calculate Power Usage Effectiveness and interpret what it does and does not measure.","Recommend energy-efficiency measures for data centres, networks and end-user devices.","Analyse the environmental, health and social dimensions of e-waste, including informal recycling at Agbogbloshie.","Evaluate a green IT improvement plan for a Ghanaian organisation in financial and environmental terms."],"prerequisites":["Familiarity with data centres, servers and cloud computing from earlier modules.","Basic arithmetic with kilowatts (kW) and kilowatt-hours (kWh)."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Green computing (green IT)","definition":"The environmentally responsible design, manufacture, use and disposal of computing resources."},{"term":"Power Usage Effectiveness (PUE)","definition":"The ratio of total energy used by a data centre facility to the energy delivered to its IT equipment; introduced by The Green Grid and standardised as ISO/IEC 30134-2."},{"term":"IT equipment energy","definition":"Energy consumed by servers, storage and network equipment that perform computing work."},{"term":"Facility overhead","definition":"Energy used by cooling, power conversion and distribution losses, lighting and other non-IT systems."},{"term":"Virtualisation","definition":"Running multiple virtual machines on one physical server so that hardware is used more fully and fewer servers are needed."},{"term":"Free cooling","definition":"Using outside air or water, rather than energy-intensive mechanical refrigeration, to remove heat when conditions allow."},{"term":"E-waste (WEEE)","definition":"Discarded electrical and electronic equipment, which contains both valuable materials and hazardous substances."},{"term":"Embodied emissions","definition":"Greenhouse gas emissions generated in manufacturing and transporting a product before it is used."},{"term":"Informal recycling","definition":"Recovery of materials outside regulated facilities, often using hazardous methods such as open burning or acid leaching."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### The scope of green computing\n\nMurugesan (2008) described a holistic approach to green IT spanning four areas: **green use** (reducing energy consumption of computers and systems), **green disposal** (reuse, refurbishment and proper recycling), **green design** (energy-efficient and environmentally sound components and systems) and **green manufacturing** (minimising waste and pollution in production). This lifecycle view matters because a focus only on electricity use misses the substantial environmental impact of manufacturing devices, especially short-lived ones such as smartphones and laptops, where embodied emissions can be a large share of lifetime impact.\n\n### Where IT energy goes\n\nEnergy use in IT falls into three broad places:\n\n1. **Data centres**: servers, storage and networking, plus cooling and power infrastructure. Large cloud data centres are usually far more efficient per unit of computing than small server rooms, because of scale, specialised cooling and high utilisation.\n2. **Networks**: mobile base stations, fibre networks and core routers. In Ghana, many telecom towers in areas with unreliable grid power rely on diesel generators, making network energy both costly and carbon-intensive.\n3. **End-user devices**: PCs, laptops, phones, printers and displays. Individually small, they are numerous, and their manufacture has a large footprint.\n\n### Inside a data centre: IT load and overhead\n\nServers convert almost all the electricity they consume into heat, which must be removed. Cooling systems (chillers, computer room air conditioners, fans and pumps), uninterruptible power supplies (UPS), transformers and lighting all consume energy without doing computing work. The ratio between total facility energy and IT energy is therefore a key indicator of facility efficiency.\n\n### Energy-efficiency measures\n\n**IT layer**\n\n- **Virtualisation and consolidation**: many traditional servers run at low utilisation while still drawing a large share of their peak power. Consolidating workloads onto fewer, well-utilised hosts cuts both IT and cooling energy.\n- **Retiring \"zombie\" servers**: decommissioning servers that are powered on but no longer perform useful work.\n- **Efficient hardware**: newer processors and storage typically deliver more work per watt; solid-state storage reduces power for many workloads.\n- **Power management**: enabling processor power states and scheduling non-urgent batch work at times when renewable energy or cheaper power is available.\n- **Efficient software**: well-designed code, appropriate data retention and avoiding unnecessary computation reduce demand at source.\n\n**Facility layer**\n\n- **Hot-aisle/cold-aisle containment**: arranging racks so that cold supply air and hot exhaust air do not mix.\n- **Raising temperature set points** within equipment manufacturers'' and industry guidance ranges, reducing chiller work.\n- **Free cooling and economisers** where climate allows; in hot, humid climates such as southern Ghana opportunities are more limited, so efficient chillers, liquid cooling and careful airflow management matter more.\n- **Efficient power infrastructure**: modern UPS systems with high efficiency at typical loads, and minimising conversion steps.\n- **On-site renewables and storage**: solar photovoltaic panels with batteries can reduce diesel generator use during outages.\n\n**Organisational layer**\n\n- **Cloud migration** can reduce energy if workloads move from inefficient server rooms to efficient hyperscale facilities, but only if the cloud resources are right-sized and switched off when not needed.\n- **Procurement standards** specifying energy efficiency, repairability and take-back.\n- **Device lifecycle extension**: keeping laptops and phones in service longer through repair and refurbishment reduces embodied emissions.\n\n### Electronic waste\n\nE-waste is one of the fastest-growing waste streams globally. It contains valuable materials (copper, aluminium, gold, palladium) and hazardous substances (lead, mercury, cadmium, brominated flame retardants). When recycled properly in regulated facilities, e-waste can be a source of secondary raw materials, an idea often called \"urban mining\". When handled informally, it can expose workers and communities to toxic substances.\n\nGhana''s Parliament passed the **Hazardous and Electronic Waste Control and Management Act, 2016 (Act 917)**, which provides for the control and management of hazardous and electronic waste and introduced an advance eco-levy on imported electrical and electronic equipment intended to fund proper management. Implementation and enforcement have faced practical challenges, as in many countries.\n\n### Agbogbloshie: an accurate and careful account\n\nAgbogbloshie, in central Accra near the Korle Lagoon and the Odaw River, was for many years the site of a large informal scrap and recycling area as well as a food market and residential settlement. Workers there dismantled discarded vehicles, appliances and electronic equipment to recover metals for sale. Some practices, notably the **open burning of cables and plastic casings to recover copper**, released smoke containing hazardous substances, and researchers documented contamination of soil and health concerns among workers and nearby residents.\n\nSeveral points require care when discussing the site:\n\n- It was frequently described in international media as \"the world''s largest e-waste dump\". Researchers working in Ghana have challenged this framing, noting that the site was primarily a scrap metal recycling and trading hub, that much of the material processed was generated within Ghana and the wider region from used equipment at the end of its life, and that the label obscured the site''s economic and social complexity.\n- Thousands of people, many of them migrants from northern Ghana, earned livelihoods there, and the informal sector played a real role in recovering materials that would otherwise have been dumped.\n- The imported second-hand electronics trade is complex: much of it consisted of functioning or repairable equipment meeting real demand, though some was close to the end of its life on arrival.\n- In July 2021 the authorities demolished the scrapyard; recycling activity dispersed to other locations, so the underlying challenge of safe e-waste management was not eliminated.\n\nThe managerial lesson is that responsible e-waste management requires collection systems, safe recycling technology, decent work for informal workers, enforcement against dumping, and producer responsibility, not merely the removal of a visible site."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### Power Usage Effectiveness\n\nThe Green Grid introduced PUE as a simple metric of data centre infrastructure efficiency:\n\n`PUE = Total facility energy / IT equipment energy`\n\nA PUE of 1.0 would mean all energy goes to IT equipment, with zero overhead, which is a theoretical ideal. A PUE of 2.0 means that for every kilowatt-hour used by IT, another kilowatt-hour is used by cooling and power infrastructure. The reciprocal, sometimes called Data Centre infrastructure Efficiency (DCiE), expresses IT energy as a percentage of total energy.\n\n**What PUE does not measure.** PUE says nothing about whether IT equipment does useful work: a facility full of idle \"zombie\" servers can still have a good PUE. Indeed, virtualising and removing servers can *worsen* PUE while cutting total energy, because the fixed overhead is spread over less IT load. PUE also ignores the carbon intensity of the electricity and water consumption, which is why The Green Grid also defined companion metrics such as Carbon Usage Effectiveness (CUE) and Water Usage Effectiveness (WUE). PUE should be measured over a full year, because cooling demand varies with weather.\n\n### The lifecycle view\n\n| Lifecycle stage | Main impacts | Green IT responses |\n|---|---|---|\n| Design and manufacture | Mining, energy and chemicals; embodied emissions | Efficient designs, recycled materials, modularity |\n| Distribution | Transport emissions, packaging | Consolidated shipping, reduced packaging |\n| Use | Electricity, cooling, water | Efficiency, virtualisation, renewables |\n| End of life | Hazardous substances, lost materials | Reuse, refurbishment, certified recycling, take-back |"},{"heading":"Worked Example","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Densu Data Services* (a fictional Accra colocation provider) meters its facility over a year. The IT equipment draws an average of 400 kW, and total facility load averages 720 kW.\n\nStep 1: Current PUE.\n`PUE = 720 / 400 = 1.8`\nFor every 1 kWh delivered to IT, a further 0.8 kWh is used by overhead.\n\nStep 2: Annual energy (8,760 hours in a year).\nIT energy = `400 × 8,760 = 3,504,000 kWh`\nTotal energy = `720 × 8,760 = 6,307,200 kWh`\n\nStep 3: Target improvement. Containment, chiller upgrades and UPS replacement are projected to reduce PUE to 1.5 with the same IT load.\nNew total load = `400 × 1.5 = 600 kW`\nNew total energy = `600 × 8,760 = 5,256,000 kWh`\nAnnual saving = `6,307,200 − 5,256,000 = 1,051,200 kWh`\n\nStep 4: Financial value. Using an *illustrative* tariff of GHS 2.00 per kWh (an assumption for calculation only, not a quoted tariff):\nSaving = `1,051,200 × 2.00 = GHS 2,102,400` per year.\n\nStep 5: Carbon value. Using an *illustrative* grid emission factor of 0.4 kg CO2e per kWh (an assumption; real factors must be taken from official national or supplier sources):\nEmissions avoided = `1,051,200 × 0.4 = 420,480 kg ≈ 420 tonnes CO2e` per year.\n\nStep 6: Interpretation. The improvement saves about 17% of total facility energy (`1,051,200 / 6,307,200 ≈ 0.167`). If the firm also consolidated servers, reducing IT load to 320 kW while overhead fell only slightly, PUE might rise even though total energy fell, which shows why PUE should be reported alongside absolute energy and carbon figures."},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Akwaaba Microfinance* (fictional) runs an on-premises server room in Kumasi with ageing servers at low utilisation, domestic split air-conditioners running continuously at low set points, and a diesel generator that runs frequently during supply interruptions. Its laptops are replaced every three years and old equipment is stored in a cupboard or sold to informal collectors.\n\nA green IT plan might include:\n\n1. **Measure**: install metering to establish IT and total energy, generator fuel use and a baseline PUE.\n2. **Consolidate and virtualise**: move workloads onto two modern hosts, decommission unused servers and move suitable workloads to a cloud provider, considering data protection obligations.\n3. **Cooling**: seal the room, arrange equipment to separate hot and cold air, and raise set points within manufacturer guidance.\n4. **Power**: add solar photovoltaic panels and battery storage sized to reduce generator running hours.\n5. **Devices**: extend laptop life to four or five years with battery replacement and repair; buy energy-efficient models.\n6. **E-waste**: contract a licensed recycler, wipe data securely before disposal, and donate functioning equipment to schools where appropriate, in line with Act 917.\n7. **Report**: track energy cost, fuel use and emissions quarterly.\n\nThe business case combines lower electricity and fuel costs, improved resilience during outages, reduced data-breach risk from discarded drives, and reputational benefits with investors and partners who increasingly ask about environmental performance."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Machine learning for data centre cooling at Google\n\nIn 2016 Google and its AI research subsidiary DeepMind publicly reported that they had applied machine learning to data centre cooling. Using historical sensor data on temperatures, power, pump speeds and set points, neural networks predicted future PUE and temperatures and recommended actions to operators; Google reported a substantial reduction in the energy used for cooling at the facility where it was tested. Google later described moving towards more autonomous control of cooling, with safety constraints and the ability for operators to override.\n\nLessons for managers:\n\n- Once basic measures are in place, data and analytics can unlock further efficiency gains.\n- Comprehensive metering is a prerequisite; you cannot optimise what you do not measure.\n- Automated control of physical infrastructure needs safety limits and human override.\n- Efficiency is a competitive advantage for cloud providers, which is one reason why large cloud facilities tend to achieve lower PUE than small server rooms."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Grid, solar and generator power\"] --> B[\"Total facility energy\"]\n    B --> C[\"IT equipment: servers, storage, network\"]\n    B --> D[\"Overhead: cooling, UPS losses, lighting\"]\n    C --> E[\"Useful computing work\"]\n    C --> F[\"Heat\"]\n    F --> D\n    G[\"PUE = Total facility energy / IT equipment energy\"]\n    B -.-> G\n    C -.-> G\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** A low PUE means a data centre is environmentally friendly. **Correction:** PUE measures only infrastructure overhead; idle servers, carbon-intensive electricity and water use are not captured.","**Misconception:** Moving to the cloud is automatically green. **Correction:** Cloud facilities are usually more efficient, but over-provisioned or idle cloud resources still consume energy; the electricity source also matters.","**Misconception:** Energy use is the only environmental impact of IT. **Correction:** Manufacturing (embodied emissions), water use and end-of-life waste are also significant.","**Misconception:** Agbogbloshie was simply a dump for foreign e-waste. **Correction:** It was a complex informal scrap recycling hub that processed much locally generated material and supported many livelihoods, although its burning practices caused serious environmental and health harm.","**Misconception:** Recycling is always the best end-of-life option. **Correction:** Reuse and refurbishment usually deliver greater environmental benefit than recycling, which should follow when equipment can no longer be used."],"examMistakes":["Inverting the PUE formula (IT energy divided by total energy is DCiE, not PUE).","Forgetting to convert average kW to annual kWh by multiplying by 8,760 hours.","Presenting assumed tariffs or emission factors as real data without labelling them.","Describing Agbogbloshie with sensational or inaccurate claims, or ignoring livelihoods and local generation of waste.","Proposing green measures without a business case or measurement plan."],"criticalQuestions":["If consolidation lowers total energy but raises PUE, how should a data centre manager report performance to the board?","In Ghana''s climate, which data centre efficiency measures are most and least promising? Why?","Should Ghana restrict imports of used electronics, or would that harm affordability and digital inclusion? Weigh the arguments.","How could informal e-waste workers be integrated into a safer formal recycling system rather than displaced?","Is it better for a Ghanaian bank to build an efficient local data centre or to use a foreign hyperscale cloud region? Consider energy, sovereignty and resilience."],"exercises":["**PUE calculation.** A server room has an average IT load of 50 kW and total load of 95 kW. Calculate PUE, annual total energy and the annual saving if PUE falls to 1.6. *Expected output:* PUE = 1.9; total energy = 832,200 kWh; saving at PUE 1.6 = 131,400 kWh, with workings shown.","**Green IT audit.** Audit an office or computer laboratory you have access to. *Expected output:* an inventory of devices, estimated usage hours, observed waste (devices left on, idle equipment) and five prioritised recommendations.","**E-waste policy.** Draft a one-page e-waste policy for an illustrative Ghanaian SME. *Expected output:* sections on reuse, data sanitisation, licensed recyclers, record-keeping and compliance with Act 917."],"takeaways":["Green computing covers design, manufacture, use and disposal, not only electricity.","PUE = total facility energy / IT energy; 1.0 is the ideal, and it must be read alongside absolute energy, carbon and water.","Virtualisation, containment, efficient cooling and power, renewables and device-life extension are core measures.","Ghana''s climate, tariffs and supply interruptions make efficiency and solar especially valuable.","E-waste requires reuse, safe recycling and producer responsibility; Act 917 provides the legal framework.","Agbogbloshie should be described accurately: a complex informal recycling hub with serious harms, demolished in 2021."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"Murugesan, S. (2008). Harnessing green IT: Principles and practices. *IT Professional, 10*(1), 24-33.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"The Green Grid. (2012). *PUE: A comprehensive examination of the metric* (White Paper No. 49). The Green Grid.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"International Organization for Standardization & International Electrotechnical Commission. (2016). *Information technology, Data centres, Key performance indicators: Part 2: Power usage effectiveness (PUE)* (ISO/IEC 30134-2:2016).","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Republic of Ghana. (2016). *Hazardous and Electronic Waste Control and Management Act, 2016 (Act 917)*.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"The Green Grid","url":"https://www.thegreengrid.org/","description":"The Green Grid. Official site of the consortium that developed PUE, with resources on data centre efficiency metrics.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"International Energy Agency","url":"https://www.iea.org/","description":"IEA. Official site with analysis of energy use by data centres, networks and digital technologies.","verification":"unverified"},{"title":"United Nations Environment Programme","url":"https://www.unep.org/","description":"UNEP. Official site with material on e-waste, chemicals and sustainable consumption.","verification":"verified"}],"revisionSummary":"Green computing reduces the environmental impact of IT across its lifecycle. In data centres, PUE (total facility energy divided by IT energy) measures infrastructure overhead but not useful work, carbon or water, so it must be reported with absolute figures. Efficiency comes from virtualisation, retiring idle servers, efficient hardware, airflow containment, efficient cooling and power, renewables and longer device life. E-waste needs reuse, safe recycling and producer responsibility under Act 917; Agbogbloshie illustrates both harms of informal recycling and the risks of oversimplified narratives.\n\n- PUE = total / IT; DCiE = IT / total.\n- Annual kWh = average kW × 8,760.\n- Consolidation can raise PUE while cutting total energy.\n- Ghana: hot climate, high tariffs, generator use make efficiency and solar valuable.\n- Reuse before recycling; never dispose of drives without data sanitisation.\n- Label assumed tariffs and emission factors as illustrative."}'::jsonb, 3048, 1, true)
  on conflict (id) do update set module_id = excluded.module_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, topics = excluded.topics, estimated_minutes = excluded.estimated_minutes, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, source = excluded.source, content = excluded.content, word_count = excluded.word_count, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, is_published = excluded.is_published;
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, slug, title, summary, topics, estimated_minutes, difficulty, source, content, word_count, sort_order, is_published) values ('fef7b8bc-7a65-58e9-a198-12a44727d39a', '5b3b4db5-3611-52d0-8ee3-a393924d5603', 'sustainable-innovation-and-environmental-impact', 'Sustainable Innovation and Technology''s Environmental Impact', 'Analyse the direct and enabling environmental effects of technology, explain carbon accounting scopes 1-3 and the energy footprint of AI, and evaluate sustainable innovation strategies such as circular business models.', array['Sustainable innovation', 'Technology and environmental impact']::text[], 45, 'intermediate', 'supplementary', '{"introduction":"Technology has a double relationship with the environment. On one hand it is part of the problem: manufacturing devices consumes energy and scarce minerals, data centres and networks draw large amounts of electricity, and discarded electronics create hazardous waste. The rapid growth of artificial intelligence has made these questions newly urgent, as training and running large models requires substantial computing infrastructure. On the other hand technology is part of the solution: smart grids integrate renewable energy, sensors cut water and fertiliser use on farms, digital services replace some physical travel, and data allows organisations to measure and manage their emissions.\n\nSustainable innovation is the deliberate pursuit of new products, processes and business models that create economic value while reducing environmental and social harm. For businesses this is no longer a matter of public relations alone. Investors, regulators, large customers and employees increasingly expect credible measurement and disclosure of environmental impact. This lesson equips you to assess technology''s environmental effects honestly, to understand carbon accounting at an overview level, to discuss AI''s energy use qualitatively and accurately, and to evaluate innovation strategies that align profit with sustainability.","objectives":["Distinguish the direct, enabling and systemic (rebound) environmental effects of technology.","Explain the Greenhouse Gas Protocol''s scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions and apply them to an IT-intensive business.","Discuss, qualitatively and accurately, the energy and resource implications of AI.","Apply sustainable innovation frameworks, including the triple bottom line and the circular economy, to a business case.","Evaluate claims of \"green\" technology and identify the risk of greenwashing."],"prerequisites":["The previous lesson on green computing and energy efficiency, including PUE and e-waste.","General understanding of business strategy and innovation."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Sustainable innovation","definition":"Innovation in products, processes or business models that creates value while reducing environmental and social harm."},{"term":"Triple bottom line","definition":"A framework, associated with John Elkington, for assessing organisational performance on economic, social and environmental dimensions (\"profit, people, planet\")."},{"term":"Circular economy","definition":"An economic model that keeps products and materials in use for as long as possible through design for durability, reuse, repair, remanufacturing and recycling, rather than a linear \"take, make, dispose\" model."},{"term":"Scope 1 emissions","definition":"Direct greenhouse gas emissions from sources owned or controlled by an organisation, such as its generators and vehicles."},{"term":"Scope 2 emissions","definition":"Indirect emissions from the generation of purchased electricity, heat, steam or cooling consumed by the organisation."},{"term":"Scope 3 emissions","definition":"All other indirect emissions in the value chain, upstream (such as purchased goods, including IT equipment and cloud services) and downstream (such as use of sold products)."},{"term":"Rebound effect","definition":"The tendency for efficiency improvements to lower costs and thereby increase consumption, offsetting some or all of the expected savings."},{"term":"Greenwashing","definition":"Misleading claims that exaggerate the environmental benefits of a product, service or organisation."},{"term":"Enabling effect","definition":"Environmental benefit that a technology produces by changing other activities, such as reducing travel or optimising energy use."}],"body":[{"heading":"Detailed Explanation","markdown":"### Three orders of environmental effect\n\nResearchers on ICT and sustainability commonly distinguish three levels of effect, which together provide a balanced assessment.\n\n1. **Direct (first-order) effects**: the footprint of the technology itself, including mining, manufacturing, transport, electricity for operation, cooling, water use and end-of-life waste.\n2. **Enabling (second-order) effects**: changes the technology causes in other processes, such as route optimisation that reduces truck fuel, smart irrigation that reduces water use, videoconferencing that replaces some flights, or digital receipts that replace paper.\n3. **Systemic (third-order) effects**: longer-term changes in behaviour, markets and society, including **rebound effects**. When a technology makes something cheaper or easier, people may do more of it. The economist William Stanley Jevons observed in the nineteenth century that more efficient steam engines led to greater, not lower, coal consumption, an idea now known as the Jevons paradox.\n\nA credible assessment considers all three. A ride-hailing app may reduce empty taxi trips (enabling) but also increase total car travel if it attracts people away from walking or buses (rebound).\n\n### Carbon accounting: scopes 1, 2 and 3\n\nThe Greenhouse Gas (GHG) Protocol, developed by the World Resources Institute and the World Business Council for Sustainable Development, is the most widely used framework for corporate emissions accounting. Its Corporate Standard groups emissions into three scopes.\n\n| Scope | Definition | IT-related examples for a Ghanaian bank |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scope 1 | Direct emissions from owned or controlled sources | Diesel burned in backup generators at branches and the data centre; fuel for company vehicles; refrigerant leaks from owned air-conditioning |\n| Scope 2 | Indirect emissions from purchased electricity, heat or cooling | Grid electricity used by the head office, branches and on-premises servers |\n| Scope 3 | All other value-chain emissions | Manufacturing of purchased laptops, ATMs and servers; cloud services bought from providers; business travel; employee commuting; disposal of equipment |\n\nSeveral points matter for IT managers:\n\n- **Moving to the cloud shifts emissions between scopes.** Electricity used by on-premises servers is scope 2 for the bank; once the same workload runs in a cloud provider''s data centre, it becomes part of the bank''s scope 3 (purchased services). Emissions do not disappear, although they may fall if the provider is more efficient or uses cleaner power.\n- **Scope 3 is usually the largest and hardest to measure.** It depends on data from suppliers, which is often estimated.\n- **Location-based and market-based scope 2.** The GHG Protocol''s scope 2 guidance allows reporting based on the average emissions of the local grid (location-based) and based on contractual instruments such as renewable energy purchases (market-based).\n- **Emission factors** convert activity data (kWh, litres of fuel) into tonnes of CO2-equivalent; they must come from authoritative, documented sources.\n\n### The environmental footprint of AI\n\nArtificial intelligence, especially large-scale generative AI, has become a prominent sustainability concern. The main points can be stated accurately without precise figures, which vary widely by model, hardware, data centre and electricity source:\n\n- **Training** large models requires running thousands of specialised accelerators (GPUs or similar) for weeks or months, consuming substantial electricity. Strubell, Ganesh and McCallum (2019) helped draw attention to the energy and carbon costs of training large natural-language models.\n- **Inference**, the everyday use of trained models to answer queries, is far less intensive per request but occurs billions of times, so in aggregate it can exceed the energy used for training.\n- **Data centre growth**: the International Energy Agency and others have highlighted AI as a significant driver of growth in data centre electricity demand, creating pressure on electricity grids in some regions and on companies'' climate targets.\n- **Water use**: data centre cooling often consumes water directly, and electricity generation consumes water indirectly.\n- **Hardware and materials**: accelerators require advanced semiconductor manufacturing, with its own energy, water and mineral demands, and short hardware refresh cycles increase e-waste.\n- **Location and timing matter**: the same computation can have very different emissions depending on the carbon intensity of the local grid and the time of day.\n- **Mitigation options** include using smaller or more efficient models where adequate, reusing pre-trained models rather than training from scratch, efficient hardware, high-utilisation data centres, scheduling flexible work when renewable supply is high, and choosing providers powered by low-carbon electricity.\n\nAI can also produce enabling benefits, such as optimising energy systems, improving weather and climate prediction, and reducing waste in logistics and agriculture. The net effect depends on how and for what AI is used.\n\n### Sustainable innovation\n\nSustainable innovation operates at several levels:\n\n- **Product innovation**: energy-efficient, durable, repairable or recyclable products.\n- **Process innovation**: cleaner, less wasteful production and operations.\n- **Business model innovation**: new ways of creating and capturing value, such as product-as-a-service (leasing devices and taking them back), refurbishment marketplaces, sharing platforms and pay-as-you-go access to solar energy.\n- **Systemic innovation**: changes across value chains and infrastructure, such as smart grids or circular supply chains."},{"heading":"Theories, Frameworks and Models","markdown":"### The Porter hypothesis and the natural-resource-based view\n\nPorter and van der Linde (1995) argued that well-designed environmental regulation can stimulate innovation that reduces costs and improves competitiveness, because pollution often signals wasted resources. Hart (1995) proposed a natural-resource-based view of the firm, in which capabilities in pollution prevention, product stewardship and sustainable development can become sources of competitive advantage. Both perspectives challenge the assumption that environmental performance and profit are always in conflict, although critics note that win-win outcomes are not guaranteed.\n\n### The triple bottom line\n\nElkington''s (1997) triple bottom line asks organisations to account for social and environmental performance alongside financial results. Its value is in broadening what managers measure; its weakness is that the three dimensions are hard to compare, and it can be used selectively. Elkington himself later called for the concept to be applied more rigorously rather than as a reporting slogan.\n\n### The circular economy\n\nThe Ellen MacArthur Foundation popularised the circular economy as an alternative to the linear model. For IT the key loops, in order of preference, are:\n\n1. **Maintain and extend life** (repair, software support, battery replacement).\n2. **Reuse and redistribute** (second-hand markets, donation).\n3. **Refurbish and remanufacture** (restoring devices or components).\n4. **Recycle** materials when the above are no longer possible.\n\nThe inner loops preserve more value and avoid more embodied emissions than recycling.\n\n### Evaluating green claims: a checklist\n\n| Question | Warning sign of greenwashing |\n|---|---|\n| Is the claim specific and measurable? | Vague words such as \"eco-friendly\" or \"green\" with no data |\n| Does it cover the whole lifecycle? | Highlighting a small benefit while ignoring a large impact elsewhere |\n| Are boundaries and scopes clear? | \"Carbon neutral\" claims that omit scope 3 or rely only on offsets |\n| Is there independent verification? | Self-declared labels and unverifiable statistics |\n| Are rebound effects considered? | Efficiency claims that ignore increased use |"},{"heading":"Ghanaian Business Application","markdown":"**Illustrative scenario.** *Sankofa Devices* (a fictional Accra start-up) proposes a circular business model for laptops sold to universities, schools and SMEs. Rather than selling new devices outright, it offers refurbished, business-grade laptops on a subscription that includes repairs, battery replacement, software updates and guaranteed take-back after three to five years. Returned devices are refurbished again or stripped for parts, and residual components are passed to a licensed recycler in line with the Hazardous and Electronic Waste Control and Management Act, 2016 (Act 917).\n\nAnalysis using this lesson''s frameworks:\n\n- **Direct effects**: extending device life reduces embodied emissions per year of use and reduces e-waste; refurbishment workshops use some energy.\n- **Enabling effects**: affordable devices support digital learning and SME productivity.\n- **Rebound**: lower prices could increase total device numbers, though if they replace new purchases the net effect is likely positive.\n- **Business model**: recurring revenue, customer lock-in through service quality, and value recovery from returned devices; risks include supply of quality used devices, warranty costs, and competition from cheap imported second-hand equipment.\n- **Carbon accounting**: for a client university, the subscription affects scope 3 (purchased goods and services); Sankofa can provide documented estimates to support the client''s reporting.\n- **Social dimension**: the start-up could recruit and train technicians from the informal repair sector, offering safer, formal work.\n\nA second **illustrative scenario** concerns a telecom tower company replacing diesel generators at off-grid sites with solar and battery hybrids, reducing scope 1 emissions, fuel costs, theft of diesel and maintenance visits, at the cost of higher upfront capital."},{"heading":"International Case Study","markdown":"### Fairphone\n\nFairphone is a Dutch social enterprise that emerged from a campaign about conflict minerals in electronics and began selling smartphones in 2013. Its strategy is a well-documented example of sustainable innovation through product and business model design. Later models are modular, allowing users to replace components such as the battery, screen and camera with basic tools, and the company emphasises long software support periods, sourcing of fairer and recycled materials, and improving working conditions in its supply chain.\n\nLessons for managers:\n\n- Durability and repairability can be designed in, directly addressing embodied emissions and e-waste.\n- A sustainability mission can define a niche brand, but scale, cost and competition with large manufacturers remain challenging.\n- Transparency about supply chains and remaining shortcomings builds credibility and reduces greenwashing risk.\n- Regulatory developments, such as moves in the European Union towards repairability and replaceable batteries, can turn early sustainable innovators'' practices into industry norms, consistent with the Porter hypothesis."},{"heading":"Diagram","markdown":"```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Technology\"] --> B[\"Direct effects: manufacturing, electricity, water, e-waste\"]\n    A --> C[\"Enabling effects: optimised energy, logistics, agriculture, less travel\"]\n    A --> D[\"Systemic effects: behaviour change, rebound (Jevons paradox)\"]\n    B --> E[\"Net environmental impact\"]\n    C --> E\n    D --> E\n    E --> F[\"Carbon accounting: Scope 1 direct, Scope 2 purchased energy, Scope 3 value chain\"]\n    F --> G[\"Sustainable innovation: products, processes, circular business models\"]\n```"}],"misconceptions":["**Misconception:** Digital technology is inherently environmentally friendly because it is \"virtual\". **Correction:** It has substantial physical footprints in manufacturing, electricity, water and waste.","**Misconception:** Moving workloads to the cloud eliminates their emissions. **Correction:** Emissions shift from scope 2 to scope 3; they may fall if the provider is more efficient or uses cleaner power, but they do not vanish.","**Misconception:** Efficiency improvements always reduce total consumption. **Correction:** Rebound effects can offset savings when lower costs increase use.","**Misconception:** AI''s environmental impact comes only from training. **Correction:** Inference at scale, hardware manufacture and water use also matter, and aggregate inference can exceed training energy.","**Misconception:** Recycling is the core of the circular economy. **Correction:** Life extension, reuse and refurbishment preserve more value and should come first; recycling is the last loop."],"examMistakes":["Confusing scope 2 (purchased electricity) with scope 3 (value chain), especially for cloud services.","Quoting precise statistics about AI or data centre energy without a reliable source.","Discussing only negative or only positive effects of technology rather than direct, enabling and systemic effects together.","Treating the triple bottom line or circular economy as slogans without applying them to the case.","Accepting green claims at face value without considering lifecycle, scope and verification."],"criticalQuestions":["Should a Ghanaian firm prioritise reducing its scope 1 diesel emissions or its scope 3 emissions from purchased IT? What data would you need to decide?","Is it ethical for organisations to adopt generative AI widely when its energy demands are uncertain and growing? Under what conditions is it justified?","Can the circular economy succeed in Ghana where an informal repair and reuse culture already exists? How could formal businesses build on it rather than displace it?","When does carbon offsetting become greenwashing?","Does the Porter hypothesis hold for small firms in developing economies, or mainly for large firms in rich economies?"],"exercises":["**Scope classification.** Classify twelve emission sources of an illustrative Ghanaian telecom company (tower generators, office electricity, purchased handsets for resale, staff flights, cloud hosting, refrigerant leaks, fleet fuel, customer phone charging, network equipment manufacture, employee commuting, leased office cooling, waste disposal). *Expected output:* a table assigning each to scope 1, 2 or 3 with justification.","**Three-order assessment.** Choose one technology (for example drone delivery, mobile money, or an AI chatbot for customer service). *Expected output:* a table of direct, enabling and systemic effects with an overall judgement.","**Greenwashing review.** Find a published sustainability claim by a technology company. *Expected output:* an evaluation using the five-question checklist and a rewritten, more credible version of the claim.","**Circular business model canvas.** Design a circular model for mobile phones in Ghana. *Expected output:* a short canvas covering value proposition, customers, take-back, refurbishment, revenue, partners and risks."],"takeaways":["Assess technology through direct, enabling and systemic (rebound) effects.","GHG Protocol: scope 1 direct, scope 2 purchased energy, scope 3 value chain; cloud moves emissions into scope 3.","AI''s footprint includes training, large-scale inference, hardware and water; location and model choice matter.","Sustainable innovation spans products, processes, business models and systems.","Circular strategies prioritise life extension and reuse over recycling.","Specific, lifecycle-based, verified claims avoid greenwashing."],"furtherReading":[{"text":"World Resources Institute & World Business Council for Sustainable Development. (2004). *The greenhouse gas protocol: A corporate accounting and reporting standard* (Rev. ed.). WRI/WBCSD.","url":"https://ghgprotocol.org/","verification":"verified"},{"text":"Elkington, J. (1997). *Cannibals with forks: The triple bottom line of 21st century business*. Capstone.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Porter, M. E., & van der Linde, C. (1995). Toward a new conception of the environment-competitiveness relationship. *Journal of Economic Perspectives, 9*(4), 97-118.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Hart, S. L. (1995). A natural-resource-based view of the firm. *Academy of Management Review, 20*(4), 986-1014.","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Strubell, E., Ganesh, A., & McCallum, A. (2019). Energy and policy considerations for deep learning in NLP. In *Proceedings of the 57th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics* (pp. 3645-3650).","url":null,"verification":"citation"},{"text":"Ellen MacArthur Foundation. (2013). *Towards the circular economy* (Vol. 1). Ellen MacArthur Foundation.","url":null,"verification":"citation"}],"videos":[{"title":"Greenhouse Gas Protocol","url":"https://ghgprotocol.org/","description":"WRI and WBCSD. Official site with standards, guidance and training material on scope 1, 2 and 3 accounting.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"Ellen MacArthur Foundation","url":"https://www.ellenmacarthurfoundation.org/","description":"Ellen MacArthur Foundation. Official site with explanatory material and videos on the circular economy.","verification":"verified"},{"title":"International Energy Agency","url":"https://www.iea.org/","description":"IEA. Official site with analysis of energy demand from data centres and AI.","verification":"unverified"}],"revisionSummary":"Technology''s environmental impact combines direct effects (manufacture, energy, water, waste), enabling effects (efficiency gains elsewhere) and systemic effects, including rebound. The GHG Protocol divides emissions into scope 1 (direct), scope 2 (purchased energy) and scope 3 (value chain); cloud adoption shifts emissions into scope 3. AI adds energy, water and hardware demands through training and especially large-scale inference, but also offers enabling benefits. Sustainable innovation, guided by the Porter hypothesis, the natural-resource-based view, the triple bottom line and the circular economy, seeks value with lower harm while avoiding greenwashing.\n\n- Direct + enabling + systemic = net impact.\n- Scope 1 generators; scope 2 grid power; scope 3 purchased IT and cloud.\n- AI: training plus inference plus hardware plus water; choose efficient models and clean power.\n- Circular loops: maintain, reuse, refurbish, then recycle.\n- Fairphone: designed-in repairability as sustainable innovation.\n- Test green claims for specificity, lifecycle scope and verification."}'::jsonb, 2914, 2, true)
